"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3430],{3430:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"subject":"Forensic Medicine","slug":"forensic-medicine","questions":[{"bankId":"qb-18611a16b9","n":1,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"A 14-year-old rape victim presents with 26 weeks of pregnancy. What should not be done?","options":["A female doctor should examine the patient","Vaginal swab examination is not necessary","No need to confirm pregnancy by UPT","Termination of pregnancy with her consent by a Gynaecologist"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Termination of pregnancy with her consent by a Gynaecologist\\n\\nAccording to the MTP Act 2021 amendments, pregnancy beyond 24 weeks of gestation cannot be terminated. Also, the guardian\'s written consent is required in the case of a minor girl.\\n\\n• Up to 20 weeks of pregnancy, any qualified practitioner can perform an abortion. However, he must have undergone compulsory training.\\n• Between 20-24 weeks of pregnancy, the opinion of a second practitioner must be obtained to justify performing the abortion.\\n• The woman to be wanting an abortion should be above 18 years of age to consent. If not, consent can be obtained from the parents or guardian when she is a minor or mentally unsound.\\n\\nIn the cases of sexual assault of a female child, a female doctor is appointed for the examination (Option A). In case, such a doctor is unavailable, then the head of the institution can nominate a woman for that purpose. These examinations are carried out in the presence of a parent or guardian.\\n\\nVaginal swab samples are typically collected within 96 hours (ideally up to 72 hours), as the chances of detecting evidence decrease significantly after this period. (Option B)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8971017880","n":2,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"A 30-year-old drowsy male was brought to the casualty. His pulse was 130/min, respiratory rate -30/min. His lab reports are given below. He showed improvement on nasogastric aspiration and administration of sodium bicarbonate, calcium gluconate, and 4-methyl pyrazole. What substance did he most likely consume?\\n\\nABG - metabolic acidosis\\n\\nTotal serum calcium - 6.8 mg/dL\\n\\nBlood Urea – 100 mg/dl\\n\\nCreatinine – 3.4 mg/dl\\n\\nUrine analysis - Calcium oxalate crystals.","options":["Formaldehyde","Ethylene glycol","Paraldehyde","Methyl alcohol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ethylene glycol\\n\\nThe given scenario point towards ethylene glycol poisoning.\\n\\nEthylene glycol\xa0is converted to\xa0glyoxyaldehyde\xa0by alcohol dehydrogenase.\xa0Glyoxyaldehyde causes kidney damage.\\n\\nMethanol is converted to formaldehyde\xa0by alcohol dehydrogenase. Formaldehyde causes retinal damage\xa0like blurring of vision/loss of vision.\\n\\nThe principles of treatment for both these conditions are the same. It predominantly involves using fomipezole or ethanol to inhibit the metabolic conversion of these relatively non-toxic alcohols to their toxic metabolites.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0d9ee986e5","n":3,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"A 35-year-old male came to the OPD with complaints of an increase in his heart rate and palpitations. On examination, there was episodic tachycardia and occasional extrasystole. Ocular examination showed visual field defects. Which of the following is the likely cause?","options":["Cocaine","Nicotine","Cannabis","Atropine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Nicotine\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0nicotine poisoning.\\n\\nChronic poisoning of nicotine also known as nicotine addiction leads to a condition called\xa0tobacco heart.\xa0It is\xa0characterized by irregularities of the heart with occasional extrasystoles and angina. It also causes amblyopia and narrowing of the field of vision.\\n\\nEffects on CVS and eyes by different poisons are given below:\\n\\nPoison\\nEffect on CVS\\nEffect on ocular system\\n\\nCocaine\\n\\n1. Tachycardia\\n2. Systemic arterial hypertension\\n3. MI (due to coronary vasoconstriction)\\n4. Tachyarrhythmias\\n5. Dilated cardiomyopathy\\n6. Aortic dissection & rupture\\n\\n1. Madarosis (Loss of eyebrow/eyelashes)\\n2. Corneal abrasions/ulcerations\\n3. CRAO\\n4. Bilateral blindness\\n\\nNicotine\\n\\n1. Irregularities of heart with occasional extrasystole\\n2. Episodes of chest pain suggestive of angina\\n\\n1. Amblyopia\\n2. Narrowing of field of vision\\n3. Blindness\\n\\nCannabis\\n\\n1. Tachycardia\\n2. Palpitation\\n3. Hypertension\\n\\n1. Bloodshot eyes (conjunctival congestions),\\n2. Occasional mydriasis\\n3. Nystagmus\\n\\nAtropine\\n\\n1. Tachycardia\\n2. Arrhythmias\\n\\n1. Dilated pupil (Mydriasis)\\n2. Diplopia","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9c251969ab","n":4,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"A person was brought dead and an autopsy was performed to find the cause of death. The following findings were noted on examination. What is the probable cause of death?","options":["Throttling","Strangulation","Hanging","Mugging"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Throttling\\n\\nThe given autposy images are suggestive of throttling.\\n\\nIn throttling, the following mechanisms may lead to death:\\n\\n• Occlusion of\xa0blood\xa0supply to the blood to the brain - carotid arteries\\n• Occlusion of the airway - minor role\\n• Carotid sinus pressure may result in vagus nerve stimulation resulting in cardiac arrest (vasovagal shock)\\n\\nOther signs in deaths due to asphyxia:\\n\\n• Petechial hemorrhages in the face, eyebrows, eyelids, forehead, ears, conjunctiva, sclera\\n\\n• When they occur in the visceral pleura, they are called\xa0Tardieu’s spots\\n\\n• Congested larynx with slight oozing\\n• Marked venous congestion of internal organs\\n• Emphysematous, congested\xa0 lungs with serosanguinous fluid exudates in alveoli\\n• Dark-coloured blood due to accumulation of CO2\\n\\nOption B: Strangulation is a very generalised term used to describe deaths due to violent asphyxia. Eg- ligature strangulation, throttling etc\\n\\nOption C:\xa0 In the case of hanging, there will be a ligature mark, injury to the cervical spine etc.\\n\\nOption D: In case of mugging the neck is compressed or squeezed by holding it between the crook of the elbow or knee. The attack is usually made from behind. Though a victim killed by this method shows signs of asphyxia and other struggle marks, the examination neck will not show any evidence of violence, as the compression was affected by the soft muscles of the arm or leg.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/5c23648e1ce31547a256.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-60a5f62833","n":5,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"An autopsy was performed to find the cause of death of a person brought dead to the casualty and it showed a fracture of the thyroid cartilage and bruising around the neck. The cause of death is?","options":["Hanging","Throttling","Strangulation","Mugging"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Throttling\\n\\nBoth bruising around the neck\xa0and the fracture of the thyroid cartilage\xa0are findings seen in throttling.\xa0Throttling/manual strangulation is when bare hands are used to compress the neck and strangulate.\\n\\nMechanism of death:\\n\\n• Occlusion of blood vessels supplying blood to the brain (carotid arteries).\\n• Occlusion to airway probably plays a minor role if any, in causing death\\n• Carotid sinus pressure may result in vagus nerve stimulation, resulting in cardiac arrest (vagal inhibition)\\n\\nAutopsy findings:\\n\\n• Bruising of neck: due to the grasping of the neck by the assailant.\\n• Abrasions on the neck: fingernail marks from assailant or victim\\n• Fracture of thyroid cartilage\\n• Inward fracture of greater cornu of the hyoid bone.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3adfa11db2","n":6,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Black foot is seen in which poisoning","options":["Arsenic","Lead","Mercury","Phosphorus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Arsenic\\n\\nBlackfoot\'s\xa0disease\xa0 is a form of arsenic induced peripheral vascular disease resulting in gangrene.\\n\\n• Arsenic is a heavy metallic inorganic irritant poison. Metallic arsenic is not poisonous as it is insoluble in water and cannot be absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract. However arsenious oxide or arsenic trioxide (sankhyal or somalkar) is poisonous.\\n• They are found in shell fish, cod, and haddock.\\n• Arsenic compounds act by inactivating the sulfhydril enzymes, which in turn interfere with the cellular metabolism, in the liver, lungs, intestinal wall, and spleen. Arsenic can replace phosphorus in the bones where it may remain for years. It also gets deposited in the hairs.\\n• Chronic poisoning with arsenic presents with a sequence of five different set of manifestations.\\n\\n• Gastrointestinal : The victim presents with gradual weight loss, malnutrition, fatigue, loss of appetite, cirrhosis of liver, nausea, vomiting, etc.\\n• Catarrhal changes : \xa0The victim presents with running nose, headache, conjunctivitis, bronchial catarrh, etc.\\n• Raindrop pigmentation : It is known to produce milk and roses complexion initially, followed by patchy brown pigmentation of the skin (especially face), which resembles the raindrops. It might also show hyperkeratosis of the skin of the palm and soles (Black Foot), which is prone to change into basal cell carcinoma at a later stage. The scalp may also show alopecia (baldness).\\n• Meese’s lines : The victim’s nail manifests with whitish lines 1 to 2 mm breadth across the nail of the finger and toes representing the deposition of the poison as a result of high sulphhydril content of the keratin.\\n• Arsenical neuritis : The victim presents with polyneuritis, optic neuritis, anesthesias, paresthesias, atrophy of extensors resulting in wrist and foot drop, etc.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-140de916a1","n":7,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Criminal responsibility of Insane is defined in:","options":["McNaughten’s Rule","Currens Rule","Durham’s Rule","American Law Institute Test"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. McNaughten’s Rule\\n\\n• McNaughten’s Rule was formed by the Judges of McNaughten’s case, to clarify the common law of insanity in England during 1843. It is similar to IPC 84/ BNS 22 which states “Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who at the time of doing it by reason of unsoundness of mind is incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or that he is doing what is either wrong or contrary to law”.\\n• This forms the basis of the ‘test’ of insanity that the jury should be instructed to apply in each case of alleged insanity.\\n• The rules are:\\n\\n• Every man is presumed to be sane and to possess a sufficient degree of reason to be responsible for his crimes, until the contrary be proved to their satisfaction.\\n• To establish an insanity defence, it must be clearly proved that, at the time of the committing of the act, the accused was laboring under ‘such a defect of reason, from a disease of the mind, as not to know the nature and quality of the act he was doing; or if he did know it, that he did not know he was doing what was wrong’.\\n\\n• Curren\'s rule of 1964 states that an accused person is not criminally responsible, if at the time of commiting the act, he did not have the capacity to regulate his conduct to the requirements of the law, as a result of mental disease or defect.\\n• Durham \'s rule of 1954 States that an accused person is not criminally responsible if his unlawful act is the product of mental disease or mental defect.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8efb20419a","n":8,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"First carpal bone to ossify:","options":["Lunate","Capitate","Hamate","Scaphoid"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Capitate\\n\\nCapitate is the first carpal bone and pisiform is the last\xa0carpal bone to ossify.\\n\\nAge of ossification centres of carpal bones:\\n\\nCarpal bone\\nAge of ossification\\n\\nCapitate\\n1-3 months\\n\\nHamate\\n\\n2-4 months\\n\\nTriquetrum\\n\\n2-3 years\\n\\nLunate\\n2-4 years\\n\\nScaphoid\\n4-6 years\\n\\nTrapezium\\n4-6 years\\n\\nTrapezoid\\n4-6 years\\n\\nPisiform\\n8-12 years","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7fa52c98ef","n":9,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"In a case of a gun shot injury to skull with entry wound at left temporal region surrounded by abrasion collar & tattooing and a exit wound at right temporal region along with metal fragments of bullet inside the bullet track. Which of the following should be preserved to determine the range of firing ?","options":["Hair","Bullet pieces","Blood","Clothes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Hair\\n\\nThe main objective of the study of a firearm wound (Ballistics) is to establish the range and direction of the firing. The ultimate purpose is to determine the weapon used for firing.\\n\\nFirearm wounds are of two fundamental types: wounds of entry and wound of exit.\\n\\nThe wound of entry is the wound through which the bullet or pellets enter the body, and the wound of exit is the wound through which the bullet emerges out of the body.\\n\\nTo determine the Range of firing, the following parameters are used:\\n\\n• Entry wound size and shape\\n• Blast effect – Muzzle end imprint and edge eversion\\n• Blackening, Tattooing, Singeing of hair, Abrasion collar, and Grease collar.\\n\\nIn the above options, Hair should be preserved in order to determine the range of firing which can be examined for carbonization/desiccation in close range, also from lead deposition around the wound to assess the range of firing.\\n\\nCharacteristics\\nContact shot\\nClose Shot\\nNear Shot\\nDistant Shot\\n\\nRange\\nIn-touch with skin\\n2.5-7.5 cm\\n30-60 cm\\n>60 cm\\n\\nSize\\nLargest size\\nBullet size\\nSmaller\\nSmallest\\n\\nShape\\nStellate\\nCircular\\nCircular\\nCircular\\n\\nBlast effect\\n\\nMuzzle end imprint\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nEdge eversion\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nOther findings\\n\\nBlackening\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nTattooing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nSingeing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nAbrasion collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n\\nGrease collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-396a94fdde","n":10,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Jack knife position causes death due to:","options":["Wedging","Burking","Positional asphyxia","Traumatic asphyxia"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Positional asphyxia\\n\\nJack-knife position\xa0leads to\xa0positional asphyxia.\\n\\nIt is a position where the victim\'s thighs and knees are driven against the chest causing indirect compression of the chest and restriction of breathing movements.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-129f1993b0","n":11,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Match the following:\\n\\n1) Cocaine\\n\\n2) LSD\\n\\n3) Abrus precatorius\\n\\n4) Capsaicin\\n\\na) Hunan hand\\n\\nb) White lady\\n\\nc) Purple wedge\\n\\nd) Gunchi","options":["1=a, 2=b, 3=c, 4=d","1=b, 2=c, 3=d, 4=a","1=d, 2=a, 3=b, 4=c","1=c, 2=d, 3=a, 4=b"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1=b, 2=c, 3=d, 4=a\\n\\n• Common popular street names for cocaine are—Snuff, Rock, Crack, Coke, Snow, Cadillac, White lady, etc\\n• Common popular street names for LSD are –acid, blotter acid, blue caps, blue dots, brown caps, crackers, Deeda, green caps, orange wedges, Paisley caps, pink dots, pink wedges, purple Owsleys, purple wedges, The Beast, The Chief, The Ghost, The Hawk, white lightning, window panes, yellow caps, yellow dots, 25.\\n• Common names of Abrus precatorius are Jequirity bean, rosary pea, Buddhist rosary bead, rosary bead, Indian bead, Indian liquorice, Seminole bead, prayer head, crab’s eye, weather plant, lucky bean, ojo de pajaro, gulagunchi, rati, gunja\\n• Capsaicin, active principle of red chilly or capsicum cause Dermatitis and burning of hands and fingers known as ‘Hunan hand’ which is common among the pickle industry workers, who use their hands for handling chilly paste or powder for prolonged period.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-21a05838b7","n":12,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Order of appearance of rigor mortis:","options":["Orbicularis oculi, facial muscles, thorax, upper limb","Orbicularis oculi, neck, facial muscles, upper limb, thorax","Neck, muscles of eyelid, upper limb, thorax","Neck, muscles of eyelid, facial muscles, thorax, upper limb"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Orbicularis oculi, facial muscles, thorax, upper limb\\n\\nThe correct order is orbicularis oculi, facial muscles, thorax, and upper limb.\\n\\nNysten\'s rule\xa0describes the sequential onset of\xa0rigor mortis\xa0in various\xa0muscle groups.\\n\\nRigor mortis begins from the head and progresses down the body:\\n\\n• The order of appearance of rigor mortis is heart (left chamber)\xa0→\xa0eyelids\xa0→\xa0muscles of the neck\xa0→ lower jaw\xa0→\xa0muscles of the face\xa0→ muscles of the chest(thorax)\xa0→ upper limb\xa0→\xa0abdomen\xa0→ lower limb\xa0→ fingers and toes (proximo-distal progression)\\n\\nIt disappears in the same order of appearance.\\n\\nNote that in option B, the upper limb is mentioned before thorax and hence is not the answer.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8696109cd0","n":13,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Which of the following is false regarding teeth features and ethnicity?","options":["In Africanoids the cusps of molars are wide and deep and shovel shaped cusps in incisors","Caucasians have carabelli cusps","Upper third molar is most commonly absent in Mongolians","Prominent lingual ridge and labial ridge in mongols"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. In Africanoids the cusps of molars are wide and deep and shovel shaped cusps in incisors\\n\\nThe cusps of molars are wide and deep and cusps of incisors are shovel-shaped in Mongoloid races.\\n\\nAfricanoid\xa0races tend to have large teeth and often have more cusps on their molars, even up to eight, with two lingual cusps on the mandibular first premolars as an additional common finding.\\n\\nIn Caucasian races, the lateral incisors in the upper jaw are usually smaller than the central. They also have long pointed canine roots. Small nodules on the lingual surface of maxillary molars, called Carabelli’s cusp\xa0are most common in Caucasian races.\\n\\nIn mongoloid teeth, enamel pearls and small nodules of enamel on the tooth surface, are much more frequent. The condition of bull-tooth or taurodontism\xa0is most common in Mongoloid people. The pulp cavity of molars is wide and deep, and the roots are fused and bent. A congenital lack of the third upper molar is most commonly seen. The lingual and labial ridges are more prominent.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a8ebe8b5ec","n":14,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Which of the following is true regarding corporobasal index of the sacrum?","options":["Breadth of fifth lumbar vertebra X 100 / breadth of all sacral vertebra","Breadth of first sacral vertebra X 100 / breadth of fifth lumbar vertebra","Breadth of fifth lumbar vertebra X 100 / breadth of base of sacrum","Breadth of first sacral vertebra X 100 / breadth of base of sacrum"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Breadth of first sacral vertebra X 100 / breadth of base of sacrum\\n\\nCorporobasal index is given by the formula:\\n\\nCorporobasal index = Breadth of first sacral vertebra x 100 / breadth of the base of the sacrum.\\n\\nCorporobasal index of the sacrum is one of the indices used to determine the sex of the individual.\\nIn males, it is >42 and in females, it is <42.\\n\\nOther indices to determine the sex of the individual are:\\n\\nIndex\\nFormula\\nMale\\nFemale\\n\\nWashburn\\nischiopubic index\\nPubic length in mm x100\\nIschial length in mm\\n73-94\\n91-115\\n\\nSciatic notch index\\nWidth of the sciatic notch\xa0 x100\\nDepth of sciatic notch\\n4-5\\n5-6\\n\\nSacral index\\n\\nTransverse diameter of base of sacrum X100\\nAnterior length of sacrum\\n\\n<114\\n>114\\n\\nSternal\xa0index\\nLength of manubrium X100\\nLength of body\\n46.2\\n54.3\\n\\nMedullary index of\\nlong bones\\nDiameter of medulla X 100\\nDiameter of whole bone","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4bc39685ea","n":15,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"A girl was allegedly kidnapped by a man. In court, she says that she is a major and is married to the man. X-ray images of her elbow, wrist and pelvis are given. What is the approximate age of the girl ?","options":["14 to 15 years","16 to 17 years","18 to 19 years","21 to 22 years"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 21 to 22 years\\n\\nThe most approximate age of this girl is 21-22 years.\\n\\nSince the ischial tuberosity is fused in this scenario the girl must be aged >21 years.\\n\\nBone/ joint\\nMaximum age at fusion\\nInterpretation of given x-ray\\n\\nElbow joint centres (lower end of humerus, upper end of radius, olecranon to ulna)\\n16 years\\nFused, >16 years\\n\\nWrist joint centres (lower end of ulna and radius)\\n19 years\\nFused, > 19 years\\n\\nHip bone centres (head of femur, greater and lesser trochanter, ischial tuberosity)\\n22 years\\nFused, > 21 years","img":false,"images":["/bimg/5267c17b04181cf012a7.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-a1b94e0864","n":16,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"A registered medical practitioner is requested by a survivor of sexual violence to examine her. Within what time must the practitioner take a vaginal swab to look for the presence of spermatozoa?","options":["Within 72 hours","Within 36 hours","Within 5 days","Within 48 hours"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Within 72 hours\\n\\nA vaginal swab of a rape victim demonstrates spermatozoa if the vaginal swab is taken within 72 hours after an assault.\\n\\nHowever, a vaginal swab can be taken within 96 hours to demonstrate semen.\\n\\nEvidence on the outside of the body and on materials such as clothing can be collected even after 96 hours.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4301acbb99","n":17,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"According to POCSO, aggravated sexual assault includes:","options":["Sexual assault done by police officer","Threatening","Gang penetrative sexual assault","All of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Sexual assault done by police officer\\n\\nSexual assault done by a police officer is an aggravated sexual assault according to POCSO (Protection of Children from Sexual Offences).\\n\\nThe POCSO act defines a child as any person below eighteen years of age. It defines various forms of sexual offenses which include:\\n\\n1. Sexual harassment -\xa0Threatening\\n2. Sexual assault -\xa0Any act with sexual intent that involves physical contact without penetration.\\n3. Aggravated sexual assault -\xa0Similar to sexual assault along with the criteria explained under the term aggravated\\n4. Penetrative sexual assault -\xa0An act with sexual intent that involves physical\xa0penetration\xa0of the penis or any object into the vagina, mouth, urethra, or anus.\\n5. Aggravated penetrative sexual assault -\xa0Similar to penetrative sexual assault along with the criteria explained under the term aggravated\\n6. Pornography\\n\\nThe term aggravated\xa0is used for those assaults done:\\n\\n• By a person in a position of trust or authority such as a police officer, security force, relative, doctor\\n• In the course of communal violence\\n• On mentally & physically disabled\\n• Gang sexual assault\\n• Using deadly weapons\\n• Causing grievous hurt\\n\\nDepending on if there was penetration or not, it can be aggravated sexual assault (if there is no penetration) or aggravated penetrative sexual assault (if there was penetration). Both are different.\\n\\nThe Act prescribes stringent punishment graded as per the gravity of the offence, with a maximum term of rigorous imprisonment for life and fine.\\n\\nNote-\xa0Gang penetrative sexual assault comes under aggravated penetrative sexual assault and not under aggravated sexual assault. Therefore, option C cannot be correct in this case.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4edfab2c7d","n":18,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"All of the following statements are true about the given condition except:","options":["Swabs should be taken immediately after moistening with sterile water","It is most commonly caused by incisors and canines","Photograph is taken with two scales at right angle to one another in the vertical plane","3D imaging is possible in the given condition"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Photograph is taken with two scales at right angle to one another in the vertical plane\\n\\nThe above image shows a human\xa0bite mark. The photograph is taken with two scales at the right angle to one another in the horizontal plane.\\n\\nHuman bites are usually semi-circular or crescentic caused by the front teeth (incisors, canine).\\n\\nSwabs of bite marks should be taken immediately using a swab moistened with sterile water. A sample of the control area adjacent to the mark and of the victim\'s saliva should also be taken using swabs moistened with sterile water.\\n\\n3D imaging is done in which the image is measurable with high precision.\\n\\nA plastic substance such as rubber is laid over the bite mark which produces a permanent negative cast.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/c6e5fc48cf0940fc5fa4.png"]},{"bankId":"qb-cb0d84e047","n":19,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"An 18-year-old girl was brought to the OPD. Genital examination revealed the findings as given below. These findings are indicative of:\\n\\nLabia majora - separated\\n\\nLabia minora- flabby\\n\\nFourchette tear present\\n\\nRoomy vagina seen with intact hymen","options":["True virgin","False virgin","Premenstrual stage","Molestation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. False virgin\\n\\nThe given findings are suggestive of a\xa0false virgin.\\n\\nThe examination findings of a separated labia majora, a flabby labia minora, and a fourchette tear are indicative of forceful penetration.\\n\\nHymen usually gets ruptured with the first act of sexual intercourse. However, in some cases, hymen remains intact\xa0in spite of sexual intercourse. It is indicative of a false virgin.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-dc05dcf21e","n":20,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Bullet fingerprinting involves:","options":["Primary markings","Secondary markings","Both","None"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Both\\n\\nBullet fingerprinting involves both primary and secondary markings.\\n\\nPrimary markings\xa0are formed when a bullet passes through a barrel and the sides are marked by the rifling of the barrel (which is specific to the make and model of the gun).\\n\\nIrregularities on the inner surface of barrel itself which are specific for that particular weapon are also seen on the bullet and are called\xa0secondary markings.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5210b2c581","n":21,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Crown Rump Length is 21 cm, length of lower limb is 10 cm, gestational age of the fetus will be:","options":["6 to 7 months","4 to 5 months","7 to 8 months","Term"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 6 to 7 months\\n\\nGestational age of this fetus is 6-7 months.\\n\\nCrown rump length + length of lower limb = crown heel length\\n\\nRule of Haase enables the estimation of the age of the fetus in lunar months from its crown-heel length (in cm).\\n\\nAccording to the rule,\\n\\n• Age in months = Square root of crown-heel length (when the crown-heel length is 25 cm or less/ first 5 months).\\n• Age in months = crown-heel length divided by 5 (when the crown-heel length is more than 25 cm/ last 5 months).\\n\\nHere, crown heel length = Crown rump + lower limb length = 31 cm.\\n\\nHence, gestational age = 31 / 5 = 6-7 months.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-72c301b938","n":22,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"First-hand knowledge rule is applicable to:","options":["Common witness","Hand writing expert","Doctor","Hostile witness"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Common witness\\n\\nThe first-hand knowledge rule is applicable to a common witness who gives evidence about the facts observed or perceived by him.\\n\\nAn expert witness is someone who has been trained or is skilled or has knowledge and experience and is able to draw conclusions from the facts observed by him. Doctors, handwriting/ fingerprints/ firearms experts come under this category.\\n\\nA hostile witness is a person who is supposed to have some interest or motive for concealing a part of the truth or for giving completely false evidence.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c8786c9cd3","n":23,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Identify the correct pair\\ni- Mummification, ii- Adipocere, iii- Marbling, iv- Lividity of death\\n\\ni\\nMummification\\n\\nii\\nAdipocere\\n\\niii\\nMarbling\\n\\niv\\nLividity of death","options":["i-4, ii-3, iii-2, iv-1","i-3,ii-4, iii-2, iv-1","i-3, ii-1, iii-4, iv-2","i-3, ii-4, iii-1, iv-2"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. i-3, ii-4, iii-1, iv-2\\n\\nImage 1. Marbling:\xa0It is the discoloration of superficial veins due to the formation of\xa0sulphmethemoglobin,\xa0which\xa0starts in\xa024 hours\xa0and becomes prominent in\xa036-48 hours.\\n\\nImage 2. Lividity of\xa0death:\xa0Postmortem lividity (suggillation, postmortem hypostasis/postmortem staining/livor mortis/suggillation/vibices)\xa0develops in the dependent portions of the body due to engorged vessels showing through the skin.\\n\\nImage 3.\xa0Mummification:\xa0It occurs in the absence of moisture in the air and continuous action of dry or warmed air.\\n\\nImage 4. Adipocere:\xa0It forms under warm and humid weather due to hydrolysis of lipids.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/7e3604ce5c0e723c5456.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-e02a4a14eb","n":24,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Identify the following post-mortem change.","options":["Tattooing","Suggilation","Putrefaction","Decomposition"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Suggilation\\n\\nThe image shows suggilation.\\n\\nPostmortem lividity (suggilation/ postmortem hypostasis/ postmortem staining/ livor mortis/ vibices)\xa0develops in the dependent portions of the body due to engorged vessels showing through the skin.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/3d96088eef759dd4f44b.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-e637f9b02c","n":25,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Identify the injury in the image below.","options":["Abrasion","Laceration","Bruise","Imprint"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Abrasion\\n\\nThe given image shows an\xa0abrasion.\\n\\nAn abrasion is the most superficial of injuries. It\xa0does not penetrate the full thickness of the epidermis.\\n\\nA\xa0pure abrasion does not bleed, as blood vessels are confined to the dermis. However, the dermal papillae are corrugated in nature. Thus, many abrasions enter the dermis and bleeding commonly occurs.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/100e0ae656923e477393.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-ea35b040d1","n":26,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"If a person starts to serve a second employer, the new employer is responsible for his actions by:","options":["Vicarious liability","Doctrine of negligent choice","Contributory negligence","Borrowed servant doctrine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Borrowed servant doctrine\\n\\nWhen an employee is being borrowed, the lending employer temporarily surrenders control over this worker and the borrowing employer temporarily assumes control. This is known as borrowed servant doctrine.\\n\\nVicarious liability is by which the employer is responsible for his employees’ negligence if such activity occurs in the course of the employment and within its scope.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d1c56d4a5f","n":27,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"In case of professional misconduct, the patient\'s records on demand should be provided within:","options":["72 hours","42 hours","24 hours","7 days"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 72 hours\\n\\nIn case of professional misconduct, the patient\'s records on demand should be provided within 72 hours.\\n\\nIf a doctor does not maintain the medical records of the patients for a period of three years as per the regulation, and refuses to provide the same within 72 hours, when the patient or his/her authorized representative makes a request for it, then it is professional misconduct.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-86583336bd","n":28,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Match the following: i- Heroin ii- Eve iii-Joint iv-LSD 1-Ganja 2-Acid 3-Brown sugar 4-Amphetamine","options":["i-2, ii-1, iii-4, iv-3","i-3, ii-2, iii-1, iv-4","i-4, ii-2 iii-1, iv-3","i-3, ii-4, iii- 1, iv-2"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. i-3, ii-4, iii- 1, iv-2\\n\\nHeroin\\nBrown sugar, Smack, Dope\\n\\nGanja\\nJoint , Reefer, Weed, Pot, Grass,\xa0Purple haze\\n\\nEthylene dioxy\\n\\nmethamphetamine\\n\\nEve\\n\\nLSD\\nAcid","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9b228184af","n":29,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Name the type of skull fracture.","options":["Depressed fracture","Hinge fracture","Pond fracture","Comminuted fracture"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hinge fracture\\n\\nThe image shows hinge fracture/ motorcyclists fracture. Here, the base of the skull is divided into 2 halves, each moving independently of the other like a hinge.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/8a81e3eb240851155cd8.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-d4590d701b","n":30,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Paradoxical undressing is seen in:","options":["Heat stress","Voyeurism","Hypothermia","LSD overdose"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Hypothermia\\n\\nThe phenomenon of paradoxical undressing is seen in hypothermia victims.\\n\\nIts occurrence is attributed to a paradoxical feeling of heat and may lead to an erroneous assumption of a sexual offense.\\n\\nFatal hypothermia-induced stupor, loss of powers of judgment, disorientation, and increasing incapacitation are associated with this phenomenon.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c5323b585b","n":31,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"What is the cause of death in smoke inhalation?","options":["Histotoxic hypoxia","Ischemic hypoxia","Anemic hypoxia","All of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Anemic hypoxia\\n\\nVictims of smoke inhalation are affected by carbon monoxide poisoning.\xa0In carbon monoxide poisoning, the cause of death is anemic hypoxia.\\n\\nCarbon monoxide has a higher affinity to hemoglobin compared to oxygen and hence the amount of hemoglobin available for oxygen transport is markedly reduced. It displaces oxygen from its combination with hemoglobin and forms a relatively stable compound known as carboxyhemoglobin. It thus reduces the oxygen content of the blood, and hence, that of the tissues. It acts as a chemical asphyxiant and produces death due to anemic anoxia.\\n\\nIt is important to note that in the case of\xa0anemic hypoxia, O2\xa0content is low but dissolved O2\xa0is within normal limits\xa0so it does not stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors.\\n\\nNote:\xa0Carbon monoxide poisoning causes both anemic (due to CO-Hb) and histotoxic (due to inhibition of cytochrome C oxidase) hypoxia. But the main cause of death is due to anemic hypoxia.\\n\\nOption A - Histotoxic hypoxia- The most common example is cyanide poisoning. Cyanide inhibits important enzymes in the oxidative phosphorylation process, which is essential to the survival of our cells. Additional oxygen supplementation will not be able to overcome this inhibition, so it will have limited benefit. Hyperbaric oxygen may be helpful to some extent.\\n\\nOption B - Stagnant hypoxia refers to ischemic hypoxia that results from inadequate circulation due to conditions such as heart failure and hypoxia in the setting of shock. In such conditions, administering intravenous fluids can improve the patient\'s condition because it is the circulation that is affected. If the shock is prolonged it can result in acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which can be treated with positive pressure ventilation and not simply by increasing oxygen administration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-47ac4c1125","n":32,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the image shown?","options":["Only the roots of this plant are poisonous","Causes AV block","Drug of choice for poisoning is Atropine","Mitha zaher is one of the variety of this plant"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Only the roots of this plant are poisonous\\n\\nThe image is of\xa0aconite.\xa0All\xa0parts of this plant (especially the roots and seeds) are poisonous.\\n\\nVarieties of this plant are monkhood,\xa0mitha zaher, bish, and\xa0bikh.\\n\\nIt is a cardiac poison and causes AV block, bradycardia. Atropine is used in the treatment of its toxicity.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/5ab0b1ba4dc57c31e98c.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-8e454ebf83","n":33,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Which of the following trait is a feature of the female skull?","options":["Larger teeth","Frontal and maxillary sinuses are larger","Frontal and parietal eminences are larger","Supratympanic crest is present"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Frontal and parietal eminences are larger\\n\\nFrontal and parietal eminences are larger in a female skull.\\n\\nDifferentiating features between male and female skull:\\n\\nTrait\\nMale\\nFemale\\n\\nGeneral size\\nLarge\\nSmall\\n\\nArchitecture\\nRugged\\nSmooth\\n\\nSupraorbital ridges\\nProminent\\nLess prominent\\n\\nMastoid process\\nProminent\\nLess prominent\\n\\nOccipital region\\nMarked muscle markings\\nNo marked muscle markings\\n\\nFrontal eminences\\nSmall\\nLarge\\n\\nParietal eminences\\nSmall\\nLarge\\n\\nOrbits\\nSquare with round margins\\nRound with sharp margins\\n\\nMandible\\nLarge, broad ramus\\nSmall, small ramus\\n\\nChin shape\\nU-shaped\\nV-shaped\\n\\nNote: The features given above develop after puberty and are modified by senility, so are applicable only between the 20th and the 55th year.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bbdece8050","n":34,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"A 28-year-old man presented with dry mouth, urinary retention, and constipation after consuming an unknown substance. On examination, his PR - 110bpm, and his pupils were dilated. What did he most likely consume?","options":["Heroin","Morphine","Malathion","Belladona"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Belladona\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario with\xa0dilatation of the pupil indicates\xa0Atropa belladonna\xa0consumption.\\n\\nAtropa Belladonna acts by inhibiting the muscarinic effects of acetylcholine. All the parts of the plants are toxic. The active principle is 1-hyoscyamine.\\n\\nHeroin, morphine, and malathion cause constriction of the pupil.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a1ea6a1354","n":35,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"An 11-year-old girl was found to be quiet and shy in her classroom with intermittent crying, for the past few days. It was discovered that her uncle was touching her genitalia inappropriately. The duty of the principal is to report this to?","options":["Magistrate","Police","Child Welfare Committe","Parents"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Police\\n\\nThe principal has to report this case to the police.\\n\\nThe POCSO Act, 2012 is a comprehensive law for the protection of children from the offences of sexual assault, sexual harassment, and pornography. This is done while safeguarding the interests of the child at every stage of the judicial process by incorporating child-friendly mechanisms for reporting, recording of evidence, investigation, and speedy trial of the offence through designated special courts.\\n\\nSection 21(1) of the POCSO Act, 2012 requires mandatory reporting of cases of child sexual abuse to the law enforcement authorities and applies to everyone including parents, doctors, and school personnel. Failure to report a suspicion of child abuse is an offence under the Act.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bd2df126a3","n":36,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Identify the plant which produces a deliriant toxin.","options":["Erythroxylum cocca","Datura","Hyoscine","Digitalis purpura/cannabis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Erythroxylum cocca\\n\\nThe given plant is\xa0Erythroxylum cocca.\\n\\nCocaine is the principal alkaloid found in the cultivated varieties of coca.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/43becb93c3c3fe7eae37.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-41be3437a3","n":37,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"What is bullet wipe?","options":["Residual from barrel of gun","Blackening","Gutter fracture of skull","Tattooing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Residual from barrel of gun\\n\\nBullet wipe is due to the grease, soot or debris from the barrel of the gun.\\n\\nWhile examining the clothing of a victim of bullet injury, there is a deposition of various residues surrounding the entrance defect at various ranges known as Bullet wipe. It helps in determining the wounds of inlet and outlet upon the body.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1f9aec2826","n":38,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Which of the following postmortem changes is shown in the given image?","options":["Marbling","Algor mortis","Rigor mortis","Postmortem lividity"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Marbling\\n\\nThe postmortem change shown above is marbling.\\n\\nThe blood in the vessels is hemolyzed, which stains the vessel walls and the adjacent tissues, giving rise to a marbled appearance. The marbling of the skin becomes prominent about 36–48 hours after death in summer and is distinctly appreciable in the superficial veins of the abdomen, shoulders, chest, and inguinal region.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/50666b4a398a1f944cc5.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-a6b3f9ded8","n":39,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents with the following injury that he sustained on being tackled by an opponent during the course of a football match. As the Casualty medical officer, you would enter the wound description in the case sheet as ____________________","options":["Incised wound","Abrasion","Laceration","Bruise"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Laceration\\n\\nThe given image shows\xa0a laceration.\\n\\nA laceration is a tear or a split in the skin, mucous membrane, muscle, or any internal organ, with a depth spanning more than the covering epithelium of the skin or an organ, and is produced by blunt force.\\n\\nFeatures of laceration:\\n\\n• Margins are irregular with pieces of tissue attached in between called tissue tags or bridges\\n• Bruising is\xa0seen around the margin\\n• Deeper tissues are unevenly divided with tissue tags\\n• Hair bulbs are crushed\\n• Depth of the wound contains abundant foreign material such as dust particles, paint material of the vehicle involved, etc\\n• In skin lacerations, usually one of the margins overhangs the other, which could help in assessing the direction of the force\\n• A laceration, on healing, produces a permanent scar.\\n\\nClassification of a laceration:\\n\\n• Split laceration (incised - like laceration)\\n\\n• This occurs when the skin is crushed between two hard objects\\n• For example, during a fall, the scalp gets crushed between the skull from inside and hard ground outside. This results in a wound, which usually mimics an incised one unless examined closely.\\n\\n• Stretch laceration\\n\\n• As the name implies, it occurs due to the over-stretching of the skin, beyond its elasticity.\\n• This typically occurs in the setting of a fractured femur, where the fracture fragment may stretch and pierce the skin to project out\\n\\n• Avulsed laceration\\n\\n• An avulsion is a lesion where the skin only separates by tearing from the underlying tissues or peels off and overhangs the wound\\n• This is common in road traffic accidents.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/2e511982287041f68205.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-f658f5634f","n":40,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A 14-year-old girl comes to the OPD with her mother. History of penovaginal penetration by the neighbour is reported to the doctor. The girl admits to giving the neighbor the consent for the same and says she does not want to be examined. As a medical officer, which of the following should you do?\\n\\n1. Document informed refusal\\n2. Do not inform the police\\n3. Counsel the mother and daughter\\n4. Examine with necessary force\\n5. Inform the police","options":["1,2,3 are true","2,3,4 are true","1,3,5 are true","3,4,5 are true"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1,3,5 are true\\n\\nIn the given scenario 1, 3, 5 are true.\\n\\nThe scenario describes a 14-year-old girl with a history of sexual intercourse with her neighbor albeit with consent. Consent for intercourse is valid only after 18 years in an unmarried girl. As she is less than 18 years, it is presumed to be a medicolegal case of sexual assault.\\n\\nExamination of a victim of sexual assault should be done after the consent of the victim (if she is less than 12 years of age or unsound mind, consent from the guardian needs to be taken). If she denies examination, counseling has to be done regarding the need for examination and how it could be held up against her in the court of law. If she still refuses for part or whole of examination or sample collection, an informed refusal needs to be documented.\\n\\nInformation to the police is given in all medicolegal cases.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a891110dff","n":41,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A 22-year-old woman was found dead at home. She was brought to the mortuary by the police and the following findings were noted on examination. Which of the following is the likely cause of her death?","options":["Strangulation by ligature","Hanging","Throttling","Drowning"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Throttling\\n\\nIn the given case scenario and autopsy images showing carotid intimal tear and muscle hematoma with nail marks on neck and absence of ligature mark point towards a diagnosis of throttling.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A- Strangulation by ligature - ligature mark with signs of a struggle would\'ve made this diagnosis more likely.\\n\\nOption B- Hanging - ligature mark with a suicide note would\'ve made it more likely.\\n\\nOption C- Drowning- Body recovered from a pond or other water body with other suggestive features like frothing etc\xa0 would\'ve made this more likely.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/b44f1780d33838a3496f.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-e89a5d4794","n":42,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A middle-aged man was brought to the ER after an assault with a sharp axe. Identify the type of wound?","options":["Incised wound","Chop wound","Lacerated wound","Abrasion"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Chop wound\\n\\nThe given wound caused by an axe, is likely to be a chop wound.\\n\\nChop wounds are so-called because of the action in which they are produced. Depending on the type of instrument, they may resemble an incised or a lacerated wound. The shape of the wound may give clues to the type of weapon. It would be a deep, well-margined wound, gaping\xa0with limited damage to the surrounding skin.\\n\\nFor example, in the given image, the wound resembles an injury produced by a sharp axe. Such wounds may be inflicted with homicidal intent on the skull/scalp.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/4f0d0673b7929569f38f.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-fbf623c4b6","n":43,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A patient comes to the casualty with life-threatening injuries. There was no time for taking consent. The doctor started doing life-saving procedures with all precautions, and despite best efforts, the death of the patient occurred. The doctor is not responsible under which doctrine?","options":["Res ipsa loquitor","Doctrine of anticipation","Doctrine of extended consent","Doctrine of conjugated consent"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Doctrine of anticipation\\n\\nUnder IPC 92, the doctor can perform life-saving procedures in a patient with life-threatening injuries without obtaining consent. The guiding principle is whether the delay in treatment in order to obtain consent would result in harm to the patient. This is\xa0the doctrine of anticipation.\\n\\nUnder some circumstances like emergencies, informed consent may be neither possible nor necessary.\\n\\nIPC 92 states that nothing is an offense by reason of any harm which it may cause to a person for whose benefit it is done in good faith, even without that person’s consent, if the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent, or if that person is incapable of giving consent, and has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\n\'Res Ipsa Loquitor\' means \'the thing or fact speaks for itself\'. It is obvious evidence for the negligent act committed. For example, swabs left in the abdominal cavity post laparotomy.\\n\\nThe doctrine of extended consent: It is used in unanticipated conditions during surgery.\xa0This comes into play when a surgeon encounters an unanticipated abnormality within the field of surgery. It is called the “extension doctrine,” and it assumes that the surgeon is using reasonable judgment. For example, a surgeon incurred no liability for draining some ovarian cysts during the course of an appendectomy. The condition must be one that was unforeseen, and the patient must not have expressly refused such an intervention.\\n\\nDoctrine of conjugated consent - Dummy option (No such consent has been defined).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f139e789f5","n":44,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A surgeon performs a hysterectomy for uterine fibroids after obtaining relevant informed consent from the patient. Despite adequate precaution, the ureter was injured intraoperatively. Under which of the following principles will the doctor not be held responsible for this act?","options":["Novus actus interveniens","Medical maloccurrence","Res ipsa loquitur","Physician error"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Medical maloccurrence\\n\\nThe doctor will not be responsible for the above act under medical\xa0maloccurrence.\\n\\nMedical Maloccurrence/Inevitable Accident: Every human being responds differently to different drugs as well as to different treatment modalities. On certain occasions, despite all proper care given by the doctor during treatment, the patient fails to respond properly or may suffer some adverse reactions to the drug which may lead to severe injuries or permanent deformities. This is known as a medical maloccurrence/inevitable accident.\\n\\nIf the doctor can prove this before the court, it will be an absolute defence against malpractice.\\n\\n\'Res ipsa loquitur\' means \'the thing or fact speaks for itself\'. It is obvious evidence of the negligent act committed. For example, swabs left in the abdominal cavity post laparotomy.\\n\\n\'Novus actus interveniens\': This essentially means that the negligence at the core of the lawsuit stems from the actions of a third party and that this negligence was unforeseeable. The viability of this defence heavily relies on the specific details of each case. For instance, if a doctor writes a prescription that is allegedly illegible, and the pharmacist misreads it, fails to verify it with the doctor, and then dispenses the wrong medication to the patient, resulting in an injury, the doctor might use this defence in such a scenario.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-94f1aadc9a","n":45,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Identify the condition shown in the image.","options":["Postmortem lividity","Marbling","Filigree burns","Subcutaneous emphysema"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Marbling\\n\\nThe image shows postmortem marbling. It is a visible postmortem change that is characterized by linear branching patterns on skin that correspond to the course of superficial veins.\\n\\nIntestinal bacteria infest the venous system as blood acts as a nutrient medium for their proliferation. There is subsequent hemolysis which stains the vessel walls and the adjacent tissues, giving rise to marbling. This becomes prominent in about\xa036–48 hours\xa0after death in the summer and is distinctly appreciable in the superficial veins of the abdomen, shoulders, chest, and inguinal region.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/7752372e4a8315ad1c64.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-a23dfa3d18","n":46,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Identify the image shown below","options":["Shot gun wound","Tattooing","Arsenic poisoning","Graze wound"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Shot gun wound\\n\\nThe given image shows entry wound of a shot gun from a mid-range or long distance.\\n\\n• There is a central round hole, with multiple pellet holes scattered around the central hole.\\n• This wound is most likely to have been caused by a shotgun from a range of 1-5 m.\\n\\nFollowing are the appearances of shot gun entry wound from different distance:\\n\\nAppearance of shotgun entry wounds at different ranges:\\n\\nContact shot\\n\\n• Soft tissues - single circular wound about the diameter of the muzzle with muzzle imprint and no soot soiling, tattooing, singeing.\\n• Bone - same as with soft tissues, but wound may be ragged and split because of gas rebound.\\n\\n<30 cm\\n\\n• Circular wound with surrounding soot soiling.\\n• Burnt hairs (singeing).\\n• Punctate skin lesions from powder particles (tattooing).\\n\\n30cm - 1m\\n\\n• Circular rat-hole wound.\\n• Soot soiling.\\n• Powder tattooing.\\n\\n1 - 5 meters\\n\\n• Central rat-hole wound.\\n• No soot, tattooing +/-.\\n• Satellite pellet holes around the periphery.\\n\\n>5 meters\\n\\n• Diffuse pellet pattern\\n• No central hole, no soot soiling, tattooing, singeing.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/35a9ec6182ede7fed3eb.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-4047a4ecae","n":47,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Identify the seeds shown in the image below.","options":["Tropicana canna","Nigella sativa","Datura seeds","Opium seeds"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Nigella sativa\\n\\nThe seeds shown in the given image are of the plant\xa0Nigella sativa. These are also known as black cumin or kalonji.\\n\\nThe seeds are the most commonly used parts of the plant in traditional medicine. Seeds have a pungent and bitter taste with a considerable amount of oil inside.\xa0The seeds are used as a spice in cooking. They are also used as a food preservative and the most often used therapeutic agent is its oil.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/c3e99f0ca1d4d03a8cb7.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-8062a0a4e0","n":48,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Identify the type of skull fracture in the given image?","options":["Ring","Depressed","Fissured","Comminuted"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Fissured\\n\\nThe given image shows a fissured fracture of skull.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/40c3f131d835a2a7cb46.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-bd9f234402","n":49,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Match the following poisons with their probable mechanism of action:\\nA. Zinc chloride\\nB. Chloral hydrate\\nC. Quinine\\nD. Potassium carbonate\\n1.Abortifacient\\n2. Irritant\\n3. Corrosive\\n4. Stupefying","options":["A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-4,B-3, C-2,D-1","A-4, B-2, C-1,D-3","A-2, B-3, C-4,D-1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3\\n\\nFor the above-given options, the correct match is given as\\n\\nPoison\\nMechanism\\n\\nA.Zinc Chloride\\n2. Irritant\\n\\nB.Chloral hydrate\\n4. Stupefying\\n\\nC. Quinine\\n1. Abortifacient\\n\\nD.Potassium\\ncarbonate\\n3. Corrosive\\n\\nRationale for the classification\\n\\nZinc chloride also known as butter of zinc is highly irritant to the eyes on topical application. It can cause erosive esophagitis on ingestion. It has a fatal dose of 350mg. It could\'ve also been classified as a corrosive, but since potassium carbonate is an alkali with stronger corrosive action, it is better classified as an irritant for this question\\n\\nChloral hydrate is a CNS depressant. It is also known as knockout drops because of its rapid action and used in robbery or rape to quickly render the victim helpless.\\n\\nQuinine is a cardiotoxic drug with the additional action of being an abortifacient. It\'s overdose may lead to an echolic action (contract the pregnant uterus)\\n\\nPotassium carbonate is a caustic alkali with a predominant action of corrosive due to its high pH.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c01fc70ab3","n":50,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Match the following weapons with the possible injuries caused by them:\\n\\nA. Axe\\n1. Incised wound\\n\\nB. RTA\\n2. Tram track bruise\\n\\nC. Blade\\n3. Grazed abrasion\\n\\nD. Lathi\\n4. Chop wound","options":["A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2","A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2","A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3","A-4, B-1, C-3, D-2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2\\n\\nThe correctly matched sets of the above-mentioned weapons and the possible injuries are as follows:\\n\\nA. Axe\\n4. Chop wounds\\n\\nB. Road traffic accident\\n3. Graze abrasions\\n\\nC. Blade\\n1. Incised wound\\n\\nD. Lathi\\n2. Tram track bruise\\n\\nMechanism of injury with the above mentioned weapons:\\n\\nAxe - it is a heavy weapon that may or may not be sharpened. Injury is produced by chopping action of the weapon and it may be in a spectrum of an incised wound to laceration depending upon the sharpness of the blade.\\n\\nRoad traffic accident - rubbing of epidermis tangential to a rough or hard surface gives rise to graze abrasion also called brush burn. It can occur in a road traffic accident after a pedestrian is being hit by a vehicle and the victim is slid/grazed for some distance before he comes to a halt.\\n\\nBlade - it gives rise to a clean-cut wound which is usually deepest at its starting. Tailing of the wound indicates the direction of injury. Length is the biggest parameter of this type of wound.\\n\\nLathi- It is a blunt force injury giving rise to a patterned abrasion with 2 parallel lines and a normal unbruised area on site of impact or stick/lathi. The lathi forces the blood from the site of impact to either side which gives rise to tramline appearance.\\n\\nThe image below shows a chop wound inflicted on the scalp:\\n\\nThe image below shows a schematic illustrating heaping up of epidermis in a graze abrasion or brush burn:\\n\\nThe image below shows production of a tramline bruise associated with a lathi strike:","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f418cc05d6","n":51,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"The punishment for the death of a patient by spurious drug:","options":["5 years","7 years","Life imprisonment","1 year"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Life imprisonment\\n\\nThe punishment for the death of a patient by a spurious drug is\xa0imprisonment for life.\\n\\nIn case of an adulterated, spurious, or any drug that when used\xa0by any person for or in the diagnosis, treatment, mitigation, or\xa0prevention of any disease or disorder is likely to cause ill health\xa0or is likely to cause such harm on his body as would amount to\xa0grievous hurt within the meaning of Section 320 of IPC, shall\xa0be punishable with imprisonment for a term that shall not be\xa0less than 3 years but that may extend to a term of life.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-01fef1fa4c","n":52,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"What is the approximate age of a person in whom the medial end of the clavicle is fused and also the sternum is completely fused","options":["<18 years","18-22 years","22-25 years",">25 years"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. >25 years\\n\\nApproximate age for the given person with fused medial end of clavicle and completely fused sternum is most likely >25 years.\\n\\nAge\\nAge determination\\n\\n<5 years\\nThe appearance of ossification centers\\n\\n5-16 years\\nAnalysis of hand and wrist\\n\\n>16 years(male)\\nComplete fusion of distal radius or ulna\\n\\n>16 years(female)\\nComplete fusion of distal ulna\\n\\n>18 years(male)\\nComplete fusion of distal femur or proximal tibia\\n\\n>18 years(female)\\nComplete fusion of iliac crest\\n\\n>21 years\\nComplete fusion of medial end of clavicle\\n\\nThe sternum is formed from the fusion of Manubrium, 4 sternebrae(body), and the xiphoid process. The fusion of sternebrae occurs from below upwards, followed by its fusion to the xiphoid process and then fusion of manubrium to the fused sternum.\\n\\nManubrio-sternal joint\\n60-70 years\\n\\n1st and 2nd Sternebrae\\n25 years\\n\\n2nd and 3rd sternebrae\\n20 years\\n\\n3rd and 4th sternebrae\\n15 years\\n\\nXiphoid process and body\\n40-45 years","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d41a8d50e1","n":53,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following is false regarding the management of snakebite in India?","options":["Atropine and neostigmine are very useful in the management of krait bite","Neostigmine and ventilatory support are used in treatment along with ASV","ASV is the mainstay of treatment","Current ASV in India is not effective against humped pit viper"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Atropine and neostigmine are very useful in the management of krait bite\\n\\nAtropine is not useful in the management of krait bite. The toxin in common krait\'s venom acts presynaptically, hence atropine is not used in the treatment of envenomation by krait.\\n\\nAs the\xa0cobra venom acts postsynaptically, atropine can be used in the management.\\n\\nASV(Anti Snake Venom) is the mainstay of snakebite treatment.\\n\\nASV used in India is effective against:\\n\\n• Cobra\\n• Common krait\\n• Russel viper\\n• Saw scaled viper\\n\\nNeostigmine and ventilatory support are used in the treatment of snakebite.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b783504f49","n":54,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following is seen in freshwater drowning?\\n\\nA. Haemodilution\\nB. Hypokalemia\\nC. Hyponatremia\\nD. Arrhythmia","options":["A,B,C,D","A,B,C","B,C,D","A,C,D"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. A,C,D\\n\\nIn cases of freshwater drowning, hyperkalemia is seen due to hemolysis of swollen RBCs and not hypokalemia.\\n\\nFreshwater is hypotonic to plasma and is rapidly absorbed from the alveoli and into the RBCs. The RBCs finally swell up and lyse resulting in the release of intracellular potassium causing hyperkalemia. Increased intravascular blood volume combined with hyperkalemia causes arrhythmia.\\n\\nSeawater is hypertonic to plasma and causing a shift of fluid from the blood to alveoli causing pulmonary edema. There is a net decrease in blood volume causing a fall in blood pressure.\\n\\nThe table below summarises the cause of death in different types of drowning.\\n\\nType of drowning\\nCause of death\\n\\nFreshwater drowning\\n\\nSeawater drowning\\n\\nDisruption of surfactant\\n\\nDry drowning\\n\\nLaryngeal spasm/Vagal inhibition\\n\\nPost immersion Syndrome / Secondary drowning\\n\\n• Cerebral anoxia\\n• Pneumonia\\n• Pulmonary edema\\n• Chemical pneumonitis\\n• Metabolic acidosis","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2bc47e6a6c","n":55,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"You are posted as an intern in casualty when an MLC case is brought by the police with the following appearance. She was declared brought dead. What would you mark as the likely cause of death before sending the patient for postmortem?","options":["Drowning","Hanging","Strangulation","Traumatic asphyxia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Drowning\\n\\nThe given image shows frothing from nostrils and no clear signs of facial or conjunctival congestion. It is likely a case of drowning.\\n\\nFrothing may also be seen in throttling or strangulation, but it might be associated with signs of conjunctival congestion, facial congestion, patterned bruise on neck etc. In the absence of these features, it is unlikely to be caused by strangulation.\\n\\nTraumatic asphyxia is unlikely as the face of this patient is all whitened without signs of congestion or any obvious signs of trauma","img":false,"images":["/bimg/8a37c2e0efd69e0e18c0.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-5d523382a8","n":56,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"You are working as a medical officer at a PHC. A 17-year-old girl presents to you after an alleged sexual assault, the girl refuses physical examination. Which of the following steps would you take to proceed in this case?\\n\\n1. Start treatment for STD and pregnancy\\n2. Inform the police\\n3. Record refusal of consent\\n4. Take vaginal samples\\n5. Start medical examination","options":["1, 2 and 3","2, 3 and 5","1, 2 and 5","2 and 3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1, 2 and 3\\n\\nIn the given scenario, you need to start treatment for STI, inform the police, and record refusal of consent for examination.\\n\\nExamination of a sexual assault victim shouldn\'t be forced upon, but an informed refusal is to be documented after explaining to them the consequences of choice.\\n\\nMedical treatment and testing for pregnancy have to be done for all victims of sexual assault free of cost and police should be informed as per Section 397 of BNSS (357-C of CrPc).\\n\\nAs the girl is under 18 years of age, as per POCSO (The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences) act 2012, the medical practitioner should initiate treatment for exposure to sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) including prophylaxis for identified STDs (including HIV if suspected) and possible pregnancy and emergency contraceptives should be discussed with the victim and her parent/guardian.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8097f214e7","n":57,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"\\"Salience\\" in the context of substance dependence refers to which of the following?","options":["Tolerance requiring increasing doses","Withdrawal symptoms on stopping","Progressive neglect of other interests due to substance use","Inability to control onset or termination of use"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Progressive neglect of other interests due to substance use\\n\\nSalience = substance use becomes the dominant activity in the person\'s life, with progressive neglect of other pleasures, interests, and responsibilities.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d33edb0752","n":58,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"35-year-old man with a history of bipolar and substance use disorders comes to the emergency department due to depression, auditory hallucinations, and suicidal ideation. His medications include lithium and escitalopram. The patient has a history of 5 psychiatric hospitalizations and 2 past suicide attempts, including overdose on his medications and attempted hanging. The patient has been using \\"anything I can get my hands on\\" because his depression is unbearable. He is hospitalized and placed on suicide precautions. His dose of escitalopram is increased to target his depression, and risperidone is added to treat the hallucinations. His lithium level is 1.0 mEq/L. On the second day of hospitalization, the patient reports muscle pains, abdominal cramping, nausea, and diarrhea. His temperature is 37.2\xb0 C (99\xb0 F), blood pressure is 130/85 mm Hg, and pulse is 84/min. The patient is alert and restless, and his pupils are dilated bilaterally. Bowel sounds are hyperactive and neurologic examination is normal. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for his symptoms?","options":["Serotonin syndrome","Cocaine withdrawal","Lithium toxicity","Opioid withdrawal"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Opioid withdrawal\\n\\nThe patient’s symptoms—abdominal cramping, nausea, diarrhea, myalgias, restlessness, dilated pupils, and hyperactive bowel sounds—are classic for opioid withdrawal. He has a history of polysubstance use (“anything I can get my hands on”), and hospitalization abruptly stopped opioid intake, precipitating withdrawal. Normal temperature and neurologic exam also support withdrawal rather than toxicity.\\n\\nA. Serotonin syndrome: Would show hyperthermia, clonus, hyperreflexia, and autonomic instability\\n\\nB. Cocaine withdrawal: Causes depression, fatigue, hypersomnia—not GI hyperactivity.\\n\\nC. Lithium toxicity: Occurs at higher levels and causes tremor, ataxia, confusion","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5a39549e3e","n":59,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 15-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with a displaced fracture shaft femur following a road traffic accident. He is conscious, oriented, and understands the nature of the proposed surgery. His parents are available in the hospital. Regarding consent in this patient, all of the following statements are correct except:","options":["The patient is competent to provide valid consent for a general physical examination","Written informed consent from the parents/guardian is required before major operative intervention","If the parents refuse surgery and delay would endanger the patient\'s life, the emergency doctrine permits life-saving treatment without consent","Since the patient understands the procedure and is older than 12 years, he can independently provide legally valid consent for surgery"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Since the patient understands the procedure and is older than 12 years, he can independently provide legally valid consent for surgery\\n\\nThe legal age for giving valid consent depends on the nature of the medical intervention. A child 12 years or older can legally consent to a general physical examination (GPE), provided they possess sufficient understanding. However, major diagnostic or therapeutic procedures, including surgery and anesthesia, require valid consent from an individual 18 years or older. Therefore, a 15-year-old cannot independently consent for surgery, and consent must be obtained from a parent or legal guardian.\\n\\nIn emergency situations where immediate treatment is necessary to save life or prevent serious harm, treatment may proceed without consent under the Emergency Doctrine (Section 26, Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, formerly Section 92 IPC), if obtaining consent is not feasible.\\n\\nHigh-Yield Points\\n\\n• ≥12 years: Can consent for General Physical Examination (GPE).\\n• ≥18 years: Required for consent to major procedures/surgery.\\n• Substituted consent: Given by parents/guardian for minors.\\n• Emergency doctrine: Consent not required for life-saving treatment when delay is dangerous.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8709c01830","n":60,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 17-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents due to a recent change in behavior. The patient was previously polite and soft-spoken, but he has become intermittently irritable, impatient, and rude over the past few weeks. His parents initially dismissed his new behavior as a \\"phase\\", however, they grew more concerned last night when they discovered that he had stolen money from their wallets and later found him pacing in his room at 4:00 AM. When questioned by his father about his strange behavior, the patient said, \\"I have to be careful because I\'m being followed by CIA agents.\\" Temperature is 36.6\xb0C (97.9\xb0F), blood pressure is 164/98 mmHg, pulse is 124/min, and respirations are 18/min. On physical examination, the patient appears fearful. He is sweating profusely and his pupils are dilated. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","options":["Amphetamine intoxication","Anticholinergic poisoning","Opioid withdrawal","Cannabis intoxication"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Amphetamine intoxication\\n\\nThis adolescent shows acute stimulant intoxication: recent behavioral change, paranoia and persecutory delusions, irritability, insomnia (awake pacing at 4 AM), tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, and mydriasis. Amphetamines increase synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine, leading to sympathetic overdrive and psychotic symptoms that can closely mimic primary psychosis, especially in teenagers.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\n• B. Anticholinergic poisoning: Causes mydriasis and agitation but also dry skin, dry mucosa, urinary retention, and hyperthermia—absent here\\n• C. Opioid withdrawal: Features mydriasis and autonomic symptoms but includes GI distress and myalgias, not paranoia.\\n• D. Cannabis intoxication: Typically causes euphoria, relaxation, or mild anxiety—not severe sympathetic activation or frank psychosis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-33a2f97d09","n":61,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 19-year-old college student is brought to the psychiatry outpatient department by his parents because of progressive social withdrawal, suspiciousness, and hearing voices for the past 3 months. There is a family history of schizophrenia in his maternal uncle. Which of the following substance use histories is the most likely contributing environmental risk factor for his current illness?","options":["Long-term heroin use","Long-term cannabis use","Long-term barbiturate use","Occasional psilocybin (magic mushroom) use"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Long-term cannabis use\\n\\n• Cannabis is the recreational drug with the strongest evidence linking chronic use to an increased risk of schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders, particularly in genetically susceptible individuals.\\n• The risk is:\\n\\n• Dose-dependent (higher with frequent/heavy use)\\n• Greater with early initiation (adolescence)\\n• Higher with high-potency cannabis (high THC content)\\n• Increased in individuals with a family history of psychosis\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\n• A. Long-term heroin use: Incorrect. Heroin causes opioid dependence, respiratory depression, and withdrawal symptoms but is not a recognized risk factor for schizophrenia.\\n• C. Long-term barbiturate use: Incorrect. Barbiturates can cause dependence, cognitive impairment, and overdose but have not been shown to increase the risk of schizophrenia.\\n• D. Occasional psilocybin use: Incorrect. Psilocybin produces transient hallucinations and perceptual disturbances during intoxication but has not been consistently associated with the development of chronic schizophrenia.\\n\\nEducational Objective:\\n\\n• Cannabis use during adolescence is an established environmental risk factor for schizophrenia.\\n• The risk is greatest in individuals with genetic susceptibility, and earlier onset, frequent use, and high-potency cannabis further increase the likelihood of developing psychotic disorders.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c8e0594273","n":62,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 19-year-old female presents 18 hours after alleged sexual assault. Vaginal swab is collected. Which of the following is the most reliable method to confirm the presence of spermatozoa when the specimen is more than 12 hours old?","options":["Normal saline wet mount","H&amp;E smear examination","Acid phosphatase test followed by p30 (PSA) detection","Florence test"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Acid phosphatase test followed by p30 (PSA) detection\\n\\nWhen a vaginal specimen is collected >12 hours after intercourse, intact spermatozoa may be absent or degraded. Acid phosphatase is a screening test for seminal fluid, as it is present in high concentration in semen. Confirmation is best done by detecting p30 (prostate-specific antigen, PSA), which is highly specific for seminal plasma and remains detectable longer than sperm cells.\\n\\nA. Normal saline wet mount: Useful only within a few hours; sperm motility is quickly lost.\\n\\nB. H&E smear: Requires intact sperm; sensitivity decreases after 12 hours.\\n\\nD. Florence test: Detects choline crystals; nonspecific and outdated.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1b9ff97b67","n":63,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by the warden of his hostel for strange behavior. He was found locked out of his room, where the patient admitted to attending a party. The patient appears anxious. His pulse is 105/min, and blood pressure is 142/85 mmHg. Examination shows dry mucous membranes and bilateral conjunctival injection. Further evaluation is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Tactile hallucinations","Pupillary constriction","Synesthesia","Impaired reaction time"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Impaired reaction time\\n\\nThis patient’s presentation is most consistent with cannabis intoxication. Key clues include anxiety, tachycardia, dry mucous membranes, and bilateral conjunctival injection (red eyes) after attending a party. Cannabis (THC) acts on CB1 receptors in the CNS, impairing attention, coordination, and short-term memory, leading to slowed reaction time, which is a classic and commonly tested effect.\\n\\nA. Tactile hallucinations: More typical of stimulant intoxication (eg, cocaine, “cocaine bugs”).\\n\\nB. Pupillary constriction: Seen with opioid intoxication, not cannabis.\\n\\nC. Synesthesia: Characteristic of hallucinogens like LSD, not marijuana.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8855f06794","n":64,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 19-year-old student presented with sustained dental injuries in a street fight. Examination reveals that his permanent lower right canine tooth was missing. According to the FDI system, what dental numbering would you give to this tooth while writing his case sheet?","options":["43","21","33","75"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 43\\n\\nIn the FDI (F\xe9d\xe9ration Dentaire Internationale) tooth numbering system, permanent teeth are designated by two digits. The first digit denotes the quadrant and the second digit denotes the tooth position from the midline.\\n\\nQuadrant 4 = permanent lower right jaw\\n\\nTooth number 3 = canine\\n\\nHence, the permanent lower right canine is numbered 43, which is the correct entry for the case sheet.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nB. 21: Permanent upper left central incisor.\\n\\nC. 33: Permanent lower left canine.\\n\\nD. 75: Deciduous (primary) lower left second molar, not a permanent tooth.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ce14adc2b8","n":65,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 2-day-old girl is in the newborn nursery with persistent crying, tremors, tachypnea, sneezing, and diarrhea. She was born vaginally and had been breastfeeding well until several hours ago when she became tachypneic. Her mother has poorly controlled schizophrenia and did not receive prenatal care. The patient\'s mother also had a positive hepatitis C antibody test during postnatal laboratory testing. On physical examination, the girl has increased tone in all extremities. Chest radiograph shows normal lung fields. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for treatment of the newborn\'s symptoms?","options":["Flumazenil","Folic acid","Methadone","Naloxone"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Methadone\\n\\nThe newborn has neonatal abstinence syndrome (NAS)—irritability, high-pitched crying, tremors, hypertonia, tachypnea, sneezing, and diarrhea—most consistent with in-utero opioid exposure. Mothers with poor prenatal care and hepatitis C commonly have opioid use disorder. Methadone (or morphine) is used to treat NAS by relieving withdrawal and then slowly tapering.\\n\\nA. Flumazenil: Benzodiazepine antagonist; ineffective and unsafe in NAS.\\n\\nB. Folic acid: Prevents neural tube defects; no role postnatally for these symptoms.\\n\\nD. Naloxone: Can precipitate acute, severe withdrawal in opioid-exposed neonates and is avoided.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-101ab706c1","n":66,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department due to sudden-onset facial redness. The symptoms were first noticed by the parents about 1 hour ago. The mother also noticed an opened bottle of a vitamin supplement with a few capsules spilled on the floor. This patient most likely ingested a vitamin that has which of the following biochemical functions?","options":["Coenzyme in hydroxylation of prolyl and lysyl residues","Component of coenzyme A","Precursor for flavin adenine dinucleotide production","Precursor for nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide production"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Precursor for nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide production\\n\\nThe key clinical clue here is sudden facial flushing in a child after ingesting vitamin capsules.\\n\\nThis is classic for niacin (Vitamin B3) toxicity, which causes:\\n\\n• Cutaneous vasodilation → flushing (face, neck)\\n• Warmth, itching, sometimes hypotension\\n\\nCorrect answer: Niacin → precursor of NAD⁺ / NADP⁺\\n\\nBiochemical function:\\n\\nNiacin is converted into:\\n\\n• NAD⁺ (Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide)\\n• NADP⁺ (Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate)\\n\\nThese act as coenzymes in oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions.\\n\\nKey functions of NAD⁺ / NADP⁺:\\n\\n• Electron carriers in cellular respiration\\n\\n• Glycolysis\\n• TCA cycle\\n• Oxidative phosphorylation\\n\\n• NADPH is used in:\\n\\n• Fatty acid synthesis\\n• Glutathione reduction (antioxidant defense)\\n• Cytochrome P450 reactions","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0c2f0e457c","n":67,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 20-year-old girl was brought to the OPD. Genital examination revealed the findings as given below: 1. Labia majora- separated 2. Labia minora- flabby 3. Fourchette tear present 4. Roomy vagina seen with intact hymen What is true about this girl from the options below?","options":["True virgin","False virgin","Premenstrual stage","Molestation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. False virgin\\n\\nA false virgin is a woman who has had previous sexual intercourse but still has an intact hymen. In this case, features such as separated labia majora, flabby labia minora, roomy vagina, and a fourchette tear indicate previous sexual activity and loss of vaginal tone, which are inconsistent with a true virgin. The presence of an intact hymen does not rule out intercourse, as the hymen may be elastic or remain unruptured despite penetration.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nA. True virgin: Would typically have firm labia, narrow vagina, and intact fourchette.\\n\\nC. Premenstrual stage: Does not cause these genital changes.\\n\\nD. Molestation: Involves sexual assault without penetration and usually lacks such chronic changes.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6d613878d0","n":68,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 20-year-old student presents with episodes of intense anxiety, depersonalization, and visual distortion after using cannabis. He reports that objects appear larger or smaller than usual and time feels slowed down. On examination, he is tachycardic with conjunctival injection. Which of the following best explains the neurobiological basis of these perceptual disturbances?","options":["Increased NMDA receptor activity in the cortex","Inhibition of GABA leading to increased dopaminergic activity","Direct stimulation of serotonin 5-HT2A receptors","Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase in synaptic cleft"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Inhibition of GABA leading to increased dopaminergic activity\\n\\nCannabis (THC) acts on CB1 receptors in the central nervous system. These receptors are located presynaptically and inhibit GABA release. This causes disinhibition of dopaminergic neurons, leading to increased dopamine activity. The result is altered perception, euphoria, anxiety, and time distortion.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption A is incorrect because NMDA receptor activity is not the primary mechanism in cannabis effects\\n\\nOption C is incorrect because 5-HT2A stimulation is seen with hallucinogens like LSD\\n\\nOption D is incorrect because acetylcholinesterase inhibition is seen in organophosphate poisoning","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bbf9c80b2b","n":69,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 24-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after ingesting a large quantity of phenobarbital tablets in a suicide attempt. He is drowsy, hypotensive, and has shallow respiration. After initial stabilization, the toxicologist decides to enhance drug elimination by alkalinizing the urine. The elimination of which of the following drugs is commonly enhanced by alkaline diuresis?","options":["Amphetamines","Phenobarbitone","Phencyclidine","Theophylline"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Phenobarbitone\\n\\nAlkaline diuresis is used to enhance the renal elimination of weakly acidic drugs, such as phenobarbitone and salicylates. Increasing the urinary pH (>7.5) converts these drugs into their ionized form, reducing their passive reabsorption from the renal tubules (ion trapping) and thereby increasing urinary excretion.\\n\\nPhenobarbitone overdose is a classic indication for urinary alkalinization, provided there are no contraindications.\\n\\nHigh-yield: Urinary Manipulation in Poisoning\\n\\n| Alkaline Diuresis (Weak Acids) | Acid Diuresis (Weak Bases*)\\n\\n| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------\\n\\n| Phenobarbitone | Amphetamines\\n\\n| Salicylates | Phencyclidine (PCP)\\n\\n| Methotrexate | Cocaine\\n\\n| Chlorpropamide | Quinine (limited role)\\n\\nMechanism: Ion trapping—ionized drugs cannot be passively reabsorbed from the renal tubules, increasing urinary excretion.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eb19af0497","n":70,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 24-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain, repeated vomiting, and profuse watery diarrhea after ingesting seeds shown in the image during a traditional remedy attempt. Within a few hours, he develops dehydration and hypotension. On examination, there is no neurological deficit, but marked gastrointestinal irritation is present. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of toxicity of the ingested substance?","options":["Inhibition of cytochrome oxidase leading to cellular hypoxia","Ribosome inactivation causing inhibition of protein synthesis","Direct irritant action causing severe inflammation of gastrointestinal mucosa","Blockade of acetylcholinesterase leading to cholinergic crisis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Direct irritant action causing severe inflammation of gastrointestinal mucosa\\n\\nThe image shows seeds of Croton tiglium, which contain croton oil, a potent irritant toxin.\\n\\nKey features of croton poisoning:\\n\\n• Severe gastrointestinal irritation\\n• Vomiting and profuse diarrhea\\n• Rapid dehydration and shock\\n• No prominent neurotoxicity\\n\\nMechanism:\\n\\n• Croton oil contains phorbol esters\\n• These act as strong irritants causing inflammation of mucosa and increased intestinal secretion\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A is seen in cyanide poisoning\\n\\nOption B is seen in abrin/ricin poisoning\\n\\nOption D is seen in organophosphate poisoning","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-95c178ad0d","n":71,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 24-year-old man sustains a blow to the right temporoparietal region with a cricket bat. He loses consciousness briefly, regains full consciousness for several hours, and then rapidly deteriorates. All of the following statements regarding this condition are correct except:","options":["The commonest source of bleeding is the middle meningeal artery","The hematoma usually crosses cranial sutures because it lies between the dura and arachnoid","The classical lucid interval is seen more commonly in extradural hematoma than subdural hematoma","Urgent evacuation by burr hole or craniotomy is the definitive treatment"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. The hematoma usually crosses cranial sutures because it lies between the dura and arachnoid\\n\\nThe patient has a classical extradural (epidural) hematoma (EDH) due to rupture of the middle meningeal artery, usually following a temporal bone fracture at the pterion. CT demonstrates a biconvex (lens-shaped) hyperdense collection that does not cross sutures because the dura is firmly attached at the sutural margins.\\n\\nThe characteristic lucid interval is present in about 40% of EDH cases. Failure to diagnose EDH during the lucid interval may amount to medical negligence. Definitive treatment is urgent surgical evacuation by burr hole or craniotomy.\\n\\nHigh-Yield:\\n\\n• EDH → Middle meningeal artery\\n• Lens/Biconvex CT appearance\\n• Does not cross sutures\\n• Lucid interval → Classical feature\\n• Coup injury > Contrecoup injury","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3e9464e7c2","n":72,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 25-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with recurrent episodes of severe generalized muscle spasms after ingestion of an unknown poison. On examination: 1. He is fully conscious and oriented between spasms 2. Spasms are triggered by minimal stimuli such as noise or touch 3. There is marked opisthotonus during episodes 4. Pupils are constricted and there is profuse salivation 5. Cyanosis occurs during spasms due to respiratory muscle involvement Regarding strychnine poisoning, which of the above statements are correct?","options":["1, 2, 3 and 5","1, 3 and 4 only","2, 4 and 5 only","1, 2, 3, 4 and 5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 3 and 5\\n\\n1. Consciousness is preserved in strychnine poisoning, which is a key differentiating feature from many other causes of convulsions.\\n2. Spasms are stimulus-sensitive, triggered by minimal external stimuli such as light, sound, or touch.\\n3. Opisthotonus is a classical feature due to severe extensor muscle spasms.\\n4. Pupillary constriction and profuse salivation are not typical; autonomic features are usually minimal compared to organophosphorus poisoning.\\n5. Cyanosis occurs due to involvement of respiratory muscles and prolonged spasms, which can lead to death.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b1d4f7268b","n":73,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 26-year-old man with depressed mental status is brought to the emergency department by police. Due to fear of arrest, he swallowed a handful of pills as the officers approached him. On examination, the patient responds to painful stimuli but is somnolent. Pupils are <2 mm. Respirations are 6/min. Lungs are clear to auscultation. Which of the following changes to temperature, BP and Tidal volume respectively are most likely present in this patient?","options":["Increase, decrease, increase","Decrease, decrease, increase","Decrease, increase, increase","Decrease, decrease, decrease"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Decrease, decrease, decrease\\n\\nThe vignette describes opioid overdose (CNS depression + miosis + respiratory depression).\\n\\n• Pathophysiology:\\n\\n• Opioids stimulate μ-opioid receptors in the brainstem\\n• ↓ Respiratory drive → ↓ rate and ↓ tidal volume\\n• CNS depression → ↓ sympathetic tone\\n\\n• Physiological changes:\\n\\n• Temperature ↓ → impaired thermoregulation\\n• Blood pressure ↓ → vasodilation + CNS depression\\n• Tidal volume ↓ → hypoventilation\\n\\n• Classic triad:\\n\\n• Coma + pinpoint pupils + respiratory depression","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-74e9453fe1","n":74,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 27-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after being assaulted during a street fight. On examination, there is a superficial bruise over the left forearm. During the medicolegal evaluation, the forensic expert explains that the color of a bruise changes predictably over time due to the sequential breakdown of hemoglobin, which can help estimate the age of the injury. What is the typical sequence of color changes observed in a common superficial bruise?","options":["Red → Blue → Brown → Green → Yellow → Normal","Red → Blue → Green → Brown → Yellow → Normal","Red → Blue → Brown → Yellow → Green → Normal","Red → Yellow → Blue → Brown → Green → Normal"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Red → Blue → Brown → Green → Yellow → Normal\\n\\nThe color changes in a superficial bruise (contusion) occur due to the progressive degradation of hemoglobin released from extravasated red blood cells.\\n\\nThe typical sequence is:\\n\\n• Red (0–24 hours): Due to oxygenated hemoglobin.\\n• Blue/Purple (1–3 days): Due to deoxygenated hemoglobin.\\n• Brown (4–5 days): Due to the formation of hemosiderin.\\n• Green (5–7 days): Due to biliverdin.\\n• \xb7Yellow (7–14 days): Due to bilirubin.\\n• Normal skin color (>2 weeks): Following complete resorption of blood pigments.\\n\\nAlthough the exact timing may vary depending on the depth, size, and location of the bruise, this sequence is classically used in forensic medicine to estimate the approximate age of an injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-06429aed4a","n":75,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 27-year-old man leaves the men\'s room of a bar after smoking an unknown substance. He soon becomes disoriented and belligerent. The man displays uncoordinated, jerky movements of his extremities and assaults a bouncer who tries to calm him. When police and emergency medical services personnel arrive, he fights off 4 officers before being restrained and appears immune to pain. In the emergency department, the patient has visual hallucinations, cannot cooperate with the interview, and alternates between agitation and sedation. He is hypertensive and tachycardic, and examination shows vertical nystagmus. Which of the following is the primary mechanism of action of the drug most likely used by this patient?","options":["Dopamine receptor antagonism","GABA-A receptor modulation","Mu-opioid receptor agonism","N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor antagonism"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor antagonism\\n\\nThis presentation is classic for PCP (phencyclidine) intoxication: violent agitation, dissociation, analgesia (“superhuman strength”), hypertension, tachycardia, hallucinations, and vertical nystagmus. PCP primarily blocks NMDA (glutamate) receptors, causing dissociative anesthesia and psychosis.\\n\\nWhy others are incorrect:\\n\\nA. Dopamine receptor antagonism: Seen with antipsychotics; would reduce agitation, not cause violent delirium.\\n\\nB. GABA-A receptor modulation: Benzodiazepines/alcohol cause sedation and ataxia, not severe aggression or nystagmus.\\n\\nC. Mu-opioid receptor agonism: Causes miosis, respiratory depression, and sedation—not hallucinations or combativeness.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bcad90773f","n":76,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 28-year-old man is assaulted and presents with the following injuries: 1. Fracture of nasal bone without displacement 2. Loss of one incisor tooth following trauma 3. Laceration over forearm causing weakness of finger movements for 10 days, with full recovery later 4. Blunt trauma abdomen requiring ICU admission due to internal bleeding but no surgery performed Which of the following injuries qualify as grievous hurt under Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita?","options":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 2 and 4 only\\n\\n1.\xa0\xa0\xa0 Fracture of nasal bone qualifies as grievous hurt because any fracture of bone, whether displaced or not, is included.\\n\\n2.\xa0\xa0\xa0 Loss of tooth qualifies as grievous hurt as fracture or dislocation of a tooth is specifically included.\\n\\n3.\xa0\xa0\xa0 Temporary weakness of finger movements does not qualify because there is no permanent impairment. Grievous hurt requires permanent loss or impairment of function.\\n\\n4.\xa0\xa0\xa0 Internal injury causing life-threatening condition qualifies under “hurt which endangers life,” even if surgery is not performed","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-847cbd9ff9","n":77,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 28-year-old man is brought to the outpatient psychiatry clinic by his family because he has been sleeping almost the entire day for the past week. He reports profound fatigue, difficulty concentrating, increased appetite, and vivid unpleasant dreams. He says that nothing feels enjoyable anymore and has become irritable and depressed. His family states that, until a few days ago, he had been attending parties every night, staying awake for long periods, and spending excessive amounts of money. Physical examination reveals no tremors, diaphoresis, or autonomic instability. Vital signs are normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Cocaine intoxication","Cocaine withdrawal","Opioid withdrawal","Alcohol withdrawal"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cocaine withdrawal\\n\\nThis patient has the classic \\"crash\\" phase of cocaine withdrawal, characterized by:\\n\\n• Hypersomnolence (sleeping excessively)\\n• Fatigue\\n• Depressed mood and anhedonia\\n• Hyperphagia (increased appetite)\\n• Vivid, unpleasant dreams\\n• Psychomotor slowing and poor concentration\\n• Strong craving for cocaine\\n\\nUnlike alcohol or opioid withdrawal, cocaine withdrawal does not produce significant autonomic hyperactivity (eg, tremors, tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis).\\n\\nWhy the other options are incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Cocaine intoxication: Euphoria, mydriasis, tachycardia, hypertension, agitation, decreased appetite, insomnia.\\n• C. Opioid withdrawal: Mydriasis, lacrimation, rhinorrhea, piloerection, diarrhea, yawning, muscle aches.\\n• D. Alcohol withdrawal: Tremors, anxiety, tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, seizures, delirium tremens.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-be60a19d77","n":78,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 28-year-old man is found hanging from a ceiling fan. On autopsy, a ligature mark is present around the neck. Regarding hanging, which of the following statements are correct? A. Ligature mark is usually oblique, non-continuous, and located above the thyroid cartilage B. Dribbling of saliva is a characteristic feature of antemortem hanging C. Fracture of hyoid bone is common in young individuals D. Face is typically congested and cyanosed in typical hanging E. Suspension need not be complete for death to occur","options":["B, C, D","C, D, E","A, B, D","A, B, E"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. A, B, E\\n\\n• A is correct: Ligature mark in hanging is typically oblique, above thyroid cartilage, and non-continuous\\n• B is correct: Dribbling of saliva is a classical sign of antemortem hanging\\n• C is incorrect: Hyoid fracture is more common in elderly, not young\\n• D is incorrect: In typical hanging, face is usually pale, not congested (congestion seen in partial/asphyxial hanging)\\n• E is correct: Complete suspension is not required; even partial hanging can cause death","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8eb6cbdb85","n":79,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 28-year-old motorcyclist sustains multiple superficial skin injuries after skidding on the road. On examination, the abrasions show a heaped-up epithelial tag at one end of the wound. All of the following statements regarding abrasions are correct except:","options":["Abrasion is a loss of the superficial epidermis produced most commonly by tangential force","The epithelial tag indicates the direction of movement of the causative force","Graze abrasions are commonly encountered in road traffic accidents due to sliding over a rough surface","Corneal abrasion is classified as simple hurt because it heals without permanent disability"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Corneal abrasion is classified as simple hurt because it heals without permanent disability\\n\\n• Abrasion is the most medicolegally important wound and consists of loss of the superficial epidermis.\\n• Most abrasions heal without scarring and are classified as simple hurt, except corneal abrasion, which is considered grievous hurt because it may permanently impair vision.\\n• An epithelial tag is formed by the heaping up of detached epidermis at the terminal end of the abrasion and helps determine the direction of force.\\n• Graze abrasions (brush burns/gravel rash) result from tangential friction, whereas pressure and imprint abrasions occur due to perpendicular compression.\\n\\nHigh-Yield Pearls:\\n\\n• Most medicolegally significant wound → Abrasion\\n• Epithelial tag → Indicates direction of force\\n• Graze abrasion → Sliding injury (RTA)\\n• Corneal abrasion → Exception (Grievous hurt)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d56e9f3a18","n":80,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 3-year-old, previously healthy boy is brought to the emergency department due to accidental drug ingestion. His mother found him playing with his grandfather\'s pill bottles earlier today, and later, he developed nausea and vomited twice. The patient also began breathing rapidly and appeared ill. His grandfather recently had a myocardial infarction and takes multiple medications. Laboratory studies reveal high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Treatment with intravenous sodium bicarbonate infusion is begun. This therapy is most likely to provide a beneficial effect via which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Decreasing cardiotoxic effects of the drug","Decreasing charged form of the drug in plasma","Increasing drug trapping in the gastric lumen","Increasing excretion of the drug in the urine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Increasing excretion of the drug in the urine\\n\\nA 3-year-old with:\\n\\n• Accidental ingestion\\n• Nausea + vomiting\\n• Tachypnea (compensatory hyperventilation)\\n• High anion gap metabolic acidosis\\n\\nGrandfather post-MI → likely medications include aspirin (salicylates).--> This is classic salicylate poisoning.\\n\\nSalicylates are weak acids. In alkaline urine:\\n\\n• Weak acids become ionized (A⁻ form)\\n• Ionized drugs cannot diffuse back across renal tubular membranes\\n• They remain trapped in urine\\n• Renal excretion increases\\n\\nThis is called urinary alkalinization.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eb063dd66c","n":81,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 30-year-old woman reports an alleged assault in which she sustained bleeding injuries inside her house. By the time the forensic team arrives, the floor has been thoroughly cleaned with detergent and water. Although no visible blood stains remain, the investigating officer requests a test that can detect latent blood stains on the cleaned surface. Which of the following methods is most commonly used to visualize and detect blood stains, even after they have been washed away?","options":["Infrared photography","Luminol test","Ultraviolet light","Magnifying lens"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Luminol test\\n\\nThe Luminol test is a highly sensitive presumptive test for blood that is widely used in forensic investigations to detect latent or washed-away blood stains. Luminol (3-aminophthalhydrazide) reacts with the peroxidase-like activity of the heme component of hemoglobin in the presence of an oxidizing agent, producing a characteristic blue-white chemiluminescent glow in a dark environment.\\n\\nBecause of its high sensitivity, luminol can detect minute quantities of blood, even after repeated attempts to clean the crime scene. However, as it is a presumptive test, positive results must be confirmed with additional forensic tests.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b97a52a7c1","n":82,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 32-year-old farmer presents with excessive salivation, sweating, and miosis after pesticide exposure. He is treated with atropine and pralidoxime and initially improves. However, 48 hours later, he develops progressive weakness of neck flexors, proximal limb muscles, and respiratory muscles, while muscarinic symptoms have resolved. Pupils are normal and there are no fasciculations. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this delayed neurological deterioration?","options":["Persistent inhibition of acetylcholinesterase at muscarinic synapses","Desensitization and dysfunction of nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction","Organophosphate-induced delayed axonal neuropathy due to neuropathy target esterase inhibition","Rebound cholinergic crisis due to inadequate atropine dosing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Desensitization and dysfunction of nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction\\n\\nIntermediate syndrome (IMS) is a delayed neuromuscular complication of organophosphorus poisoning, occurring 24–96 hours after the acute cholinergic phase has resolved.\\n\\n• Mechanism:\\n\\no\xa0\xa0\xa0 Prolonged acetylcholine exposure → dysfunction/desensitization of nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction\\n\\n• Clinical features:\\n\\no\xa0\xa0\xa0 Weakness of neck flexors, proximal limb muscles, and respiratory muscles\\n\\no\xa0\xa0\xa0 No prominent muscarinic symptoms at this stage\\n\\n• Major risk:\\n\\no\xa0\xa0\xa0 Respiratory failure → requires ventilatory support\\n\\nTimeline differentiation:\\n\\n• Acute phase (minutes–hours) → muscarinic + nicotinic excess\\n• Intermediate syndrome (1–4 days) → pure motor weakness (NMJ dysfunction)\\n• Delayed neuropathy (weeks later) → distal axonal degeneration (NTE inhibition)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8b7b83e852","n":83,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 32-year-old man is found dead at the scene of a firearm injury. During the medicolegal examination, the investigating officer notes that the deceased is firmly clutching the trigger of the weapon in his hand. The forensic expert explains that this finding is due to cadaveric spasm, an instantaneous form of muscle stiffening that occurs at the moment of death. Which of the following statements regarding cadaveric spasm is incorrect?","options":["It is observed with sudden death","The stage of primary relaxation is absent","It can be simulated by cold stiffening","It indicates the time since death"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. It indicates the time since death\\n\\nCadaveric spasm (instantaneous rigor) is a rare postmortem phenomenon in which voluntary muscles that were actively contracting immediately before death remain contracted without passing through the stage of primary flaccidity. It occurs at the moment of death and is usually associated with sudden, emotionally charged, or violent deaths.\\n\\nUnlike rigor mortis, cadaveric spasm does not help estimate the postmortem interval (time since death). Instead, it is of forensic significance because it may preserve the last voluntary act of the deceased, such as tightly gripping a weapon, grass, or hair.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\n• A. It is observed with sudden death: Correct. Cadaveric spasm is typically associated with sudden violent deaths, emotional stress, fear, exhaustion, electrocution, cerebral hemorrhage, firearm injuries, and certain convulsant poisonings (e.g., strychnine).\\n• B. The stage of primary relaxation is absent: Correct. In cadaveric spasm, become rigid immediately after death, bypassing the normal stage of primary flaccidity.\\n• C. It can be simulated by cold stiffening: Correct. Cold stiffening caused by freezing may mimic cadaveric spasm but resolves after thawing and should be distinguished during forensic examination.\\n\\nHigh-yield: Cadaveric Spasm vs Rigor Mortis\\n\\n| Feature | Cadaveric Spasm | Rigor Mortis\\n\\n| ------- | --------------- | ------------\\n\\n| Onset | Immediate, at the moment of death | Begins 1–2 hours after death\\n\\n| Primary relaxation | Absent | Present\\n\\n| Muscles involved | Usually a single group of voluntary muscles (commonly hands) | All muscles of the body\\n\\n| Association | Sudden violent or emotional death | Occurs after all deaths\\n\\n| Forensic significance | Preserves the last voluntary act (e.g., grasping a weapon) | Useful for estimating postmortem interval\\n\\n| Use in estimating time since death | No | Yes","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2069e892cb","n":84,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 32-year-old prisoner has been on a prolonged hunger strike to protest prison conditions. He is conscious, mentally competent, and repeatedly refuses oral or enteral nutrition after being informed of the medical consequences of his decision. The prison authorities request the attending physician to initiate force-feeding to preserve his life. According to Indian constitutional principles, force-feeding in this situation is:","options":["Lawful under Article 21 of the Constitution","Unlawful under Article 21 of the Constitution","Lawful under Article 19 of the Constitution","Unlawful under Article 19 of the Constitution"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Unlawful under Article 21 of the Constitution\\n\\nA competent prisoner who voluntarily undertakes a hunger strike and refuses nutrition after giving informed refusal has the right to bodily autonomy. Force-feeding such an individual is considered unlawful under Article 21 of the Constitution of India, which guarantees the right to life and personal liberty, including the right to bodily integrity and informed refusal of medical treatment.\\n\\nThe Declaration of Malta on Hunger Strikers also states that a physician should respect the autonomous decision of a mentally competent individual who refuses food, even if the refusal may result in death. Physicians should continue to provide medical assessment, counseling, and supportive care, but should not administer nutrition against the patient\'s informed wishes.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1fdd6238b0","n":85,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 32-year-old restrained driver collides head-on with a concrete divider at high speed. He has a transverse lumbar vertebral fracture, mesenteric tear requiring laparotomy, and a linear patterned bruise extending across the lower abdomen. There are no steering-wheel chest injuries. Which of the following statements regarding this injury pattern is most correct?","options":["The vertebral injury is a jefferson fracture produced by axial compression, and liver injury is the commonest associated abdominal injury","The patient has sustained a classic seat-belt injury characterized by a Chance fracture due to hyperextension of the spine","The vertebral injury is a Chance fracture caused by sudden hyperflexion over the lap belt, with mesenteric injury being the most common associated intra-abdominal injury","The combination is characteristic of dashboard injury and is commonly associated with posterior shoulder dislocation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. The vertebral injury is a Chance fracture caused by sudden hyperflexion over the lap belt, with mesenteric injury being the most common associated intra-abdominal injury\\n\\nA Chance fracture is a horizontal (transverse) flexion-distraction injury of the spine caused by sudden hyperflexion over a lap-type seat belt during rapid deceleration. It typically involves the posterior vertebral elements and is highly associated with seat-belt syndrome.\\n\\nSeat-belt injuries commonly produce:\\n\\n• Patterned abrasion/ecchymosis across the abdomen (\\"seat-belt sign\\")\\n• Chance fracture\\n• Mesenteric tears (most common)\\n• Small bowel injury\\n• Occasionally pancreatic and duodenal injuries\\n\\nThis injury pattern should be distinguished from:\\n\\n• Dashboard injury → Posterior hip dislocation, patellar fracture\\n• Steering-wheel injury → Sternal fracture, patterned chest bruise, ladder-rung tear of the aorta\\n• Windshield injury → Sparrow-foot facial lacerations","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4c24945186","n":86,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 32-year-old trekker is brought with complaints of progressive weakness of limbs that began in the feet and ascended over 2 hours. He is fully conscious, pupils are normal, and sensory examination is intact. No fasciculations are seen. He had chewed seeds from a roadside plant with a hollow stem showing flowers as shown below. Respiratory effort is rapidly worsening. Which toxin is most likely responsible?","options":["Aconite","Succinylcholine","Coniine","Muscarine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Coniine\\n\\nThe description of a roadside plant with a hollow stem and purple blotches points to poison hemlock (Conium maculatum). Its toxin coniine acts as a nicotinic (Nm) receptor agonist initially, then causes neuromuscular blockade, leading to rapidly progressive ascending motor weakness with intact sensation and normal pupils. The rapid progression to respiratory failure fits hemlock poisoning. Lack of fasciculations can be seen as paralysis evolves quickly.\\n\\nA. Aconitine: Causes GI symptoms and severe arrhythmias with paresthesias.\\n\\nB. Succinylcholine: Drug exposure (anesthetic), not plant ingestion; causes fasciculations.\\n\\nD. Muscarine: Produces SLUDGE symptoms (salivation, lacrimation, bradycardia, miosis).","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-46d10fe867","n":87,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 34-year-old farmer was spraying with insecticides 4 hours ago and now presents with excessive salivation, pinpoint pupils, fasciculations, sweating, vomiting. Pulse 46/min, BP 90/60. Laboratory test shows pseudocholinesterase level is 42% of normal. Which of the following best describes his clinical condition?","options":["No toxicity — reassurance only","Symptomatic organophosphate poisoning","Mild exposure — no treatment needed","Delayed neuropathy due to OP poisoning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Symptomatic organophosphate poisoning\\n\\nThis patient has acute symptomatic organophosphate (OP) poisoning. The classic cholinergic toxidrome is evident: excessive salivation, sweating, vomiting, pinpoint pupils (miosis), fasciculations, bradycardia, and hypotension. The reduced pseudocholinesterase level (42% of normal) confirms significant OP exposure. Symptoms occurring within hours indicate acute toxicity requiring urgent treatment with atropine and pralidoxime.\\n\\nA: Reassurance is inappropriate due to clear cholinergic symptoms\\n\\nC: Mild exposure would be asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic\\n\\nD: Delayed neuropathy occurs 1–3 weeks later, presenting with motor weakness, not acute cholinergic signs.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4646b2a97d","n":88,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by his employer after a workplace incident. He is a heavy-machinery operator and was found drowsy and slurring his words at the start of his shift. He has a two-year history of polysubstance use and was in a rehabilitation programme six months ago, from which he defaulted. On examination he is arousable but inattentive, with horizontal nystagmus, ataxic gait, and pupils 3 mm and reactive. Blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg and pulse is 88/min. There are healed needle marks in the left antecubital fossa. His employer requests a urine drug screen as part of the company\'s fitness-for-duty protocol. The laboratory performs a standard immunoassay panel with confirmatory gas chromatography–mass spectrometry. His last reported use of each substance is more than 7 days ago. Which of the following substances is most likely to still be detectable in this patient\'s urine?","options":["Cocaine","Heroin","Chronic heavy cannabis","Ethanol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Chronic heavy cannabis\\n\\nTHC-COOH (11-nor-9-carboxy-Δ⁹-THC) is highly lipophilic, sequestered in adipose tissue, and released slowly. In chronic daily users, urine remains positive for up to 30 days or more — the longest detection window of any common drug of abuse. All the other listed agents clear well within 7 days.\\n\\nUrinary detection windows — high yield table\\n\\n| Substance | Urinary metabolite detected | Detection window\\n\\n| --------- | --------------------------- | ----------------\\n\\n| Ethanol | Ethanol / EtG, EtS | 7–12 hr (EtG up to 80 hr)\\n\\n| Cocaine | Benzoylecgonine | 2–4 days\\n\\n| Heroin | 6-monoacetylmorphine (6-MAM), morphine | 6-MAM: <8 hr; morphine 2–3 days\\n\\n| Amphetamine/Methamphetamine | Parent drug + amphetamine | 2–3 days\\n\\n| Cannabis (single use) | THC-COOH | \\\\~3 days\\n\\n| Cannabis (chronic heavy) | THC-COOH | Up to 30+ days ✅\\n\\n| Benzodiazepines (short-acting) | Oxazepam etc. | \\\\~3 days\\n\\n| Benzodiazepines (long-acting) | Nordiazepam, oxazepam | Up to 30 days\\n\\n| Barbiturates (long-acting) | Parent drug | Up to 3 weeks\\n\\n| Phencyclidine | PCP | Up to 8 days\\n\\n| Methadone | EDDP | \\\\~3 days\\n\\n| LSD | 2-oxo-3-hydroxy-LSD | 1–3 days (not on standard panels)\\n\\n• SAMHSA \\"Federal 5-panel\\": Cannabis, Cocaine, Opiates, Amphetamines, Phencyclidine (mnemonic: \\"CCOAP\\")\\n• Not detected on routine panels: LSD, GHB, ketamine, inhalants, synthetic cannabinoids (\\"spice\\"), fentanyl (needs a dedicated assay), buprenorphine, most designer drugs\\n• Immunoassay = screening (sensitive, cross-reactive) → GC-MS/LC-MS = confirmation (specific; gold standard)\\n• Hair analysis detects use over \\\\~90 days; meconium detects third-trimester fetal exposure\\n• A positive urine screen indicates exposure, not impairment — a crucial medicolegal caveat","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-43eec16783","n":89,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 34-year-old man with a known history of G6PD deficiency undergoes a minor dermatological procedure. A topical anesthetic cream containing lidocaine and prilocaine is applied under occlusion for a prolonged duration. Soon after, he develops acute dyspnea, cyanosis, and headache. Pulse oximetry shows oxygen saturation of 85% that does not improve despite administration of 100% oxygen. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this patient?","options":["Hemodialysis","Intravenous vitamin C","Intravenous corticosteroids","Intravenous methylene blue"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Intravenous vitamin C\\n\\nThis patient has methemoglobinemia due to topical lidocaine/prilocaine, which oxidize hemoglobin iron to the ferric (Fe\xb3⁺) state, impairing oxygen delivery. Clues include cyanosis, dyspnea, headache, and low pulse oximetry unresponsive to 100% oxygen. Although methylene blue is standard therapy, it is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency because it can precipitate hemolysis and worsen oxidative stress. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a safe alternative that reduces methemoglobin nonenzymatically.\\n\\nA. Hemodialysis: Ineffective, as methemoglobin is intracellular.\\n\\nC. Corticosteroids: No role in treating methemoglobinemia.\\n\\nD. Methylene blue: Contraindicated in G6PD deficiency.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-dcea3e2eb8","n":90,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the emergency department due to bloody emesis. She had 2 episodes of vomiting bright red blood and feels lightheaded and dizzy. The patient was recently diagnosed with factitious disorder after being hospitalized multiple times for a myriad of symptoms and undergoing several invasive procedures. Physical examination shows scattered ecchymoses. Rectal examination shows maroon-colored, guaiac-positive stool. After much questioning, she admits to having ingested rat poison several days ago. Immediate treatment of this patient should include which of the following?","options":["Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC)","Cryopreipitate","Fresh frozen plasma","Vit K injection"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC)\\n\\nThis patient has active, life-threatening bleeding (hematemesis, melena, hemodynamic symptoms) due to ingestion of warfarin-type rat poison, which causes deficiency of vitamin K–dependent clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X). In such situations, immediate reversal is required. PCC rapidly replaces these clotting factors and provides fast hemostasis, making it the treatment of choice in acute severe bleeding.\\n\\n• Vitamin K: Essential but has a delayed onset (6–24 hours); not sufficient alone initially.\\n• Fresh frozen plasma: Slower, requires large volumes, risk of overload.\\n• Cryoprecipitate: Used for fibrinogen deficiency, not anticoagulant toxicity.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a4463a98b6","n":91,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 35-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after attending a party. He is disoriented, has slurred speech, horizontal nystagmus, and is unable to recall events from the past few hours despite apparently purposeful behavior during that time. His vital signs are stable. Which of the following best explains his clinical state and the most likely blood alcohol concentration (BAC)?","options":["Retrograde amnesia; BAC 80–200 mg/dL","Anterograde amnesia (blackout); BAC 200–300 mg/dL","Delirium tremens; BAC &gt;300 mg/dL","Korsakoff syndrome; BAC 30–80 mg/dL"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Anterograde amnesia (blackout); BAC 200–300 mg/dL\\n\\nAcute alcohol intoxication is classified based on blood alcohol concentration (BAC), with specific neurobehavioral features corresponding to each level.\\n\\n• 80–200 mg/dL: impaired judgment, incoordination, mood changes\\n• 200–300 mg/dL:\\n\\n• Alcoholic blackout (anterograde amnesia)\\n• Slurred speech, nystagmus\\n• Behavior may appear purposeful despite memory loss\\n\\n• >300 mg/dL:\\n\\n• CNS depression → respiratory compromise, coma, death\\n\\nImportant concept:\\n\\n• Alcoholic blackout = anterograde amnesia\\n\\n• Inability to form new memories during intoxication\\n• Distinguished from retrograde amnesia\\n• No confusion at the time of events\\n\\n• Legal limit in India: \\\\~30 mg/dL","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2ac0a9add3","n":92,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 37-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by ambulance after his sister found him unresponsive. Several days ago, he was discharged from an inpatient addiction centre, where he was treated for multiple substance use disorders and long-standing bipolar disorder. The patient\'s sister states that he has been depressed and has not taken any of his prescribed medications since discharge. Temperature is 36.7\xb0 C (98.1\xb0 F), blood pressure is 80/40 mm Hg, pulse is 62/min, and respirations are 10/min. The patient is obtunded and unresponsive to verbal commands. Physical examination shows 2-mm pupils. The patient is ventilated by bag valve mask, and supplemental oxygen and intravenous fluids are initiated. Which of the following should be administered next?","options":["Buprenorphine","Lithium carbonate","Naloxone","Naltrexone"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Naloxone\\n\\nThe patient has opioid overdose features: unresponsiveness, respiratory depression, hypotension, bradycardia, and pinpoint pupils. Naloxone is a rapid-acting opioid antagonist that reverses μ-opioid effects and should be given immediately after airway support.\\n\\nA. Buprenorphine: Partial opioid agonist; used for maintenance, not acute overdose.\\n\\nB. Lithium carbonate: Mood stabilizer; no role in coma or respiratory depression.\\n\\nD. Naltrexone: Long-acting opioid antagonist; not used in emergencies and may precipitate withdrawal.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eda588a5bd","n":93,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 38-year-old woman is brought for medicolegal autopsy after being found unconscious with multiple bruises over the neck. External examination reveals abrasions and contusions around the neck. On internal examination, there is extensive hemorrhage in the neck muscles along with fractures of the hyoid bone and thyroid cartilage. The forensic pathologist concludes that death resulted from external compression of the neck. The above findings are most suggestive of:","options":["Strangulation","Hanging","Choking","Gagging"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Strangulation\\n\\nStrangulation is caused by external compression of the neck using hands (manual strangulation) or a ligature (ligature strangulation), resulting in obstruction of the airway and neck vessels. The force applied directly to the neck commonly produces:\\n\\n• Fracture of the hyoid bone\\n• Fracture of the thyroid cartilage\\n• Hemorrhage and bruising of the neck muscles\\n• Soft tissue injury around the larynx and surrounding structures\\n\\nThese findings are highly suggestive of strangulation, particularly manual strangulation, and are important medicolegal indicators of homicidal violence.\\n\\nHigh-yield: Hanging vs Strangulation\\n\\n| Feature | Strangulation | Hanging\\n\\n| ------- | ------------- | -------\\n\\n| Mechanism | External compression of the neck | Body weight causes neck compression\\n\\n| Neck muscle hemorrhage | Marked | Minimal or absent\\n\\n| Hyoid/thyroid cartilage fracture | Common, especially in manual strangulation | Less common; more frequent in elderly\\n\\n| Ligature mark | Horizontal, completely encircles the neck | Oblique, incomplete, above the thyroid cartilage\\n\\n| Manner of death | Usually homicidal | Usually suicidal","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ceadef9081","n":94,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 42-year-old man is admitted to the hospital after sustaining fractures of his right femur and tibia in a motor vehicle collision. The patient becomes agitated, hypervigilant, and paranoid 12 hours after admission. He repeatedly tells the nurses that he can hear people in the corridor making insulting remarks about him. When hospital staff check the area near the patient\'s room, no one is found. Medical history is unremarkable, but psychiatric history is significant for cocaine, marijuana, and alcohol misuse. On examination, the patient is alert and oriented. Temperature is 37.2\xb0C (99\xb0F), blood pressure is 135/87 mmHg pulse is 85/min, and respirations are 18/min. He is slightly tremulous and diaphoretic. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?","options":["Alcoholic hallucinosis","Brief psychotic disorder","Cocaine withdrawal","Delirium tremens"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Alcoholic hallucinosis\\n\\nThis patient has alcoholic hallucinosis, characterized by vivid auditory hallucinations with intact orientation and clear sensorium, typically occurring within 12–48 hours of alcohol cessation. He is alert, oriented, mildly tremulous, and diaphoretic, supporting alcohol withdrawal without global cognitive impairment. The hallucinations are persecutory and occur without delirium, which is classic for alcoholic hallucinosis.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\n• B. Brief psychotic disorder: Requires ≥1 psychotic symptom lasting >1 day and is not linked to substance withdrawal.\\n• C. Cocaine withdrawal: Causes depression, fatigue, hypersomnia—not hallucinations or autonomic signs.\\n• D. Delirium tremens: Presents later (48–96 hours) with severe autonomic instability and altered consciousness, which are absent here.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8609d2f1d1","n":95,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 42-year-old woman files a case of medical negligence against her gynecologist for complications that she claims occurred during a hysterectomy performed 3 years ago. She states that she developed chronic pelvic pain and wants compensation. Which legal defense is most appropriate for the doctor?","options":["Res ipsa loquitur","Res indicata","Res judicata","Therapeutic misadventure"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Res indicata\\n\\nRes Indicata = Defense of limitation in medical negligence cases.\\n\\nSuit for compensation due to negligence must be filed within 2 years from date of alleged negligence (Consumer Protection Act).\\n\\nHere, patient filed after 3 years → time-barred case → Res Indicata applies.\\n\\nA. Res judicata = matter already decided\\n\\nC. Res ipsa loquitur = thing speaks for itself (prima facie negligence)\\n\\nD. Therapeutic misadventure = acceptable risk despite due care","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8a8593d0b0","n":96,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 43-year-old man is found wandering in the street in winter and is brought to the emergency department by a passing motorist. The patient is confused and unable to provide any history. He has no previous hospital records. The patient appears disheveled and lethargic but follows commands. The oral mucosa is moist, and there are extensive dental caries. The patient has no cervical lymphadenopathy, but there is bilateral, nontender swelling of the cheeks consistent with salivary gland enlargement. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this latter finding?","options":["Alcoholism","Mumps","Pleomorphic adenoma","Salivary gland stone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Alcoholism\\n\\nChronic alcoholism commonly causes bilateral, painless parotid gland enlargement due to sialadenosis (noninflammatory salivary gland hypertrophy). This is associated with malnutrition, autonomic neuropathy, and metabolic changes seen in long-term alcohol use. Supporting clues include disheveled appearance, confusion, winter wandering, poor dentition with dental caries, and preserved moist mucosa, which argue against dehydration or obstruction.\\n\\nB. Mumps: Causes acute, painful parotitis with fever and systemic symptoms, usually unilateral or tender initially\\n\\nC. Pleomorphic adenoma: Typically presents as a unilateral, slow-growing, painless parotid mass\\n\\nD. Salivary gland stone: Causes episodic pain and swelling, especially during meals, often unilateral.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6ded759ac4","n":97,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 44-year-old man hospitalized for acute cholecystitis is being evaluated for anxiety and agitation. He underwent an open cholecystectomy without having any operative complications. Two days after admission, the patient experiences anxiety and tremulousness, and he becomes irritable, severely agitated, and verbally abusive to the nursing staff. The patient does not use tobacco or illicit drugs but admits to drinking 6-8 beers daily for the last several years. Blood pressure is 160/90 mmHg and pulse is 110/min. Examination shows hand tremors bilaterally. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for this patient?","options":["Carbamazepine","Chlorpromazine","Haloperidol","Chlordiazepoxide"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Chlordiazepoxide\\n\\nThis patient is experiencing alcohol withdrawal, suggested by tremors, anxiety, agitation, hypertension, and tachycardia occurring 48 hours after hospitalization, when alcohol intake abruptly stopped. Chlordiazepoxide, a long-acting benzodiazepine, is first-line therapy as it enhances GABA-A activity, preventing progression to seizures or delirium tremens and providing smooth withdrawal control.\\n\\nA. Carbamazepine: Can be used for mild withdrawal but is not first-line in hospitalized, symptomatic patients.\\n\\nB. Chlorpromazine: An antipsychotic that does not treat withdrawal physiology and lowers seizure threshold.\\n\\nC. Haloperidol: May control agitation but should only be adjunctive; it does not prevent seizures or autonomic instability.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bbcbea5933","n":98,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 45-year-old male is brought dead to the hospital. The doctor ties a ligature around the base of the fingers and notices no bluish discoloration or edema. Which test is the doctor using to check for the absence of circulation?","options":["Icard’s test","Magnus test","Diaphanous test","Pressure test"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Magnus test\\n\\nThe Magnus test is used to confirm the absence of circulation after death. The described procedure — a ligature at the base of the finger checking for distal bluish discoloration/edema — is textbook Magnus.\\n\\nMechanism: In a living person, the ligature blocks low-pressure venous return while higher-pressure arterial inflow continues → blood pools distally → the finger tip becomes cyanotic (blue-red) and swollen. In a dead body there is no circulation, so no congestion develops → the confirmation of death.\\n\\n| Option | Test | Principle | Positive sign in the living\\n\\n| ------ | ---- | --------- | ---------------------------\\n\\n| b ✓ | Magnus | Ligature at finger base → venous block, arterial patent | Distal cyanosis + edema\\n\\n| a | Icard\'s | Subcutaneous/IV fluorescein injection | Skin turns greenish-yellow as dye circulates\\n\\n| c | Diaphanous (webbing) | Fingers spread against a bright light | Web spaces glow translucent red/pink (dead → opaque yellow)\\n\\n| d | Pressure / Nail-bed | Press the nail → release | Blanching then rapid re-perfusion (pink returns)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f47d23821f","n":99,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 45-year-old woman is brought to the hospital after she collapsed during a Coldplay concert on a hot summer day. The patient has a history of fibromyalgia and takes amitriptyline. Temperature is 40.5 C (104.9 F), blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg, pulse is 110/min, and respirations are 22/min. The skin is warm and red. She is disoriented. Neurologic examination shows no focal findings. If this patient’s medication contributed to her current condition, which of the following mechanisms is most likely responsible?","options":["Impaired cardiovascular response to heat stress","Impaired dissipation of body heat","Increased heat production by increasing muscle tone","Increased peripheral vasoconstriction"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Impaired dissipation of body heat\\n\\nThis patient has heat stroke (core temperature >40\xb0C, altered mental status, hypotension). Amitriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant, has strong anticholinergic properties. Anticholinergics inhibit sweating by blocking muscarinic receptors in sweat glands, which prevents evaporative heat loss, the body’s primary cooling mechanism during heat exposure. As a result, heat cannot be effectively dissipated, leading to dangerous hyperthermia.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\n• Impaired cardiovascular response: Seen with beta-blockers, not TCAs.\\n• Increased heat production by muscle tone: Typical of malignant hyperthermia or NMS.\\n• Increased peripheral vasoconstriction: Would reduce heat loss but is not caused by amitriptyline.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2ec9756879","n":100,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 46-year-old farmer presents with chronic progressive dyspnea and dry cough. Chest X-ray shows diffuse centrilobular ground-glass nodules and upper-lobe predominant fibrosis. BAL reveals lymphocytic predominance. He frequently sprays crops with bluish fungicidal solutions. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","options":["Chronic silicosis","Farmer’s lung","Vineyard sprayer’s lung","Hard metal pneumoconiosis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vineyard sprayer’s lung\\n\\nCopper causes ILD which is referred to vineyard sprayers lung. It is due to inhalation of copper sulphate mixture called Bordeaux mixture. Chronic exposure causes progressive dyspnea, dry cough, upper-lobe–predominant fibrosis, and centrilobular ground-glass nodules.\\n\\nBAL showing lymphocytic predominance strongly supports hypersensitivity pneumonitis rather than fibrotic pneumoconiosis.\\n\\nWhy not other options:-\\n\\n• Chronic silicosis: Shows egg-shell calcification and silicotic nodules, not lymphocytic BAL.\\n• Farmer’s lung: Similar pathology but classically linked to moldy hay exposure, not fungicide spraying.\\n• Hard metal pneumoconiosis: Associated with cobalt/tungsten exposure and giant cell interstitial pneumonia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-dc3e9c2b34","n":101,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department due to accidental drug ingestion. His mother states the patient was playing by himself earlier today and 2 hours later she found him unresponsive in the bathroom. There was an empty bottle of hydrocodone-acetaminophen next to him, but she does not know how many pills were in it. The emergency medical team found the patient stuporous and with bradypnea. His mental status and respirations promptly improved after 1 dose of intravenous naloxone was administered, and he was transported to the hospital. On arrival, the patient is sleepy but arouses easily to voice and follows simple instructions. Vital signs, including respirations, are normal. One hour later, he has worsening lethargy, bradypnea, and miosis. Which of the following most likely accounts for this patient\'s current clinical deterioration?","options":["Acetaminophen-induced liver failure","Incomplete CNS penetrance of naloxone","Partial agonistic effect of naloxone","Short half-life of naloxone"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Short half-life of naloxone\\n\\nNaloxone is a competitive opioid antagonist used to reverse opioid overdose, but it has a short half-life (≈30–90 minutes).\\n\\n• “Re-narcotization”:\\n\\n• Many opioids (e.g., hydrocodone) have a longer duration of action than naloxone\\n• After initial reversal, naloxone effect wears off first\\n• Residual opioid → recurrence of CNS depression, bradypnea, miosis\\n\\n• Clinical implication:\\n\\n• Patients require close monitoring and repeated naloxone doses or infusion\\n• Especially important in long-acting opioid overdose\\n\\n• Other Options:\\n\\n• Acetaminophen toxicity → delayed hepatotoxicity (not acute CNS depression)\\n• Incomplete CNS penetrance → naloxone acts centrally effectively\\n• Partial agonist effect → naloxone is a pure antagonist","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-78bc3cb00f","n":102,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 50-year-old male passed away due to a myocardial infarction. The family requested embalming for preservation of the body before the funeral. Which of the following statements about embalming is incorrect?","options":["Produces permanent chemical rigidity that mimics rigor mortis","Sodium citrate is used in embalming fluid as an anticoagulant","Embalming makes it easier to interpret postmortem injuries","Femoral artery is commonly used for injecting embalming fluid"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Embalming makes it easier to interpret postmortem injuries\\n\\n• This statement is incorrect.\\n• Embalming alters tissues by causing dehydration, discoloration, swelling, and chemical fixation, which can obscure or distort injuries, making forensic interpretation difficult. Therefore, embalming is avoided before medicolegal autopsy.\\n• Produces permanent chemical rigidity: True—embalming causes formalin-induced fixation mimicking rigor mortis.\\u2028Sodium citrate as anticoagulant: True—it prevents clotting of blood.\\u2028Femoral artery commonly used: True—frequently used for arterial injection during embalming.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3c8ee35b8a","n":103,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 55-year-old diabetic man with a chronic foot ulcer is advised by his surgeon to undergo daily dressings and strict glycemic control. Despite repeated instructions, he frequently misses follow-up visits and does not take his insulin regularly. He returns after 3 weeks with a worsened infection requiring amputation. He files a case of medical negligence against the surgeon, claiming improper treatment. Which legal principle will best protect the doctor?","options":["Res ipsa loquitur","Vicarious liability","Contributory negligence","Therapeutic misadventure"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Contributory negligence\\n\\nThis case illustrates contributory negligence, where the patient’s own actions significantly contributed to the adverse outcome. Despite appropriate medical advice—regular dressings and strict glycemic control—the patient failed to comply with follow-up and insulin therapy, leading to worsening infection and eventual amputation. When a patient does not adhere to treatment instructions, the doctor cannot be held solely responsible, and this principle offers strong legal protection.\\n\\nA. Res ipsa loquitur: Applies when negligence is obvious without explanation, which is not true here.\\n\\nB. Vicarious liability: Concerns liability for subordinates’ actions.\\n\\nC. Therapeutic misadventure: Refers to unavoidable adverse effects despite due care.\\n\\n• Novus actus interveniens: Involves a new independent event breaking causation, not patient noncompliance.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0d125dfc5f","n":104,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 57-year-old man is brought to the emergency department due to lethargy and altered mental status. His family suspects a suicide attempt. Medical history is significant for dilated cardiomyopathy and major depressive disorder. On arrival, blood pressure is 76/46 mm Hg, pulse is 38/min, and respirations are 16/min. Pupils are 3 mm and reactive. Oropharynx is normal. Examination shows bilateral wheezing. There is no peripheral edema. Capillary refill is 3 seconds. There is no diaphoresis. What is the best antidote?","options":["Digibind","Glucagon","Hemodialysis","NaHCO3"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Glucagon\\n\\nThis patient most likely has a β-blocker overdose (eg, propranolol, metoprolol, carvedilol), suggested by:\\n\\n• History of dilated cardiomyopathy → likely prescribed a β-blocker.\\n• Suicide attempt → intentional overdose.\\n• Bradycardia (38/min) and profound hypotension (76/46 mm Hg) → hallmark features.\\n• Bilateral wheezing → β₂-receptor blockade causing bronchospasm.\\n• Normal-sized, reactive pupils and absence of diaphoresis → argues against cholinergic poisoning.\\n\\nWhy Glucagon?\\n\\nGlucagon is the specific antidote for severe β-blocker toxicity.\\n\\nMechanism of action:\\n\\n• Binds to glucagon receptors on cardiac myocytes.\\n• Activates adenylyl cyclase independently of β-adrenergic receptors.\\n• Increases intracellular cAMP, leading to:\\n\\n• ↑ Heart rate (positive chronotropy)\\n• ↑ Myocardial contractility (positive inotropy)\\n\\n• Thus, it bypasses the blocked β-receptors.\\n\\nWhy the other options are incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Digibind (Digoxin immune Fab) ❌\\n\\n• Used for digoxin toxicity.\\n• Digoxin overdose typically presents with nausea, vomiting, visual disturbances (yellow-green vision), hyperkalemia, and characteristic arrhythmias.\\n\\n• C. Hemodialysis ❌\\n\\n• Not an effective treatment for most β-blockers because they are highly protein-bound and have a large volume of distribution.\\n• It is useful for toxins such as lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, and salicylates.\\n\\n• D. Sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃) ❌\\n\\n• Antidote for tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose, which presents with wide QRS complexes, ventricular arrhythmias, hypotension, anticholinergic features (mydriasis, dry skin, urinary retention).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-86180f9666","n":105,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 60-year-old patient with advanced metastatic carcinoma complains of intractable pain unresponsive to oral opioids. He is given a mixture of morphine, cocaine, and brandy in syrup for comfort and improved mood. This preparation is known as:","options":["Paraldehyde mixture","Brompton’s cocktail","Barbiturate elixir","Haloperidol analgesic mix"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Brompton’s cocktail\\n\\nBrompton’s Cocktail = mixture of:\\n\\n• Morphine or diamorphine\\n• Cocaine\\n• Alcohol (brandy)\\n• Chloroform water or syrup","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b8f6c4b13a","n":106,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 62-year-old man is found dead in his bedroom. During the medicolegal examination, the forensic expert records the rectal temperature, ambient temperature, body weight, and notes that the deceased was wearing thick clothing. These parameters are entered into a specialized nomogram. Which of the following is this nomogram primarily used for?","options":["Estimating fetal age","Estimating stature from long bones","Estimating time since death from body temperature","Estimating postmortem interval from rigor mortis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Estimating time since death from body temperature\\n\\nHenssge\'s nomogram is the most widely accepted method for estimating the postmortem interval (PMI) from body cooling (algor mortis).\\n\\nUnlike Newton\'s law of cooling, Henssge\'s method incorporates multiple variables that influence body temperature after death.\\n\\nParameters used\\n\\n• ✔ Rectal temperature\\n• ✔ Ambient temperature\\n• ✔ Body weight\\n• ✔ Correction factors:\\n\\n• Clothing\\n• Wind\\n• Water immersion\\n• Contact surface\\n• Body position\\n\\nThese variables improve the accuracy of PMI estimation, particularly during the first 24 hours after death.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0cdda40b96","n":107,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 68-year-old farmer collapses while working in extreme summer heat. On examination, his temperature is 41.2\xb0C, blood pressure is 80/50 mmHg, pulse rate is 140/min, and he is unconscious. Skin is hot and dry. Which of the following is the most correct statement?","options":["Thermoregulation remains intact and the patient is most likely suffering from heat syncope","The diagnosis requires only elevated body temperature irrespective of CNS findings","Impaired thermoregulation, CNS dysfunction, and absence of sweating","Postmortem examination is classically characterized by Wischnewsky spots in the stomach"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Impaired thermoregulation, CNS dysfunction, and absence of sweating\\n\\nHeat stroke is a medical emergency characterized by:\\n\\n• Core body temperature >40.5\xb0C\\n• CNS dysfunction (confusion, coma, seizures)\\n• Failure of thermoregulation\\n• Classical (non-exertional) heat stroke → Anhidrosis (absence of sweating)\\n\\nExertional heat stroke may still present with profuse sweating.\\n\\nPostmortem caloricity is a recognized autopsy finding.\\n\\nNEET PG\\n\\n• Heat cramps → Sodium loss\\n• Heat syncope → Cerebral hypoperfusion\\n• Heat stroke → CNS dysfunction + Hyperthermia\\n• Exertional heat stroke → Sweating may be present","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7dc9106d53","n":108,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 68-year-old homeless man is found unresponsive in a park during winter. Autopsy reveals a cold, pale body with erosive hemorrhagic lesions on gastric mucosa, especially near the fundus and absence of any significant trauma. Which finding is most supportive of hypothermia as the cause of death?","options":["Washerwoman skin on hands","Purple discoloration of nail beds","Wischnewski spots on gastric mucosa","Dark brown postmortem staining on dependent parts"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Wischnewski spots on gastric mucosa\\n\\n• Winter ; Cold, pale body\\n• Erosive hemorrhagic lesions in gastric mucosa (especially fundus)\\n• No significant trauma\\n\\nFeatures suggestive of - Hypothermia.\\n\\n❌Option A. Washerwoman skin on hands\\n\\n• Can be seen with prolonged exposure to moisture (like drowning).\\n• Not specific for hypothermia.\\n\\n❌Option B. Purple discoloration of nail beds\\n\\n• That’s cyanosis. Very nonspecific.\\n• Can occur in many causes of death.\\n\\n✅ Option C. Wischnewski spots on gastric mucosa\\n\\nWischnewski spots are:\\n\\n• Dark brown/black hemorrhagic erosions in gastric mucosa\\n• Classically associated with hypothermia\\n• Mechanism : due to stress-induced hemorrhage + acid digestion of blood\\n\\n❌Option D. Dark brown postmortem staining on dependent parts\\n\\nThat’s livor mortis. Completely nonspecific.\\n\\nFinal Answer:C — Wischnewski spots on gastric mucosa.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-54666903c6","n":109,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 68-year-old man suffers severe hypoxic brain injury following a prolonged cardiac arrest and is diagnosed with a persistent vegetative state (PVS). He has no advance directive (living will) and is unable to express his wishes. He remains dependent on mechanical ventilation and artificial life support. After extensive discussions, the treating team and the legally authorized family members decide to withdraw life-sustaining treatment. This scenario is best classified as which of the following?","options":["Voluntary passive euthanasia","Involuntary passive euthanasia","Non-voluntary passive euthanasia","Involuntary active euthanasia"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Non-voluntary passive euthanasia\\n\\nThe patient is incapable of making or communicating a decision regarding continuation or withdrawal of treatment because he is in a persistent vegetative state and has no living will.\\n\\nThe decision to withdraw life support is therefore made by the legally authorized surrogate/family (and, depending on jurisdiction, the treating team and court), making it non-voluntary.\\n\\nSince the intervention involves withholding or withdrawing life-sustaining treatment, it is passive euthanasia.\\n\\nThus, the scenario represents: ✅ Non-voluntary passive euthanasia\\n\\nClassification of euthanasia\\n\\n1. Voluntary euthanasia\\n\\n• Patient competently requests euthanasia.\\n• Decision is made by the patient.\\n\\n2. Non-voluntary euthanasia\\n\\n• Patient cannot express a choice (e.g., coma, PVS, severe dementia, neonates).\\n• Decision is made by surrogates or legal authorities.\\n\\n3. Involuntary euthanasia\\n\\n• Performed against the patient\'s expressed wishes.\\n• Ethically and legally unacceptable.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c08345f158","n":110,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 69-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he was found by police trying to break into the back office of a grocery store, claiming that he worked there and that he had left his keys in the office. The owner of the grocery denied that the patient was employed. The patient\'s medical problems include hypertension, hyperlipidemia, major depressive disorder, alcohol use disorder, and type 2 diabetes mellitus. The patient answers questions without difficuity but provides conflicting information to different examiners. He is unable to account for how he ended up in the grocery store; however, he does provide detailed accounts of his previous job as an English professor, even reciting Shakespeare\'s Macbeth at length. He does not appear troubled by his inability to recall events accurately. The remainder of the examination is within normal limits. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this patient\'s symptoms?","options":["Alzheimer disease","Dissociative amnesia","Korsakoff syndrome","Marchiafava-Bignami disease"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Korsakoff syndrome\\n\\nThis patient has Korsakoff syndrome, a chronic neuropsychiatric condition due to thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, most commonly from long-standing alcohol use disorder. Hallmark features include anterograde and retrograde amnesia, confabulation (providing fabricated or inconsistent stories), and lack of insight or distress about memory gaps. Preservation of remote memories (eg, detailed recall of his past as an English professor) with inability to recall recent events is classic.\\n\\nA. Alzheimer disease: Causes progressive memory loss with impaired insight and does not feature confabulation early.\\n\\nB. Dissociative amnesia: Typically follows psychological trauma and lacks confabulation.\\n\\nD. Marchiafava-Bignami disease: Causes corpus callosum demyelination with severe neurologic deficits, not isolated memory disturbance.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-46605dd1f5","n":111,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 72-year-old alcoholic is found unconscious outdoors during winter. On arrival, his core body temperature is 29\xb0C. During examination, he has stopped shivering, deep tendon reflexes are depressed. All of the following statements regarding this patient are correct except:","options":["The patient has moderate hypothermia","Shivering is expected to disappear at this stage","Hide and die syndrome is seen at this stage","Rewarming is the cornerstone of management"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Hide and die syndrome is seen at this stage\\n\\nHypothermia is defined as a core body temperature <35\xb0C.\\n\\n• Mild: 35–32\xb0C\\n• Moderate: 32–28\xb0C → Shivering stops, reflexes diminish, J (Osborn) waves appear\\n• Severe: <28\xb0C → Paradoxical undressing and hide-and-die syndrome may occur.\\n\\nTreatment consists of active/passive rewarming, depending on severity.\\n\\nHigh-Yield:\\n\\n• Moderate hypothermia → Osborn (J) wave\\n• Severe hypothermia → Paradoxical undressing\\n• Treatment → Rewarming, not cooling","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-3f3a827057","n":112,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A blood-stained shirt recovered from a crime scene is sent to the forensic laboratory. Preliminary tests confirm that the stain is blood. The investigating officer now wants to determine whether the blood is of human origin or from another animal species. Which of the following tests is most appropriate?","options":["Teichmann test","Takayama test","Precipitin test","Florence test"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Precipitin test\\n\\nThe Precipitin test is a species-specific serological test used to determine the species origin of blood.\\n\\nIt is based on an antigen-antibody reaction:\\n\\n• Human blood proteins act as antigens.\\n• They react with anti-human serum.\\n• Formation of a precipitate confirms that the blood is human.\\n\\nWhy the other options are incorrect?\\n\\nA. Teichmann test ❌\\n\\n• Confirmatory test for blood\\n• Detects hemin crystals\\n• Does not determine species origin.\\n\\nB. Takayama test ❌\\n\\n• Confirmatory test for blood\\n• Detects hemochromogen crystals\\n• Does not identify species.\\n\\nD. Florence test ❌\\n\\n• Used to detect semen\\n• Demonstrates choline crystals\\n• Not a blood test.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d079f1f2f8","n":113,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A body in extreme cold shows rigor mortis starting only after 6 hours and lasting 48 hours. This pattern suggests:","options":["Delayed onset, prolonged duration","Early onset, short duration","Early onset, prolonged duration","Delayed onset, short duration"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Delayed onset, prolonged duration\\n\\nLow temperature slows biochemical reactions: onset of rigor is delayed and, once established, the duration is prolonged (decomposition/putrefaction also delayed). So rigor appears later and persists longer than normal.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-93c5a1cff7","n":114,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A body is found suspended from a tree. External examination reveals an oblique, incomplete ligature mark above the thyroid cartilage with a distinct suspension gap. Internal examination demonstrates an intimal tear of the carotid artery. Which additional finding would most strongly support the same diagnosis?","options":["Jefferson’s fracture","Hangman\'s fracture","Complete transverse ligature mark below thyroid cartilage","Extensive soft tissue contusions around the neck"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hangman\'s fracture\\n\\nThe external findings are classical of hanging—an oblique, incomplete ligature mark above the thyroid cartilage with a suspension gap. The Amussat sign (intimal tear of the carotid artery) further supports hanging. In judicial (long-drop) hanging, sudden hyperextension of the neck may produce a Hangman\'s fracture (fracture of the C2 vertebra). The remaining options are more characteristic of manual or ligature strangulation.\\n\\n| Hanging | Strangulation\\n\\n| ------- | -------------\\n\\n| Oblique ligature mark | Transverse ligature mark\\n\\n| Above thyroid | Below thyroid\\n\\n| Suspension gap | Complete encirclement\\n\\n| Amussat sign | Soft tissue contusions","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c24fae354e","n":115,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A body is removed from a lake. On autopsy, liquid identical to the drowning medium (lake water with algae) is found in the sphenoid sinus. There are no skull base fractures. This autopsy feature is termed:","options":["Sign of Cullen","Sign of Sveshnikov","Battle’s sign","Buschke’s sign"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sign of Sveshnikov\\n\\n• Body removed from lake ; Liquid identical to drowning medium (lake water with algae)\\n• Found in sphenoid sinus ; No skull base fracture\\n\\nSo water has entered the sphenoid sinus during drowning, not due to trauma. ✔️ Sign of Sveshnikov\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nCullen’s Sign\\n\\n• Bluish discoloration around the umbilicus\\n• Seen in acute pancreatitis, ruptured ectopic pregnancy, intra-abdominal hemorrhage.\\n\\nBattle’s sign\\n\\n• Ecchymosis over mastoid region (behind ear)\\n• It indicates: Basilar skull fracture, especially middle cranial fossa fracture\\n\\nBushke\'s sign\\n\\n• Buschke-Ollendorff sign is a clinical finding used in dermatology to diagnose secondary syphilis, characterized by deep dermal tenderness when a papular lesion is pressed, often with a dull probe or pinhead\\n\\n✔️ Sign of Sveshnikov = Drowning medium in sphenoid sinus without skull base fracture.\\n\\nThis supports antemortem drowning because water is actively inhaled and forced into sinuses during respiration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a070a7638f","n":116,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A boy collapses instantly after jumping into cold water. Autopsy after 8 hours shows rigor mortis fully developed. What does the rigor pattern indicate?","options":["Rigor mortis confirms death occurred before immersion","Fully developed rigor proves prolonged agony before death.","Rigor mortis is influenced primarily by postmortem interval and environmental conditions rather than the cause of death","Fully developed rigor confirms secondary drowning."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Rigor mortis is influenced primarily by postmortem interval and environmental conditions rather than the cause of death\\n\\nRigor mortis is the postmortem stiffening of muscles caused by depletion of ATP, which prevents the separation of actin and myosin filaments after death.\\n\\nThe development of rigor mortis depends mainly on:\\n\\n• Time since death (postmortem interval)\\n• Environmental temperature\\n\\n• Warm temperatures → accelerate rigor.\\n• Cold temperatures → delay the onset and prolong its duration.\\n\\n• Premortem muscular activity\\n\\n• Convulsions, strenuous exercise, or violent struggle deplete ATP before death and may cause earlier onset of rigor.\\n\\nImportantly, the cause of death itself (e.g., drowning, hanging, poisoning) does not produce a characteristic rigor mortis pattern. Therefore, finding fully developed rigor 8 hours after death does not establish drowning, secondary drowning, or whether the victim died before entering the water.\\n\\nWhy the other options are incorrect\\n\\nA. Rigor mortis confirms death occurred before immersion. — Incorrect\\n\\n• Rigor mortis cannot determine whether death occurred before or after immersion.\\n• This distinction is made using other forensic findings, such as evidence of drowning (e.g., frothy fluid in airways, diatoms, water in the stomach, washerwoman changes), not rigor.\\n\\nB. Fully developed rigor proves prolonged agony before death. — Incorrect\\n\\n• Although prolonged muscular activity may hasten rigor by depleting ATP, fully developed rigor alone does not prove prolonged agony.\\n• It is influenced by multiple factors, especially time since death and ambient temperature.\\n\\nD. Fully developed rigor confirms secondary drowning. — Incorrect\\n\\n• Secondary drowning (more accurately termed delayed pulmonary edema after submersion) is a clinical diagnosis in survivors, not a postmortem diagnosis based on rigor mortis.\\n• Rigor mortis has no role in diagnosing secondary drowning.\\n\\nHigh-yield forensic points\\n\\n• Onset of rigor mortis: Usually 1–2 hours after death.\\n• Fully developed: Around 6–12 hours.\\n• Persists: Approximately 18–36 hours, then disappears as decomposition begins.\\n• Cold weather: Delays onset and prolongs duration.\\n• Warm weather: Accelerates onset and resolution.\\n• Strenuous activity/convulsions before death: May lead to earlier onset of rigor due to rapid ATP depletion.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c5e42f6172","n":117,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A boy who is accused of rape has been brought for medicolegal examination. He was claiming to be 16 years but was suspected to be 18 years. Which X-ray will be done to confirm?","options":["Elbow and Ankle Joint","Wrist and Knee Joint","Head and Shoulder Joint","Elbow and Hip Joint"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Wrist and Knee Joint\\n\\nFor age estimation in adolescents around 16–18 years, X-ray examination of wrist and knee joints is most useful. The wrist (distal radius and ulna) helps assess fusion of epiphyses, which usually completes around 17–18 years in boys. The knee joint (lower end of femur, upper end of tibia) also shows characteristic epiphyseal fusion patterns occurring between 16–18 years, making this combination reliable to differentiate between 16 and 18 years.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nA. Elbow and ankle joint: Elbow fuses earlier; ankle is less precise in this age group.\\n\\nC. Head and shoulder joint: Skull sutures are unreliable for this age.\\n\\nD. Elbow and hip joint: Hip fusion is later and less discriminatory for 16–18 years.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8dd5384bea","n":118,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A child who is a victim under POCSO act is brought to the department of forensic medicine for age estimation. The X-ray image of the hand is shown below. What is the likely age of the child?","options":["4 years","9 years","17 years","13 years"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 13 years\\n\\nAt around 13 years, a hand–wrist X-ray typically shows all carpal bones ossified (including pisiform) with well-formed epiphyses of the phalanges/metacarpals and early/ongoing fusion in some phalanges, but distal radius/ulna epiphyses are not yet fully fused (fusion at 18-19 years). That combination fits >12yrs but <18-19 years.\\n\\nIn addition, epiphyses of first metacarpal is not fused (fuses at 16 years) so the range is further narrowed down to 12-15 years.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-cb4f1f512c","n":119,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A deceased individual is found with an abrasion over the forehead. The wound is pale yellow, dry, parchment-like, and located over a bony prominence. Histopathological examination shows no inflammatory reaction. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?","options":["Fresh antemortem abrasion","Antemortem abrasion approximately 12 hours old","Postmortem abrasion","Graze abrasion caused by indirect direct force"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Postmortem abrasion\\n\\nPostmortem abrasions typically occur over bony prominences due to dragging or handling of the body after death. They appear pale yellow, dry, parchment-like, and lack vital reaction, differentiating them from antemortem injuries.\\n\\nAntemortem abrasions are reddish, may ooze serum, and demonstrate vital inflammatory changes microscopically.\\n\\nAntemortem vs Postmortem Abrasion\\n\\n• Antemortem: Anywhere on body, red, vital reaction present.\\n• Postmortem: Bony prominences, pale/yellow, parchment-like, no vital reaction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f4b1df2578","n":120,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A doctor used the same needle used in a patient with HIV to inject in another patient. The latter patient on testing found to be infected with HIV. The doctor is punishable for this negligence according to which BNS (Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita)?","options":["271","272","273","274"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 271\\n\\nUnder the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), Section 271 deals with a negligent act likely to spread infection dangerous to life. In this case, the doctor reused a needle from an HIV-positive patient, clearly amounting to negligence(lack of due care) that resulted in transmission of a life-threatening infection. Hence, punishment is attracted under Section 271.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nB. 272: Applies to malignant (intentional) acts done with knowledge to spread infection, not negligence.\\n\\nC. 273: Relates to disobedience of quarantine rules, not applicable here.\\n\\nD. 274: Deals with adulteration of food or drink intended for sale\\n\\nThus, Section 271 is the appropriate provision.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-253f8b24b0","n":121,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A fetus delivered after prolonged intrauterine death shows overlapping cranial bones on radiography. The skin is peeling with reddish discoloration. Which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":["The condition represents aseptic autolysis.","The radiological finding is known as Spalding sign and usually appears 4–7 days after fetal death.","The earliest external sign is skin slippage occurring as early as 12 hours.","Gas in the great vessels (Robert\'s sign) usually appears after Spalding sign."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Gas in the great vessels (Robert\'s sign) usually appears after Spalding sign.\\n\\nThis fetus has maceration, indicating intrauterine death.\\n\\n• Earliest external sign → reddening and skin slippage (\\\\~12 hours)\\n• Robert\'s sign (gas in great vessels) → as early as 12 hours\\n• Spalding sign (overlapping skull bones) → 4–7 days\\n\\nThus Robert\'s sign precedes Spalding sign.\\n\\n| Sign | Time\\n\\n| ---- | ----\\n\\n| Skin slippage | \\\\~12 h\\n\\n| Robert\'s sign | \\\\~12 h\\n\\n| Ball\'s sign | Early\\n\\n| Spalding sign | 4–7 days","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-73c95e7457","n":122,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A forensic expert is assessing the accuracy of different skeletal elements for sex determination according to Krogman\'s criteria. All of the following statements are correct, except:","options":["Pelvis alone determines sex with approximately 95% accuracy","Skull alone has approximately 90% accuracy","Pelvis combined with a long bone provides approximately 98% accuracy","Femur alone determines sex with approximately 95% accuracy"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Femur alone determines sex with approximately 95% accuracy\\n\\nAccording to Krogman\'s accuracy, the pelvis is the single most accurate bone for sex determination (≈95%). The skull alone has an accuracy of about 90%, while long bones (including the femur) have a lower accuracy of ≈80%. Combining the pelvis with either the skull or a long bone increases the accuracy to ≈98%, and a complete skeleton provides nearly 100% accuracy.\\n\\n| Skeletal Material | Accuracy\\n\\n| ----------------- | --------\\n\\n| Pelvis | 95%\\n\\n| Skull | 90%\\n\\n| Long bones (Femur) | 80%\\n\\n| Pelvis + Skull | 98%\\n\\n| Pelvis + Long bone | 98%\\n\\n| Complete skeleton | 100%","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-557ca7bb64","n":123,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A forensic expert performs Gettler\'s test in a suspected drowning case. Chloride concentration in the left side of the heart is significantly lower than that in the right side. Which sequence of events best explains this finding?","options":["Salt-water aspiration → Hemoconcentration → Increased left-sided chloride","Fresh-water aspiration → Hemodilution → Decreased left-sided chloride","Fresh-water aspiration → Pulmonary edema → Increased chloride","Dry drowning → Vocal cord spasm → Equal chloride concentration"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Fresh-water aspiration → Hemodilution → Decreased left-sided chloride\\n\\nIn fresh-water drowning, hypotonic water enters the pulmonary circulation, causing hemodilution. Consequently, blood reaching the left side of the heart has a lower chloride concentration than the right side. In contrast, salt-water drowning causes hemoconcentration with increased left-sided chloride. This principle forms the basis of Gettler\'s test, although the test is not useful in dry drowning or hydrocution.\\n\\n| Type of drowning | Left heart chloride\\n\\n| ---------------- | -------------------\\n\\n| Fresh water | Decreased\\n\\n| Salt water | Increased\\n\\n| Dry drowning | Not useful\\n\\n| Hydrocution | Not useful","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-90cb6465df","n":124,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A forensic expert receives only a skull for examination. The cephalic index is calculated as 83. Which combination of race classification and skull type is most appropriate?","options":["Dolichocephalic – Africans","Mesaticephalic – Indians","Brachycephalic – Japanese","Mesaticephalic – Europeans"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Brachycephalic – Japanese\\n\\nThe cephalic index = maximum skull breadth \xd7 100 / maximum skull length. A value of 83 falls in the 80–85 range, indicating a brachycephalic skull, classically associated with the Japanese.\\n\\n| Cephalic Index | Skull Type | Classical Association\\n\\n| -------------- | ---------- | ---------------------\\n\\n| 70–75 | Dolichocephalic | Aryans, Africans\\n\\n| 75–80 | Mesaticephalic | Indians, Chinese, Europeans\\n\\n| 80–85 | Brachycephalic | Japanese","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8d211074ba","n":125,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A forensic pathologist is examining a venous specimen. Microscopy reveals laminations with alternating light and dark bands as shown below. What can be commented about the clot?","options":["Antemortem clot formation","Postmortem clot formation","Clot age cannot be determined","Refer to a specialised forensic pathologist"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Antemortem clot formation\\n\\nThe described laminations with alternating light and dark bands represent Lines of Zahn, which are characteristic of antemortem thrombi. These lines are formed due to blood flow during life, with pale layers of platelets and fibrin alternating with darker layers rich in red blood cells. Their presence confirms that the clot formed while circulation was active, making it antemortem in origin.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\n• Postmortem clot formation: Postmortem clots are gelatinous, non-adherent, and lack Lines of Zahn.\\n• Clot age cannot be determined: While exact age cannot be calculated, timing (antemortem vs postmortem) can be determined here.\\n• Refer to a specialised forensic pathologist: The finding is classic and does not require further referral.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-4363e8af69","n":126,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A hair strand is recovered from the hoodie of a suspect involved in a hit-and-run case. On microscopic examination, the medulla occupies less than one-third of the hair diameter, with thin, fragmented segments. The cortex is well developed, and the cuticle shows imbricate scales. Which of the following is the most likely identification?","options":["Guard hair of a dog","Cat hair","Human scalp hair","Wool fiber of sheep"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Human scalp hair\\n\\nThe described features are classic for human scalp hair. In humans, the medullary index is < 1/3 of the hair diameter, and the medulla—if present—is often thin, fragmented, or discontinuous. The cortex is well developed, and the cuticle shows imbricate scales, which is typical of human hair. These findings are commonly used in forensic comparison.\\n\\nA. Guard hair of a dog: Has a thick, continuous medulla with a high medullary index (>1/2).\\n\\nB. Cat hair: Shows a very wide, continuous medulla with characteristic banding.\\n\\nC. Wool fiber (sheep): Has coronal scales, not imbricate, and a different fiber structure.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-24aad6002b","n":127,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A journalist publishes a news article naming a 24-year-old woman who was the victim of gang rape, without her written consent, while the trial is ongoing. Under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) 2023, which section is attracted, and what is the maximum punishment?","options":["Section 72 — imprisonment up to 2 years and fine","Section 73 — imprisonment up to 3 years only","Section 228A — imprisonment up to 2 years and fine","Section 64 — imprisonment up to 7 years and fine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Section 72 — imprisonment up to 2 years and fine\\n\\nDistractor analysis:\\n\\n| Option | Why right/wrong\\n\\n| ------ | ---------------\\n\\n| A. Section 72 — imprisonment up to 2 years and fine ✅ | BNS Section 72 — disclosure of identity of victim of offences under Sections 64–71 (rape and allied offences). Punishment: imprisonment of either description up to 2 years AND fine\\n\\n| B. Section 73 — imprisonment up to 3 years only ❌ | Section 73 is the related but distinct offence — printing/publishing matter relating to court proceedings of such cases without court permission (also 2 yrs + fine, not 3 yrs)\\n\\n| C. Section 228A — imprisonment up to 2 years and fine❌ | 228A is the repealed IPC section — classic exam trap. Correct concept, wrong statute post–1 July 2024\\n\\n| D. Section 64 — imprisonment up to 7 years and fine❌ | Section 64 is punishment for rape itself (min 10 yrs, not 7 — old IPC 376 minimum) — tests whether the student confuses the substantive offence with the disclosure offence","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2b0ebefe28","n":128,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A Juvenile Justice Board consists of:","options":["A Sessions Judge only","Principal Magistrate + 2 social workers","Child Welfare Committee","Police Inspector only"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Principal Magistrate + 2 social workers\\n\\nUnder the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, a Juvenile Justice Board (JJB) is constituted to deal with children in conflict with law. It consists of a Principal Magistrate (a Judicial Magistrate of First Class) and two social workers, of whom at least one must be a woman. This composition ensures a balance of legal expertise and child-centric, rehabilitative approach.\\n\\nA: Sessions Judge alone does not constitute JJB\\n\\nC: Child Welfare Committee is a separate body for children in need of care and protection.\\n\\nD: Police Inspector has no role in JJB composition.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-019c37e78e","n":129,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A man is brought after being shot with a shotgun. The firing distance is most consistent with:","options":["Contact shot","Close shot (&lt;1 m)","Near shot (1–2 m)","Intermediate shot (2–4 m)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Near shot (1–2 m)\\n\\nThe rat-hole appearance is a classic feature of a near shotgun wound (1–2 m).\\n\\nAt this distance:\\n\\n• Pellets have not yet dispersed\\n• Single central defect persists\\n• Burning, blackening and tattooing are absent\\n• Satellite pellet holes have not yet appeared\\n\\nHigh-Yield:\\n\\n| Distance | Characteristic finding\\n\\n| -------- | ----------------------\\n\\n| Contact | Stellate wound over bony prominence\\n\\n| <1 m | Burning + blackening + tattooing\\n\\n| 1–2 m | Rat-hole appearance\\n\\n| 2–4 m | Satellite pellet holes begin\\n\\n| >4 m | Complete pellet dispersion","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-365be3631b","n":130,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A man is brought dead with two gunshot wounds. The forensic resident notes that one wound has a clear grease collar. The senior asks him to identify entry vs exit wound based on this finding. Which of the following statements is true regarding the reliability of a grease collar in differentiating wounds?","options":["Grease collar is present in both entry and exit wounds","Grease collar is the most reliable sign of a close-range shot","Grease collar is seen only in entry wounds","Grease collar indicates a contact shot"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Grease collar is seen only in entry wounds\\n\\nA grease (wipe) collar is formed when grease, dirt, and residues on the bullet surface are wiped off as it enters the skin, producing a dark ring around the entry wound. This occurs due to friction between the bullet and skin and is considered a reliable marker of an entry wound, irrespective of firing distance. It helps differentiate entry from exit wounds, especially when other features are ambiguous.\\n\\nA: Exit wounds lack a grease collar because the bullet exits cleanly.\\n\\nB: Grease collar does not indicate firing range.\\n\\nD: Contact shots are identified by muzzle imprint and soot, not grease collar.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c90baae7ef","n":131,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A man is convicted for murder solely based on CCTV footage showing him assaulting the victim, corroborated by an eyewitness account. Which of the following best describes the nature of the evidence?","options":["CCTV footage is circumstantial evidence, whereas eyewitness testimony is hearsay evidence.","Both CCTV footage and eyewitness testimony constitute direct evidence.","CCTV footage is documentary evidence only and cannot be treated as direct evidence.","Eyewitness testimony is indirect evidence because human memory is fallible."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Both CCTV footage and eyewitness testimony constitute direct evidence.\\n\\nEvidence directly proving the occurrence of the event without requiring inference is direct evidence.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n| Evidence | Classification\\n\\n| -------- | --------------\\n\\n| Eyewitness | Direct evidence\\n\\n| CCTV recording | Direct electronic evidence\\n\\n| DNA | Circumstantial evidence\\n\\n| Fingerprints | Circumstantial evidence\\n\\n| Blood group matching | Circumstantial evidence","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f5581e2c76","n":132,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A man running a 40-kilometre marathon on a hot summer afternoon suddenly collapsed before completing his race. On examination, his body temperature was 102 degrees and was profusely sweating. His pupils seem to be dilated. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Heat exhaustion","Classical Heat stroke","Heat cramps","Exertional heat stroke"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Heat exhaustion\\n\\nHeat exhaustion occurs due to prolonged physical exertion in a hot environment, leading to dehydration and salt loss. The key distinguishing features are profuse sweating, moderately elevated body temperature (usually < 104\xb0F / 40\xb0C), weakness, and collapse. In this case, the runner collapsed during a marathon in hot weather, had a temperature of 102\xb0F, and was profusely sweating, which strongly favors heat exhaustion rather than heat stroke.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nB. Classical heat stroke: Occurs in elderly or infirm, with hot dry skin and very high temperature (>104\xb0F).\\n\\nC. Exertional heat stroke: Has CNS dysfunction and hot skin; sweating may be absent.\\n\\nD. Heat cramps: Present with painful muscle cramps without collapse or significant hyperthermia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4657009a09","n":133,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A patient develops vertebral fractures during ECT and sues the psychiatrist. The psychiatrist had used general anesthesia and a muscle relaxant (succinylcholine) and followed hospital protocols. Which legal principle protects him?","options":["Res ipsa loquitur","Bolam test","Bolitho modification","Montgomery rule"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bolam test\\n\\nThe Bolam test (Bolam v. Friern Hospital Management Committee, 1957) holds that a doctor is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion — even if others would have done differently.\\n\\nHere the psychiatrist:\\n\\n• Used general anaesthesia + succinylcholine (the standard modification to prevent ECT convulsion-related fractures),\\n• Followed hospital protocols → conformed to accepted professional practice.\\n\\nVertebral fracture is a known, recognised complication of ECT that can occur despite proper muscle relaxation. Since he adhered to the accepted standard of care, Bolam shields him from negligence liability.\\n\\nWhy the others are wrong\\n\\n| Option | Principle | Why it doesn\'t apply here\\n\\n| ------ | --------- | -------------------------\\n\\n| A | Res ipsa loquitur (\\"the thing speaks for itself\\") | This would help the patient (plaintiff), not protect the doctor. It shifts the burden onto the doctor when harm ordinarily doesn\'t occur without negligence. But here proper care was taken and the complication is recognised — so res ipsa doesn\'t attach.\\n\\n| B ✓ | Bolam test | ✅ Protects a doctor who followed accepted responsible practice\\n\\n| C | Bolitho modification | Refines/limits Bolam — the accepted practice must also be logical and defensible to a judge. It narrows the doctor\'s protection, so it\'s not the protecting principle; it\'s the check on it.\\n\\n| D | Montgomery rule | Concerns informed consent / disclosure of material risks (patient-centred). Relevant to consent, not to standard of technical care — not the issue here.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d667814f1d","n":134,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A skull is recovered from a partially decomposed body. During forensic examination, the coroner wants to estimate the age based on skull sutures. Which suture provides the most reliable estimate of age in a skull forensically?","options":["2","3","4","1"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1\\n\\nMost reliable suture for age estimation (skull):\xa0Sagittal suture\\n\\n• Starts closing earliest\\n• Shows predictable pattern of endocranial → ectocranial fusion\\n• Used as the primary suture for age estimation in adult skulls\\n\\nSkull sutures — age of closure\\n\\nMajor sutures\\n\\n• Sagittal/ coronal suture\\n\\n• Begins closing: 25 years\\n• Complete: 40 years\\n\\n• Lambdoid suture\\n\\n• Begins: 30 years\\n• Complete: 45 years\\n\\nImportant exam points\\n\\n• First to close: Metopic suture\\n• Last to close: Lambdoid suture\\n• Closure is endocranial → ectocranial","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-7fc2cce258","n":135,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A skull shows: • Mesaticephalic cephalic index • Prominent brow ridges • Narrow nasal aperture • Sharp nasal sill • High bridge nose The race is:","options":["Mongoloid","Negroid","Caucasoid","Australoid"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Caucasoid\\n\\nThe skull features described are characteristic of the Caucasoid race. A mesaticephalic skull, prominent brow ridges, narrow nasal aperture, sharp nasal sill, and a high nasal bridge are classic anthropological markers of Caucasoid skulls. The sharp nasal sill and narrow nasal opening are especially important discriminators, as they reflect well-developed nasal bones adapted to colder climates.\\n\\nA. Mongoloid: Typically shows a rounder skull, flatter face, and less prominent nasal bridge.\\n\\nB. Negroid: Has a broad nasal aperture with a guttered nasal sill and prognathism.\\n\\nD. Australoid: Shows robust features with wide nasal aperture and less sharp nasal sill.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c77e8dc6f9","n":136,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A surgeon obtains a written informed consent from a patient for elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy. During surgery, an unexpected bleeding hepatic artery aneurysm is discovered that cannot be controlled laparoscopically and poses an immediate threat to the patient\'s life. The surgeon converts to an open procedure and repairs the aneurysm without obtaining fresh consent. Which of the following best justifies the surgeon\'s action?","options":["Blanket (open) consent obtained at admission permits any additional surgical procedure deemed necessary","Doctrine of extended consent permits any additional procedure the surgeon considers beneficial","The surgeon is protected under the emergency doctrine because the additional procedure was immediately necessary to save life","The doctrine of loco parentis authorizes the surgeon to act in the patient\'s best interest"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. The surgeon is protected under the emergency doctrine because the additional procedure was immediately necessary to save life\\n\\nIn India, blanket (open) consent and the doctrine of extended consent are not legally valid for performing unrelated additional procedures. As a general rule, specific informed consent must be obtained for each planned intervention.\\n\\nHowever, when an unexpected life-threatening condition is encountered during a procedure and obtaining fresh consent is impractical because delay would jeopardize the patient\'s life, the surgeon may proceed under the Emergency Doctrine. This doctrine allows additional life-saving procedures only, and does not extend to non-essential or elective interventions.\\n\\nThe doctrine of loco parentis applies to teachers or persons temporarily acting in place of parents for minors and has no relevance in this scenario.\\n\\nHigh-Yield Points\\n\\n• Written informed consent is the gold standard.\\n• Blanket/Open consent: Invalid in India.\\n• Extended consent: Not recognized in India except for unexpected life-saving procedures.\\n• Emergency doctrine: Permits treatment without fresh consent only to save life or prevent serious irreversible harm.\\n• Loco parentis: Applies to teachers/guardians temporarily acting for minors, not to treating surgeons.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8ae1ff67b7","n":137,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A surgeon performs a standard procedure that results in a recognized complication despite following accepted guidelines and exercising reasonable skill. Another equally competent surgeon states that he would have used a different technique. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?","options":["The surgeon is negligent because a safer alternative technique existed.","The surgeon is criminally negligent because the complication was preventable.","The surgeon is not negligent if the chosen method is accepted by a responsible body of similarly skilled practitioners.","The surgeon is liable because the outcome, rather than the standard of care, determines negligence."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. The surgeon is not negligent if the chosen method is accepted by a responsible body of similarly skilled practitioners.\\n\\nThe Bolam principle (1957) states that a doctor is not negligent merely because another doctor would have adopted a different approach. The standard is whether the conduct is supported by a responsible body of similarly skilled medical professionals. A recognized complication despite reasonable care does not amount to negligence.\\n\\nEssential Components of Medical Negligence\\n\\n| Essential Element | Meaning\\n\\n| ----------------- | -------\\n\\n| Duty of care | Doctor-patient relationship exists\\n\\n| Breach (Dereliction) | Failure to exercise reasonable skill and care\\n\\n| Direct causation | Breach directly causes injury\\n\\n| Damage | Patient suffers harm\\n\\nTypes of Medical Negligence\\n\\n| Feature | Civil Negligence | Criminal Negligence | Contributory Negligence\\n\\n| ------- | ---------------- | ------------------- | -----------------------\\n\\n| Degree | Lack of reasonable care | Gross/wanton negligence | Negligence by both doctor and patient\\n\\n| Court | Civil/Consumer Court | Criminal Court | Civil Court\\n\\n| Burden of proof | Plaintiff | Prosecution | Doctor (to establish patient\'s contribution)\\n\\n| Punishment | Compensation | Imprisonment/Fine | Reduction in compensation\\n\\n| Example | Wrong prescription | Surgery on wrong limb | Patient ignores follow-up advice","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a65a58b88c","n":138,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A suspected encounter case is being investigated. The crime scene reveals multiple entry wounds over the victim’s chest with a distinct trail of burnt-orange residue around one entry wound. Autopsy shows that this particular projectile has traveled much deeper and straighter through tissues compared to the others, with minimal deformation and evidence of internal burning along the track. Which of the following best explains the ballistic behavior of this projectile?","options":["It was a tracer bullet, which allows continued velocity and visible trajectory after impact","It was a dum-dum bullet that expands rapidly and slows significantly on tissue impact","It was a hollow-point bullet designed to fragment and reduce penetration depth","It was a soft-point bullet intended to mushroom inside tissue causing early energy loss"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. It was a tracer bullet, which allows continued velocity and visible trajectory after impact\\n\\n• Burnt-orange residue around entry wound\\n• Internal burning along wound track --> Deep, straight penetration ; Minimal deformation\\n\\nWhy A is correct (Tracer bullet)\\n\\nTracer bullets contain a pyrotechnic compound at the base.\\n\\nWhen fired, it burns brightly to show the bullet’s path.\\n\\n• Leaves burnt/orange residue\\n• Can cause burning along the wound track\\n• Does NOT deform much --> Travels deep and straight\\n\\n❌ Option B – Dum-dum bullet\\n\\n• Expands rapidly ; Causing severe tissue destruction.\\n• Slows down quickly\\n• Does NOT travel deep and straight\\n• No burning effect\\n\\n❌ Option C – Hollow-point bullet\\n\\n• Designed to expand\\n• Fragments or mushrooms\\n• Reduces penetration depth\\n• Causes large cavity, not straight deep path\\n• No burning property\\n\\n❌ Option D – Soft-point bullet\\n\\n• Expands inside tissue ; Causes early energy release.\\n• No minimal deformation ; No internal burning.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-59703efb3e","n":139,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A terminally ill patient asks the doctor to stop feeding tubes and allow natural death. This practice is called:","options":["Dysthanasia","Orthotanasia","Cacotanasia","Active euthanasia"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Orthotanasia\\n\\nOrthotanasia refers to allowing a patient to die naturally by withholding or withdrawing life-prolonging measures(eg, feeding tubes, ventilatory support) when treatment is futile, at the patient’s informed request. The intent is not to cause death but to avoid unnecessary suffering and respect patient autonomy. This is ethically and legally accepted in many jurisdictions.\\n\\nA. Dysthanasia: Prolongs the dying process using aggressive, futile treatments, increasing suffering.\\n\\nB. Cacotanasia: Involves non-voluntary or involuntary euthanasia, ethically unacceptable\\n\\nC. Active euthanasia: Entails actively causing death (eg, lethal injection), which is legally prohibited in most countries.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c156179a49","n":140,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A video shows a middle-aged factory worker attempting to hold his hands outstretched. The neurological finding demonstrated in the video is classically associated with chronic exposure to which of the following toxic substances?","options":["Lead","Mercury","Arsenic","Manganese"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Mercury\\n\\nThe video demonstrates Danbury tremor (Hatter\'s shakes), a characteristic neurological manifestation of chronic mercury poisoning. It is an intention/postural tremor that typically:\\n\\n• Begins in the hands\\n• Progresses to involve the lips, tongue, arms, and legs\\n• Is moderately coarse and interrupted by jerky (myoclonic) movements\\n• Eventually interferes with fine motor tasks such as writing, shaving, eating, and dressing.\\n\\nThe term \\"Danbury shakes\\" originated from hat factory workers in Danbury, Connecticut, where chronic inhalation of mercury vapor during felt hat manufacturing caused this distinctive syndrome. It gave rise to the famous phrase \\"mad as a hatter.\\"\\n\\nWhy the other options are incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Lead: Produces peripheral neuropathy (eg, wrist drop), abdominal colic, anemia, and encephalopathy rather than the characteristic Danbury tremor.\\n• C. Arsenic: Causes painful sensorimotor neuropathy, gastrointestinal symptoms, skin pigmentation, and Mees\' lines.\\n• D. Manganese: Produces a parkinsonian syndrome (manganism) with rigidity and bradykinesia rather than the classic hatter\'s tremor.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4b21462b02","n":141,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A video shows an agitated young man who is disoriented and combative. On examination, his eyes are shown below. He appears to have reduced pain perception despite struggling with multiple attendants. Which substance is the most likely cause of these findings?","options":["Cocaine","Phencyclidine (PCP)","Cannabis","Heroin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Phencyclidine (PCP)\\n\\nThe video demonstrates horizontal and vertical nystagmus, a classic finding of phencyclidine (PCP, \\"Angel Dust\\") intoxication.\\n\\nTypical features of PCP toxicity include:\\n\\n• Horizontal and vertical nystagmus (highly characteristic)\\n• Violent or bizarre behavior\\n• Agitation and psychosis\\n• Analgesia with decreased response to pain\\n• Hypertension and tachycardia\\n• Muscle rigidity and hyperthermia (severe cases)\\n\\nPCP is a noncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist, producing dissociative anesthesia and hallucinations.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e37d2c77d3","n":142,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following are correct statements except:","options":["Integrins and fibronectin work together for tissue remodelling.","Potassium levels in vitreous humor fall linearly after death and are a useful measure of time of death.","Suspended animation can be seen after electrocution.","Industrial workers most frequently develop inorganic lead poisoning through inhalation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Potassium levels in vitreous humor fall linearly after death and are a useful measure of time of death.\\n\\nAfter death, potassium levels in vitreous humor rise linearly, not fall. This increase occurs due to breakdown of retinal cells and diffusion of intracellular potassium into vitreous fluid. Because this rise is relatively predictable, vitreous potassium estimation is commonly used for postmortem interval (PMI) assessment.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nIntegrins and fibronectin: They interact to mediate cell adhesion, migration, and tissue remodeling.\\n\\nSuspended animation after electrocution: Can occur due to transient cardiac or respiratory arrest mimicking death.\\n\\nInorganic lead poisoning: Most commonly occurs via inhalation in industrial settings, especially battery and paint workers.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-91d760869c","n":143,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following produce permanent alteration of fingerprints, except:","options":["Leprosy","Radiation injury","Deep electrical burn","Dermatitis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Dermatitis\\n\\n• Dermatitis causes only partial and temporary atrophy of fingerprint ridges.\\n• Permanent alterations occur after destruction of the dermal papillae.\\n\\nPermanent alterations include:\\n\\n• Leprosy\\n• Radiation\\n• Electrocution\\n• Deep burns\\n\\nTemporary alterations:\\n\\n• Dermatitis\\n• Eczema\\n• Superficial abrasions\\n\\n| Condition | Effect on fingerprints\\n\\n| --------- | ----------------------\\n\\n| Dermatitis | Temporary\\n\\n| Celiac disease | Complete atrophy\\n\\n| Leprosy | Permanent\\n\\n| Radiation | Permanent","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4401713b3b","n":144,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding chronic arsenic poisoning (arsenicosis) are true, except:","options":["Aldrich-Mees\' lines may also be seen in thallium poisoning.","Peripheral sensory-motor polyneuropathy may clinically resemble Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome.","Chronic arsenic poisoning commonly produces diffuse depigmentation without hyperkeratosis.","Long-standing arsenicosis increases the risk of basal cell carcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Chronic arsenic poisoning commonly produces diffuse depigmentation without hyperkeratosis.\\n\\nChronic arsenic poisoning (hydroarsenicism) classically produces raindrop pigmentation (mixed hyper- and hypopigmentation) with palmoplantar hyperkeratosis. Other characteristic findings include Mees\' lines, peripheral neuropathy, black foot disease, and increased risk of skin cancers.\\n\\n| Feature | Chronic arsenic poisoning\\n\\n| ------- | -------------------------\\n\\n| Skin | Raindrop pigmentation + palmoplantar hyperkeratosis\\n\\n| Nails | Aldrich-Mees\' lines\\n\\n| Nervous system | Peripheral neuropathy (resembles GBS)\\n\\n| Vascular | Black foot disease\\n\\n| Malignancy | SCC and BCC","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e2d0ff977a","n":145,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding cocaine poisoning are true, except:","options":["Cocaine acts predominantly as a sympathomimetic agent by increasing synaptic catecholamines.","Body packer syndrome results from rupture of internally concealed cocaine packets and may produce fatal cardiovascular or cerebrovascular complications.","Chronic intranasal cocaine abuse may cause nasal septal perforation and peripheral gangrene.","Formication (\\"cocaine bugs\\" or Magnan\'s symptom) represents a visual hallucination in which patients repeatedly see insects crawling on their body."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Formication (\\"cocaine bugs\\" or Magnan\'s symptom) represents a visual hallucination in which patients repeatedly see insects crawling on their body.\\n\\n• A. Cocaine acts predominantly as a sympathomimetic agent by increasing synaptic catecholamines: Correct. Cocaine blocks reuptake of norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin, producing marked sympathomimetic effects.\\n• B. Body packer syndrome results from rupture of internally concealed cocaine packets and may produce fatal cardiovascular or cerebrovascular complications: Correct. Rupture of cocaine packets in body packers can cause massive overdose leading to myocardial infarction, stroke or sudden death.\\n• C. Chronic intranasal cocaine abuse may cause nasal septal perforation and peripheral gangrene: Correct. Chronic cocaine abuse is associated with septal perforation, black staining of teeth/tongue and peripheral gangrene due to vasoconstriction.\\n• D. Formication (\\"cocaine bugs\\" or Magnan\'s symptom) represents a visual hallucination in which patients repeatedly see insects crawling on their body: Incorrect. Magnan\'s symptom (\\"cocaine bugs\\") is a tactile hallucination (formication) in which patients feel insects crawling under the skin—not a visual hallucination.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-471967456f","n":146,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding cognizable and non-cognizable offences are correct except:","options":["In a cognizable offence, police may arrest the accused without obtaining a warrant from the Magistrate.","Police may commence investigation into a cognizable offence without prior permission from the Magistrate.","In a non-cognizable offence, police can arrest without a warrant if they have reasonable suspicion of guilt.","Murder, rape, and robbery are classical examples of cognizable offences."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. In a non-cognizable offence, police can arrest without a warrant if they have reasonable suspicion of guilt.\\n\\nUnder BNSS (earlier CrPC), offences are broadly classified into cognizable and non-cognizable based on the powers of the police.\\n\\n| Feature | Cognizable offence | Non-cognizable offence\\n\\n| ------- | ------------------ | ----------------------\\n\\n| Definition | Serious offence | Less serious offence\\n\\n| Arrest | Without warrant permitted | Warrant required\\n\\n| Investigation | Police can investigate without Magistrate\'s permission | Magistrate\'s permission generally required before investigation\\n\\n| Examples | Murder, rape, robbery, dacoity, kidnapping | Simple hurt, defamation, public nuisance, minor assault\\n\\n| Police powers | Greater | Limited","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5181a5609b","n":147,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding fingerprint identification are correct, except:","options":["Fingerprints begin developing between the 12th and 16th week of intrauterine life","Monozygotic twins have identical DNA fingerprints but different fingerprints","Arch is the least common pattern encountered in forensic practice","Fingerprints remain unchanged throughout life unless permanently destroyed by deep dermal injury"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Arch is the least common pattern encountered in forensic practice\\n\\nComposite is actually the least common fingerprint pattern.\\n\\nGalton\'s classification:\\n\\n• Loop: 60–65% (most common)\\n• Whorl: Second most common\\n• Arch: Third most common\\n• Composite: Least common","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a5cef9a9de","n":148,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding forensic age estimation from dentition are correct, except:","options":["Mixed dentition extends from approximately 6–11 years, and the number of permanent teeth present during this period can be estimated by (Age − 5) \xd7 4","Gustafson\'s method is applicable only after eruption of the third molar and is based on six secondary dental changes, among which root transparency is the most reliable criterion","Boyde\'s method estimates age in infants by counting daily incremental lines in enamel under electron microscopy, and the neonatal line is formed on the 7th day after birth","Permanent teeth replacing deciduous teeth are called successional teeth, whereas permanent molars are superadded teeth"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Boyde\'s method estimates age in infants by counting daily incremental lines in enamel under electron microscopy, and the neonatal line is formed on the 7th day after birth\\n\\nThe neonatal line (NL) appears on the 2nd–3rd day after birth, not the 7th day. It represents the transition from intrauterine to extrauterine enamel formation and is an important marker of live birth.\\n\\n| Method | Age group | Principle | High-yield fact\\n\\n| ------ | --------- | --------- | ---------------\\n\\n| Boyde | Infants | Incremental enamel lines | 1 line/day\\n\\n| Gustafson | >25 years | Secondary tooth changes | Root transparency most reliable\\n\\n| Mixed dentition | 6–11 years | Permanent teeth count | (Age − 5) \xd7 4","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c88b3afdea","n":149,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding metallic irritant poisoning are true, except:","options":["Blood is a more reliable specimen than urine for estimation of lead poisoning.","Urine is more useful than blood for assessment of arsenic poisoning.","Bone, hair, and nails may be preserved for heavy metal analysis.","Acute heavy metal poisoning is most commonly caused by lead, whereas chronic poisoning is most commonly caused by arsenic."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Acute heavy metal poisoning is most commonly caused by lead, whereas chronic poisoning is most commonly caused by arsenic.\\n\\nThe reverse is true.\\n\\n• Most common acute heavy metal poisoning → Arsenic\\n• Most common chronic heavy metal poisoning → Lead\\n\\nSamples collected for heavy metal poisoning:\\n\\n• Lead: Blood (most reliable)\\n• Arsenic: Urine (most reliable)\\n• Bone, hair, nails: Useful for chronic exposure\\n\\n| Heavy metal | High-yield fact\\n\\n| ----------- | ---------------\\n\\n| Acute poisoning | Arsenic\\n\\n| Chronic poisoning | Lead\\n\\n| Best sample (Lead) | Blood\\n\\n| Best sample (Arsenic) | Urine\\n\\n| Chronic exposure | Hair, nails, bone","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-db03a01ec9","n":150,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding ossification centers used for medicolegal age estimation are correct, except:","options":["The medial end of the clavicle is the last epiphysis to appear and fuse in the body, appearing at 18–19 years and fusing by 21–22 years","At the elbow, all epiphyses fuse at approximately 16 years","Appearance of the pisiform reliably indicates that the child has completed 12 years of age","Conjoint epiphysis at the elbow is formed by fusion of the capitulum, trochlea and lateral epicondyle"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Appearance of the pisiform reliably indicates that the child has completed 12 years of age\\n\\nThe pisiform appears between 9–12 years. Its presence only indicates that the child is at least around 9 years old, not necessarily ≥12 years.\\n\\n| Ossification center | Appearance\\n\\n| ------------------- | ----------\\n\\n| Capitate | 2 months\\n\\n| Hamate | 3 months–1 year\\n\\n| Triquetrum | 3 years\\n\\n| Lunate | 4 years\\n\\n| Scaphoid | 5 years\\n\\n| Trapezium | 5–6 years\\n\\n| Trapezoid | 5–6 years\\n\\n| Pisiform | 9–12 years","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-cc93ce9499","n":151,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding snake identification are true, except:","options":["Large ventral scales extending across the entire breadth of the abdomen favor a venomous snake.","Presence of two fang marks confirms venom injection into the victim.","Venomous snakes generally possess compressed tails, whereas non-venomous snakes usually do not.","Cobras and kraits are exceptions to the rule of small head scales seen in venomous snakes."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Presence of two fang marks confirms venom injection into the victim.\\n\\n• A. Large ventral scales extending across the entire breadth of the abdomen favor a venomous snake: Correct. Venomous snakes usually have large ventral scales extending across the abdomen.\\n• B. Presence of two fang marks confirms venom injection into the victim: Incorrect. Fang marks indicate a bite but do not prove venom injection. Dry bites are common.\\n• C. Venomous snakes generally possess compressed tails, whereas non-venomous snakes usually do not: Correct. Tail compression is a useful morphological clue for venomous snakes.\\n• D. Cobras and kraits are exceptions to the rule of small head scales seen in venomous snakes: Correct. Cobras and kraits are important exceptions regarding head scale patterns.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b8902df519","n":152,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements regarding this plant poisoning are true, except:","options":["Swallowing intact seeds usually produces little or no toxicity because the tough seed coat prevents release of the toxin.","Toxin is concentrated in the residual seed cake.","Crushed seeds are considerably more toxic than intact seeds due to release of the toxalbumin.","The Oil itself retains sufficient ricin to produce severe systemic toxalbumin poisoning."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. The Oil itself retains sufficient ricin to produce severe systemic toxalbumin poisoning.\\n\\n• A. Swallowing intact seeds usually produces little or no toxicity because the tough seed coat prevents release of the toxin: Correct. Intact castor seeds are usually non-toxic because the hard seed coat remains undigested, preventing release of ricin.\\n• B. Toxin is concentrated in the residual seed cake: Correct. Ricin remains in the residual seed cake after oil extraction. Purified castor oil is essentially non-toxic because ricin is water soluble and not oil soluble.\\n• C. Crushed seeds are considerably more toxic than intact seeds due to release of the toxalbumin: Correct. Crushing the seeds releases ricin, a potent toxalbumin that inhibits protein synthesis by inactivating the 60S ribosomal subunit.\\n• D. The Oil itself retains sufficient ricin to produce severe systemic toxalbumin poisoning: Incorrect. Castor oil is non-toxic and does not contain clinically significant ricin. Ricin remains in the residue after oil extraction.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-6b5c1e3819","n":153,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"An 18-month-old boy is brought to the OPD due to language regression. He said several words at his 1-year appointment but now no longer speaks any words at all. His moods have also become more unpredictable over the past 4 months with frequent tantrums. The parents tried to bring him in sooner for evaluation, but they live in an impoverished part of the city and experienced financial difficulties with transportation to the office. On physical examination, the boy is quiet and maintains appropriate eye contact throughout the visit. Hemoglobin is 9 g/dL. Which of the following enzymes is most likely inhibited in this patient?","options":["5-Aminolevulinate dehydratase","Bilirubin glucuronyl transferase","Porphobilinogen deaminase","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 5-Aminolevulinate dehydratase\\n\\nLanguage regression + irritability/tantrums in a toddler from an impoverished area, along with anemia (Hb 9 g/dL), strongly suggests lead poisoning (old paint/dust exposure). Lead inhibits heme synthesis—classically ALA dehydratase(and also ferrochelatase, though not listed). This causes ↓ heme → microcytic anemia and neurotoxicity (developmental regression, behavioral changes).\\n\\nWhy others are incorrect:\\n\\n• Bilirubin glucuronyl transferase: Crigler-Najjar/Gilbert → unconjugated jaundice, not neuroregression + anemia pattern.\\n• Porphobilinogen deaminase: Acute intermittent porphyria (abdominal pain, neuropathy) in older patients; no anemia.\\n• Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase: Porphyria cutanea tarda (photosensitivity, blistering), not this presentation.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eb002d7f0e","n":154,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"An entry firearm wound lacks an abrasion collar despite otherwise resembling an entry wound. There is no burning, blackening, tattooing or grease collar. Which type of projectile best explains this finding?","options":["Dum-dum bullet","Ricochet bullet","Tumbling bullet","Tracer bullet"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ricochet bullet\\n\\nA ricochet bullet behaves differently after striking an intermediate surface.\\n\\n| Feature | Ricochet bullet\\n\\n| ------- | ---------------\\n\\n| Hits intermediate object | Yes\\n\\n| Gyroscopic spin | Lost\\n\\n| Abrasion collar | Absent\\n\\n| Flame/smoke/gunpowder effects | Absent","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-56f1e25878","n":155,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"An old man who was lying on the roadside is brought to the causalty by the police. On detailed history, he reveals that he has had no food for the past few weeks. On examination, he is severely emaciated. What is the likely finding in the gall bladder?","options":["Gall bladder distended","Atrophy of gall bladder","Gall stones","Cholecystitis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Gall bladder distended\\n\\nIn chronic starvation, there is absence of fat and protein in the duodenum.\\n\\n• This leads to reduced stimulation of I cells → ↓ cholecystokinin (CCK) secretion.\\n• CCK is the main hormone responsible for gallbladder contraction.\\n• Low CCK → gallbladder becomes hypomotile (does not contract properly).\\n• Meanwhile, the liver continues to produce bile continuously.\\n• Due to poor emptying, bile accumulates within the gallbladder.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7942a8f2f6","n":156,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Arrange the organs in order of putrefaction (early to late): 1. Larynx 2. Kidney 3. Skin 4. Brain 5. Prostate","options":["1-2-3-4-5","5-1-4-2-3","1-4-2-5-3","4-1-2-3-5"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1-4-2-5-3\\n\\nPutrefaction occurs at different rates depending on water content, enzymatic activity, bacterial load, and tissue density.\\n\\n• The larynx (1) putrefies early due to its moist mucosa and exposure to airway bacteria.\\n• The brain (4) follows soon because of its high water and lipid content, which accelerates autolysis.\\n• The kidney (2) decomposes next owing to its rich blood supply and high metabolic activity.\\n• The prostate (5) resists decomposition longer due to its dense fibromuscular tissue.\\n• The skin (3) is among the last to putrefy because of its keratinized protective barrier.\\n\\nOther sequences incorrectly place resistant structures too early or highly autolytic organs too late.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b0157b7f77","n":157,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Arsenic poisoning may mimic all of the following conditions except:","options":["Addison’s disease due to hyperpigmentation and GI disturbances","Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome due to ascending peripheral neuropathy","Diabetes insipidus due to renal concentrating defects","Korsakoff’s syndrome due to neurocognitive impairment"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Diabetes insipidus due to renal concentrating defects\\n\\n• Arsenic poisoning can mimic several medical conditions due to its multisystem effects.\\n• It causes hyperpigmentation, GI symptoms, and hypotension, resembling Addison’s disease.\\n• It can produce ascending peripheral neuropathy, clinically similar to Guillain–Barr\xe9 syndrome, and chronic exposure may lead to neurocognitive impairment, mimicking Korsakoff’s syndrome.\\n• However, arsenic does not cause diabetes insipidus, as it does not produce a primary defect in ADH secretion or renal concentrating ability.\\n• Hence, this condition is not mimicked by arsenic poisoning.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-903bcac135","n":158,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Burden of proof lies of the following parties except:","options":["Criminal negligence: Prosecution","Res ipsa liqutor: Government","Civil negligence: Plaintiff","Contributory negligence: Doctor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Res ipsa liqutor: Government\\n\\nIn criminal negligence, the burden of proof lies on the prosecution, which must prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt. In civil negligence, the plaintiff bears the burden to establish negligence on the balance of probabilities. In contributory negligence, the doctor/defendant must prove that the patient’s own negligence contributed to the harm.\\n\\nHowever, in res ipsa loquitur, the burden shifts to the defendant (doctor/hospital) to explain that there was no negligence once the plaintiff establishes that the injury would not ordinarily occur without negligence. The government does not bear the burden here, making this pairing incorrect.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-12b12451cd","n":159,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Casualty Medical Officer is examining a patient who is displaying symptoms of muscle twitching, numbness, and tingling in the extremities. The patient has a history of potential poisoning. Upon investigation, it is revealed that the patient was exposed to a toxic substance. What toxic substance is most likely responsible for the development of tetany in this patient?","options":["Oxalic acid","Carbolic acid","Sulphuric acid","Nitric acid"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Oxalic acid\\n\\nTetany (muscle twitching, numbness, tingling) is a manifestation of hypocalcemia. Oxalic acid causes tetany because it binds calcium in the blood to form insoluble calcium oxalate, leading to a rapid fall in ionized calcium levels. This hypocalcemia increases neuromuscular excitability, producing carpopedal spasm and tetany, which are characteristic findings in oxalic acid poisoning.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\n• Carbolic acid (phenol): Causes CNS depression, corrosive injury, and cardiac toxicity—not tetany.\\n• Sulphuric acid: A strong corrosive causing tissue burns and shock, not hypocalcemia.\\n• Nitric acid: Causes corrosive injury and methemoglobinemia, not tetany.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2a3cbe363f","n":160,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Catamite refers to:","options":["Active agent in sodomy","Any passive agent in sodomy","Small child acting as passive agent in sodomy","A female acting as passive agent in sodomy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Small child acting as passive agent in sodomy\\n\\nThe term catamite is a forensic–medicolegal term referring specifically to a young boy (child) who is the passive recipient in sodomy. It is derived from historical and legal descriptions and is used to emphasize both the age and passive role of the victim. The term is important in forensic practice because involvement of a child implies aggravated sexual offense with severe legal consequences.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f75938d2ef","n":161,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Chronic arsenic exposure is associated with all of the following except","options":["Hyperkeratosis","Alopecia","Lung, prostate, skin cancer","Hypotension"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Hypotension\\n\\nChronic arsenic exposure classically causes skin and systemic toxicity. Long-term exposure leads to hyperkeratosis, especially on palms and soles, along with hyperpigmentation (“raindrop” pattern). Arsenic is a well-known human carcinogen, strongly associated with skin cancer (SCC, BCC) and also lung and prostate cancers due to its mutagenic effects on DNA and impaired DNA repair mechanisms.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nHyperkeratosis: A hallmark feature of chronic arsenic toxicity.\\n\\nAlopecia: Can occur due to toxic effects on rapidly dividing hair follicle cells.\\n\\nLung, prostate, skin cancer: Established long-term complications of arsenic exposure.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e4cabf34bf","n":162,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Dr. Sharma, a cardiologist in New Delhi, is under investigation after being accused of overprescribing medications in exchange for incentives from a pharmaceutical company. In the context of medical regulation in India, which authority is primarily responsible for issuing a formal warning to Dr. Singh?","options":["Central Government","State Medical Council","National Medical Commission","Medical Council of India"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. State Medical Council\\n\\nDisciplinary control over doctors in cases of professional misconduct is primarily exercised by the State Medical Council (SMC)\\n\\n• They can issue warning, suspension, or remove name from state register\\n\\nOther examples of professional misconduct:\\n\\n1. Minor breaches of professional etiquette\\n\\n• Not displaying registration number on prescription\\n• Improper maintenance of medical records\\n• Not issuing proper receipts/bills\\n\\n2. Advertisement-related violations\\n\\n• Self-advertisement (e.g., claiming superiority, guaranteed cure)\\n\\n3. Improper use of titles\\n\\n• Using unrecognized qualifications\\n• Misleading prefixes/suffixes (e.g., “specialist” without certification)\\n\\n4. Prescription-related minor issues\\n\\n• Writing brand names without justification\\n• Not using generic names (as advised)\\n• Illegible prescriptions (if no harm caused)\\n\\n6. Fee-related issues\\n\\n• Overcharging (not gross exploitation)\\n• Lack of transparency in fees\\n\\n7. Consent/documentation lapses (non-serious)\\n\\n• Incomplete informed consent documentation\\n• Missing signatures/details but no adverse outcome","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2e1b898d9f","n":163,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"During a forensic examination of a shooting incident, a medical examiner discovers both shotgun pellets and rifle bullets at the crime scene. The examiner notes the differences in their design and function. Which statement about these ammunition types is not correct?","options":["Shotgun cartridges contain multiple projectiles that spread upon firing.","Wads are used in all types of cartridges","Rifle bullets are generally elongated with a pointed tip","Rifle bullets and shotgun pellets are made of lead"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Wads are used in all types of cartridges\\n\\nThis statement is not correct. Wads are characteristic of shotgun cartridges, where they separate the powder from the shot, seal gases, and help control pellet spread. Rifle cartridges do not use wads; instead, they contain a single bullet seated directly in the cartridge case with propellant.\\n\\nA. Shotgun cartridges: Contain multiple pellets that disperse after firing—true.\\n\\nC. Rifle bullets: Are typically elongated and pointed for stability and penetration—true.\\n\\nD. Both rifle bullets and shotgun pellets are commonly made of lead (often jacketed in rifles)—true.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bf349bc04a","n":164,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"During autopsy of a newborn, the lungs weigh 60 g and float on water. The stomach and intestine also contain air bubbles, while the diaphragm lies opposite the 6th rib. Which of the following additional findings is most likely?","options":["Wreden\'s test positive for gelatin in middle ear","Placquet ratio of 1:70","Fodere\'s lung weight approximately 30 g","Lung-to-body weight ratio approximately 1:35"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Lung-to-body weight ratio approximately 1:35\\n\\nThe baby has clearly respired.\\n\\nEvidence:\\n\\n• Lung floats\\n• Air in stomach (Breslau\'s second life test)\\n• Diaphragm lowered to 5th–6th rib\\n• Lung weight ≈60 g\\n\\nPlacquet ratio:\\n\\n• Live born → 1:35\\n• Dead born → 1:70\\n\\n| Test | Live born\\n\\n| ---- | ---------\\n\\n| Lung flotation | Floats\\n\\n| Lung weight | 60 g\\n\\n| Placquet ratio | 1:35\\n\\n| Wreden test | Negative","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8309c084f6","n":165,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"During medicolegal evaluation after a road traffic accident, a patient complains of neck pain, headache, dizziness, and restricted neck movements. Imaging shows no cervical fracture or dislocation. The video of the mechanism is shown below. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Atlantoaxial dislocation","Whiplash injury","Cervical spondylosis","Brown-S\xe9quard syndrome"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Whiplash injury\\n\\nHigh-Yield Forensic Pearls\\n\\n• Mechanism: Sudden hyperextension followed by hyperflexion of the cervical spine.\\n• Typical scenario: Rear-end motor vehicle collision.\\n• Common injuries: Cervical muscle and ligament strain, facet joint injury, disc injury.\\n• Symptoms: Neck pain, stiffness, headache, dizziness, reduced range of motion.\\n• Imaging: Often normal, as most injuries involve soft tissues rather than fractures.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a705e424c1","n":166,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Dying deposition is more important than dying declaration because it is:","options":["Made to a Magistrate","Oath is taken","Made in presence of accused","Cross-examination is permitted"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cross-examination is permitted\\n\\nA dying deposition is considered more important than a dying declaration because it is recorded on oath, in the presence of the accused, and allows cross-examination, thereby satisfying principles of natural justice. The opportunity for cross-examination makes it stronger and more reliable evidence in court.\\n\\nA: Being made to a Magistrate also applies to dying declarations\\n\\nB: Oath alone does not make it superior.\\n\\nC: Presence of accused is important, but without cross-examination, reliability is still limited","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-928dde0909","n":167,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Ewing’s postulates are used to establish:","options":["Cause of death from toxicology","Identity of unknown remains","Causal relationship between trauma and death","Time since death by entomology"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Causal relationship between trauma and death\\n\\nThese postulates should be satisfied before a relationship between Trauma and New growth is accepted.\\n\\n1. Evidence of previous integrity of the injured part.\\n\\n2. Undeniable and Adequate trauma must be proved.\\n\\n3. There must be a proof of reasonable time interval between injury and appearance of tumor.\\n\\n4. Disease must develop at the exact site of injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e2bda261e3","n":168,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Henssge’s nomogram is used for:","options":["Estimating fetal age","Estimating stature from long bones","Estimating time since death from body temperature","Estimating postmortem interval from rigor mortis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Estimating time since death from body temperature\\n\\nOption C: Estimating time since death from body temperature.\\n\\nHenssge Nomogram uses:\\n\\n✔ Measured rectal temperature\\n\\n✔ Ambient temperature\\n\\n✔ Body weight\\n\\n✔ Correction factors (clothing, environment)\\n\\n• Gives a more accurate PMI (postmortem interval) than simple Newton’s cooling law.\\n\\n❌ Why Other Options Are Wrong\\n\\nOption A. Estimating fetal age\\n\\nThat’s done using:\\n\\n• Crown-rump length , Haase rule.\\n\\nB. Estimating stature from long bones\\n\\nThat uses:\\n\\n• Trotter & Gleser formula\\n• Regression equations\\n\\nCompletely different field (forensic anthropology).\\n\\nD. Estimating postmortem interval from rigor mortis\\n\\nRigor mortis gives a rough timeline, but:\\n\\n• It is subjective , varies widely\\n• No nomogram involved\\n\\nHenssge specifically uses body cooling, not rigor.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-97245478c1","n":169,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify plant shown below.","options":["Cocaine","Hyoscine","Tobacco","Tetrahydro-cannabinol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hyoscine\\n\\nThe image depicts Datura (thorn apple/Jimson weed), a poisonous plant of the Solanaceae family. Its principal toxic alkaloids are:\\n\\n• Hyoscine (Scopolamine) ✅\\n• Hyoscyamine\\n• Atropine\\n\\nThese alkaloids are competitive muscarinic receptor antagonists, producing the classic anticholinergic syndrome.\\n\\nClinical features of Datura poisoning\\n\\nMnemonic: \\"Dry as a bone, Red as a beet, Blind as a bat, Hot as a hare, Mad as a hatter\\"\\n\\n• Dry mouth\\n• Dry, hot skin\\n• Flushing\\n• Mydriasis with blurred vision\\n• Tachycardia\\n• Hyperthermia\\n• Urinary retention\\n• Delirium, hallucinations, agitation\\n\\nTreatment\\n\\n• Supportive care\\n• Activated charcoal (if early presentation)\\n• Benzodiazepines for agitation\\n• Physostigmine (specific antidote in severe anticholinergic toxicity)","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-028c62fcf0","n":170,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the correct statement with regards to the changes around an entry wound: 1. Grease collar - Due to the deposition of the bullet wipe 2. Burn injuries - Occur due to flame 3. Blackening – Due to epidermal deposit of smoke 4. Tattooing - Due to dermal deposition of burnt gunpowder","options":["1, 2, 3, 4","1, 2, 3","2, 3","2, 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 3\\n\\nAround an entry wound, a grease (wipe) collar is formed due to bullet wipe as grease and dirt on the bullet are deposited on the skin (true). Burn injuries occur in close or contact shots due to flame and hot gases (true). Blackening is caused by epidermal deposition of smoke/soot and can be wiped off (true).\\n\\nTattooing, however, is due to dermal deposition of unburnt gunpowder particles, not burnt gunpowder, making statement 4 incorrect.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5fc7db38d9","n":171,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the correct statements about the following: 1. Occurs due to sulf-methHb in blood vessels 2. Begins by 24hrs 3. Greenish-black in colour 4. Called as Arborisation flower","options":["1, 2, 3, 4","1, 2, 3","1, 3","2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 3\\n\\n• The statements describe marbling, an early sign of putrefaction.\\n• Marbling occurs due to the formation of sulfhemoglobin (sulf-metHb) in superficial blood vessels --> when hemoglobin reacts with hydrogen sulfide produced by intestinal bacteria.\\n• It typically begins around 24–36 hours after death ; appears as a greenish-black discoloration following the venous pattern, especially over the abdomen and trunk.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-91c483ad00","n":172,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the correct statements: 1. Superfetation results in the birth of twins that can have different fathers and are born at the same time. 2. ”In camera\\" refers to a closed proceeding in court where only selected individuals are allowed to be present. 3. Adipocere formation occurs due to the saponification of fats through an enzymatic process, primarily under warm, moist, and anaerobic conditions 4. A body is deemed unclaimed after 96hrs of a car accident","options":["1, 2, 4","1, 3","2, 3","2, 3, 4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 2, 3\\n\\nStatement 2 is correct: In camera refers to a closed court proceeding conducted privately, with only the judge, parties involved, and permitted individuals present. It is commonly used in sexual offense cases to protect victim identity and privacy.\\n\\nStatement 3 is correct: Adipocere formation results from saponification of body fats due to enzymatic and bacterial action under warm, moist, anaerobic conditions, leading to a waxy, soap-like substance that preserves tissues.\\n\\nStatement 1 is incorrect: Superfetation leads to fetuses of different gestational ages, often born at different times; different fathers is superfecundation.\\n\\nStatement 4 is incorrect: Under the Delhi Anatomy Act, 1953, and the Delhi police manual, a body is considered unclaimed after 72 hours, and the police are authorized to dispose of it. So the 72-hour rule is correct specifically for Delhi\\n\\nBut the period is state-specific ⚠️\\n\\n| Jurisdiction / rule | Waiting period before \\"unclaimed\\"\\n\\n| ------------------- | ---------------------------------\\n\\n| Delhi (Anatomy Act 1953 / police manual) | 72 hours\\n\\n| Kerala (Anatomy Rules 1960) | 24 hours if the nearest relative is in the same district; 72 hours if the relative is outside the district\\n\\n| Odisha (state regulation) | No autopsy can be conducted on an unclaimed body until 96 hours have passed","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-17bc214d22","n":173,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the false statement regarding teeth and ethnicity:","options":["In Africanoids, a marrow and elongated skull with square orbits is seen","Caucasians have small nodules on the lingual surface of maxillary molars","Mongolians have brachycephalic skulls","Mesaticephalic skull is found in Africanoids"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Mesaticephalic skull is found in Africanoids\\n\\nCraniofacial and dental traits vary among ethnic groups and are useful in forensic identification, but must be interpreted as patterns, not absolutes:\\n\\n• Africanoid features\\n\\n• Typically, dolichocephalic (long, narrow skull)\\n• Square orbits, broad nasal aperture\\n• NOT mesaticephalic\\n\\n• Caucasoid features\\n\\n• May show Carabelli cusp (small nodule on lingual surface of maxillary molars)\\n\\n• Mongoloid features\\n\\n• Typically, brachycephalic skull\\n• Shovel-shaped incisors (high-yield dental feature)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-851b98e156","n":174,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the following plant:","options":["Datura alba","Argemone Mexicana","Abrus precatorius","Erthyroxylum coca"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Abrus precatorius\\n\\nAbrus precatorius (rosary pea) is identified by its small, hard, shiny seeds that are bright red with a characteristic black spot. These seeds contain abrin, a highly potent toxalbumin, making the plant important in toxicology. The plant is a climber with compound leaves, and the distinctive seed appearance is a classic exam clue.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-ade910b57b","n":175,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the incorrect pair of BNS:","options":["313 - Criminal abortion without the consent of mother","28 - Invalid consent","22 - McNaughten’s rule of insanity","227 - Definition of Perjury"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 313 - Criminal abortion without the consent of mother\\n\\nUnder the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), Section 313 deals with causing miscarriage without the consent of the woman, and not “mother” as stated in the option. The legal terminology is strictly “woman”, irrespective of marital status or motherhood. Hence, pairing Section 313 with “without the consent of mother” is incorrect.\\n\\nWhy other options are correct:\\n\\nB. 28 – Invalid consent: Correctly defines circumstances where consent is not free or legally valid.\\n\\nC. 22 – McNaughten’s rule: Correctly deals with acts of persons of unsound mind.\\n\\nD. 227 – Definition of perjury: Correct; relates to giving false evidence under BNS.\\n\\nThus, option A is the incorrect pair.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-31ee87cb4c","n":176,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the test which follows the principle ‘Prior knowledge of the events/activity will influence the suspect\'s reaction’?","options":["Truth Serum","Polygraph","Hypnosis","Brain mapping"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Brain mapping\\n\\nBrain mapping (BEOS – Brain Electrical Oscillation Signature profiling) is based on the principle that prior knowledge or experience of an event produces specific brain responses when the subject is exposed to related stimuli. If the suspect has actually participated in the crime, the brain shows recognition responses (experiential knowledge) rather than simple information processing. This makes it distinct from stress- or deception-based tests.\\n\\nA. Truth serum: Lowers inhibitions but does not objectively detect prior knowledge.\\n\\nB. Polygraph: Measures autonomic arousal (stress), not memory recognition.\\n\\nC. Hypnosis: Increases suggestibility and recall but is unreliable and easily confounded.\\n\\nHence, brain mapping best fits the stated principle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1d489e28de","n":177,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Identify the wound:","options":["Entry Close range","Entry Contact range","Entry Intermediate range","Exit wound"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Exit wound\\n\\nGunshot wounds are differentiated based on morphology, margins, and associated features:\\n\\n• Exit wound characteristics:\\n\\n• Irregular, everted margins\\n• Larger, more ragged appearance\\n• No abrasion collar, soot, or tattooing\\n• Caused by bullet deformation and tumbling as it exits\\n\\n• Entry wound (contrast):\\n\\n• Small, round/oval, inverted margins\\n• Abrasion collar present\\n• May show soot/blackening (close/contact range) or tattooing (intermediate range)","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-a1c039f54a","n":178,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"In a legal case involving a medical malpractice claim, the court requires specific documents and evidence from the hospital where the incident occurred, the court issues a ‘Duces tecum’ to the Hospital. What does ‘Duces tecum’ refer to in legal proceedings?","options":["Summon","Panchnama","Conduct money","Hostile witness"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Summon\\n\\nDUCES TECUM :A summons requiring a person to appear in court AND bring specified documents or records.\\n\\n• In medicolegal cases, hospitals are commonly served a summons duces tecum to submit medical records, case sheets, investigation reports, consent forms, etc. The term \'summons\' means “bring with you.”\\n• In a malpractice case, this would mean:\\n\\n• Produce hospital records\\n• Case sheets\\n• Investigation reports\\n• Consent forms\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\n• Panchanama → Police documentation of scene or inquest (Indian context)\\n• Conduct money → Travel allowance paid to witness\\n• Hostile witness → Witness who turns against the party calling them","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e15c3b247b","n":179,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"In Breslau\'s second life test, organ tested is:","options":["Brain","Heart","Lung","Stomach and Intestine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Stomach and Intestine\\n\\nBreslau\'s second life test is used in forensic medicine to determine whether a newborn survived long enough after birth to swallow air.\\n\\nThe test examines the stomach and intestines for the presence of swallowed air.\\n\\n• Positive test: Stomach and intestines float in water due to the presence of swallowed air, indicating the infant lived after birth.\\n• Negative test: Stomach and intestines sink, suggesting the infant did not survive long enough to swallow air.\\n\\nTypes of Breslau\'s Tests\\n\\n1. Breslau\'s First Life Test (Hydrostatic Lung Test)\\n\\n• Organ tested: Lungs\\n• Purpose: Determines whether respiration has occurred.\\n• Positive test: Lungs float.\\n\\n2. Breslau\'s Second Life Test (Gastrointestinal Float Test)\\n\\n• Organ tested: Stomach and intestines\\n• Purpose: Determines whether the infant survived long enough to swallow air after birth.\\n• Positive test: Stomach and intestines float.\\n\\nWhy the other options are incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Brain – Not used in Breslau\'s life tests.\\n• B. Heart – Not examined in Breslau\'s tests.\\n• C. Lung – Tested in Breslau\'s first life test, not the second.\\n\\nNEET PG Pearl\\n\\n| Test | Organ Tested | Indicates\\n\\n| ---- | ------------ | ---------\\n\\n| Breslau\'s First Life Test | Lungs | Respiration has occurred\\n\\n| Breslau\'s Second Life Test | Stomach & Intestines | Infant survived long enough to swallow air\\n\\n| Ploucquet\'s Test | Lungs (lung-body weight ratio) | Evidence of respiration\\n\\n| Fodere\'s Test | Middle ear | Presence of air after birth","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c831a401a7","n":180,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Law does not consider the following doctrine in charges of criminal negligence:","options":["Vicarious liability","Contributory negligence","Res ipsa loquitur","Novus actus interveniens"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Vicarious liability\\n\\nVicarious liability transfers responsibility from servant to master (employee → employer/hospital). Criminal law does not transfer culpability — actus reus + mens rea must attach to the individual accused. So the doctrine is fundamentally not entertained in criminal negligence. A hospital may be civilly liable for a resident\'s error, but only the individual doctor faces criminal prosecution.\\n\\nB. Contributory negligence: Though not a defense, it may be considered factually\\n\\nC. Res ipsa loquitur: Used as an evidentiary aid to infer negligence\\n\\nD. Novus actus interveniens: Recognized as it may break the chain of causation.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-44d1b4e8e9","n":181,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Leading Questions are permitted in all except:","options":["Cross Examination","Dying Deposition","Hostile witness","Re-examination"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Re-examination\\n\\nLeading questions are generally permitted in cross-examination and when dealing with a hostile witness, as the purpose is to test credibility and extract facts. They may also be allowed in certain circumstances like recording statements (e.g., dying deposition depending on context).\\n\\nHowever, in re-examination, leading questions are not permitted because the aim is only to clarify ambiguities arising from cross-examination, not to suggest answers.\\n\\nCore concept:\\n\\n• Cross-examination → leading questions allowed\\n• Hostile witness → leading questions allowed\\n• Re-examination → leading questions not allowed","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-94dd723ff7","n":182,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Match the Court in List I with the maximum sentencing power in List II.","options":["A-3, B-1, C-4, D-5, E-2","A-1, B-3, C-4, D-5, E-2","A-3, B-5, C-2, D-1, E-4","A-3, B-1, C-2, D-5, E-4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-3, B-1, C-4, D-5, E-2\\n\\nSentencing Powers of Criminal Courts (High Yield)\\n\\n| Court | Maximum punishment\\n\\n| ----- | ------------------\\n\\n| Supreme Court | Any punishment including death sentence\\n\\n| High Court | Any punishment including death sentence\\n\\n| Sessions Court | Any punishment; lowest court that can award death sentence (requires High Court confirmation)\\n\\n| Assistant Sessions Judge | Up to 10 years imprisonment\\n\\n| Chief Judicial Magistrate (CJM) | Up to 7 years imprisonment\\n\\n| Judicial Magistrate First Class (JMFC) | Up to 3 years imprisonment\\n\\n| Judicial Magistrate Second Class | Up to 1 year imprisonment","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-222fe3554e","n":183,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Match the firearm term (List I) with its correct description (List II).","options":["A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4","A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4\\n\\n| Term | High-yield fact\\n\\n| ---- | ---------------\\n\\n| A. Calibre | 2. Distance between two opposite lands of a rifled barrel\\n\\n| B. Gauge | 3. Number of spherical lead balls of bore diameter that can be made from 1 pound of lead\\n\\n| C. Helixometer | 1. Instrument used to examine rifling inside the barrel\\n\\n| D. Rifling | 4. Spiral grooves impart spin\\\\, increasing bullet stability and accuracy\\n\\nExam Pearls:\\n\\n• Higher gauge number → Smaller bore diameter.\\n• Rifled guns fire bullets; smooth-bore guns fire pellets (lead shots).","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-a1f4ea0d64","n":184,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Match the following forensic tests with their application in identification of blood stains and seminal stains. Choose the correct match:","options":["A-2, B-2, C-3, D-4, E-5","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4, E-5","A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4, E-5","A-2, B-2, C-3, D-5, E-4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-2, B-2, C-3, D-4, E-5\\n\\n• Kastle–Meyer test → presumptive test for blood (phenolphthalein reaction)\\n• Benzidine test → also presumptive test for blood (peroxidase activity)\\n• Takayama test → confirmatory microcrystal test for blood (hemochromogen crystals)\\n• Florence test → detects choline crystals in semen\\n• Acid phosphatase test → screening test for semen","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-9cd7a5abca","n":185,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Match the following poisons and their probable mechanism of action: A. Zine Chloride B. Chloral hydrate C. Quinine D. Potassium carbonate 1. Abortifacient 2. Irritant 3. Corrosive 4. Stupefying","options":["A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1","A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3","A-2. B-3. C-4, D-1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3\\n\\nA) Zinc chloride → Irritant (2): Zinc chloride acts as a chemical irritant, producing local tissue irritation and inflammation when ingested or applied.\\n\\nB) Chloral hydrate → Stupefying (4): Chloral hydrate is a sedative–hypnotic, causing CNS depression and hence classified as a stupefying poison.\\n\\nC) Quinine → Abortifacient (1): Quinine can stimulate uterine contractions and has been historically associated with abortion, hence classified as an abortifacient.\\n\\nD) Potassium carbonate (K₂CO₃) → Corrosive (3): Being a strong alkali, it causes tissue damage by liquefaction necrosis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6330fa95b0","n":186,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Match the following with the respective ages of fusion:","options":["A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4","A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3","A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2","A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3\\n\\nA) Medial end of clavicle → 21–25 years\\n\\nB) Sacrum as a single bone → 22–25 years\\n\\nC) Crista scapulae → 50 years\\n\\nD) Lambdoid suture → 45 years\\n\\nThe medial end of clavicle is the last epiphysis to fuse, completing around 21–25 years. The sacrum fuses into a single bone by early to mid-20s. Crusta scapulae is a degenerative age change, seen in older adults (\\\\~50 years). Lambdoid suture closure occurs late in adulthood (\\\\~45 years) and is only a supportive age indicator.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-02302b4cdd","n":187,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Match the following: Column A (Feature): 1. Patchy lividity 2. Fully developed lividity 3. Fixed lividity 4. Lividity does not shift on position change Column B (Time after death): a. < 1 hour b. 1–3 hours c. 6–8 hours d. 8–12 hours","options":["1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d","1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d","1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b","1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d\\n\\nPostmortem lividity:\\n\\n• Gravitational pooling of blood in the dependent parts of the body after death.\\n• After the heart stops, blood circulation stops → Gravity takes over → Blood settles in the lowest parts of the body.\\n\\nStepwise Development of Lividity:\\n\\n1. Patchy lividity\\n\\n• Begins around 30 minutes to 2 hours\\n• Appears as faint, mottled, non-confluent patches.\\n• Easily blanchable\\n• So early stage = patchy.\\n\\n2. Fully developed lividity\\n\\n• Around 6–8 hours\\n• Confluent, well-marked discoloration --> may shift slightly if body moved\\n• This is the “maximum intensity but not yet fixed” stage.\\n\\n3. Fixed lividity\\n\\n• Around 8–12 hours (sometimes up to 12–18 hrs depending on temperature)\\n• Hemolysis occurs, does not shift when body position is changed\\n• This is when lividity becomes permanent.\\n\\nEXTRA EDGE\\n\\nColour of lividity/postmoertem stasis:\\n\\n• Normal → Bluish-purple\\n• Cherry red → Carbon monoxide poisoning\\n• Bright pink →Hypothermia\\n• Chocolate brown → Methemoglobinemia\\n• Greenish → Putrefaction starting\\n\\nColor can hint at cause of death.**","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-760a42917f","n":188,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Match the type of bruise in List I with the most appropriate association in List II.","options":["A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2","A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2","A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2\\n\\nPatterned bruises reproduce the shape of the causative object and are of great medicolegal significance.\\n\\n| Patterned bruise | Characteristic association\\n\\n| ---------------- | --------------------------\\n\\n| Six-penny bruise | Coin-shaped bruise due to fingertip pressure; classically seen in manual throttling and battered baby syndrome\\n\\n| Butterfly bruise | Produced by pinching; commonly associated with child abuse\\n\\n| Tramline (Railway line) bruise | Caused by impact with a rod, lathi, cane, or stick, producing two parallel lines of bruising\\n\\n| Battle\'s sign | Ecchymosis over the mastoid region, suggestive of middle cranial fossa fracture\\n\\n• Raccoon eyes (Spectacle hematoma) → Anterior cranial fossa fracture","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-01a30af806","n":189,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"On opening the skull, the forensic pathologist identifies a soft, friable extradural collection of blood with a characteristic honeycomb appearance. The extradural hematoma described is most consistent with:","options":["Coagulopathy","Heat hematoma","Postmortem blunt force injury","Antemortem blunt force injury"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Heat hematoma\\n\\nA heat hematoma (burn hematoma) is a postmortem artefact seen in bodies exposed to intense heat, such as fire. It results from heat-induced rupture of dural venous channels and diploic veins, leading to the accumulation of blood in the extradural space.\\n\\nUnlike a traumatic extradural hematoma, a heat hematoma is typically soft, friable, and has a characteristic honeycomb appearance due to gas bubbles within the clot. Recognizing these features is essential to avoid misinterpreting the finding as evidence of head injury.\\n\\n| Feature | Heat Hematoma | Traumatic Extradural Hematoma\\n\\n| ------- | ------------- | -----------------------------\\n\\n| Appearance | Honeycomb | Smooth\\n\\n| Consistency | Soft, friable | Firm, jelly-like\\n\\n| Laterality | Often bilateral | Usually unilateral\\n\\n| Suture lines | May cross suture lines | Does not cross suture lines\\n\\n| Cause | Exposure to intense heat (rupture of venous channels) | Blunt trauma (usually rupture of the middle meningeal artery)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4fb1367997","n":190,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"One of the following is a sure sign of rape:","options":["Ruptured hymen in a girl aged 20 years","Presence of semen in the posterior fornix in a girl age 20 years","Presence of semen in the posterior fornix in a girl aged 16 years","Presence of semen in the posterior fornix in a married girl aged 18 years"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Presence of semen in the posterior fornix in a girl aged 16 years\\n\\nA “sure sign of rape” implies a situation where intercourse itself is legally rape irrespective of consent.\\n\\n• Age < 18 years → statutory rape\\n• Presence of semen confirms intercourse → hence rape is established\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nA. Ruptured hymen in a girl aged 20 years\\n\\n• Not specific → can occur due to non-sexual causes or consensual intercourse\\n\\nB. Semen in posterior fornix in a girl aged 20 years\\n\\n• Indicates intercourse only → may be consensual\\n\\nD. Semen in posterior fornix in a married girl aged 18 years\\n\\n• Age ≥ 18 → consent valid\\n• Marriage irrelevant here → not necessarily rape","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-17a571468e","n":191,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Postmortem hypostasis is best differentiated from a bruise by:","options":["Colour","Location","Failure to blanch on pressure","Cutting the skin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cutting the skin\\n\\nBRUISE (Contusion)\\n\\n-Blood extravasates (leaks out) into tissues due to trauma during life.\\n\\nBlunt force → capillary rupture → blood escapes into interstitial tissue.\\n\\nClassic features :\\n\\n✔️ Occurs before death\\n\\n✔️ Shows vital reaction\\n\\n✔️ Inflammatory cells present\\n\\n✔️ Hemoglobin breakdown over time (color changes: red → blue → green → yellow)\\n\\n✔️ Cannot be washed away\\n\\nON MICROSCOPY:\\n\\n• RBCs outside vessels\\n• Fibrin\\n• Leukocyte infiltration\\n• Tissue reaction\\n\\nLIVIDITY (Livor Mortis)\\n\\nPassive gravitational pooling of blood inside vessels after death.\\n\\nNo circulation → gravity pulls blood to dependent parts.\\n\\nClassic features:\\n\\n✔️ Occurs after death\\n\\n✔️ Blood remains inside vessels (early stage)\\n\\n✔️ No inflammatory response\\n\\n✔️ Blanches in early stage\\n\\n✔️ Distribution follows gravity\\n\\nMicroscopy (early stage ):\\n\\n• RBCs inside dilated vessels\\n• No extravasation\\n• No inflammation\\n\\nLate fixed lividity can sometimes look like a bruise because RBCs leak after hemolysis.\\n\\nBut difference:\\n\\n• No inflammation\\n• No macrophages\\n• No tissue repair reaction","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9f6b85f3a6","n":192,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Regarding inquests conducted in India, all of the following statements are correct except:","options":["Police inquest is conducted under Section 194 BNSS and remains the most commonly practiced form of inquest in India.","Magistrate\'s inquest is considered superior to police inquest and is conducted in selected medico-legal situations.","The Coroner\'s system continues to function in several Indian states alongside the Magistrate\'s inquest.","The Medical Examiner system is regarded as scientifically superior but is not followed in India."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. The Coroner\'s system continues to function in several Indian states alongside the Magistrate\'s inquest.\\n\\nIndia primarily follows the Police Inquest system, while the Magistrate\'s Inquest is reserved for specific circumstances. The Coroner and Medical Examiner systems are not followed in India.\\n\\nComparison of Inquest Systems\\n\\n| Feature | Police Inquest | Magistrate Inquest | Coroner\'s Inquest | Medical Examiner System\\n\\n| ------- | -------------- | ------------------ | ----------------- | -----------------------\\n\\n| Relevant provision | Section 174 CrPC/194 BNSS | Section 196 CrPC | No longer followed in India | Not followed in India\\n\\n| Conducted by | Investigation Officer (minimum rank: Head Constable) | Judicial/Executive Magistrate | Coroner | Qualified forensic pathologist\\n\\n| Status | Most common in India | Superior to police inquest | Abolished in India | Considered scientifically best\\n\\n| Used for | Most unnatural deaths | Custodial death, custodial rape, suspected dowry death, exhumation | Historical system | Developed countries","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bee6258612","n":193,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Regarding postmortem staining in poisoning, all are correctly matched, except:","options":["Carbon monoxide — Cherry-red hypostasis","Cyanide — Brick-red hypostasis","Hydrogen sulphide — Bluish-green hypostasis","Opiates — Pink hypostasis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Opiates — Pink hypostasis\\n\\nBlack hypostasis is characteristic of opiate poisoning. Pink hypostasis is seen in hypothermia.\\n\\n| Poison | Postmortem staining\\n\\n| ------ | -------------------\\n\\n| CO | Cherry red\\n\\n| Cyanide | Brick/Bright red\\n\\n| H₂S | Bluish green\\n\\n| Opiates | Black\\n\\n| Hypothermia | Pink","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d59bff7d77","n":194,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Regarding stab wounds, all of the following statements are correct except:","options":["A penetrating wound enters a body cavity but does not exit it","A perforating wound has both an entry and an exit wound","Determining the direction of injury is possible even in complete penetration of weapon","The amount of wound gaping depends on the width of the weapon"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. The amount of wound gaping depends on the width of the weapon\\n\\nThe gaping of stab and incised wounds is determined predominantly by the orientation of the wound relative to Langer\'s (cleavage) lines, not merely by the width of the weapon.\\n\\n• Wound perpendicular to Langer\'s lines → Maximum gaping\\n• Wound parallel to Langer\'s lines → Minimal gaping\\n\\nA hilt mark indicates complete penetration of the weapon and may help estimate the depth and direction of injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-282174b900","n":195,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Sameer was tried in a court in the case of Sanjay’s murder in the park. Sahil appeared in court and stated that he saw Sameer with an axe in his hand while passing the park. What is this evidence known as?","options":["Hostile","Indirect","Direct","Hearsay"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Indirect\\n\\nIndirect (circumstantial) evidence is evidence that does not directly prove the principal fact in issue but helps infer it. In this case, Sahil did not witness the murder of Sanjay; he only saw Sameer with an axe in the park. This fact alone does not directly establish that Sameer committed the murder, but it forms a circumstance that can link the accused to the crime when combined with other evidence.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nA. Direct: Direct evidence would be witnessing the actual act of murder.\\n\\nC. Hostile: No indication the witness turned against the prosecution.\\n\\nD. Hearsay: The statement is based on Sahil’s own observation, not on what he heard from others","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-510fc4856c","n":196,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Skull of a dead person was found during investigation. Police asked the relatives to bring the photo of deceased. Which method is being used for identification of dead body?","options":["Anthropometry test","Dactylography","Superimposition","DNA fingerprinting"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Superimposition\\n\\nSuperimposition is a forensic identification technique used when skeletal remains (especially skull) are compared with antemortem photographs.\\n\\n• The skull is superimposed over a photograph of the missing person\\n• Matching of facial landmarks (orbit, nasal aperture, jaw, teeth, contours) is assessed\\n• Helps determine whether the skull could belong to that individual\\n\\n• Key use:\\n\\n• When only skull or skeletal remains are available\\n• When photographs of the suspected individual are available\\n\\n• Limitations:\\n\\n• It is supportive, not confirmatory\\n• Cannot establish identity with absolute certainty (unlike DNA)\\n\\n• Other Options:\\n\\nOption A. Anthropometry → body measurements (obsolete)\\n\\nOption B. Dactylography → fingerprints (requires soft tissue)\\n\\nOption D. DNA fingerprinting → confirmatory but not based on photo comparison","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-071a58759d","n":197,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"The following ossification centres are present before birth except:","options":["Talus","Calcaneum","Mandible","Head of femur"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Head of femur\\n\\nMost primary ossification centres appear before birth, while certain secondary centres appear after birth.\\n\\n• The talus and calcaneum are among the earliest tarsal bones to ossify in utero (around 5th–6th fetal month).\\n• The mandible also begins ossification early in fetal life (around 6–8 weeks) from membrane bone.\\n• In contrast, the head of femur is a secondary ossification centre that appears after birth, usually around 1 year of age, making it the correct exception.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d39604cd3c","n":198,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"What is the cause of the lesion shown in the image below?","options":["Lightning strike","Electrocution","Marbling","Trickling down of acid"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lightning strike\\n\\nThe image shows Lichtenberg figures (ferning pattern)—a classic sign of lightning strike injury.\\n\\n• Caused by high-voltage atmospheric electricity\\n• Leads to transient capillary rupture and dilation in the skin\\n• Produces arborescent (tree-like) erythematous markings\\n• Key characteristics:\\n\\n• Not true burns (no tissue destruction)\\n• Transient → fade within hours to days\\n• Pathognomonic for lightning injury\\n\\n• Differentiation from others:\\n\\n• Electrocution (household current): entry & exit wounds, deep burns\\n• Marbling: postmortem decomposition change\\n• Acid injury: irregular corrosive burns, not branching pattern","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-ef91952ed2","n":199,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"What is the likely age of the patient?","options":["10 yr","13 yrs","15 yrs","18 yrs"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 13 yrs\\n\\nAge estimation from elbow X-ray is based on the appearance and fusion sequence of secondary ossification centers (CRITOE):\\n\\n• C – Capitellum (\\\\~1 year)\\n• R – Radial head (\\\\~3 years)\\n• I – Internal (medial) epicondyle (\\\\~5 years)\\n• T – Trochlea (\\\\~7 years)\\n• O – Olecranon (\\\\~9–10 years)\\n• E – External (lateral) epicondyle (\\\\~11–12 years)\\n\\nPresence of all CRITOE centers without fusion = \\\\~13 years\\n\\nFusion begins in mid–late adolescence (≈14–17 years)\\n\\nIn this case:\\n\\n• Most ossification centers are present but not fused\\n• Indicates early adolescence (\\\\~12–14 years)","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-4b0c2e15e0","n":200,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which additional finding regarding this sign is correct?","options":["It represents fragmentation of retinal vessels and appears within minutes","It develops only when the eyelids remain open and usually appears after 3–6 hours","It is an indicator of decomposition and develops after 24 hours","It is best appreciated after rigor mortis becomes generalized"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. It develops only when the eyelids remain open and usually appears after 3–6 hours\\n\\nEarly ocular postmortem changes help estimate time since death.\\n\\n| Sign | Site | Time since death\\n\\n| ---- | ---- | ----------------\\n\\n| Kevorkian (railway tracking) sign | Retina | Within minutes\\n\\n| Tache noire | Sclera | 3–6 hours (eyes open)\\n\\nHigh-Yield Pearl:\\n\\n• Kevorkian = Retina.\\n• Tache noire = Sclera + Open eyes.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-4af6ec148b","n":201,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following are Methanol poisoning derivatives?","options":["Formic acid + Oxalic acid","Formic acid + Lactic acid","Glycolic acid + Oxalic acid","Oxalic acid + Lactic acid"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Formic acid + Lactic acid\\n\\nMethanol is metabolized in the liver by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde, which is rapidly converted to formic acid—the principal toxic metabolite responsible for metabolic acidosis and visual toxicity. Methanol poisoning also leads to lactic acidosis due to tissue hypoxia and impaired oxidative phosphorylation. Hence, formic acid and lactic acidare the key derivatives seen in methanol toxicity.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nA. Formic acid + Oxalic acid: Oxalic acid is a metabolite of ethylene glycol, not methanol.\\n\\nC. Glycolic acid + Oxalic acid: Both are metabolites of ethylene glycol poisoning.\\n\\nD. Oxalic acid + Lactic acid: Oxalic acid again points to ethylene glycol, not methanol.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-173d1e958e","n":202,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following best explains the mechanism and key clinical feature of poisoning by this plant?","options":["Blocks glycine receptors in spinal cord → generalized spasms","Increases GABA action → profound CNS depression","Inhibits spinal cord → flaccid paralysis without convulsions","Causes uncontrolled ACh release → Muscarinic symptoms"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Inhibits spinal cord → flaccid paralysis without convulsions\\n\\nInhibits spinal cord → flaccid paralysis without convulsions\\n\\nThe plant shown is Yellow Jasmine (Gelsemium) — classically Gelsemium sempervirens.\\n\\nToxic Principle:\\n\\nContains gelsemine, an alkaloid with central inhibitory action, predominantly at the level of the spinal cord and medulla.\\n\\nMechanism of Toxicity:\\n\\n• Acts as a central nervous system depressant\\n\\n• Inhibits spinal motor neurons\\n\\n• Suppresses reflex activity\\n\\n• No convulsive phase\\n\\nIt produces a curare-like effect, but centrally mediated.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nA. Blocks glycine receptors → spasms → Seen in Strychnine poisoning\\n\\nB. Increases GABA → CNS depression → Sedative/hypnotic drugs\\n\\nD. Uncontrolled ACh release→ Organophosphate poisoning","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-adaa3bfa3b","n":203,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following features may be in favor of antemortem bruise? 1. Discoloration predominantly over the dependent parts. 2. Ill-defined margins that merge with the surrounding. 3. Deep reddish-black discoloration of subcutaneous tissues. 4. The surface appears swollen.","options":["2, 3, 4","1, 4","2, 3","1, 2, 3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 2, 3, 4\\n\\nAn antemortem bruise is produced during life when blood is extravasated into tissues due to trauma, accompanied by a vital reaction. Such bruises typically show ill-defined margins merging with surrounding tissues, deep reddish-black discoloration of subcutaneous tissues due to infiltrated blood, and the surface appears swollen because of associated inflammation and edema. These features indicate active circulation at the time of injury.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nDiscoloration predominantly over dependent parts (1): This is characteristic of postmortem lividity, not bruising.\\n\\nAny option including statement 1 is incorrect, as dependent discoloration reflects gravitational pooling after death, not trauma.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-357ccaa12c","n":204,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following is a classic pattern of injury seen in pedestrian children involved in road traffic accidents?","options":["Tailgating","Whiplash","Waddell’s Triad","Terrible Triad"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Waddell’s Triad\\n\\nWaddell’s Triad--> classic injury pattern in Child pedestrians hit by a car\\n\\n• Femur fracture (or long bone fracture of leg) – First impact from bumper\\n• Thoracic / abdominal injury – Child rolls onto hood of car\\n• Head injury – Child then hits windshield or ground\\n\\nWhy not others?\\n\\n• Whiplash → Seen in rear-end collisions (occupants, not pedestrians)\\n• Tailgating → Driving behavior, not injury pattern\\n• Terrible triad → Elbow injury pattern\\n\\nOnly Waddell’s triad describes pediatric pedestrian injury pattern.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-18e9a10ad8","n":205,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following is not a treatment option in alcohol withdrawal delirium?","options":["Lorazepam","Antipsychotics","Chlordiazepoxide","High calorie diet with multivitamins"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Antipsychotics\\n\\nAlcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens) is primarily due to CNS hyperexcitability from sudden alcohol cessation. The cornerstone of treatment is benzodiazepines (eg, lorazepam, chlordiazepoxide) which enhance GABA activity and prevent seizures and autonomic instability. Supportive care is essential, including a high-calorie diet with multivitamins, especially thiamine, to prevent Wernicke’s encephalopathy.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\n• Lorazepam: First-line treatment; safe in liver disease.\\n• Chlordiazepoxide: Long-acting benzodiazepine; commonly used.\\n• High-calorie diet with multivitamins: Important supportive therapy.\\n\\nAntipsychotics may worsen seizures and are not primary treatment; used only cautiously as adjuncts if severe agitation persists.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-23a39562f0","n":206,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following statements regarding the PG artefact is incorrect?","options":["It was first described by Prinsloo and Gordon.","It appears as hemorrhage over the posterior surface of the esophagus.","It is a definitive sign of death due to manual strangulation.","It results from postmortem leakage of blood from the esophageal venous plexus."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. It is a definitive sign of death due to manual strangulation.\\n\\nThe Prinsloo-Gordon artefact is a postmortem artefact that may simulate antemortem hemorrhage. It consists of hemorrhagic discoloration on the posterior wall of the esophagus, caused by postmortem leakage of blood from the esophageal venous plexus. Because it develops after death, it should not be interpreted as evidence of neck compression or manual strangulation.\\n\\nCareful correlation with other autopsy findings is essential to distinguish this artefact from true traumatic hemorrhage.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\n• Option A. Described by Prinsloo and Gordon: Correct. The artefact was originally described by Prinsloo and Gordon, after whom it is named.\\n• Option B. Hemorrhage on the posterior surface of the esophagus: Correct. The characteristic finding is a postmortem hemorrhagic discoloration involving the posterior esophageal wall.\\n• Option D. Caused by leakage of blood from the venous plexus after death: Correct. The lesion results from postmortem extravasation of blood from the esophageal venous plexus, making it an artefact rather than a true injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e3600014c6","n":207,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following statements regarding the Zsigmondy system of tooth notation is true?","options":["Uses a universal numbering from 1 to 32 for permanent teeth","Uses numbers 1-5 for primary teeth","Uses quadrant brackets (┘ └ ┐ ┌) with numbers 1–8 for permanent teeth","Is the same as the FDI two-digit system"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Uses quadrant brackets (┘ └ ┐ ┌) with numbers 1–8 for permanent teeth\\n\\nThe Zsigmondy system identifies teeth by dividing the mouth into four quadrants, represented by L-shaped brackets, and numbers 1–8 are assigned from the midline to the third molar for permanent teeth.\\n\\n• This system is widely used in forensic odontology because it clearly shows tooth position.\\n\\n• A. Uses a universal numbering from 1 to 32 for permanent teeth: Universal system uses numbers 1–32, not quadrant brackets\\n• B. Uses numbers 1-5 for primary teeth: Primary teeth in Zsigmondy use Roman numerals I–V, but this option is incomplete and misleading alone.\\n• D. Is the same as the FDI two-digit system: FDI uses a two-digit numeric code, not brackets.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":2}]},{"bankId":"qb-2dbc22bbb6","n":208,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of these steps is included in the management of snake bite? 1. Wash with water and spirit 2. Debride to avoid the spread 3. Kill the snake 4. Wound-site anti-venom administration 5. Immobilization 6. Apply a broad firm bandage","options":["1, 2, 3","1, 4, 5","5, 6","2, 5"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 5, 6\\n\\nThe cornerstone of snakebite first aid is immobilization of the affected limb and application of a broad, firm pressure bandage (pressure immobilization technique), especially useful for neurotoxic/elapid bites. These measures slow lymphatic spread of venom and reduce systemic absorption until definitive care is given.\\n\\n1. Wash with water and spirit: Spirit/alcohol is not recommended and may worsen tissue injury.\\n\\n2. Debride: Early debridement is contraindicated.\\n\\n3. Kill the snake: Unnecessary and dangerous.\\n\\n4. Wound-site antivenom: Antivenom is given IV, not locally.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e4f171a3e7","n":209,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which test pair is correctly matched for toxicology screening?","options":["Kastle–Meyer → Cyanide","Duquenois–Levine → Arsenic","Harrison &amp; Gilroy → Firearm residue","Coombs test → Mercury"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Harrison & Gilroy → Firearm residue\\n\\nThe Harrison and Gilroy test is used in forensic toxicology to detect lead residues, commonly applied in firearm discharge residue (FDR/GSR) analysis, especially from bullets and primer residues.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nA. Kastle–Meyer is a presumptive blood test.\\n\\nB. Duquenois–Levine is used for cannabis (marijuana) detection.\\n\\nD. Coombs test is an immunohematology test for autoimmune hemolytic anemia, not toxicology.\\n\\nHence, the correctly matched pair is Harrison & Gilroy → firearm/lead residues.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-99e30c1a3a","n":210,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which tooth resists decomposition the most and is commonly used for identification?","options":["Lower central incisor","Canine","First molar","Second molar"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Canine\\n\\nThe canine tooth is the most resistant to decomposition and is therefore most commonly used for forensic identification. Canines have the longest roots, thick enamel, and a robust crown, which allow them to withstand fire, trauma, and postmortem degradation better than other teeth. Their position in the dental arch also protects them from early loss.\\n\\nA. Lower central incisor: Small size and short root make it more prone to loss and destruction.\\n\\nC. First molar: Although large, it is more susceptible to caries and early postmortem damage.\\n\\nD. Second molar: Less robust than canines and often affected by decay or periodontal disease.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-36b1b915e1","n":211,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"While studying the x-ray of a child brought to the casualty, the doctor notices a \\"string of beads\\" appearance in the paravertebral region. Which of the following conditions is this finding most likely associated with?","options":["Hanging attempts","Battered baby","Munchausen syndrome","Sudden infant death syndrome"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Battered baby\\n\\nA “string of beads” appearance in the paravertebral region on X-ray --> refers to multiple healing rib fractures at different stages;\\n\\nespecially involving the posterior ribs near the costovertebral junctions.\\n\\nMechanism of injury : repeated squeezing or shaking, classically seen in battered baby syndrome (child abuse).\\n\\nThe posterior rib fractures occur due to --> forceful compression of the infant’s thorax.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\n• Hanging attempts: Show cervical spine or hyoid injuries, not rib beadings.\\n• Munchausen syndrome: A psychiatric condition, not a radiologic finding.\\n• Sudden infant death syndrome: Has no characteristic X-ray skeletal findings.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-2e3caa96d4","n":212,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"You are a forensic expert evaluating a gunshot wound on a victim brought into a district hospital. Using your expertise in ballistic injuries, you recognize these wound patterns as indicative of a certain range of firing distance. What is the most likely distance between the victim and the firearm based on the wound characteristics presented in the image?","options":["&lt;2 cm","5 cm-30 cm","2 m-4 m","30 cm-1 m"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 2 m-4 m\\n\\nThe image describes an entry wound of a shotgun injury with rat-hole appearance with some dispersion suggestive of a intermediate wound (2-4m)\\n\\nShotgun wounds - by range\\n\\n1. Contact shot (muzzle touching skin)\\n\\n• Single large irregular / stellate wound\\n• Due to gas expansion under skin\\n• Blackening, burning, singeing of hair\\n• Soot and powder inside wound track\\n• Muzzle imprint may be present\\n• No pellet dispersion (acts as one mass)\\n\\n2. Close range (< 1 m)\\n\\n• Single round/oval entry wound\\n• Blackening + tattooing present\\n• Wad may enter wound\\n• Margins inverted\\n\\n3. Near range (1–2 m)\\n\\n• Central main defect + surrounding satellite pellet holes \\"rat-hole\\"\\n• Beginning of pellet dispersion\\n• Tattooing may be present (less than close range)\\n• No burning\\n\\n4. Intermediate range (2–4 m)\\n\\n• Multiple separate pellet entry wound with a larger central single defect - as seen here\\n• Increasing dispersion with distance\\n• No blackening, burning, or tattooing\\n\\n5. Distant range (> 4 m)\\n\\n• Widely scattered multiple small pellet wounds\\n• Maximum dispersion\\n• Individual entry wounds","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-51fd86c342","n":213,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"You notice tearing of the skin at the ends of a stab with a kitchen knife on a victim. What is this finding known as?","options":["Sparrow’s foot","Wedging","Fish tails","Swallow tails"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Fish tails\\n\\nFish tailing refers to splitting or tearing of the skin at one or both ends of a stab wound, commonly seen when a single-edged knife (eg, kitchen knife) is used. It occurs due to movement of the weapon or victim, or when the blunt back of the knife stretches and tears the skin, producing a forked appearance. This helps in weapon characterization, suggesting a single-edged blade.\\n\\nA. Sparrow’s foot: Head or body forcefully impacting shattered, toughened (non-laminated) windshield glass during a vehicular collision.\\n\\nB. Wedging: Refers to tissue compression by blunt force.\\n\\nD. Swallow tails: Used for Laceration ends","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9521f541fa","n":214,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"A 24-year-old male was brought dead to the emergency after hanging. There was a dry, hard, parchment-like ligature mark 1.5 cm wide in the anterior middle third of the neck; the mark was directed posteriorly, upwards and laterally. The left eye was closed, while the right was open, and saliva drooling of saliva was present on the right side. Compression of which of the following structures explains the right eye opening?","options":["Right internal carotid artery (ICA)","Right internal jugular vein (IJV)","Cervical sympathetic chain","Right vagus nerve"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cervical sympathetic chain\\n\\nIn hanging, characteristic findings include asymmetry of eye opening and salivary dribbling, which help determine the side of compression.\\n\\n• Compression of the cervical sympathetic chain → loss of sympathetic supply on that side\\n• Leads to features of Horner’s syndrome:\\n\\n• Ptosis (eye closed)\\n• Miosis\\n• Anhidrosis\\n\\nApplication to the case:\\n\\n• Left eye closed → left sympathetic chain compressed\\n• Right eye open → right sympathetic chain intact\\n• Saliva dribbling on right side → indicates oblique ligature mark with pressure asymmetry\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• ICA: Cerebral ischemia, not eye-opening changes\\n• IJV: Venous congestion, cyanosis\\n• Vagus nerve: Affects heart rate/voice, not eyelid position","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-88221c1921","n":215,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"A dead body is discovered with multiple injuries consistent with a violent assault. Examination reveals one bruise on the right side of the neck and three linear bruises on the left side, along with curved, semilunar abrasions beneath the angle of the jaw and a fractured hyoid bone. Additional injuries are noted on the left thigh, back, scapula, pelvis, and shoulder, with associated fractures. Based on these findings, what is the most likely cause of death?","options":["Mugging","Throttling","Gagging","Ligature strangulation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Throttling\\n\\nThrottling (manual strangulation) is characterized by finger and thumb pressure marks on the neck, along with internal injuries such as hyoid bone fracture.\\n\\n• Asymmetrical bruises on neck:\\n\\n• Single bruise on one side (thumb)\\n• Multiple bruises on opposite side (fingers)\\n\\n• Semilunar (crescent-shaped) abrasions:\\n\\n• Caused by fingernails\\n\\n• Fractured hyoid bone:\\n\\n• Strong indicator of manual strangulation\\n\\nAssociated findings:\\n\\n• Signs of struggle/assault:\\n\\n• Injuries on thigh, back, scapula, pelvis, shoulder\\n\\n• May have congestion, petechiae, cyanosis\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• Ligature strangulation: Continuous ligature mark, not finger marks\\n• Gagging: No neck compression signs\\n• Mugging: Non-specific trauma, lacks classical neck findings","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-47c12e6108","n":216,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"A dead body shows the following finding as in the image. What is the case?","options":["Hanging","Gagging","Garrotting","Smothering"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Smothering\\n\\nSmothering is a form of asphyxial death caused by external obstruction of the mouth and nose, typically by a hand, cloth, or soft object.\\n\\n• Abrasions and bruises around mouth and nose\\n• Lacerations of lips and gums\\n• Teeth impressions on lips (bite marks)\\n• Blood-stained mouth due to struggle\\n• Possible nail marks on face\\n\\nMechanism:\\n\\n• Obstruction of external air passages → hypoxia → death\\n• Often associated with struggle, especially in homicidal cases\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Hanging: Ligature mark on neck\\n• Gagging: Obstruction inside mouth, not external compression\\n• Garroting: Ligature strangulation with force from behind","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-94e49e88a5","n":217,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"A man develops sudden difficulty breathing and is unable to speak while eating. Based on the image shown, the most appropriate immediate management is:","options":["Heimlich maneuver","Direct laryngoscopy and removal of the foreign body","Back blows followed by chest thrusts","Initiate cardiopulmonary resuscitation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Heimlich maneuver\\n\\nThe image shows the universal choking sign (hands clutching the neck), indicating severe foreign body airway obstruction (FBAO). In a conscious adult who cannot speak, cough, or breathe, the immediate management is the Heimlich maneuver (abdominal thrusts) to expel the obstructing object.\\n\\nThese findings indicate complete airway obstruction in a conscious adult. The first-line treatment is the Heimlich maneuver.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Direct laryngoscopy and removal of the foreign body: Performed only if basic life support maneuvers fail or in a hospital setting with a visible foreign body.\\n• C. Back blows followed by chest thrusts: Recommended for infants (<1 year), not conscious adults.\\n• D. Initiate cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR): Indicated only if the patient becomes unconscious.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-67a896c955","n":218,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"A man is found dead in a swimming pool. On autopsy, both the lungs and stomach are filled with water, and there is mucoid froth around the nose and mouth. What is the most likely mode of death in this case?","options":["Cardiac arrest","Dry drowning","Near drowning","Wet drowning"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Wet drowning\\n\\nWet drowning is characterized by aspiration of water into the lungs and stomach, along with fine, persistent froth at the mouth and nostrils.\\n\\n• Water enters airways → lungs fill with fluid → impaired gas exchange → hypoxia\\n• Swallowing of water → water present in stomach\\n• Froth formation:\\n\\n• Due to mixing of air + water + mucus\\n• Appears as fine, white, persistent (mucoid) froth\\n\\nAutopsy findings:\\n\\n• Heavy, waterlogged lungs\\n• Frothy fluid in airways\\n• Water in stomach\\n• May have diatoms in organs (if antemortem drowning)\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Dry drowning: No water in lungs (laryngospasm)\\n• Near drowning: Survival after drowning attempt\\n• Cardiac arrest: No typical drowning features","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2c30f124a5","n":219,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"A woman is discovered dead in a locked room with a heat stove burning. At autopsy, her body shows bright cherry-red discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes. Which of the following is the most likely cause of death?","options":["H2S poisoning","CO poisoning","Cyanide poisoning","Methane poisoning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. CO poisoning\\n\\nCarbon monoxide (CO) poisoning is characterized by bright cherry-red discoloration of skin and mucous membranes due to formation of carboxyhemoglobin.\\n\\n• CO binds hemoglobin with 200–250\xd7 greater affinity than oxygen\\n• Forms carboxyhemoglobin → impaired oxygen delivery → tissue hypoxia\\n• Common source:\\n\\n• Burning stoves in closed/poorly ventilated rooms\\n\\nClinical and autopsy features:\\n\\n• Cherry-red skin/lividity (classic but not always present)\\n• Headache, dizziness, confusion → coma → death\\n• Often occurs in enclosed spaces\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• H₂S (hydrogen sulfide): Rotten egg smell, greenish discoloration\\n• Cyanide: Bitter almond odor, also causes hypoxia but different mechanism\\n• Methane: Simple asphyxiant, no cherry-red color","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e7a6ad65e8","n":220,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"An Autopsy was conducted on a male with history of drowning. However, no water was found in his lungs, and he was determined to have experienced ‘dry drowning’. What is the cause of death:","options":["Asphyxia","Vagal inhibition","Laryngospasm","Ventricular fibrillation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Laryngospasm\\n\\nDry drowning occurs due to reflex laryngospasm, which prevents water from entering the lungs and leads to asphyxia without fluid in the airways.\\n\\n• Water entering upper airway → reflex closure of vocal cords (laryngospasm)\\n• Results in airway obstruction → hypoxia → death\\n• Hence, lungs may appear dry on autopsy\\n\\nTypes of drowning:\\n\\n• Wet drowning: Water present in lungs (common)\\n• Dry drowning: Due to laryngospasm, no water in lungs\\n• Immersion syndrome: Sudden death due to vagal inhibition\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Asphyxia: Final mechanism, but laryngospasm is the cause\\n• Vagal inhibition: Seen in sudden immersion (cold water), not typical drowning\\n• Ventricular fibrillation: Not primary mechanism here","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2f851d318c","n":221,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"Cadaveric spasm is seen in all except:","options":["Suicidal firearm wounds","Traumatic asphyxia","Drowning","Electrocution"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Traumatic asphyxia\\n\\nCadaveric spasm is an instantaneous stiffening of a group of voluntary muscles at the moment of death, without passing through primary relaxation.\\n\\n• Occurs in sudden, violent deaths with intense emotion or physical activity\\n• Commonly involves hands gripping objects (weapon, weeds, etc.)\\n• Helps reconstruct circumstances at time of death\\n\\nCommon situations where seen:\\n\\n• Suicidal firearm injuries → weapon tightly held\\n• Drowning → weeds/mud grasped in hands\\n• Electrocution → object firmly held due to tetanic contraction\\n\\nWhy not traumatic asphyxia:\\n\\n• Death is gradual due to compression of chest\\n• Lacks sudden intense neuromuscular activity required for cadaveric spasm","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ea5fdf928c","n":222,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"In a case of drowning, the following organism was isolated from the internal organs. What is true regarding this organism?","options":["It is a unicellular bacteria","Not resistant to acid","Only present in bone marrow","Outer wall contains silica and chlorophyll"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Outer wall contains silica and chlorophyll\\n\\nThe organism shown is a diatom, which is important in diagnosis of drowning.\\n\\n• Diatoms are unicellular algae, not bacteria\\n• They possess:\\n\\n• Silica-containing cell wall (frustule)\\n• Chlorophyll for photosynthesis\\n\\n• Have a characteristic symmetrical, glass-like appearance\\n\\nIn drowning: --> Water containing diatoms enters lungs → bloodstream → deposited in organs (bone marrow, liver, brain)\\n\\nPresence of diatoms in internal organs supports antemortem drowning\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Unicellular bacteria: Incorrect → diatoms are algae\\n• Not acid resistant: Silica wall makes them acid resistant\\n• Only in bone marrow: Found in multiple organs, not restricted","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-6b6018a976","n":223,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Asphyxia","text":"Which of the following is incorrect about heat exhaustion?","options":["Loss of thermoregulatory mechanism","Orthostatic hypotension is seen","Altered consciousness is seen","Core body temp is &lt;105\xb0F"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Loss of thermoregulatory mechanism\\n\\nHeat exhaustion is due to fluid and electrolyte depletion with intact thermoregulation, making option A (loss of thermoregulatory mechanism) incorrect.\\n\\n• Core temperature: Usually < 40\xb0C (104–105\xb0F)\\n• Mechanism: Thermoregulatory system is still functioning (sweating present)\\n• Clinical features:\\n\\n• Profuse sweating\\n• Orthostatic hypotension\\n• Weakness, dizziness\\n• May have mild confusion but no severe CNS dysfunction\\n\\nDifferentiation from heat stroke:\\n\\n• Heat exhaustion:\\n\\n• Thermoregulation intact\\n• Sweating present\\n• Mild/no altered consciousness\\n\\n• Heat stroke:\\n\\n• Failure of thermoregulation\\n• Core temp > 40\xb0C\\n• Severe CNS symptoms (delirium, coma)\\n• Often dry skin","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-17e13798d2","n":224,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Ballistics","text":"A firearm entry wound is shown below. What is the most likely range of firing?","options":["Close","Contact","Distant","Exit"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Distant\\n\\nA distant-range firearm entry wound is characterized by the presence of an abrasion collar (contusion collar) and a grease/wipe collar, with absence of burning, blackening (soot), and tattooing (powder stippling) because the flame, smoke, and unburnt powder particles do not reach the target at long distances.\\n\\nWhy is C correct?\\n\\n• Abrasion collar ✓ – Produced as the bullet stretches and abrades the skin while entering.\\n• Grease (wipe) collar ✓ – Formed by deposition of grease, dirt, lead, and lubricant from the bullet onto the wound margins.\\n• No tattooing or blackening ✓ – Indicates the muzzle was beyond the range of powder particle deposition, consistent with distant firing.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Close range: Tattooing, blackening, and sometimes burning are usually present.\\n• B. Contact range: Muzzle is in contact with the skin; features include muzzle imprint, searing, blackening within the wound, and possible stellate tears (especially over bony areas).\\n• D. Exit wound: Typically lacks an abrasion and grease collar and is often irregular with everted margins.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-289093120c","n":225,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Ballistics","text":"How much is the distance between the victim and the firearm from the gunshot entry based on the wound given below?","options":["Contact Entry","Close Contact","Distant Range","Near Range"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Near Range\\n\\nThe image shows a central entry wound surrounded by multiple punctate reddish-brown spots over the skin.\\n\\nKey features :\\n\\n• Central round defect → entry wound\\n• Surrounding peppering (stippling/tattooing) → due to unburnt powder particles\\n• Absence of black soot deposition → not very close/contact\\n\\nRange determination logic:\\n\\n| Feature | Interpretation\\n\\n| ------- | --------------\\n\\n| Tattooing (stippling) | Present → firearm discharged at intermediate distance\\n\\n| Soot/blackening | Absent → rules out contact/close contact\\n\\n| Burning/singeing | Absent\\n\\n| Wide dispersion of particles | Suggests some distance from muzzle","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-40c4f1bce1","n":226,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Ballistics","text":"Identify the range of the firearm wound:","options":["Close shot entry wound","Close shot exit wound","Distant shot entry wound","Distant shot exit wound"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Close shot entry wound\\n\\nA close-range firearm entry wound : Identified by the presence of burning, blackening (soot deposition) and tattooing around the entry site.\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Central circular entry wound with inverted margins\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Blackening due to soot, which can be wiped off\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Tattooing due to unburnt powder particles, which cannot be wiped off\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0 Possible burning or singeing of surrounding skin in very close shots\\n\\nThe combination of blackening and tattooing --> indicates a close-range discharge; confirms that the wound is an entry wound.\\n\\nRange of Firearm Injuries (Rifle)\\n\\n1. Contact Range\\n\\n• Muzzle in direct contact with skin\\n• Cruciate (star-shaped) wound due to gas expansion under skin\\n• Cherry-red coloration (carboxyhemoglobin formation)\\n• Blackening, burning, tattooing (BBT) present inside the wound track\\n\\n2. Close Range\\n\\n• Muzzle near but not touching skin\\n• Blackening, burning, tattooing (BBT) present externally\\n• Grease collar (GC) and abrasion collar (AC) well developed\\n\\n3. Near / Intermediate Range\\n\\n• Tattooing (stippling) present (unburnt powder particles)\\n• No burning or blackening\\n• Grease collar and abrasion collar present\\n\\n4. Distant Range\\n\\n• Only grease collar (GC) and abrasion collar (AC) seen\\n• No tattooing, blackening, or burning","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-aebd7587fc","n":227,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Ballistics","text":"Identify the source of trauma in the image below.","options":["Scissor","Rod","Axe","Bullet"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Bullet\\n\\nFirearm (bullet) injuries produce characteristic wound patterns that help identify the source and type of trauma. Key features of entry wound (as seen in image):\\n\\n• Small, circular/oval defect\\n• Inverted margins\\n• Surrounding abrasion collar (contusion ring)\\n• May show blackening/tattooing depending on range\\n\\nHigh-velocity projectile causes:\\n\\n• Penetrating injury\\n• Local tissue destruction\\n• Possible cavitation effect\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• Scissor: Incised/stab wound with clean margins\\n• Rod: Blunt trauma → contusions/lacerations\\n• Axe: Chop wound → deep, gaping with bone cuts","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-78333d0648","n":228,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Ballistics","text":"In a gunshot case, blackening is noted surrounding a firearm entry wound. Blackening occurs mainly due to:","options":["Heat fire effect","Muzzle of fireman weapon","Unburnt gunpowder particles","Black smoke particles"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Black smoke particles\\n\\nBlackening around a firearm entry wound is due to deposition of black smoke (soot) particles from the discharge of the weapon.\\n\\n• Occurs in close-range firing\\n• Soot consists of carbon particles produced during combustion of gunpowder\\n• Appears as black discoloration around the entry wound\\n• Can be wiped off (unlike tattooing)\\n\\nDifferentiation from other findings:\\n\\n• Tattooing (stippling):\\n\\n• Due to unburnt gunpowder particles\\n• Causes abrasions\\n• Cannot be wiped off\\n\\n• Burns: Due to flame/heat effect, not primary cause of blackening","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-4776eda6e8","n":229,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"A 14-year-old girl was kidnapped by a male. The police arrested him, but he died in police custody. The inquest is conducted by","options":["Superintendent of Police","Coroner","Medical examiner","Judicial Magistrate"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Judicial Magistrate\\n\\nBNSS 196 or section 176 Cr. P.C. deals with magistrate inquest in which the cause of death is not under natural circumstances. Magistrate inquest may be conducted by the District Magistrate, Sub-Divisional Magistrate, Tahsildar, or any other Executive Magistrate empowered by the State Government.\\n\\nIndications for Magistrate Inquest:\\n\\n* Death under police custody\\n\\n* Death due to police firing\\n\\n* Death in prison, reformatories, borstal schools\\n\\n* Dowry death\\n\\n* Death in a psychiatric hospital\\n\\n* Exhumation\\n\\n* Death or disappearance of any person, or rape of a woman who is under the custody of police or any other person authorized by the court.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-740ebd536a","n":230,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"A dead body was found with a wound on the neck, with clean-cut edges, crushed tissues along disruption of the vertebral bones. Identify the type of wound.","options":["Incised wound","Chop wound","Lacerated wound","Avulsion"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Chop wound\\n\\nThe given description of a wound with sharp edges accompanied by underlying tissue and bone injury is likely to be a chop wound.\\n\\nCHOP WOUNDS (Slash wounds)\xa0are\xa0deep gaping wounds caused by a blow with the sharp cutting edge of a fairly heavy weapon, like\xa0a hatchet, an axe, sword, broad heavy knife, chopper,\xa0saber, or meat cleaver.\\n\\n* The dimensions of the wound\xa0correspond to the\xa0crosssection of the penetrating portion of\xa0the blade.\\n\\n* The margins are sharp and may show slight\xa0abrasion and bruising with marked destruction of\xa0underlying organs.\\n\\n*\\n\\nMostly homicidal; maybe accidental due to power fans, bandsaws, or ship or airplane propellers; rarely suicidal.\\n\\n*\\n\\nThey are\xa0usually\xa0seen on the exposed portions of the body like\xa0the head, face, neck, shoulders, and extremities.\\n\\nChop wounds are so-called because of the action in which they are produced. Depending on the type of instrument, they may resemble an incised or a lacerated wound. The shape of the wound may give clues to the type of weapon.\\n\\nThe below image shows a chop wound inflicted on the scalp.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a4b529ff93b0e11e6a85.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-fbfa8017d1","n":231,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"A man is brought by his wife, as he calls other women, and achieves sexual gratification by talking obscenity and sharing obscene pictures. What does the patient most likely have?","options":["Frotterurism","Voyeurism","Scatologia","Coprophilia"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Scatologia\\n\\nTelephone and computer scatology-\xa0it involves obscene phone calling or sending of inappropriate\xa0videos/images to an unsuspecting person.\\n\\nParaphilias or perversions are sexual stimuli or acts that are deviations from normal sexual behaviors but are necessary for some people to experience arousal and orgasm.\\n\\nImportant paraphilic disorders are:\\n\\n* Exhibitionism- Urge to expose genitals to a stranger or unsuspecting person.\\n\\n* Fetishism- It is the use of objects for sexual gratification. Eg. shoes, gloves, pantyhose, and stockings. The objects can be used to attain gratification directly or the person may ask the partner to wear them during sexual intercourse.\\n\\n* Frotteurism-\xa0is usually characterized by a man\'s rubbing his penis against the buttocks or other body parts of a fully clothed woman to achieve orgasm.\\n\\n* Pedophilia- Defined as the presence of recurrent, intense sexual urges or fantasies involving children under 13 years of age, persisting for at least six months.\\n\\n* Masochism- takes its name from the activities of Leopold von Sacher-Masoch, a novelist whose characters derived sexual pleasure from being abused and dominated by women.\\n\\n* Sexual sadism- The person attains sexual arousal from the physical and psychosocial suffering\xa0of another person.\\n\\n* Voyeurism- It is also called scoptophilia. The person has an urge to watch an unsuspecting person naked or in sexual activity.\\n\\n* Transvestism-\xa0Fantasies and sexual urge to dress in opposite gender clothing.\\n\\n* Telephone and computer scatology-\xa0it involves obscene phone calling or sending of video images to an unsuspecting partner.\\n\\n* Necrophilia- it is an obsession with obtaining sexual gratification from dead bodies\\n\\n* Zoophilia-\xa0having an urge to involve in sexual activity like\xa0intercourse, masturbation, or oral-genital contact with an animal.\\n\\n* Coprophilia- Sexual pleasure associated with a desire to defecate on the partner or to be defected on.\\n\\n* Urophilia- the sexual pleasure associated with the desire to urinate on a partner or to be urinated on.\\n\\n* Hypoxyphilia-\xa0is the desire to achieve an altered state of consciousness secondary to hypoxia while experiencing orgasm. They may use volatile nitrite or nitrous oxide to produce hypoxia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-99160778e4","n":232,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"A man was found to be lying dead on the ground. His friend saw a snake passing in the area and took a picture of it and showed it to the doctor (image below). What is the most probable cause of death of this man?","options":["Shock","Respiratory failure","Coagulopathy","Renal failure"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Respiratory failure\\n\\nThe snake in the given image with single white bands on the body is a Common krait\xa0which is neurotoxic\xa0and the most probable cause of death in this patient is respiratory failure.\\n\\nBites of Elapidae (Cobras, Kraits, Mambas, Coral Snakes) present with a local bite reaction which is generally mild with minimal swelling, blistering or superficial necrosis. However\xa0their neurotoxic effects are prominent and early symptoms usually include vomiting, hypersalivation, headache, the heaviness of the eyelids, blurred vision, tingling sensation around the mouth, hyperacusis, dizziness, vertigo, and ‘gooseflesh’.\\n\\nPtosis\xa0is the earliest neuroparalytic manifestation followed\\n\\nby ophthalmoplegia.\\n\\nThis is followed by paralysis of the\xa0facial muscles, palate, jaws, tongue, vocal cords, neck\xa0muscles. Muscles of deglutition become progressively\xa0flaccidly paralyzed.\\n\\nA respiratory arrest may occur due to obstruction of the upper airway by the paralyzed tongue or inhaled vomitus, or due to paralysis of intercostal muscles and diaphragm.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A and C: Viper snake bite (hemotoxic) can lead to shock, haemorrhage, and consumptive coagulopathy.\\n\\nOption D: Sea snake bites (myotoxic) often result in death due to cardiac arrest caused by hyperkalemia from renal failure or due to respiratory failure.\\n\\nPearl PF1118: Mechanism of action of snake venom\\n\\nSnake\\nToxicity\\nMain features\\n\\nViperidae\\n\\n• Pit viper\\n• Saw-scaled viper\\n\\nVasculotoxic\\n\\nLocal reaction\\n\\n• severe\\n• edema and necrosis\\n\\nAnticoagulant effect - widespread bleeding\\n\\nElapidae\\n\\n• Cobra\\n• Krait\\n\\nNeurotoxic\\n\\nLocal reaction\\n\\n• mild\\n\\nNeurotoxicity leads to muscle paralysis\\n\\nHydrophidae\xa0(Sea snakes) are now considered subfamily of elapidae\\n\\nMyotoxic\\nGeneralized rhabdomyolysis\\n\\nSaw scaled viper:\\n\\nGreen pit viper:\\n\\nCommon krait:\\n\\nKing cobra:\\n\\nSea snake:\\n\\nPearl PF1119: Identification of Poisonous Snakes\\n\\nSnake\\nIdentification\\n\\nCommon Cobra\\nHooded with spectacle mark\\n\\nKing cobra\\nHooded without spectacle mark\\n\\nCommon krait\\nSingle/double white bands on body\\n\\nRussell viper\\n\\nThree rows of diamond shape black/brown spots.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/77f9c54a8401a758f4c6.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1be73a0369f6cab702c9.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9d63684e7e8287b4f11b.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/6e4b63ee06ac34bf5445.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/442ebbd19f09bd82e3a3.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0558772b077da8582ba7.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f36c181f3971d8787ba3.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f7319f35f3a1433b729c.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/47433fc3a315fecf5e2d.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/7ee51ce7170dcc75e6fe.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-7763dda43b","n":233,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"A patient was brought to the casualty with greenish color skin and rotten egg odor. The most probable poisoning could be ________.","options":["Carbon monoxide","Nitrite","Cyanide","Hydrogen sulphide"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Hydrogen sulphide\\n\\nHydrogen Sulphide poisoning causes Rotten egg odour and Bluish-green post mortem hypostasis.\\n\\nIt is almost always accidental,\xa0especially in sewer workers.\xa0It is often found in large quantities in sewers,\xa0cesspools, privy vaults and tannery vats.\\n\\nHydrogen sulphide in combination with C02\xa0and methane formed in sewers is known as SEWER GAS.\\n\\nH2 S interferes with cellular\xa0respiration by inhibiting the action of cytochrome\xa0oxidase.\\n\\nPearl PF1110: Characteristic odour of toxins\\n\\nToxins\\nOdour emitted\\n\\nArsenic, Phosphorous Malathion, Thallium,\xa0aluminium phosphide\\nGarlic odour\\n\\nCannabis\\nOdour of burnt rope\\n\\nCarbolic acid\\nPhenolic odour\\n\\nCyanide\\nSmell of bitter almonds\\n\\nEthyl alcohol\\nFruity odour\\n\\nHydrogen sulphide\\nRotten egg\\n\\nZinc phosphide\\nFishy / Garlicky odour\\n\\nKerosene, Organophosphorous-insecticides\\nSmell of kerosene\\n\\nPearl PF1096: Color of Postmortem Hypostasis in Various Conditions\\n\\nPoisoning/Condition\\nPostmortem staining\\n\\nCarbon monoxide\\n\\nCherry red\\n\\nCyanides\\n\\nBrick red/dark pink\\n\\nPotassium chlorate\\n\\nChocolate brown\\n\\nPhosphorous\\nDark brown\\n\\nNitrites\\n\\nRed brown\\n\\nHydrogen sulphide\\n\\nBluish green\\n\\nOpium\\nGrayish/black\\n\\nAsphyxia\\nDark purple\\n\\nHypothermia\\nBright pink\\n\\nSeptic abortion (Cl. Welchii infection)\\nBrown/pale bronze","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6409a46e78","n":234,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"During autopsy, the doctor tied the bronchus and immersed the lung in water to check whether it floats or sinks. This test is identified as ______ .","options":["Gettler\'s test","Ploucquet’s test","Hydrostatic test","Diatom\'s test"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Hydrostatic test\\n\\nHydrostatic test is done to determine whether the newborn has respired or not.\xa0It is also known as\xa0Floatation test/ Breslau’s first life test/ Raygat’s test.\\n\\nIt is based on the principle that the specific gravity of the unrespired lungs varies from 1.04 to 1.05 and that of \xa0the respired lungs is 0.94, because of the increase in volume due to inhalation of air.\\n\\nProcedure:\\n\\n* Put each lung separately into a jar of water, see whether\xa0it sinks or floats.\\n\\n* Cut each into 10 to 12 pieces observe the blood exuding.Test each of them for buoyancy.\\n\\n* Pick up the floating pieces, and press firmly under water.\xa0Observe the nature of bubbles streaming up. Note whether\xa0they continue to float or not.\\n\\nInterpretation:\\n\\n* If the lung pieces float in water, it means that respiration has taken place, indicating a live birth.\\n\\n* If the pieces sink, respiration has not taken place, which means it is a stillborn.\\n\\nA respired lung (live birth) may sink in water in the following conditions :\\n\\n* Lung edema\\n\\n* Lung collapse\\n\\n* Pneumonia\\n\\n* Congenital syphilis\\n\\nAn unrespired lung (still birth) may float due to gases of decomposition, as seen in putrefaction, or due to artificial inhalation.\\n\\nHydrostatic test is not necessary when:\\n\\n* The fetus is born before 180 days of gestation.\\n\\n* The fetus is a monster and thereby incapable of leading a separate existence.\\n\\n* The fetus shows signs of intra-uterine macerations.\\n\\n* The umbilical cord has separated and the umbilicus has cicatrised.\\n\\n* The stomach contains milk showing active digestive system.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nGettler\'s test\xa0is done for drowning. It estimates\xa0the chloride content of blood from both sides of the heart.\\n\\n* Normally, chloride content is equal in the right and left chambers of the heart (approx. 600 mg/100 ml).\\n\\n* In freshwater drowning, due to hemodilution, the chloride content is decreased on the left side of the heart by 50%.\\n\\n* In saltwater drowning due to hemoconcentration, the chloride content is increased in the left side of the heart by 30-40%.\\n\\nPloucquet’s test:\xa0Weight of lungs\xa0Before respiration:\xa01/70 of body weight, after respiration:\xa01/35 of body weight\\n\\nDiatom\'s test: Diatoms are a class of microscopic unicellular algae found suspended in most natural waters. The presence\xa0of diatoms in the brain or bone marrow indicates antemortem inhalation of water/ antemortem drowning. Diatoms are not found in the brain or bone marrow in case of dry drowning and when a dead body is thrown into the water.\xa0At the present time, the diatom test should be used only as an indicative aid and not as legal proof of drowning.\\n\\nPearl PF1099: Hydrostatic Test/Floatation Test/Raygat\'s Test\\n\\nIt is based on the lung changes in a foetus after respiration.\\n\\nPrinciple : Respired air remains in the lungs as residual air which cannot be removed after death. Hence lung floats in water.\\n\\nFalse negative test in:\\n\\n* Atelectasis\\n\\n* Pulmonary edema\\n\\n* Pneumonia\\n\\n* Congenital syphilis\\n\\n* Air absorbed from air passages if circulation continued for long time after cessation of respiration\\n\\n* More air exhaled from the lungs compared to air entering during inspiration\\n\\n* Obstruction by alveolar duct membrane\\n\\nFalse positive test in:\\n\\n* Putrefied foetus\\n\\n* Artificial inflation\\n\\nHydrostatic test is not required when\\n\\n* Fetus is monstrous\\n\\n* Foetus is macerated or mummified.\\n\\n* Foetus is born before 180 days of gestation.\\n\\n* Stomach contains milk.\\n\\n* Umbilical cord has separated and scar has been formed.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-372f65ef81","n":235,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Identify the fingerprint pattern shown in the image.","options":["Whorl","Composite","Loop","Arch"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Arch\\n\\nThe above fingerprint pattern shows an Arch.\\n\\nArches:\\n\\n* Plain arch – when it is wave-like (from one side of the\xa0finger to another side).\\n\\n* Tented arch – when the arch is sharp and spike-like. (as shown in the image above)\\n\\nClassification of fingerprint pattern:\\n\\n(1) Loops (about 60 to 70 percent),\\n\\n(a) radial,\\n\\n(b) ulnar.\\n\\n(2) Whorls (about 25 to 35\xa0percent),\\n\\n(a) concentric,\\n\\n(b) spiral,\\n\\n(c) double spiral,\\n\\n(d) almond-shaped.\\n\\n(3) Arches (about 6 to 7 percent).\\n\\n(a) plain,\\n\\n(b) tented,\\n\\n(c) exceptional.\\n\\n(4) Composite\xa0(about one to two percent).\\n\\n(a) central pocket loops,\\n\\n(b) lateral pocket loops,\\n\\n(c) twinned loops,\\n\\n(d)\xa0accidentals\\n\\nThe image below shows various fingerprint patterns.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/e1e3d5ad5450c4b37462.gif"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/c61717782dc802843aa1.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-667e2daabe","n":236,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Identify the type of abuse as shown in the image below.","options":["Bagging","Snorting","Sniffing","Huffing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Bagging\\n\\nThe above image shows bagging, a type of volatile substance abuse. It\xa0refers to inhaling and exhaling into\xa0a bag that has been filled with a small amount of a\xa0volatile substance.\\n\\nSniffing/snorting is done by\xa0inhaling directly from the neck of a container, such\xa0as jerry cans and petro-fillers.\\n\\nHuffing is the act of inhaling chemical vapours from a cloth soaked with a volatile substance, which is then held close to the nose and mouth to achieve an intoxicating effect.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/d8b8c895719362b3d7e4.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-1603707b56","n":237,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"When a victim of an unlawful act was dying, the police recorded the dying declaration. The doctor certifies that the victim was conscious and mentally oriented. The dying declaration will be considered invalid if?","options":["It is not done by the doctor","The victim is not dead","Declarant is not expecting death","The victim didn\'t sign the declaration"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. The victim is not dead\\n\\nIf the victim is not dead, then the dying declaration is invalid\xa0and the victim has to testify in court.\\n\\nDying declaration: It is a written\xa0or oral statement of a person, who is dying as\xa0a result of some unlawful act, relating to the\xa0material facts of the cause of his death or bearing on\xa0the circumstances.\\n\\nIn India, the dying declaration is accepted under IEA section 32 and BSA 26 (a).\\n\\nProcedure:\\n\\n* As soon as the severely injured person is brought to the hospital, the medical officer should inform the magistrate to have a dying declaration recorded.\\n\\n* The doctor can record it himself or herself if he thinks that the person will not survive till the arrival of the magistrate.\\n\\n* Treatment should not be delayed while recording dying declaration.\\n\\n* The doctor has to ascertain that the person is of sound mind (compos mentis).\\n\\n* If the person dies or becomes unconscious while recording the dying declaration, the doctor must record the information obtained up to that point.\\n\\n* Usually recorded in the forms of questions and answers in the patient\'s own language\\n\\n* No suggestions or leading questions\\n\\n* No outsider is allowed to be present\\n\\n* After completing the writing, it has to be read back to the patient who has to approve it by signing/thumb impression. The signature of two witnesses is also mandatory.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fc7a5a27a5","n":238,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A 28-year-old woman, recently married, was found dead at home. A suicide note was recovered, in which she mentioned that she was tortured for dowry by her in-laws. Under which section will the inquest be conducted? BNS - Bhartiya Nyaya Sanhita","options":["BNS Section 106","BNSS Section 194","BNS Sec 89","BNSS section 196"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. BNSS section 196\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a female who died due to suicide because of dowry pressure by in-laws\xa0suggests a possibility of dowry death. A magistrate\'s inquest is done under BNSS Section 196 (Section 176 CrPC).\\n\\nBNSS 196 (Section 176 CrPC) deals with magistrate\'s inquest in which the cause of death is not under natural circumstances. Magistrate inquest may be conducted by the District Magistrate, Sub-Divisional Magistrate, Tahsildar, or any other Executive Magistrate empowered by the State Government.\\n\\nIndications for Magistrate Inquest:\\n\\n* Dowry death\\n\\n* Custodial rape\\n\\n* Death in police custody or police interrogation\\n\\n* Death in police firing\\n\\n* Death in prison, reformatories, or Borstal school\\n\\n* Death in a psychiatric hospital\\n\\n* Cases of exhumation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Section 106 BNS (304A IPC) defines the punishment for causing the death of a person by any rash or negligent act, not amounting to culpable homicide, the punishment is imprisonment for up to 2 years and a fine.\\n\\nOption B: \xa0BNSS Section 194 (Section 174 CrPC) deals with the police inquest. It is done by suicide, homicide, killing by any animal or machinery or accident, and death under circumstances that raise suspicion of another person committing the offence.\\n\\nOption C: BNS Sec 89 (IPC 313) deals with the causation of miscarriage of a woman with a child (and/or quick with child) without her consent. Only the person causing the abortion is liable to punishment.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7385163ec4","n":239,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A 30-year-old apparently healthy man who was carrying laxatives and enema apparatus developed abdominal pain at the airport and an X-ray was done which appears as shown below. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Bezoar syndrome","Pica due to anemia","Body packer syndrome","Constipation due to fecolith"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Body packer syndrome\\n\\nThe given image shows multiple cylindrical capsules in the stomach and intestine consistent with a diagnosis of body packer syndrome.\\n\\nBody packers are individuals who swallow illegal costly drugs like heroin, cocaine with the purpose of smuggling. Usually, the drugs are carefully wrapped in latex or plastic and swallowed, to be excreted later on with the help of laxatives. Drugs are usually of similar radio-opacity as fecal material and may be difficult to detect on X-rays. CT scan may be done to aid in diagnosis.\\n\\nBody stuffer syndrome refers to the act of ingesting or inserting drugs into the rectum or vagina when a person fears being caught, typically to hide evidence of possession. Drug toxicity may be seen due to systemic absorption\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nBezoar formation is generally seen in mentally disturbed individuals due to the consumption of indigestible substances\\n\\nPica is eating things which are non-edible like chalk, mud, etc seen in patients of Iron deficiency anemia\\n\\nFecolith may not be seen in stomach like this and may not be perfectly shaped","img":false,"images":["/bimg/13f55e9df2fd5fb4014b.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-268fccb66b","n":240,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A case of a road traffic accident which occurred 20 mins ago was brought for autopsy. Postmortem examination reveals stiff neck, upper limbs, and lower limbs and primary relaxation is absent. What is this suggestive of?","options":["Rigor mortis","Heat stiffness","Cadaveric spasm","Cold stiffness"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cadaveric spasm\\n\\nSince the voluntary muscles are involved with no primary relaxation this is suggestive of cadaveric spasm.\\n\\nCadaveric spasm doesn’t indicate time since death. It only indicates the\xa0manner of death.\\n\\nCadaveric spasm/Instantaneous rigor/cataleptic rigidity/death clutch:\\n\\n* It is a phenomenon where a\xa0group of muscles that are in a state of contraction at the time of death remains contracted after death till molecular death occurs.\\n\\n* It occurs immediately after death. There is no stage of primary relaxation.\\n\\n* It is seen in cases of deaths coupled with emotional disturbances like in case of excitement, fear, severe pain, exhaustion at the time of death, cerebral hemorrhage, injury to the nervous system, drowning, strychnine poisoning.\\n\\n* It is an ante mortem phenomenon and it indicates the\xa0manner of death and not the time since death.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n* In case of drowning, weeds and grass caught in hand during struggle remain firmly grasped even after death. This indicates that the person was alive when entering the water.\\n\\n* In case of suicides, weapons remain firmly grasped in the hand.\\n\\nPearl PF1552: Difference between Cadaveric Rigidity (Rigor mortis) and Cadaveric Spasm (Instantaneous rigor)\\n\\nTrait\\nCadaveric rigidity\\nCadaveric spasm\\n\\nProduction\\nFreezing and exposure to temperature above 650C will produce rigor\\nCannot be produced by any method after death\\n\\nMechanism\\nReduction of ATP\\nNot clear\\n\\nPredisposing factors\\nNil\\nSudden death, excitement, fear etc\\n\\nTime of onset\\n1 – 2 hours after death\\nInstantaneous\\n\\nMuscles involved\\nAll muscles – both voluntary and involuntary\\nSingle group of voluntary muscles\\n\\nMuscle stiffening\\nNot marked: can be overcome by moderate force\\nMarked: Great force is required to overcome\\n\\nMolecular death\\nOccurs\\nDoesn\'t occur\\n\\nBody heat\\nCold\\nWarm\\n\\nElectrical stimuli\\nMuscles do not respond\\nMuscles respond\\n\\nMuscular reaction\\nAcidic\\nAlkaline\\n\\nMedicolegal importance\\nIndicates time of death\\nIndicates manner of death","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e950c11020","n":241,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A dead fetus was found in a water canal. The crown heel length was found to be 25 cm. What is the approximate age of the fetus?","options":["6 months","5 months","4 months","3 months"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 5 months\\n\\nThe Crown heel length of a 5-month-old fetus would be 25 cm.\\n\\nAccording to the Rule of Hasse:\\n\\n* During the first five months of pregnancy, the square root of the length gives the approx. age of the fetus in months.\\n\\n* During the last five months, the length in centimeters is divided by five gives the age in months.\\n\\nAge of fetus in first five months = square root of the crown heel length in cm.\\n\\nAge of the fetus during first 5 months of pregnancy = square root of the length\\n\\nSo, crown heel length =\xa0square root of 25 is 5, hence 5 months.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4d1b4fcd31","n":242,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A female faked pregnancy and still presents a child later to blackmail a man. What is this child called as?","options":["Supposititious child","Superfecundation","Posthumous child","Superfetation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Supposititious child\\n\\nThere is no real gestation or substitution of the child\xa0in the supposititious child or fictitious child.\\n\\nA woman may pretend pregnancy and\xa0delivery and later produce a living child as her own, or\xa0she may substitute a male child for a female child born\xa0of her, or for an abortion. This is done for obtaining\xa0money or for the purpose of claiming property. Other\xa0legal issues involved are blackmailing a man or\xa0bringing a charge of breach of the promise of marriage\xa0against a man.\\n\\nIn such cases, the woman should be\xa0examined for signs of pregnancy and delivery. The\xa0medical evidence is useful only when the age of the\xa0supposititious child does not correspond to the date\xa0of the pretended delivery. DNA fingerprinting will be\xa0conclusive.\\n\\nA posthumous child\xa0is a child born after the death of its father, the mother being conceived by the said father.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-84402c985e","n":243,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A gunshot case brought for autopsy shows an entry wound with inverted margins, surrounded by tattooing, but blackening and singeing of hairs is not noted. What is the most possible range of firing?","options":["Contact shot","Near shot","Close shot","Distant shot"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Near shot\\n\\nSince there is a wound with\xa0inverted margins, surrounded by tattooing and absence of\xa0blackening, singeing of hairs, the most likely range is of a near shot.\\n\\nCharacteristics\\nContact shot\\nClose Shot\\nNear Shot\\nDistant Shot\\n\\nRange\\nIn-touch with skin\\n2.5-7.5 cm\\n30-60 cm\\n>60 cm\\n\\nSize\\nLargest size\\nBullet size\\nSmaller\\nSmallest\\n\\nShape\\nStellate\\nCircular\\nCircular\\nCircular\\n\\nBlast effect\\n\\nMuzzle end imprint\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nEdge eversion\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nOther findings\\n\\nBlackening\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nTattooing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nSingeing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nAbrasion collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nGrease collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n-ve","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-50381efc04","n":244,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A person was found dead in the bushes with his hands tied. On examination of the body, there was cyanosis, petechial hemorrhages, abrasions, and bruises around the mouth and lips. There was associated facial congestion and edema. What is the least likely cause of death here?","options":["Asphyxia","Suicide","Smothering","Homicide"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Suicide\\n\\nThe least likely cause of death in this person is suicide\\n\\nThe presence of cyanosis, petechial haemorrhages, abrasions, bruises around the mouth and lips, facial congestion, and edema suggests a form of asphyxia or smothering. As the victim\'s hands are tied and there are signs of asphyxia and smothering, these findings are more consistent with homicide being the cause of death rather than self-inflicted suicide.\\n\\nClassic signs of asphyxia\\n\\n* Petechial haemorrhages\\n\\n* Congestion and edema\\n\\n* Cyanosis\\n\\n* Engorgement of the right side of the heart and abnormal fluidity of blood\\n\\nNote: The coexistence of these findings, in themselves, does not prove that death resulted from mechanical asphyxia. All these phenomena are non-specific and are in no way peculiar to this mode of death. They are of no value in proving that death resulted from mechanical asphyxia.\\n\\nSmothering:\xa0It is defined as a form of asphyxia that is caused by closing the external respiratory orifices either by the hand or by other means or blocking up the cavities of the nose and mouth by the introduction of a foreign substance.\\n\\nThey can be either :\\n\\n* Accidental smothering\\n\\n* Suicidal smothering\\n\\n* Homicidal smothering\\n\\nAccidental Smothering :\\n\\n* Most fatal smotherings are accidental\\n\\n* An epileptic or intoxicated person may smother himself accidentally by burying his face in a pillow that is impervious to air.\\n\\n* A person may accidentally fall into a large quantity of semisolid or finely divided material so that his mouth and nose are covered by the substance.\\n\\n* Children may be suffocated accidentally while playing with plastic bags\\n\\n* Smothering from plastic bags may occur due to the addictive habit of \\"glue-sniffing\\"\\n\\nSuicide by Smothering :\\n\\n* Suicidal smothering by hand is impossible\\n\\n* Suicide is possible by burying the face in a mattress or lying against the bed clothing to obstruct the nose and the mouth. It is usually seen in mental patients or prisoners.\\n\\n* Suicidal smothering can occur by tying a polythene\xa0bag over the head as well.\\n\\nHomicidal Smothering :\\n\\n* It is a simple and convenient method of asphyxial homicide that\xa0is extremely difficult to prove as it may not leave any evidence.\\n\\n* The mouth and nose are closed by a hand or cloth, or the face may be pressed into a pillow (gentle homicide).\\n\\nPetechial hemorrhages are rarely seen. Bruising of the inner surface of the lips may be seen if greater force is applied during smothering. At times, mucus and squamous respiratory epithelium from the victim may be found in the smothering material.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-dc8b0b97ae","n":245,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"An 18-year-old female was sexually assaulted by a 20-year-old male.Barberio\'s test was performed on the stains and showed yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals.Which of the following is detected by this test?","options":["Spermine","Acid phosphatase","Choline","Fructose"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Spermine\\n\\nBarberio\'s test\xa0which detects the presence of spermine\xa0,present in prostatic secretions and thus helps in the identification of seminal fluid.\\n\\nIn Barberio\'s test, a saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of picric acid is added to the spermatic fluid. This produces\xa0yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals of spermine picrate.\\n\\nOption B: Acid phosphatase test\xa0is a quantitative test for the presence of acid phosphatase enzyme.\xa0High levels of acid phosphatase from prostatic secretions\xa0are present in seminal stains (The normal vaginal secretions have low levels of acid phosphatase). Positive reactions are found till 36 hours after which the enzyme levels fall and disappear within 72 hours. In the absence of demonstrable sperms or aspermia, this test can be considered conclusive.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Florence test detects choline\xa0which is secreted by seminal vesicles. The suspected\xa0seminal stain is extracted with 10%\xa0hydrochloric\xa0acid and a drop of it is placed over a slide. A coverslip is placed over it and a drop of Florence solution (potassium iodide, iodine, and water) is made to run under the coverslip. If semen is present, dark brown rhombic or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide appear immediately.\xa0The test is not confirmatory for the presence of semen, however, a negative reaction rules out the presence of semen.\\n\\nOption D: Fructose acts as an energy source for sperm. Its presence in seminal fluid indicates a patent efferent ductal system, as fructose will be absent in its obstruction.\\n\\nThe absolute proof of semen is finding at least one unbroken spermatozoon or electrophoretic LDH isoenzyme detection of sperms.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9d7ea9d5f5","n":246,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"Identify the active toxin extracted from the poisonous plant in the image below.","options":["Calotropis gigantea","Digitalis","Ricin","1-hyoscyamine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1-hyoscyamine\\n\\nThe image shown of a cherry-like, toxic fruit\xa0is\xa0Atropa Belladonna\xa0and the active principle in it is\xa01-hyoscyamine.\\n\\nAtropa Belladonna acts by inhibiting the muscarinic effects of acetylcholine. It is a deliriant poison\xa0and all the parts of the plants are toxic.\\n\\nThe signs and symptoms, treatment and postmortem appearances are similar to datura.\xa0Clinical manifestations include fever, flushing, ileus, arrhythmia, tachycardia, urinary retention, decreased bowel sounds, choreoathetosis, myoclonus, visual and auditory hallucinations, convulsions, and coma. Agitation and delirium are the most serious problems in such patients. The pupils are often dilated and poorly reactive to light.\\n\\nAnticholinergic symptoms such as tachycardia, hypertension, flushing, dry mouth, mydriasis, and urinary retention are seen.\\n\\nDeliriant poisons are poisons that produce well-marked deliriant or confusional states, like Datura, Belladonna, Hyoscyamus, Cannabis, and Cocaine.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nIn the Italian language, ‘belladonna’ means a pretty woman. In ancient times, women used to instill drops containing atropine into their eyes for producing pupillary dilatation so as to make themselves appear more attractive.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Calotropis gigantea\xa0is an organic irritant. When ingested, it acts as a\xa0gastrointestinal\xa0and\xa0cerebrospinal poison.\\n\\nOption B: Digitalis is a cardioactive steroid that acts by inhibiting the sodium/potassium ATPase pump and is extracted from\xa0Digitalis purpurea\xa0which is also known as\xa0\'foxglove\'.Acute toxicity closely resembles that from digoxin and includes early GI symptoms followed by cardiac dysrhythmias.\\n\\nOption C: Ricin\xa0is derived from\xa0Ricinus communis in\xa0which the entire plant is poisonous but the\xa0seeds\xa0are the\xa0most poisonous. The poisonous component is the\xa0toxalbumin ricin. It is a glycoprotein that\xa0blocks\xa0protein synthesis.\\n\\nPearl PF1117: Plant poisons\\n\\nDatura stramonium (Jimsonweed) : Flower and fruit are poisonous\\n\\nRicinus communis : Seed is poisonous\\n\\nDigitalis purpurea\\n\\nAtropa belladonna\\n\\nCalotropis gigantea","img":false,"images":["/bimg/9ed33454dc132ba5dd21.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1de74a4c4a952ced57ff.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/58a03f47a52454763ff0.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f0bf974429167610f8a4.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/3b4c5057169384fcc406.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/e6d470cda4e32ccee210.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-7ed42ec608","n":247,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"Which IPC section is related to medical negligence?","options":["Sec 301/ BNS 102","Sec 304 B/ BNS 80","Sec 304 A/ BNS 106","Sec 302/ BNS 103"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sec 304 A/ BNS 106\\n\\nMedical negligence is related to IPC 304 A (Section 106 BNS).\\n\\nOffences affecting life\\n\\nIPC section\\n\\nBNS section\\n\\nSection it deals with\\n\\n299\\n\\n100\\n\\nCulpable homicide\\n\\n300\\n\\n101\\n\\nMurder (culpable homicide not amounting to murder is also explained under this section)\\n\\n301\\n\\n102\\n\\nCulpable homicide by causing death of person\xa0other than person whose death was intended\\n\\n302\\n\\n103\\n\\nPunishment for murder\\n\\n303\\n\\n104\\n\\nPunishment for murder by life convict (compulsory death sentence)\\n\\n304\\n\\n105\\n\\nPunishment for culpable homicide not amounting to murder\\n\\n304A\\n\\n106\\n\\nCausing death by negligence\\n\\n304B\\n\\n80\\n\\nDowry death\\n\\n305\\n\\n107\\n\\nAbetment of suicide of child or insane person\\n\\n306\\n\\n108\\n\\nAbetment of suicide—If any person commits suicide, whoever abets the commission of such suicide, shall be punished with imprisonment of either description for a term which may extend to ten years, and shall also be liable to fine.\\n\\n307\\n\\n109\\n\\nAttempt to Murder","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-718785251d","n":248,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"Which of the following bones is most accurate for sex determination?","options":["Skull","Pelvis","Long bones","Sternum"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pelvis\\n\\nThe whole pelvis is the most reliable bone for sex determination.\\n\\nKrogman’s degree of accuracy in sex determination from adult skeletal remains:\\n\\n* Entire skeleton – 100%\\n\\n* Pelvis alone – 95%\\n\\n* Skull alone – 90%\\n\\n* Pelvis plus skull – 98%\\n\\n* Long bones alone – 80-90%\\n\\n* Long bones and skull - 90-95%\\n\\n* Long bones and pelvis - >95%","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6a9dca70e7","n":249,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"A 24-yr-old man presents with an excessive desire for sexual intercourse. What is this called?","options":["Satyriasis","Frotteurism","Nymphomania","Masochism"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Satyriasis\\n\\nAn excess desire for sexual intercourse in males is called satyriasis.\\n\\nSatyriasis is also known as Don Juanism. In some men,\xa0sexual activity is a means to mask their deep feelings of inferiority. Some have unconscious homosexual impulses, which they deny by compulsive sexual contact with women. Many of them are no longer interested in the women after sex.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\n* Nymphomania: An\xa0excess desire for sexual intercourse in females\\n\\n* Frotteurism: Sexual gratification achieved by touching or rubbing against a nonconsenting person.\\n\\n* Masochism: The tendency to derive sexual gratification from one\'s own pain or humiliation.\\n\\nNote:\xa0Don Juanism represents hypersexuality in males.\\n\\nPearl PF1284: Sexual disorders\\n\\nPhase\\n\\nCharacteristics\\n\\nDisorder\\n\\nTreatment\\n\\nDesire\\n\\nFocuses on the patient\'s drives, motivation, and desires\\n\\nHypoactive sexual desire: Patients have a decrease or absence of sexual fantasies, desires, etc\\n\\nSexual aversion: A complete aversion to all sexual contact\\n\\nAddress issues like feelings of guilt, poor self-esteem, homosexual impulses, etc\\n\\nCouples therapy may be indicated if due to marital conflict\\n\\nArousal\\n\\nConsists of a sense of sexual pleasure with accompanying physiologic changes\\n\\nFemale sexual arousal disorder: Persistent failure to achieve or maintain sufficient sexual interest or arousal\xa0during the sexual act\\n\\nImpotence: Persistent or recurrent inability to attain or maintain an adequate erection until completion of the sexual act\\n\\nAddress issues like guilt, anxiety, and fear\\n\\nCheck for use of medications that cause vaginal dryness, like antihistamines or anticholinergics\\n\\nRelaxation techniques.\\n\\nDetermine if organic or psychological.\\n\\nConsider plethysmography or postage stamp test\\n\\nOrgasm\\n\\nPhysiologic state in which sexual tension is released and contractions are produced in various organs\\n\\nFemale orgasmic disorder and delayed ejaculation: Recurrent or persistent inability to achieve orgasm either through masturbation or sexual intercourse\\n\\nPremature ejaculation: Ejaculation before the man wishes to do so, before penetration or just after penetration\\n\\nAddress issues of guilt, fear of impregnation, etc\\n\\nUse vibrators, education, and fantasy\\n\\nBehavioral techniques such as squeeze and stop-and-go\\n\\nAddress issues of anxiety about the sexual act\\n\\nSSRIs to delay ejaculation\\n\\nPain\\n\\nSubjective sense of pain associated with the sexual act. Most likely due to dynamic factors\\n\\nGenito-pelvic pain disorder: Pain associated with sexual intercourse in either male or female. Not diagnosed when the organic cause has been found or if due to vaginal lubrication.\\n\\nPenetration disorder: Involuntary constriction of the outer one-third of the vagina that interferes with the sexual act.\\n\\nHelp with issues of anxiety and tension about the sexual act\\n\\nBehavioral techniques, such as the use of dilators and relaxation\\n\\nAddress issues of fear of impregnation, strict upbringing, religion, etc","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c2d68edf7b","n":250,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"A lucid interval is classically associated with which of the following?","options":["Subdural hemorrhage","Parenchymal hemorrhage","Extradural hemorrhage","Subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Extradural hemorrhage\\n\\nLucid interval is classically associated with Extradural (epidural) hemorrhage, although it may also be seen in a subdural hemorrhage.\\n\\nLucid interval:\\n\\n* ‘Lucid’ or ‘latent’ interval -The initial concussion is followed by a phase of recovery before sufficient blood accumulates to raise the intracranial pressure sufficiently for the patient to relapse into unconsciousness.\\n\\n* A lucid interval is a period of normal consciousness and here the person is legally bound by the laws that affect normal people.\\n\\n* The presence/absence of a lucid interval cannot be used as a diagnostic criterion for Extradural hemorrhage, as it has been found to be present only in 27% of cases.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ffa696549e","n":251,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"A patient was referred by a doctor to a radiologist for a CT scan and the doctor was given money for the referral. What is this unethical act called?","options":["Dichotomy","Medical maloccurence","Criminal negligence","Commission"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Dichotomy\\n\\nThis unethical act of receiving a commission for referring a patient is referred to as Dichotomy.\\n\\nDichotomy is an unethical act wherein a doctor receives a gift, commission or money for referring, recommending, or procuring a patient for any medical, surgical or other treatments. Fee splitting is unethical in India.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7ef1fd3dcd","n":252,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"A young teenage boy presents to the emergency department with a history of drug use at a party a couple of hours earlier. He reports symptoms including sweating, headache, anxiety, and hyperactivity. On examination, he has tachycardia and hypertension. Which of the following substances could he have ingested? LSD-lysergic acid diethylamide","options":["Alcohol","Cocaine","Opiod","LSD"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cocaine\\n\\nBased on the clinical scenario provided, the substance the boy may have ingested is likely cocaine.\\n\\nCocaine is an alkaloid deliriant extracted from erythroxylum coca.\\n\\nAcute intoxication with cocaine leads to symptoms spanning 2 stages :\\n\\n* Stage of excitement/euphoria - associated with hypervigilance, anxiety, hyperactivity along with hyper-adrenergic signs of mydriasis, tachycardia, elevated blood pressure, hyperthermia,\xa0and cardiac arrhythmias.\\n\\n* Stage of depression - associated with loss of reflexes, muscle paralysis, circulatory and respiratory failure, and coma.\\n\\nChronic intoxication is characterized by toxic delirium and tactile hallucination of bugs (cocaine bugs)\xa0or vermin crawling under the skin (formication).\\n\\nTreatment\xa0includes intravenous diazepam for controlling seizures. For ventricular arrhythmias, propranolol may be given intravenously. If the patient has psychosis, haloperidol may be given. Treatment of chronic cocaine abuse requires psychiatric support.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Shakes, hallucinations, and agitation are symptoms of alcohol withdrawal syndrome. They occur 12-24 hours following alcohol intake.\xa0Delirium tremens begin 72 to 96 hours after the last drink. It is due to the long-continued action of the poison on the brain. It occurs in chronic alcoholics due to temporary excess, sudden withdrawal of alcohol, shock after receiving an injury, such as fracture of a bone, or from acute infection, such as pneumonia, influenza, erysipelas, etc.\\n\\nOption C: \xa0Profuse sweating, diarrhea, and piloerection (gooseflesh) are symptoms of opioid withdrawal. They are associated with nausea, vomiting, lacrimation, rhinorrhea, muscle cramps, and generalized body aches. These symptoms manifest about 12-24 hours after the stoppage and recede within 7-10 days of last use.\\n\\nOption D: \xa0Synesthesia or seeing smells and hearing colors and flashback phenomenon (emotional and psychological experiences of the previous LSD) are features of lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD) intoxication. Other features include dry mouth, dilated pupils, tachycardia, hypertension, mood changes, visual hallucinations, alterations in time perceptions, vision, and hearing. Side effects are psychosis, depression, panic attacks, and schizophrenic episodes.\\n\\nPearl PF1338: Substances of abuse\\n\\nSubstance\\n\\nWithdrawal symptoms\\n\\nIntoxication features\\n\\nAlcohol\\n\\nTremors, delirium, seizures\\n\\nBlackouts, nystagmus and slurring of speech\\n\\nBZD\\n\\nTremors,\xa0insomnia, anxiety\\n\\nLethargy and drowsiness.\\n\\nOpioid/ Heroin\\n\\nDiarrhea,\xa0yawning, mydriasis,\xa0lacrimation, piloerection, cold turkey phenomenon\\n\\nEuphoria, depressed mental status, miosis, respiratory depression, constipation.\\n\\nNicotine/ Tobacco\\n\\nIncreased appetite\\n\\nShows a biphasic pattern.\\n\\nEarly stimulatory symptoms of vomiting, hypertension and tachycardia.\xa0Later depressive symptoms of hypotension and bradycardia.\\n\\nCannabis\\n\\nIrritability, sweating, tremors, decreased appetite\\n\\nIncreased appetite, euphoria, conjunctival injection, dry mouth,\xa0panic,\xa0synesthesia, impaired time perception, amotivational\xa0syndrome, run amok and\xa0flashbacks.\\n\\nAmphetamine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts, unpleasant dreams\\n\\nAcute:\xa0Sympathetic\xa0overactivity, paranoid hallucinatory syndrome, violent behavior, psychosis and diaphoresis.\\n\\nChronic: Runs & crashes, choreiform movements, tooth decay. Can resemble schizophrenia.\\n\\nCocaine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts\\n\\nAcute: Sympathetic overactivity, agitation, euphoria, chest pain, seizures and mydriasis\\n\\nChronic: Cocaine psychosis (Magnan sign) and black pigmentation of the tongue.\\n\\nLSD/ Haluccinogen\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nSynesthesia, brisk tendon reflex, tachycardia, hypertension, panic, ataxia, visual hallucinations, euphoria and dysphoria.\\n\\nPhencyclidine (PCP)\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nTachycardia, hypertension, ataxia, hallucinations, violent behavior, dissociation, amnesia and nystagmus.\\n\\nPearl PF1804: Plant poisons\\n\\nDatura stramonium (Jimsonweed) : Flower and fruit are poisonous\\n\\nRicinus communis : Seed is poisonous\\n\\nDigitalis purpurea\\n\\nAtropa belladonna\\n\\nCalotropis gigantea","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1de74a4c4a952ced57ff.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/58a03f47a52454763ff0.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f0bf974429167610f8a4.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/3b4c5057169384fcc406.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/e6d470cda4e32ccee210.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-bfc7bc604f","n":253,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Identify the odor produced by the change seen in the image below.","options":["Odorless","Pungent","Putrid","Offensive"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Odorless\\n\\nThe image shows a mummified body and it is odorless.\\n\\nMummification is a modification of putrefaction. Dehydration or drying and shrivelling of the cadaver occurs from water\'s evaporation, but the body\'s natural appearance and general facial features are preserved.. The mummified body is odourless. It is a late sign of death.\\n\\nIt begins in the exposed parts of the body like the face, hands, and feet. Then it extends to the entire body including the internal organs which may later disappear due to putrefaction and maggot activity. During the process of mummification, the whole body loses weight up to 60 to 70% and all the internal organs become a single mass.\\n\\nFactors necessary for the production of mummification are the absence of moisture in the air and the continuous action of dry or warm air. It occurs in bodies buried in shallow graves in dry sandy soils, where evaporation of body fluids is very rapid due to the hot dry winds in summer. Chronic arsenic or antimony poisoning favours the process. Differential decomposition is a condition in which some parts of the body show mummification and other parts show adipocere formation.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/440b660874f9aad79066.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-a59d6f5b0c","n":254,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Identify the plant shown in the image below.","options":["Parthenium hysterophorus","Datura stramonium","Digitalis purpurea","Calotropis gigantea"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Parthenium hysterophorus\\n\\nThe image shown is of the weed\xa0Parthenium hysterophorus.\\n\\nParthenium hysterophorus is an invasive weed that colonizes and causes environmental, agricultural, and medical hazards. They grow very quickly and colonize among other plants causing disruption of the ecosystem. The chemicals like parthenin, hysterin, hymenin, and ambrosin present in the plant, disrupt the growth of the surrounding crops.\\n\\nIn terms of the medical hazards, the pollens and other parts of these plants are shown to cause bronchitis, asthma, contact dermatitis, hay fever, and other allergies.\\n\\nThe contact dermatitis caused by it is called \'Parthenium dermatitis\'.\xa0An image of the most common type of\xa0\'Parthenium dermatitis\' called \'airborne contact dermatitis\' is shown below:\\n\\nParthenium is also believed to have some health benefits and is used by alternate systems of medicine in conditions like rheumatic pain, diarrhea, urinary tract infections, dysentery, malaria, and neuralgia.\\n\\nPearl PF1117: Plant poisons\\n\\nDatura stramonium (Jimsonweed) : Flower and fruit are poisonous\\n\\nRicinus communis : Seed is poisonous\\n\\nDigitalis purpurea\\n\\nAtropa belladonna\\n\\nCalotropis gigantea","img":false,"images":["/bimg/99845f20e9a0b7adfa1d.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/98cc3c37f6d2425caa07.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1de74a4c4a952ced57ff.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/58a03f47a52454763ff0.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f0bf974429167610f8a4.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/3b4c5057169384fcc406.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/e6d470cda4e32ccee210.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-7cf38a3b6b","n":255,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Identify the postmortem finding in a patient with a stab wound on the left side of the chest.","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Fat necrosis","Hemothorax","Pulmonary emobolism"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cardiac tamponade\\n\\nThe postmortem finding shows blood in the pericardium and it is a case of cardiac tamponade.\\n\\nCardiac tamponade\xa0is the accumulation of fluid in the pericardial space significant enough to cause an obstruction to the inflow of blood to the ventricles. This reduces cardiac output and arterial pressure causing hypotension.\\n\\nThey can be caused due to a variety of injuries like:\\n\\n* Crush injuries, like traffic accidents in which the wheels pass over the heart, cause rapid displacement of blood from the vessels into the chambers which increase the intracardiac pressure. The increased intracardiac pressure causes the heart to burst open and then the blood escapes into the pericardium.\\n\\n* Stab injuries, where the left or right ventricle is pierced which allow blood to escape through the myocardium into the pericardium. The blood in the pericardium causes external compression of the heart thereby reducing myocardial contractility, which is fatal. If the left ventricle is pierced then the thickness of the wall may restrict the bleeding allowing time for surgical management. However, a stab of the right ventricle is rapidly fatal as the rate of flow of blood into the pericardium is higher than its removal from the pericardial sac. Around 200 to 300 ml of blood can interfere with myocardial contractility and cause death.\\n\\n* Fractures of ribs or sternum,\xa0can cause minor injuries in which the victim may live for some hours or days.However, death may occur from shock, hemorrhage, hemothorax, or cardiac tamponade if it is prolonged or a major injury.\\n\\nIt is characterized by:\\n\\n* Hypotension.\\n\\n* Pulsus paradoxus.\\n\\n* Increased pulse rate.\\n\\n* Prominent X descent.\\n\\n* Muffled heart sound.\\n\\n* Ewart’s sign\xa0(bronchial breathing at left angle of scapula).\\n\\n* Beck’s triad: Falling BP, Raised JVP, Small quiet heart.\\n\\nInvestigation:\\n\\n* X-ray chest -\xa0Big globular heart.\\n\\n* ECG -\xa0Low voltage, Electrical alternans.\\n\\n* ECHO -\xa0Echo free zone around the heart.\\n\\n* Cath -\xa0Diastolic collapse of right atrium and right ventricle.\\n\\nTreatment: Drainage of fluid.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/c73d4f47c7f2b5a57f58.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-7a1b2d46f2","n":256,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Identify the test depicted in the image below that is conducted on the victim\'s clothing to detect blood stains.","options":["Barberio test","Florence test","Takayama test","Teichmann test"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Teichmann test\\n\\nThe test shown in the image below is the Teichmann test, which is used to detect blood stains. It shows brownish-black rhombic crystals of haemin or haematin chloride.\\n\\nTeichmann test (Haemin Crystal Test):\\n\\n* This test is used to detect suspected bloodstains.\\n\\n* A small piece of suspected material is placed on a glass slide. Then, 2-3 drops of glacial acetic acid and a small crystal of sodium chloride are added to the glass slide and covered with a coverslip. The slide is then heated.\\n\\n* If bloodstain is present, faint yellowish-red to brownish-black rhombic crystals of haemin or haematin chloride appear.\\n\\n* When hydrogen peroxide is added, the haematin crystals give bubbles of gas.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Barberio test: It is used to detect seminal fluid.\xa0When a saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of picric acid is added to the spermatic fluid, yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals of spermine picrate are produced.\\n\\nOption B: Florence test:\xa0This test is used to detect semen. The suspected\xa0seminal stain is extracted with 10%\xa0hydrochloric\xa0acid, and a drop of it is placed over a slide. A coverslip is placed over it, and a drop of Florence solution (potassium iodide, iodine, and water) is made to run under the coverslip. If semen is present, dark brown rhombic or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide appear immediately.\\n\\nOption C: Takayama test (Haemochromogen crystal test):\xa0This test is used to detect suspected bloodstains. A small piece of suspected material is added to a glass slide. Then, 2-3 drops of Takayama reagent are added and covered with a coverslip. If it is a bloodstain, pink feathery crystals of haemochromogen or reduced alkaline haematin appear in a few minutes.\\n\\nPearl PF0395: Tests to detect seminal and blood stains\\n\\nTests for seminal stains\\n\\n* Barberio\'s test\\n\\n* Florence test\\n\\n* Acid phosphatase test\\n\\n* Creatine phosphokinase test\\n\\n* Immunological test to detect Prostate Specific Antigen\\n\\nTests for blood stains\\n\\n* Benzidine test\\n\\n* Phenophthalein (Kastle Meyer) test\\n\\n* Takayama hemochromogen crystal test\\n\\n* Teichmann’s hemin crystal test\\n\\n* Spectroscopic test","img":false,"images":["/bimg/def6d9fc5a5d154ab728.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1316da77b2ca9080b03b.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-6eab0c33b9","n":257,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"In a case of drowning, the following finding is seen at autopsy. What is it called?","options":["Cadaveric spasm","Cutis anserina","Rigor Mortis","Washerwoman’s hands"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cadaveric spasm\\n\\nThe image shown above represents a firm hand grip on weeds and mud, found in a victim of drowning. It is indicative of the last action performed by the victim. This is an example of a cadaveric spasm.\\n\\nCadaveric spasm/Instantaneous rigor/cataleptic rigidity/death clutch:\\n\\n* It is a phenomenon where a\xa0group of muscles\xa0that are in a state of contraction at the time of death remains contracted after death till molecular death occurs.\\n\\n* It occurs immediately after death. There is\xa0no stage of primary relaxation.\\n\\n* It is seen in cases of\xa0deaths coupled with emotional disturbances\xa0like in case of excitement, fear, severe pain, exhaustion at the time of death, cerebral hemorrhage, injury to the nervous system, drowning, strychnine poisoning.\\n\\n* It is an\xa0ante mortem phenomenon\xa0and it indicates the\xa0manner of death and not the time since death.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Cutis anserina (goose-skin or goose-flesh), in which the skin has a granular and puckered appearance can be seen on the anterior surfaces of the body, especially on the thighs.\xa0 It is produced by the spasm of the erector pilae muscles, attached to each hair follicle, and can occur in the living when the skin comes in contact with cold water. It can occur on submersion of the body in cold water immediately after death, while the muscles were still warm and irritable. It can also be produced by rigor mortis of the erector muscles.\\n\\nOption C: Rigor mortis\xa0is a\xa0postmortem\xa0phenomenon indicative of molecular death. It is useful in estimating the\xa0time since death.\xa0The mechanism of rigor mortis is due to\xa0muscle ATP breakdown\xa0below the critical level.\xa0\xa0It appears 2-3 hours after death and is usually preceded by postmortem flaccidity. It involves both the voluntary and involuntary muscles of the body and persists for about 12-24 hours.\\n\\nOption D: Washerwoman’s hands\xa0is a specific change that is seen in the skin of the palm and sole, which comprises of wrinkled, swollen, whitish sodden appearance of\xa0hands or feet. It can also be considered as water maceration of skin of the hand, due to absorption\xa0of water into the outer layers of skin.\xa0Though the washerwomen’s hand and feet changes are usually seen in dead bodies recovered from water, this cannot be considered as a sign of antemortem drowning.\\n\\nPearl PF0229: Differentiating features between Antemortem amd Postmortem drowning\\n\\nAntemortem drowning\\nPostmortem drowning\\n\\nFoam/froth at nostrils or mouth\\nNot present\\n\\nCadaveric spasm\\nNot present\\n\\nLungs usually are voluminous, bulky, water-logged and over-inflated ,called as emphysema aquosum.\\nPassive collection of water\xa0 in the lungs without any formation of columns of froth,has been called as oedema aquosum.\\n\\nPresence of foam, fluid, debris, weeds, vegetation, etc. in the bronchi and bronchioles.\\nForeign matter may passively find access only to the upper respiratory passage.\\n\\nDemonstration of diatoms in the lungs, liver, brain, bone marrow, etc.\\nDiatoms may reach the lungs but not to the distant organs because of absence of circulation.\\n\\nStomach may contain water and foreign material like sand, weed, dirt etc which may be swallowed while struggling for life.\\nWater could enter the stomach. However, quantity of foreign matter may become the decisive issue.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/02cca75ddf17358a3232.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-5e94412259","n":258,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"What is the upper limit of Medical Termination of Pregnancy , according to the MTP Act of 2021?","options":["28 weeks","22 weeks","24 weeks","16 weeks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 24 weeks\\n\\nThe upper limit of MTP is 24 weeks.\\n\\nThe opinion of one doctor is sufficient for abortions up to 20 weeks. For abortions from 20-24 weeks, the opinions of 2 doctors are required.\\n\\nMedical\xa0termination of pregnancy\xa0is legal in India for therapeutic, eugenic, humanitarian, and eugenic reasons. It should be conducted only by registered medical practitioners in government hospitals, or other private hospitals that are recognized by the government. The registered medical practitioner should have assisted in conducting 25 cases of medical termination of pregnancy in a recognized hospital. If a person or hospital goes against this rule, they are liable to be punished rigorously for a period of 2 years, which may extend to 7 years.\\n\\nPearl PF1107: Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act - MTP Act 1971\\n\\nMTP Act 1971\\n\\nIndications :\\n\\n* Therapeutic : When continuation of pregnancy endangers the life of the woman, or may injure her physical or mental health.\\n\\n* Eugenic : The risk of child being born with serious mental or physical abnormality. This includes first trimester viral infections, maternal drug intake (thalidomide, cortisone, antimitotics), xray or radioisotope treatment of the mother, insanity of parents and viral hepatitis.\\n\\n* Humanitarian : Pregnancy resulted from rape.\\n\\n* Social : Pregnancy resulted from failure of contraceptives.\\n\\nRules :\\n\\n* Only a registered medical practitioner who is qualified (one who has assisted in performance of 25 cases of MTP in a recognized hospital) can terminate a pregnancy.\\n\\n* MTP should be carried out in a government or government recognised hospital.\\n\\n* Private hospitals may take up MTP if they obtain a license from the chief medical officer of the district.\\n\\n* The consent of the woman is required for conducting an abortion. The written consent of the guardian is required if the woman is a minor or mentally ill. Consent of the husband is not necessary.\\n\\n* If the woman is not willing, abortion can not be performed on the request of the husband alone.\\n\\n* The woman need not produce proof of her age. The statement of the woman that she is over 18 years of age is accepted.\\n\\n* It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint was lodged with the police.\\n\\n* Professional secrecy has to be maintained.\\n\\n* If the period of pregnancy is below 20 weeks, it can be terminated on the opinion of a single doctor.\\n\\n* If the period of pregnancy is between 20-24 weeks, two doctors must opine that there is an indication. Once there is an indication, the termination can be done by any one doctor.\\n\\n* In an emergency, the termination can be done by a single doctor, even without the required training, even after 20 weeks, without consulting a second doctor, in a private hospital which is not recognised.\\n\\n* The termination of pregnancy by a person, who is not a registered medical practitioner (the person concerned), or in an unrecognised hospital (administrative head) shall be punished with rigorous imprisonment for a term not less than 2 years, but which may extend to 7 years.\\n\\nConfidentiality :\\n\\nThe head of the hospital must maintain a register for recording the details of MTP. This register is a secret document which should be kept under safe custody for five years, and each patient is given a reference number. The name of the patient should not be entered anywhere, any case sheet or operation theatre register or any other record.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6d7c2a884b","n":259,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Which of the following is an abnormal method used for criminal abortion?","options":["Dilatation and curettage","Mefipristone","Abortion stick","Vacuum aspiration"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Abortion stick\\n\\nAbortion stick is an abnormal method used for criminal abortion among the given options.\\n\\nThis is a thin wood or bamboo stick that ranges in length from 12 to 18 cms. This stick is wrapped in cotton-wool or a piece of fabric at one end or for the majority of its length and soaked in marking nut juice, calotropis, jequirity, asafoetida, or paste made of arsenious oxide, arsenic sulphate, mercuric chloride, and red lead, among other things. Professional abortionists (dhais) insert it into the vaginal or uterine os and keep it there until uterine contractions begin.\\n\\nDilatation and curettage,\xa0Mefipristone,\xa0Vacuum aspiration are the routine medical methods used in legal abortion.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-122c215404","n":260,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Which of the following options describes the symptoms seen classically in a patient bitten by the snake shown in the image below?","options":["Non-poisonous, dry bite","Severe edema ,necrosis and bleeding","Severe muscle necrosis , rhabdomyolysis","Neurotoxicity and muscle paralysis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Neurotoxicity and muscle paralysis\\n\\nThe image given above shows a\xa0common krait\xa0. The venom of this snake is neurotoxic and can cause muscle paralysis. The local reaction is generally mild.\\n\\nThe neurotoxicity symptoms that would be seen in the patient include:\\n\\n* headache\\n\\n* blurred vision\\n\\n* perioral paraesthesia\\n\\n* hyperacusis\\n\\n* dizziness\\n\\n* vertigo\\n\\n* gooseflesh\\n\\n* Vomiting\\n\\n* Hypersalivation\\n\\nNeurotoxicity effects are completely reversible in response to antivenom or anticholinesterase or they may wear off spontaneously in about a week.\\n\\nPearl PF1118: Mechanism of action of snake venom\\n\\nSnake\\nToxicity\\nMain features\\n\\nViperidae\\n\\n• Pit viper\\n• Saw-scaled viper\\n\\nVasculotoxic\\n\\nLocal reaction\\n\\n• severe\\n• edema and necrosis\\n\\nAnticoagulant effect - widespread bleeding\\n\\nElapidae\\n\\n• Cobra\\n• Krait\\n\\nNeurotoxic\\n\\nLocal reaction\\n\\n• mild\\n\\nNeurotoxicity leads to muscle paralysis\\n\\nHydrophidae\xa0(Sea snakes) are now considered subfamily of elapidae\\n\\nMyotoxic\\nGeneralized rhabdomyolysis\\n\\nSaw scaled viper:\\n\\nGreen pit viper:\\n\\nCommon krait:\\n\\nKing cobra:\\n\\nSea snake:","img":false,"images":["/bimg/5c73ebe6e34f3d03291d.webp"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1be73a0369f6cab702c9.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9d63684e7e8287b4f11b.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/6e4b63ee06ac34bf5445.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/442ebbd19f09bd82e3a3.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0558772b077da8582ba7.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-d7d219c4ed","n":261,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"A 50-year-old factory worker was brought to the emergency room with complaints of headache, vomiting, and blurring of vision after he consumed local spirit. Which of the following is used for the treatment of his condition?","options":["Fomepizole","Flumazenil","N-acetyl cysteine","Naloxone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Fomepizole\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0methanol toxicity. Ethanol and fomepizole is used for the treatment of this condition.\\n\\nMethanol poisoning is caused due to ingestion of illicit liquor which contains ethanol adulterated with methanol. Methanol is metabolized to formaldehyde and formic acid by alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenases, respectively. The metabolites of ethanol are responsible for the toxic effects.\\n\\nClinically, patients present with headache, vomiting, abdomen pain, uneasiness, confusion, delirium, and coma. Formic acid specifically damages the retina and optic nerve and results in blurring of vision and blindness.\\n\\nElevated levels of formic acid and other organic acids result in\xa0metabolic acidosis in arterial blood gas analysis(ABG).\\n\\nTreatment of methanol poisoning includes gastric lavage with sodium bicarbonate if the patient is brought within 2 hours of ingesting methanol to combat acidosis. Ethanol infusion since ethanol is preferentially metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase over methanol and helps by reducing the rate of generation of formaldehyde and formic acid.\\n\\nFomepizole is a competitive inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase and the drug of choice for methanol poisoning. Folate therapy with calcium leucovorin reduces blood formate levels by enhancing its oxidation. Hemodialysis is used in severe cases of methanol toxicity.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Flumazenil is the inverse agonist of benzodiazepine receptors and is used for the treatment of benzodiazepine overdose.\\n\\nOption C: N-acetyl cysteine is used as an antidote for the treatment of paracetamol poisoning.\\n\\nOption D: Naloxone is a competitive opioid antagonist used for the treatment of opioid overdose.\\n\\nPearl PF1590: Specific antidotes for poisoning\\n\\nANTIDOTE\\nPOISONING\\n\\n4-methyl pyrazole\\nMethanol and ethylene glycol\\n\\n100% oxygen\\nCarbon monoxide, cyanide, methemoglobinemia\\n\\nAmmonium chloride\\nPhencyclidine, amphetamine and strychnine\\n\\nAmyl nitrate\\nCyanide, hydrogen sulphide\\n\\nAtropine\\nCarbamate and organophosphorus poisoning\\n\\nBupropion\\nNicotine\\n\\nCalcium disodium edetate\\nCadmium, chromium, cobalt, copper, lead, magnesium, nickel, uranium, zinc\\n\\nCalcium gluconate\\nPrecipitates fluorides, magnesium and oxalates\\n\\nChlorpromazine\\nAmphetamine\\n\\nDeferoxamine\\nIron, aluminium\\n\\nDiazepam\\nAmphetamine, chloroquine, alcohol withdrawal\\n\\nDicobalt edetate\\nCyanide\\n\\nDimercaprol (BAL)\\nAntimony, arsenic, copper, lead, mercury, nickel and gold\\n\\nDiphenhydramine\\nPhenothiazines and related drugs\\n\\nDisodium calcium edetate\\nLead\\n\\nDMPS *, (Unithiol)\\nMercury\\n\\nDMSA **\\nLead\\n\\nD-Penicillamine (Cuprimine)\\nCopper, lead, mercury, chromate, nickel, zinc, arsenic\\n\\nEthanol\\nMethanol and ethylene glycol\\n\\nFab fragment\\nDigoxin, digitoxin, oleander tea\\n\\nFlumazenil\\nBenzodiazepine\\n\\nGlucagon\\nPropranolol and other beta-blockers toxicity\\n\\nHydroxocobalamin\\nCyanide\\n\\nLabetalol hydrochloride\\nHypertensive crisis due to cocaine\\n\\nMethionine\\nParacetamol\\n\\nMethylene blue\\nMethemoglobinaemia\\n\\nN-acetylcysteine\\nParacetamol, acetaminophen toxicity\\n\\nNalorphine\\nMorphine\\n\\nNaloxone (Naltrexone)\\nNarcotic, opiates, CNS depressants\\n\\nNicotinamide\\nVacor poisoning, phenylurea pesticide toxicity\\n\\nPancuronium bromide\\nNeuromuscular blocking agents\\n\\nPenicillamine\\nCopper\\n\\nPhysostigmine salicylate\\nComa, convulsions from anticholinergics\\n\\nPralidoxime (2-PAM)\\nOrganophosphorus insecticides, nerve agents\\n\\nPropranolol\\nCocaine intoxication, beta adrenergics\\n\\nProtamine sulphate\\nHeparin overdose\\n\\nPrussian blue\\nThallium\\n\\nPyridoxine (Vit. B6)\\nIsoniazid, hydrazine toxicity\\n\\nSodium bicarbonate\\nUrinary alkalinisation for salicylates, phenobarbital\\n\\nSodium nitrite\\nCyanide\\n\\nSodium thiosulphate\\nCyanide\\n\\nVitamin K\\nWarfarin\\n\\nPearl PF1642: Certain specific clinical features diagnostic of poisons and poisoning\\n\\nClinical features\\nPossible causes\\n\\nPupillary constriction (Miosois)\\nBarbiturates, caffeine, carbamates, carbolic acid (phenol), clonidine, methyl dopa, nicotine, opiates, organophosphates, parasympathomimetics.\\n\\nPupillary dilatation (Mydriasis)\\nAlcohol (constricted in coma), amphetamines, antihistamines, benzodiazepines, carbon monoxide, cocaine, cyanide, datura (atropine), ephedrine\\n\\nHippus (alternate constriction and dilatation of pupils)\\nAconite, alcohol, barbiturates.\\n\\nNystagmus\\nAlcohol, barbiturates, carbamazepine, phencyclidine, phenytoin\\n\\nPinpoint pupils, and reduced respiratory rate\\nOpioids, cholinesterase inhibitors (organophosphorus or carbamate insecticides), clonidine, phenothiazines, Pontine hemorrhage\\n\\nCyanosis\\nAny CNS depressant or agent causing methemoglobinemia\\n\\nNeedle tracks, pinpoint pupils, and reduced respiratory rate\\nIV Opioids\\n\\nDilated pupils or mid-point pupils, and reduced respiratory rate\\nBenzodiazepines\\n\\nDilated pupils, tachycardia\\n\\n• Tricyclic antidepressants – dry mouth, warm peripheries, may also be twitchy or have seizures\\n• Amphetamines, ecstasy, cocaine – may also be hallucinating or agitated\\n• Anticholinergic drugs such as benzhexol, benztropine – may also have hyperreflexia and myoclonus\\n• Antihistamines – may also be drowsy\\n\\nIncreased salivation\\nOrganophosphorus or carbamate insecticides\\n\\nHypothermia\\n\\nLithium, tricyclic antidepressants, anticholinergics, antihistamines\\n\\nHyperthermia\\n\\n• Amphetamines, ecstasy, cocaine (also hypertension, tachycardia, agitation, rhabdomyolysis)\\n• Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (also confusion, fluctuating consciousness, rigidity, tremor, autonomic instability, sweating, rhabdomyolysis)\\n• Serotonin syndrome (also agitation, clonus, tremor, hyperreflexia, sweating, tachycardia)\\n• Salicylates including aspirin (also tachycardia, metabolic acidosis, restlessness, hyperventilation)\\n\\nHypertension\\nAmphetamine, ephedrine, cocaine, clonidine, MAO inhibitors, thyroid hormones\\n\\nHypotension\\nCyanide, alcohol, digitalis, carbon monoxide, narcotics, barbiturates\\n\\nAbdominal cramps, diarrhea, tachycardia, restlessness, hallucinations\\nWithdrawal from: Alcohol, benzodiazepines, opioids\\n\\nBradycardia\\nBeta-blockers, calcium antagonists (not dihydropyridines), digoxin, opioids, organophosphorus insecticides, centrally acting alpha agonists, e.g. clonidine\\n\\nSeizures\\nTricyclic antidepressants, theophylline, antihistamines, anticonvulsants, non-steroidal drugs, phenol, phenothiazines, isoniazid, cocaine, carbon monoxide, organophosphorus insecticides, strychnine","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5fdfe6035e","n":262,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"A public witness of the incident is asked to attend the court and record his evidence. Which type of evidence is provided by the witness to the court?","options":["Oral evidence","Hearsay evidence","Subpoena","Circumstantial evidence"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Oral evidence\\n\\nThe evidence\xa0provided by a public witness\xa0of the incident to the court is oral evidence.\\n\\nOral evidence is given by a person who has personal knowledge of the facts associated with a particular incident. It is the evidence given by an eyewitness. The oral evidence should be direct whenever possible according to Section 60 of the Indian Evidence Act(IEA). It includes all the statements made by the witness in relation to the inquiry which is permitted by the court.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Hearsay evidence is a type of evidence given by a person who has no personal knowledge of the facts in relation to the incident but only repeats what he heard others saying.\\n\\nOption C: Subpoena is also known as summons. It is an authoritative call to appear in court. It is a document compelling the attendance of a witness in a Court of law under penalty, on a particular day, time, and place, for the purpose of giving evidence. It is issued by the Court. Section 61 to 69 of CrPc deals with the summons.\\n\\nOption D: Circumstantial evidence\xa0is also known as indirect evidence.\xa0It is not the direct testimony of an eye witness but has a bearing upon the fact of the other and subsidiary facts which are relied upon as consistent. This type of evidence needs the Court of Law to make reasonable inferences based on the information presented.\\n\\nIPC section\\n\\nSection it deals with\\n\\n191\\n\\nGiving false evidence (perjury)\\n\\n192\\n\\nFabricating false evidence\\n\\n193\\n\\nPunishment for false evidence\\n\\n• Judicial proceedings: up to 7 yrs of imprisonment and fine\\n• Any other case: up to 3 yrs of imprisonment and fine\\n\\n196\\n\\nUsing evidence known to be false\\n\\n197\\n\\nIssuing or signing false certificate\\n\\n201\\n\\nCausing disappearance of evidence of offence or giving false information to screen offender","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4e43ece104","n":263,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"According to the law, nothing is an offence if a person is of an unsound mind and is not able to understand that the nature and consequences of his/her act are wrong. Which rule of insanity applies in this case?","options":["Durham\'s rule","Mc Naughten\'s rule","Curren\'s rule","Irresistible impulse rule"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Mc Naughten\'s rule\\n\\nMc Naughten’s rule is the correct answer. Mc Naughten’s rule\xa0states “Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who at the time of doing it by reason of unsoundness of mind is incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or that he is doing what is either wrong or contrary to law”.\\n\\n* The rules are:\\n\\n* McNaughten\'s rule\xa0states that every man is to be presumed to be sane, and possesses a sufficient degree of reason to be responsible for his crimes until the contrary is proved.\\n\\n* To establish\xa0an insanity defense, it must be clearly proved that, at the\xa0time of the committing of the act, the accused was laboring under ‘such a defect of reason, from a disease of the mind, as not to know the nature and quality of the act he was doing; or if he did know it, that he did not know he was doing what was wrong’.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A and C:\xa0Durham\'s rule\xa0and\xa0Curren\'s rule\xa0are the other tests for insanity.\\n\\nDurhams\'s rule\xa0states that an accused person is not criminally responsible if his unlawful act is the product of mental disease or mental defect.\\n\\nCurren’s rule\xa0states that an accused person is not criminally responsible if, at the time of committing the act, he did not have the capacity to regulate his conduct to the requirements of the law as a result of mental disease or defect.\\n\\nOption D: Irresistible impulse test- This rule is similar to Curren\'s rule.\\n\\nPearl PF1095: BNS Section 22, McNaughten\'s rule\\n\\nMcNaughten\'s rule, Section 22 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023: \xa0Criminal responsibility of the insane.\\n\\nIt states that an accused is not criminally responsible if he was suffering from:\\n\\n* Defect of reason and unsoundness of mind:\xa0the law\xa0recognizes only those diseases which impair the thinking abilities of the mind. So, not all medically insane people are legally insane, as emotional disturbances that affect the mind are not considered if they do not directly affect cognition.\\n\\n* That he did not know the nature and the quality of the act he is doing:\xa0\xa0the law needs the unsoundness of mind to exist at the time of the act to such a degree that the person is not aware of what he is doing and whether it is right or wrong to do. Absence of a motive or forethought is also considered as a criteria for determining unsoundness of mind.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c9788844bf","n":264,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"All of the following changes occur in chronic starvation except?","options":["Hyperthermia","Exhaustion","Heart atrophy","Hypotension"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Hyperthermia\\n\\nAll of the following changes occur in chronic starvation except hyperthermia. Hypothermia is seen in chronic starvation.\\n\\nThe changes that occur in chronic starvation are as follows: loss of well-being, hunger, rapid weight loss, easy fatigue, lethargy, cachexia, anemia, hypotension, hypothermia, and polyuria. Edema of the lower limbs is seen due to hypoproteinemia. There is increased susceptibility to infections.\\n\\nBlood sugar, cholesterol, protein, and chloride levels are decreased.\xa0Non-protein nitrogen and urea plasma free acids and ketone bodies are raised.\\n\\nDeath occurs due to exhaustion, brown atrophy of heart and intercurrent infection. Hypothermia and dehydartion may also play a role in\xa0 causing death.\\n\\nPostmortem examination of internal organs reveals:\\n\\nAtrophied and darker muscles, brown atrophy of the heart, atrophy of the stomach and intestines, shrunken spleen, atrophied kidneys and decreased blood volume suggestive of anemia.\xa0 The gall bladder may be distended with bile.\\n\\nPearl PF1956: Internal Findings of Starvation\\n\\n* General – Fat is absent from subcutaneous tissues and muscles.\\n\\n* Brain – is pale and soft with congested meningeal vessels (at times).\\n\\n* Heart – is smaller due to brown atrophy. Heart chambers are usually empty, pale and collapsed, and show hypostatic congestion.\\n\\n* Gastro intestinal tract: Stomach is small, contracted, and empty. Mucous membrane of stomach and upper part of intestine is stained with bile. Large intestine has hard fecal matter.\\n\\n* Gall bladder - is unusually distended. It contains dark and filled with dry and inspissated bile.\\n\\n* Liver - shows necrosis\\n\\n* Spleen, kidneys and pancreas\xa0are small and shrunken\\n\\n* Urinary bladder - is empty","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-78abd5222f","n":265,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Following the recovery of a beheaded skull bone by the police, the relatives of a missing person want to confirm his identity. Which of the following techniques can be used to figure out a person\'s identity using the beheaded skull bone and photograph of the person?","options":["Superimposition","Anthropometry","DNA fingerprinting","Cheiloscopy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Superimposition\\n\\nThe technique used to\xa0figure out a person\'s identity using the beheaded skull bone and photograph of the person is superimposition.\\n\\nThe superimposition\xa0technique is applied to the skull to determine whether it belongs to the person in the photograph. The following points can be used for comparisons such as the eyes, nasion, prosthion, zygomas, lower border of the nose, supraorbital ridges, lower border of the zygomas, angle of the jaw, external auditory meatus, teeth and the lower border of the upper jaw.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Anthropometry is based on the principle that the skeletal dimensions do not change after the age of 21 years and the variation in the size of the different parts to one another. Descriptive data such as the color of the hair, eyes, complexion, and body marks are recorded. Body measurements are estimated such as height, anteroposterior diameter of head and trunk, the span of outstretched arms, the length of the left middle finger, left little finger, left forearm, left foot, length, and breadth of right ear, and color of left iris from the photographs.\\n\\nOption C: DNA fingerprinting\xa0for forensic analysis is done to detect DNA from small amounts of samples from the crime scene.\xa0DNA fingerprinting\xa0uses restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLPs) or Polymerase chain reaction(PCR) to identify a person\'s\xa0unique pattern\xa0of\xa0DNA.\\n\\nOption D: Cheiloscopy is also known as lip printing. Here, the fissures and grooves on the lip characteristic of a person are examined.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f47202d018","n":266,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Identify the type of homicide caused by smothering and traumatic asphyxia.","options":["Mugging","Garroting","Burking","Bansdola"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Burking\\n\\nBurking is a type of homicidal asphyxial death comprising of smothering and traumatic asphyxia. In burking, the assaulter throws the victim to the ground and kneels or sits on the chest of the victim preventing chest movements. He/she then covers the nose and mouth of the victim with his/her hands.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Mugging is a type of strangulation caused by holding the neck of the victim\xa0in the crook of the elbow.\xa0 Pressure is then exerted either on the front of the larynx or at one or both sides by the forearm and upper arm. In mugging, the attack is\xa0usually made from behind. Death occurs due to reflex cardiac arrest or asphyxia.\\n\\nThe below image show strangulation by mugging.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Garrotting\xa0involves attacking the victim from\xa0behind\xa0using a\xa0ligature. The ligature is thrown around the unaware victim’s neck and then tightened by twisting it with a lever such as a rod or stick within the ligature. Earlier in Spain, garroting was an official method of judicial execution.\\n\\nOption D: In bansdola, one strong bamboo or stick is placed across the back of the neck and another across the front. The victim is squeezed to death by tieing both ends of the stick with rope.\\n\\nOn examination, a bruise can be seen in the centre of the neck across the windpipe corresponding to the width of the stick or the substance used. A similar mark can be seen on the back of the neck if two sticks are used.\\n\\nSometimes two sticks are used across the back and front of the upper part of the chest and squeezed forcibly. This causes laceration of the muscles, and rib fractures and interferes with respiration.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/7ff262ff9f922fd3a181.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/2c4f891727b02527760e.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-8d67673282","n":267,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"In which poisoning is cherry red hypostasis seen on postmortem examination?","options":["CO Poisoning","Hydrogen sulphide","Yellow phosphorus","Hydrogen cyanide"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. CO Poisoning\\n\\nCherry red hypostasis seen on postmortem examination is seen in\xa0CO poisoning.\\n\\nPostmortem appearance in CO poisoning:\\n\\n* Bright cherry red discoloration of the skin, mucosa, blood, tissues, and internal organs.\\n\\n* Congestion of the lungs with pink fluid blood.\\n\\n* Pleural and pericardial hemorrhages are commonly seen.\\n\\n* Bilateral symmetrical necrosis and cavitation of basal ganglia (globus pallidus and putamen) is the most characteristic feature of delayed deaths.\\n\\n* Punctiform and ring form hemorrhages in the white matter of the brain.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: In hydrogen sulfide poisoning, postmortem hypostasis is bluish-green in color.\\n\\nOption C: In yellow phosphorus poisoning, postmortem hypostasis is dark brown or yellow in color\\n\\nOption D: In hydrogen cyanide poisoning, postmortem hypostasis is bright red in color.\\n\\nPearl PF1096: Color of Postmortem Hypostasis in Various Conditions\\n\\nPoisoning/Condition\\nPostmortem staining\\n\\nCarbon monoxide\\n\\nCherry red\\n\\nCyanides\\n\\nBrick red/dark pink\\n\\nPotassium chlorate\\n\\nChocolate brown\\n\\nPhosphorous\\nDark brown\\n\\nNitrites\\n\\nRed brown\\n\\nHydrogen sulphide\\n\\nBluish green\\n\\nOpium\\nGrayish/black\\n\\nAsphyxia\\nDark purple\\n\\nHypothermia\\nBright pink\\n\\nSeptic abortion (Cl. Welchii infection)\\nBrown/pale bronze\\n\\nPearl PF1116: Phosphorus poisoning\\n\\nFeatures\\n\\nPhosphorus\\n\\nChemistry\\n\\n2 forms - White and Red Phosphorus\\n\\nWhite Phosphorus is toxic, Red Phosphorus is not.\\n\\nMechanism of action\\nProtoplasmic irritant poison\\n\\nOrgans affected\\n\\nLiver\\n\\nBone\\n\\nKidneys\\n\\nCharacteristic clinical findings\\n\\nSmoky stool syndrome\\n\\nGarlicky odor\\n\\nLiver necrosis\\n\\nPhossy jaw\\n\\nTreatment\\n\\nSymptomatic therapy","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-06ea5ed277","n":268,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Inquest in a case of custodial death is handled by _______","options":["Police inspector","Jail superintendent","Superintendent of police","Executive magistrate"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Executive magistrate\\n\\nAn inquest in a case of custodial death is handled by the Executive Magistrate.\\n\\nMagistrate inquest\xa0(Section 196 BNSS) is an inquest conducted by a district magistrate, subdivisional magistrate, and magistrate of the first-class rank or any other magistrate as empowered by the state government, such as collector, deputy collector or Tehsildar (executive magistrate).\\n\\nIt is conducted in cases of:\\n\\n* Death under police custody\\n\\n* Death due to police firing\\n\\n* Death in prison, reformatories, borstal schools\\n\\n* Dowry death\\n\\n* Death in a psychiatric hospital\\n\\n* Exhumation\\n\\n* Death or disappearance of any person, or rape of a woman who is under the custody of police or any other person authorized by the court.\\n\\nIn the above-mentioned scenarios, a Magistrate can conduct the inquest independently or in addition to the police inquest.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7a1fd38bde","n":269,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Postmortem examination of a dead body reveals contraction of the hands with absent primary relaxation. What is this suggestive of?","options":["Rigor mortis","Livor mortis","Cadaveric spasm","Algor mortis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cadaveric spasm\\n\\nPostmortem examination of a dead body with contraction of the hands and the\xa0absence of primary\xa0relaxation is suggestive of cadaveric spasm.\\n\\nCadaveric spasm/Instantaneous rigor is a phenomenon where a\xa0group of muscles that are in a state of contraction at the time of death remains contracted after death till molecular death occurs. It occurs immediately after death. There is no stage of primary relaxation.\\n\\nIt is most commonly seen in voluntary muscles, especially hands. It is seen in cases of death associated with emotional disturbances like cases of excitement, fear, severe pain, and exhaustion at the time of death. It is an antemortem phenomenon and it indicates the\xa0manner of death and not the time since death.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Rigor mortis (cadaveric rigidity or death stiffening) refers to the\xa0stiffening\xa0and\xa0shortening\xa0of\xa0muscles following death.\xa0The order of appearance of rigor mortis: Myocardium\xa0of the heart is affected first.\xa0Nysten\'s rule\xa0describes the sequential onset of rigor mortis in the various muscle groups. It starts in the eyelids, neck, lower jaw, and muscles of the face and progresses downwards to involve the muscles of the chest, upper limbs, abdomen, and lower limbs. The fingers and toes are affected last.\\n\\nOption B: Livor mortis/Postmortem hypostasis/Postmortem staining/Postmortem lividity/Suggilation\xa0refers to bluish-purple or reddish-purple discoloration\xa0of the superficial layers of the dermis.\xa0It is an\xa0early\xa0postmortem change that occurs due to\xa0the\xa0stagnation of blood\xa0in the\xa0vessels of the\xa0dependent parts\xa0due to the effect of gravity.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Algor mortis/postmortem cooling\xa0is one of the early changes occurring after death. The body loses heat mainly by conduction and convection after death.\\n\\nPearl PF1552: Difference between Cadaveric Rigidity (Rigor mortis) and Cadaveric Spasm (Instantaneous rigor)\\n\\nTrait\\nCadaveric rigidity\\nCadaveric spasm\\n\\nProduction\\nFreezing and exposure to temperature above 650C will produce rigor\\nCannot be produced by any method after death\\n\\nMechanism\\nReduction of ATP\\nNot clear\\n\\nPredisposing factors\\nNil\\nSudden death, excitement, fear etc\\n\\nTime of onset\\n1 – 2 hours after death\\nInstantaneous\\n\\nMuscles involved\\nAll muscles – both voluntary and involuntary\\nSingle group of voluntary muscles\\n\\nMuscle stiffening\\nNot marked: can be overcome by moderate force\\nMarked: Great force is required to overcome\\n\\nMolecular death\\nOccurs\\nDoesn\'t occur\\n\\nBody heat\\nCold\\nWarm\\n\\nElectrical stimuli\\nMuscles do not respond\\nMuscles respond\\n\\nMuscular reaction\\nAcidic\\nAlkaline\\n\\nMedicolegal importance\\nIndicates time of death\\nIndicates manner of death","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/3e379dba48d861412ab3.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-43acd45a22","n":270,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Study of death in all aspects is known as _________________","options":["Thanatology","Forensic anthropology","Toxicology","Molecular death"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Thanatology\\n\\nThanatology is the study of death in all aspects.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Forensic anthropology is the study of physical anthropology for forensic purposes that deal with the identification and analysis of skeletonized remains known to be or suspected of a human being.\\n\\nOption C: Forensic toxicology deals with medical and legal aspects of toxic chemicals on human beings.\\n\\nOption D: Molecular death is the death of cells and tissues individually after death. It usually occurs after 1-2 hours of death. The changes are due to metabolic dysfunction and structural disintegration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8f5204e96c","n":271,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Which of the following is not a feature of the stage of excitement in acute cocaine poisoning?","options":["Feeling of well-being","Cocaine bugs","Tingling and numbness in hands and feet","Dilated pupils , rapid pulse and sweating."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cocaine bugs\\n\\nCocaine bugs are not a feature of the stage of excitement in acute cocaine poisoning.\\n\\nCocaine is obtained from the leaves of Erythroxylum coca. Cocaine can be administered\xa0by\xa0snorting,\xa0intravenous route,\xa0or\xa0smoking.\\n\\nThe manifestations of cocaine intoxication occur in two stages- the\xa0stage of excitement and the stage of depression.\\n\\nIn the stage of excitement, the features seen are dry mouth, dysphagia, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypertension,\xa0dilated pupils, and\xa0increased temperature. There may be a feeling of well-being, and the patient may be excited, restless, and talkative. This progresses into a calm, dull state.\xa0Neurological manifestations include\xa0exaggerated reflexes, tremors or convulsions, hallucinations, tingling, and\xa0numbness in the hands and feet.\\n\\nThe stage of depression is characterized by feeble respiration, excessive sweating, collapse, convulsions, and death.\\n\\nChronic cocaine users may have the feeling of insects crawling on their skin or the presence of grains of sand under the skin giving rise to an itching sensation. This is known as\xa0cocaine bugs or Magnan\'s symptom. Ulceration of the nasal septum may be seen in chronic abusers who sniff cocaine.\\n\\nTreatment includes gastric lavage with warm water containing potassium permanganate, charcoal, or tannic acid if taken\xa0orally. When it is through the nose or throat, a\xa0wash with water is done. In\xa0case of intravenous intoxication, a\xa0ligature is applied above the site of the injection.\\n\\nThe antidote for cocaine intoxication is inhalational amyl nitrite.\xa0Other measures include control of convulsions, airway, and circulatory stabilization, administration of IV thiamine, and naloxone.\\n\\nPearl PF0409: Common names of Drugs of abuse\\n\\nDrug of abuse\\nCommon name\\n\\nCocaine\\nWhite lady, Coke, Blow, Snow\\n\\nCannabis\\nWeed, Pot, Reefer, Ganja, Mary Jane, Hash, Indian hemp\\n\\nHeroin\\nSmack, Dope, Brown sugar, Snowball\\n\\nKetamine\\nVitamin K, Special K, Super K\\n\\nLSD\\nAcid\\n\\nMethamphetamine\\nIce, Meth, Crystal,\\n\\nMDMA\\nEcstasy\\n\\nPhencyclidine\\nAngel dust\\n\\nForms of cannabis:\\n\\nBhang:\xa0Decoction prepared from dried leaves and fruit shoot\\n\\nMajoon:\xa0Sweet prepared from bhang\\n\\nGanja:\xa0Prepared from flower tops of female plant and smoked\\n\\nHashish/Charas:\xa0Most potent; resin exudate from flowering heads that\'s smoked\\n\\nPearl PF0408: Substances of abuse\\n\\nSubstance\\n\\nWithdrawal symptoms\\n\\nIntoxication features\\n\\nAlcohol\\n\\nTremors, delirium, seizures\\n\\nBlackouts, nystagmus and slurring of speech\\n\\nBZD\\n\\nTremors,\xa0insomnia, anxiety\\n\\nLethargy and drowsiness.\\n\\nOpioid/ Heroin\\n\\nDiarrhea,\xa0yawning, mydriasis,\xa0lacrimation, piloerection, cold turkey phenomenon\\n\\nEuphoria, depressed mental status, miosis, respiratory depression, constipation.\\n\\nNicotine/ Tobacco\\n\\nIncreased appetite\\n\\nShows a biphasic pattern.\\n\\nEarly stimulatory symptoms of vomiting, hypertension and tachycardia.\xa0Later depressive symptoms of hypotension and bradycardia.\\n\\nCannabis\\n\\nIrritability, sweating, tremors, decreased appetite\\n\\nIncreased appetite, euphoria, conjunctival injection, dry mouth,\xa0panic,\xa0synesthesia, impaired time perception, amotivational\xa0syndrome, run amok and\xa0flashbacks.\\n\\nAmphetamine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts, unpleasant dreams\\n\\nAcute:\xa0Sympathetic\xa0overactivity, paranoid hallucinatory syndrome, violent behavior, psychosis and diaphoresis.\\n\\nChronic: Runs & crashes, choreiform movements, tooth decay. Can resemble schizophrenia.\\n\\nCocaine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts\\n\\nAcute: Sympathetic overactivity, agitation, euphoria, chest pain, seizures and mydriasis\\n\\nChronic: Cocaine psychosis (Magnan sign) and black pigmentation of the tongue.\\n\\nLSD/ Haluccinogen\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nSynesthesia, brisk tendon reflex, tachycardia, hypertension, panic, ataxia, visual hallucinations, euphoria and dysphoria.\\n\\nPhencyclidine (PCP)\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nTachycardia, hypertension, ataxia, hallucinations, violent behavior, dissociation, amnesia and nystagmus.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6e1be2ca2c","n":272,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Which of the following tests cannot be used to detect blood stains?","options":["Barberio\'s test","Takayama\'s test","Teichman\'s test","Spectroscopy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Barberio\'s test\\n\\nBarberio\'s test\xa0is not used for detecting blood stains. It helps in the identification of semen.\\n\\nBarberio\'s test is used to detect the presence of spermine picrate, a component of prostatic secretion. A drop of the stain of spermatic fluid taken in a glass slide is charged with a saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of\xa0picric acid. Under microscopic examination, the\xa0needle-shaped yellowish\xa0spermine picrate crystals\xa0indicate the presence of semen.\\n\\nOption B: Takayama\'s test- It is also known as the hemochromogen crystal test. It is a more reliable test to detect old blood stains. 2-3\xa0drops of Takayama\'s reagent are added to the suspected material on the glass slide. A positive test result is indicated by the presence of pink feathery crystals of reduced hemochromogen or reduced alkaline hematin in clusters.\\n\\nOption C: Teichman\'s test- This test is also used to detect blood stains. It is also called as hemin crystal test. A small crystal of sodium chloride and glacial acetic acid is used for the test. Faint yellowish-red to brownish crystals of hemin or hematin chloride in clusters are seen if the suspected material is stained with blood. Teichman\'s test may be negative if the blood stain is old.\\n\\nOption D: Spectroscopy-\xa0\xa0It is the most reliable test to detect the presence of both old and recent blood stains. Characteristic dark bands are produced depending on the type of blood pigment when the blood solution is examined under a spectroscope.\\n\\nPearl PF1864: Tests to detect seminal and blood stains\\n\\nTests for seminal stains\\n\\n* Barberio\'s test\\n\\n* Florence test\\n\\n* Acid phosphatase test\\n\\n* Creatine phosphokinase test\\n\\n* Immunological test to detect Prostate Specific Antigen\\n\\nTests for blood stains\\n\\n* Benzidine test\\n\\n* Phenophthalein (Kastle Meyer) test\\n\\n* Takayama hemochromogen crystal test\\n\\n* Teichmann’s hemin crystal test\\n\\n* Spectroscopic test","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a99f6a2e6d","n":273,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"A 22-year-old mentally challenged pregnant female was brought to the hospital for MTP. Consent in this case will be given by?","options":["Doctor","Magistrate","Self consent","Legal guardian"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Legal guardian\\n\\nA legal guardian provides consent in the case of a mentally ill patient.\\n\\nConsent of parent/legal guardian is needed if the age of the patient is <18 years or if the patient is mentally ill.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9abf73b641","n":274,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"A 25-year-old female was raped two years back. Who has the authority to impose a death sentence on the convicted accused?","options":["Magistrate’s courts","Session court","District court","Assistant Sessions Court"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Session court\\n\\nSessions court can pass a death sentence but it has to be confirmed by the high court.\\n\\nIt is the highest judicial tribunal for the district and is located in the district headquarters. The court is presided over by the Sessions Judge, appointed by the High Court. High Court may also appoint Additional Sessions Judges and Assistant Sessions Judges to exercise jurisdiction in the court of sessions.\\n\\nIt takes up only the cases of criminal offences referred by Magistrate’s courts.\\n\\nMagistrate’s courts are criminal courts presided over by the Judicial/ Metropolitan Magistrates. In every district, the State Government after consultation with the High Court may establish as many Courts of Judicial Magistrate as it may consider necessary. Judicial judges can pass a sentence of imprisonment for 7 years only and cannot pass a death sentence.\\n\\nDistrict Court primarily handles civil litigation matters.\\n\\nAssistant Sessions Court has the jurisdiction to issue prison sentences of up to ten years along with imposing unlimited fines.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-81801d7d9e","n":275,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"A farmer is brought to the hospital following a snake bite on the right foot. Two fang marks were observed on the dorsum of the foot along with minimal pain, swelling and bleeding at the site. The vitals were stable. Which of the following is the immediate step of management?","options":["Give polyvalent snake antivenom 5000 units","Use sling or splint for right lower limb (pressure) immobilization","Look for dead snake brought by relatives","Clean the wound"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use sling or splint for right lower limb (pressure) immobilization\\n\\nThe immediate management of snakebite will include immobilization using a sling or splint.\\n\\nImmobilization is done to minimize the spread of the venom. Hospitalization, followed by ASV administration is the definitive management of snake bites.\\n\\nMeasures that\xa0can be done\xa0in the case of snakebite:\\n\\n* Apply firm pressure over the bitten area, which delays the absorption of venom\\n\\n* Pressure immobilization is recommended for elapid and sea-snake bites, but not for viper bites as it may cause local necrosis\\n\\n* Clean the wound with soap and water, or iodine and cover it with a sterile dressing\\n\\n* Immediately apply a broad firm bandage (Sutherland wrap) on the bitten area and around the limb. As much of the limb should be bandaged as possible. It should be tight enough to occlude the superficial venous and lymphatic return, but not the arterial or deep venous flow.\\n\\n* In bites on the trunk, head, or neck, apply firm pressure over the bitten area\\n\\n* Immobilize the limb, as movement can accelerate the spread of venom\\n\\n* Make the patient lie on one side in the recovery position so that the airway is clear, in case of vomiting or fainting\\n\\nMeasures\xa0contraindicated\xa0in the case of\xa0snakebite:\\n\\n* Suction\xa0and\xa0incision\xa0of the wound\\n\\n* Electric shock treatment of wound site\\n\\n* Application of tourniquets\xa0because they obstruct arterial flow and cause ischemia\\n\\n* Ice water immersion\xa0of the bitten limb","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d101ad2136","n":276,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"A newly married female patient who is 22 weeks pregnant came in for MTP on the grounds of contraceptive failure. MTP can be performed after?","options":["MTP cannot be done","Opinion of 1 doctor","After permission from the court","Opinion of 2 doctors"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. MTP cannot be done\\n\\nThe upper limit for MTP is 20 weeks in cases of contraceptive failure, so the procedure cannot be performed for this patient at 22 weeks of pregnancy.\\n\\nUpper limit of MTP:\\n\\n* Up to 24 weeks of gestation in victims of rape and incest, fetal anomalies, or congenital malformations\\n\\n* Up to 20 weeks in case of contraception failure (any female with her partner)\\n\\n* At any gestational age for substantial fetal anomalies decided by the medical board\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of an obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nOnly\xa0the\xa0patient/mother\'s consent\xa0is required for\xa0MTP;\xa0her partner\'s consent is not required.\xa0Consent of\xa0parent/guardian\xa0is needed if the\xa0age of the\xa0patient\xa0is\xa0<18 years\xa0or if the patient is\xa0mentally ill.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age. It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint be lodged with the police.\\n\\nPearl PF1638: Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act - MTP Act 1971\\n\\nMTP Act with 2021 amendments\\n\\nIndications:\\n\\n* Therapeutic - to save the life of the mother\\n\\n* Eugenic - to prevent the\xa0risk of the child being born with serious mental or physical abnormality i.e. first-trimester viral infections, teratogenic drug intake, radiation exposure\\n\\n* Humanitarian -\xa0pregnancy resulted from rape or incest\\n\\n* Social -\xa0pregnancy resulted from failure of contraceptives\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n* Only a registered medical practitioner who is qualified (one who has assisted in the performance of 25 cases of MTP in a recognized hospital) can terminate a pregnancy.\\n\\n* MTP should be carried out in a government or government-recognized hospital.\xa0Private hospitals may take up MTP if they obtain a license from the chief medical officer of the district.\\n\\n* The termination of pregnancy by a person, who is not a registered medical practitioner (the person concerned), or in an unrecognised hospital (administrative head) shall be punished with rigorous imprisonment for a term not less than 2 years, but which may extend to 7 years.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nThe consent of the woman is required for conducting an abortion. The written consent of the guardian is required if the woman is minor or mentally ill. Consent of the husband or partner is not necessary.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age.\xa0It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint was lodged with the police.\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state\\n\\nUpper limit of MTP:\\n\\n* Up to 24 weeks of gestation in victims of rape and incest, fetal anomalies, or congenital malformations\\n\\n* Up to 20 weeks in case of contraception failure (any female with her partner)\\n\\n* At any gestational age for substantial fetal anomalies decided by the medical board\\n\\nConfidentiality:\\n\\nA register for recording the details of MTP must be kept for at least 5 years. The patient\'s name must not be entered, only the reference number is used.\xa0Unlawful disclosure of patient\'s details is punishable with 1-year imprisonment\xa0\xb1 fine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-337c078ae8","n":277,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"During a police investigation, the police found some bones in the open space. Which of the following tests can be used to differentiate between human and animal bones?","options":["Teichmann test","Precipitin test","Barberio test","Takayama test"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Precipitin test\\n\\nThe test used to differentiate human bones from animal bones is the precipitin test.\\n\\nThe origin of skin, flesh, bone, blood, or even secretions, such as saliva, milk, and semen, is determined by this test. Precipitin test\xa0is used for\xa0species\xa0identification of semen, blood, or saliva, and a positive test confirms human origin. The precipitin test identifies the presence of proteins that are found only in human blood.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nTeichmann test and Takayama test (Options A and D) are used to confirm the presence of blood but cannot be used for species identification of blood.\\n\\nBarberio test (Option C) is used for the detection of spermatic fluid.\\n\\nPearl PF0395: Tests to detect seminal and blood stains\\n\\nTests for seminal stains\\n\\n* Barberio\'s test\\n\\n* Florence test\\n\\n* Acid phosphatase test\\n\\n* Creatine phosphokinase test\\n\\n* Immunological test to detect Prostate Specific Antigen\\n\\nTests for blood stains\\n\\n* Benzidine test\\n\\n* Phenophthalein (Kastle Meyer) test\\n\\n* Takayama hemochromogen crystal test\\n\\n* Teichmann’s hemin crystal test\\n\\n* Spectroscopic test","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8c8fd967c6","n":278,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"During an autopsy, what is commonly used to preserve blood?","options":["NaF","Formalin","Rectified spirit","NaCl"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. NaF\\n\\n10 mg/ml of sodium or potassium fluoride and potassium oxalate (anti-coagulant) is used to preserve blood.\\n\\nOther preservatives used in toxicology postmortem include:\\n\\n* Saturated NaCl solution: Avoided in poisoning from corrosive acids, alkalis, corrosive sublimate, and aconite.\\n\\n* Fluoride should be added to urine, CSF, and vitreous humor if alcohol estimation is required and if analysis for cocaine, cyanide, and CO is being done.\\n\\nA preservative is not necessary if :\\n\\n* The viscera can be analyzed in 24 hours\\n\\n* If the sample can be stored in a refrigerator or icebox\\n\\n* Bone, hair, nails specimens\\n\\n* Lung for inhaled poisons\\n\\n* The viscera should not be preserved in formaldehyde because extraction of non-volatile poisons becomes difficult.\\n\\nIn cases of chronic poisoning with heavy metals such as lead, arsenic, mercury, antimony, copper, etc., the shaft of a long bone, strands of scalp hair, fingernails or toenails, and a wedge of the quadriceps muscle should also be taken for sampling.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2added8697","n":279,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"In which type of poisoning will there be increased secretion along with diarrhea?","options":["Cannabis","Ketamine","Crack","Cocaine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ketamine\\n\\nIncreased secretion along with diarrhea can be seen in ketamine poisoning.\\n\\nKetamine is a phencyclidine derivative that inhibits NMDA (N-methyl-D-aspartate) receptors resulting in a decrease in excitatory stimulation by glutamate in the brain. It is a strong analgesic and amnestic agent.\\n\\nUses:\\n\\n* Ketamine is the intravenous agent of choice for patients with shock, low cardiac output states, and reactive airway diseases.\\n\\n* It is also used in patients with congenital heart diseases, especially with right-to-left shunts\\n\\n* Perioperative analgesia\\n\\nOther adverse effects:\\n\\n* Hallucination is the most common side effect of ketamine.\\n\\n* Emergence reaction (vivid dreaming, illusion, etc)\\n\\n* Increased salivation, nausea, vomiting\\n\\n* Increases brain oxygen consumption and metabolic rate leading to increased intracranial tension.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nCannabis: Large doses of cannabis causes nausea, anxiety, confusion, hallucinations and delusions. This is followed by giddiness, confusion, drowsiness and dilated pupils.\\n\\nCocaine: Acute intoxication mainly causes euphoria, hypervigilance, agitation, tactile hallucination, and finally respiratory failure or cardiac arrest.\\n\\nCrack: This is obtained by heating the cocaine with sodium bicarbonate to yield a hard white mass. This material gives off a cracking sound when smoked and hence the name.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eb468f4757","n":280,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"What is the best method to preserve a blood-stained handkerchief recovered from a crime scene?","options":["Dry over spirit lamp and preserve it","Stained area folded and preserved in plastic bag","Rinse with water to prevent contamination","Stained area folded and preserved in paper bag"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Stained area folded and preserved in paper bag\\n\\nThe stained handkerchief should be folded and preserved in a paper bag.\\n\\nPreservation of Blood stains:\\n\\n* The stained item should be allowed to air dry at room temperature without applying additional heat, as heat can degrade the stain.\\n\\n* Failure to dry stained clothing may result in putrefaction, making it challenging or impossible to determine whether the blood is of human or animal origin. It\'s advisable to label the stained object with initials and the date. If labelling is not feasible, attaching a tag or similar identifier is necessary.\\n\\n* Avoid using air-tight containers to prevent trapped moisture, which can promote bacterial growth and damage the stains. Each garment should be individually wrapped to prevent contact with other clothing, ensuring that no two stains touch each other.\\n\\n* Placing a clean sheet of paper between each stain is recommended.\\n\\n* All types of stains should be sent to the State Forensic Science Laboratory for examination.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e9429dff23","n":281,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"What is the most commonly affected organ in an air blast due to shockwave?","options":["Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lung\\n\\nLungs are considered to be severely damaged organs in the air due to variations in density between the alveolar walls and the contained air.\\n\\nA blast causes the most damage at an interface between tissues in contact with the atmosphere, which is why the lung usually suffers the most severe damage.\\n\\nBlast injury is a complex type of physical trauma resulting from direct or indirect exposure to an explosion. Primary blast injuries result from the rapid overpressure or shock waves produced by an explosion. These injuries result from the dramatic changes in barometric pressure projecting outwards from the point of detonation. They predominantly cause damage to air-filled hollow organs of the body from rapid pressure change (barotrauma). This includes the middle ear, lungs, and GIT.\\n\\nThe most sensitive and most frequently injured hollow organs are the tympanic membranes > lungs.\\n\\nBlast damage to the lungs is the most common cause of life-threatening injury following an explosion.\\n\\nSolid organs are the most resistant to blast injuries as the parenchymal disruption requires very high levels of overpressure. The liver is the least vulnerable to blast wave (primary blast) injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ab61973d96","n":282,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"When measuring the barrel of a rifled firearm, the caliber of the weapon depends on?","options":["Distance between 2 opposite grooves","Distance between 2 opposite lands","No of lead balls prepared from one pound of lead metal","Ratio between length of cartilage &amp; bullet"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Distance between 2 opposite lands\\n\\nCaliber is measured as the distance between two diametrically opposite lands.\\n\\nIt is the internal dimension of the barrel and is measured in millimetres or inches.\\n\\nNote: For a smooth barrel weapon, it is measured as the distance between two diametrically opposite points on the inner circumference.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/effab2a3c9029363e071.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-3f450628d4","n":283,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"Which of the following is a punishable offence with regard to MTP guidelines?","options":["MTP done up to 24 weeks of pregnancy","MTP done at government authorized center","MTP done by experienced RMP","Non entry in MTP register &amp; not sending reports to DHS"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Non entry in MTP register & not sending reports to DHS\\n\\nA register for recording the details of medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) must be kept for at least 5 years and reports must be sent to the Department of Health Services (DHS).\\n\\nA register for recording the details of medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) must be kept for at least 5 years, from the end of the calendar year it relates to. The patient\'s name must not be entered; only the reference number is used.\\n\\nUnlawful disclosure of a patient\'s details is punishable with 1-year imprisonment with or without a fine. Only the patient\'s consent is required for MTP; her male partner\'s consent is not required. Consent of parent/guardian is needed if the age of the patient is <18 years or if the patient is mentally ill.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2baed54586","n":284,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"Which of the following tests is used to identify old and dried seminal fluid on an undergarment?","options":["Teichmann test","Barberio test","Precipitin test","Takayama test"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Barberio test\\n\\nBarberio\'s test can be used to identify old and dried seminal fluid on an undergarment.\\n\\nThis test detects the presence of spermine, which is one of the constituents of semen. A drop of stain taken in a glass slide is charged with picric acid. Under microscopic examination, the needle-shaped yellowish spermine picrate crystals indicate the presence of semen.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nTeichmann test (Haemin Crystal Test): is used to detect bloodstains.\\nStain used: Glacial acetic acid and a small crystal of sodium chloride\\nFinding: If bloodstain is present, faint yellowish-red to brownish-black rhombic crystals of haemin or haematin chloride appear. When hydrogen peroxide is added, the haematin crystals give bubbles of gas\\n\\nPrecipitin test is an antigen-antibody reaction test used to distinguish between species (human or animal). It is done to confirm if the stain is of human origin.\\n\\nTakayama test (Haemochromogen crystal test) is used to detect bloodstains.\\nStain used: Takayama reagent\\nFinding: If bloodstain is present, pink feathery crystals of haemochromogen or reduced alkaline haematin appear in a few minutes\\n\\nPearl PF1864: Tests to detect seminal and blood stains\\n\\nTests for seminal stains\\n\\n* Barberio\'s test\\n\\n* Florence test\\n\\n* Acid phosphatase test\\n\\n* Creatine phosphokinase test\\n\\n* Immunological test to detect Prostate Specific Antigen\\n\\nTests for blood stains\\n\\n* Benzidine test\\n\\n* Phenophthalein (Kastle Meyer) test\\n\\n* Takayama hemochromogen crystal test\\n\\n* Teichmann’s hemin crystal test\\n\\n* Spectroscopic test","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ed8268ff71","n":285,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A 10-year-old boy fell and injured his arm. Upon examination, a brownish-black scab is observed. What is the estimated time of injury?","options":["12-24 hours","2-3 days","4-5 days","More than 7 days"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 4-5 days\\n\\nIn the clinical scenario of a 10-year-old boy who fell and sustained an arm injury with a brownish scab, the estimated time of the injury is approximately 4-5 days.\\n\\nDefinition: Scab is the destruction of the skin which usually involves the superficial layer of epidermis only.\\n\\nAbrasions are superficial wounds produced due to injury to the epidermis. They are usually caused by friction against a rough surface or by compression.\\n\\nTypes of abrasions:\\n\\n* Linear abrasions (scratches): Caused by relative movement of a pointed object on the skin.\\n\\n* examples: nail, thorn, the point of sword or knife\\n\\n* Grazed abrasions (sliding /scrapping/grinding abrasion): Caused by relative movement of a rough surface on a wide area of skin\\n\\n* brush burns- tangential rubbing over the surface, usually the ground\\n\\n* friction burns are extensive excoriated areas without bleeding\\n\\n* Examples: fall, a victim being dragged on the ground, road traffic accidents\\n\\n* Imprint abrasions (patterned abrasions): Caused by the impact of a rigid hard rough surface over a localized area of the skin without any movement; usually show the pattern of the object causing the\\n\\n* examples: tire, grills, bicycle chain, bite marks\\n\\n* Pressure abrasions (pressure abrasion): Caused by crushing of the superficial layers of the epidermis.\\n\\n* Examples: ligature marks around the neck in hanging\\n\\nPearl PF2099: Age of bruise and abrasion\\n\\nAge and color change of bruise/contusion:\\n\\nColour\\nDue to\\nDuration\\n\\nRed\\nHaemoglobin (fresh blood)\\nFresh\\n\\nBlue\\nDeoxygenated haemoglobin\\nFew hours to 3 days\\n\\nBluish black/ brown\\nHemosiderin\\n4th day\\n\\nGreen\\nHematoidin\\n5 to 6 days\\n\\nYellow\\nBilirubin\\n7 to 12 days\\n\\nNormal colour\\nPigment removed by phagocytes\\n2 weeks\\n\\nThe image given below shows the age and colour change of bruise:\\n\\nAge of abrasion:\\n\\nColour of scab\\nDuration\\n\\nBright red\\n12-24 hrs\\n\\nReddish brown\\n2-3 days\\n\\nDark brown to brownish black\\n4-7 days\\n\\nScab dries shrinks and falls off\\nAfter 7 days","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/edcecc740b404a813e90.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-b15a1767d3","n":286,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A body brought for autopsy was cold and had developed rigor mortis in all 4 limbs and livor mortis at the back, there is no sign of putrefaction. What is the estimated time since death?","options":["6-12 hrs","12-24 hrs","24-48 hrs","36-48 hrs"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 6-12 hrs\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario of a body brought for autopsy, which was cold and showed rigor mortis in all four limbs along with livor mortis on the back, but no signs of putrefaction, the estimated time of death would be approximately 6-12 hours, since putrefaction has not yet occurred.\\n\\nRigor mortis is a postmortem phenomenon indicative of molecular death. It is useful in estimating the time since death. The mechanism of rigor mortis is due to muscle ATP breakdown below the critical level. \xa0It appears 2-3 hours after death and is usually preceded by postmortem flaccidity. It involves both the voluntary and involuntary muscles of the body and persists for about 12-24 hours.\\n\\nPostmortem hypostasis/Postmortem staining/Postmortem lividity/Livor mortis/Suggilation refers to bluish-purple or reddish-purple discolouration of the superficial layers of the skin due to the collection of blood in toneless capillaries.\\n\\nIt is an early postmortem change that occurs due to engorged vessels showing through the skin seen on the dependent parts due to the pooling of blood under the effect of gravity. This staining starts as mottled patches 1-3 hours following death and develops to a uniform area of staining in 3-6 hours. Full development and fixation of hypostasis occur within 6-12 hours. This finding usually develops within 4 hours and persists until putrefaction.\\n\\nDecomposition or putrefaction refers to the disintegration of the body tissues after death. Multiplication of the bacteria begins within 4 hours after death and by 18 to 36 hours gases collect in the body causing bloating and distortion. The body then undergoes putrefaction and liquefaction of the tissues 5 to 10 days after death.\\n\\nPearl PF2094: Order of Putrefaction\\n\\n* Larynx and trachea – first organ to putrify\\n\\n* Stomach, intestine, and spleen\\n\\n* Liver and lungs\\n\\n* Brain\\n\\n* Heart\\n\\n* Kidney and bladder\\n\\n* Prostate and uterus – virgin uterus resists putrefaction whereas uterus soon after delivery putrefies rapidly.\\n\\n* Skin, muscle, tendon\\n\\n* Bones\\n\\nNOTE: This is only a generalised order of putrefaction, as each case must be assessed as per the attending factors, both endogenous and exogenous.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-49e3f78507","n":287,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A boy is severely beaten by 4 to 5 people and arrives at the hospital with serious injuries. A dying declaration is recorded. Which of the following statements about dying declarations in India is false?","options":["Compos mentis should be assessed","It must always be recorded by a magistrate","No leading questions are asked","An oath is not administered before recording a dying declaration."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. It must always be recorded by a magistrate\\n\\nThe statement that is not applicable to the dying declaration is it must be always recorded by a magistrate.\\n\\nWhile it is ideal for a Magistrate to record a dying declaration, there is no legal provision that states that it must always be recorded by a magistrate. The statement can be recorded by a magistrate, the doctor, the village headman, a police officer, or any member of the public.\\n\\nA dying deposition, on the other hand, must always be recorded by a Magistrate.\\n\\nA dying declaration (Sec. 32 IEA) is a form of a written or oral statement made by a person whose imminent death is a result of an unlawful act. The statement made relates to the facts of the cause of death and the circumstances surrounding it.\\n\\nProcedure:\\n\\n* As soon the severely injured person is brought to the hospital, the medical officer should inform the magistrate to have a dying declaration recorded. The fitness of the declarant to make the statement should be examined first.\\n\\n* The doctor can record it himself or herself if he thinks that the person will not survive till the arrival of the magistrate. In such a case, the police or the doctor concerned must note down the reason as to why waiting for the magistrate for recording this statement was not feasible.\\n\\n* Treatment should not be delayed while recording dying declaration.\\n\\n* An oath is not administered in these circumstances because of the belief that the dying person tells the truth. (Option D)\\n\\n* Before documenting a dying declaration, the doctor has to ascertain that the person is conscious and of sound mind with normal mental faculties (compos mentis).(Option A)\\n\\n* If the person dies or becomes unconscious while recording the dying declaration, the doctor must record the information obtained upto that point.\\n\\n* Usually recorded in the form of a simple narrative in the patient\'s own language.\\n\\n* No suggestions, prompting, help, or leading questions from other people are allowed. (Option C)\\n\\n* No outsider is allowed to be present\\n\\n* After completing writting, it has to be read back to the patient who has to approve it by signing/thumb impression. The signature of two witnesses is also mandatory.\\n\\nA dying deposition is a form of oral evidence that is recorded in the presence of the accused or their lawyer, with the provision of cross-examination. There is no legal provision for a dying deposition in the Indian Evidence Act (it is not followed in India).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0ea8fb88b1","n":288,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A doctor received a summons from the criminal court to provide evidence as an expert witness, but two days earlier, he had also received a summons from the civil court. What should the doctor do in this situation?","options":["Attend the summons for the criminal case","Attend the summons in the civil court","Attend the court for the summons received first and inform the other court accordingly.","Inform both the courts and wait"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Attend the summons for the criminal case\\n\\nWhen a doctor receives a summons from the civil and criminal courts, priority should be given to attending the summons for the criminal case first.\\n\\nA summons is a document compelling the attendance of a witness in a Court of law under penalty, on a particular day, time, and to give evidence.\\n\\nPriority of summons\\n\\nCourts\\nPriority\\n\\nHigher courts vs lower courts\\nHigher courts\\n\\nCriminal courts vs civil courts\\nCriminal courts\\n\\nCourts of equal status\\nThe court from which the summons was first received\\n\\nCommon IPC Sections dealing with summons include:\\n\\nIPC Section\\n\\nBNS Section\\n\\nDeals with\\n\\n172\\n\\n206\\n\\nAbsconding to avoid service of summons or other proceedings\\n\\n174\\n\\n208\\n\\nNon-attendance in obedience to an order from a public servant\\n\\n176\\n\\n211\\n\\nOmission to give notice/ information to public servant by a person legally bound to give it\\n\\n177\\n\\n212\\n\\nFurnishing false information","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-39818d5342","n":289,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A female died due to an unnatural cause within 7 years of marriage, and allegations have been raised. Under which section will the inquest be conducted? BNS - Bhartiya Nyaya Sanhita","options":["304A IPC/ 106 BNS","174 CrPC/194 BNSS","176 CrPC/196 BNSS","313 IPC/ 89 BNS"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 176 CrPC/196 BNSS\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a female who died due to an unnatural cause within 7 years of marriage suggests a possibility of dowry death. A magistrate inquest is done under BNSS Section 196 (Section 176 CrPC).\\n\\nBNSS 196 (Section 176 CrPC) deals with magistrate inquest in which the cause of death is not under natural circumstances. Magistrate inquest may be conducted by the District Magistrate, Sub-Divisional Magistrate, Tahsildar, or any other Executive Magistrate empowered by the State Government.\\n\\nIndications for Magistrate Inquest:\\n\\n* Dowry death\\n\\n* Custodial rape\\n\\n* Death in police custody or police interrogation\\n\\n* Death in police firing\\n\\n* Death in prison, reformatories, Borstal school\\n\\n* Death in a psychiatric hospital\\n\\n* Cases of exhumation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Section 106 BNS (304A IPC) defines the punishment for causing the death of a person by any rash or negligent act, not amounting to culpable homicide, the punishment is imprisonment for up to 2 years, and a fine.\\n\\nOption B: \xa0BNSS Section 194 (Section 174 CrPC) deals with the police inquest. It is done in suicide, homicide, killing by any animal or machinery or accident, and death under circumstances that raise suspicion of another person committing the offence.\\n\\nOption D: BNS Sec 89 (IPC 313) deals with the causation of miscarriage of a woman with child (and/or quick with child) without her consent. Only the person causing the abortion is liable to punishment.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eb582ba0ed","n":290,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A person was stabbed in the thigh with a knife, resulting in a wound measuring 1 cm in length and 3 cm in depth. How would you classify this wound?","options":["Penetrating wound","Perforating wound","Crush injury","Incised wound"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Penetrating wound\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario, a person is stabbed in the thigh with a knife, resulting in a wound measuring 1 cm in length and 3 cm in depth is suggestive of a penetrating wound.\\n\\nA penetrating wound is an injury that occurs when an object, such as a knife, bullet, or any sharp instrument, breaks through the skin and enters deeper tissues of the body. These types of wounds can damage organs, blood vessels, and nerves, depending on the depth and location of the penetration. Penetrating wounds may lead to significant blood loss, infection, or trauma to vital structures, requiring prompt medical attention.\\n\\nTreatment typically involves controlling bleeding, preventing infection, and repairing any internal damage, often requiring surgical intervention. The severity of a penetrating wound depends on the force of impact, the object involved, and the area affected.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: A perforating wound occurs when an object penetrates the body and exits, creating both an entry and an exit wound. These wounds can cause significant internal damage, including injury to organs, blood vessels, and tissues.\\n\\nOption C: A crush injury occurs when a part of the body is subjected to intense pressure or force, typically from a heavy object. This can lead to tissue damage, fractures, nerve injury, and in severe cases, organ damage or compartment syndrome.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0An incised wound is clean-cut, regular, well-defined caused by sharp lightweight weapons like a knife, razor, scalpel, etc. Incised wounds will have a characteristic head of wound and tailing of a wound.\\n\\nThe edges are well-defined and usually everted, free from contusions and abrasions. It is usually spindle-shaped. The depth is more at the commencement and progressively becomes shallower, known as tailing. Tailing determines the direction of the force applied when creating the wound.\\nThe image below shows two spindle-shaped incisions:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/34e6e7604d30795669eb.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-d27b5908e3","n":291,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"During a forensic examination, the police presented a skull bone for analysis, and the cephalic index was determined to be 85. Which race does the skull likely belong to?","options":["Mongoloids","Negroes","Aryans","Europeons"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Mongoloids\\n\\nA skull bone with a cephalic index of 85 belongs to the Mongoloid race.\\n\\nCephalic Index is used to determine the race of the individual.\\n\\nCranial index = Maximum skull breadth x 100 / Maximum skull length.\\n\\nThere are three main racial groups: Caucasian, Mongoloid and Negroid. All the skulls cannot be accurately differentiated into the 3 broad categories because of the racial mixing.\\n\\nThe skull offers the best evidence of racial origins.\\n\\nType of skull\\nCephalic index\\nRace(s)\\n\\nDolichocephalic\\n70-74.9\\nPure Aryans(Option C),\xa0Aborigines, Negroes(Option B)\\n\\nMesaticephalic\\n75-79.9\\nEuropeans(Option D),\xa0Chinese, Indians\\n\\nBrachycephalic\\n80-84.9\\nMongolian\\n\\nThe skull of an Indian is \'Caucasian with a few Negroid features\'.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ed8d2accbf","n":292,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"During the court proceedings, the defense lawyer asks a leading question, which the prosecutor appeals against. The judge grants the appeal. Such questions are permitted during which of the following?","options":["Re-examination","Cross examination","Examination in chief","Dying declaration"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cross examination\\n\\nLeading questions are not allowed in all of the given options except cross-examination, i.e leading questions are allowed during cross-examination.\\n\\nAs per section 141 of the Indian Evidence Act, a leading question is one which is suggestive of the answer that is desired or one which includes a fact and leads to the admitting of a conclusive answer, such as yes or no.\\n\\nCross-examination is one wherein the witness is questioned by the opposite party\'s lawyer, i.e the lawyer for the accused or the defense lawyer. It is undertaken with the objective\xa0to demonstrate favorable facts to their case, to test the accuracy of the witness statements, to unearth new or old facts, and to discredit the witness.\\n\\nThe sequence of examination while recording evidence is as follows:\\n\\n* Examination-in-Chief/direct examination by the party that summoned the witness to elicit all the facts from the witness. Leading questions are not allowed\xa0(option C). The only exception is if the judge is satisfied that the witness is hostile.\\n\\n* Cross-examination\xa0is done by the defence lawyer.\\n\\n* Re-examination\xa0is done by the party that summoned the witness to correct or clarify or detail the statements made by the witness during cross-examination. Leading questions are not allowed (option A).\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption D: A dying declaration is an oral or written statement made by a person who is dying. The doctor should certify that the person is conscious and with normal mental faculties before the declaration is recorded. It is recorded by the executive magistrate, or a doctor in the presence of two witnesses, if there is no time for the magistrate to come. Leading questions should not be asked.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0c97728490","n":293,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"Magistrate\'s inquest is done in _________","options":["Death due to police firing","Death due to drowning","Death by bullet injury","When the family wants to reconduct the inquest"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Death due to police firing\\n\\nMagistrate\'s inquest is done in the case of death due to police firing.\\n\\nBNSS 196 or section 176 Cr. P.C. deals with magistrate inquest in which the cause of death is not under natural circumstances.\xa0A magistrate’s inquest is an inquest conducted by a District Magistrate, Subdivisional Magistrate, and magistrate of the first class rank or any other magistrate as empowered by the State Government, such as Collector, Deputy Collector, or Tehsildar (Executive Magistrates).\\n\\nSpecial Magistrate’s inquests are held in cases of:\\n\\n* Lock-up deaths\\n\\n* Deaths while under police interrogation\\n\\n* Deaths in prison\\n\\n* Deaths in police custody\\n\\n* Deaths due to police firing\\n\\n* Exhumation\\n\\n* Alleged dowry death\\n\\n* In all cases where the police normally conduct inquests, magistrate can hold an additional inquest, or in place of the police inquest.\\n\\nNote: It is practised throughout India. However, it is not held routinely but held only on special indications mentioned above. In any case of unnatural death, the magistrate may hold an inquest instead of/ in addition to the police inquest.\\n\\nPolice Inquest: The inquest is held by a police officer (called Investigating Officer) not below the rank of Senior Head Constable. It is practised throughout India.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-11248af9fc","n":294,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"Two construction workers were working together when a hammer slipped from one worker\'s hand and struck the other on the head. An X-ray revealed a fracture. What type of fracture could this be?","options":["Linear fracture","Hinge fracture","Ring fracture","Depressed skull fracture"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Depressed skull fracture\\n\\nThe clinical history and the X-ray image are suggestive of a depressed skull fracture of the frontal bone.\\n\\nA depressed skull fracture happens when a section of the skull bone is driven inward following a traumatic impact. This injury creates a noticeable indentation or depression in the skull and typically results from a direct blow to the head.\\n\\nThe\xa0management of\xa0depressed skull fractures:\\n\\n* The\xa0first step\xa0is to order a\xa0CT scan\xa0to know the degree of depression and to exclude the presence of an\xa0intracranial hematoma\xa0or\xa0contusion.\\n\\n* The\xa0depressed fracture is elevated\xa0and managed as an open fracture (surgery)\xa0if:\\n\\n* The\xa0degree of depression\xa0is\xa0greater than the thickness of the adjacent skull.\\n\\n* The wound is open\xa0and grossly\xa0contaminated.\\n\\n* There are focal neurological deficits.\\n\\n* Less severe depressed fractures\xa0are managed by the\xa0closure of the overlying scalp laceration, if present.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: A Linear fracture is a type of skull fracture where the bone breaks in a thin, straight line without displacement. It is often caused by blunt force trauma and typically does not involve significant damage to surrounding tissues.\\n\\nOption B: A Hinge fracture is a type of skull base fracture where the skull splits along the base, resembling an open hinge. It is usually caused by severe trauma and can be associated with brain injury and damage to vital structures.\\n\\nOption C: A Ring fracture is a circular fracture around the base of the skull, typically occurring in the posterior fossa around the foramen magnum. It is often caused by severe trauma, such as a fall from a height or forceful impact on the head. These occur\xa0If the kinetic energy of the fall is not absorbed by fractures of the legs, pelvis, or spine, the impact is transmitted up the cervical spine. This force may be rammed into the skull, carrying a circle of occipital bone with it.\\n\\nPearl PF0368: Types of skull vault fracture\\n\\nDiastatic fracture -\xa0Separation of sutures due to blow with blunt weapon or from cerebral edema. It is also called sutural fracture.\\n\\nFissured fracture/Linear fracture:\\n\\n* Linear skull fractures are breaks in the bone that traverse the full thickness of the skull from the outer to inner table.\\n\\n* They are usually fairly straight with no bone displacement.\\n\\n* The common cause of injury is blunt force trauma where the impact energy transferred over a wide area of the skull.\\n\\nPond fracture - Occurs in children due to elasticity of bones. It is also known as indented fracture.\\n\\nDepressed fracture -\xa0Caused by a heavy weapon with a small striking surface. Its shape indicates the type of weapon which is produced. So it is also called\xa0\'signature fracture\'.\\n\\nComminuted fracture - It is also called spider web fracture and has a stellate appearance where there is no displacement of fracture.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/a79c66e7ed581539e4db.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/eae5abbe1fad753d9109.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/044521e4c9c24dba9aab.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/fcbd33c2a0f18189e010.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/bf333a72545ec5279936.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d5287acdc26c37577e57.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-bfb97770f6","n":295,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A 10-year-old child will have __?","options":["8 permanent &amp; 16 temporary teeth","16 permanent &amp; 8 temporary teeth","12 permanent &amp; 8 temporary teeth","12 permanent &amp; 12 temporary teeth"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 16 permanent & 8 temporary teeth\\n\\nA 10-year-old will have 16 permanent and 8 temporary teeth.\\n\\nMixed dentition is the stage when both primary and permanent teeth are present, typically occurring between 6 and 12 years of age. During this period, the total number of teeth usually remains constant at 24.\\n\\nAt 10 years, a child will have 16 permanent (8 incisors, 4 premolars, 4 molars)and 8 temporary teeth (4 molars, 4 canines).\\n\\nTeeth distribution during the mixed dentition period:\\n\\nAGE (years)\\n\\nPermanent Teeth\\nTemporary teeth\\n\\n9\\n12\\n12\\n\\n10\\n16\\n8\\n\\n11\\n20\\n4\\n\\n12\\n24\\n0\\n\\nBy around 14 years, there are 28 permanent teeth, and no temporary teeth.\\n\\nNote: (Age in years - 5) x 4 formula is also used to calculate the number of permanent teeth in mixed dentition, crudely. However, it\'s not used in standard practice and hence, not used for calculation for this question.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bced811ef2","n":296,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A body was brought in for post-mortem examination, and the following can be seen. What type of wounds are these?","options":["Defense wounds","Antemortem wound","Postmortem wound","Hesitation cuts"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Defense wounds\\n\\nThe given image shows irregular and ragged cut wounds\xa0over the palmar surface of the hands and fingers, which is suggestive of defense wounds.\\n\\nDefense wounds\xa0are the wounds that occur as a result of the victim\'s attempt to save himself from an injury.\\n\\nIt can be active or passive. Active defense wounds occur when the victim tries to grasp the weapon, while passive wounds result when the victim raises the hands, arms, or legs in an attempt to protect the eyes, head, or neck.\\n\\nThe morphology of the wound depends upon the type of weapon and the attack. In a sharp injury due to a single-edged weapon, a single cut may be seen on the palm or the bends of fingers. In the case of a double-edged sharp weapon, cuts are seen on both palm and fingers.\xa0The cuts are irregular in-depth and are ragged.\\n\\nThe defense wounds signify homicide, and in females, they often indicate sexual assault.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions B and C: Antemortem and postmortem wounds are of medicolegal importance while evaluating trauma. They can be differentiated by the following features:\\n\\nFeature\\nAntemortem wounds\\nPostmortem wounds\\n\\nEdges\\nSwollen edges with wound gapes\\nClosely approximated with no wound gapes\\n\\nHemorrhage\\nArterial type of hemorrhage\\nVenous hemorrhage\\n\\nSpurting\\nSpurting of blood seen with firmly coagulated blood\\nNo spurts and no clotting\\n\\nVital reaction\\nInflammation and repair seen\\nNo signs of inflammation and repair\\n\\nOption D: Hesitation cuts are a feature of self-inflicted wounds or suicide. It typically appears as multiple, small, and superficial wounds on the skin, at the beginning of an incised wound. The common sites are the throat, wrist, and front of the chest.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/0e48028f392e01d40ea9.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-ffed7e8bd5","n":297,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A child presents to the emergency department with complaints of severe abdominal pain and vomiting. The parents give a history of the child ingesting 10-20 ferrous sulfate tablets. Which of the following can be used in the management of this condition?","options":["Deferoxamine","Activated charcoal","Dimercaprol","Penicillamine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Deferoxamine\\n\\nThe clinical scenario points to iron overdose, which is managed with deferoxamine (also known as\xa0desferrioxamine).\\n\\nLarge amounts of ferrous salts can be fatal in children. Initial symptoms include abdominal pain, diarrhea, vomiting, and gastrointestinal bleeding. In severe cases, cyanosis and hyperventilation due to metabolic acidosis can develop. This can sometimes be followed by an asymptomatic phase. Later, hypoglycemia and hepatic and renal failure can develop. Pyloric stenosis and gastric scarring can develop due to its corrosive nature. Ultimately, if left untreated, the child can die.\\n\\nThe gastric contents can be used for an iron color test to estimate the iron concentration in plasma. As iron tablets are radiopaque, an X-ray can be used to assess the number of pills taken. Deferoxamine, an iron chelator has to be administered intramuscularly. Side effects include pruritus, wheals, rash, and anaphylaxis.\\n\\nDeferiprone and deferasirox are other oral iron chelators used to treat iron overload in thalassemia patients.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Activated charcoal can be used in the initial management of acetaminophen and opioid overdose. It is used for gastrointestinal decontamination to prevent further absorption of the poison.\\n\\nOption C: Dimercaprol,\xa0also known as BAL (British anti-Lewisite), is used to treat arsenic, mercury poisoning, and Wilson\'s disease.\\n\\nOption D: D-penicillamine (Cuprimine) can be used to treat scleroderma, Wilson\'s disease, and rheumatoid arthritis. Side effects include nephrotoxicity, hematologic derangements, and a characteristic rash that involves the neck and axillae—elastosis perforans serpiginosa.\xa0It is associated with membranous glomerulonephritis, drug-induced lupus, and myasthenia gravis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-42e18e4b2c","n":298,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A deceased male was posted for a post-mortem examination. His relatives reported that he had a tattoo over his skin, which was not found during the autopsy. Which of the following would you examine to determine the absence of his tattoo?","options":["Lymph nodes","Liver","Arteries","Veins"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lymph nodes\\n\\nThe lymph nodes\xa0should be examined in this case.\\n\\nSome amount of material or dye used for creating tattoo marks is transported through lymph to the regional lymph nodes.\\n\\nA\xa0faded tattoo\xa0mark can be\xa0visualized\xa0by:\\n\\n* Ultraviolet light\\n\\n* Infrared photography\\n\\n* Rubbing the part and examining it with a magnifying lens\\n\\nIn\xa0decomposed\xa0bodies, the tattoo marks can be visualized by\xa0removing\xa0the\xa0epidermis\xa0with a moist cloth. The\xa0lymph nodes\xa0near a tattoo mark also show a deposit of the\xa0pigment.\\n\\nHence in cases where a tattoo mark has disappeared\xa0naturally or has been removed, the lymph nodes must be examined before making any conclusions.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eda512171d","n":299,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A male was brought with the following injury in a gunshot incident. What is the possible range from which the weapon inflicted injury?","options":["Contact shot","Close shot","Near shot","Distant shot"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Contact shot\\n\\nIn the given scenario, based on the stellate-shaped wound seen, it seems to be a\xa0contact shot\xa0entry wound.\\n\\nA contact shot entry wound on the skull typically has a stellate shape due to high-energy impact.\\n\\n* Firearm wounds are fundamental of two main types: wounds of entry and wounds of exit. The wound of entry is the wound through which the bullet or pellets enter the body, and the wound of exit is the wound through which the bullet emerges out of the body.\\n\\n* To determine the range of firing, the following parameters are used:\\n\\n* The entry wound size and shape\\n\\n* Blast effect:\xa0Muzzle end imprint and edge eversion\\n\\n* Blackening, tattooing, singeing of hair, abrasion collar, and grease collar\\n\\n* Hair should be preserved to determine the range of firing which can be examined for carbonization/desiccation at close range, and also from lead deposition around the wound to assess the range of firing.\\n\\nCharacteristics\\nContact shot\\nClose Shot\\nNear Shot\\nDistant Shot\\n\\nRange\\nIn-touch with skin\\n2.5-7.5 cm\\n30-60 cm\\n>60 cm\\n\\nSize\\nLargest size\\nBullet size\\nSmaller\\nSmallest\\n\\nShape\\nStellate\\nCircular\\nCircular\\nCircular\\n\\nBlast effect\\n\\nMuzzle end imprint\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nEdge eversion\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nOther findings\\n\\nBlackening\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nTattooing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nSingeing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nAbrasion collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n\\nGrease collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n\\nThe image given below shows characteristics of shotgun wounds at various ranges:\\n\\nPearl PF2153: Types of bullets:\\n\\n1. Ricochet bullet: Bullet first strikes an intermediate object and then after being reflected strikes the target. It produces a key hole entry wound .\\n\\n2.\xa0Yawning bullet: Travels in an irregular fashion.\\n\\n3.\xa0Tandem (Piggy Back) Bullet Effect: Two bullets are ejected one after the other; first bullet failed to leave the barrel and is ejected by subsequently fired bullet. This occurs due to defect in the weapon or due to faulty ammunition or with loaded firearms unused for several years.\xa0Tandem cartridge (also called duplex cartridge) is a single cartridge containing two bullets.\\n\\n4.\xa0Frangible bullet: Fragments on hitting the target.\\n\\n5.\xa0Dum Dum bullet: Tip is chiseled out, fragments extensively on impact.\\n\\n6.\xa0Tumbling bullet: \xa0Rotates end on end during its motion. This produces an atypical entry wound .\\n\\n7.\xa0Souvenir bullet: \xa0Bullet left in the body for a long time and covered with dense fibrous connective tissue.\\n\\n8.\xa0Tracer bullet: This type of bullet leaves a visible mark or trace while in flight so that the gunner can observe the strike of shot. Rear portion of bullet is filled with mixture of barium nitrate, strontium nitrate and powdered magnesium which is ignited by the burning powder. Mixture burns and sheds red sparks which makes the projectile visible to naked eye which enables shooter to make aiming corrections.\\n\\n9.\xa0Incendiary bullet: It is filled with white phosphorous and ignites the target.\\n\\nFrom left to right : Blank with frangible wooden bullet, ball, tracer and incendiary bullets\\n\\nPearl PF2237: Products of Firing and its Effects\\n\\nFlame and hot air\\n\\nSmoke/soot (completely burnt powder)\\n\\nPartially burnt and unburnt powder particle\\n\\nBullet\\n\\nScorching\\n\\nBurning and singeing\\n\\nBlackening /smudging\\n\\nTattooing/peppering/powder strippling\\n\\nWound of entry\\n\\nGrease collar\\n\\nAbrasion collar\\n\\nWound of exit\\n\\nSoiling of the Wound:\\n\\nSmudging/blackening:\\n\\n* It is due to the deposition of smoke particles, lead or metal of which the missile is made of.\\n\\nTattooing/peppering\\n\\n* It is due to deposition of fine or coarse gunpowder particles, which become truly impregnated into and mark the skin, and cannot be washed or scrubbed off.\\n\\nMedicolegal importance\\n\\n* Smudging and tattooing effects can help to assess the range of firing, as below\\n\\n* If both are present, the range of firing is close or near shot.\\n\\n* If both are absent, the range of firing is a distant shot.\\n\\n* If both are found deeper, i.e. on the inner aspect of the wound, the range is contact shot.\\n\\n* If only tattooing is present, the range is near shot.\\n\\n* Thus as the range increases, blackening fades first, followed by tattooing, the reason being their causative agents cannot travel beyond a fixed distance.\\n\\nPearl PF2060: Summary of Wound of entry for Rifled Firearm at different Ranges\\n\\nCharacteristics\\n\\nContact shot\\n\\nClose shot\\n\\nNear shot\\n\\nDistant shot\\n\\nRange\\n\\nIn-touch with skin\\n\\n2.5-7.5 cm\\n\\n30-60 cm\\n\\n>60 cm\\n\\nSize\\n\\nLargest size\\n\\nBullet size\\n\\nSmaller\\n\\nSmallest\\n\\nShape\\n\\nStellate\\n\\nCircular\\n\\nCircular\\n\\nCircular\\n\\nMuzzle end imprint\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nEdge eversion\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nBlackening\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nTattooing\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nSingeing\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nAbrasion collar\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nGrease collar\\n\\nNegative\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nPositive\\n\\nContact shot:\\n\\nThis is a contact gunshot entrance wound. Since the barrel contacts the skin, the gases released by the fired round go into the subcutaneous tissue and cause the star-shaped laceration. Note also the grey-black discoloration from the soot, as well as the faint abrasion ring.\\n\\nClose shot:\\n\\n* The wound has been washed and there is stippling. This indicates a close-range gunshot wound because both stippling and fouling are present.\\n\\nDistant shot:\\n\\n* Distant range entrance gunshot wound with eccentric abrasion rim.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/b01a3ff1e14ca61e3b4b.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/93da3c7b1082dde235c0.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0ce6f007faf3f9b148cc.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/038b968c58a0ba87ef58.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/c949e8731a4c3e71ccfd.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/efe09792c59503b0f314.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/6f0f6ff5f61edf9f8d2d.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/af892a09b0bdee53ed4a.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a61a6108bdd931b2d7f2.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/b064055de69895edbdf0.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/8b663732dcb976d3e7a2.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/40c709c13f92429b34f7.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/b3bc533ee7b46e3b1265.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/4570c09495cb6e9d7d7d.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/28333b5d925e816a75af.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/443e8ba840f65640c537.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-a24f651e2e","n":300,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A newly married female patient, 21 weeks pregnant, underwent a fetal USG which showed myelomeningocele of the fetus. The doctor proposed medical termination of pregnancy (MTP). Which of the following is mandatory in this case? a. Patient\'s consent b. Patient\'s partner\'s consent c. Opinion of 1 doctor d. Opinion of 2 doctors","options":["c only","d only","a and d","b and d"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. a and d\\n\\nOnly\xa0the\xa0patient/mother\'s consent\xa0is required for\xa0MTP;\xa0her partner\'s consent is not required.\xa0Consent of\xa0parent/guardian\xa0is needed if the\xa0age of the\xa0patient\xa0is\xa0<18 years\xa0or if the patient is\xa0mentally ill.\\n\\n2 doctors\' opinion is required as the patient\'s gestational age is >20 weeks.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nThe consent of the woman is required for conducting an abortion. The written consent of the guardian is required if the woman is minor or mentally ill. Consent of the husband or partner is not necessary.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age. It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint be lodged with the police.\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state\\n\\nPearl PF1638: Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act - MTP Act 1971\\n\\nMTP Act with 2021 amendments\\n\\nIndications:\\n\\n* Therapeutic - to save the life of the mother\\n\\n* Eugenic - to prevent the\xa0risk of the child being born with serious mental or physical abnormality i.e. first-trimester viral infections, teratogenic drug intake, radiation exposure\\n\\n* Humanitarian -\xa0pregnancy resulted from rape or incest\\n\\n* Social -\xa0pregnancy resulted from failure of contraceptives\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n* Only a registered medical practitioner who is qualified (one who has assisted in the performance of 25 cases of MTP in a recognized hospital) can terminate a pregnancy.\\n\\n* MTP should be carried out in a government or government-recognized hospital.\xa0Private hospitals may take up MTP if they obtain a license from the chief medical officer of the district.\\n\\n* The termination of pregnancy by a person, who is not a registered medical practitioner (the person concerned), or in an unrecognised hospital (administrative head) shall be punished with rigorous imprisonment for a term not less than 2 years, but which may extend to 7 years.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nThe consent of the woman is required for conducting an abortion. The written consent of the guardian is required if the woman is minor or mentally ill. Consent of the husband or partner is not necessary.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age.\xa0It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint was lodged with the police.\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state\\n\\nUpper limit of MTP:\\n\\n* Up to 24 weeks of gestation in victims of rape and incest, fetal anomalies, or congenital malformations\\n\\n* Up to 20 weeks in case of contraception failure (any female with her partner)\\n\\n* At any gestational age for substantial fetal anomalies decided by the medical board\\n\\nConfidentiality:\\n\\nA register for recording the details of MTP must be kept for at least 5 years. The patient\'s name must not be entered, only the reference number is used.\xa0Unlawful disclosure of patient\'s details is punishable with 1-year imprisonment\xa0\xb1 fine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c295574c75","n":301,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A post-mortem cooling graph is plotted. What is the ideal shape of this graph?","options":["Hyperbolic","Sigmoid","S-shaped","Parabolic"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sigmoid\\n\\nThe graph plotted during post-mortem cooling is ideally sigmoid or inverted S-shaped.\\n\\nAlgor mortis/postmortem cooling is one of the early changes occurring after death. After death, the body loses heat mainly by conduction and convection. Up to the first hour, a temperature plateau may be seen due to the heat generated by residual metabolic activities of dying tissues. This is followed by a linear rate of cooling over the next 12 to 16 hours. The temperature rarely reaches the ambient temperature, most likely due to bacterial action with decomposition setting in.\\n\\nThe graph with the changes occurring after death plotted against time is as follows:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/24fb785327c3c3b3e898.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-4734169ea3","n":302,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"After receiving a complaint from a security guard, the police recovered a beheaded skull bone near a park. During the investigation, the police suspected that it could be that of a known missing person. The missing person\'s relatives were called in to confirm the identity. Which of the following techniques can be used to figure out a person\'s identity using the beheaded skull bone and photograph of the person?","options":["Superimposition","Anthropometry","DNA fingerprint","Cheiloscopy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Superimposition\\n\\nThe technique used to\xa0figure out a person\'s\xa0identity\xa0using the\xa0beheaded skull bone\xa0and\xa0photograph\xa0of the person is\xa0superimposition. The superimposition\xa0technique is applied to the skull to determine whether it belongs to the person in the photograph.\\n\\nThe following points can be used for comparisons such as the eyes, nasion, prosthion, zygomas, lower border of the nose, supraorbital ridges, lower border of the zygomas, angle of the jaw, external auditory meatus, teeth and the lower border of the upper jaw.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Anthropometry\xa0is based on the principle that the\xa0skeletal dimensions\xa0do\xa0not change\xa0after the age of 21 years and the variation in the size of the different parts to one another.\xa0Descriptive data\xa0such as the color of the hair, eyes, complexion, and body marks are recorded.\xa0Body measurements\xa0are\xa0estimated\xa0such as height, anteroposterior diameter of head and trunk, the span of outstretched arms, the length of the left middle finger, left little finger, left forearm, left foot, length, and breadth of right ear, and color of left iris from the\xa0photographs.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0DNA fingerprinting\xa0for forensic analysis is done to\xa0detect DNA\xa0from small amounts of samples from the crime scene.\xa0DNA fingerprinting\xa0uses\xa0restriction fragment length polymorphism\xa0(RFLPs) or\xa0Polymerase chain reaction(PCR) to identify a person\'s\xa0unique pattern\xa0of\xa0DNA.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Cheiloscopy\xa0is also known as\xa0lip printing. Here, the fissures and grooves on the lip characteristic of a person are examined.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f81a462908","n":303,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"As per the Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act, until when can an MTP be performed on grounds of contraceptive failure?","options":["24 weeks","After 24 weeks","20 weeks","17 weeks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 20 weeks\\n\\nAs per the Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act of 2021,\xa0the upper limit for termination of pregnancy on grounds of contraceptive failure is 20 weeks.\\n\\nThe MTP Act of 2021 specifies special circumstances for termination of pregnancy beyond 20 weeks till 24 weeks of gestation. These include:\\n\\n* Survivor of sexual assault, rape or incest.\\n\\n* Minors\\n\\n* Mentally ill women including mental retardation\\n\\n* Change of marital status during the ongoing pregnancy (widowhood and divorce)\\n\\n* Women with physical disabilities (Major disability criteria - Rights of persons with disabilities act - 2016)\\n\\n* Women with pregnancy in humanitarian settings or disaster or emergency situations as declared by the government.\\n\\n* Fetal malformations that have a substantial risk of being incompatible with life or if the child is born, may suffer from serious physical or mental abnormalities to be seriously handicapped. (Option A)\\n\\nTermination of pregnancy can be done only up to 20 weeks based on grounds of contraception failure.\\n\\n* Until 20 weeks of gestation - Opinion of one doctor\\n\\n* 20-24 weeks of gestation - Opinion of two doctors\\n\\n* The name and particulars of the woman whose pregnancy has been terminated cannot be disclosed. It can only be revealed to a person in force authorised by law. The ground for failure of contraception is extended to women and their partners.\\n\\nPearl PF1638: Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act - MTP Act 1971\\n\\nMTP Act with 2021 amendments\\n\\nIndications:\\n\\n* Therapeutic - to save the life of the mother\\n\\n* Eugenic - to prevent the\xa0risk of the child being born with serious mental or physical abnormality i.e. first-trimester viral infections, teratogenic drug intake, radiation exposure\\n\\n* Humanitarian -\xa0pregnancy resulted from rape or incest\\n\\n* Social -\xa0pregnancy resulted from failure of contraceptives\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n* Only a registered medical practitioner who is qualified (one who has assisted in the performance of 25 cases of MTP in a recognized hospital) can terminate a pregnancy.\\n\\n* MTP should be carried out in a government or government-recognized hospital.\xa0Private hospitals may take up MTP if they obtain a license from the chief medical officer of the district.\\n\\n* The termination of pregnancy by a person, who is not a registered medical practitioner (the person concerned), or in an unrecognised hospital (administrative head) shall be punished with rigorous imprisonment for a term not less than 2 years, but which may extend to 7 years.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nThe consent of the woman is required for conducting an abortion. The written consent of the guardian is required if the woman is minor or mentally ill. Consent of the husband or partner is not necessary.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age.\xa0It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint was lodged with the police.\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state\\n\\nUpper limit of MTP:\\n\\n* Up to 24 weeks of gestation in victims of rape and incest, fetal anomalies, or congenital malformations\\n\\n* Up to 20 weeks in case of contraception failure (any female with her partner)\\n\\n* At any gestational age for substantial fetal anomalies decided by the medical board\\n\\nConfidentiality:\\n\\nA register for recording the details of MTP must be kept for at least 5 years. The patient\'s name must not be entered, only the reference number is used.\xa0Unlawful disclosure of patient\'s details is punishable with 1-year imprisonment\xa0\xb1 fine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7ae1c472cb","n":304,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"In a suspected murder case, the court summoned a doctor to provide his opinion and testify as a witness. This form of evidence is commonly referred to as ____?","options":["Oral Evidence","Expert witness","Hostile witness","Common witness"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Expert witness\\n\\nThe doctor in this scenario is referred to as an expert witness.\\n\\nAs per Section 39 (1) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA)\xa0(earlier Section 45 of the Indian Evidence Act), an\xa0expert witness\xa0is a person who is\xa0skilled\xa0in technical or scientific matters and capable of drawing opinions and conclusions from the facts observed by him or by others.  Examples:\xa0Doctor, firearm expert, fingerprinting expert, handwriting expert.\\n\\nWitness: A witness is a person who gives evidence regarding facts.\xa0Sections 124 to 139 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA) (earlier Sections 118 to 134 of the Indian Evidence Act) deal with various aspects of witnesses.\\n\\nOther types of witnesses:\\n\\n* A common witness is a person who gives evidence about the facts observed by them.\xa0The first-hand knowledge rule\xa0refers to the concept that the witness must show that he was\xa0capable of perceiving the fact\xa0by one of his own senses and that\xa0he actually observed this fact. It applies to all common witnesses who give\xa0evidence\xa0about the\xa0facts observed\xa0or\xa0perceived\xa0by him/her.\\n\\nA\xa0doctor\xa0can be\xa0both a common\xa0and an\xa0expert witness.\\n\\nCommon witness - When he describes the wounds on the body.\\n\\nExpert witness - When he says that the wounds were antemortem or post-mortem, or they were suicidal, homicidal, or accidental, or gives an opinion regarding the cause of death.\\n\\n* A\xa0hostile witness\xa0is one who has some interest or\xa0motive\xa0for\xa0concealing part\xa0of the truth or for\xa0giving completely false evidence\xa0(Sec 227 of BNS/Sec 191 IPC). There must be some evidence to prove that the witness has retracted from his earlier statement or is not desirous of disclosing the truth, or has exhibited an element of hostility. If a witness is declared as hostile,\xa0he can be\xa0cross-examined by the same side\'s lawyer.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2c0d28a22a","n":305,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Revised Identify the plant shown below:","options":["Digitalis Purpurea","Aconite Napellus","Nicotiana Tabacum","Cerbera Thevetia"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cerbera Thevetia\\n\\nThe plant shown above is the Cerbera Thevetia.\\n\\nThevetin is a cardiac poison derived from the plant Cerbera Thevetia (yellow oleander). All parts of the plant are poisonous. Symptoms of intoxication are characterized by vomiting, diarrhea, headache, dizziness, mydriasis, and loss of muscular power. Cardiovascular signs include a weak and irregular pulse rate with hypotension. Possible complications are heart block and cardiorespiratory failure.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nDigoxin is a cardiac poison derived from the roots, leaves, and seeds of the plant Digitalis Purpurea (shown below).\\n\\nAconite is a cardiac poison derived mainly from the root and root tubers of the plant Aconite Napellus (shown below).\\n\\nNicotine is a plant alkaloid derived from the\xa0Nicotiana Tabacum plant shown in the image below.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/8772ccbba221b22b5316.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a2638755ff4718e040a0.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/ccb364f7811beba953ca.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/58d344c3464f601afc7f.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-a3a2543013","n":306,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A 15-year-old boy presents to the ER with complaints of muscle twitching and tremors. He gives a history of consuming crushed seeds from a plant. Shortly after, he begins to experience refractory seizures and assumes a characteristic posture with arching of the back and rigid extension of the limbs. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Tetanus","Clostridium","Botulism","Strychnine poisoning"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Strychnine poisoning\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette suggests the diagnosis of strychnine poisoning.\\n\\nStrychnine poisoning occurs if a person ingests crushed seeds of Strychnos nux vomica. If swallowed uncrushed, the seeds of nux vomica have no poisonous action. The fatal dose is 15–30\xa0mg of strychnine or one crushed seed.\\n\\nStrychnine is a competitive antagonist of glycine at the postganglionic receptors in the motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord. This leads to increased activity in the motor neurons.\\n\\nPatients typically present with\\n\\n* Convulsions are clonic initially but eventually become tonic.\\n\\n* Risus sardonicus results from the contraction of the jaws and facial muscles, in which the corners of the mouth are drawn back.\\n\\n* The convulsions are most marked in anti-gravity muscles so that the body typically arches in hyperextension, the posture known as opisthotonus.\\n\\n* If the spasm involves the abdominal muscles, the body bends forward (emprosthotonos), or to the side (pleurosthotonus).\\n\\n* If death is preceded by a severe convulsion, as in strychnine poisoning, it causes an increase in the body temperature known as postmortem caloricity.\\n\\nThe first step in the management of strychnine poisoning is to control convulsions with benzodiazepines or barbiturates to control seizures, and the patients should be placed in a dark room to avoid triggering further convulsions. Activated charcoal can be used to adsorb unabsorbed poison after a thorough stomach wash. Acidification of urine can be done to increase the excretion of strychnine.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Tetanus - Tetanus toxin initially binds to receptors on the presynaptic membranes of motor neurons and migrates to the spinal cord and brainstem. The toxin diﬀuses to inhibitory neurons (γ-aminobutyric acid) from the brainstem and inhibits its function.\\n\\nOption B: Clostridium - Clostridium tetani secrete 2 types of toxins, namely, tetanolysin and tetanospasmin. \xa0The toxin peptide degrades synaptobrevin, a protein required for the attachment of neurotransmitter vesicles on the presynaptic membrane, before its release. The motor neurons are not inhibited as the release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters glycine and GABA is blocked. This causes hyperreflexia, muscle spasms, and spastic paralysis result.\\n\\nOption C: Botulism - Botulinum toxin targets the presynaptic terminals of motor neurons and inhibits the release of acetylcholine. As a result, muscle paralysis occurs, leading to the temporary weakening or cessation of muscle activity in the affected area.\\n\\nPearl PF0407: Signs and Symptoms of Strychnine Poisoning\\n\\n* Mnemonic to remember signs and symptoms.\\n\\n* We know that\xa0CRAMPing of muscles leads to the exhaustive death of the person in strychnine poisoning. Therefore:\\n\\n* Convulsions\\n\\n* Reflex increased\\n\\n* Awful taste\\n\\n* Mind is conscious\\n\\n* Pupils dilated\\n\\n* Clonic-tonic convulsions\\n\\n* Opisthotonos: arching back\\n\\n* Emprosthotonos: arching forward\\n\\n* Pleurothotonus: bending sideward\\n\\n* Risus Sardonicus: fixed contraction of the facial muscles resulting in a peculiar distorted grin.\\n\\nMedico-legal implications:.\\n\\n* Although a poison, in relatively low doses can be used as a medical stimulant for respiration.\\n\\nStrychnine poisoning case may resemble tetanus.\\n\\nStrychnine plant\\n\\nStrychnine seeds","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/161fbfdaf4a5110cbd53.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/2cf556501953462279ca.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-8cce142591","n":307,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A family was found dead in a closed room without windows, and smoke coming from a woodfire was present. Which of the following features is likely to be seen on their bodies?","options":["Cherry red hypostasis","Brown pigmention","Cyanosis","Blackish discoloration"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cherry red hypostasis\\n\\nIn the case of a family found dead in a closed room with smoke from a wood fire, there is likely to be cherry red discoloration on their bodies. This discoloration is due to carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning, which can occur from smoke in an enclosed space.\\n\\nThe colour of postmortem staining in CO poisoning is cherry red. Carbon monoxide is a byproduct of incomplete combustion of organic materials, such as wood. When inhaled, CO binds to hemoglobin in the blood with an affinity approximately 240 times greater than that of oxygen, forming carboxyhemoglobin (COHb). This reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood, leading to tissue hypoxia.\\n\\nThe cherry red discolouration is a hallmark of CO poisoning. This occurs because COHb imparts a bright red colour, distinct from the darker hue associated with deoxygenated hemoglobin.\\n\\nPostmortem hypostasis/Postmortem staining/Postmortem lividity/Livor mortis/Suggilation refers to bluish-purple or reddish-purple discolouration of the superficial layers of the skin due to the collection of blood in toneless capillaries.\\n\\nIt is an early postmortem change that occurs due to engorged vessels showing through the skin seen on the dependent parts due to the pooling of blood under the effect of gravity.\\n\\nThis staining starts as mottled patches 1-3 hours following death and develops to a uniform area of staining in 3-6 hours. Full development and fixation of hypostasis occur within 6–12 hours. This finding usually develops within 4 hours and persists until putrefaction. Fixation of hypostasis occurs earlier in the summer. It is delayed in asphyxial deaths and in intracranial lesions.\\n\\nPearl PF1096: Color of Postmortem Hypostasis in Various Conditions\\n\\nPoisoning/Condition\\nPostmortem staining\\n\\nCarbon monoxide\\n\\nCherry red\\n\\nCyanides\\n\\nBrick red/dark pink\\n\\nPotassium chlorate\\n\\nChocolate brown\\n\\nPhosphorous\\nDark brown\\n\\nNitrites\\n\\nRed brown\\n\\nHydrogen sulphide\\n\\nBluish green\\n\\nOpium\\nGrayish/black\\n\\nAsphyxia\\nDark purple\\n\\nHypothermia\\nBright pink\\n\\nSeptic abortion (Cl. Welchii infection)\\nBrown/pale bronze","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-528c179aa4","n":308,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A man who was arrested died while he was in police custody. Who will conduct the inquest in this case?","options":["Superintendent of police","Coroner","Medical examiner","Judicial Magistrate"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Judicial Magistrate\\n\\nA magistrate inquest will be carried out for death under police custody.\\n\\nBNSS 196/Section 176 Cr. P.C. deals with magistrate inquest in which the cause of death is not under natural circumstances. Magistrate inquest may be conducted by the District Magistrate, Sub-Divisional Magistrate, Tahsildar, or any other Executive Magistrate empowered by the State Government.\\n\\nWhile conducting this inquiry, the Magistrate has all the powers that they would normally have when investigating an offence. This ensures an independent and impartial inquiry into the circumstances of\xa0death, disappearance,\xa0or\xa0rape\xa0occurring while a person or woman is in police or judicial custody.\\n\\nA death is considered\xa0custodial when it occurs in any form of detention, including while the person is being arrested, detained, escaping, or attempting to escape from custody.\xa0 The Judicial Magistrate or Metropolitan Magistrate conducting the inquiry must, within\xa024 hours of the death, forward the body to the nearest civil surgeon or other qualified medical professional appointed by the government for postmortem examination.\\n\\nIndications for Magistrate Inquest:\\n\\n* Death under police custody\\n\\n* Death due to police firing\\n\\n* Death in prison, reformatories, borstal schools\\n\\n* Dowry death\\n\\n* Death in a psychiatric hospital\\n\\n* Exhumation\\n\\n* Death or disappearance of any person, or rape of a woman who is under the custody of police or any other person authorized by the court.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0194 BNSS or Section 174 CrPC deals with the police inquest. It is done in suicide, homicide, killing by any animal or machinery or accident, and death under circumstances that raise suspicion of another person committing the offence. In all cases where the police normally conduct inquests, the magistrate can hold an additional inquest, or in place of the police inquest.\\n\\nOption B: Coroner\'s Inquest is not practised in India. It is done in the U.K. and the U.S.A. The coroner, who is usually a first-class magistrate or attorney, conducts an inquest in all unnatural and suspicious deaths. The doctor is summoned to his Court to give evidence at the inquest.\\n\\nOption C: The medical examiner\'s system is not followed in India. It is followed in certain states of the USA and Japan. A medical practitioner known as a \'Medical Examiner\' is appointed to perform the functions of a Coroner. He gets first-hand information on the investigation and crime scene, which, when correlated with autopsy findings, carves out a better picture.\\nThough he is not entitled to any judicial powers, the inquest conducted in the presence of a doctor makes it superior to any other method.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1445e87dfd","n":309,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A patient was brought to the emergency department following a road traffic accident. During an autopsy, apparent bleeding was seen on the surface of his brain. Which type of hemorrhage would not be washed away under direct running water?","options":["Epidural hemorhage","Subdural hemorrhage","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Intraparenchymal hemorrhage"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Subarachnoid hemorrhage\\n\\nSubarachnoid haemorrhage will persist when washed under running water during an autopsy.\\n\\nSubarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH) is bleeding in the space between the brain and the arachnoid membrane which is the subarachnoid space. During an autopsy, the blood from a subarachnoid haemorrhage is not easily washed away by running water. This is because it is located in this deep layer, which can trap the blood within the brain\'s sulci and gyri.\\n\\nWhile doing the autopsy, there is typically some fresh blood present. However, it cannot be washed off because the blood is situated beneath the very thin and transparent arachnoid layer.\\n\\nThis makes the blood from a SAH less likely to be displaced compared to haemorrhages located in other regions, such as epidural or subdural spaces. Thus, despite running water during an autopsy, the blood from a subarachnoid haemorrhage often remains in place, making it detectable even after washing.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5bf36b21c2","n":310,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A vegetable vendor was found dead in his apartment, and his body had undergone advanced stages of decomposition. Which of the following methods can be used to identify him a week later?","options":["Fingerprints","Dental records","Face recognition","DNA profiling"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. DNA profiling\\n\\nDNA profiling is the most reliable method for identifying a deceased person whose body has undergone advanced decomposition.\\n\\nDNA profiling can also aid in identifying skeletonized or severely decomposed human remains. This process typically involves comparing the genetic profile from the remains with reference samples from relatives, often the victim\'s parents.\\n\\nDNA profiling, also known as genetic fingerprinting, is a forensic method used to identify individuals based on their unique genetic makeup, is precise and has high specificity. While decomposition can damage biological tissues, DNA can often be recovered from highly decomposed bodies. DNA can be extracted from bones, teeth, or preserved tissue samples.\\n\\nIn contrast, other methods like visual identification (face recognition), fingerprint analysis, or dental records may be compromised or unfeasible when a body is in an advanced state of decomposition, making DNA profiling the preferred and most reliable choice for identification in such scenarios.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7da6bcde65","n":311,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A young female patient presented with a burn injury. She is covered with burns on both her upper limbs, the front of her chest and the front of her abdomen. Calculate the percentage of burns according to the rule of nines.","options":["27%","54%","36%","45%"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 36%\\n\\nThe percentage of burns calculated in the given patient is 36%\xa0according to Wallace’s rule of nine.\\n\\nCalculation:\\n\\n* Burns on both upper limbs (9% for each limb) would add to 18%\\n\\n* Burns on the front of her chest would add to 9%\\n\\n* Burns on the front of her abdomen would add to 9%\\n\\nTherefore the total percentage of burns in the given patient is 18+9+9 = 36%.\\n\\nPearl PF0275: Wallace\'s rule of 9\\n\\nBody part\\n\\n%TBSA\\n\\nBody part\\n\\n%TBSA\\n\\nHead/neck\\n\\n9%\\n\\nLower back\\n\\n9%\\n\\nR-UL\\n\\n9%\\n\\nR- thigh\\n\\n9%\\n\\nL-UL\\n\\n9%\\n\\nL-thigh\\n\\n9%\\n\\nAnterior chest\\n\\n9%\\n\\nR- leg\\n\\n9%\\n\\nUpper back\\n\\n9%\\n\\nL- leg\\n\\n9%\\n\\nAnterior abdomen\\n\\n9%\\n\\nGenitals\\n\\n1%\\n\\nTotal\\n\\n54%\\n\\n46%","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/474c32f080e82337fb1e.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-d2720258f8","n":312,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"According to the MTP Act, termination of pregnancy due to contraceptive failure can be done up to what period of gestation?","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","18 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 20 weeks\\n\\nAccording to the MTP Act, termination of pregnancy due to contraception failure can be done up to 20 weeks of gestation.\\n\\nUpper limit of MTP:\\n\\n* Up to 24 weeks of gestation in victims of rape and incest, fetal anomalies, or congenital malformations\\n\\n* Up to 20 weeks in case of contraception failure (any female with her partner)\\n\\n* At any gestational age for substantial fetal anomalies decided by the medical board\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of an obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nOnly\xa0the\xa0patient\'s consent\xa0is required for\xa0MTP;\xa0her partner\'s consent is not required.\xa0Consent of\xa0parent/guardian\xa0is needed if the\xa0age of the\xa0patient\xa0is\xa0<18 years\xa0or if the patient is\xa0mentally ill.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age. It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint be lodged with the police.\\n\\nPearl PF1638: Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act - MTP Act 1971\\n\\nMTP Act with 2021 amendments\\n\\nIndications:\\n\\n* Therapeutic - to save the life of the mother\\n\\n* Eugenic - to prevent the\xa0risk of the child being born with serious mental or physical abnormality i.e. first-trimester viral infections, teratogenic drug intake, radiation exposure\\n\\n* Humanitarian -\xa0pregnancy resulted from rape or incest\\n\\n* Social -\xa0pregnancy resulted from failure of contraceptives\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n* Only a registered medical practitioner who is qualified (one who has assisted in the performance of 25 cases of MTP in a recognized hospital) can terminate a pregnancy.\\n\\n* MTP should be carried out in a government or government-recognized hospital.\xa0Private hospitals may take up MTP if they obtain a license from the chief medical officer of the district.\\n\\n* The termination of pregnancy by a person, who is not a registered medical practitioner (the person concerned), or in an unrecognised hospital (administrative head) shall be punished with rigorous imprisonment for a term not less than 2 years, but which may extend to 7 years.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nThe consent of the woman is required for conducting an abortion. The written consent of the guardian is required if the woman is minor or mentally ill. Consent of the husband or partner is not necessary.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age.\xa0It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint was lodged with the police.\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state\\n\\nUpper limit of MTP:\\n\\n* Up to 24 weeks of gestation in victims of rape and incest, fetal anomalies, or congenital malformations\\n\\n* Up to 20 weeks in case of contraception failure (any female with her partner)\\n\\n* At any gestational age for substantial fetal anomalies decided by the medical board\\n\\nConfidentiality:\\n\\nA register for recording the details of MTP must be kept for at least 5 years. The patient\'s name must not be entered, only the reference number is used.\xa0Unlawful disclosure of patient\'s details is punishable with 1-year imprisonment\xa0\xb1 fine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f761ed910b","n":313,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"Following the death of a person, his family who lived abroad was unable to reach him, and hence his body needed to be preserved. Which of the following substances can typically be detected in his body, even if the person did not consume the substance shortly before death?","options":["Alcohol","Aconite","Arsenic","Morphine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Alcohol\\n\\nAlcohol can normally be detected in visceral organs, even if the person did not consume the substance shortly before death.\\n\\nAfter death, the body undergoes various biological processes, including putrefaction. During putrefaction, bacteria present in the gastrointestinal tract and other tissues can ferment carbohydrates, leading to the production of alcohol.\\n\\nTherefore, the presence of alcohol in visceral organs during an autopsy can sometimes be attributed to postmortem production rather than recent ingestion.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nAconite, arsenic, and morphine are not naturally produced by the body or formed after death. These substances must be ingested or administered. Aconite and morphine would only be detected if consumed before death, and arsenic needs prior exposure to be present in the body. Finding these substances in the body indicates pre-death exposure, unlike alcohol, which can sometimes be produced after death.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-151c9c918f","n":314,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"Identify the postmortem change shown in the image below.","options":["Corneal bleed","Tache noire","Kevorkian sign","Vitreous hemorrhage"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Tache noire\\n\\nThe image shows Tache noire.\\n\\nIf the eyelids remain open for a few hours after death, a layer of cell debris and mucus forms a yellow triangle on the sclera at either side adjacent to the iris. The base of the triangle is towards the margin of the cornea and the apex is towards the canthus of the eye. This debris turns brown when the dust settles on them. It appears within 3-4 hours of death.\\n\\nOther postmortem changes occurring in the eye:\\n\\n* Cornea: Dull, and opaque due to the lack of lacrimal moistening.\\n\\n* Flaccidity of eyeballs occurs due to a fall in intraocular pressure.\\n\\n* Pupils- Soon after death, pupils are slightly dilated due to the relaxation of the iris muscles. At the onset of rigor, the pupils become constricted.\\n\\n* Increase in potassium concentration in the vitreous. It is helpful in determining the time since death.\\n\\n* Kevorkian sign(Option C)- It is characterized by fragmentation of the blood column in the retinal vessels. It appears within minutes after death and lasts for about 1 hour.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/656534fbd2a85c59e90b.webp"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/eb0f52f2871e05461dc9.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-7772cbb203","n":315,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"In a case of alcohol abuse, which of the following preservatives is used to preserve blood during an autopsy?","options":["NaF+ K oxalate","Rectified spirit","NaCl","Formalin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. NaF+ K oxalate\\n\\nSodium fluoride (NaF) (10 mg/ml) and potassium oxalate (anti-coagulant)\xa0can be used to preserve blood during an autopsy.\\n\\nSodium Fluoride (NaF) inhibits glycolysis by preventing the breakdown of glucose, which could alter the blood alcohol content. It also has antimicrobial properties to prevent the growth of bacteria and fungi and potassium oxalate acts as an anticoagulant to prevent blood clotting and helps analyse the sample.\\n\\nA preservative is not necessary if:\\n\\n* The viscera can be analysed in 24 hours\\n\\n* If the sample can be stored in a refrigerator or icebox\\n\\n* Bone, hair, nail specimens\\n\\n* Lung for inhaled poisons\\n\\n* The viscera should not be preserved in formaldehyde because extraction of non-volatile poisons becomes difficult.\\n\\nIn cases of chronic poisoning with heavy metals such as lead, arsenic, mercury, antimony, copper, etc., the shaft of a long bone, strands of scalp hair, fingernails or toenails, and a wedge of the quadriceps muscle should also be taken for sampling.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\n* NaCl (Sodium Chloride): While useful in various laboratory settings, sodium chloride does not prevent glycolysis or microbial growth in blood samples, making it unsuitable for preserving blood for alcohol content analysis.\\n\\n* Rectified Spirit: It is a highly concentrated form of ethanol. Adding rectified spirit to a blood sample intended for alcohol analysis would contaminate the sample and invalidate the results.\\n\\n* Formalin: Primarily used for preserving tissues by fixing them, formalin is not suitable for preserving blood samples for alcohol analysis, as it can interfere with the chemical assays used to measure ethanol levels.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e1fc19215b","n":316,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"In which of the following cases will gastric lavage be contraindicated?","options":["Ingestion of hydrocarbons","Ingestion of iron tablets","Within one hour of consuming an unknown poison","Strychnine poisoning"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Strychnine poisoning\\n\\nGastric lavage will be contraindicated in a case of strychnine poisoning. Strychnine is a highly toxic substance that causes severe convulsions and muscle rigidity, which can lead to complications during the procedure, such as aspiration or further injury due to the patient\'s agitation or convulsions.\\n\\nContraindications of Gastric lavage:\\n\\n* Corrosive poisonings like sulphuric acid and kerosene (except carbolic acid).\\n\\n* Convulsants/CNS stimulants (Eg: Strychnine)\\n\\n* Comatose patients\\n\\n* Volatile poison\\n\\n* Upper GI pathology (E.g: esophageal varices)\\n\\n* Hypothermic patient\\n\\n* Severe heart disease\\n\\n* Advanced Pregnancy\\n\\nPearl PF1111: Mechanism of action of corrosives\\n\\nCorrosive\\nMechanism of Action\\nType of necrosis\\n\\nStrong acids\\nHydrogen ions penetrate tisues and lead to cell death and\xa0eschar formation.\xa0This prevents further hydrogen ion penetration and protects against deeper injury.\\nCoagulation necrosis\\n\\nStrong alkalis\\nHydroxide ion easily penetrates tissues, leading to dissolution of cell membranes. Alkali are associated with deep tissue injury.\\nLiquefaction necrosis\\n\\nHydrofluoric acid\\nHydrogen ions cause direct cellular damage, as with other acids. Also, fluoride ions scavenge intracellular cations, such as calcium and magnesium and inhibit Na/K ATPase, disrupting cell membranes. This leads to\xa0hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia\xa0 and\xa0liquefaction necrosis\\nLiquefaction necrosis\\n\\nCarbolic acid\\nCarbolic acid also acts by the same mechanism as other strong acids, but it also hardens the GI mucosa, producing a\xa0leather bottle stomach and anesthetizes the nerve endings. This\xa0allows for gastric lavage.\\nCoagulation necrosis\\n\\nOxalic acid\\nActs by the same mechanism as other strong acids, but also scavenges calcium ions, leading to\xa0hypocalcemia\xa0and\xa0Calcium oxalate renal stones.\\nCoagulation necrosis","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-66f4c95022","n":317,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"Which of the following has been banned by the Honourable Supreme Court of India for being infamous among doctors in sexual assault cases?","options":["PV speculum","Foleys catherter insertion","Colposcopy","Two-finger test"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Two-finger test\\n\\nThe two-finger test has been banned by the Honourable Supreme Court of India due to its controversial and problematic nature in sexual assault cases. This test, used to assess a victim\'s sexual history and physical condition, was deemed unethical and unreliable and was criticised for being invasive and degrading, often failing to provide accurate evidence while infringing on the dignity of the survivor.\\n\\nThe Supreme Court\'s decision aimed to protect the rights of victims and ensure that forensic examinations in sexual assault cases adhere to ethical and scientific standards.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2defef15af","n":318,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A 45-year-old chronic alcoholic man presents with altered consciousness, disorientation and severe agitation. He reports his last alcohol intake was 48 hours ago. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Delirium tremens","Alcohol induced psychosis","Opioid withdrawal","Alcohol induced hallucinations"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Delirium tremens\\n\\nThe clinical scenario of a 45-year-old chronic alcoholic man with altered consciousness, disorientation and severe agitation and his last alcohol consumption 48 hours ago points to\xa0delirium tremens.\\n\\nDelirium tremens is a medical emergency that occurs during alcohol withdrawal. It is an acute condition and usually develops within 72 hours after the last drink. It has symptoms of both delirium and alcohol withdrawal. The clinical features of delirium tremens are:\\n\\n* Altered sensorium with disorientation\\n\\n* Perceptual distortions, most frequently tactile or visual hallucinations\\n\\n* Autonomic hyperactivity with tachycardia, fever, anxiety, insomnia, and hypertension. And sweating\\n\\n* Fluctuating levels of psychomotor activity, ranging from agitation to lethargy\\nAltered sleep-wake cycle\\n\\nWernicke’s encephalopathy (alcoholic encephalopathy) is associated with thiamine deficiency. Alcohol abuse may lead to thiamine deficiency because of poor nutritional habits or malabsorption. It is an acute neurological disorder characterised by:\\n\\n* Global confusion\\n\\n* Ocular abnormalities like horizontal nystagmus, gaze palsy, anisocoria, and sluggish pupillary reflex\\n\\n* Ataxia—mainly\xa0affecting the gait\\n\\n* Vestibular dysfunction\\n\\nIt may resolve with thiamine therapy or progress to Korsakoff\'s syndrome.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Alcohol-induced psychosis is a severe mental condition triggered by excessive alcohol use, leading to hallucinations, delusions, and confusion. It typically occurs during withdrawal or chronic alcohol abuse and requires immediate medical intervention.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Opioid\xa0withdrawal can cause symptoms like drug craving, feelings of temperature change, musculoskeletal pain, and GI distress. Signs of opioid withdrawal include confusion, drowsiness, vomiting, and diarrhoea. It is not typically life-threatening, especially compared to alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, which can lead to seizures or delirium tremens. Hence, opioid withdrawal is rarely fatal.\\n\\nOption D: Alcohol-induced hallucinations occur due to excessive alcohol use or withdrawal, causing false visual, auditory, or tactile perceptions. They are distinct from delirium tremens and can persist even with clear consciousness.\\n\\nPearl PF1275: Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome\\n\\nManifestation\\n\\nSymptoms/signs\\n\\nOnset since last drink (hours)\\n\\nTremulousness\\n\\nContinuous tremor of > 8 Hz (or)\xa0Bursts of tremor activity < 8 Hz\\n\\n6–8\\n\\nAlcoholic hallucinosis/\\n\\nPsychotic and perceptual symptoms\\n\\nVisual, auditory or tactile; intact orientation; stable vital signs.\\n\\n8-12\\n\\nSeizures\xa0(rum fits)\\n\\nSingle or multiple generalized tonic-clonic\\n\\n12–24\\n\\nDelirium tremens\\n\\nConfusion, agitation, fever, tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis,\xa0impaired orientation\\n\\n< 72","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8c842f0494","n":319,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A dead person was discovered in a swimming pool. On autopsy, his stomach and lungs were full of water, and there was mucoid froth in his nose and mouth. What is the most likely cause of death?","options":["Dry drowning","Wet drowning","Near drowning","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Wet drowning\\n\\nThe above case scenario with frothing is suggestive of wet drowning.\\n\\nGiven below are the features of antemortem drowning:\\n\\n* Froth –\xa0The presence of copious amounts of fine, shaving-lather-like froth at the mouth and the nostrils is the most characteristic finding of antemortem drowning. It is caused by the vigorous agitation of the tracheal and bronchial mucous secretions, edema fluid, and alveolar surfactant during respiratory efforts.\\n\\n* Cutis anserina – The contraction of the erector pilorum muscles causes puckering and a grainy appearance of the skin. This resembles goose skin; hence, it is called cutis anserina.\\n\\n* Emphysema aquosum – overinflation\xa0of the lungs\\nPaltauf’s spots – subpleural\xa0hemorrhages that reflect hemolysis within intra-alveolar hemorrhages\\n\\n* Washerwoman’s hand –\xa0Maceration of the skin on the palms and soles gives a wrinkled, sodden, and bleached appearance due to submersion.\\n\\n* Cadaveric spasm – Gravel, weed, soil, grass, etc. are firmly held in the clenched hands due to cadaveric spasm. This is a vital sign of antemortem drowning. The material also indicates the place of submersion.\\n\\n* Rigour mortis – This appears early in cases of a violent antemortem struggle.\\n\\n* Face – It is bloated and discoloured, with cyanosis.\\n\\n* Eyes – Conjunctiva are suffused and pupils dilated.\\n\\n* Tongue – It may be swollen and protruded.\\n\\n* Antemortem injuries – These may be sustained by striking objects, such as rocks, during the fall into the water body. Examination for such injuries must be performed only after the body is dry.\\n\\nInternal findings:\\n\\n* Lungs – They appear voluminous and distended, showing ballooning. The cut section will exude copious amounts of frothy, bloodstained fluid.\\n\\n* Larynx, trachea, and bronchioles – The presence of sand, silt, and aquatic vegetation in the trachea and lower respiratory tract are characteristic findings of antemortem drowning.\\n\\n* Heart and blood vessels – A Gettler test can be done to estimate the chloride content in the right and left sides of the heart to differentiate between freshwater and saltwater drowning.\\n\\n* Diatom test –\xa0Diatoms are microscopic algae. Their presence in the organs, particularly in the bone marrow, is suggestive of antemortem drowning.\\n\\n* Stomach and small intestines – These organs may contain water in some cases. Water present in the small intestine is positive evidence of death by drowning.\\n\\n* Middle ear – The presence of water in the middle ear is highly suggestive of antemortem drowning.\\n\\n* Brain – It is congested and oedematous, with loss of the grey-white junction.\\n\\nOther options:\\nOption A: Water would not be found in the lungs or stomach in the case of dry drowning.\\nOption C: A near drowning occurs when a person survives a drowning incident, even if complications cause their death later. This term does not apply in this scenario because the person has already died.\\nOption D: In this instance, cardiac arrest is a consequence rather than the main cause. Due to oxygen deprivation, the drowning incident caused the cardiac arrest. The initial cause of drowning is directly indicated by the presence of water in the stomach and lungs.\\n\\nPearl PF0229: Differentiating features between Antemortem amd Postmortem drowning\\n\\nAntemortem drowning\\nPostmortem drowning\\n\\nFoam/froth at nostrils or mouth\\nNot present\\n\\nCadaveric spasm\\nNot present\\n\\nLungs usually are voluminous, bulky, water-logged and over-inflated ,called as emphysema aquosum.\\nPassive collection of water\xa0 in the lungs without any formation of columns of froth,has been called as oedema aquosum.\\n\\nPresence of foam, fluid, debris, weeds, vegetation, etc. in the bronchi and bronchioles.\\nForeign matter may passively find access only to the upper respiratory passage.\\n\\nDemonstration of diatoms in the lungs, liver, brain, bone marrow, etc.\\nDiatoms may reach the lungs but not to the distant organs because of absence of circulation.\\n\\nStomach may contain water and foreign material like sand, weed, dirt etc which may be swallowed while struggling for life.\\nWater could enter the stomach. However, quantity of foreign matter may become the decisive issue.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-90020ab122","n":320,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A female body was brought in for autopsy. The body was discovered in a closed room with a heat stove running during the dead body recovery, with cherry red discolouration on the skin and mucosal regions. What is the most probable cause of her death?","options":["CO poisoning","Cyanide poisoning","H2S poisoning","Methane poisoning"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. CO poisoning\\n\\nIn the given case of a female found dead in a closed room with smoke from a heat stove, with cherry red discolouration on the skin and mucosal regions, the most probable cause of her death is carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning, which can occur from smoke in an enclosed space.\\n\\nThe colour of postmortem staining in CO poisoning is cherry red. Carbon monoxide is a byproduct of incomplete combustion of organic materials, such as wood. When inhaled, CO binds to haemoglobin in the blood with an affinity approximately 240 times greater than that of oxygen, forming carboxyhaemoglobin (COHb). This reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood, leading to tissue hypoxia.\\n\\nThe cherry red discolouration is a hallmark of CO poisoning. This occurs because COHb imparts a bright red colour, distinct from the darker hue associated with deoxygenated haemoglobin.\\n\\nPostmortem hypostasis/postmortem staining/postmortem lividity/livor mortis/suggilation refers to bluish-purple or reddish-purple discolouration of the superficial layers of the skin due to the collection of blood in toneless capillaries.\\n\\nIt is an early postmortem change that occurs due to engorged vessels showing through the skin seen on the dependent parts due to the pooling of blood under the effect of gravity.\\n\\nThis staining starts as mottled patches 1-3 hours following death and develops to a uniform area of staining in 3-6 hours. Full development and fixation of hypostasis occur within 6–12 hours. This finding usually develops within 4 hours and persists until putrefaction. Fixation of hypostasis occurs earlier in the summer. It is delayed in asphyxial deaths and in intracranial lesions.\\n\\nPearl PF1096: Color of Postmortem Hypostasis in Various Conditions\\n\\nPoisoning/Condition\\nPostmortem staining\\n\\nCarbon monoxide\\n\\nCherry red\\n\\nCyanides\\n\\nBrick red/dark pink\\n\\nPotassium chlorate\\n\\nChocolate brown\\n\\nPhosphorous\\nDark brown\\n\\nNitrites\\n\\nRed brown\\n\\nHydrogen sulphide\\n\\nBluish green\\n\\nOpium\\nGrayish/black\\n\\nAsphyxia\\nDark purple\\n\\nHypothermia\\nBright pink\\n\\nSeptic abortion (Cl. Welchii infection)\\nBrown/pale bronze","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-40dfdf4ddc","n":321,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A male went missing from his house and was not found for a long time, so his son assumed he was dead. His son subsequently claimed ownership of his father\'s property. As per Section 111 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), when will the son be able to inherit the paternal property?","options":["5 years","2 years","7 years","10 years"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 7 years\\n\\nAs per Section 111 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), the son will be able to inherit the paternal property after seven years.\\n\\nThe important sections under BSA include:\\n\\nBSA Section\\n\\nProvision for\\n\\n2\\n\\nDefinitions of terms such as \'evidence\', \'fact\',\xa0and \'conclusive proof\'\\n\\n26 (i)\\n\\nDying declaration\\n\\n39\\n\\nExpert witness\\n\\n116\\n\\nBirth during marriage: conclusive proof of legitimacy\\n\\n157\\n\\nHostile witness\\n\\nPearl PF2173: Sexual Offences\\n\\nBNS Section\\nSection it deals with\\n\\nPunishment\\n\\nPrevious IPC section\\n\\n63\\nDefinition of Rape\\n\\n375\\n\\n64(1)\\n\\nPunishment for Rape\\n\\nRigorous imprisonment of not less than 10 years which may extend to imprisonment for life.\\n\\n376\\n\\n65(1)\\nPersons committing the offence of rape on a woman under16 years of age\\nRigorous imprisonment of not less than 20 years which may extend to imprisonment for life.\\n376\\n\\n66\\nThe person committing an offense of rape and inflicting injury which causes death or causes the woman to be in a persistent vegetative state.\\nRigorous imprisonment of not less than 20 years may extend to imprisonment for life or with death.\\n376A\\n\\n65(2)\\nThe person committing an offense of rape on a woman under 12 years of age\\nRigorous imprisonment of not less than 20 years but which may extend to imprisonment for life with fine or with death\\n376AB\\n\\n67\\nSexual intercourse by the husband upon his wife during separation, without her consent\\nImprisonment for not less than 2 years but which may extend to 7 years and with a fine.\\n376B\\n\\n68\\nSexual intercourse by a person in authority (police officer, public servant, member of armed forces, management or staff of a jail, remand home or other places of custody and management or staff of a hospital) who induces or seduces any female inmate of such jail, remand home, place, or institution to have sexual intercourse with him, such sexual intercourse not amounting to the offense of rape.\\nRigorous imprisonment for not less than 5 years but which may extend to 10 years and with a fine.\\n376C\\n\\n70(1)\\nGang Rape\\nRigorous imprisonment for not less than 20 years which may extend to imprisonment for life with fine\\n376D\\n\\n70(2)\\nGang rape on a woman under 18 years of age\\nRigorous imprisonment for life and with fine\\n376DA\\n\\n71\\nRepeat offenders\\nImprisonment for life or with death\\n376E\\n\\n74\\nAssault or use of criminal force to woman with intent to outrage her modesty\\nImprisonment for not less than one year, extending up to five years and with fine.\\n354\\n\\nAs per the The Gazette of India, from the Ministry of Law and Justice, Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita Document 2023\\n\\nPearl PF2152: Consent\\n\\nBNS Section\\n\\nProvisions under the Act\\n\\nPrevious IPC section\\n\\n25\\n\\nAct not intended and not known to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt, done by consent is not an offense.\\n\\n87\\n\\n26\\n\\nAn act done by consent in good faith for a person’s benefit is not an offense.\\n\\n88\\n\\n27\\n\\nAn act done in good faith for the benefit of a child under 12 years of age or an insane person, with the consent of a parent or guardian is not an offense.\\n\\n89\\n\\n28\\n\\nConsent is invalid if\\n\\n• Given under fear or misconception.\\n• Given by an insane person\\n\\n90\\n\\n29\\n\\nThe exceptions in sections 25, 26, and 27 do not extend to acts that are offenses independently of any harm which they may cause, or be intended to cause or be known to be likely to cause, to the person giving the consent, or on whose behalf the consent is given.\\n\\n30\\n\\nThe act is done in good faith without consent in\xa0the following situations:\\n\\n• Emergency (No need to take consent during emergencies)\\n• The person is incapable of giving consent\\n• The person has no guardian to give consent\\n\\n92\\n\\n33\\n\\nAct causing slight harm is not an offense\\n\\n95\\n\\nAs per the The Gazette of India, from the Ministry of Law and Justice, Bharata Nyaya Samhita Document 2023\\n\\nPearl PF1600: Criminal Responsibilities\\n\\nBNS Section\\n\\nPrevious IPC Section\\n\\nDeals with\\n\\n18\\n\\n-\\n\\nA person is not liable for an accident in doing a lawful act\\n\\n20\\n\\n82\\n\\nChild under 7 years is not liable for act.\\n\\n21\\n\\n83\\n\\nChild of 7–12 years if not attained proper mental maturity is not liable for act.\\n\\n22\\n\\n84 \xa0-McNaughten\'s rule\\n\\nA person of unsound mind is not liable for act.\\n\\n23\\n\\n85\\n\\nA person who under involuntary intoxication has commited an act is not liable for the act.\\n\\n24\\n\\n86\\n\\nA person who under voluntary intoxication commits an act is liable for the act.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2214db6df9","n":322,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A patient is brought in a comatose state with low blood pressure, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils. What is the appropriate antidote in this situation?","options":["Naloxone","Naltrexone","Flumazenil","Atropine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Naloxone\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette suggests opioid poisoning, which is treated with naloxone.\\n\\nNaloxone is a competitive pure opioid antagonist that reverses all effects of an opioid overdose except sedation. It is a pan-antagonist with an affinity for all known opioid receptors. It stimulates the release of prolactin in women.\\n\\nOpioid-induced respiratory depression is mediated by the action of \xb5 receptors (\xb52) in the brainstem respiratory centres.\xa0Naloxone is an opioid antagonist that can reverse the respiratory depression induced by opioids.\\n\\nOpioid Overdose:\\n\\nSymptoms:\\n\\n* Triad of coma, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils (miosis)\\n\\n* Hypotension\\n\\n* Bradycardia\\n\\n* Shallow breathing\\n\\n* Hypothermia\\n\\nCharacteristics of opioid toxicity:\\n\\n* Opioid intoxication toxidrome—respiratory and mental status depression, analgesia, miosis, orthostatic hypotension, nausea and vomiting, histamine release resulting in localized urticaria and bronchospasm, ileus secondary to decreased GI motility, and urinary retention secondary to increased vesical sphincter tone.\\n\\n* Miosis is not universally present from intoxication with every opioid; normal or even enlarged pupils have been documented secondary to diphenoxylate, meperidine, morphine, pentazocine, and propoxyphene toxicity.\\n\\n* Opioid-induced acute lung injury, previously known as non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, is an uncommon complication associated with heroin overdose.\\n\\nAirway protection and ventilatory maintenance are the most important treatment steps for opioid intoxications because respiratory depression is the major morbidity and the cause of essentially all the mortality.\\n\\n* After adequate ventilation is ensured, administer\xa0naloxone.\\n\\n* A single dose of activated charcoal can be administered in case opioid ingestion occurred within one hour.\\n\\nOpioid\xa0withdrawal symptoms (mnemonic—O MY WITHDRAWALS):\\n\\n* Mydriasis\\n\\n* Yawning\\n\\n* Weakness\\n\\n* Insomnia, irritability\\n\\n* Tremors, tachycardia\\n\\n* Hyperthermia, hypertension\\n\\n* Diarrhoea\\n\\n* Rhinorrhea\\n\\n* Apneic\xa0attacks\\n\\n* Weight loss\\n\\n* Anxiety\\n\\n* Lacrimation\\n\\n* Sweating\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Naltrexone is a long-acting, pure opioid antagonist used for maintenance therapy in opioid and alcohol dependence, but it is not suitable for acute overdose because it is oral and slow-acting.\\n\\nOption C: Flumazenil acts as an antagonist to benzodiazepines and is employed to counteract their sedative effects or to manage benzodiazepine overdose in emergency situations.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Atropine is used in the treatment of cholinergic toxicity due to organophosphates (DDT, parathion, malathion). Symptoms of cholinergic toxicity include bradycardia, respiratory depression, and autonomic symptoms of salivation, lacrimation, urination, and defecation.\\n\\nPearl PF0755: Pure opioid antagonists\\n\\nNaloxone: A short-acting opioid antagonist with a duration of action of 1-2 hours. Used in opioid overdose and respiratory depression in newborn due to perinatal opioid use.\\n\\nNaltrexone: A Long-acting opioid antagonist with a with effects lasting till 48 hours. Used for prevention of relapse in opioid addiction treatment. It is associated with hepatotoxicity. It does not suppress craving in opioid addiction but suppresses craving in chronic alcoholics.\\n\\nNalmefene: A Long-acting opioid antagonist with a half-life of 8-10 hours administered intravenously. Used in opioid overdose like naloxone.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-63daec2e9f","n":323,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A woman underwent surgery, but the surgeon discovered a cyst and removed her ovaries. She is of reproductive age with an incomplete family. What consent was violated?","options":["Informed consent","Implied consent","Res ipsa loquitur","Doctrine of anticipation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Informed consent\\n\\nThis kind of consent is informed consent\xa0since the patient has been made aware of the benefits and complications and has agreed to the procedure.\\n\\nInformed consent is one given by the patient after understanding the nature of the condition, proposed treatment, outcomes of the recommended treatment, alternative options to treatment, and the\xa0risks and benefits involved. The patient can accept or reject the procedure. This is also called the rule of full disclosure.\\n\\n\\"Consent\\" refers to a voluntary agreement, compliance, or permission. It can be expressed or implied.\xa0Expressed consent can be oral or written and refers to consent in which the terms are clearly stated in distinct and explicit language.\\n\\n\\"Opt out\\" refers to a patient\'s refusal of the procedure, test, or treatment. It is the physician\'s duty to disclose the risks or consequences of refusal adequately and appropriately to the patient.\\n\\nOption B: Implied consent is that which is not written or expressly stated. It is implied that the patient is giving consent to examination in a general sense. For example, when a patient visits the hospital with an illness, it implies that he has given consent to a physical examination to find out the nature of his illness. Another example of implied consent is when a patient exposes his arm for injection. It is legally effective.\\n\\nOption C: The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur (the thing or the act speaks for itself) is a situation whereby an obvious gross act of negligence leads to damage to the patient. For example, prescribing high doses of NSAIDs to a patient with a history of perforated peptic ulcer. The negligent act is so obvious that the patient need not prove the negligence.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0According to BNS Section 30 (IPC 92), a doctor is permitted to perform life-saving procedures on a patient with life-threatening injuries. For example, in a case where conservative measures have failed to control intractable postpartum haemorrhage, an emergency hysterectomy can be performed without obtaining consent in order to save the patient\'s life. The guiding principle is whether the delay in treatment in order to obtain consent would result in harm to the patient. This is the doctrine of anticipation. Under some circumstances, like emergencies, informed consent may be neither possible nor necessary.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-38ed0451a7","n":324,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"Following exposure to a blast, a patient presents with lung injury and tympanic membrane rupture. Which level of blast wave effect is responsible for these injuries?","options":["Secondary","Primary","Quaternary","Tertiary"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Primary\\n\\nThe primary level of blast wave effect is responsible for these injuries.\\n\\nA blast causes the most damage at an interface between tissues in contact with the atmosphere, which is why the lung usually suffers the most severe damage. Lungs are considered to be severely damaged organs in the air due to variations in density between the alveolar walls and the contained air.\\n\\nBlast injury is a complex type of physical trauma resulting from direct or indirect exposure to an explosion. Primary blast injuries result from the rapid overpressure or shock waves produced by an explosion. These injuries result from the dramatic changes in barometric pressure projecting outwards from the point of detonation. They predominantly cause damage to air-filled hollow organs of the body from rapid pressure change (barotrauma). This includes the middle ear, lungs, and GIT.\\n\\nBlast injuries are classified as:\\n\\n* Primary injuries – effect of transmitted blast waves on gas-containing structures. External injuries are absent and are characterised by internal injuries. The middle ear, lungs, and gastrointestinal tract are the most susceptible to this.\\n\\n* Secondary injuries – injuries caused by shrapnel and other airborne debris propelled by the explosion. These injuries may affect any part of the body and sometimes result in penetrating trauma. Most casualties are caused by secondary injuries.\\n\\n* Tertiary injuries – caused by blast wind that can throw victims against solid objects.\\n\\n* Quaternary injuries – all other injuries caused by explosions are not included in the above three categories. e.g., flash burns, crush injuries, respiratory effects due to toxic dust, gas, etc.\\n\\nThe most sensitive and most frequently injured hollow organs are the tympanic membranes and lungs.\\n\\nBlast damage to the lungs is the most common cause of life-threatening injury following an explosion. Solid organs are the most resistant to blast injuries, as the parenchymal disruption requires very high levels of overpressure. The liver is the least vulnerable to blast wave (primary blast) injury.\\n\\nPearl PF2146: Types of Blast Injuries\\n\\nBlast injury type\\n\\nMechanism of injury\\n\\nHealth impact\\n\\nPrimary\\n\\nRapid crushing over pressure\\n\\nTympanic membrane (most common), Lung (2nd most common), eyes, GIT\\n\\nSecondary\\n\\nFlying debris\\n\\nMARSHAL’s triad (triad of abrasions, contuisions, lacerations)\\n\\nTertiary\\n\\nBlast wind\\n\\nVictim is thrown in air and strikes other objects leading to fracture and blunt trauma\\n\\nQuaternary\\n\\nAny complicating factor not in the other three categories\\n\\nBurns, histotoxic anoxia, and crush injuries\\n\\n* The specific pulmonary injury of air blast is known as blast lung.\\n\\n* When the explosion is in the water the pressure changes are called underwater blast. Intestinal damage is very common in underwater blast injuries.\\n\\n* Solid blast refers to the wave of energy that spreads through the rigid structure when an explosive is detonated near it and people in contact with its structure can be injured.\\n\\n* Caecum is most sensitive to intestinal blast injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-82c34fb0d4","n":325,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"In a bomb blast explosion, a person suffered a crush injury as a building fell on top of him. Which type of bomb blast injury is most commonly observed?","options":["Quaternary","Tertiary","Secondary","Primary"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Quaternary\\n\\nA building crushing a person classifies as a quarternary blast injury.\\n\\nBlast injuries are classified as:\\n\\n* Primary injuries – effect\xa0of transmitted blast waves on gas-containing structures. External injuries are absent and are characterised by internal injuries. The middle ear, lungs, and gastrointestinal tract are the most susceptible to this.\\n\\n* Secondary injuries –\xa0injuries caused by shrapnel and other airborne debris propelled by the explosion. These injuries may affect any part of the body and sometimes result in penetrating trauma. Most casualties are caused by secondary injuries.\\n\\n* Tertiary injuries – caused\xa0by blast wind that can throw victims against solid objects.\\n\\n* Quaternary injuries –\xa0all other injuries caused by explosions are not included in the above three categories. e.g., flash burns, crush injuries, respiratory effects due to toxic dust, gas, etc.\\n\\nPearl PF2146: Types of Blast Injuries\\n\\nBlast injury type\\n\\nMechanism of injury\\n\\nHealth impact\\n\\nPrimary\\n\\nRapid crushing over pressure\\n\\nTympanic membrane (most common), Lung (2nd most common), eyes, GIT\\n\\nSecondary\\n\\nFlying debris\\n\\nMARSHAL’s triad (triad of abrasions, contuisions, lacerations)\\n\\nTertiary\\n\\nBlast wind\\n\\nVictim is thrown in air and strikes other objects leading to fracture and blunt trauma\\n\\nQuaternary\\n\\nAny complicating factor not in the other three categories\\n\\nBurns, histotoxic anoxia, and crush injuries\\n\\n* The specific pulmonary injury of air blast is known as blast lung.\\n\\n* When the explosion is in the water the pressure changes are called underwater blast. Intestinal damage is very common in underwater blast injuries.\\n\\n* Solid blast refers to the wave of energy that spreads through the rigid structure when an explosive is detonated near it and people in contact with its structure can be injured.\\n\\n* Caecum is most sensitive to intestinal blast injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-83271e8a9f","n":326,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"On the same day, a doctor received two summonses: one to appear in court as an expert witness in a rape and murder case, and another for medical negligence after amputating the wrong leg of a patient. What should the physician do in this situation?","options":["Attend the summons for the murder case","Attend the summons for the medical negligence case","Attend the court for the summons received first and inform the other court accordingly","Inform both the courts and wait"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Attend the summons for the murder case\\n\\nIf a witness is summoned by both a criminal and a civil court on the same day, priority should be given to attending the summons for the criminal case first, which is the murder and rape case in this scenario. He should also notify the civil court of his inability to attend, providing valid reasons.\\n\\nA summons is a document compelling the attendance of a witness in a court of law under penalty, on a particular day and time, to give evidence.\\n\\nIn this situation, the physician has a legal and ethical obligation to appear in court as an expert witness for the rape and murder case. This is a criminal case, and a summons to testify in a criminal proceeding is given priority. The medical negligence case is a civil case, and it is of a lower priority.\xa0The principle here is that the state\'s interest in a criminal prosecution, particularly a serious one involving a felony like murder, outweighs the private interests in a civil lawsuit.\\n\\nPriority of summons:\\n\\nCourts\\nPriority\\n\\nHigher courts vs lower courts\\nHigher courts\\n\\nCriminal courts vs civil courts\\nCriminal courts\\n\\nCourts of equal status\\nThe court from which the summons was first received\\n\\nCommon IPC sections dealing with summons include:\\n\\nIPC Section\\n\\nBNS Section\\n\\nDeals with\\n\\n172\\n\\n206\\n\\nAbsconding to avoid service of summons or other proceedings\\n\\n174\\n\\n208\\n\\nNon-attendance in obedience to an order from a public servant.\\n\\n176\\n\\n211\\n\\nOmission to give notice/information to public servant by a person legally bound to give it\\n\\n177\\n\\n212\\n\\nFurnishing false information","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e7da313253","n":327,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"The railway police has brought a person with suspected poisoning. On examination, he was confused with slurred speech, hot skin, dry mouth, and dilated pupils. What is the probable poisoning?","options":["Cocaine","Datura","Cannabis","Alcohol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Datura\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0Datura poisoning.\\n\\nDatura has two main types: Datura alba with white flowers and Datura nigra with purple flowers. The fruit is spiny and contains many small, yellowish-brown seeds. All parts of the plant are toxic, especially the seeds and fruits. Datura contains alkaloids like atropine, hyoscyamine, and scopolamine, which are potent anticholinergic agents. They block muscarinic receptors both peripherally and centrally, resulting in the classical anticholinergic toxidrome.\\n\\nThis typical presentation is seen in children who accidentally ingest datura seeds, especially from roadside or railway plants, where datura often grows wild—hence known as \\"railway poison\\". Signs of poisoning include 8 D\'s:\\n\\n* Dryness of mouth\\n\\n* Dysphagia\\n\\n* Dilated pupils\\n\\n* Drunken gait\\n\\n* Delirium\\n\\n* Dry-hot skin\\n\\n* Drowsiness\\n\\n* Death due to respiratory failure.\\n\\nGastric emptying is often delayed, and the small, plentiful seeds can become trapped among the gastrointestinal folds after ingestion. Thus gastric decontamination can be an important therapy.\\n\\nPhysostigmine, a reversible acetylcholine esterase antagonist, is an effective treatment for severe anticholinergic poisoning.\\n\\nThe given image shows a flower and fruit of datura, respectively:\\n\\nPearl PF0031: Substances of abuse\\n\\nSubstance\\n\\nWithdrawal symptoms\\n\\nIntoxication features\\n\\nAlcohol\\n\\nTremors, delirium, seizures\\n\\nBlackouts, nystagmus and slurring of speech\\n\\nBZD\\n\\nTremors,\xa0insomnia, anxiety\\n\\nLethargy and drowsiness.\\n\\nOpioid/ Heroin\\n\\nDiarrhea,\xa0yawning, mydriasis,\xa0lacrimation, piloerection, cold turkey phenomenon\\n\\nEuphoria, depressed mental status, miosis, respiratory depression, constipation.\\n\\nNicotine/ Tobacco\\n\\nIncreased appetite\\n\\nShows a biphasic pattern.\\n\\nEarly stimulatory symptoms of vomiting, hypertension and tachycardia.\xa0Later depressive symptoms of hypotension and bradycardia.\\n\\nCannabis\\n\\nIrritability, sweating, tremors, decreased appetite\\n\\nIncreased appetite, euphoria, conjunctival injection, dry mouth,\xa0panic,\xa0synesthesia, impaired time perception, amotivational\xa0syndrome, run amok and\xa0flashbacks.\\n\\nAmphetamine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts, unpleasant dreams\\n\\nAcute:\xa0Sympathetic\xa0overactivity, paranoid hallucinatory syndrome, violent behavior, psychosis and diaphoresis.\\n\\nChronic: Runs & crashes, choreiform movements, tooth decay. Can resemble schizophrenia.\\n\\nCocaine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts\\n\\nAcute: Sympathetic overactivity, agitation, euphoria, chest pain, tactile hallucination, seizures and mydriasis\\n\\nChronic: Cocaine psychosis (Magnan sign) and black pigmentation of the tongue.\\n\\nLSD/ Haluccinogen\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nSynesthesia, brisk tendon reflex, tachycardia, hypertension, panic, ataxia, visual hallucinations, euphoria and dysphoria.\\n\\nPhencyclidine (PCP)\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nTachycardia, hypertension, ataxia, hallucinations, violent behavior, dissociation, amnesia and nystagmus.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/b413d836c216c9551dc5.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/775bfe8a880544857d68.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-cd107b8fbb","n":328,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"A 19-year-old female patient presented to emergency unconscious. A non-contrast CT revealed hydrocephalus, requiring urgent neurosurgical intervention. The patient had nobody accompanying or any identity proof. What should the doctor do ?","options":["Do not operate without consent","Wait for arrival of relatives","Operate without consent","Take consent by calling the relatives"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Operate without consent\\n\\nAs the situation is an emergency, the person is unconscious and is not accompanied by anyone, the doctor can operate without consent.\\n\\nSection 30 BNS (IPC 92) deals with acts done in good faith without consent.\\n\\nThe following situations would apply :\\n\\n* Emergency (No need to take consent during emergencies)\\n\\n* The person is incapable of giving consent\\n\\n* The person has no guardian to give consent\\n\\nPearl PF1602: Consent\\n\\nIPC Section\\n\\nProvisions under the Act\\n\\n87\\n\\nAct not intended and not known to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt, done by consent is not an offense.\\n\\n88\\n\\nAct done by consent in good faith for person’s benefit is not an offense.\\n\\n89\\n\\nAct done in good faith for the benefit of a child under 12 years of age or an insane person, with the consent of a parent or guardian is not an offense.\\n\\n90\\n\\nConsent is invalid if\\n\\n• Given under fear or misconception.\\n• Given by an insane person\\n\\n92\\n\\nAct which is done in good faith without consent.\\n\\nThe following situations would apply :\\n\\n• Emergency (No need to take consent during emergencies)\\n• The person is incapable of giving consent\\n• The person has no guardian to give consent\\n\\n95\\n\\nAct causing slight harm is not an offence","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2bca6acd6b","n":329,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"A 40-year-old patient was brought by the police in an unconscious state to the casualty with a history of unknown poisoning. On examination, the patient had needle track marks over the upper limbs. He also had increased blood pressure, increased heart rate and hyperthermia. Which poisoning should be suspected here?","options":["Heroine","Cocaine","Cannabis","LSD"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cocaine\\n\\nThe history of poisoning with hypertension, tachycardia and hyperthermia is suggestive of cocaine poisoning.\\n\\nTrack marks are visible signs on the skin, typically scars or puncture wounds, resulting from repeated intravenous drug injections, commonly associated with substance abuse.\\n\\nCocaine is an alkaloid deliriant extracted from\xa0Erythroxylum coca.\\n\\nAcute intoxication with cocaine leads to symptoms spanning 2 stages-\\n\\n* Stage of excitement/euphoria - associated with hypervigilance, anxiety, hyperactivity along with hyper-adrenergic signs of mydriasis, tachycardia, elevated blood pressure, hyperthermia,\xa0and cardiac arrhythmias.\\n\\n* Stage of depression - associated with loss of reflexes, muscle paralysis, circulatory and respiratory failure, and coma.\\n\\nToxic delirium and tactile hallucinations—also referred to as \\"cocaine bugs\\" or formication—are hallmarks of chronic intoxication. The sensation of insects creeping under the skin is known as formication.\\n\\nCommon complications include\xa0cerebrovascular accidents\xa0and\xa0myocardial ischemia.\\n\\nTreatment\xa0includes intravenous diazepam for controlling seizures. For ventricular arrhythmias, propranolol may be given intravenously. If the patient has psychosis, haloperidol may be given. Treatment of chronic cocaine abuse requires psychiatric support.\\n\\nFollowing an abrupt cessation of long-term cocaine use, cocaine withdrawal occurs. The phases are as follows -\\n\\n* Crash phase (9 hours - 96 hours): Anorexia, depression, excessive craving, hypersomnia, fatigue.\\n\\n* Normal mood, anxiety, and anhedonia (4 - 7 days)\\n\\n* Extinction phase (7 - 10 days): Absence of withdrawal symptoms but increased susceptibility to relapse.\\n\\nTreatment for withdrawal\xa0includes bromocriptine and amantadine. They are useful in reducing cocaine craving.\\n\\nPearl PF1338: Substances of abuse\\n\\nSubstance\\n\\nWithdrawal symptoms\\n\\nIntoxication features\\n\\nAlcohol\\n\\nTremors, delirium, seizures\\n\\nBlackouts, nystagmus and slurring of speech\\n\\nBZD\\n\\nTremors,\xa0insomnia, anxiety\\n\\nLethargy and drowsiness.\\n\\nOpioid/ Heroin\\n\\nDiarrhea,\xa0yawning, mydriasis,\xa0lacrimation, piloerection, cold turkey phenomenon\\n\\nEuphoria, depressed mental status, miosis, respiratory depression, constipation.\\n\\nNicotine/ Tobacco\\n\\nIncreased appetite\\n\\nShows a biphasic pattern.\\n\\nEarly stimulatory symptoms of vomiting, hypertension and tachycardia.\xa0Later depressive symptoms of hypotension and bradycardia.\\n\\nCannabis\\n\\nIrritability, sweating, tremors, decreased appetite\\n\\nIncreased appetite, euphoria, conjunctival injection, dry mouth,\xa0panic,\xa0synesthesia, impaired time perception, amotivational\xa0syndrome, run amok and\xa0flashbacks.\\n\\nAmphetamine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts, unpleasant dreams\\n\\nAcute:\xa0Sympathetic\xa0overactivity, paranoid hallucinatory syndrome, violent behavior, psychosis and diaphoresis.\\n\\nChronic: Runs & crashes, choreiform movements, tooth decay. Can resemble schizophrenia.\\n\\nCocaine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts\\n\\nAcute: Sympathetic overactivity, agitation, euphoria, chest pain, seizures and mydriasis\\n\\nChronic: Cocaine psychosis (Magnan sign) and black pigmentation of the tongue.\\n\\nLSD/ Haluccinogen\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nSynesthesia, brisk tendon reflex, tachycardia, hypertension, panic, ataxia, visual hallucinations, euphoria and dysphoria.\\n\\nPhencyclidine (PCP)\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nTachycardia, hypertension, ataxia, hallucinations, violent behavior, dissociation, amnesia and nystagmus.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7dfd824261","n":330,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"A child after eating an unknown fruit ,has been brought to your emergency department with the following signs and symptoms - Dry mouth, irrelevant slurred speech, dry and hot skin, dilated pupils. On examination there was tachycardia and hyperthermia.What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":["Poppy seeds poisoning","Datura poisoning","Mushroom poisoning","Cannabis poisoning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Datura poisoning\\n\\nThe clinical syndrome in the question is that of Datura poisoning.\\n\\nDhatura:\\n\\nActive principle:\\n\\n* Scopolamine\\n\\n* Hyoscyamine\\n\\n* Trace amount of atropine\\n\\n* The active principle called “Dhaturine” on heating would yield atropine\\n\\n* Seeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked and produce delirium, hallucinations, and seizures along with other classic anticholinergic effects, such as mydriasis, tachycardia, dry mouth and skin, blurred vision, urinary retention, and hyperthermia.\\n\\nMode of Action:\\n\\n* Anticholinergic\\n\\nToxic manifestations of Datura:\\n\\n* Mnemonic: \\"hot as a hare, blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, and mad as a hatter\\"\\n\\n* Also, remember the D\'s of Datura\\n\\n* Dry hot skin (Temp rises upto 42 C, a scarlet rash may also be appreciated).\\n\\n* Dilatation of pupils along with loss of accommodation\\n\\n* Diplopia\\n\\n* Dryness of the mouth and throat, unquenchable thirst\\n\\n* Dysphagia\\n\\n* Dilatation of cutaneous blood vessels\\n\\n* Dysarthria (Difficulty in talking)\\n\\n* Drunken gait\\n\\n* Delirium\\n\\n* Drowsiness\\n\\n* Death due to respiratory or cardiac failure\\n\\nTreatment\\n\\n* Gastric emptying is often delayed, and the small, plentiful seeds can become trapped among the gastrointestinal folds after ingestion; thus gastric decontamination can be an important therapy.\\n\\n* Physostigmine in severe toxicity given 1-2 mg IM/IV after every half hour as required. It is a physiological antidote. Pilocarpine may also be given.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nIn India, datura is employed mainly as a stupefying poison prior to robbery, kidnapping and rape. It is sometimes known as rail-road poison, as it is commonly encountered during a journey. Many a time, robbers disguised as saints offer ‘prasad’ mixed with dhatura seeds and rob the passengers.\\n\\nPearl PF1117: Plant poisons\\n\\nDatura stramonium (Jimsonweed) : Flower and fruit are poisonous\\n\\nRicinus communis : Seed is poisonous\\n\\nDigitalis purpurea\\n\\nAtropa belladonna\\n\\nCalotropis gigantea\\n\\nPearl PF1569: Poisoning and its antidotes\\n\\nPoisoning\\nAntidotes\\n\\nOrganophosphate, carbamate & early mushroom\\nAtropine\\n\\nAtropine, belladonna & datura\\nPhysostigmine\\n\\nAcetaminophen\\nAcetyl cystine\\n\\nBenzodiazepine\\nFlumazenil\\n\\nOpioids\\nNaloxone\\n\\nAcute iron poisoning\\nDesferrioxamine\\n\\nChronic iron poisoning\\nDeferiprone\\n\\nCyanide poisoning\\n\\nHydroxocobalamin\\n\\nSodium thiosulphate\\n\\nAmyl nitrite\\n\\nSodium nitrite\\n\\nBeta-blocker poisoning\\nGlucagon","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/504b5aef5a00de351c41.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1ec74b374ef1cdd04ed0.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1de74a4c4a952ced57ff.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/58a03f47a52454763ff0.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f0bf974429167610f8a4.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/3b4c5057169384fcc406.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/e6d470cda4e32ccee210.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-bcadc14267","n":331,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"A man was found dead due to starvation. Which of the following findings will be seen on autopsy?","options":["Distended gall bladder","Enlarged liver","Expanded lungs","Hypertrophied heart"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Distended gall bladder\\n\\nIn starvation, the gall bladder is distended.\\n\\nIn starvation,\xa0bile does not get secreted into the duodenum due to the absence of food entering the stomach and intestine. So bile gets accumulated in the gall bladder and gets distended.\\n\\nStarvation:\\n\\n* Feeling of intense hunger lasts for 30–48 hours.\\n\\n* Person can survive for 10–12 days without food and water and for 6–8 weeks without food alone.\\n\\n* Loss of 40% of body weight is usually fatal.\\n\\n* Loss of 70–90% of body fat and 20% of body protein is fatal.\\n\\n* There is reduction in weight and size of all organs except brain.\\n\\nOther features of starvation:\\n\\n* Dry, lustreless hair\\n\\n* Brittle nails\\n\\n* Pale skin with edema around ankles\\n\\n* Loss of fat in subcutaneous tissue, in the omentum\\n(sparing of fat in female breast and orbit till later)\\n\\n* Atrophy of muscles and darkening due to lipochrome\\n\\n* The heart is atrophied with empty chambers\\n\\n* Pale and collapsed lung\\n\\n* Shrunken liver with necrosis\\n\\n* Tissue paper-like intestinal mucosa\\n\\n* Empty bladder","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-878e9d1406","n":332,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"A person who has committed a criminal offense was caught by the police and later brought to a criminal court. The court directs the police to transfer the case to juvenile courts if it is proved that the accused is ___years old.","options":["21","17","20","24"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 17\\n\\nThe trial of any juvenile (<18 years) of age is done as per the juvenile justice act.\\n\\n* According to the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2000,\xa0juvenile means a boy or girl who has not completed 18 years of age. They are tried in juvenile courts and not punished with death or imprisonment. They however may be sent to remand home, asked to pay fine\\n\\n* Recent update:\xa0Parliament of India passed the\xa0Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act - 2015\xa0which came into effect on 15th\xa0January 2016. It states that\xa0juveniles in conflict with the law in the age group of 16–18, involved in Heinous Offences, can be tried as adults.\\n\\n* Heinous offenses are those which are punishable with imprisonment of seven years or more if done by adults.\\n\\nPearl PF1600: Criminal Responsibilities\\n\\nBNS Section\\n\\nPrevious IPC Section\\n\\nDeals with\\n\\n18\\n\\n-\\n\\nA person is not liable for an accident in doing a lawful act\\n\\n20\\n\\n82\\n\\nChild under 7 years is not liable for act.\\n\\n21\\n\\n83\\n\\nChild of 7–12 years if not attained proper mental maturity is not liable for act.\\n\\n22\\n\\n84 \xa0-McNaughten\'s rule\\n\\nA person of unsound mind is not liable for act.\\n\\n23\\n\\n85\\n\\nA person who under involuntary intoxication has commited an act is not liable for the act.\\n\\n24\\n\\n86\\n\\nA person who under voluntary intoxication commits an act is liable for the act.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e8481cf1b4","n":333,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"Cherry red post mortem lividity is seen in:","options":["Opioid poisoning","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Hydrogen sulphide poisoning","Phosphorous poisoning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Carbon monoxide poisoning\\n\\nCherry red postmortem lividity is characteristic of Carbon monoxide poisoning.\\n\\nCarbon monoxide poisoning:\\n\\n* Cherry red discoloration of the skin, mucosa, post-mortem lividity (hypostasis), blood, and viscera, etc.\\n\\n* If the blood is taken in a test tube and diluted with water and held against a light or white background, the pink color will be better appreciated.\\n\\nPostmortem staining in poisonings:\\n\\nPoisoning\\nColor of lividity\\n\\n1. Carbon monoxide\\nCherry red\\n\\n2. Cyanide\\nBright red\\n\\n3. Phosphorous\\nDark brown\\n\\n4. Opium\\nBlack\\n\\n5. Hydrogen sulphide\\nBlue-green\\n\\n6.Nitrate,Nitrite,Aniline\\nDark brown\\n\\n7. Pottasium cyanide\\nDeep blue\\n\\n8. In clostridium perfringens infection\\nBronze\\n\\n9. Hypothermia\\nBright pink","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-badac65f61","n":334,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"Gutter fracture of skull bone is associated with impact due to ___.","options":["a sharp object","a blunt object","fall from height","a bullet"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. a bullet\\n\\nA\xa0gutter fracture of the skull is\xa0a furrow in the outer table of the skull, ordinarily the result of a glancing blow by a missile from a rifled firearm.\\n\\nGutter fractures refer to furrows in the outer table of the skull, as a result of a blow by a missile from a rifled firearm. These may be accompanied by comminuted depressed fractures of the inner table of the skull.\\n\\nPearl PF0368: Types of skull vault fracture\\n\\nDiastatic fracture -\xa0Separation of sutures due to blow with blunt weapon or from cerebral edema. It is also called sutural fracture.\\n\\nFissured fracture/Linear fracture:\\n\\n* Linear skull fractures are breaks in the bone that traverse the full thickness of the skull from the outer to inner table.\\n\\n* They are usually fairly straight with no bone displacement.\\n\\n* The common cause of injury is blunt force trauma where the impact energy transferred over a wide area of the skull.\\n\\nPond fracture - Occurs in children due to elasticity of bones. It is also known as indented fracture.\\n\\nDepressed fracture -\xa0Caused by a heavy weapon with a small striking surface. Its shape indicates the type of weapon which is produced. So it is also called\xa0\'signature fracture\'.\\n\\nComminuted fracture - It is also called spider web fracture and has a stellate appearance where there is no displacement of fracture.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/044521e4c9c24dba9aab.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/fcbd33c2a0f18189e010.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/bf333a72545ec5279936.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d5287acdc26c37577e57.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-66ddcc85ba","n":335,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"In Barberio\'s test ,yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals are seen under microscope. Which of the following is detected by this test?","options":["Semen","Blood","Sputum","CSF"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Semen\\n\\nBarberio\'s test\xa0helps in the identification of semen.\\n\\nBarberio\'s test detects the\xa0presence of\xa0spermine, which is one\xa0of the constituents of semen. A drop of the stain of spermatic fluid taken in a glass slide is charged with saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of\xa0picric acid. Under microscopic examination, the\xa0needle-shaped yellowish\xa0spermine picrate crystals\xa0indicate the presence of semen.\\n\\nPearl PF1864: Tests to detect seminal and blood stains\\n\\nTests for seminal stains\\n\\n* Barberio\'s test\\n\\n* Florence test\\n\\n* Acid phosphatase test\\n\\n* Creatine phosphokinase test\\n\\n* Immunological test to detect Prostate Specific Antigen\\n\\nTests for blood stains\\n\\n* Benzidine test\\n\\n* Phenophthalein (Kastle Meyer) test\\n\\n* Takayama hemochromogen crystal test\\n\\n* Teichmann’s hemin crystal test\\n\\n* Spectroscopic test","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2ced62717e","n":336,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"In judicial hanging , the cause of death is due to ________.","options":["vagal inhibition","cervical vertebral fracture dislocation","cerebral hypoxia","asphyxia"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. cervical vertebral fracture dislocation\\n\\nIn judicial hanging ,the cause of death is due to cervical vertebral fracture and\xa0rupture of brainstem between pons and medulla.\\n\\nJudicial hanging\\n\\n* Face of the person is covered with dark mask ,he is made to stand on a platform above trapdoors ,which open downwards when blot is drawn.\\n\\n* Rope is looped around the neck and person allowed to drop for 5-7 meters according to weight ,age and build of person.\\n\\n* Sudden stop causes fracture dislocation usually at level of C-2\xa0 ,C-3\xa0 OR C-3\xa0 C-4 vertebra and rupture of brainstem between pons and medulla.(hangman fracture).\\n\\n* Death is instantaneous but heart beat may continue for 15-20 minutes and spasmodic muscle jerking may occur for considerable time.\\n\\n* Asphyxia signs are not seen in properly performed judicial hangings.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-922509bb63","n":337,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"In which type of hanging does weight of the head, arms and chest act as a constricting force?","options":["Complete","Partial","Typical","Atypical"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Partial\\n\\nThe weight of the head, arms, and chest acts as a constricting force in partial hanging.\\n\\nTypes of hanging:\\n\\n* Complete hanging: Here the weight of the entire body acts as a constricting force.\\n\\n* Partial hanging: The bodies are partially suspended, the weight of the head (5-6 kgs), arms, and chest act as a constricting force.\\n\\n* Typical hanging: In this, the ligature runs from the midline above the thyroid cartilage symmetrically upward on both sides of the neck to the occipital region.\xa0A knot of the ligature is on the backside of the neck.\\n\\n* Atypical hanging: The knot of the ligature is anywhere other than on the backside of the neck.\xa0Usually, the knot is near the mastoid process or angle of mandible. Occasionally, it may be under the chin.\\n\\nIn India, the knot is tied under the angle of the right jaw. On drawing the bolt, the person drops to the length of the rope. The sudden stoppage of the moving body associated with the position of the knot causes the head to be jerked violently.\\n\\nJudicial hanging:\\n\\n* The face of the person is covered with a dark mask, stand on a platform above trapdoors,which open downwards when the blot is drawn.\\n\\n* Rope is looped around the neck and the person is allowed to drop for 5-7 meters according to weight, age and build.\\n\\n* Sudden stop causes fracture dislocation at the level C-2, C-3 or C-3/C-4 vertebra and rupture between pons and medulla (hangman fracture).\\n\\n* Death is instantaneous, but the heartbeat may continue for 15-20 minutes and spasmodic muscle jerking may occur for a considerable time.\\n\\n* Asphyxial signs are not seen in correctly performed judicial hangings.\\n\\nPearl PF1105: Difference between strangulation by Ligature and Hanging\\n\\nTrait\\n\\nHanging\\n\\nStrangulation by ligature\\n\\nNature of death\\n\\nMostly suicidal\\n\\nMostly homicidal\\n\\nFace\\n\\nUsually pale and petechiae rare\\n\\nCongested, livid and marked with petechiae\\n\\nTongue\\n\\nSwelling and protrusion is less marked\\n\\nSwelling and protrusion is more marked\\n\\nBleeding\\n\\nBleeding from nose, mouth and ears not common\\n\\nBleeding from nose, mouth and ears may be found\\n\\nSaliva\\n\\nOften dribbles out of the mouth\\n\\nSuch dribbling is very rare\\n\\nLigature mark\\n\\nOblique, non-continuous, placed high up in the neck between the chin and the larynx.\\n\\nThe subcutaneous tissue underneath the mark is usually hard, white and glistening\\n\\nHorizontal or transverse, continuous around the neck, low down in the neck below or across the thyroid.\\n\\nSubcutaneous tissue underneath may be ecchymosed\\n\\nAbrasions and\\n\\necchymoses\\n\\nRare\\n\\nCommon\\n\\nInjury to muscles of\\n\\nneck\\n\\nRare\\n\\nCommon\\n\\nCarotid arteries\\n\\nInternal coats usually ruptured in violent cases of a long drops(Amussat\'s sign)\\n\\nDamage to internal coat is rare\\n\\nHyoid bone\\n\\nFracture of the hyoid bone less common\\n\\nFracture of the hyoid bone is uncommon (common in manual strangulation)\\n\\nThyroid cartilage\\n\\nFracture is less common\\n\\nFracture is more common\\n\\nCervical vertebrae\\n\\nFracture and/or dislocation common in judicial hanging\\n\\nFracture and/or dislocation—rare\\n\\nPearl PF2130: Difference between Ante mortem and Post-mortem Hanging\\n\\nCharacteristics\\nAntemortem\\nPost-mortem\\n\\nSalivary dribbling mark at the angle\\nof mouth, the opposite side of the knot\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nFecal/ urinary discharge\\nNot very common\\n-ve\\n\\nLigature mark\\n\\nDirection\\nOblique\\nCircular\\n\\nContinuous/not\\nNoncontinuous\\nContinuous\\n\\nLevel in the neck\\nHigh up above the level\\nthyroid cartilage\\nLow, below\\nthe level of the thyroid cartilage\\n\\nParchmentisation\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nVItal reaction\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nKnot\\nSingle, simple, on\\none of the side of neck/ rarely on\\nchin the or occiput\\nMore than one, granny or reef\\ntype on occiput/chin\\n\\nTongue may or may not be\\nprotruded out, bitten at the tip\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nInjuries\xa0Imprint abrasion/ Bruise\\nAlways +ve and with vital\xa0reaction\\n+ ve/ -ve and if +ve no vital reaction\\n\\nElongation of neck\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nPM staining\\n\\nAbove ligature mark\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nIn lower limbs\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n\\nGlove-stocking like distribution\\n+ve\\nIndependent parts of body\\nat the time of death\\nand position at death\\n\\nLung Surface\\nEmphysematous bulla\\n- ve\\n+ve\\n\\nEvidence of Injury\\n\\nSelf-infliction\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nStruggle evidence\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nTear of carotid artery intima\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nFracture of hyoid\\n\\n+ve\\n\\n+ve\\n\\nCyanosis\\n\\nDeeply+ ve\\n\\n- ve/ faintly +ve\\n\\nPoint of suspension\\n\\nCompatible with antemortem\\nself-suspension\\n\\nNot so","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/eb2cb98e58492d8bda2b.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-2d2a5ceda1","n":338,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"A 28-year-old male is brought to the emergency. On examination, his PR is 50 bpm, BP is 80/60 mmHg, RR is 7 breaths/min and his temperature is 95 F. He has pin point pupils and is disoriented. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":["Benzodiazepine overdose","Cocaine intoxication","Opioid withdrawal","Opioid intoxication"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Opioid intoxication\\n\\nOpioid intoxication is the most probable diagnosis in the above given clinical scenario. Opioids include morphine, heroin, codeine, etc.\\n\\nSymptoms of opioid overdose:\\n\\n* Triad of coma, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils(miosis)\\n\\n* Hypotension\\n\\n* Bradycardia\\n\\n* Shallow breathing\\n\\n* Hypothermia\\n\\nCharacteristics of opioid toxicity:\\n\\n* Opioid intoxication toxidrome -\xa0respiratory and mental status depression, analgesia, miosis, orthostatic hypotension, nausea and vomiting, histamine release resulting in localized urticaria and bronchospasm, ileus secondary to decreased GI motility, and urinary retention secondary to increased vesical sphincter tone.\\n\\n* Miosis is not universally present from intoxication with every opioid; normal or even enlarged pupils have been documented secondary to diphenoxylate, meperidine, morphine, pentazocine, and propoxyphene toxicity.\\n\\n* Opioid-induced acute lung injury, previously known as non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema, is an uncommon complication associated with heroin overdose.\\n\\nOpioid receptor\\nActions\\n\\nmu\\n\\nAnalgesia\\n\\nSedation\\n\\nMiosis\\n\\nRespiratory depression\\n\\nCough suppression\\n\\nEuphoria\\n\\nDecreased GI motility\\n\\nkappa\\n\\nAnalgesia\\n\\nSedation\\n\\nMiosis\\n\\nDecreased GI motility\\n\\nDysphoria\\n\\nHallucinations\\n\\ndelta\\n\\nAnalgesia\\n\\nAntidepressant effect\\n\\nAirway protection and ventilatory maintenance are the most important treatment steps for opioid intoxications, because respiratory depression is the major morbidity and the cause of essentially all the mortality.\\n\\n* After adequate ventilation is ensured, administer naloxone.\\n\\n* Single-dose activated charcoal can be administered in case opioid ingestion occured within one hour.\\n\\nOpioid\xa0withdrawal symptoms (mnemonic- O MY WITHDRAWALS):\\n\\n* Mydriasis\\n\\n* Yawning\\n\\n* Weakness\\n\\n* Insomnia, irritability\\n\\n* Tremors, tachycardia\\n\\n* Hyperthermia, hypertension\\n\\n* Diarrhea\\n\\n* Rhinorrhoea\\n\\n* Apnoeic attacks\\n\\n* Weight loss\\n\\n* Anxiety\\n\\n* Lacrimation\\n\\n* Sweating\\n\\nPearl PF1338: Substances of abuse\\n\\nSubstance\\n\\nWithdrawal symptoms\\n\\nIntoxication features\\n\\nAlcohol\\n\\nTremors, delirium, seizures\\n\\nBlackouts, nystagmus and slurring of speech\\n\\nBZD\\n\\nTremors,\xa0insomnia, anxiety\\n\\nLethargy and drowsiness.\\n\\nOpioid/ Heroin\\n\\nDiarrhea,\xa0yawning, mydriasis,\xa0lacrimation, piloerection, cold turkey phenomenon\\n\\nEuphoria, depressed mental status, miosis, respiratory depression, constipation.\\n\\nNicotine/ Tobacco\\n\\nIncreased appetite\\n\\nShows a biphasic pattern.\\n\\nEarly stimulatory symptoms of vomiting, hypertension and tachycardia.\xa0Later depressive symptoms of hypotension and bradycardia.\\n\\nCannabis\\n\\nIrritability, sweating, tremors, decreased appetite\\n\\nIncreased appetite, euphoria, conjunctival injection, dry mouth,\xa0panic,\xa0synesthesia, impaired time perception, amotivational\xa0syndrome, run amok and\xa0flashbacks.\\n\\nAmphetamine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts, unpleasant dreams\\n\\nAcute:\xa0Sympathetic\xa0overactivity, paranoid hallucinatory syndrome, violent behavior, psychosis and diaphoresis.\\n\\nChronic: Runs & crashes, choreiform movements, tooth decay. Can resemble schizophrenia.\\n\\nCocaine\\n\\nDepressed mood, suicidal thoughts\\n\\nAcute: Sympathetic overactivity, agitation, euphoria, chest pain, seizures and mydriasis\\n\\nChronic: Cocaine psychosis (Magnan sign) and black pigmentation of the tongue.\\n\\nLSD/ Haluccinogen\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nSynesthesia, brisk tendon reflex, tachycardia, hypertension, panic, ataxia, visual hallucinations, euphoria and dysphoria.\\n\\nPhencyclidine (PCP)\\n\\nNothing significant\\n\\nTachycardia, hypertension, ataxia, hallucinations, violent behavior, dissociation, amnesia and nystagmus.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f101554be4","n":339,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"A farmer is brought to the hospital 8 hours later, following a snake bite. He complained of headache, blurred vision, tingling sensation around the mouth, dizziness, vertigo, and multiple episodes of vomiting since the bite. He has signs of respiratory depression and a pulse 50. The local wound showed mild swelling and blistering. There are no apparent bleeding manifestations. He was administered 10 vials of antivenom. Which of the following is the next step of management?","options":["Check the blood coagulation profile","Neostigmine with atropine","Epinephrine injection","I.V hydrocortisone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Neostigmine with atropine\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario\xa0is suggestive of a neurotoxic\xa0snake bite.\xa0Since this patient has already passed 8 hours without any bleeding manifestations, the bite of a hemotoxic snake is unlikely and the better management would be to address his neurotoxic symptoms with the\xa0administration of neostigmine with atropine.\\n\\nThe\xa0neurotoxic symptoms\xa0are\xa0reversible with\xa0the use of\xa0antivenoms\xa0or\xa0anticholinesterase drugs, such as neostigmine. Atropine is administered to counter the muscarinic side effects of neostigmine.\\n\\nNeurotoxic symptoms include:\\n\\n* Headache\\n\\n* Blurred vision\\n\\n* Perioral paraesthesia\xa0(tingling sensation around the mouth)\\n\\n* Vomiting\\n\\n* Dizziness\\n\\n* Vertigo\\n\\n* Hyperacusis\\n\\n* Gooseflesh\\n\\n* Hypersalivation\\n\\nRespiratory failure\xa0is precipitated either by airway obstruction or paralysis of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles,\xa0at this stage or later. If it progresses to paralysis, it initially presents with\xa0ptosis and external ophthalmoplegia,\xa0which may appear between a few minutes after the bite or after some hours.\\n\\nASV(Anti Snake Venom) is the\xa0mainstay\xa0of snakebite treatment. Neurotoxicity effects may wear off spontaneously in about a week and are\xa0completely reversible\xa0in response to antivenom or anticholinesterase. Atropine can be used in the management of venom that acts postsynaptically. Ventilatory support is provided in cases complicated with severe respiratory depression, in the event of failure of other measures of management.\\n\\nASV used in India is effective against:\\n\\n* Cobra\\n\\n* Common krait\\n\\n* Russel viper\\n\\n* Saw scaled viper\\n\\nMeasures that\xa0can be done\xa0in the case of snakebite:\\n\\n* Apply firm pressure over the bitten area, which delays the absorption of venom\\n\\n* Pressure immobilization is recommended for elapid and sea-snake bites, but not for viper bites as it may cause local necrosis\\n\\n* Clean the wound with soap and water, or iodine and cover it with a sterile dressing\\n\\n* Immediately apply a broad firm bandage (Sutherland wrap) on the bitten area and around the limb. As much of the limb should be bandaged as possible. It should be tight enough to occlude the superficial venous and lymphatic return, but not the arterial or deep venous flow.\\n\\n* In bites on the trunk, head, or neck, apply firm pressure over the bitten area\\n\\n* Immobilize the limb, as movement can accelerate the spread of venom\\n\\n* Make the patient lie on one side in the recovery position so that the airway is clear, in case of vomiting or fainting\\n\\nMeasures\xa0contraindicated\xa0in the case of\xa0snakebite:\\n\\n* Suction\xa0and\xa0incision\xa0of the wound\\n\\n* Electric shock treatment of wound site\\n\\n* Application of tourniquets\xa0because they obstruct arterial flow and cause ischemia\\n\\n* Ice water immersion\xa0of the bitten limb","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bfea725248","n":340,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"A man regularly derives sexual gratification by inflicting pain upon his partner. He would often produce cuts and cigarette burns on his partner\'s arms. This is best described as?","options":["Voyeurism","Fetishism","Sadism","Masochism"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sadism\\n\\nThe given scenario is suggestive of\xa0sexual sadism.\xa0It is seen more commonly in men. It develops due to early experiences of brutality in sex.\\n\\nThe sadist may bite, beat, whip, produce cuts or cigarette burns etc on their sexual partner. In extreme cases, the victim\'s nipples may be bitten off, items such as a bottle or sticks are inserted into the vagina. Blunt trauma causing fractures and rupture of internal organs may also be seen.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A - Voyeurism:\xa0it is the act of obtaining sexual gratification by observing an unsuspecting person who is naked, disrobing or engaging in sexual activity.\\n\\nOption B - Fetishism:\xa0it is a form of sexual behaviour in which gratification is associated with an abnormal stimulus which may be an object, activity or part of the body.\\n\\nOption D - Masochism:\xa0this is opposite to sadism. Sexual gratification is obtained by suffering the pain.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fcf19e4f5f","n":341,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"During the examination of a victim of assault, you notice a wound with ragged, irregular, and uneven margins with tears diverging at an angle from the end of the main wound and typical ‘swallow tails’. Bleeding from this wound is not significant and shows crushed blood vessels and hair bulbs. You would document this wound as a/an ___________________","options":["Incised wound","Chopped wound","Stab wound","Lacerated wound"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Lacerated wound\\n\\nThe characteristics described are consistent with that of a lacerated wound\\n\\nThe characteristics of a lacerated wound:\\n\\nThe margins are ragged, irregular, and uneven. Tearing at the ends of a laceration, at angles diverging from the main one itself are called\xa0swallow tails.\\n\\nThe edges of the laceration may indicate a direction, in which the blow or force was applied.\xa0Depth of the wound shows bridges of irregularly torn fibrous tissue, blood vessels, and nerves and can be soiled by mud, sand, glass, brick particles, vegetation, machine oils, hair, or fibers. These materials embedded in the wound are of great medicolegal value.\\n\\nLacerated wounds do not bleed much as the vessels are crushed and torn, thus facilitating hemostasis. The exception to this is injuries to the scalp, where temporal arteries may bleed freely as they are firmly bound and unable to contract.\xa0Lacerations over the hairy areas will show hair bulbs crushed and buried into the depth of the wound. Because of the crushing and tearing nature of the production of lacerations, there can be associated abrasion or bruising.\\n\\nSwallow tail in a Lacerated wound\\n\\nFishtails -\xa0These may be seen in stab wounds where the skin splits at the corner.\\n\\nChop wounds: called because of the action in which they are produced. Depending on the type of instrument, they may resemble an incised or a lacerated wound. The shape of the wound may give clues to the type of weapon. For example, in the given image, the wound resembles an injury produced by a sharp axe. Such wounds may be inflicted with homicidal intent on the skull/scalp. Chop wounds are usually deep gaping wounds caused by a blow with the sharp cutting edge of a fairly heavy weapon like an axe, sword, hatchet etc","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/79bd8a889f0a52ac6dc7.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/ce473dcd6ce2555bfb81.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a4b529ff93b0e11e6a85.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-a7819acf55","n":342,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Till when can you perform MTP on the grounds of contraceptive failure?","options":["12 weeks","24 weeks","20 weeks","After 24 weeks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 20 weeks\\n\\nAs per the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Bill 2020, the induction of MTP on grounds of contraceptive failure can be done only until until 20 weeks.\\n\\nThe MTP Act of 2021 specifies special circumstances for termination of pregnancy beyond 20 weeks till 24 weeks of gestation. These include:\\n\\n* Survivor of sexual assault, rape or incest.\\n\\n* Minors\\n\\n* Mentally ill women including mental retardation\\n\\n* Change of marital status during the ongoing pregnancy (widowhood and divorce)\\n\\n* Women with physical disabilities (Major disability criteria - Rights of persons with disabilities act - 2016)\\n\\n* Women with pregnancy in humanitarian settings or disaster or emergency situations as declared by the government.\\n\\n* Fetal malformations that have a substantial risk of being incompatible with life or if the child is born, may suffer from serious physical or mental\\n\\n* abnormalities to be seriously handicapped. (Option A)\\n\\nTermination of pregnancy can be done only up to 20 weeks based on grounds of contraception failure.\\n\\n* Until 20 weeks of gestation - Opinion of one doctor\\n\\n* 20-24 weeks of gestation - Opinion of two doctors\\n\\n* The name and particulars of the woman whose pregnancy has been terminated cannot be disclosed. It can only be revealed to a person in force authorised by law. The ground for failure of contraception is extended to women and their partners.\\n\\nPearl PF1638: Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act - MTP Act 1971\\n\\nMTP Act with 2021 amendments\\n\\nIndications:\\n\\n* Therapeutic - to save the life of the mother\\n\\n* Eugenic - to prevent the\xa0risk of the child being born with serious mental or physical abnormality i.e. first-trimester viral infections, teratogenic drug intake, radiation exposure\\n\\n* Humanitarian -\xa0pregnancy resulted from rape or incest\\n\\n* Social -\xa0pregnancy resulted from failure of contraceptives\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n* Only a registered medical practitioner who is qualified (one who has assisted in the performance of 25 cases of MTP in a recognized hospital) can terminate a pregnancy.\\n\\n* MTP should be carried out in a government or government-recognized hospital.\xa0Private hospitals may take up MTP if they obtain a license from the chief medical officer of the district.\\n\\n* The termination of pregnancy by a person, who is not a registered medical practitioner (the person concerned), or in an unrecognised hospital (administrative head) shall be punished with rigorous imprisonment for a term not less than 2 years, but which may extend to 7 years.\\n\\nConsent:\\n\\nThe consent of the woman is required for conducting an abortion. The written consent of the guardian is required if the woman is minor or mentally ill. Consent of the husband or partner is not necessary.\\n\\nIf the woman is not willing, abortion cannot be performed at the request of the husband alone. The woman need not produce proof of her age.\xa0It is enough for the woman to say that she was raped, and it is not necessary that a complaint was lodged with the police.\\n\\nOpinion of doctors:\\n\\n* Till 20 weeks - 1 doctor\'s opinion\\n\\n* 20 to 24 weeks - 2 doctors\' opinion\\n\\n* For substantial fetal anomalies - approval from the medical board consisting of obstetrician, paediatrician, radiologist and a person appointed by the state\\n\\nUpper limit of MTP:\\n\\n* Up to 24 weeks of gestation in victims of rape and incest, fetal anomalies, or congenital malformations\\n\\n* Up to 20 weeks in case of contraception failure (any female with her partner)\\n\\n* At any gestational age for substantial fetal anomalies decided by the medical board\\n\\nConfidentiality:\\n\\nA register for recording the details of MTP must be kept for at least 5 years. The patient\'s name must not be entered, only the reference number is used.\xa0Unlawful disclosure of patient\'s details is punishable with 1-year imprisonment\xa0\xb1 fine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b4850395bd","n":343,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following conditions gives the haematological picture as shown below?","options":["Minamata disease","Saturnism","Chronic iron toxicity","Arsenic poisoning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Saturnism\\n\\nThe given haematological picture showing basophilic stippling with Cabot\'s ring is suggestive of chronic lead poisoning\xa0also called as plumbism or saturnism.\\n\\nClinical features of lead poisoning include\\n\\n* A - Anemia with basophilic stippling.\\n\\n* B - Burtonian line (blue stippled line on gums).\\n\\n* C - Constipation & Abdominal Colic\\n\\n* D - wrist Drop & foot Drop\\n\\n* E - Encephalopathy\\n\\n* F - Facial pallor -earliest sign.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption A : Minamata disease is chronic mercury poisoning can show anemia secondary to GI hemorrhage.\\n\\nOption C : Chronic iron toxicity, can present with leucocytosis and anemia (due to blood loss).\\n\\nOption D : Arsenic poisoning can induce microcytic hypochromic anemia or hemolytic anemia.\\n\\nPearl PF1115: Lead Poisoning/Plumbism/Saturnism\\n\\nFeatures\\nLead\\n\\nSource of exposure\\n\\nOccupational exposure\\n\\nLead paint\\n\\nRoute of absorption\\n\\nChildren - GI tract\\n\\nAdults - Respiratory tract\\n\\nMechanism of action\\n\\nbinds to sulfhydryl groups and inhibits\\n\\n• ALA dehydratase\\n• ferrochelatase\\n\\ninhibits pyrimidine 5\' nucleotidase\\n\\ncompetes with calcium\\n\\nOrgans affected (acute toxicity)\\n\\nGI tract\\n\\nBone marrow\\n\\nPeripheral nerves\\n\\nOrgans affected (chronic toxicity)\\n\\nCNS\\n\\nRenal\\n\\nCharacteristic clinical findings\\n\\nA - Anemia with basophilic stippling\\n\\nB - Burtonian line (blue stippled line on gums)\\n\\nC - Constipation and colic (abdominal pain)\\n\\nD - Drop (wrist and foot drop) due to neuropathy\\n\\nE - Encephalopathy-\xa0Mostly seen in infants presenting with severe ataxia, vomiting, lethargy, stupor, convulsions, and coma\\n\\nF - Facial pallor (Earliest sign)\\n\\nDiagnosis\\nBlood lead levels\\n\\nTreatment\\nCalcium EDTA; succimer(DMSA)","img":false,"images":["/bimg/240a74ed1efb311287a7.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-1d1d73dbb3","n":344,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following features aids in the differentiation of a true bruise from an artificial bruise?","options":["Positive chemical tests","Progression of the color of bruise over time","Regular and well-defined margins","Presence of vesicles"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Progression of the color of bruise over time\\n\\nOne of the features of differentiation of a true bruise from an artificial bruise is the progression of the color of the bruise over time.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A, C, and D: Positive chemical tests, regular and well-defined margins, and the presence of vesicles are features of an artificial bruise.\\n\\nArtificial bruises are caused by the application of certain irritant substances such as the juice of marking nut, calotropis, or plumbago rosea to simulate true bruises.\\n\\nThe following features can be used to distinguish between the two categories.\\n\\nTrait\\nArtificial bruise\\nTrue bruise\\n\\nSite\\nAccessible parts\\nAnywhere\\n\\nColor\\nDark brown\\nTypical color progression over time\\n\\nShape\\nIrregular\\nRounded\\n\\nMargins\\nWell defined and regular\\nIll-defined and irregular\\n\\nVesicles\\nPresent\\nAbsent\\n\\nRedness/ Inflammation\\nPresent in the surrounding skin\\nPresent over bruise\\n\\nItching\\nPresent\\nAbsent\\n\\nChemical tests\\nPositive\\nNegative","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a7681111071e759c8f17.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-fa6e651025","n":345,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following statements does not apply during cross-examination in a court of law?","options":["Witness is examined by the defense lawyer","Leading questions are not allowed","Objective of cross-examination is to weaken case of opponent","There is no time limit to cross examination"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Leading questions are not allowed\\n\\nLeading questions are allowed\xa0in a\xa0cross-examination\xa0according to\xa0Section 146 (4) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA). However, they are not allowed during the direct examination conducted by the summoning counsel.\\n\\nIf the judge is satisfied that the witness is\xa0hostile, leading questions may be allowed during direct examination.\\n\\nLeading questions (Section 146(1) of BSA)\xa0are defined as questions that suggest an answer that is favorable for the case of the summoning counsel or in alignment with their claim.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions 1, 3, 4 -\xa0In cross-examination, the witness is\xa0examined by the defense lawyer\xa0or adverse party with an\xa0objective\xa0to elicit favorable facts to their case, to test the accuracy of the witness statements, to unearth new or old facts, and also to discredit the witness.\\n\\nWhile leading questions are allowed in cross-examination, the Judge can disallow indecent questions (Section 154 of BSA) or questions asked with an intention to insult or offend the witness in any form (Section 155 of BSA).\\n\\nThere is\xa0no time limit\xa0to the duration of cross-examination.\\n\\nThe sequence of examination in the recording of evidence is as follows -\\n\\nSequence of examination\\nObjective\\n\\n• Examination-in-Chief/Direct examination by the party that summoned the witness\\nTo elicit the facts of the case from the witness. Leading questions are not allowed.\\n\\n• Cross-examination by the defense lawyer\\nTo elicit facts favorable to their case and test the reliability of the witness. Leading questions are allowed.\\n\\n• Re-examination by the party that summoned the witness\\nTo resolve doubts that formed during cross-examination and re-emphasize the party\'s perspective","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8b6376628d","n":346,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following tests is used to differentiate human blood from the blood of other species?","options":["Teichmann test","Precipitin test","Barberio test","Takayama test"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Precipitin test\\n\\nThe test used to differentiate human blood from the blood of other species is the precipitin test.\xa0Specific antiserum is made to react with the seminal fluid, blood or saliva. Precipitin test\xa0is used for\xa0species\xa0identification of semen, blood, or saliva, and a positive test confirms human origin. The precipitin test identifies the presence of proteins that are found only in human blood.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nTeichmann test and Takayama test (Options A and D) are used to confirm the presence of blood but cannot be used for species identification of blood.\\n\\nBarberio test (Option C) is used for the detection of spermatic fluid.\\n\\nPearl PF0395: Tests to detect seminal and blood stains\\n\\nTests for seminal stains\\n\\n* Barberio\'s test\\n\\n* Florence test\\n\\n* Acid phosphatase test\\n\\n* Creatine phosphokinase test\\n\\n* Immunological test to detect Prostate Specific Antigen\\n\\nTests for blood stains\\n\\n* Benzidine test\\n\\n* Phenophthalein (Kastle Meyer) test\\n\\n* Takayama hemochromogen crystal test\\n\\n* Teichmann’s hemin crystal test\\n\\n* Spectroscopic test","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ea52b6ad8a","n":347,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"You are working as an internal medicine consultant at a private clinic. A patient with chronic renal disease requiring hemodialysis is admitted under you. You advise diet and fluid control and keep him under observation. However, the patient progresses to take ayurvedic supplements and sometimes skips your instructions without disclosing it. His conditions worsen and he develops difficulty in breathing. His attendant files a complaint in court alleging the negligence of the doctor. Which of the following acts as a defence for the doctor in court?","options":["Vicarious liability","Res ipsa loquitur","Therapeutic misadventure","Contributory negligence"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Contributory negligence\\n\\nIn the given case, since the patient did not adhere to the proper instructions of the doctor, it was partly or wholly his fault. This is termed contributory negligence. Proper documentation as to how ordinary care was not exercised on part of the patient can be useful in a court of law.\\n\\nContributory negligence\xa0occurs when a\xa0patient\xa0or his\xa0personal attendant\xa0shows unreasonable conduct or absence of ordinary care, which when combined with the negligence of the doctor, has contributed to the injury. In this case, the patient’s or attendant’s negligence should be the direct, proximate cause and without which the injury would not have occurred\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Vicarious liability is not a defence against professional negligence. Vicarious liability (liability for the actions\xa0of another): An employer is responsible not only\xa0for his own negligence but also for the negligence\xa0of his employees.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Res ipsa loquitur means \'the thing speaks for itself\'. It is used as supportive evidence of negligence on part of the doctor eg: leaving behind a scalpel or scissor whilst operating leading to sepsis/peritonitis speaks for itself.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Therapeutic misadventure may serve as a defence against negligence. It basically refers to an adverse outcome that could not have been anticipated despite reasonable care. eg: a rare side effect of a drug or blood loss due to an anomalous artery despite exercising caution.\\n\\nPearl PF1453: Contributory negligence\\n\\nContributory negligence occurs when a patient or his personal attendant shows unreasonable conduct or absence of ordinary care, which when combined with the negligence of the doctor, has contributed to the injury. In this case, the patient’s or attendant’s negligence should be the direct, proximate cause and without which the injury would not have occurred.\\n\\nSo, contributory negligence occurs when both the doctor and patient are negligent. The direct or proximate cause of the injury should be the patient’s negligence, which means that if the patient was not negligent, the injury could have been prevented.\\n\\nContributory negligence usually occurs when the doctor is negligent and the patient:\\n\\n* Fails to give an accurate medical history, and as a result, the doctor may misdiagnose or mistreat the patient.\\n\\n* Fails to follow the doctor’s instructions\\n\\n* Refuses to take the treatment\\n\\n* Leaves the hospital against doctor’s advice\\n\\n* Does not inform the doctor or seek further medical assistance if the symptoms persist or worsens.\\n\\nContributory negligence is a defence for doctor’s negligence in civil cases. However, from case to case, the liability of a doctor may vary from complete non-liability to substantial liability for the damages. Under the doctrine of comparative negligence, the court has the right to hold both parties liable for their negligent actions.\\n\\nLimitations / Exceptions to contributory negligence:\\n\\nA. The doctor will be held guilty in the following conditions:\\n\\n1. The doctor failed to provide proper instructions to the patient\\n\\n* Ex: Doctor applies a tight plaster cast (doctor is negligent) to the patient’s leg and did not tell the patient to inform if he develops any numbness (no proper instruction). The patient develops numbness and has not informed the doctor (patient is negligent). After few days patient developed a permanent injury.\\n\\n2.\xa0Last clear chance doctrine: It is applicable when the doctor fails to take necessary action to prevent damage due to a negligent act of the patient, even after getting a clear chance to take such action.\\n\\n* Ex: Doctor applies a tight plaster cast (doctor is negligent) to the patient’s leg and told the patient to inform if he develops any numbness (gave proper instruction). The patient developed numbness and has not informed the doctor (patient is negligent). Next day patient informed the doctor, but the doctor did not remove the cast and said he was busy (There was a clear last chance, but the doctor neglected again). After few days patient developed a permanent injury.\\n\\nB. The doctor/person will not be held guilty in the following conditions:\\n\\n1.\xa0Avoidable consequences rule: It occurs when an injury is caused due to doctor’s negligence, and the patient has done a negligent act, which further aggravated the injury caused by the doctor, and the patient could have avoided the consequence by not doing such act. In such cases, the doctor need not pay anything, and the patient will be held guilty.\\n\\n* Ex: A patient had a lacerated wound. The doctor examined the patient and had not done any dressing or provided any antibiotics (the doctor is negligent). However, the doctor instructed the patient to avoid contamination and asked him to visit the clinic next week. When the patient went home, he applied cow dung on his wound every day thinking that the wound would heal faster and didn\'t visit the doctor after one week. The wound got infected and developed pus and aggravated the injury.\\n\\n* Here, despite the doctor being negligent in the first instance, the patient is responsible for his injury as he performed an unnecessary, avoidable act, which aggravated the injury. The doctor need not pay anything.\\n\\n2.\xa0Good Samaritan doctrine: A person who assists another person who is in serious danger cannot be charged with contributory negligence. unless such assistance is reckless or rash.\\n\\n* Ex: A person was travelling on a road and saw a car accident, where the victim was unconscious and was stuck inside the car. He called the ambulance. The paramedical staff instructed him not to mobilize the victim until they arrived. Meanwhile, the car was caught in fire (serious danger), and the person tried to remove the victim carefully out of the car (assisted the victim and was not reckless). However, the victim already had a cervical bone fracture due to the accident, which got mobilized while he was being removed out of the car and as a result, suffered an injury to the spine that led to paralysis (victim’s injury was aggravated). In such a case, the good samaritan doctrine would apply and the person who tried to help, will not be charged under contributory negligence.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-cda7e4ead8","n":348,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"A 10-year-old male child came to the casualty with difficulty in walking and pain in the perianal region. On subjecting the specimen from the perianal region to a test, yellow needle-shaped crystals are seen under the microscope. What is the test done?","options":["Barberio’s test","Florence test","Teichmann test","Acid phosphatase test"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Barberio’s test\\n\\nBarberio’s test is a micro chemical test for detection of semen, based on identification of spermine crystals.\\n\\n• Reagent: Saturated picric acid\\n• Positive result: Yellow, needle-shaped crystals (spermine picrate)\\n• Indicates presence of seminal fluid\\n\\nClinical/forensic relevance:\\n\\n• Used in sexual assault cases, including perianal/anal specimens\\n• Helps confirm presence of semen\\n\\nDifferentiation from other tests:\\n\\n• Florence test: Brown rhombic crystals (choline)\\n• Acid phosphatase test: Screening test for semen (enzyme-based)\\n• Teichmann test: Confirms blood (hemin crystals)","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-544e7f0e71","n":349,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"A child was born after 8 months of the father’s death. Grandparents filed a case that the baby might not be their son’s, but DNA came positive. What is such a child called?","options":["Suppositious child","Fictitious child","Posthumous child","Illegitimate child"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Posthumous child\\n\\nA posthumous child is one who is born after the death of the father.\\n\\n• The child is conceived before the father’s death but delivered later\\n• Such a child is considered legitimate if paternity is established (e.g., DNA test)\\n• Has legal rights of inheritance from the deceased father\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Suppositious child: A falsely substituted child (not biologically related)\\n• Fictitious child: Imaginary/non-existent\\n• Illegitimate child: Born outside lawful marriage (not applicable here)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8929d27857","n":350,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"A man was brought in by the police, accused of sexual assault. The police claim that he is 19 years old, whereas his school certificate mentions 17 years, with signs of overwriting. Which of the following X-rays would be the best investigation for his age determination?","options":["Medial end of clavicle, elbow, and sacrum","Sternum and skull","Sternum, skull, and sacrum","Medial end of clavicle, wrist, and pelvis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Medial end of clavicle, wrist, and pelvis\\n\\nWhen age is disputed (around 17–19 years), radiological age estimation using multiple ossification centers is the most reliable approach.\\n\\nUse a combination of X-rays of different skeletal sites to assess epiphyseal fusion and narrow the age range.\\n\\nBest combination (as in answer):\\n\\n•\\n\\nMedial end of clavicle + Wrist + Pelvis\\n\\nKey fusion points\\n\\n• Medial end of clavicle\\n\\n• Appears: 18–19 yrs\\n• Fuses: 20–21 yrs\\n• Most important for ≥18 yrs\\n\\n• Wrist (distal radius & ulna)\\n\\n• Fusion: 16–19 yrs\\n• Helps confirm near 19 yrs\\n\\n• Pelvis (iliac crest, ischial tuberosity)\\n\\n• Fusion: 20–21 yrs\\n• If not fused → <20 yrs\\n\\nNot useful for 18 yrs\\n\\n• Elbow → fused by \\\\~16 yrs\\n• Shoulder → fused by 17–18 yrs\\n• Skull sutures → fuse late (40+ yrs)\\n• Sternum → fuse 25–60 yrs\\n\\nFinal takeaway: ESHA ki Wrist (watch the FMT video if you dont what that means :)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d9830d3a1c","n":351,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"A woman brings her daughter alleging a history of sexual assault. The mother claims the girl is under 18, but the doctor suspects otherwise. Which X-ray investigation can assist in determining if the girl is over 18 years based on epiphyseal fusion?","options":["Elbow and shoulder joint","Medial end of clavicle, sternum","Medial end of clavicle, wrist and pelvis","Skull, sternum, pelvis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Medial end of clavicle, wrist and pelvis\\n\\nAge determination around 18 years relies on assessing late-fusing epiphyses.\\n\\nThe medial end of clavicle is the most reliable indicator at this threshold (secondary center appears \\\\~18 years, fuses \\\\~21 years).\\n\\nWrist (radius/ulna) and pelvic epiphyses (iliac crest, ischial tuberosity, femoral head) provide supportive evidence, as their fusion spans 17–21 years.\\n\\nElbow/shoulder fuse earlier, sternum has a wide fusion range, and skull sutures are irrelevant—hence medial end of clavicle + wrist + pelvis is optimal.\\n\\nPelvis:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-cba221e351","n":352,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"A young male is brought in for age estimation for medico-legal purposes. Based on the X-ray provided, what is the estimated age?","options":["10–12 years","12–15 years","15–17 years","&gt;18 years"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 15–17 years\\n\\nThe age estimation in medicolegal practice relies on the appearance and fusion of ossification centers. In this X-ray, the proximal humeral epiphysis is fused with the shaft, while the medial end of the clavicular epiphysis is not yet fused, indicating an age of approximately 15–17 years. Correlation with other skeletal radiographs and dental examination improves accuracy.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. 10–12 years: Proximal humeral epiphysis is not yet fused at this age.\\n• B. 12–15 years: Epiphyseal fusion around the shoulder is still incomplete.\\n• D. >18 years: By this age, most shoulder epiphyses are fused, and the medial clavicular epiphysis is approaching or has completed fusion (typically 22–25 years, the last epiphysis to fuse).\\n\\n| Ossification Center | Fusion Age (Approx.)\\n\\n| ------------------- | --------------------\\n\\n| Elbow | 14–16 years\\n\\n| Proximal humerus | 16–18 years\\n\\n| Distal radius & ulna | 17–19 years\\n\\n| Iliac crest | 19–21 years\\n\\n| Medial end of clavicle (last epiphysis) | 22–25 years\\n\\nExam Tip:\\n\\n• Medial clavicular epiphysis is the last epiphysis in the body to fuse and is frequently used for medicolegal age estimation around 18–25 years.\\n• Shoulder X-rays showing fused proximal humerus but unfused medial clavicle generally indicate late adolescence (≈15–17 years in exam-based interpretation).","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-994aa35402","n":353,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"All the following are used for identification of a dead body except:","options":["Muscle","Blood","Nail","Hair"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Nail\\n\\nIn forensic identification, biological samples containing rich DNA sources are preferred for establishing identity.\\n\\n• Commonly used samples:\\n\\n• Blood: High DNA yield → most reliable\\n• Muscle/tissues: Useful in decomposed bodies\\n• Hair (with root): Contains nuclear DNA\\n\\n• Nails:\\n\\n• Mainly composed of keratin (dead tissue)\\n• Poor DNA yield unless nail root is present\\n• Hence, not routinely used for identification\\n\\nForensic significance:\\n\\n• Proper sample selection improves accuracy of DNA profiling\\n• Helps in mass disasters, decomposed bodies, and unidentified remains","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6f6715a3b0","n":354,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"An X-ray image of the elbow joint is shown. The most likely age of the individual is:","options":["8–10 years","10–13 years","14–16 years","18–20 years"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 8–10 years\\n\\nAge estimation from an elbow X-ray is based on the appearance of the secondary ossification centers, remembered by the mnemonic CRITOE.\\n\\nCRITOE sequence of ossification:\\n\\n| Ossification center | Approximate age of appearance (years)\\n\\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------------\\n\\n| C – Capitellum | 1\\n\\n| R – Radial head | 3\\n\\n| I – Internal (medial) epicondyle | 5\\n\\n| T – Trochlea | 7\\n\\n| O – Olecranon | 9\\n\\n| E – External (lateral) epicondyle | 11\\n\\nIn this X-ray:\\n\\n• Capitellum, radial head, medial epicondyle, and trochlea are ossified.\\n• Olecranon ossification center is present.\\n• Lateral epicondyle has not yet appeared/fused.\\n\\nThis corresponds to an age of approximately 8–10 years.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. 10–13 years: Lateral epicondyle (E) should also be visible.\\n• C. 14–16 years: Fusion of several epiphyses begins.\\n• D. 18–20 years: Complete fusion of elbow ossification centers is expected.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-0009a1001f","n":355,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"Examination of suspicious stain shows the image below. Which test is this?","options":["Barberio","Florence","Teichmann","Takayama"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Barberio\\n\\nThe Barberio test is a microchemical test used to detect seminal stains by identifying spermine crystals.\\n\\n• Reagent: Saturated picric acid\\n• Positive test shows:\\n\\n• Yellow, needle-shaped crystals of spermine picrate\\n\\n• Indicates presence of semen\\n\\nForensic significance:\\n\\n• Helps confirm seminal stains in sexual assault cases\\n• More specific than preliminary tests\\n\\nComparison with other tests:\\n\\n• Florence test: Detects choline → brown rhombic crystals\\n• Acid phosphatase test: Screening test for semen (enzyme-based)\\n• Teichmann / Takayama tests: Used for blood identification, not semen","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-b64a03a619","n":356,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"Identify the type of fingerprint pattern depicted in the image below:","options":["Loops","Whorls","Arches","Composite"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Whorls\\n\\nUnderstand the basic fingerprint (dermatoglyphic) patterns used in forensic identification. A whorl is characterized by concentric or spiral ridges with two triradii (deltas) and is the second most common fingerprint pattern after loops. Fingerprints are unique, permanent, and remain unchanged throughout life, making them a reliable method of personal identification.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Loops: The most common pattern (60–70%), with ridges entering from one side, looping around, and exiting the same side; one delta is present.\\n• C. Arches: The least common pattern (5–10%), with ridges passing from one side to the other without looping; no delta is present.\\n• D. Composite: A mixed pattern containing features of two or more basic patterns (e.g., central pocket loop, double loop, accidental); not seen in this image.\\n\\n| Fingerprint Pattern | Key Feature | No. of Deltas\\n\\n| ------------------- | ----------- | -------------\\n\\n| Loop | Ridges loop back to the same side | 1\\n\\n| Whorl | Circular/spiral concentric ridges | 2\\n\\n| Arch | Ridges pass straight across | 0\\n\\n| Composite | Combination of basic patterns | Usually 2 or more","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-966898a58f","n":357,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"Which dental notation system corresponds to the tooth numbering layout shown below?","options":["Universal","Haderup","Palmer","FDI"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Palmer\\n\\nThe diagram shows teeth numbered 1–8 from the midline outward in each quadrant, with the same sequence mirrored in upper and lower jaws.\\n\\nThis layout corresponds to the Palmer notation system, which uses numbers 1–8 for each quadrant (traditionally with quadrant brackets/symbols).","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-d66850c908","n":358,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Human Identification","text":"Which of the following tests is not used to detect seminal stains?","options":["Barberio test","Florence test","Acid phosphatase test","Reinsch test"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Reinsch test\\n\\nDetection of seminal stains in forensic examination relies on identifying spermatozoa or semen-specific biochemical markers.\\n\\nTests used for seminal stain detection:\\n\\n• Florence test: Detects choline crystals (brown rhombic crystals)\\n• Barberio test: Detects spermine crystals (needle-shaped crystals)\\n• Acid phosphatase test: Screening test for prostatic enzyme (high in semen)\\n\\nWhy Reinsch test is NOT used:\\n\\n• Reinsch test is used for detection of heavy metals (e.g., arsenic, mercury)\\n• Has no role in semen identification","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e6a85c842b","n":359,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A 24-year-old man who was accused of murder was taken into police custody. As a part of the interrogation, the prisoner was kept in a prone position with both his wrists and ankles bound behind his back and secured with a rope. What is this method known as?","options":["Choke/carotid hold","Carotid sleeper hold","Hog tie","Bar arm"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Hog tie\\n\\nThis type of physical restraint in which a person is placed in prone position with his wrists and ankles bound behind his back and secured by a rope is known as hog-tying or total appendage restraint procedure. This can cause breathing difficulty and the person can become unresponsive. Rarely, death may occur due to asphyxia, soon following the physical restraint.\\n\\nCarotid hold also known as choke hold or bar arm involves placing the forearm straight across the front of the neck. The free hand grips the wrist and pulls it back causing obstruction of the airway and carotids resulting in immediate unconsciousness. Fracture of the hyoid or larynx can occur with greater force. Pressure on the carotid bodies can cause sudden cardiac arrest.\\n\\nIn carotid sleeper hold, also known as lateral vascular neck restraint, the arm is placed about the neck with the antecubital fossa centered at the midline of the neck. The free hand grips the wrist and the arm is pulled backward, compressing carotid arteries and jugular veins but not vertebral arteries or trachea. The person loses consciousness in 10 to 15 seconds and regains consciousness within 10 to 20 seconds upon releasing the pressure. Here, the neck structures are not damaged. Death may rarely occur due to catecholamine release or hypoxia which can trigger myocardial infarction or dysrhythmias in patients with ischemic heart disease.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f1b7ebc826","n":360,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A patient came to the casualty after a road traffic accident. A lacerated wound of 4x1 cm, which is bone-deep, is noted over the scalp of the forehead. Which of the following is the correct mechanism of this lacerated wound?","options":["Shearing force","Impact of a semi sharp-edged object","Overstretching of skin and subcutaneous tissue","Tissue crushed between weapon and underlying bone"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Tissue crushed between weapon and underlying bone\\n\\nThe description of the wound given in the question suggests a split laceration and the mechanism is\xa0the tissue being crushed between the underlying bone (i.e. skull) and the weapon.\\n\\nA laceration is a tear or a split in the skin, mucous membrane, muscle, or any internal organ produced by blunt force. The margins are irregular and may show minute tears and bruising.\xa0Tears that are seen at the ends, diverging away from the main laceration are called\xa0swallowtails.\xa0In skin lacerations, usually one of the margins overhangs the other. This helps in assessing the direction of the force.\\n\\nClassification of a laceration:\\n\\n• Split laceration (incised-like laceration): It\xa0occurs when the skin is\xa0crushed between two hard objects.\xa0For example, during a fall, the scalp gets crushed between the skull from inside and hard ground outside. This results in a wound, which usually mimics an incised one unless examined closely.\\n\\n• Incised-looking lacerated wound\xa0is a wound with the\xa0linear splitting of the tissue caused by blunt force.\xa0It is seen where the skin overlies an area of the bone where there is scanty underlying subcutaneous tissue such as the\xa0scalp, forehead, shin, etc.\\n\\n• Avulsed laceration:\xa0A lesion where the\xa0skin separates\xa0or peels off by tearing from the underlying tissues. An avulsion is a laceration produced by a shearing force delivered at an acute angle to tear off a portion of a traumatized surface or viscus from its attachments.\xa0It may\xa0overhang the wound. It is also called\xa0flaying.\xa0This is common in road traffic accidents. (Option A)\\n• Tears:\xa0Tearing of skin and subcutaneous tissue can occur from localized impact by or against irregular or semi-sharp objects.\xa0It may be due to blows by broken glass, or a fall on a rough projected object. (Option B)\\n• Stretch laceration:\xa0It occurs due to the\xa0over-stretching of the skin, beyond its elasticity.\xa0This is seen in wounds produced from running over by a motor vehicle, and when a fracture produces a sudden deformity of the bone. (Option C)\\n• Cut laceration:\xa0Injury by a heavy sharp-edged instrument. For example, cut from ax, chopper, etc\\n\\nThe image shows a split laceration which is incised looking laceration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c4c55940c9","n":361,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A pelvis is brought for forensic examination. Which of the following characteristics will identify the given pelvis as a male?\\n\\n1. Triangular obturator foramen\\n2. U-shaped pubic angle\\n3. Everted ischial tuberosity\\n4. Large greater sciatic notch\\n5. Large acetabulum\\n6. Deep preauricular sulcus","options":["2,3,4,5","4,5,6","Only 5","1 and 3"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Only 5\\n\\nAmong the given options,\xa0a large acetabulum (only 5)\xa0is the characteristic of a male pelvis.\\n\\nTriangular obturator foramen, U-shaped pubic angle, everted ischial tuberosity, large greater sciatic notch, deep preauricular sulcus are characteristics of a female pelvis.\\n\\nThe differences between male and female pelvis are summarized in the table below.\\n\\nCharacteristic\\nMale pelvis\\nFemale pelvis\\n\\nGeneral Morphology\\nMassive, \xa0rougher, marked muscle attachments\\nLess massive, slender, smoother\\n\\nOverall shape\\nDeep funnel\\nFlat bowl\\n\\nPreauricular sulcus\\nNarrow, shallow\\nBroad, deep\\n\\nAcetabulum\\nLarge, wider, deeper\\nSmall, narrower\\n\\nBody of pubis\\n\\nNarrow, triangular, thick\\n\\nBroad, square\\n\\nObturator foramen\\n\\nLarge, oval with base upward\\n\\nSmall, triangular with apex forwards\\n\\nSubpubic angle\\nV-shaped, narrow, (70o)\\nU-shaped, rounded, wide, (90o)\\n\\nGreater sciatic notch\\n\\nSmaller, narrower, deeper\\n\\nLarger, wider, shallower\\n\\nIschial Tuberosity\\nInverted\\nEverted, more widely separated\\n\\nPelvic Brim\\nHeart-Shaped\\nCircular or elliptical\\n\\nPelvic outlet\\nSmaller\\nLarger\\n\\nSacroiliac articulation\\n\\nLarge extends to 21/2 to 3 vertebrae\\n\\nSmall, oblique, extends to 2 to 21/2 vertebrae\\n\\nSacroiliac joint surface\\n\\nLarge and less sharply angulated\\n\\nL-shaped and elevated anteriorly\\n\\nSacrum\\n\\nLonger, narrower, with more evenly distributed curvature, promontory well marked. Body of first sacral vertebra larger.\\n\\nShorter, wider; upper\\nhalf almost straight, curve forward in the lower half, promontory less marked. Body of first sacral vertebra small","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-275eedaa02","n":362,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A person comes to the OPD with a burn injury to the right thigh. On examination, a soddened injury is seen along with erythema, blisters, and a line of demarcation that extends down the limb. What is the most likely cause of the injury?","options":["Electric burn","Chemical burn","Dry heat","Moist heat"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Moist heat\\n\\nThe\xa0soddened injury with erythema, blisters, and a line of demarcation that extends down the limb is highly suggestive of a\xa0scald injury caused by moist heat.\\n\\nScald injury is an injury that results from the application of liquid above 60\xb0C or from steam i.e moist heat.\xa0Redness appears immediately followed by blistering in a few minutes. Removal of the blistered skin leaves a raw, pink surface. Blisters are surrounded by a hyperaemic zone.\xa0Scalds appear soddened and bleached but the hair does not get singed. The skin does not get blackened or charred.\\n\\nScald injuries are limited to the area of contact and are more severe at the initial point of contact. It can occur through clothing, which worsens damage due to a longer period of contact with the hot liquid and a reduced cooling effect of evaporation.\\n\\nIt can be\\n\\n• Immersion burns: Accidental or deliberate immersion in hot liquid, usually water.\\n• Splash or spill burns: Usually accidental.\\n• Steam burns: Exposure to superheated steam.\\n\\nClinical degree of scalding:\\n\\n• Erythema (reddening)\\n• Vesication (blister formation)\\n• Necrosis of dermis when deeper layers of skin are involved.\\n\\nThe image below shows a scald burn due to moist heat.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Electric burns are injuries caused due to electric current, characterized by puckering of the skin at the edges of the burns. It is surrounded by an area of pallor. There is no red line at the point of entry and exit.\xa0The characteristic marks seen at the entry point are called Joule burns, or electrical burns/marks.\xa0They are chalky white, shallow, centrally collapsed blisters with a raised border.\\n\\nThe image below shows electric marks.\\n\\nOption B: Chemical burns by acid or alkalis appear as ulcerated patches without blisters. The hair is not singed and the red line of demarcation is absent. Sometimes, the burn shows distinct coloration and is usually uniform in character. Strong acids produce dark leathery burns on the skin. Strong alkalis cause the skin to slough and leave moist, slimy, greyish areas.\\n\\nOption C: Dry heat injuries are caused by a flame, via direct conduction or radiation to the skin.\xa0These are characterized by singeing of the hair and blackening of the skin. Vesication may or may not be present.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8789aab82b","n":363,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A person working in a dye factory presented with nausea, vomiting, dark bloody stools, conjunctivitis, and a burning sensation in the throat and stomach. Which poisoning do you suspect in this case?","options":["Potassium permanganate","Arsenic","Thallium","Lead"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Arsenic\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of an industrial worker presenting with nausea, vomiting, dark bloody stools, conjunctivitis, burning in the throat and stomach is suggestive of acute\xa0arsenic poisoning.\\n\\nThere are 3 types of arsenic poisoning,\\n\\n• Gastroenteric type is a common form and resembles bacterial food poisoning. There is a sweetish metallic taste. Features include:\\n\\n• GIT:\xa0Constriction in the throat and dysphagia, burning, and colicky pain in the esophagus, stomach, and bowel occur. Intense thirst and severe vomiting are constant symptoms. Involuntary and frequent\xa0dark-colored, stinking, and bloody stools that later become colorless, odorless, and watery resembling rice-water stools of cholera. A garlicky odor of breath and feces may be noted\\n• Renal: Oliguria, uremia, albuminuria, red cell casts.\\n• Cardiovascular system: Acute circulatory collapse with vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, ventricular tachycardia, and fibrillation.\\n• Central nervous system: Headache, vertigo, hyperthermia, convulsions, coma. Peripheral neuropathy is more sensory than motor.\\n• Death is usually due to circulatory failure.\\n\\n• Fulminant type: Massive doses of arsenic when rapidly absorbed cause death in one to three hours. Death occurs due to shock and peripheral vascular failure. Arsenic also has a direct action on the heart muscle. In this type gastrointestinal symptoms are absent.\\n• Narcotic type: The gastrointestinal symptoms are minimal. There is giddiness, formication, delirium. Arseniuretted hydrogen when inhaled as a gas, destroys hemoglobin and causes hemolysis, hemoglobinuria, and renal failure. Death is instantaneous.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Repeated stomach washes with\xa0warm water and milk.\\n• Emetics should not be used\\n• Oral hydrated ferric oxide is given in small doses. It converts toxic arsenic to non-toxic ferric arsenite\\n• Dimercaptosuccinic acid (DMSA) is preferred, if not available\xa0British anti-Lewisite (BAL) is used.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Potassium permanganate causes burning pain from the mouth to the stomach, intense thirst, dysphagia, stridor, and a persistent cough. The oral cavity is discolored, inflamed, and superficially corroded. Soon after poisoning the color is purple-brown, but later it appears coal-black.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Thallium poisoning is characterized by a triad of symptoms of acute gastrointestinal upset, painful combined polyneuropathy, and hair loss after poisoning. Other symptoms include\xa0muscle and joint pain, convulsions, optic neuritis, delirium. Loss of the outer third of eyebrows is significant. Maculo-papular butterfly-shaped skin eruption on the face is characteristic. There may be Mees lines on the nails.\\n\\nOption D: Acute\xa0Lead poisoning\xa0causes dry throat, burning abdominal pain, peripheral circulatory collapse, insomnia, paraesthesias, depression, coma, and death. Cerebellar ataxia is common in children with acute lead poisoning. A screening test for lead poisoning is the presence of porphyrinuria, mainly due to coproporphyrin III. Peripheral smear shows basophilic stippling. A Burtonian line may be visible on the gums.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fe6e9328b0","n":364,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A skull was recovered from a forest. According to inquest papers, a girl had gone missing 15 days back. The skull was sent to the forensic research lab. Which of the following would identify it as a female skull?\\n\\n1. Large frontal and parietal eminence\\n2. Heavy cheek bones\\n3. Smooth glabella\\n4. Square orbits\\n5. Narrow mastoid","options":["1 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 5","3, 4 and 5"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 3 and 5\\n\\nLarger frontal and parietal eminences, smooth glabella, and narrow mastoid are features of\xa0a female skull.\\n\\nDifferentiating features between male and female skull:\\n\\nTrait\\nMale\\nFemale\\n\\nGeneral size\\nLarger, longer (dolichocrania)\\nSmaller, rounder (brachycrania)\\n\\nArchitecture\\nRugged\\nSmooth\\n\\nGlabella\\nRough\\nSmooth\\n\\nSupraorbital ridges\\nProminent\\nLess prominent\\n\\nCheek bones\\nHeavier, laterally arched\\nLighter, compressed\\n\\nZygomatic arch\\nMore prominent\\nLess prominent\\n\\nMastoid process\\nProminent, wide and long\\nLess prominent, narrow and short\\n\\nOccipital region\\nMarked muscle markings\\nNo marked muscle markings\\n\\nFrontal eminences\\nSmall\\nLarge\\n\\nParietal eminences\\nSmall\\nLarge\\n\\nOrbits\\nSquare with round margins\\nRound with sharp margins\\n\\nMandible\\nLarge, broad ramus\\nSmall, small ramus\\n\\nChin shape\\nU-shaped\\nV-shaped\\n\\nNote: The features given above develop after puberty and are modified by senility, so are applicable only between the 20th and the 55th year.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-254bff1854","n":365,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"An 18-year-old female was sexually assaulted by a 20-year-old male. There was presence of white stains on the clothes of the victim. Barberio\'s test was performed on the stains. Which of the following is detected by this test?","options":["Spermine","Acid phosphatase","Choline","Fructose"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Spermine\\n\\nBarberio\'s test detects the presence of spermine\xa0which is present in prostatic secretions and thus helps in the identification of seminal fluid.\\n\\nIn Barberio\'s test, a saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of picric acid is added to the spermatic fluid. This produces\xa0yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals of spermine picrate.\\n\\nOption B: Acid phosphatase test\xa0is a quantitative test for the presence of acid phosphatase enzyme.\xa0High levels of acid phosphatase from prostatic secretions\xa0are present in seminal stains (The normal vaginal secretions have low levels of acid phosphatase). Positive reactions are found till 36 hours after which the enzyme levels fall and disappear within 72 hours. In the absence of demonstrable sperms or aspermia, this test can be considered conclusive.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Florence test detects choline\xa0which is secreted by seminal vesicles. The suspected\xa0seminal stain is extracted with 10%\xa0hydrochloric\xa0acid and a drop of it is placed over a slide. A coverslip is placed over it and a drop of Florence solution (potassium iodide, iodine, and water) is made to run under the coverslip. If semen is present, dark brown rhombic or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide appear immediately.\xa0The test is not confirmatory for the presence of semen, however, a negative reaction rules out the presence of semen.\\n\\nOption D: Fructose acts as an energy source for sperm. Its presence in seminal fluid indicates a patent efferent ductal system, as fructose will be absent in its obstruction.\\n\\nThe absolute proof of semen is finding at least one unbroken spermatozoon or electrophoretic LDH isoenzyme detection of sperms.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e87d172c8f","n":366,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Arrange the following in the correct order of symptomatology seen in iron toxicity:\\n\\n1. Pyloric stricture\\n2. Asymptomatic phase\\n3. Gastrointestinal bleeding\\n4. Liver failure and hypoglycemia","options":["2 -> 3 -> 1 -> 4","2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 1","3 -> 2 -> 4 -> 1","3 -> 4 -> 1 -> 2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 3 -> 2 -> 4 -> 1\\n\\nThe correct order of symptomatology in iron toxicity is 3 -> 2 -> 4 ->1.\\n\\nSymptoms of iron toxicity are divided into 4 stages:\\n\\n• Stage 1 (few hours after ingestion): vomiting, abdominal pain, gastrointestinal bleeding, shock, acidosis, and coma\\n• Stage 2 (6-24 hours): Asymptomatic phase\\n• Stage 3 (24-48 hours): Metabolic acidosis, jaundice, hypoglycemia, shock, coma with hepatic and renal failure.\\n• Stage 4 (1-2 weeks): Late complications such as gastric stricture and pyloric stenosis.\\n\\nIron exerts its toxic effects by increasing capillary permeability, release of hydrogen ions, inhibition of mitochondrial function, and direct corrosive action on the gastric mucosa. Ingestion of 20 to 30 grams is considered to be fatal and the fatal period is around 24-30 hours. Desferrioxamine is the mainstay of treatment with supportive gastric lavage, administration of egg and milk to form iron-protein complexes, oral MgOH solution, and hemodialysis in severe cases.\\n\\nPostmortem changes in iron toxicity include:\\n\\n• Hemorrhagic necrosis of gastric mucosa with perforation of gastric or jejunal wall\\n• Acute hepatic necrosis\\n• Degenerative renal tubular changes","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ea9a642611","n":367,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Following a blast, a man was brought from the scene to the emergency with multiple small puncture wounds to the right side of the chest, below the nipple, and multiple small abrasions and bruising over the left lower abdomen. Which of the following mechanisms is responsible for these injuries?","options":["Flying missiles","Blast of air","Explosion burns","Building collapse"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Flying missiles\\n\\nThe given history of\xa0multiple small puncture wounds to the right side of the chest, below the nipple, and multiple small abrasions and bruising over the left lower abdomen\xa0are highly suggestive of blast injuries sustained due to flying missiles.\\n\\nBlast injury is a complex type of physical trauma resulting from direct or indirect exposure to an explosion causing a sudden increase or decrease in atmospheric pressure.\\n\\nT.K.Marshall’s (Marshall\'s triad) described the characteristic injuries\xa0associated\xa0with bomb explosions. It includes:\\n\\n• Abrasions\\n• Bruises/contusions\\n• Puncture lacerations\\n\\nBlast injuries are classified as:\\n\\n• Primary injuries: Effect of transmitted blast waves on gas-containing structures. The external injuries are absent and are characterized by internal injuries. The middle, ear, lungs, and gastrointestinal tract are the most susceptible to this.\\n• Secondary injuries: Injuries caused by shrapnel and other airborne debris propelled by the explosion. These injuries may affect any part of the body and sometimes result in penetrating trauma. Most casualties are caused by secondary injuries.\\n• Tertiary injuries: These are caused by\xa0blast wind that can throw victims against solid objects.\\n• Quaternary injuries: All other injuries caused by explosions are not included in the above three categories. eg, flash burns, crush injuries, respiratory effects due to toxic dust, gas, etc.\\n\\nOption B: Blast of air or blast wave cause primary injuries which involve internal organs without external injury.\\n\\nOption C: Explosion burns: When the bomb explodes, the temperature of the explosive gases can exceed 2000\xb0C, and the heat radiated momentarily can cause flash burns. It is a quaternary type of blast injury.\\n\\nOption D: Building collapse can cause crush injuries which is a quaternary blast injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b4d20cb72e","n":368,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Match the following in relation to fingerprint changes.\\n\\n1)\xa0Incomplete atrophy of ridges\\na) Radiation\\n\\n2) Loss of pattern with ridge atrophy\\nb) Scleroderma\\n\\n3) Altered ridges\\nc) Celiac disease\\n\\n4) Permanent loss of fingerprints\\nd) Acromegaly\\n\\n5) Distance between ridges changed but pattern retained\\ne) Dermatitis","options":["1-e, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a, 5-d","1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-e, 5-a","1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b, 5-e","1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-e, 5-b"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-e, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a, 5-d\\n\\nThe correctly matched option is\xa01-e, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a, 5-d.\\n\\n1. Incomplete atrophy of ridges\\ne. Dermatitis\\n\\n2.\xa0Loss of pattern with ridge atrophy\\nc. Celiac disease\\n\\n3. Altered ridges\\nb. Scleroderma\\n\\n4. Permanent loss of fingerprints\\na. Radiation\\n\\n5. Distance between ridges changed but pattern retained\\nd. Acromegaly\\n\\nThe study of ridge patterns in the skin is Dactylography/ Galton-Henry system/ Dermatoglyphics.\xa0Fingerprints are the impressions of patterns produced by the papillary or epidermal ridges present on the fingertips.\\n\\nSome changes in fingerprints include:\\n\\n• Moderate epidermal ridge atrophy and loss of pattern in\xa0celiac disease\\n• Incomplete atrophy of the ridges in dermatitis\\n• Ridge alteration occurs in eczema, acanthosis nigricans, scleroderma, and dry or atrophic skin\\n• Permanent impairment of the fingerprint pattern occurs in leprosy, electric injury, and after exposure to radiation\\n• Distance between the ridges is changed but the\xa0pattern is not altered in\xa0infantile paralysis, rickets, and acromegaly\\n\\nClassification of fingerprint ridges:\\n\\n• Loops: radial or ulnar\\n• Whorls: concentric, spiral, double spiral, or almond-shaped\\n• Arches:\xa0plain, tented, exceptional\\n• Composite: They are a combination of 2 or more types\\n\\nThe fingerprint patterns are not inherited and cannot be used to prove paternity. The fingerprint pattern differs even between identical twins.\\n\\nEdgeoscopy is the study of edges of friction ridges which normally show seven characteristic features.\xa0Ridgeology is the study of friction ridges which are composed of the edges of ridge units that vary in size, shape, and alignment.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8a3bb7260e","n":369,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Match the following postmortem findings and their time of appearance:\\n\\nA. Adipocere formation\\n1. 24 hrs\\n\\nB. Relaxation of urethral sphincter\\n2. 2-3 days\\n\\nC. Marbling of veins\\n3. 4-5 days\\n\\nD. Liquefaction of tissues\\n4. 5-10 days","options":["A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4","A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3","A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4\\n\\nThe correct match is A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4.\\n\\nAdipocere formation starts in 4-5 days.\\nRelaxation of urethral sphincter by 2-3 days.\\nMarbling of veins starts in 24 hours.\\nLiquefaction of tissues by 5-10 days.\\n\\nIn postmortem marbling, blood in the vessels is haemolysed to sulfmeth-hemoglobin, which stains the vessel walls and the adjacent tissues, giving rise to a marbled appearance. The marbling of the skin begins by about 24 hours and becomes prominent in 36–48 hours after death. The image below shows postmortem marbling which is distinctly appreciable in the superficial veins of the abdomen, shoulders, chest, and inguinal region.\\n\\nUrethral sphincter relaxation is of two types;- Primary and Secondary. Primary relaxation occurs before rigor mortis sets in. Secondary relaxation occurs at the time of decomposition i.e. around 2-3 days.\\n\\nAdipocere formation, also called corpse wax, grave wax, or mortuary wax is a late sign of death. A wax-like organic substance is formed by the anaerobic hydrolysis or hydrogenation of fat in tissues by bacteria like Clostridium welchii. It has a greyish-white appearance with a distinctive offensive or sweetish smell. In a country like India, adipocere formation begins within 4-5 days and takes several weeks to complete.\\n\\nLiquefaction or colliquative changes begin 5 days after death and become prominent in 10 days. Soft tissues change into semisolid masses which are black and get separated from bones and fall off.\\n\\nNote: The first visible sign of putrefaction is greenish (due to sulfmeth-hemoglobin) discolouration of the anterior abdominal wall, usually in the right iliac fossa.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1c36fa602b","n":370,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Match the following weapons with their possible injury type\\n\\nA. Axe\\n1. Incised wound\\n\\nB. RTA\\n2. Tram track bruise\\n\\nC. Blade\\n3. Grazed abrasion\\n\\nD. Lathi\\n\\n4. Chop wound","options":["A-4,B-3,C-1,D-2","A-1,B-3,C-4,D-2","A-1,B-2,C-4,D-3","A-4,B-1,C-3,D-2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-4,B-3,C-1,D-2\\n\\nFor the above-given weapons, possible injury type is matched as under:\\n\\nA. Axe\\n4. Chop wounds\\n\\nB. RTA\\n3. Graze abrasions\\n\\nC. Blade\\n1. Incised wound\\n\\nD. Lathi\\n2. Tram track bruise\\n\\nMechanism of injury with the above weapons\\n\\nAxe - it is a heavy weapon that may or may not be sharpened. Injury is produced by chopping action of the weapon and it may be in a spectrum of an incised wound to laceration depending upon the sharpness of the blade.\\n\\nRTA - rubbing of epidermis tangential to a rough or hard surface gives rise to graze abrasion also called brush burn. It can occur in an RTA after a pedestrian is being hit by a vehicle and the victim is slid/grazed for some distance before he comes to a halt.\\n\\nBlade - it gives rise to a clean-cut wound which is usually deepest at its starting. Tailing of the wound indicates the direction of injury. Length is the biggest parameter of this type of wound.\\n\\nLathi- It is a blunt force injury giving rise to a patterned abrasion with 2 parallel lines and a normal unbruised area on site of impact or stick/lathi. The lathi forces the blood from the site of impact to either side which gives rise to tramline appearance.\\n\\nThe above image shows a chop wound inflicted on scalp.\\n\\nThe above image shows a schematic illustrating heaping up of epidermis in a graze abrasion or brush burn\\n\\nThe above image shows production of a tramline bruise associated with a lathi strike","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-85fda0bf53","n":371,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Match the following with the respective ages of fusion:\\n\\nA. Medial end of clavicle\\n1. 22-25 years\\n\\nB. Sacrum as a single bone\\n2. 50 years\\n\\nC. Crista scapulae\\n3. 45 years\\n\\nD. Lambdoid suture\\n4. 21-25 years","options":["A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4","A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3","A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2","A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3\\n\\nThe correct match is\xa0A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3.\\n\\nThe respective ages of fusion are:\\n\\n• The medial end of clavicle at 21-25 years\\n• Sacrum as a single bone at 22-25 years\\n• Crista scapulae at 50 years\\n• Lambdoid suture at 45 years\\n\\nThe secondary ossification center at the medial end of the clavicle appears at 18-19 years of age and fuses by 20-22 years of age. This occurs 2 years earlier in females. Thus the complete fusion of the medial end of clavicle can be used for\xa0establishment of age of 21 years\xa0in medicolegal cases.\\n\\nThe 5 sacral vertebrae are separated by cartilage till puberty. Fusion of epiphysis begins after puberty and ossification start from below upwards. Sacrum becomes a single bone by the age of 25 years.\\n\\nOssification of scapulae:\\n\\n• 30-35 years: lipping starts on ventral margin of glenoid cavity\\n• 35-40 years: irregular lipping around the clavicular facet and inferior surface of acromion process.\\n• 45 years: localized bony atrophy\\n• 50 years: Cristae scapulae\\n\\nLambdoid suture starts closing endocranially at 25-35 years and completes fusion by 45-50 years. Endocranial fusion occurs before ectocranial fusion.\\n\\nThe sagittal suture is used for age determination even though age estimation by skull sutures is not considered to be very reliable. The last skull suture to fuse is the squamous temporal suture, which fuses after 80 years of age.\\n\\nAge\\nAge determination\\n\\n<5 years\\nAppearance of ossification centers\\n\\n5-16 years\\nAnalysis of hand and wrist\\n\\n>16 years(male)\\nComplete fusion of distal radius or ulna\\n\\n>16 years(female)\\nComplete fusion of distal ulna\\n\\n>18 years(male)\\nComplete fusion of distal femur or proximal tibia\\n\\n>18 years(female)\\nComplete fusion of iliac crest\\n\\n>21 years\\nComplete fusion of medial end of clavicle","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1143af2f91","n":372,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"On conducting the autopsy on a victim of hanging, the ligature mark is seen at the lower 1/3rd of the neck. The victim is seen to have a protruded tongue. He was found with his head hanging to his left side with saliva dribbling from the left angle of his mouth. Both the pupils are dilated and the right eye is open. Compression of which of the following structures is the probable reason for an open right eye in this case?","options":["Right internal jugular vein","Cervical sympathetic chain","Left vagus nerve","Right internal carotid artery"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cervical sympathetic chain\\n\\nCompression of the cervical sympathetic chain is the probable reason for an open right eye in this case.\\n\\nDilated pupils are seen when the ligature knot presses on the cervical sympathetic chain, the eye on the same side may remain open and its pupil dilated termed as le facie sympathique. It indicates antemortem hanging.\\n\\nDribbling of saliva is the surest sign of antemortem hanging.\xa0This occurs due to the increased salivation before death due to the stimulation of the salivary glands by the ligature or by congestive hypoxia. Stimulation of pterygopalatine ganglion also causes increased salivation.\\n\\nDribbling of saliva occurs from the angle of the mouth opposite to the side of the knot. When the knot is on the nape of the neck, it occurs across the middle of the lower lip.\\n\\nThe ligature mark\xa0is usually above the hyoid bone.\xa0It runs obliquely, backward, upwards, is non continuous, and towards the point of suspension. The mark is noncontinuous because of a gap at the nape of the neck. When the knot is in an inverted V shape when it is in contact with the skin, because the ligature material extends downward on both sides. The skin is usually depressed/grooved, yellowish-brown, dry, hard, with small abrasions called rope burns at its edges.\\n\\nProtrusion of tongue results\xa0from the pressure on floor of the mouth by ligature. It is usually swollen and blue. Injuries include bite marks with or without underlying small hemorrhages (‘marginal’ hemorrhages).\\n\\nTardieu’s spots may be present on the forehead, over the eyelids, under the conjunctiva, and near the\xa0temple.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A-\xa0In partial hanging, internal jugular veins are compressed, but vertebral arteries continue to supply blood to the head, resulting in congestion of the face and petechiae.\xa0When suspension is complete, face is pale and petechial haemorrhages are relatively uncommon due to the complete obstruction of the arterial system.\\n\\nOption C-\xa0Vagus nerve compression leads to a sudden halt of the progression of the pure asphyxial process due to the superimposition of vagal inhibition. In these cases, the face remains pale.\\n\\nOption D- Right internal carotid artery compression can cause transverse tears of carotid artery intima, known as the Amussat\'s sign. It\xa0is seen with antemortem hanging. These are usually seen in obese victims and in posteriorly placed knots.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f758132dcb","n":373,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"The dead body of a 20-year-old man found in the sea was brought in for postmortem examination. Which of the following findings would you see in seawater drowning?\\n\\n1. Hypernatremia\\n2. Hyponatremia\\n3. Hyperkalemia\\n4. Myocardial anoxia\\n5. Hemodilution","options":["1 and 3","1 and 4","2, 3 and 5","2, 4 and 5"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1 and 4\\n\\nIn cases of seawater drowning, hypernatremia\xa0and myocardial anoxia are seen. Seawater being\xa0hypertonic to plasma causes a shift of fluid from the blood to alveoli causing pulmonary edema. There is a net decrease in blood volume causing hemoconcentration and a fall in blood pressure.\\n\\nThis hemoconcentration in seawater drowning causes increased viscosity of blood and\xa0myocardial anoxia leading to\xa0heart failure. Hyperkalemia is not seen in seawater drowning because RBCs get shrunken in contrast to freshwater drowning.\\n\\nFreshwater is hypotonic to plasma and is rapidly absorbed from the alveoli into the RBCs resulting in hemodilution and hyponatremia. The RBCs finally swell up and lyse resulting in the release of intracellular potassium causing hyperkalemia. Increased intravascular blood volume combined with hyperkalemia causes arrhythmia.\\n\\nThe table below summarises the cause of death in different types of drowning.\\n\\nType of drowning\\nCause of death\\n\\nFreshwater drowning\\n\\nSeawater drowning\\n\\nDisruption of surfactant\\n\\nDry drowning\\n\\nLaryngeal spasm/Vagal inhibition\\n\\nPost immersion Syndrome / Secondary drowning\\n\\n• Cerebral anoxia\\n• Pneumonia\\n• Pulmonary edema\\n• Chemical pneumonitis\\n• Metabolic acidosis","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0512a4a6cf","n":374,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"The friend and victim had alcohol and got into an argument over a common girlfriend, following which the friend kills the victim by sitting on his chest and covering his nose and mouth. This is an example of?","options":["Burking","Smothering","Traumatic asphyxia","Overlaying"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Burking\\n\\nThe given scenario is an example of burking.\\n\\nBurking is a method of homicidal smothering and traumatic asphyxia where the assaulter kneels or sits on the chest (traumatic asphyxia) and close the nose and mouth with his hands (smothering). This was the method of killing performed by William Burke and William Hare.\\n\\nImage shows burking\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Smothering is a form of asphyxia that is caused by closing the external respiratory orifices either by the hand or by other means or blocking up the cavities of the nose and mouth by a foreign substance, such as mud, paper, cloth, etc.\\n\\nBelow image shows smothering\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Traumatic asphyxia results from respiratory arrest due to mechanical fixation of the chest, by a heavy substance or object. The normal movements of the chest wall are prevented. There is a gross compression of the chest and usually abdomen by a powerful force, due to which chest expansion and diaphragmatic lowering are prevented. For example stampede deaths, crushing by falls of the earth in coal mines, or earthquakes.\\n\\nOption D: Overlaying or compression suffocation results due to compression of the chest, so as to prevent breathing. It occurs when an intoxicated parent or other person shares a bed with an infant. The thoracic movements are limited and respiratory exchange is either reduced or completely prevented which leads to death. It is always accidental.\\n\\nBelow image below shows overlaying.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-52e882d2a1","n":375,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Which of the following is true about the the cause of the given finding?","options":["Lightning strike","Electrocution","Marbling","Trickling down of acid"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lightning strike\\n\\nThe given finding is known as Lichtenberg\'s figures or filigree burns which are pathognomonic for lightning injury.\xa0It is a superficial, thin, tortuous, fern-like pattern of erythema in the skin. It indicates the path taken by the electrical discharge and tends to follow skin creases and long axis of the body.\\n\\nThey are also known as arborescent burns or feathering as they have a resemblance to the branches of a tree. Lichtenberg\'s figures occur due to the rupture of small vessels. They are usually seen over the shoulder or flank within an hour of injury and disappear in 1-2 days.\\n\\nMedico-legal aspect: Its recognition may be lifesaving in the unaccompanied comatose patient and is important because even delayed resuscitation of lightning victims can be very successful.\\n\\nLesions seen in electrical burns or electrocution are:\\n\\n• Joule burns:\xa0When the skin has been in firm contact with an electrical conductor, this may split the layers of the epidermis or the epidermal-dermal junction and produce a raised blister,\xa0called\xa0Joule burns.\\n• Spark lesion:\xa0When the contact is less firm, an air gap (albeit narrow) exists between skin and conductor, the current jumps the gap as a spark. This causes the outer skin keratin to melt over a small area. On cooling, the keratin fuses into a hard brownish nodule, usually raised above the surrounding surface, the so-called\xa0spark lesion.\xa0The heat generated by the current may melt the calcium phosphate, which is radiologically seen as typical round dense foci known as bone pearls\xa0or wax drippings.\\n• Crocodile skin effect\xa0is caused by\xa0high-tension current burns. In high-voltage burns, such as those sustained from high-tension grid transmission cables, where the voltage is in the multi-kilovolt range, sparking may occur over many centimeters. This can cause multiple spark lesions giving rise to a crocodile-skin effect.\\n\\nThe image given below shows Joule burns\\n\\nThe image given below shows\xa0high voltage burns due to electrocution causing multiple spark lesions. Inset shows crocodile skin effect\\n\\nImage below shows electrical marks\\n\\nMarbling\xa0is a postmortem finding seen when blood in the vessels is haemolysed to sulfmeth-hemoglobin, which stains the vessel walls and the adjacent tissues, giving rise to a marbled appearance.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/d44647138eb032518bbd.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-f5f75a50da","n":376,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Which of the following rules are used in case as tests of insanity?\\n\\n1. Durham\'s rule\\n2. Curren\'s rule\\n3. Ashley\'s rule\\n4. Murphy\'s rule","options":["1, 2, 3 and 4","2, 3, 4 only","1, 2 only","3, 4 only"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 2 only\\n\\nDurham\'s rule and Curren\'s rule are used in cases as tests for insanity.\\n\\nDurhams\'s rule states that an accused person is not criminally responsible if, his unlawful act is the product of mental disease or mental defect.\\n\\nCurren’s rule states that an accused person is not criminally responsible if, at the time of committing the act, he did not have the capacity to regulate his conduct to the requirements of the law as a result of mental disease or defect.\\n\\nIn India, the law of Criminal responsibility is under Section 84 I.P.C. It\xa0states that \\"Nothing is an offense which is done by a person who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or what he is doing is either wrong or contrary to the law”. This is slightly different from\xa0McNaughten\'s rule followed in Britain.\\n\\nMcNaughten\'s rule\xa0states that every man is to be presumed to be sane, and possesses a sufficient degree of reason to be responsible for his crimes until the contrary is proved.\\n\\nOther rules to assess the criminal responsibility of insane persons:\\n\\n• The Irresistible Impulse\\n• Federal rule or American Law Institute test (ALT)\\n• The Brawner rule.\\n\\nNote: Ashley\'s rule is used to differentiate males and females based on the combined length of the manubrium and sternum. It is considered to be male sternum when the combined length is >149mm, while if the combined length is\xa0<149mm, then it is considered female.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5e1f9a9035","n":377,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"A lady underwent cholecystectomy and now she presents with abdominal pain, one month later. An erect abdomen x-ray was taken, and the image is given below. The following offense comes under _______.","options":["Novus actus interveniens","Res ipsa loquitur","Res judicata","Respondeat superior"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Res ipsa loquitur\\n\\nThe x-ray shows a pair of surgical scissors left behind in the abdomen after surgery. The offense comes under res ipsa loquitur. It is obvious evidence of the negligent act committed.\\n\\n\\"Res ipsa loquitur\\"\xa0means \\"the thing or fact speaks for itself.\\"\xa0The negligent act is so obvious that the patient need not prove the negligence. He has to merely state what according to him was the negligent act.\\n\\nThis doctrine applies to both civil and criminal negligence. All of the following conditions should be fulfilled for this doctrine:\\n\\n• In the absence of negligence, there would not have been any injury usually\\n• The doctor had complete control over the injury-producing instrument or treatment.\\n• The patient was not a part of contributory negligence.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Novus actus interveniens: A person is responsible not only for his actions but also for the logical consequences of those actions. This principle applies to cases of assault and accidental injury.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Res judicata\xa0(Section 337, BNSS) -If a question of negligence against a doctor has already been decided by a court in a dispute between the doctor and his patient, the patient will not be allowed to contest the same in another legal proceeding, based on the same set of facts. Multiple appeals cannot be made.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0The principle of respondeat superior\xa0is used to explain vicarious liability, which explains that the employer is responsible for his employees’ negligence if such an act occurs in the course of the employment and within its scope.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/e34f5125161e7104f473.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-cd2255639f","n":378,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"A male patient was presented with a history of consumption of an unknown substance. He had increased sweating, lacrimation, diarrhea, muscle weakness, and bradycardia. What\'s the management?","options":["Atropine","Calcium gluconate","Pralidoxime","Obidoxime"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Atropine\\n\\nThe\xa0clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0organophosphate consumption and atropine\xa0is used for its management. Atropine is a muscarinic receptor antagonist and it\xa0competitively blocks the acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors.\\n\\nOrganophosphates are powerful acetylcholinesterase\xa0inhibitors. It is absorbed by inhalation or through the skin. Mucous membranes and the gastrointestinal tract are also the sites for absorption.\\n\\nThe predominant initial signs are of muscarinic excess which includes:\xa0(DUMBBELSS)\\n\\n• Diarrhoea\\n• Urination\\n• Miosis\\n• Bronchospasm\\n• Bradycardia\\n• Emesis\\n• Lacrimation\\n• Salivation\\n• Sweating\\n\\nNicotinic manifestations include twitching of eyelids, tongue and facial muscles along with tachycardia which then leads to paralysis. CNS stimulation results in headache, tremors, ataxia, coma and death.\\n\\nAtropine in larger doses can lead to atropine toxicity. The features are depicted in the following image:\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Calcium gluconate\xa0is used as the antidote for oxalic acid poisoning.\\n\\nOption C: Pralidoxime\xa0is also used in organophosphate poisoning which is used to counter the nicotinic effects of organophosphates.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Obidoxime\xa0is used in\xa0organophosphate poisoning if pralidoxime is not available.\\n\\nNote:\xa0In OP poisoning, the frequency of occurrence is Muscarinic --> CNS --> Nicotinic signs and symptoms. The definitive treatment, however, is atropine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5da749d969","n":379,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"A male patient working in the matchbox industry for the past 20 years reports to the dentist with multiple sinuses and fistulae in and around his jaw with foul-smelling discharge. Which of the following chemicals are responsible for this?","options":["Bromine","Antimony","Fluorine","Phosphorus"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Phosphorus\\n\\nThe clinical scenario is suggestive of a phossy jaw, which occurs due to chronic phosphorus poisoning. Phossy jaw refers to osteomyelitis and necrosis of the jaw, with foul-smelling pus discharging multiple sinuses.\\n\\nFrequent inhalation of phosphorus fumes causes necrosis of the lower jaw in the region of a decayed tooth. Patients initially present with toothache, followed by swelling of the jaw, loosening of the teeth, necrosis of gums, and sequestration of the bone in the mandible producing considerable facial disfigurement.\\n\\nChronic phosphorus poisoning is common among the workers of phosphorus industries (matchbox factories), who have had chronic exposure to phosphorus fumes for months to years. It is managed surgically and by prevention of further exposure to the poison.\\n\\nPhosphorus is a non-metallic, inorganic irritant poison that affects cellular oxidation. It exists in two forms, white phosphorus, a highly toxic form, and red phosphorus, the non-toxic form.\\n\\nFulminant poisoning is seen when more than one gram is consumed, and death occurs within 12 hours due to peripheral vascular collapse. In acute poisoning, they present with pain in the throat and abdomen, with intense thirst, nausea, diarrhea, and severe abdominal pain. System toxicity occurs after 2–3 days and results in hematemesis, enlarged tender liver, jaundice, pruritus, and hemorrhages. The gastric and intestinal content have garlic odor and are luminous.\\n\\nGastric lavage with potassium permanganate oxidizes phosphorus into phosphoric acid and phosphates. Activated charcoal can be used to adsorb the poison.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Bromine\xa0is a\xa0corrosive substance\xa0that causes necrosis of skin and tissues.\\n\\nOption B: The symptoms of antimony poisoning, both acute and chronic are similar to that of arsenic. Inhalation of fumes causes a flu-like illness called metal fume fever. In cases of chronic poisoning with heavy metals such as lead, arsenic, mercury, antimony, copper, etc.,\xa0the shaft of a long bone, strands of scalp hair, fingernails or toenails, and a wedge of the quadriceps muscle should be taken for sampling.\\n\\nOption C: Prolonged ingestion of fluorides results in the mottling of teeth, an increase in the density of bones,\xa0lipping of bony margins of vertebrae, which sometimes produce pressure on the spinal cord leading to paraplegia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a6d3bf3b4a","n":380,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"A man was brought to the emergency department with a history of assault by a mob using heavy blunt objects and sharp objects. On examination, crushing of the hair bulb was seen. Identify the type of wound.","options":["Clean cut incised wound","Lacerated wound","Chop wound","Avulsion injury"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lacerated wound\\n\\nThe type of wound in which the crushing of the hair bulb is seen is a lacerated wound.\\n\\nCut lacerations may be produced by blunt weapons or heavy relatively sharp-edged weapons such as axe, hatchets, and choppers. Scalp lacerations can be found in association with bruising and abrasions. Fascial strands, hair bulbs, nerves, and vessels, that run in the depth of the wound, are irregularly torn and crushed.\\n\\nMargins are irregular, ragged, and uneven. The ends are pointed or blunt. The edges of lacerations, especially over the skull are undermined. The depth of the wound varies according to the thickness of the soft parts at the site of the injury and the degree of force applied.\\n\\nMedicolegal importance of laceration:\\n\\n• The type of laceration indicates the cause of the injury and the shape of the weapon.\\n• Foreign bodies found in the wound may indicate the circumstances in which the crime was committed.\\n• The age of the injury can be determined.\\n\\nThe image below shows a lacerated wound.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: An incised wound is clean-cut, regular, and well-defined. Bruising does not occur when caused by light-cutting weapons but bruising and some irregularity of the margins may be present when heavy and not-so-sharp-edged weapons like a shovel, Kassi, axe, tangi, etc. have been used.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Chop wounds are caused by a blow with the sharp cutting edge of a fairly heavy weapon like an axe, sword, or hatchet.\xa0The margins of the wound depending on the condition of the weapon, a sharp margin with slight bruising is seen with a sharp-edged weapon, and a ragged and bruised margin is seen with a blunt edge. Undermining occurs in the direction towards which the chop is made.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Avulsion or shearing laceration\xa0is a lesion where the\xa0skin separates\xa0or peels off by tearing from the underlying tissues. It is due to shearing force delivered at an acute angle. Commonly seen in road traffic accidents.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/b701382bc8ff50dec053.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-d36305a993","n":381,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"A person of eonism derives pleasure from __________.","options":["Wearing clothes of opposite sex","Kissing and licking of anus by a sexual partner","Underclothing of female","Seeing a female undressing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Wearing clothes of opposite sex\\n\\nA person of eonism (transvestism) derives pleasure from wearing clothes of the opposite sex. It is usually found in males. His dress, manner, occupational interests, and associations are all designed to increase his feeling of being a woman.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nSexual pleasure from\\n\\n• Kissing and licking of anus by a sexual partner - Anilingus\\n• Sexual interest in nonliving objects that are used to generate sexual excitement.\xa0 -Fetichism\\n• Seeing a female undressing - Voyeurism or scoptophilia","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-45df452b0c","n":382,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Fingerprint first develops in how many weeks of intrauterine life?","options":["12-16","20-24","28-32","32-36"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 12-16\\n\\nFingerprint first develops in 12-16 weeks of intrauterine life. It is complete by 24 weeks. In identical twins\xa0also the pattern is different.\\n\\nThe image given below shows the different patterns of fingerprints:\\n\\nChanges might occur in fingerprints in certain conditions, some of them are given below:\\n\\nFingerprint changes\\n\\nCondition\\n\\nIncomplete atrophy of ridges\\nDermatitis\\n\\nLoss of pattern with ridge atrophy\\nCeliac disease\\n\\nAltered ridges\\nScleroderma\\n\\nPermanent loss of fingerprints\\nRadiation\\n\\nDistance between ridges changed but pattern is retained\\nAcromegaly","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-43857be071","n":383,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Gas shadow is present in the great vessels of a fetus in the case of intrauterine demise. What is this sign called?","options":["Roberts sign","Spalding sign","Ball sign","Halo sign"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Roberts sign\\n\\nGas shadow in the great vessels of a fetus in intrauterine death is called the\xa0Roberts sign. It is the earliest sign and is\xa0seen within 12 hours of death. The gas shadow may be present within the chambers of the heart or in large vessels of the fetus.\\n\\nSigns of intrauterine death:\\n\\n• Earliest sign\xa0- reddening of the skin with peeling in\xa012 hours\\n• Robert\'s sign\xa0-\xa0radiographic finding showing\xa0gas in the great vessels like the aorta in\xa012 hours\\n• Large blebs\xa0containing a\xa0red serous\xa0or\xa0serosanguineous fluid\xa0appear at around\xa024 hours\\n• Serous cavities may contain a turbid reddish fluid after\xa048 hours\\n• Spalding\'s sign (Option B) radiographic finding is seen as a\xa0loss\xa0of\xa0alignment\xa0and\xa0over-riding\xa0of\xa0bones\xa0of\xa0the\xa0cranial vault. This occurs\xa0due to shrinkage of the brain after death. It is\xa0seen\xa07 days after\xa0fetal death.\\n• A\xa0sweetish\xa0and\xa0disagreeable odor\\n• Sodden\xa0and\xa0coppery red/purplish skin\xa0(never greenish as with putrefaction) with blisters, and cuticle peeling.\xa0The skin detaches easily and leaves moist and greasy areas\\n• Distended abdomen\xa0and thick, red, and soft umbilical cord\\n• Abnormally mobile or flexible joints\\n• Soft, edematous, and loss of morphology of the organs or viscera (lungs and uterus remain unaffected for long period)\\n• The body is soft and pliable and flattens out when placed on an even surface\\n\\nOption C: Ball sign is\xa0hyperflexion of the spine with crowding of ribs seen in\xa0intrauterine death.\\n\\nOption D: Halo sign\xa0is a radiological sign of\xa0intrauterine fetal death showing an\xa0increase in soft tissue width between subcutaneous fat and the cranial vault.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b369604d84","n":384,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Hooch tragedy is related to:","options":["Phosphorus","Mercury","Ethanol","Methanol"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Methanol\\n\\nHooch tragedy is\xa0due to the\xa0consumption of cheap illicit liquor containing methanol, particularly by the lower socio-economic\xa0classes.\\n\\nThe central organ for metabolism is the liver. Methanol or methyl alcohol is first oxidized to formaldehyde with the help of alcohol dehydrogenase. It is further metabolized to formic acid via aldehyde dehydrogenase.\xa0These metabolites are responsible for the adverse effects of intoxication.\\n\\nFeatures seen are ophthalmological complications such as blindness, and optic neuropathy. Brain edema, acute renal failure, and severe metabolic derangement are also seen.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-03ed98eef6","n":385,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Identify the given plant.","options":["Datura alba","Argemone mexicana","Nerium odorum","Papaver somniferum"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Argemone mexicana\\n\\nThe given plant is Argemone mexicana.\\n\\nThe flowers are yellow in color. All parts of the plant are poisonous. Oil extracted from argemone seeds is commonly used as an\xa0adulterant\xa0in\xa0mustard oil.\xa0Sanguinarine\xa0and\xa0dihydrosanguinarine\xa0are the toxic alkaloids present in the oil and it causes\xa0epidemic dropsy.\\n\\nEpidemic dropsy is characterized by vomiting, diarrhea, edema of the feet, and generalized anasarca.\xa0Some patients may develop\xa0hypersecretory glaucoma. It can lead to death due to\xa0cardiac failure.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Nerium odorum -\xa0Flowers are usually white, pink, dark red, or pale yellow in colour.\xa0All parts of the plant including nectar, are poisonous. It contains several cardiac glycosides such as\xa0neriodorin, neriodorein, and karabin. The action of\xa0neriodorin mimics digitalis\xa0causing death due to cardiac failure. It can resist heat and hence can be found even in burnt dead bodies.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Papaver somniferum -\xa0The flowers are white in color. Opium is the dry juice of this plant. Morphine is isolated from opium. The clinical features include\xa0euphoric feeling in the\xa0stage of excitement followed by headache, giddiness, and a tendency to sleep in the\xa0stage of stupor.\xa0The stage\xa0of narcosis is characterized by a patient in a deep coma, who cannot be aroused.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/0e6c3c9664eec56ca497.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-479aa39d31","n":386,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Identify the pattern of abrasion shown in the image below.","options":["Ligature mark","Graze abrasion","Pressure abrasion","Imprint abrasion"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pressure abrasion\\n\\nThe given image shows an abrasion due to ligature mark, which is a\xa0pressure abrasion.\\n\\nAbrasions are superficial wounds produced due to\xa0injury to\xa0the epidermis. They are usually caused by friction against a rough surface or by compression.\\n\\nTypes of abrasions:\\n\\n• Linear\xa0abrasions (scratches): Caused by relative movement of a pointed object on the skin\\n\\n• Examples: nail, thorn, the point of sword or knife\\n\\n• Grazed\xa0abrasions (sliding /scrapping/grinding abrasion): Caused by relative movement of a rough surface on a wide area of skin\\n\\n• brush burns- tangential rubbing over the surface, usually the ground\\n• friction burns are extensive excoriated areas without bleeding\\n• Examples: fall, a victim being dragged on the ground, road traffic accidents\\n\\n• Imprint\xa0abrasions (patterned abrasions): Caused by the impact of a rigid hard rough surface over a localized area of the skin without any movement; usually show the pattern of the object causing them\\n\\n• Examples: tire,\xa0grills, bicycle chain, bite marks\\n\\n• Pressure abrasions (pressure abrasion): Caused by crushing of the superficial layers of the epidermis\\n\\n• Examples: ligature marks around the neck in hanging","img":false,"images":["/bimg/6da284a0f4878a7ac162.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-ce49a23a59","n":387,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Identify the postmortem change given in the image.","options":["Marbling","Tattooing","Lividity","Filigree burns"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Marbling\\n\\nThe postmortem change shown above is marbling,\xa0which is an external\xa0sign\xa0of\xa0putrefaction.\\n\\nIn marbling, the\xa0superficial veins\xa0of roots of the limb, thighs, sides of the abdomen, and shoulders are\xa0stained\xa0greenish-brown\xa0or purplish-red, in a\xa0linear branching pattern. It is usually prominent\xa036–48 hours after death\xa0occurs due to the formation of\xa0sulfhemoglobin\xa0subsequent to\xa0hemolysis\xa0of red blood cells.\\n\\nIn putrefaction,\xa0bacteria\xa0from the\xa0gut\xa0enter into and\xa0multiply\xa0in the\xa0surrounding tissues,\xa0eventually spreading through the blood vessels to other parts of the body.\xa0The bacterium\xa0Clostridium welchii\xa0is largely responsible for the\xa0putrefactive changes\xa0spreading through the body, due to its production of the enzyme\xa0lecithinase. The\xa0first external sign\xa0of putrefaction in a body lying outside in the air is a\xa0greenish discoloration\xa0of the\xa0skin\xa0over the region of the\xa0cecum.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Tattooing\xa0around the entry wound of a bullet is due to\xa0the deposition of\xa0unburnt powder particles. These are difficult to remove. They are also called stippling or peppering. This is seen\xa0up to 50 cm\xa0of firing\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Postmortem hypostasis/Postmortem staining/Postmortem lividity/Livor mortis/Suggilation\xa0refers to bluish-purple or reddish-purple discoloration\xa0of the superficial layers of the dermis.\xa0It is an\xa0early\xa0postmortem change that occurs due to\xa0the stagnation of blood in the\xa0vessels of the dependent parts\xa0due to the effect of gravity.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Filigree burns also known as\xa0Lichtenberg\'s flowers are\xa0superficial, thin, irregular, and tortuous markings on the skin indicative of a\xa0lightning strike.\xa0They resemble the branches of a tree and are usually found over the skin of the shoulders or the flanks.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/290bba8eb6bc5c07707d.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-61a69319c0","n":388,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Match the following plant poisons with their active principles.\\n\\n1. Datura\\na. Atropine\\n\\n2. Opium\\nb. Abrin\\n\\n3. Abrus precatorius\\nc. Thebaine\\n\\n4. Nux vomica\\nd. Strychnine","options":["1-b,2-c,3-d,4-a.","1-a,2-c,3-b,4-d.","1-a,2-b,3-c,4-d.","1-c,2-b,3-a,4-d."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1-a,2-c,3-b,4-d.\\n\\nThe correct match is\xa01-a,2-c,3-b,4-d.\\n\\nThe active ingredients of\xa0Datura\xa0include the anticholinergic alkaloids atropine, scopolamine, and hyoscyamine. The seeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked.\\n\\nManifestations of Datura toxicity:\\n\\n• Dry hot skin (Temperature rises up to 42 C, a scarlet rash may also be appreciated).\\n• Dilatation of pupils along with loss of accommodation\\n• Diplopia\\n• Dryness of the mouth and throat, unquenchable thirst\\n• Dysphagia\\n• Dilatation of cutaneous blood vessels\\n• Dysarthria (Difficulty in talking)\\n• Drunken gait\\n• Delirium\\n• Drowsiness\\n• Death due to respiratory or cardiac failure\\n\\nMorphine, codeine, and thebaine are natural opioids derived from poppy\xa0Papaver somniferum.\\n\\nAbrin\xa0is a toxalbumin of the plant\xa0Abrus precatorius,\xa0and it acts by\xa0inhibiting protein synthesis.\\n\\nToxic principles\xa0of\xa0Abrus precatorius:\\n\\n• Abrin (a toxalbumen)\\n• Abrine (an amino acid)\\n• Abralin (a glucoside)\\n\\nAbrin acts by inhibiting protein synthesis, thereby, resulting in cell death. The clinical features after oral ingestion of these seeds include abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, bloody diarrhea, and miosis. Faintness, dyspnea, and convulsions can also occur.\\n\\nThe poison obtained from Strychnos nux-vomica\xa0seeds is\xa0strychnine.\xa0Strychnine acts at the\xa0ventral horn\xa0by\xa0blocking\xa0the postganglionic\xa0glycine receptor\xa0sites in the\xa0spinal cord\xa0and\xa0brainstem.\xa0Glycine\xa0is an\xa0inhibitory\xa0neurotransmitter\xa0that acts at the\xa0postsynaptic membranes of ventral horn\xa0motor neurons. Hence, the blockade of these receptors results in unopposed muscle contraction, resulting in the classical clinical features:\\n\\n• Tonic-clonic seizures\\n• Opisthotonus (body arched in hyperextension)\\n• Risus sardonicus","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a25a828f79","n":389,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Match the following:\\n\\nTooth\\nAge of eruption\\n\\nA. Canine\\n1. 6-7 years\\n\\nB. Lateral incisor\\n2. 8-9 years\\n\\nC. First molar\\n3. 11-12 years\\n\\nD. Third molar\\n4. 17-25 years","options":["A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4","A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4\\n\\nThe correct option is Option C: A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4.\\n\\nThe time of emergence of secondary dentition or permanent teeth:\\n\\nTooth\\nEmergence\\n\\nFirst molar\\n6-7 years\\n\\nCentral incisors\\n6-8 years\\n\\nLateral incisors\\n7-9 years\\n\\nFirst premolar\\n9-11 years\\n\\nSecond premolar\\n10-12 years\\n\\nCanine\\n11-12 years\\n\\nSecond molar\\n12-14 years\\n\\nThird molar\\n17-25 years\\n\\nThe time of emergence of primary dentition or temporary teeth:\\n\\nTooth\\nEmergence\\n\\nMandibular central incisor\\n6-8 months\\n\\nMaxillary central incisor\\n7-9 months\\n\\nMaxillary lateral incisor\\n7-9 months\\n\\nMandibular lateral incisor\\n10-12 months\\n\\nFirst molar\\n12-14 months\\n\\nCanine\\n17-18 months\\n\\nSecond molar\\n20-30 months\\n\\nThe\xa0deciduous teeth all emerge into the mouth by\xa02.5-3 years.\\n\\nNote:\xa06-13\xa0years is the age of\xa0mixed dentition. The first\xa0molar emerges at 6-7 years. The second molar emerges at 12 years, and the third molar emerges beyond 18 years of age.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2d0339d496","n":390,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Pick the mechanism of cold water drowning.","options":["Asphyxia","Laryngeal spasm","Vagal inhibition","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vagal inhibition\\n\\nIn cold water drowning or immersion syndrome or hydrocution, death occurs due to vagal inhibition, leading to cardiac arrest.\\n\\nHydrocution/ immersion syndrome refers to sudden death, following immersion in cold water. Death results from vagal inhibition\xa0of the heart, i.e. cardiac arrest occurring due to increased stimulation of the vagus nerve.\\n\\nVagus stimulation occurs due to the following mechanisms:\\n\\n• Sudden entry of water into the nasopharynx or larynx\\n• Falling or diving into the water in a manner, so that it suddenly strikes the abdomen, especially the epigastric region\\n• Sudden in-rush of cold water into the ears\\n\\nTypes of drowning:\\n\\n• Wet drowning/ typical drowning\\n• Atypical drowning/dry drowning\\n\\n• Immersion syndrome/ hydrocution\\n• Post-immersion syndrome/ secondary drowning/ near-drowning\\n• Submersion of the unconscious/ shallow-water drowning","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-88da2842d1","n":391,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Poisoning with which of the following agents would cause hypertension?","options":["Organophosphorus","Paracetamol","Arsenic","Cocaine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cocaine\\n\\nPoisoning with cocaine cause hypertension. This is due to the inhibition of norepinephrine at the adrenergic terminals leading to increased\xa0sympathetic stimulation. This effect occurs at all concentrations of cocaine and causes\xa0hypertension\xa0and\xa0ventricular ectopics.\\n\\nCocaine is obtained from the plant Erythroxylum coca.\xa0The symptoms of cocaine poisoning are divided into two stages:\\n\\n1. Stage of excitement: This stage is characterized by features like dysphagia, dryness in the mouth, tachycardia, and dilated pupils. The reflexes are exaggerated and are associated with tingling and numbness in the hands and feet.\\n2. Stage of depression: This stage presents with feeble respiration, profuse sweating, convulsions, and death. In most cases, the cause of death is cardiopulmonary failure or vascular collapse.\\n\\nChronic users develop a feeling of insects crawling through the skin or the presence of grains of sand under the skin. This is pathognomic for cocaine poisoning and is known as cocaine bugs or Magnan\'s symptoms. Ulceration and sometimes perforation of the nasal septum are seen in chronic abusers due to the sniffing habit.\\n\\nThe image of the nasal septum of a chronic abuser is shown below:\\n\\nThe image of the plant is shown below:\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Organophosphorous poisoning produces autonomic instability. Both tachycardia and bradycardia can be seen, however, usually it is the parasympathetic effects which predominate.\\n\\nOption B: Paracetamol poisoning causes hypotension, tachycardia, diaphoresis, etc.\\n\\nOption C: Arsenic poisoning causes vasodilation, ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation, etc.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f0f8b2f1d3","n":392,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Post-mortem examination of a patient reveals the following. What is this finding known as?","options":["Hesitation cuts","Grazed abrasion","Laceration","Patterned bruise"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Hesitation cuts\\n\\nThe finding is known as hesitation cuts. It is a feature of suicide. They are small multiple cuts which involve only the superficial skin. They are seen at the beginning of the incised wound. The throat, wrist and front of the chest\xa0are the most common sites. Unintentional cuts\xa0are seen on the fingers in which the razor blade are held.\\n\\nOther options:\\nOption B: Grazed abrasion\xa0occurs when there is movement between skin and some rough surface. The epidermis is destroyed and shows uneven longitudinal parallel lines in the direction of the force. Cleaning and dressing is the treatment.\\n\\nOption C: In laceration tearing of the skin, mucous membrane, muscles or internal organs occurs due to blunt force application. The tissues are crushed and stretched beyond their elasticity limit.\\n\\nOption D: In\xa0Patterned bruise, it mirrors\xa0the pattern of the object used. The tissue is crushed if heavier objects are used and will produce a solid contusion pattern.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/211c29570503dd0e450d.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-1167d204fc","n":393,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Sagar was being tried in a court for the murder of Mohan in the park. Shikhar appeared in court and said he saw Sagar carrying a knife in his hand while in the park. What is this evidence known as?","options":["Direct","Indirect","Hostile","Hearsay"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Indirect\\n\\nThis evidence is known as indirect evidence. Indirect evidence is also known as circumstantial evidence.\xa0It is not the direct testimony of an eyewitness. It has an effect on other facts and subsidiary facts which are depended upon as consistent.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Direct evidence is the evidence of a fact that is actually in issue. Eg: The witness saw A shooting B, prescription, consent form\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Hostile evidence is given by someone who is supposed to have some interest or motive for hiding part of the truth. He might give completely false evidence too.\\n\\nOption D: Hearsay\xa0evidence is given by someone\xa0about something that he did not personally witness. Sometimes it might be obtained from a third party. It is presented in the court to claim\xa0that the facts contained in the statement are true.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-670cd06141","n":394,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Spanish windlass is a type of which strangulation method?","options":["Bansdola","Garrotting","Mugging","Throttling"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Garrotting\\n\\nSpanish windlass is an improvised version of garrotting, and it involves\xa0tightening an iron collar (Spanish windlass) around the neck and forcing a spike against the neck into the spinal cord.\\n\\nThis was a form of judicial execution once employed in\xa0Spain. The rod-like object used to tighten the ligature is called a\xa0Spanish windlass;\xa0this may be a ruler, stick, or screwdriver.\\n\\nGarrotting involves\xa0attacking the victim from behind using a ligature. The ligature is thrown over the victim’s neck and then tightened by twisting with a lever.\\n\\nThe image given below shows garrotting.\\n\\nOption A: Squeezing to death using sticks is called bansdola. The neck or the chest is squeezed between two sticks, one placed in front, and one behind. The neck may also be squeezed with a single stick in front if the victim is lying down.\\n\\nOption C: Squeezing\xa0the neck with the arm and forearm is called mugging.\xa0The image below shows mugging.\\n\\nOption D: Throttling\xa0or manual strangulation occurs due to compression of the neck by humans leading to asphyxia. Thumb impression and finger marks may be seen over the victim\'s neck.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f2d8961041","n":395,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Tattooing around the entry wound of a bullet is due to?","options":["Unburnt particles","Smoke","Flame","Muzzle impression"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Unburnt particles\\n\\nTattooing\xa0around the entry wound of a bullet is due to\xa0the deposition of unburnt powder particles. These are difficult to remove. They are also called stippling or peppering. This is seen up to 50 cm of firing\\n\\nWhen the distance between the firing and the victim is between 30-50 cm (in the case of pistols and revolvers), it is a near-shot wound. The victim is within the range of powder deposition, so there is some amount of tattooing present. But it is outside the range of flame, so there will not be any burning or singeing. There will not be any blackening, as it is outside the range of smoke as well.\\n\\nThe injuries related to firearms are:\\n\\n• Burn injuries\xa0- occur due to flame released during the firing. It is seen in contact shot wounds or distances up to 5-8 cm. Singeing of hair is caused due to burns\\n• Blackening\xa0- occurs due to the deposit of smoke. It can be removed with a wet cloth. This can be seen up to around 15 cm of firing\\n• Tattooing\xa0- occurs due to the deposition of unburnt powder particles. These are difficult to remove. They are also called stippling or peppering. This is seen up to 50 cm of firing\\n• Greasing and collaring\xa0- occur due to the deposition of the lubricant of the bullet in the tissues, during its penetration. It can be found in contact, near-shot, or distant shot wounds.\\n\\nIn a firm contact shot,\xa0the imprint of the muzzle of the gun is seen on the skin around the wound. The victim is within the range of flame, hence singeing of the hair will be present. Tattooing begins only when the muzzle-to-target distance is >1cm, like in a loose contact shot.\\n\\nWhen the distance between the victim and the firing is 5-8cm, it is called a close shot. The victim is within the range of the flame, hence, features related to flame, smoke, and powder deposition will be seen. Singeing, burning, blackening and tattooing will be present.\\n\\nWhen the distance between the victim and the firing is >50 cm, it is a distant shot. The victim lies outside the range of flame and powder deposition. Hence, features related to flame, smoke, and powder deposition are absent. Burning, blackening, and tattooing are absent.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-cd7814fc67","n":396,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"The dead body of a child was recovered from a river with froth around his mouth, which reappeared even after wiping it off. Which postmortem finding is not seen in this case?","options":["Voluminous and ballooned out lungs","High concentration of arsenic from shaft of hair","Paltauf\'s hemorrhage in sub pleura","Cadaveric spasm with mud in closed fist"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. High concentration of arsenic from shaft of hair\\n\\nThe above case scenario with frothing is suggestive of antemortem drowning.\xa0High concentration of arsenic in the shaft of hair is seen in patients with chronic arsenic poisoning.\\n\\nGiven below are the features of antemortem drowning.\\n\\n• Froth\xa0- The presence of copious amounts of fine, shaving-lather-like froth at the mouth and the nostrils is the most characteristic finding of antemortem drowning. It is caused by the vigorous agitation of the tracheal and bronchial mucous secretions, edema fluid, and alveolar surfactant during respiratory efforts.\\n• Cutis anserina\xa0- The contraction of the erector pilorum muscles causes puckering and a grainy appearance of the skin. This resembles goose skin;\xa0hence, it is called cutis anserina.\\n• Washerwoman’s hand\xa0- Maceration of the skin on the palms and soles gives a wrinkled, sodden, and bleached appearance due to submersion.\\n• Cadaveric spasm\xa0- Gravel, weed, soil, grass, etc. is firmly held in the clenched hands due to cadaveric spasm. This is a vital sign of antemortem drowning. The material also indicates the place of submersion.\\n• Rigor mortis - This appears early in cases of a violent antemortem struggle.\\n• Face - It is bloated and discolored, with cyanosis.\\n• Eyes - Conjunctiva are suffused and pupils dilated.\\n• Tongue - It may be swollen and protruded.\\n• Antemortem injuries - These may be sustained by striking objects, such as rocks, during the fall into the water body. Examination for such injuries must be performed only after the body is dry.\\n\\nInternal findings:\\n\\n• Lungs - They appear voluminous and distended show ballooning. The cut section will exude copious amounts of frothy, bloodstained fluid.\\n• Larynx, trachea, and bronchioles - The presence of sand, silt, and aquatic vegetation in the trachea and lower respiratory tract are characteristic findings of antemortem drowning.\\n• Heart and blood vessels- Gettler test can be done to estimate the chloride content in the right and left sides of the heart to differentiate between freshwater and saltwater drowning.\\n• Diatom test- Diatoms are microscopic algae. Their presence in the organs, particularly in the bone marrow, is suggestive of antemortem drowning.\\n• Stomach and small intestines- These organs may contain water in some cases. Water present in the small intestine is positive evidence of death by drowning.\\n• Middle ear - The presence of water in the middle ear is highly suggestive of antemortem drowning.\\n• Brain - It is congested, and edematous, with loss of gray-white junction.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/b6b21588c3f46647e11c.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-b058ca27ab","n":397,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"The image given below is a type of _______.","options":["Contusion","Abrasion","Laceration","Stab wound"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Contusion\\n\\nThe type of injury shown in the image is a contusion.\\n\\nA\xa0contusion\xa0is characterized by the infiltration of extravasated blood into the subcutaneous and/or subepithelial tissues. It results from the rupture of small blood vessels due to the application of\xa0blunt force.\\n\\n• A pure bruise lies beneath an\xa0intact epidermis.\\n• Bruises are caused by\xa0damage to veins, venules, and small arteries.\\n• The usual bruise from a blunt impact is\xa0situated in the subcutaneous tissues, often in the fat layer. (Because of the greater volume of soft subcutaneous tissue in fat people, they tend to bruise more easily than thin ones).\\n• Delayed bruising\xa0- It is common knowledge among lay persons, as well as doctors, that a bruise may ‘come out’ - that is, become more prominent with the passage of hours or days. This is partly caused by continued bleeding from the ruptured vessels, but mainly by the percolation of free blood from its origin deeper in the tissues upwards towards the epidermis.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Abrasion, also known as\xa0gravel rash, is the\xa0destruction of\xa0superficial layers of the epidermis. It is caused by\xa0friction against a rough surface or by compression. It is of four types:\\n\\n• Scratches\\n• Grazes\\n• Pressure abrasions\\n• Impact abrasions\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Laceration refers to a tear or split of the\xa0skin, mucous membranes, muscle, or internal organs. It is due to the application of blunt force to a broad area of the body.\xa0They are also called tears or ruptures.\xa0The laceration may indicate the cause of the injury and the shape of the blunt weapon. The age of the injury can also be determined.\\n\\nOption D: A stab wound is formed\xa0when force is delivered along the long axis of a narrow or pointed object. The object can be a\xa0knife, sword, chisel, scissors, or needle. It is\xa0deeper than its length and width on the surface of the skin. The edges are clean-cut and inverted.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/27cec6c764afc4d703fe.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-e50b656477","n":398,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"The steps in the management of snake bite include:\\n\\n1. Loose tourniquet\\n2. Debride to avoid the spread\\n3. Kill the snake\\n4. Hospitalize for anti-venom administration\\n5. Immobilization","options":["1, 2, 3, 4, and 5","1, 2, and 4","4 only","4 and 5"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 4 and 5\\n\\nThe management of snakebites includes immobilization\xa0and administration\xa0of\xa0anti-snake venom (ASV)\xa0after hospitalization.\\n\\nImmobilization is done to minimize the spread of the venom.\xa0Hospitalization, followed by ASV\xa0administration is the definitive management of snake bites.\\n\\nMeasures that can be taken\xa0in the case of snakebite:\\n\\n• Apply firm pressure over the bitten area, which delays the absorption of venom\\n• Pressure immobilization is recommended for elapid and sea-snake bites, but not for viper bites as it may cause local necrosis\\n• Immediately apply a broad firm bandage (Sutherland wrap) on the bitten area and around the limb. As much of the limb should be bandaged as possible. It should be tight enough to occlude the superficial venous and lymphatic return, but not the arterial or deep venous flow. A pressure of 50 to 70 mm Hg is maintained.\\n• In bites on the trunk, head, or neck, apply firm pressure over the bitten area\\n• Immobilize the limb, as movement can accelerate the spread of venom\\n• Make the patient lie on one side in the recovery position so that the airway is clear, in case of vomiting or fainting.\\n\\nMeasures contraindicated in the case of snakebite:\\n\\n• Suction and incision of the wound\\n• Electric shock treatment of wound site\\n• Application of tourniquets\xa0because they obstruct arterial flow and cause ischemia\\n• Ice water immersion of the bitten limb\\n• Do not attempt to kill or catch the snake as it may be dangerous.\\n• Administration of ASV locally at the wound site.\\n• Herbal/local remedies","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3073a46bff","n":399,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"To detect vaginal cells on a person who is accused of rape, which test will you use?","options":["Florence","Barberio","Lugol\'s","Precipitin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Lugol\'s\\n\\nLugol\'s iodine\xa0test will be used to detect the vaginal cells present on the penis of a rape accused. It shows brown color on filter paper due to the presence of glycogen in epithelial cells of the vagina.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Florence test\xa0is used to detect choline in\xa0that originates from seminal vesicles. It produces dark brown, rhombic,\xa0or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide if semen is present.\\n\\nOption B: Barberio test\xa0is used to detect spermatic fluid. It produces yellow rhombic crystals of spermine picrate when picric acid is added to spermatic fluid.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Precipitin test\xa0is used for species identification of semen stain. A positive test will confirm human origin.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7a31946976","n":400,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Treatment of choice for acute arsenic poisoning is :","options":["Ipecac","Penicillamine","Activated charcoal","Dimercaprol"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Dimercaprol\\n\\nDimercaprol is the treatment of choice for acute arsenic poisoning.\\n\\nArsenic is an odorless, tasteless, metallic poison. Gastrointestinal features of\xa0acute\xa0arsenic poisoning include nausea and vomiting, colicky abdominal pain, and profuse diarrhea with\xa0rice water stools.\\n\\nIn\xa0chronic\xa0arsenic poisoning, skin symptoms include erythematous lesions, and flushing and can also lead to hyperkeratotic skin and desquamation. Pigmentation is patchy and resembles\xa0raindrops\xa0on a dusty road. In the distal parts of the body, hyperkeratosis is present.\xa0\xa0Mees lines\xa0are transverse white striae of the fingernails, called striate leukonychia. Renal manifestations include chronic\xa0nephritis\xa0and urine contains albumin, casts, etc.\\n\\nArsenic can be detected in the bones for prolonged periods.\xa0Hence it can be found even in\xa0skeletonized remains\xa0of the body.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Ipecac is an emetic used in ergot and hydrocarbon poisoning.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Penicillamine\xa0is used in lead poisoning as a chelating agent.\\n\\nOption C: Activated charcoal acts as an adsorbing agent in many poisonings like aspirin, phenobarbital poisoning, etc.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-18f74204c6","n":401,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which of the following are seen in patients with chronic arsenic poisoning?\\n\\n1. Raindrop pigmentation\\n2. Peripheral neuritis\\n3. Mees line\\n4. Luminous vomit","options":["1,2,3 are correct","2,3 are correct","1,4 are correct","Only 1 is correct"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1,2,3 are correct\\n\\nAmong the given options, raindrop pigmentation, peripheral neuritis, and\xa0mees line\xa0are seen in patients with chronic arsenic poisoning.\xa0Luminous vomit\xa0is a feature of\xa0yellow phosphorous poisoning.\\n\\nArsenic is an odorless, tasteless, metallic poison. Gastrointestinal features of acute arsenic poisoning include nausea and vomiting, colicky abdominal pain, and profuse diarrhea with rice water stools.\\n\\nIn chronic\xa0arsenic poisoning, skin symptoms include erythematous lesions, flushing, and can also lead to hyperkeratotic skin and desquamation. Pigmentation is patchy and resembles raindrops on a dusty road. In the distal parts of the body, hyperkeratosis is present.\xa0 Mees lines are transverse white striae of the fingernails, called striate leukonychia. Renal manifestations include chronic nephritis and urine contains albumin, casts, etc.\\n\\nArsenic can be detected in the bones for prolonged periods. Hence it can be found even in\xa0skeletonized remains of the body.\xa0Dimercaprol\xa0is the treatment of choice for acute arsenic poisoning.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8dcea879a3","n":402,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which of the following is not a feature of cocaine intoxication?","options":["Tingling and numbness of hands and feet","Constricted pupils and bradycardia","Hypertension","Hyperthermia"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Constricted pupils and bradycardia\\n\\nConstricted pupils and bradycardia are not a feature of cocaine intoxication. Dilated pupils\xa0and rapid pulse are seen in cocaine intoxication.\\n\\nCocaine is obtained from the leaves of Erythroxylum coca. Cocaine can be administered\xa0by\xa0snorting,\xa0intravenous route,\xa0or\xa0smoking.\\n\\nThe manifestations of cocaine intoxication occur in two stages- the\xa0stage of excitement and the stage of depression.\\n\\nIn the stage of excitement, the features seen are dry mouth, dysphagia, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypertension,\xa0dilated pupils, and\xa0increased temperature. There may be a feeling of well-being, and the patient may be excited, restless, and talkative. This progresses into a calm, dull state.\xa0Neurological manifestations include\xa0exaggerated reflexes, tremors or convulsions, hallucinations, tingling, and\xa0numbness in the hands and feet.\\n\\nThe stage of depression is characterized by feeble respiration, excessive sweating, collapse, convulsions, and death.\\n\\nThe fatal dose of cocaine\xa0is 1g when taken orally.\xa0The fatal period ranges from\xa0a few minutes to hours.\\n\\nChronic cocaine users may have the feeling of insects crawling on their skin or the presence of grains of sand under the skin. This is known as\xa0cocaine bugs or Magnan\'s symptom. Ulceration of the nasal septum may be seen in chronic abusers who sniff cocaine.\\n\\nTreatment includes gastric lavage with warm water containing potassium permanganate, charcoal, or tannic acid if taken\xa0orally. When it is through the nose or throat, a\xa0wash with water is done. In\xa0case of intravenous intoxication, a\xa0ligature is applied above the site of the injection.\\n\\nThe antidote for cocaine intoxication is inhalational amyl nitrite.\xa0Other measures include control of convulsions, airway and circulatory stabilization, administration of iv thiamine, and naloxone.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-11ba8b2729","n":403,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which of the following rules of law are associated with insanity?\\n\\n1. Durham\'s rule\\n2. Curren\'s rule\\n3. Ashley\'s rule\\n4. Murphy\'s rule","options":["1, 2, 3 and 4","2, 3, 4 only","1, 2 only","3, 4 only"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 2 only\\n\\nDurham\'s rule and Curren\'s rule are laws associated with insanity.\\n\\nDurhams\'s rule states that an accused person is not criminally responsible if his unlawful act is the product of mental disease or mental defect.\\n\\nCurren’s rule states that an accused person is not criminally responsible if, at the time of committing the act, he did not have the capacity to regulate his conduct to the requirements of the law as a result of mental disease or defect.\\n\\nIn India, the law of Criminal responsibility is under BNS Section 22 (Section 84 IPC) It\xa0states that \\"Nothing is an offense which is done by a person who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or what he is doing is either wrong or contrary to the law”. This is slightly different from\xa0McNaghten\'s rule followed in Britain.\\n\\nM\'Naghten\'s rule\xa0states that every man is to be presumed to be sane, and possesses a sufficient degree of reason to be responsible for his crimes until the contrary is proved.\\n\\nOther rules to assess the criminal responsibility of insane persons:\\n\\n• The Irresistible Impulse\\n• Federal rule or American Law Institute test (ALT)\\n• The Brawner rule\\n\\nNote: Ashley\'s rule is used to differentiate males and females based on the combined length of the manubrium and sternum. It is considered to be a\xa0male sternum when the combined length is >149 mm, while if the combined length is\xa0<149 mm, then it is considered female.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4acbf27bf7","n":404,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which of these is not a cardiac poison?","options":["Aconite","Cerbera thevetia","Nicotiana tabacum","Atropa belladonna"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Atropa belladonna\\n\\nAtropa belladonna is a deliriant poison that acts by inhibiting the muscarinic effects of acetylcholine. All the parts of the plants are toxic. The active principle is 1-hyoscyamine.\\n\\nClinical manifestations of A. belladonna poisoning:\\n\\n• Fever, flushing, rash\\n• Neurological symptoms - delirium, slurred speech, headache, choreoathetosis, myoclonus,\xa0visual and auditory hallucinations, convulsions, and coma\\n• Eye symptoms -\xa0photophobia, blurred vision, dilated pupils poorly reactive to light\\n• Cardiovascular symptoms -\xa0arrhythmia, tachycardia,\xa0hypertension\\n• Gastrointestinal symptoms -\xa0severely dry mouth and throat,\xa0decreased bowel sounds, constipation, ileus\\n• Renal symptoms -\xa0urinary retention\\n\\nAnticholinergic symptoms\xa0include tachycardia, hypertension, flushing, dry mouth, mydriasis, and urinary retention.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Aconite\xa0is a cardiac poison derived mainly from the root and root tubers of the plant\xa0Aconitum napellus\xa0(shown below). Symptoms of intoxication are due to action on the voltage-gated sodium channels on the cell membrane of nerves and skeletal and smooth muscles. Symptoms include intestinal (nausea, vomiting, perioral tingling and numbness, abdominal pain, and diarrhea), cardiovascular (chest pain, palpitations, hypotension, bradycardia), and neurological (vertigo and restlessness) manifestations.\\n\\nOption B: Thevetin\xa0is a cardiac poison derived from the plant Cerbera thevetia (shown below). Symptoms of intoxication are characterized by vomiting, diarrhea, headache, dizziness, mydriasis, and loss of muscular power. Cardiovascular signs include a weak and irregular pulse rate with hypotension. Possible complications are heart block and cardiorespiratory failure.\\n\\nOption C: Nicotine is a plant alkaloid derived from the\xa0Nicotiana tabacum\xa0plant, shown in the image below. It is a\xa0cardiac poison.\\n\\nAll components of this plant are poisonous except its ripe seeds. It is a\xa0cardiac poison\xa0that initially stimulates the autonomic ganglia to produce symptoms of acute intoxication (tachycardia, hypotension, tachypnea, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and headache). These symptoms later subside to give way to respiratory depression and collapse.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-82a58ebb47","n":405,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"A 12-year-old girl is brought by a lady police sub-inspector with an alleged history of sexual violence 2 days back. She was accompanied by her mother. The registered medical practitioner should do which of the following as the next step?","options":["Take a detailed history","Preserve clothing","Take consent for genital examination","Documentation of injuries and examination findings"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Take a detailed history\\n\\nThe registered medical practitioner should take a detailed history as the next step.\\n\\nThe\xa0registered medical practitioner must take a detailed history and then take\xa0informed consent for examination. In case of refusal, an informed refusal is to be\xa0documented\xa0after explaining to them the consequences of choice. As part of the examination, they should collect specimens,\xa0preserve clothing,\xa0and document injuries and examination findings.\xa0Medical treatment and testing for pregnancy have to be done for all victims of sexual assault free of cost and police should be informed as per section\xa0357-C\xa0of CrPc.\\n\\nThe\xa0Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012\xa0considers all individuals (both male and female) below the age of 18 years as children and protects them from sexual abuse. It defines various forms of sexual offenses which include:\\n\\n• Sexual harassment: A person is said to commit sexual harassment upon a child when such person with sexual intent,\\n\\n• Carries out any act with sexual connotations\\n• makes a child exhibit his/her body\\n• shows any object to a child in any form/media for pornographic purposes.\\n\\n• Sexual assault\\n\\n• Any act with sexual intent that involves physical contact\xa0without penetration.\\n\\n• Aggravated sexual assault\\n\\n• Similar to\xa0sexual assault\xa0along with the criteria explained under the term \\"aggravated\\".\\n\\n• Penetrative sexual assault\\n\\n• An act with sexual intent that\xa0involves physical\xa0penetration.\\n\\n• Aggravated penetrative sexual assault\\n\\n• Similar to\xa0penetrative sexual assault\xa0along with the criteria explained under the term \\"aggravated\\".\\n\\n• Pornography\\n\\nThe term\xa0aggravated\xa0is used for those assaults done:\\n\\n• by a person in a position of trust or authority such as police officer, security forces, relative, doctor.\\n• in the course of communal violence.\\n• on mentally & physically disabled.\\n• gang sexual assault.\\n• using deadly weapons.\\n• causing grievous hurt\\n\\nThe Act prescribes stringent punishment graded as per the gravity of the offense, with a maximum term of rigorous imprisonment for life and fine.\xa0Section 21(1) of the POCSO Act, 2012 requires mandatory reporting of cases of child sexual abuse to law enforcement authorities and applies to everyone including parents, doctors, and school personnel.\xa0Failure\xa0to\xa0report\xa0a suspicion of child abuse is an\xa0offense\xa0under the Act.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-06fae5a26d","n":406,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"A 13-year-old female was brought by her mother to the emergency with abdominal pain for a few days. On clinical examination by the RMP, they found that she is 8 weeks pregnant. What should be your immediate next step?","options":["File an MLC and Inform Police","Obtain consent form her mother for genital examination.","Preserve the clothing","Plan/perform MTP"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. File an MLC and Inform Police\\n\\nThe registered medical practitioner should take register a medico-legal case and immediately inform the police in this case. All three other options are steps to be taken eventually in this case but registering an MLC takes priority.\\n\\nThe\xa0Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012\xa0considers all individuals (both male and female) below the age of 18 years as children and protects them from sexual abuse. It defines various forms of sexual offenses which include:\\n\\n• Sexual harassment: A person is said to commit sexual harassment upon a child when such person with sexual intent,\\n\\n• Carries out any act with sexual connotations\\n• makes a child exhibit his/her body\\n• shows any object to a child in any form/media for pornographic purposes.\\n\\n• Sexual assault\\n\\n• Any act with sexual intent that involves physical contact\xa0without penetration.\\n\\n• Aggravated sexual assault\\n\\n• Similar to\xa0sexual assault\xa0along with the criteria explained under the term \\"aggravated\\".\\n\\n• Penetrative sexual assault\\n\\n• An act with sexual intent that\xa0involves physical\xa0penetration.\\n\\n• Aggravated penetrative sexual assault\\n\\n• Similar to\xa0penetrative sexual assault\xa0along with the criteria explained under the term \\"aggravated\\".\\n\\n• Pornography\\n\\nThe term\xa0aggravated\xa0is used for those assaults done:\\n\\n• by a person in a position of trust or authority such as police officer, security forces, relative, doctor.\\n• in the course of communal violence.\\n• on mentally & physically disabled.\\n• gang sexual assault.\\n• using deadly weapons.\\n• causing grievous hurt\\n\\nThe Act prescribes stringent punishment graded as per the gravity of the offense, with a maximum term of rigorous imprisonment for life and fine.\xa0Section 21(1) of the POCSO Act, 2012 requires mandatory reporting of cases of child sexual abuse to law enforcement authorities and applies to everyone including parents, doctors, and school personnel.\xa0Failure\xa0to\xa0report\xa0a suspicion of child abuse is an\xa0offense\xa0under the Act.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8dcaa56ab4","n":407,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"A 25 year old man was brought to the casualty in a semiconscious state following a road traffic accident. Which type of injury is unlikely to be seen in this patient?","options":["Graze abrasions","Laceration Wound","Multiple Contusions","Chop Wound"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Chop Wound\\n\\nA chop wound would be unlikely to be found in this patient who has succumbed to a road traffic accident.\\n\\nInjuries that are common following a blunt impact (here road and traffic accident) include:\\n\\n• Bruises and Contusions\\n• Laceration\\n• Abrasion (patterned due to tyre marks)\\n\\nChop wounds or slash wounds are the wounds produced by chopping motion, made with heavy and sharp weapons like axes, cleave, etc. The dimensions of the wound correspond to the crosssection of the penetrating part of the weapon. The margins are sharp and show slight abrasion and bruising with the destruction of underlying organs. The presence of an incised wound on the skin with an underlying fracture or deep grove in the bone indicates a chop wound. The majority of these injuries are homicidal in nature and are typically inflicted upon the vulnerable regions of the body, such as the head, face, neck, shoulders, and extremities. Accidental injuries can be attributed to various sources such as power fans, band saws, or ship and aeroplane propellers. These incidents have the potential to cause significant lacerations to soft tissues or result in the amputation of body parts. Instances of self-inflicted lacerations with suicidal intent have seldom been observed.\\n\\nIndividuals who sustain injuries as a result of road accidents can be categorized into three categories: pedestrians, cyclists (both pedal-powered and motorized), and occupants of cars, including both drivers and passengers. Among these three categories, pedestrians are frequently the ones who sustain injuries, however, the percentage of pedestrian casualties exhibits significant variation depending on the traffic patterns observed in different nations.\\n\\nInjuries to pedestrians:\\n\\n• Primary impact injuries are caused by the first impact of the vehicle on the victim.\\n• Secondary impact injuries are further injuries caused by the vehicle.\\n• Tertiary injuries are caused when the victim strikes other objects or falls on the ground after being hit by the vehicle.\\n\\nNote: In case a pedestrian is hit by the vehicle only once (i.e only primary impact and no secondary impact by the vehicle) and then sustains injuries by falling on the road, it would be an example of secondary injury.\\n\\nInjuries to occupants:\\n\\nApproximately eighty per cent of vehicle impacts are against another vehicle or a stationary object and involve the front or front corners. Vehicle deceleration is rapid in this impact. Rarely, the car is hit from behind, causing ‘acceleration’. The least common impacts are side and \'roll-overs\'.\\nIn a typical unrestrained deceleration condition, front-seat occupants surge forward and hit car structures in front of them.\\n\\nAn unrestrained impact may cause the following:\\n\\n• The face and head following a collision with the windscreen glass, frame, or side pillars, cause skull and cervical spine fractures along with brain injury.\\n• Contact with the fascia or steering wheel can cause rib, sternal, heart, and liver damage.\\n• Hyperflexion may cause aorta tears at the descending part of the arch, where the vessel attaches to the vertebral column.\\n• The passenger\'s knees may hit the parcel shelf, causing fractures.\\n• The driver\'s legs, supported by stop and clutch pedals, may transfer impact force to the pelvis via the tibia and femur.\\n• The rebounding force from the contact may cause the head to violently swing backwards, damaging the cervical or thoracic spine.\\n\\nUnrestrained rear occupants are projected forwards and impact the back of the front seats, including headrests if equipped, during severe deceleration. They may be hurled over the seats, striking and injuring the front-seat occupants, or they may be expelled through the windscreen, which has been damaged by them or the persons in front.\\n\\nInjuries inflicted on motorcyclists following collision with other vehicles include patterned abrasions and various head, chest and spine injuries. A distinct injury can arise from the practice of \\"tail-gating\\" in which a motorcycle manoeuvres beneath the rear of a vehicle, resulting in significant cranial trauma or, in extreme cases, decapitation. The implementation of protective bars positioned at the sides and rear of trucks has effectively mitigated the occurrence of injuries resulting from bikes and vehicles passing beneath the vehicle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e4558c5081","n":408,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"A 5-year-old boy went to the temple in a village with his grandmother. He was crying inconsolably on the way back. He was taken to the emergency department within 3 hours. He had altered sensorium, cold clammy extremities, increased salivation, and excessive sweating. On examination, he was pale and had priapism with the following vitals - BP - 140/78 mmHg, HR - 152/min, RR - 36/min sPO2 of 96%. Which of the following would you use in his management?","options":["Anti-snake venom","Methylprednisolone","Prazosin","Adrenaline"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Prazosin\\n\\nThe clinical stem points to a likely diagnosis of a scorpion bite, for which\xa0Prazosin\xa0can be used in the management. It acts as a physiological antidote counteracting the symptoms of the adrenergic surge.\\n\\nThe venom is a potent\xa0autonomic stimulator\xa0that causes the release of massive amounts of\xa0catecholamines\xa0from the adrenals. It also has a direct effect on the myocardium.\\n\\nScorpion sting usually presents with\xa0localized redness\xa0and\xa0burning pain\xa0which is unable to localize due to its radiation along the dermatomes involved,\xa0localized swelling, and\xa0punctate hemorrhagic spots. Other features include headache, profuse perspiration, chest discomfort, paraesthesias, hypersalivation, cold extremities, and sometimes, priapism.\xa0Hypertension\xa0usually develops early, while\xa0pulmonary edema\xa0and\xa0myocarditis\xa0may develop later.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Paracetamol, NSAIDs, or opioids for pain.\\n• Benzodiazepines for sedation for agitation, oculomotor abnormalities, uncoordinated neuromuscular activity, and muscle spasms.\\n• Hypertension and pulmonary edema-\xa0Prazosin counteracts the adrenergic surge that can lead to these effects.\\n• Cardiogenic shock- Antivenom, prazosin, nitroglycerin, dobutamine.\\n• Cholinergic effects- Atropine.\\n• Supportive care, wound care.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1509ed5628","n":409,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"A female was brought for autopsy with an alleged history of being found at a lake in the village. All of the following are suggestive of antemortem drowning except:","options":["Water in stomach","Washerwoman\'s hands and feet","White leathery foam around mouth and nostrils","Positive diatom test"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Washerwoman\'s hands and feet\\n\\nWasherwoman\'s hands and feet\xa0are a non-specific sign of submersion. They occur due to the imbibition of water into the outer skin layers. They can be seen in antemortem as well as postmortem drowning victims.\\n\\nThe presence of water in the stomach, white leathery foam around the mouth and nostrils, and a positive diatom test are suggestive of\xa0antemortem drowning.\\n\\nGiven below are the features of antemortem drowning.\\n\\n• Froth\xa0- The presence of copious amounts of fine, shaving-lather-like froth at the mouth and the nostrils is the most characteristic finding of antemortem drowning. It is caused by the vigorous agitation of the tracheal and bronchial mucous secretions, edema fluid, and alveolar surfactant during respiratory efforts.\\n• Cutis anserina\xa0- The contraction of the erector pilorum muscles causes puckering and grainy appearance of the skin. This resembles goose skin;\xa0hence, it is called cutis anserina.\\n• Washerwoman’s hand\xa0- Maceration of the skin on the palms and soles gives a wrinkled, sodden, and bleached appearance due to submersion.\\n• Cadaveric spasm\xa0- Gravel, weed, soil, grass, etc. is firmly held in the clenched hands due to cadaveric spasm. This is a vital sign of antemortem drowning. The material also indicates the place of submersion.\\n• Rigor mortis - This appears early in cases of a violent antemortem struggle.\\n• Face - It is bloated and discolored, with cyanosis.\\n• Eyes - Conjunctiva are suffused and pupils dilated.\\n• Tongue - It may be swollen and protruded.\\n• Antemortem injuries - These may be sustained by striking objects, such as rocks, during the fall into the water body. Examination for such injuries must be performed only after the body is dry.\\n\\nInternal findings:\\n\\n• Lungs - They appear voluminous and distended and show ballooning. The cut section will exude copious amounts of frothy, bloodstained fluid.\\n• Larynx, trachea, and bronchioles - The presence of sand, silt, and aquatic vegetation in the trachea and lower respiratory tract are characteristic findings of antemortem drowning.\\n• Heart and blood vessels- Gettler test can be done to estimate the chloride content in the right and left sides of the heart to differentiate between freshwater and saltwater drowning.\\n• Diatom test- Diatoms are microscopic algae. Their presence in the organs, particularly in the bone marrow, is suggestive of antemortem drowning.\\n• Stomach and small intestines- These organs may contain water in some cases. Water present in the small intestine is positive evidence of death by drowning.\\n• Middle ear - The presence of water in the middle ear is highly suggestive of antemortem drowning.\\n• Brain - It is congested, and edematous, with loss of gray-white junction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-497c50779a","n":410,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"A young man is brought to the casualty with pinpoint pupils. Which of the following is the most likely substance he would have consumed?","options":["Datura","Opium","Alcohol","Cocaine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Opium\\n\\nPinpoint pupils can be observed in the overdose of opium.\\n\\nCauses of Miosis\\nCauses of Mydriasis\\n\\nCarbolic acid poisoning\\nDatura poisoning\\n\\nMorphine poisoning\\nCyanides\\n\\nOrganophosphorous poisoning\\nCocaine\\n\\nPontine haemorrhage\\nEther\\n\\nBarbiturate poisoning\\nPethidine\\n\\nNux vomica\\n\\nViper venom\\n\\nAmphetamine\\n\\nOpioid overdose symptoms:\\n\\n• Triad of coma, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils (miosis)\\n• Hypotension, bradycardia\\n• Shallow breathing\\n• Hypothermia\\n\\nNaloxone is also used for reversing neonatal asphyxia due to opioid usage in labour. Naloxone is a pure opioid antagonist which reverses all effects except sedation.\\n\\nNote: Naloxone is contraindicated in infants born to mothers with opiate addiction as it may precipitate acute neonatal withdrawal with seizures.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-46073d7437","n":411,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"All of the following can be used for the identification of a dead body, except?","options":["Blood","Nail","Muscle","Teeth"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Nail\\n\\nAmong the options, nail is the least preferred in the identification of a dead body.\\n\\nNails have been used in the identification of dead bodies, however, their use has been deemed uncommon. Skeletal muscles are resistant to degradation and are therefore more likely to persist during decomposition, thus making them useful for the identification of corpses. It is also important to note that few studies show the amount of identical loci decreases with time.\\n\\nSuitable samples for DNA analysis encompass several types of nucleated biological material, including but not limited to white blood cells, hair root bulb cells, and spermatozoa. In the past, a substantial quantity of samples was necessary for doing this analysis. However, the advent of the polymerase chain reaction (PCR), a technique capable of amplifying minute quantities of DNA, has enabled the identification of DNA from even the most limited samples.\\n\\nWhen obtaining samples, whether from a deceased individual, a living victim, or the accused, the size of the sample that can be collected is typically not a significant concern. The collection of samples from various locations of staining inside the scene, the deceased, the surviving victim, or the accused can provide distinct challenges. These areas may consist of minute traces of blood, semen staining, and similar substances, necessitating meticulous collection procedures to mitigate the risk of contamination.\\n\\nBlood and hair with intact follicles are utilized in both live and deceased individuals, while samples of the spleen can be obtained during post-mortem examinations. Additionally, buccal smears can be collected from living individuals. In situations where sexual assault is a potential occurrence, or in instances of suspicious deaths, it is advisable to collect vaginal, anal, and penile swabs. In the event of a potential delay in transporting samples to the laboratory, it is recommended that the samples be subjected to freezing conditions at a temperature of -20\xb0C or as specifically directed by the laboratory.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-251d60ef1d","n":412,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"An autopsy was performed on a person with a history of drowning; however, the report revealed that there was no water in his lungs, hence the case was labeled as dry drowning. What is the most likely cause of his death?","options":["Vagal inhibition","Laryngospasm","Pulmonary edema","Electrolyte imbalace"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Laryngospasm\\n\\nIn dry drowning death is due to sustained laryngeal spasm and subsequent airway closure. This occurs when there is sudden entry of water into the nasopharynx or larynx.\\n\\nType of drowning\\nCause of death\\n\\nFreshwater drowning\\n\\nSeawater drowning\\n\\nDisruption of surfactant\\n\\nDry drowning\\nLaryngeal spasm\\n\\nImmersion syndrome\\nVagal inhibition\\n\\nPost immersion Syndrome / Secondary drowning\\n\\nCerebral anoxia\\nPneumonia\\nPulmonary edema\\nChemical pneumonitis\\nMetabolic acidosis\\n\\nElectrolyte imbalance\\n\\nDry drowning:\\n\\nIn this type of drowning, water does not enter the lungs, but death results from immediate sustained laryngeal spasm due to an inrush of water into the nasopharyngeal or larynx. Thick mucus, foam and froth may develop, producing a plug.\\n\\nImmersion syndrome (Hydrocution/Submersion inhibition):\\n\\nDeath results from cardiac arrest due to vagal inhibition as a result of\\n\\n• Cold water stimulates the nerve endings of the surface of the body.\\n• Water striking the epigastrium.\\n• Cold water enters ear drums, nasal passages, pharynx and the larynx which cause stimulation of the nerve endings of the mucosa.\\n\\nSecondary drowning/Post immersion-syndrome/Near drowning:\\n\\nIt refers to a submersion victim who is resuscitated and survives for 24 hours. The patient may or may not be conscious. These patients may develop hypoxemia resulting in brain damage, electrolyte disturbances, pulmonary edema, sepsis, myocardial anoxia, etc., Death may occur from half to several hours after resuscitation in about 10-20% of people. Those who survive suffer from serious neurological damage.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-077f690ebe","n":413,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"An industrial worker sustained burn injuries to the anterior region of his chest and abdomen, as well as burns to his right upper limb and a circumferential burn to his right thigh, as a result of a cylinder explosion. What is the percentage of total burns surface area?","options":["27","36","45","53"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 36\\n\\nThe affected percentage of body surface area (BSA) is calculated using Wallace\'s rule of 9:\\n\\n• Right upper limb - 9%\\n• Circumferential right thigh - 9% (Anterior thigh 4.5% and Posterior thigh 4.5% in Adults)\\n• Anterior chest - 9%\\n• Anterior abdomen - 9%\\n\\nThus, totaling to 36%.\\n\\nNote: According to Bailey\'s Short Practice of Surgery, 28th edition, the anterior thigh accounts for 4%, while according to Current Practice of Surgery, 14th edition, the anterior thigh accounts for 4.75 %. Either percentage, when totalled gives us the best possible answer to this question to be 36%.\\n\\nThe following image shows Wallace\'s rule of 9:\\n\\nLund and Browder\'s chart is an accurate method to measure burns if it is used correctly. In this, different proportions of body surface areas in children are taken into consideration. This chart compensates for the changes in the shape of the body with age and can accurately assess the burn area in children.\\n\\nThe below image shows the Lund and Browder chart.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-01a639cf7d","n":414,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Cadaveric spasm is not seen in?","options":["Drowning","Traumatic asphyxia","Firearm suicidal wounds","Electrocution"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Traumatic asphyxia\\n\\nCadaveric spasm cannot be observed in traumatic asphyxia as the occurrence of traumatic asphyxia is sudden and hence does not give the patient or victim time to contract his muscles during the time of death.\\n\\nCadaveric spasm has been observed in the following situations:\\n\\n• Drowning death\\n• Brain Injury\\n• Fear or Excitement\\n• Electrocution\\n\\nPerte\'s syndrome, also known as traumatic asphyxia, is a clinical syndrome characterized by craniocervical cyanosis, subconjunctival haemorrhage, numerous petechiae, and accompanying neurological symptoms. This syndrome manifests due to the abrupt or intense compression of the thorax or upper abdomen, or both. Frequently observed injuries encompass pulmonary contusion, hemothorax, and pneumothorax. Effective management of the related injuries is crucial for optimal treatment outcomes. The expeditious initiation of treatment with a focus on restoring adequate oxygenation and perfusion has the potential to yield a favourable prognosis. The recoveries of the patients were shown to be correlated with the severity of their injuries as well as the presence of concomitant injuries.\\n\\nCadaveric spasm is an antemortem change that indicates the cause and manner of death. This phenomenon is alternatively referred to as instantaneous rigor or cataleptic rigidity\\n\\nThe victims do not undergo postmortem flaccidity (unlike rigor mortis), thereby directly entering a state of instantaneous rigor. This results in muscle stiffening which indicates the victim\'s attitude at the time of death. Cadaveric spasm is limited to select muscles i.e., the muscles that were in a state of contraction at the time of death. The mechanism of cadaveric spasm is thought to be due to neurogenic phenomena.\\n\\nPredisposing factors for this postmortem change include excitement, fear, fatigue, electrocution, nervous system injury, and convulsant poisons such as strychnine. It\xa0is a unique phenomenon that cannot be replicated by any other situation, and it is not possible to induce it through any means following the cessation of life. A significant amount of force is necessary to overcome stiffness. The phenomenon seamlessly transitions into typical rigor mortis and ceases to exist after rigor mortis subsides.The process of protein coagulation can be observed under microscopic analysis in burns, but not in cases of cadaveric spasm.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-51d7ea574d","n":415,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Identify the following image.","options":["Blister due to barbiturate poisoning","Blister due to antemortem burns","Edema due to chemical burn","Pemphigus vulgaris"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Blister due to antemortem burns\\n\\nThe given image of burns with blisters with surrounding hyperemia points to a diagnosis of\xa0antemortem burns.\\n\\nA zone of hyperemia (line of redness), which varies in width is present at the edge of the burnt area in antemortem burns.\xa0 It is due to edema of tissues and capillary dilatation which merges with the edge of the burn. It involves the whole thickness of the actual skin. It is permanent and persists even after death. If the entire body is burnt, a line of redness will be absent.\\n\\nThe antemortem blister appears as a raised dome. It contains serous fluid with proteins and chlorides. The base shows\xa0reddening with swollen papillae. In antemortem burns, the skin adjacent to the burnt area shows an increase in enzyme reaction.\xa0The reaction is increased for sulfhydryl groups in all layers. There is also an increase in ATP, esterases, aminopeptidases, and acid and alkaline phosphatase. Marked cellular exudation and reactive tissue changes are called vital reactions. This is characteristic of antemortem burns.\\n\\nOther options-\\n\\nOption A: Blister due to barbiturate poisoning, is commonly seen in frictional areas such as interdigital cleft, genital regions, etc. It is due to the dermo necrotic effect of barbiturates.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Chemical burns\xa0by acid or alkalis appear as ulcerated patches\xa0without blisters. The hair is\xa0not singed\xa0and the\xa0red line of demarcation\xa0is\xa0absent. Sometimes, the burn shows distinct coloration and is usually uniform in character. Strong acids produce dark leathery burns on the skin. Strong alkalis cause the skin to slough and leave moist, slimy, greyish areas.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Pemphigus vulgaris\xa0is an intraepidermal immunobullous disease. It\xa0occurs commonly between the\xa04th to 6th decades.\xa0They present with\xa0flaccid blisters.\xa0Mucosal involvement\xa0will be seen in the form of buccal and palatal erosions.\xa0Firm pressure over the lesion separates the epidermis from the dermis.\xa0Nikolsky\'s sign\xa0is\xa0positive.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/9c4e1aae89b04c091433.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-8d58bba9a4","n":416,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"In a Firearm injury, blackening surrounding the gunshot entry wound is due to?","options":["Flame effect","Unburnt powder","Smoke deposition","Muzzle of the firearm"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Smoke deposition\\n\\nBlackening\xa0occurs due to the deposits of smoke over the epidermis which can be removed with a wet cloth. This can be seen up to around 15 cm of firing.\\n\\nOther injuries related to firearms are:\\n\\n• Burn injuries - occur due to flame released during the firing. It is seen in contact shot wounds or distances up to 5-8 cm. Singeing of hair is caused due to burns.\\n• Tattooing - occurs due to the deposition of unburnt powder particles. These are difficult to remove. They are also called stippling or peppering. This is seen up to 50 cm of firing\\n• Greasing and collaring - occur due to the deposition of the lubricant of the bullet in the tissues, during its penetration. It can be found in contact, near-shot, or distant shot wounds.\\n\\nIn a firm contact shot, the imprint of the muzzle of the gun is seen on the skin around the wound. The victim is within the range of flame, hence singeing of the hair will be present. Tattooing begins only when the muzzle-to-target distance is >1cm, like in a loose contact shot.\\n\\nWhen the distance between the victim and the firing is 5-8cm, it is called a close shot. The victim is within the range of the flame, hence, features related to flame, smoke, and powder deposition will be seen. Singeing, burning, blackening and tattooing will be present.\\n\\nWhen the distance between the victim and the firing is >50 cm, it is a distant shot. The victim lies outside the range of flame and powder deposition. Hence, features related to flame, smoke, and powder deposition are absent. Burning, blackening, and tattooing are absent.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2678bddcbb","n":417,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"In an examination for drunkenness, a person smells of alcohol and his conjunctiva appears red. During the examination of motor coordination, it is noted that he can walk in a straight line and can talk normally. Which of the following statements is true?","options":["He has consumed alcohol","He has consumed alcohol and is under the influence of alcohol","He has consumed alcohol but is not under its influence","He has not consumed alcohol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. He has consumed alcohol but is not under its influence\\n\\nThe given scenario of a person who smells of alcohol but whose motor coordination is normal with no slurring of speech is suggestive that he has consumed alcohol but is not under its influence.\\n\\nSmell of alcohol\\nMotor coordination\\nInference\\n\\nPresent\\nNormal\\nConsumed alcohol, not under the influence\\n\\nPresent\\nAbnormal\\nConsumed alcohol,\xa0under the influence\\n\\nAbsent\\nNormal\\nNot consumed alcohol\\n\\nAbsent\\nAbnormal\\nCerebellar disorders\\n\\nThe effects of alcohol intoxication at various blood alcohol concentrations are listed below.\\n\\nBlood alcohol concentration (mg/dL)\\nEffects\\n\\n30-50\\nImpairment of driving and similar skills\\n\\n50-100\\nReduced inhibitions, slurred speech, laughter\\n\\n100-150\\nIncoordination,\xa0ataxia\\n\\n150-200\\nObvious drunkenness, nausea,\xa0staggering gait\\n\\n200-300\\nVomiting, stupor\\n\\n300-350\\nStupor, coma, risk of aspirating vomitus\\n\\n350-450 and above\\nDanger of death from respiratory paralysis","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8882a4f6ac","n":418,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"In death due to firearm injury, the entry wound shows only an abrasion collar. The range of the firearm is:","options":["Contact range","Close range","Near range","Distant range"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Distant range\\n\\nThe entry wound shows only an abrasion collar when the range of the firearm is distant.\\n\\nTo determine the range of firing, the following parameters are used:\\n\\n• The entry wound size and shape\\n• Blast effect – Muzzle end imprint and edge eversion\\n• Blackening, Tattooing, Singeing of hair, Abrasion collar, and Grease collar\\n• Hair should be preserved to determine the range of firing which can be examined for carbonization/desiccation\xa0in close range, also from lead deposition around the wound to assess the range of firing.\\n\\nCharacteristics\\nContact shot\\nClose Shot\\nNear Shot\\nDistant Shot\\n\\nRange\\nIn-touch with skin\\n2.5-7.5 cm\\n30-60 cm\\n>60 cm\\n\\nSize\\nLargest size\\nBullet size\\nSmaller\\nSmallest\\n\\nShape\\nStellate\\nCircular\\nCircular\\nCircular\\n\\nBlast effect\\n\\nMuzzle end imprint\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nEdge eversion\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nOther findings\\n\\nBlackening\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nTattooing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nSingeing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nAbrasion collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n\\nGrease collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n\\nAbrasion collar:\xa0Gyroscopic movement of the bullet, the skin\'s elasticity, and the grease and dirt particles on the bullet\'s surface are the major causative factors of abrasion and grease collar. They are seen on the skin around the wound of entry at all ranges. Depending on the angle of entry made by the bullet, i.e.\\n\\n• A perpendicular entry of the bullet develops the abrasion collar of rounded shape.\\n• An acute angular entry of the bullet develops the abrasion collar of an oblique or oval shape.\\n• Some contusion present in the abraded collar is known as contusion collar\\n\\nGrease collar:\xa0Mostly seen on the clothing and is seen as a black deposit, sharply outlined as if printed (better-seen on light color clothes and on fair-skinned individuals).\\n\\nMedicolegal importance:\xa0Helpful in deriving information such as wound of entry and direction of firing\\n\\nNote: A grease collar need not always be present.\xa0Hard metal jacketed bullets are associated\xa0less commonly with soiling. Often, the ring is not appreciable during gross inspection and may only be demonstrable on microscopic examination or other laboratory tests.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-24e2c8d1c7","n":419,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"In the 18th century, the British East India Company smuggled which of the following drugs to China?","options":["Cocaine","Magic mushroom","Opium","Marijuana"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Opium\\n\\nThe British East India Company smuggled opium into China in exchange for tea, silk and porcelain pots.\\n\\nOpium derived from the immature seedpods of the opium poppy (Papaver somniferum), was originally indigenous to the present-day territory of Turkey. The introduction of opium to China occurred during the late 6th or early 7th century CE, facilitated by Turkish and Arab traders.\\n\\nDuring this time, tobacco smoking moved from North America to China, resulting in widespread opium smoking. Opium addiction and imports increased in the first century of the Qing era (1644–1911/12). To address the issue, the Yongzheng emperor (1722–35) banned opium trafficking and consumption in 1729. Despite hurdles, the trade continued, and in 1796, the Jiaqing emperor banned opium imports and cultivation. Opium sales survived despite these laws.\\n\\nIn the early 18th century, the Portuguese made a lot of money importing opium from India and selling it in China. British traders became China\'s main suppliers in 1773. The British East India Company monopolized Bengali opium farming. The company developed an inexpensive and productive opium poppies cultivation method in this location. The US and other Western nations traded Turkish and Indian opium.\\n\\nThe opium trade was pursued by Britain and other European nations as a means to address their persistent trade deficit with China. In Europe, there existed a substantial and notable demand for Chinese commodities such as tea, silks, and porcelain pots. Conversely, in China, there was a very limited market for Europe\'s manufactured goods and other trade items. As a result, Europeans were required to utilize gold or silver as payment for Chinese goods. The opium trade, which resulted in a consistent demand for opium among Chinese individuals addicted to the substance, effectively addressed the persistent trade imbalance by facilitating the importation of opium from Western countries.\\n\\nOpium was not carried by the East India Company. Due to the Chinese restriction, the corporation outsourced it to “country traders”—private traders entitled to transport commodities from India to China. Smugglers on the Chinese shore bought opium from country traders. The traders gave the East India Company their sales of gold and silver. The corporation spent its gold and silver in China on profitable things to sell in England.\\n\\nOpium imports into China rose from 200 chests in 1729 to 1,000 in 1767 and 10,000 between 1820 and 1830. Although chest weight varied by origin, it averaged 140 pounds (63.5 kg). By 1838, China imported 40,000 chests annually. China lost the balance of payments to Britain for the first time. Opium distribution networks had evolved across China, frequently with corrupt officials\' help. High opium addiction levels affected imperial troops and officials.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-34f34340f6","n":420,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Match the following poisons to their site of action:\\n\\nA. Datura\\n1. CVS\\n\\nB. Nicotine\\n2. Skin\\n\\nC. Nux Vomica\\n3. CNS\\n\\nD. Nitric acid\\n4. Spinal Cord","options":["A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2","A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4","A-4, B-2,C-1, D-3","A-4, B-2,C-3, D-1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2\\n\\nPoison\\nSite of action\\n\\nA. Datura\\nCNS\\n\\nB. Nicotine\\nCardiac muscle\\n\\nC. Nux Vomica\\nSpinal Cord\\n\\nD. Nitric acid\\nSkin\\n\\nDatura poisoning\\n\\nThe common names include thorn apple, Jimson weed, and Devil\'s trumpet. The active ingredients include the anticholinergic alkaloids atropine, scopolamine, and hyoscyamine. The seeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked.\\n\\nToxic manifestations of Datura:\\n\\nMnemonic: \\"Hot as a hare, blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, and mad as a hatter\\"\\nAlso, remember the D\'s of DaturaDry hot skin (Temp rises up to 42 C, a scarlet rash may also be appreciated).\\n\\n• Dilatation of pupils along with loss of accommodation\\n• Diplopia\\n• Dryness of the mouth and throat, unquenchable thirst\\n• Dysphagia\\n• Dilatation of cutaneous blood vessels\\n• Dysarthria (Difficulty in talking)\\n• Drunken gait\\n• Delirium\\n• Drowsiness\\n• Death due to respiratory or cardiac failure\\n\\nMedico-legal importance:\\n\\nIn India, Datura is used as a stupefying poison before robbery, kidnapping, and rape. It is also known as a rail-road poison, as it is commonly encountered during a journey. Robbers disguised as saints may offer ‘prasad’ mixed with Datura seeds and rob the passengers.\\n\\nThe following are images of the Datura plant and seeds:\\n\\nNicotine is the principal constituent of tobacco responsible for its addictive behaviour. One of the newest agents introduced to aid in smoking cessation is varenicline.\\n\\nAdverse effects of nicotine intoxication:\\n\\n• Doses of 60 mg are toxic to the person and can cause respiratory paralysis.\\n• Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhoea\\n• Salivation\\n• Increased blood pressure, tachycardia, tremors and cardiac arrhythmias.\\n\\nOther cardiac poisons include Oleander and Digitalis.\\n\\nStrychnine poisoning occurs if a person ingests crushed seeds of Strychnos nux vomica. If swallowed uncrushed, the seeds of nux vomica have no poisonous action. The fatal dose is 15-30 mg of strychnine or one crushed seed.\\n\\nStrychnine is a competitive antagonist of glycine at the postganglionic receptors in the motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord. This leads to increased activity in the motor neurons. Patients typically present with risus sardonicus, convulsions that are clonic initially but eventually become tonic and are most marked in anti-gravity muscles so that the body typically arches in hyperextension, the posture known as opisthotonus. If the spasm involves the abdominal muscles, the body bends forward (emprosthotonos), or to the side (pleurosthotonus)\\n\\nNitric acid is a corrosive inorganic acid. Due to the xanthoproteic reaction, the nitric acid combines with aromatic amino acids like tyrosine to form picric acid which is responsible for the yellowish discolouration of the skin.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2326660077","n":421,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"The Blackfoot disease is an endemic peripheral vascular disease seen in toxicity of which of the following metal?","options":["Arsenic","Antimony","Barium","Cadmium"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Arsenic\\n\\nThe Blackfoot disease is due to chronic arsenic poisoning.\\n\\nIn China (Province of Taiwan) exposure to arsenic via drinking water has been shown to cause a severe disease of the blood vessels, which leads to gangrene, known as \'black foot disease\'.\xa0The Blackfoot disease is due to platelet activation & hypercoagulability of blood in peripheral arteries and is observed in endemic areas of arsenicism.\\n\\nClinicopathological findings in acute and chronic arsenic poisoning:\\n\\nSystem\\n\\nAcute\\n\\nChronic\\n\\nSkin\\n\\nDelayed hair loss (alopecia)\\n\\nMelanosis, Bowen’s disease facial edema, hyperkeratosis, cutaneous cancer, hyperpigmentation.\\n\\nNails\\n\\nnil\\n\\nMees’s lines\\n\\nNeurologic\\n\\nHyperpyrexia, convulsions, tremors, coma\\n\\nEncephalopathy, polyneuropathy, tremors, axonal degeneration\\n\\nGI tract\\n\\nAbdominal pain, dysphagia, vomiting, bloody or rice water diarrhea, mucosal erosions.\\n\\nNausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anorexia, weight loss\\n\\nLiver\\n\\nFatty infiltration\\n\\nHepatomegaly, jaundice, cirrhosis\\n\\nKidney\\n\\nTubular and glomerular changes – oliguria and uremia\\n\\nNephritic finding\\n\\nHaematologic\\n\\nthrombocytopenia, impaired folate metabolism\\n\\nBone marrow hypoplasia, anemia, leukopenia,\xa0basophilic stippling, and karyorrhexis.\\n\\nCardiovascular\\n\\nThe heart will show S-T wave abnormalities, prolonged QT interval, ventricular fibrillation, atypical ventricular tachycardia with humic acid in the Southwest coast of Taiwan\\n\\nBlackfoot disease due to platelet activation and Hypercoagulability of blood in peripheral arteries is observed in endemic areas of arsenicism.\\n\\nReproductive system\\n\\nMaternal ingestion of inorganic arsenic crosses the placental barrier and infant death shortly after birth.\\n\\n-\\n\\nOption B: The symptoms of\xa0antimony\xa0poisoning, both acute and chronic are\xa0similar to that of arsenic.\xa0Inhalation of fumes causes a flu-like illness called metal fume fever. In cases of\xa0chronic poisoning\xa0with heavy metals such as lead,\xa0arsenic, mercury,\xa0antimony, copper, etc.,\xa0the shaft of a long bone, strands of scalp hair, fingernails or toenails, and a wedge of the quadriceps muscle should be taken for sampling.\\n\\nOption C: Barium poisoning causes nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. It causes cramps and stiffness of the muscles, dilation of the pupil, paralysis of the tongue and larynx, and vertigo. There is increased blood pressure with slow and irregular pulse.\\n\\nOption D: Cadmium poisoning causes proteinuria and painful bone lesions known as Ouch-Ouch disease.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-007a0c6152","n":422,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which chelating agent is not used in heavy metal poisoning?","options":["British anti-Lewisite","Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid","Pralidoxime","Penicillamine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pralidoxime\\n\\nPralidoxime is used in organophosphate poisoning and not as a chelator in heavy metal poisoning.\\nIn organophosphate poisoning, oximes like pralidoxime (2-PAM) regenerates active acetylcholinesterase restoring neuromuscular transmission and relieves skeletal muscle end-plate block. It is usually used as an adjuvant to atropine which act as competitive inhibitors to counter the nicotinic and muscarinic effects of organophosphates.\\nPralidoxime should not be administered without concurrent atropine, in order to prevent worsening of symptoms due to transient oxime-induced acetylcholinesterase inhibition.\\nPralidoxime attaches to the anionic site of the cholinesterase enzyme, which remains unoccupied in the presence of organophosphates. It binds and forms oxime-phosphonate which diffuses away, leaving the reactivated cholinesterase.\xa0Pralidoxime is not an effective antidote to carbamates like carbaryl and may potentiate its toxicity, as the\xa0anionic\xa0site\xa0required for attachment of oximes is\xa0occupied.\\nOther options :\\nOption A : British anti-Lewisite (BAL) also known as Dimercaprol\xa0is used as an antidote for gold,\xa0arsenic\xa0(except arsine), or acute\xa0mercury\xa0poisoning (except non-alkyl mercury).\xa0It is also an adjunct to edetate calcium disodium in acute\xa0lead\xa0poisoning.\\nOption B :\xa0Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) is used for acute lead poisoning.\xa0It is\xa0not suitable\xa0for the management of\xa0chronic lead poisoning.\xa0The calcium salt of EDTA can be used in the management of heavy metal poisonings where the metal in question has a higher affinity for EDTA than calcium in vivo. Because of their\xa0lower affinity to EDTA, mercury and arsenic\xa0cannot be effectively chelated.\\n\\nOption D : Penicillamine\xa0is used as a chelator in the treatment of mild cases of lead poisoning.\xa0D-penicillamine\xa0(Cuprimine) can also be used to treat scleroderma,\xa0Wilson\'s disease, and rheumatoid arthritis. Side effects include nephrotoxicity, hematologic derangements, and a characteristic rash that involves the neck and axillae—elastosis perforans serpiginosa.\xa0It is associated with membranous glomerulonephritis, drug-induced lupus, and\xa0myasthenia gravis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1c116935e2","n":423,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following is not considered a grievous hurt?","options":["Unilateral permanent hearing loss","Emasculation","Fracture of limb","7 days admission in yellow zone of hospital"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 7 days admission in yellow zone of hospital\\n\\nAmong the options provided, a seven-day admission in the yellow zone of the hospital is not an example of grievous hurt, but any hurt which causes the sufferer to be in severe body pain for 15 days is considered to be an example of grievous hurt.\\n\\nGrievous hurt (Sec 116 BNS) is inflicted voluntarily on another person and comprises any of the eight kinds (clauses) enumerated below:\\n\\nClause 1 — Emasculation.\\nClause 2 — Permanent privation of the sight of either eye.\\nClause 3 — Permanent privation of the hearing of either ear.\\nClause 4 — Privation of any member or joint.\\nClause 5 — Destruction or permanent impairment of powers of any member or joint.\\nClause 6 — Permanent disfigurement of head or face.\\nClause 7 — Fracture or dislocation of bone or tooth.\\nClause 8 — Any hurt that endangers life or causes the sufferer to be in severe bodily pain or unable to follow his or her ordinary pursuits for 15 days.\\n\\nPunishment for grievous hurt :\\n\\nBNS Section 117(2) - previously, IPC 325: Punishment for voluntarily causing grievous hurt –imprisonment up to seven years and fine.\\nBNS Section 118(2) - previously, IPC 326: Punishment for voluntarily causing grievous hurt by dangerous weapons or means—life imprisonment or imprisonment of a minimum of one year, extendable up to ten years, and fine.\\n\\nRelated IPC\'s:\\n\\nBNS Section 115(1) - previously, IPC 321: Voluntarily causing hurt.\\nBNS Section 117(1) - previously, IPC 322: Voluntarily causing grievous hurt.\\nBNS Section 115(2) - previously, IPC 323: Punishment for voluntarily causing hurt: Imprisonment up to one year, or with a fine up to one thousand rupees or both.\\nBNS Section 118(1) - previously, IPC 324: Voluntarily causing hurt by dangerous weapons or means (3 years imprisonment) or with fine or both.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b6c7a5e9b1","n":424,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following is not true about postmortem changes?","options":["In drowning, postmortem hypostasis is seen only in the lower limbs","Greenish discolouration in right iliac fossa is the earliest sign of decomposition","Adipocere is seen in bodies recovered from water source","Cadaveric spasm is seen only in specific group of muscles"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. In drowning, postmortem hypostasis is seen only in the lower limbs\\n\\nIn the case of drowning, if the water is still, the postmortem hypostasis affects the head, chest and the limbs. This is because the chest and abdomen float higher because of the lungs and the abdominal gases.\xa0Drowning in fast-flowing water results in absent postmortem staining as the position of the body keeps on changing.\\n\\nPostmortem hypostasis/Postmortem staining/Postmortem lividity/Livor mortis/Suggilation\xa0refers to bluish-purple or reddish-purple discoloration\xa0of the superficial layers of the dermis.\xa0It is an\xa0early\xa0postmortem change that occurs due to\xa0the\xa0stagnation of blood\xa0in the\xa0vessels of the\xa0dependent parts\xa0due to the effect of gravity.\\n\\nSites of postmortem hypostasis depending on the position of the body:\\n\\nPosition of the body\\nSites of hypostasis\\n\\nSupine\\nBack except for shoulder blades, buttocks, and calves (contact pallor)\\n\\nVertical as in hanging\\nLegs, feet, forearm, and hands (glove and stocking hypostasis)\\n\\nDrowning\\nFace, the upper part of chest, hands, lower arms\\n\\nInverted like in drunken persons\\nHead and neck\\n\\nPostmortem changes are classified on the basis of time:\\n\\n• Immediate signs of death (at the time of somatic or clinical death):\\n\\n1. Insensibility and loss of voluntary power\\n2. Cessation of respiration\\n3. Cessation of circulation\\n\\n• Early signs of death. It is seen in cellular death:\\n\\n1. Pallor and loss of elasticity of the skin\\n2. Eye changes\\n3. Primary flaccidity of muscles\\n4. Algor mortis/Postmortem cooling\\n5. Postmortem lividity/Suggillation\\n6. Rigor mortis\\n\\n• Late signs of death:\\n\\n1. Putrefaction\\n2. Adipocere\\n3. Mummification\\n\\nOther options-\\n\\nOption B: The\xa0first external sign\xa0of putrefaction in a body lying outside in the air is a\xa0greenish discoloration\xa0of the\xa0skin\xa0over the region of the right iliac fossa.\xa0Sulphmethemoglobin\xa0is the agent imparting greenish color.\\n\\nOption C: Adipocere\xa0is a modification of putrefaction. The transformation is brought about by the\xa0gradual hydrolysis\xa0and\xa0hydrogenation\xa0of pre-existing fats, including olein, into higher fatty acids. It is characterized by the formation of a waxy, soft, moist, and whitish substance with a sweetish offensive or ammoniacal (early stages) odor. It is\xa0more commonly\xa0seen in bodies recovered from\xa0damp, warm environments\xa0or immersed in\xa0water.\\n\\nOption D: Cadaveric spasm\xa0- This is a\xa0vital sign\xa0of antemortem drowning.\xa0Cadaveric spasm is limited to\xa0select muscles\xa0i.e., the muscles that were in a state of contraction at the time of death. The mechanism of cadaveric spasm is thought to be due to neurogenic phenomena.\xa0Gravel, weed, soil, grass, etc. is firmly held in the\xa0clenched hands\xa0due to cadaveric spasm. The material also indicates the place of submersion.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-15391a1194","n":425,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"A middle-aged housewife consumed rodenticide powder and presented with K+ levels of 2.5meq. On examination, irregular pulse with muscle paralysis is present. ECG shows ventricular ectopics. What is the suspected poison?","options":["Aluminium phosphide","Zinc phosphide","Barium carbonate","Warafarin tablets"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Barium carbonate\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario with a presentation of hypokalemia, cardiac arrhythmia\xa0and paralysis after consumption of rodenticide poison is caused by Barium carbonate.\\n\\nBarium carbonate is used in rat poised, a local corrosive irritant that, after absorption, mainly acts on muscles including the heart muscles.\\n\\nThe initial symptoms manifest as intense gastrointestinal distress, including nausea, vomiting, and diarrhoea. Subsequently, muscle cramps and stiffness, pupil dilation, tongue and larynx paralysis, and vertigo occur. Early impacts on the heart lead to elevated blood pressure, a slow and irregular pulse, and potential cardiac arrest in systole. Among the most notable changes are a loss of reflexes and paralysis.\\n\\nTreatment involves the following steps:\\n\\n1. Gastric lavage to remove contents and administration of either sodium sulfate or magnesium sulfate solution to precipitate barium as insoluble sulfate.\\n2. Intravenous administration of 10 ml of 10% sodium sulfate every 15 minutes to convert the soluble salt into insoluble sulfate.\\n3. Purging with magnesium sulfate and repeated bowel lavage.\\n4. Symptomatic management.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Aluminium phosphide (ALP) is a solid fumigant pesticide, insecticide and rodenticide. ALP has a garlicky odour. Phosphine is a toxic agent. It acts by inhibiting the electron transport resulting from preferential inhibition of cytochrome oxidase.Severe toxicity produces ARDS, cardiac arrhythmias, congestive heart failure, pulmonary oedema, convulsions and coma. Cardiogenic shock is the most common cause of death, associated with higher mortality(35%- 100%) but does not cause hypokalaemia.\\n\\nOption B: Zinc phosphide poisoning can cause symptoms such as abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, and weakness. However, these symptoms are generally less severe compared to aluminium phosphide poisoning due to the highly toxic nature of phosphine gas released by aluminium phosphide.\\n\\nOption D: Warfarin is an anticoagulant used in rat poison but is not directly associated with hypokalemia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-41d7194677","n":426,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"A student consumed an unknown substance and came with shock and pulmonary edema. The silver nitrate breath test is positive. What is the antidote?","options":["BAL","Pralidoxime","MgSO4","Atropine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. MgSO4\\n\\nThe patient presents with clinical features suggestive of Aluminium phosphide (ALP) poisoning. Magnesium sulphate is given in ALP poisoning to correct hypomagnesemia, arrhythmias and reduce organ toxicity.\\n\\nAluminium phosphide (ALP) is a solid fumigant pesticide, insecticide and rodenticide. ALP has a garlicky odour. Phosphine is a toxic agent. It acts by inhibiting the electron transport resulting from preferential inhibition of cytochrome oxidase. Aluminium phosphite and Aluminium hypophosphite are non-toxic agents. Phosphine is a systemic poison and affects all organs of the body. The presence of HCl in the stomach accelerates this reaction.\\n\\nSigns and Symptoms include irritation of mucous membranes and acute respiratory distress in mild toxicity. Moderate toxicity produces ataxia, numbness, tremors, muscular incoordination and paralysis. Severe toxicity produces ARDS, cardiac arrhythmias, congestive heart failure, pulmonary oedema, convulsions and coma. Cardiogenic shock is the most common cause of death and is associated with higher mortality(35%- 100%)\\n\\nThere is no specific antidote. Gastric lavage should not be done with warm water as on coming in contact with water, aluminium phosphide releases phosphine and increases the concentration of toxic agent phosphine, so it is done with potassium permanganate and activated charcoal. Magnesium sulphate is used to correct hypomagnesemia, arrhythmias and reduces organ toxicity. Supportive measures to treat acidosis, shock and hemodialysis can be done.\\n\\nOn post-mortem examination, a garlic-like odour is present at the mouth, nostrils and in the gastric contents. Silver nitrate paper test is done to detect phosphine which turns the paper black.\\n\\nSilver nitrate paper test: This test is used to detect aluminium phosphide poisoning.\\n\\n• Aluminium phosphide (ALP)is used as a pesticide, insecticide, and rodenticide.\\n• When ALP comes in contact with moisture, it releases an active component called phosphine.\\n• When the silver nitrate-impregnated filter paper, comes in contact with phosphine the paper turns black in colour.\\n• The test is done in two methods:\\na. Gastric aspirate: 5 mL of gastric aspirate and 15 mL of water are put in a flask and the mouth of the flask is covered with silver nitrate-impregnated filter paper. The flask is heated at 50\xb0C for 15 to 20 minutes. If the filter paper turns black, it indicates the presence of phosphine.\\nb. Breath: The patient is asked to breathe in and out through a face mask which is impregnated with silver nitrate. If the face mask turns black, it indicates the presence of phosphine. The breath test is positive only if the patient ingests more than 6 grams of aluminium phosphide.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2424bea333","n":427,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Correctly matched antidote with toxicity","options":["Organophosphorus- Physostigmine","Atropine - L-carnitine","Benzodiazepene-Flumazenil","Arsenic-Desferrioxamine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Benzodiazepene-Flumazenil\\n\\nBenzodiazepine-Flumazenil is the correct match of toxicity with its antidote.\\n\\nFlumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine binding site of the GABA A receptor. It antagonizes both the agonist (benzodiazepines) and inverse-agonist (beta-carbolines) of this receptor.\\n\\nThe duration of clinical effects usually is only 30–60 min. It is absorbed rapidly after oral administration, less than a quarter of the drug reaches the systemic circulation. This is due to the extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism. Flumazenil is eliminated almost entirely by hepatic metabolism to inactive products with a t1/2 of about 1 hour.\\n\\nIt is primarily indicated for the management of benzodiazepine overdose and the reversal of benzodiazepine anaesthesia. Flumazenil is only available for intravenous administration. It reliably reverses the sedation, respiratory depression, and amnesia caused by benzodiazepines.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: In organophosphate poisoning (insecticide) with both muscarinic and nicotinic manifestations, antidotes administered are atropine and pralidoxime. Atropine is effective against muscarinic receptors. Pralidoxime (2-PAM) is a cholinesterase-reactivating agent. Hence, both drugs must be co-administered to treat both muscarinic and nicotinic symptoms.\\n\\nOption B: Treatment of atropine toxicity includes administration of a short-acting barbiturate or diazepam to control convulsions. Physostigmine is used as an antidote to atropine poisoning to treat delirium and coma. Other measures for symptomatic management are respiratory support and control of hyperthermia.\\n\\nOption D: In Arsenic toxicity, the antidote preferred is freshly prepared hydrated ferric oxide, which converts toxic arsenic to non-toxic ferric arsenite. DMSA (Succimer), British anti-Lewisite (BAL), and penicillamine can also be used.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-32ae539706","n":428,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Cytochrome C oxidase present in ETC can be blocked by all except?","options":["Carbon monoxide","Cyanide","Nitric Oxide","Hydrogen sulphide"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Nitric Oxide\\n\\nNitric Oxide cannot block cytochrome C oxidase present in ETC.\\n\\nNitric Oxide: While NO can modulate ETC activity and inhibit respiration temporarily, it does not universally block Complex IV like the other inhibitors. (Option C)\\n\\nCytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV) in the electron transport chain (ETC) is the terminal enzyme that facilitates the transfer of electrons to molecular oxygen, reducing it to water. Various inhibitors can block this process, leading to impaired oxidative phosphorylation and ATP synthesis:\\n\\nCarbon monoxide: CO binds strongly to the heme iron of cytochrome c oxidase in its reduced (ferrous, Fe\xb2⁺) state. This prevents oxygen from binding and accepting electrons, halting the production of water and the proton gradient. Poisoning with CO can lead to severe hypoxia at the cellular level, even in the presence of normal oxygen levels. (Option A)\\n\\nCyanide: Cyanide binds irreversibly to the ferric (Fe\xb3⁺) iron in the heme group of cytochrome c oxidase. It completely blocks electron transfer to oxygen, making it a potent inhibitor. Cyanide poisoning results in rapid cellular asphyxiation and is life-threatening. (Option B)\\n\\nHydrogen Sulfide: Inhibits Complex IV at high concentrations by binding to its heme groups, similar to cyanide. Its inhibition is dose-dependent, but it remains a strong inhibitor at high levels. (Option D)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-07478e922d","n":429,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Marshall\'s triad (punctate lacerations, bruises, and abrasions) is seen in which type of blast injury?","options":["Primary blast injury","Secondary blast injury","Tertiary blast injury","Quaternary blast injury"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Secondary blast injury\\n\\nMarshall\'s triad in a bomb explosion is seen due to secondary blast injuries.\\n\\nMarshall described the characteristic injuries associated with bomb explosions. It includes:\\n\\n• Abrasions\\n• Bruises/ contusions\\n• Puncture lacerations\\n\\nBlast injuries are classified as:\\n\\n• Primary injuries- effect of transmitted blast waves on gas-containing structures. External injuries are absent and are characterized by internal injuries. The middle ear, lungs, and gastrointestinal tract are the most susceptible to this.\\n• Secondary injuries- injuries caused by shrapnel and other airborne debris propelled by the explosion. These injuries may affect any part of the body and sometimes result in penetrating trauma. Most casualties are caused by secondary injuries.\\n• Tertiary injuries- are caused by blast wind that can throw victims against solid objects.\\n• Quaternary injuries- all other injuries caused by explosions are not included in the above three categories. eg, flash burns, crush injuries, respiratory effects due to toxic dust, gas, etc.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5b8dc27f66","n":430,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Match the following:\\n\\n1. Eonism\\na. Sexual gratification by having intercourse with animals\\n\\n2. Algolagnia\\nb. Sexual pleasure by wearing clothes of the opposite sex\\n\\n3. Frotteurism\\nc. Sexual gratification in inflicting pain\\n\\n4. Necrophilia\\nd. Sexual gratification by rubbing private parts against another person\\n\\ne. Sexual gratification by intercourse with dead bodies","options":["1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-e","1-a, 2-b 3-d, 4-c","1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-e","1-c, 2-b, 3-d, 4-e"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-e\\n\\nThe correct answer is option A: 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-e\\n\\n1. Eonism\\nb. Sexual pleasure by wearing clothes of the opposite sex\\n\\n2. Algolagnia\\nc. Sexual gratification in inflicting pain\\n\\n3. Frotteurism\\nd. Sexual gratification by rubbing private parts against another person\\n\\n4. Necrophilia\\ne. Sexual gratification by intercourse with dead bodies\\n\\nParaphilias or perversions are sexual stimuli or acts that are deviations from normal sexual behaviors but are necessary for some people to experience arousal and orgasm.\\n\\n1. Eonism (also known as transvestism) is a sexual perversion, where a person\'s whole personality is dominated by a desire to be identified as a member of the opposite sex. This is usually seen in males who obtain sexual pleasure by wearing female clothes. There is no hormonal/genital abnormality.\\n\\n2. Algolagnia is sexual pleasure derived from inflicting pain (active algolagnia) or enduring pain (passive algolagnia).\\n\\nActive algolagnia also called Sadism, is the sexual gratification obtained from acts of physical cruelty or infliction of pain upon one\'s partner. It is seen more commonly in men. To obtain sexual gratification the sadist may bite, beat, cut, burn, or otherwise mistreat or abuse his sexual partner.\\n\\n3. Frotteurism refers to the sexual gratification achieved by touching or rubbing against a non-consenting person. This behavior often occurs in busy and crowded places. It is predominantly described in men.\\n\\n4. Necrophilia is an obsession with obtaining sexual gratification from dead bodies.\\n\\nBestiality is an act of using a lower animal for sexual gratification. It is an unnatural sexual offence and is punishable in India.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b8badaf7ea","n":431,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"The compound given in the image below can be used in the treatment of which of the following poisonings?","options":["Aluminium Phosphide","Mercury","Phophorus","Arsenic"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Phophorus\\n\\nThe compound given in the image is copper sulphate. It can be used in the treatment of phosphorus poisoning.\\n\\nThe initial stage of acute phosphorus poisoning through ingestion involves severe abdominal discomfort, vomiting, and diarrhoea. Excreta and breath have a garlicky odour. Luminescent faeces and vomit are characteristically seen (smoky stool syndrome). Direct contact with the skin can result in second and third-degree burns. Shock, hematemesis due to liver failure, and renal insufficiency are potential causes of death.\\n\\nRegular inhalation of phosphorus fumes, leading to chronic phosphorus poisoning, results in necrosis of the lower jaw around a decayed tooth. This condition is known as phossy jaw and is associated with the discharge of foul-smelling pus from multiple sinuses.\\n\\nGastric lavage with potassium permanganate oxidizes phosphorus into phosphoric acid and phosphates. Activated charcoal can be used to adsorb the poison.\\n\\nStomach wash with 0.2% copper sulphate solution or 0.2 g of copper sulphate every 5 minutes until vomiting occurs. It coats the particles of phosphorus with a film of copper phosphide, which is relatively harmless. As copper has caustic properties and can cause acute poisoning, adequate care should be taken. Copper sulphate was also used for topical application in phosphorus poisoning. It is not recommended now.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Aluminium Phosphide has no antidote. Magnesium sulphate is given in ALP poisoning to correct hypomagnesemia and arrhythmias and reduce organ toxicity.\\n\\nOption B and D: British anti-Lewisite (BAL), also known as Dimercaprol, is used as an antidote for Mercury poisoning (except non-alkyl mercury) and Arsenic poisoning.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/4d90c241990d87bedfbe.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-2cb3aef4c3","n":432,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"The following finding was found in an autopsy. Identity the weapon.","options":["Bullet","Hammer","Iron rod","Scissors"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Bullet\\n\\nThe given image shows a well-defined punched-out hole in the inner table of the skull. Hence, it could most probably be an entry wound produced by a bullet.\\n\\nEntry wound: The initial contact of the missile punches a clean hole through the outer table of the skull. Where the bullet emerges internally, the inner table is then unsupported and a cone-shaped plug is detached, forming a crater that is appreciably larger than the external hole.\\n\\nBevelling: inner table\\nPunched out hole: outer table\\n\\nExit wound: If the bullet traverses the cranium and penetrates the opposite side, then the same pattern occurs, but this time with the small hole on the inner table and the crater on the outside.\\n\\nBevelling: outer table\\nPunched out hole: inner table\\n\\nThe sequence of bullet injuries is given by Puppe’s rule.\\n\\nPuppe’s rule: It is possible to determine the sequence of injuries in skull fractures caused by blunt injuries to the head because the second fracture ends at the point where a break in cohesion is already present. The fracture lines created by every other shot extend only up to the fractured lines produced by the previous shot without continuing over them. Even though this rule is true for all blunt injuries to the skull, it is of particular importance in gunshot wounds.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption B: Hammer: When a hammer is used, the fracture is circular or in the shape of an arc of a circle. The diameter of the fracture is the same as the striking surface. There are generally no radiating linear fractures. A depressed fracture is caused by blows from a heavy weapon with a small striking surface. It is also called a signature fracture as the pattern of fracture resembles the weapon or object used.\\n\\nThe following is an image of a depressed fracture:\\n\\nOption C: Iron Rod: An irregular missile such as an iron rod would produce several irregular lacerated wounds, and the exit wounds are larger than the entrance wounds.\\n\\nThe following is an image of the famous 3D model of Phineas Gage\'s skull, who survived a workplace injury of a tamping iron rod through his skull.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/4195e5ee4f46aa89f419.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-b4b79c5399","n":433,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"What is not seen in heat exhaustion?","options":["Loss of thermoregulatory mechanism","Altered sensorium","Orthostatic Hypotension","Core body temp below 105\xb0F"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Loss of thermoregulatory mechanism\\n\\nLoss of the thermoregulatory mechanism is not seen in heat exhaustion. It is seen in heat stroke.\\n\\nHeat exhaustion is caused by excessive exposure to heat, leading to nausea, vomiting, dizziness, altered sensorium(option B), thirst, fatigue, headache, sweating, and decreased urine output. Increases in sweating and dehydration can cause orthostatic hypotension(option C). Body temperature may rise but does not exceed 40.5\xb0C (105\xb0F). In heat exhaustion, the body maintains thermoregulation and no CNS dysfunction occurs. Prompt treatment is essential to prevent the progression of life-threatening heat stroke.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-937d919284","n":434,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"What is the correct order for opening cavities in case of a foetal autopsy?\\n\\n1. Head\\n\\n2. Thorax\\n\\n3. Abdomen","options":["1, 3, 2","3, 2, 1","2, 1, 3","1, 2, 3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1, 3, 2\\n\\nDuring the postmortem examination of a foetus, cranium is examined first intact along with the dura followed by the abdomen and thorax. The abdomen should be opened before the thorax to note the highest point of the diaphragm. Hence the answer is HEAD> ABDOMEN>THORAX.\\n\\nDuring the removal of body cavities, preference is given depending upon the findings. Cavities that give trace evidence are opened first as compared to cavities that give maximum evidence to reduce the various artefacts.\\n\\nBody cavities to be opened first:\\n\\n1. New Born: Abdomen> thorax\\n2. Poisoning: Cranium\\n3. Asphyxial deaths: Cranium > Thorax > Abdomen > Neck\\n\\nThe protocol for fetal autopsies differs slightly from that of adult autopsies. It includes examining the umbilicus, umbilical cord, placenta, scalp hairs, lanugo hairs, nails and their length, skin color and texture, scrotal sac wrinkling, as well as specific fetal measurements of the head, chest, and abdominal circumference.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-914ff52539","n":435,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"What is the Declaration of Oslo related to?","options":["Human Torture","Therapeutic abortion","Human experimentation","Hunger strikes"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Therapeutic abortion\\n\\nIn 1970, the World Medical Association adopted a resolution on therapeutic abortion known as the Declaration of Oslo.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Declaration of Tokyo: related to human torture and other degradation\\n\\nOption C: Declaration of Helsinki: concerned with human experimentation.\\n\\nOptions D: Declaration of Malta: related to hunger strikes.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0a1d68d188","n":436,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of the following antidotes are used to treat cyanide toxicity?\\n\\n1. Amyl nitrite\\n2. Sodium nitrite\\n3. Sodium thiosulfate\\n4. Glyceryl trinitrite","options":["1 and 2","1, 2 and 3","1, 3 and 4","3 and 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 2 and 3\\n\\nAmyl nitrite, sodium nitrite and sodium thiosulfate are antidotes used to treat cyanide toxicity.\\n\\nCyanide acts by inhibiting the activity of enzyme systems like cytochrome oxidase and carbonic anhydrase. It inhibits the final step of oxidative phosphorylation, preventing the synthesis of ATP and its usage as an energy source. Cyanide reduces the blood\'s oxygen-carrying capacity and combines with the ferric iron atom of intracellular cytochrome oxidase, inhibiting oxygen intake for cellular respiration.\\n\\nTreatment primarily aims at reversing the cyanide-cytochrome combination by providing nitrites which convert haemoglobin to methaemoglobin.\\n\\nCyanides combine with methaemoglobin to produce non-toxic cyanmethaemoglobin, which, in the presence of rhodanase and sulphate donors such as thiosulphate, transforms cyanide to thiocyanate, which is eliminated in the urine.\\n\\nThe Cyanide kit contains:\\n\\n• 21 amyl nitrite pearls\\n• Two 10 ml vials of 3% sodium nitrite\\n• Two 50 ml vials of sodium thiosulphate.\\n\\nUsing a handkerchief, a 0.2 ml ampoule of amyl nitrite is broken and held over the patient\'s nose for 15 to 30 seconds of every minute, until sodium nitrite infusion is started.\\n\\nIV administration of 0.3 g of sodium nitrite in 10 ml of sterile water is given slowly over a 5-minute interval. Sodium nitrite forms methaemoglobin (Hb-Fe2+), then competes with cytochrome oxidase for the cyanide ion, thus protecting cytochrome oxidase. Folllowing this, 25 g of sodium thiosulphate in 50% solution is given i.v. over a period of 10 minutes. It converts cyanide to non-toxic thiocyanate, which is excreted in urine.\\n\\nOther antidotes such as Hydroxocobalamin (Vit B12) and Dicobalt EDTA can also be used.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1d7be98502","n":437,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"\\"Crocodile skin\\" appearance is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":["Chronic arsenic poisoning","Mummification","Adipocere","High-voltage electric shock"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. High-voltage electric shock\\n\\nCrocodile skin appearance” is a characteristic finding in high-voltage electrical injuries.\\n\\n• Skin shows dry, leathery, cracked, and scaly appearance\\n• Resembles crocodile hide due to coagulative necrosis--> Caused by intense heat generation (Joule effect) from high-voltage current.\\n\\nPathophysiology:\\n\\n• Electrical current → deep tissue damage + surface charring--> Leads to protein denaturation and dehydration of skin\\n• Resulting in thickened, fissured skin pattern\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• Chronic arsenic poisoning: Hyperkeratosis, raindrop pigmentation\\n\\n• Mummification: Dry, shriveled body but not scaly fissured pattern\\n\\n• Adipocere: Waxy, greasy transformation of fat.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-310b88bb4e","n":438,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A 32-year-old farmer was brought to the emergency after he suddenly became unconscious while working in the field. On examination, his temperature was 105 degrees Fahrenheit and his skin turgor was decreased. Which of the following would not be seen in the patient?","options":["Tachypnea","Hypotension","Sweating","Red hot skin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sweating\\n\\n| Finding | Interpretation\\n\\n| ------- | --------------\\n\\n| Temp = 105\xb0F | Severe hyperthermia\\n\\n| Unconscious | CNS dysfunction\\n\\n| Decreased skin turgor | Dehydration\\n\\nThis presentation is heat stroke, characterized by hyperthermia (>40\xb0C), dehydration, and altered sensorium. Types of Heat Stroke\\n\\n| Type | Example\\n\\n| ---- | -------\\n\\n| Exertional | Athletes, farmers\\n\\n| Non-exertional | Elderly\\n\\nHeat stroke shows hot, dry skin due to failure of thermoregulatory sweating.\\n\\nPatients develop tachypnea, hypotension, and red hot skin from peripheral vasodilation.\\n\\n| Feature | Heat Stroke | Heat Exhaustion\\n\\n| ------- | ----------- | ---------------\\n\\n| Temperature | >40\xb0C | <40\xb0C\\n\\n| CNS symptoms | ✅ Severe | Mild\\n\\n| Sweating | ❌ Absent | ✅ Present\\n\\n| Skin | Hot, dry | Sweaty\\n\\n| Severity | Emergency | Less severe\\n\\nSweat glands fail due to:\\n\\n• Hypothalamic dysfunction\\n• Severe dehydration","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f445ee4795","n":439,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A baby is brought to the hospital with multiple injuries of varying ages involving the chest and abdomen, along with bilateral knee fractures. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Osteogenesis imperfecta","Accidental fall","Scurvy","Battered baby syndrome"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Battered baby syndrome\\n\\nBattered Baby Syndrome (Non-accidental injury/child abuse) should be suspected in infants with multiple injuries of different ages and unusual fracture patterns.\\n\\n• Hallmark features:\\n\\no Injuries at different stages of healing\\n\\no Multiple fractures, especially in non-ambulatory child\\n\\no Injuries in protected areas (chest, abdomen, back)\\n\\n• Common findings:\\n\\no Rib fractures\\n\\no Long bone fractures\\n\\no Metaphyseal “corner” fractures\\n\\no Associated soft tissue injuries\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Osteogenesis imperfecta: Fragile bones but no patterned injuries or abuse signs\\n\\n• Accidental fall: Injuries are usually single and consistent with history\\n\\n• Scurvy: Bleeding, bone pain, but not multiple fractures of different ages","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a6756ce207","n":440,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A middle-aged man presents to the emergency department with a burn on his upper thigh extending to his leg. On examination, there is soddening of the skin. There is erythema, vesicles, and a line of demarcation. What type of burn is most likely?","options":["Moist heat burn","Dry heat burn","Chemical burn","Electrical burn"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Moist heat burn\\n\\nMoist heat burns (scalds) are caused by hot liquids or steam\\n\\nCharacterised by : sodden (water-soaked) skin, erythema, vesicle (blister) formation, and well-defined margins.\\n\\nMechanism: Heat transfer via liquid → greater penetration → superficial to partial-thickness burns\\n\\n• Skin appears soft, moist, and sodden (not dry or charred)\\n• Common sites: thighs, legs, trunk (due to accidental spillage)\\n\\nWhy this diagnosis:\\n\\n• Sodden skin is the most distinguishing feature\\n• Presence of vesicles and erythema indicates partial-thickness injury\\n• Clear line of demarcation seen in scald injuries","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-9e93d80afa","n":441,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A patient sustains a head injury and is initially unconscious, followed by a lucid interval, after which neurological deterioration occurs. Which type of skull fracture is most commonly associated with this condition?","options":["Linear fracture","Depressed fracture","Hinge fracture","Ring fracture"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Linear fracture\\n\\nA lucid interval after a head injury is the classic presentation of an epidural (extradural) hematoma (EDH), most commonly caused by rupture of the middle meningeal artery following a temporal bone linear skull fracture.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Depressed fracture – More commonly causes underlying cerebral contusion or laceration rather than a typical lucid interval.\\n• C. Hinge fracture – A fracture through the base of the skull, usually due to severe lateral compression of the skull; not classically associated with EDH.\\n• D. Ring fracture – Occurs around the foramen magnum, usually after falls from height or severe axial loading; not associated with a lucid interval.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7509e036bc","n":442,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A person sustains a stab injury, as shown in the image. The wound is longer than its width, deep with clean-cut margins, and has one acute angle and one blunt angle. Which of the following is the most likely weapon responsible for this injury?","options":["Single-edged knife","Screwdriver","Broken glass piece","Double-edged dagger"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Single-edged knife\\n\\nThe shape of a stab wound provides important clues about the type of weapon used. A single-edged knife typically produces a stab wound with:\\n\\n• One acute (sharp) angle – produced by the cutting edge\\n• One blunt or squared-off angle – produced by the blunt back of the blade\\n• Clean-cut margins\\n• Depth greater than the surface length of the wound\\n\\nThe wound described and shown in the image is characteristic of a single-edged knife injury.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Screwdriver: Produces a puncture wound, usually circular, slit-like, or cruciate depending on the tip, not a clean incised stab wound with characteristic angles.\\n• C. Broken glass piece: Produces irregular lacerated/incised wounds with uneven margins and variable wound shapes.\\n• D. Double-edged dagger: Produces a stab wound with both ends acute and symmetrical, as both edges are sharp.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-edbe544d37","n":443,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A right-handed individual is found with multiple cut injuries over the left wrist. Based on the image shown, the most likely manner of injury is:","options":["Suicidal","Homicidal","Accidental","Defensive"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Suicidal\\n\\nThe image shows multiple parallel superficial incised wounds (“hesitation” or “tentative” cuts) over the left wrist of a right-handed individual. These are classic features of a suicidal incised wound.\\n\\nThe characteristic findings include:\\n\\n• Multiple parallel superficial cuts (hesitation marks) ⭐\\n• Located on an accessible site (left wrist in a right-handed person)\\n• Presence of one deeper fatal cut among several superficial cuts\\n• Typical of self-inflicted injuries\\n\\nThese features strongly suggest a suicidal manner of injury.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Homicidal: Usually presents with deeper wounds, often on inaccessible sites, and lacks hesitation marks. Defensive injuries may also be present.\\n• C. Accidental: Incised wounds are usually isolated and consistent with the mechanism of injury, not multiple parallel cuts.\\n• D. Defensive: Found on the palms, fingers, forearms, or ulnar border of the hands while trying to ward off an attack, not as multiple hesitation cuts on the wrist.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-3c72000885","n":444,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A skull specimen with a previous history of neurosurgery is shown in the image. The circular defect seen in the skull is most likely due to:","options":["Gutter fracture caused by a bullet","Injury caused by a sharp-edged weapon","Previous burr hole surgery","Depressed skull fracture"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Previous burr hole surgery\\n\\nA burr hole is a circular opening drilled into the skull by a neurosurgeon to gain access to the intracranial cavity. It is commonly performed for:\\n\\n• Evacuation of epidural hematoma (EDH)\\n• Evacuation of subdural hematoma (SDH)\\n• Placement of external ventricular drains (EVD)\\n• Intracranial pressure (ICP) monitoring\\n• Ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt insertion\\n\\nThe image shows a perfectly circular, smooth-edged defect in the skull, and the history of previous neurosurgery strongly indicates a burr hole.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Gutter fracture caused by a bullet: Appears as a groove or tangential defect caused by a grazing bullet, not a smooth circular hole.\\n• B. Injury caused by a sharp-edged weapon: Produces cut/chop marks or linear defects, not a circular opening.\\n• D. Depressed skull fracture: Shows inward displacement of fractured bone fragments with irregular margins, unlike the smooth surgical defect.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-111bd0c6de","n":445,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"A young male had a road traffic accident and was brought to emergency with stable vitals. Which wound is not expected:","options":["Chop wound","Multiple bruises","Graze abrasion","Laceration"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Chop wound\\n\\nIn road traffic accidents (RTA), injuries are typically due to blunt force trauma, not sharp weapons.\\n\\nCommon RTA injuries :\\n\\n• Abrasions (especially graze/brush abrasions) → due to friction with road surface\\n• Contusions (bruises) → due to impact\\n• Lacerations → irregular tears from blunt force\\n\\nWhy chop wound is not expected:\\n\\n• Chop wounds are caused by heavy sharp weapons (e.g., axe).\\n• Characterised by: Clean-cut margins + underlying bone injury\\n• Not seen in accidental blunt trauma like RTA.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0aacf1ae00","n":446,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Burns involving the face and chest following an explosion represent which category of blast injury?","options":["Secondary","Primary","Quaternary","Tertiary"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Quaternary\\n\\nBlast injuries are classified into four categories based on the mechanism of injury. Burns sustained during an explosion result from heat, flames, or chemical exposure and are classified as quaternary blast injuries.\\n\\n• Quaternary blast injuries include injuries other than those caused directly by the blast wave or flying debris, such as:\\n\\n• Burns\\n• Inhalation injuries\\n• Crush injuries from structural collapse\\n• Toxic exposure\\n• Exacerbation of pre-existing medical conditions\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Secondary blast injury: Caused by flying fragments or shrapnel, leading to penetrating or blunt trauma.\\n• B. Primary blast injury: Caused by the blast overpressure wave, affecting air-filled organs such as the lungs, ears, and gastrointestinal tract.\\n• D. Tertiary blast injury: Occurs when the victim is thrown against a solid object by the blast wind, resulting in blunt trauma, fractures, or head injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5fc38b10ce","n":447,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Declaration of Oslo is for?","options":["Therapeutic abortion","Torture","Hunger","Ethical aspects of medical research"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Therapeutic abortion\\n\\n| Declaration | Focus Area\\n\\n| ----------- | ----------\\n\\n| Oslo | Ethical issues in therapeutic abortion\\n\\n| Tokyo | Ethics regarding torture and cruel treatment\\n\\n| Malta | Ethics in hunger strikes\\n\\n| Helsinki | Ethics in medical research\\n\\n| Declaration | Focus Area\\n\\n| ----------- | ----------\\n\\n| Tokyo, Istanbul, Hamburg | Torture\\n\\n| Hongkong | Elderly abuse\\n\\n| Venice | Terminal illness\\n\\n| Oslo | Abortion\\n\\n| Helsinki & Nuremberg code | Human experimentation\\n\\n| Ottawa | Child abuse\\n\\n| Sydney | Brain death\\n\\n| Geneva | Hippocratic oath\\n\\n| Malta | Hunger strike\\n\\n| Lisbon | Patients’ rights\\n\\n| Washington | Biological weapons\\n\\n| Hawaii | Mentally ill","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c57a7ad021","n":448,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Identify the type of wound from the image:","options":["Defense wounds","Antemortem wound","Postmortem wound","Hesitation cuts"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Defense wounds\\n\\nDefense wounds are injuries sustained when a victim attempts to protect themselves from an assault, typically seen on exposed body parts such as the hands and forearms.\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Location: palms, fingers, dorsum of hands, or forearms\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Consist of cuts, abrasions, or lacerations.\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0May involve grasping injuries --> This is when the victim tries to hold the weapon.\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Usually irregular and multiple\\n\\nThe presence of injuries on the fingers and palm indicates an attempt to protect oneself during an attack --> Typically defence wounds.\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Antemortem wound : is a general term and not a specific type based on location or pattern.\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Postmortem wounds: lack vital reactions such as bleeding and inflammation.\\n\\n\xb7\xa0\xa0Hesitation cuts are superficial, parallel, tentative cuts typically seen near suicidal sites like wrists or neck.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-42a0bb1c8f","n":449,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"In a case of a firearm entry wound with an abrasion collar and tattooing, but no singeing or blackening, what is the likely distance between the victim and the firearm?","options":["Tight Contact","Intermediate range","Distant Range","Close range"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Intermediate range\\n\\nRrange of fire can be estimated from the characteristic features of a firearm entry wound. An abrasion collar with tattooing (powder stippling) but without singeing or blackening indicates an intermediate-range shot, where unburnt powder particles reach and embed in the skin, while flame and soot do not.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Tight contact: The muzzle is pressed against the skin, producing a muzzle imprint, burning, and soot/gases entering the wound; tattooing is usually absent.\\n• C. Distant range: Only an abrasion collar is seen; tattooing, blackening, and singeing are absent because powder and soot do not reach the target.\\n• D. Close range: Characterized by blackening (soot deposition) and often singeing of hair/clothes due to flame, in addition to tattooing.\\n\\n| Range of Fire | Characteristic Findings\\n\\n| ------------- | -----------------------\\n\\n| Tight contact | Muzzle imprint, burning, soot and gases in wound\\n\\n| Close range | Blackening + Singeing \xb1 Tattooing\\n\\n| Intermediate range | Tattooing present, no blackening or singeing\\n\\n| Distant range | Abrasion collar only\\n\\nExam Tip:\\n\\n• Tattooing (stippling) is caused by unburnt powder particles, is not washable, and is a reliable indicator of intermediate-range firing.\\n• Blackening is due to soot deposition and can be wiped off, indicating close-range firing.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-ca6b142fad","n":450,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Marshall’s triad of abrasion, bruise & punctate laceration is seen in which phase of bomb blast injury?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Quaternary"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Secondary\\n\\nMarshall’s triad (abrasion, bruise, punctate lacerations).\\n\\nCharacteristic of secondary blast injury --> caused by flying debris and shrapnel.\\n\\n• Secondary blast injury:\\n\\n• Due to projectiles (glass, metal, fragments)\\n• Produces multiple external injuries:\\n\\n• Abrasions\\n• Contusions (bruises)\\n• Punctate lacerations (small penetrating wounds)\\n\\nBlast injury classification:\\n\\n• Primary: Pressure wave → affects air-filled organs (lungs, ear, bowel)\\n• Secondary: Shrapnel → Marshall’s triad\\n• Tertiary: Body thrown → blunt trauma, fractures\\n• Quaternary: Burns, inhalational injuries, toxins","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-44a6277fbb","n":451,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Sparrow foot marks are characteristically seen in:","options":["Electrocution","Custodial torture","Motor vehicle accident","Lightning injury"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Motor vehicle accident\\n\\nSparrow foot marks are multiple, radiating superficial abrasions produced when the skin comes into contact with a rough surface during a motor vehicle accident (road traffic accident). They result from tangential frictional forces acting on the skin and are a characteristic pattern of graze (brush) abrasions.\\n\\n• In motor vehicle accidents, the body often slides or scrapes against the road surface, producing radiating, branching abrasions known as sparrow foot marks.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Electrocution: Characterized by Joule burns (electrical marks) at the point of contact.\\n• B. Custodial torture: Produces injuries such as tramline bruises, patterned contusions, and deep soft tissue injuries, not sparrow foot marks.\\n• D. Lightning injury: Classically causes Lichtenberg figures (ferning/arborescent skin markings), not sparrow foot marks.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-84927f87dd","n":452,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Sustaining a crush injury due to fall from a building during an explosion is classified under which type of blast injury?","options":["Secondary blast injury","Tertiary blast injury","Primary blast injury","Quaternary blast injury"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Tertiary blast injury\\n\\nTertiary blast injury occurs when the body is thrown by the blast wind and strikes a solid object, leading to blunt trauma and crush injuries.\\n\\n• Mechanism: Blast wave → displacement of body → impact with ground/building\\n\\n• Injuries include:\\n\\no Fractures\\n\\no Head injury\\n\\no Crush injuries\\n\\nBlast injury classification:\\n\\n• Primary: Pressure wave → ear, lung, bowel injury\\n\\n• Secondary: Flying debris → penetrating injuries\\n\\n• Tertiary: Body displacement → blunt/crush trauma\\n\\n• Quaternary: Burns, inhalational injuries, toxins","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9a98c5e8e0","n":453,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Sustaining a tympanic membrane rupture from a bomb blast is classified under which type of blast injury?","options":["Quaternary blast injury","Tertiary blast injury","Secondary blast injury","Primary blast injury"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Primary blast injury\\n\\nPrimary blast injury is caused by the direct effect of the blast pressure wave, primarily affecting air-filled organs, especially the tympanic membrane.\\n\\n• The tympanic membrane is the most sensitive organ to blast waves\\n\\n• Even low-intensity blasts can cause TM rupture\\n\\n• Other organs affected:\\n\\no Lungs (blast lung)\\n\\no Gastrointestinal tract\\n\\nBlast injury classification:\\n\\n• Primary: Pressure wave → TM rupture, lung injury\\n\\n• Secondary: Flying debris → penetrating injuries\\n\\n• Tertiary: Body thrown → blunt trauma\\n\\n• Quaternary: Burns, inhalational injuries","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-dfdced763d","n":454,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"The characteristic triad of bruise, abrasion, and laceration is most commonly associated with which type of injury?","options":["RTA","Bomb blast injury","Strangulation","Firearm injury"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bomb blast injury\\n\\nThe combination of bruise, abrasion, and laceration --> Characteristic Marshall triad of blast (bomb) injuries.\\n\\n• Primary blast injury: Pressure wave → internal organ damage\\n• Secondary blast injury: Flying debris/shrapnel → lacerations and abrasions\\n• Tertiary blast injury: Body thrown → blunt trauma causing bruises\\n\\nWhy this triad occurs:\\n\\n• Simultaneous exposure to blunt force + penetrating fragments + pressure effects\\n• Leads to mixed injury pattern (bruise + abrasion + laceration)\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• RTA: Mainly blunt trauma, not typical triad\\n• Strangulation: Neck findings (ligature marks, petechiae)\\n• Firearm injury: Entry/exit wounds, not mixed triad","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e824842442","n":455,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"The image shows a skull fracture. The most likely causative weapon is:","options":["Bullet","Heavy blunt object","Sharp-edged weapon","Pointed weapon"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Heavy blunt object\\n\\nDepressed skull fractures occur when a heavy blunt object with a small striking surface (e.g., hammer, stone, iron rod) strikes the skull. The fractured bone is driven inward, often producing a round or circular depressed defect that may resemble the shape of the weapon.\\n\\n• The image shows a localized circular depressed fracture.\\n• Such injuries are classically produced by heavy blunt weapons with a limited impact surface.\\n• Examples include:\\n\\n• Hammer\\n• Stone\\n• Iron rod\\n• Crowbar\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Bullet: A firearm injury typically shows internal or external beveling, often with radiating fractures and other ballistic features. Beveling cannot be reliably appreciated from this image alone.\\n• C. Sharp-edged weapon: Produces cut or chop fractures, which are usually linear or V-shaped rather than circular.\\n• D. Pointed weapon: Produces puncture or stab defects, not a depressed skull fracture.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-7fd7334805","n":456,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Injuries","text":"Which of the following statements are typical for classic heat stroke? I. Older patient II. Normokalaemia III. Hyponatraemia IV. Marked lactic acidosis Select the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["I and III","I and II","III and IV","II and IV"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. I and II\\n\\nClassic (non-exertional) heat stroke is a life-threatening medical emergency characterized by:\\n\\n• Core body temperature >40\xb0C\\n• Central nervous system dysfunction (confusion, delirium, seizures, coma)\\n\\nIt typically affects elderly individuals during heat waves due to impaired thermoregulation and reduced ability to dissipate heat.\\n\\n• I. Older patient ✔\\n\\n• Classic heat stroke predominantly affects elderly individuals, infants, and patients with chronic illnesses during prolonged environmental heat exposure.\\n\\n• II. Normokalemia ✔\\n\\n• Serum potassium is usually normal in classic heat stroke unless complications such as rhabdomyolysis or renal failure develop.\\n\\n• III. Hyponatremia ✘\\n\\n• Hyponatremia is not a typical feature of classic heat stroke. It is more commonly seen in exercise-associated hyponatremia due to excessive water intake.\\n\\n• IV. Marked lactic acidosis ✘\\n\\n• Marked lactic acidosis is more characteristic of exertional heat stroke because of intense muscle activity and tissue hypoxia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9e1346d0a0","n":457,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A 13-year-old girl is brought to the hospital by her mother. She complaints of pain in the abdomen. On examination her UPT is positive. What will be first step done by you as a medical officer :","options":["Inform police","Start examining","Preserve clothing","Take consent from mother"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inform police\\n\\nIn a minor (<18 years) with pregnancy, it is treated as a case of statutory sexual offence under the POCSO Act, and mandatory reporting to police is the first step.\\n\\n• Any sexual activity with a minor is legally considered sexual assault, irrespective of consent\\n• As per law, the doctor is obligated to inform police (mandatory reporting)\\n• Failure to report is punishable\\n\\nSubsequent steps:\\n\\n• Provide necessary medical care\\n• Obtain consent from guardian for examination\\n• Collect and preserve evidence\\n• Maintain confidentiality and sensitivity\\n\\nWhy not other options first:\\n\\n• Examination / evidence collection: Done after medico-legal formalities\\n• Consent: Important, but legal reporting takes priority in minors","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-997fe1ed2c","n":458,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A 16-year-old girl and a 25-year-old man report that they had consensual sexual intercourse. The girl’s parents filed a police complaint, although there is no evidence of force or injury. Which of the following statements is correct?","options":["The girl\'s consent is valid because the relationship is consensual and both parties claim to love each other.","The girl\'s consent is not legally valid because she is only 16 years old.","The parents must first prove that the sexual act was forcible before any legal action can proceed.","The man cannot be prosecuted unless the girl herself files the complaint."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. The girl\'s consent is not legally valid because she is only 16 years old.\\n\\nUnder Indian law, any sexual activity with a person below 18 years of age is considered statutory rape, irrespective of consent.\\n\\n• Age of consent in India = 18 years\\n• A person <18 years cannot give legally valid consent for sexual intercourse\\n• Such cases are governed by the POCSO Act (Protection of Children from Sexual Offences)\\n\\nLegal implications:\\n\\n• Even if the minor states the act was consensual, it is legally invalid\\n• The adult partner is liable for prosecution\\n• Complaint can be initiated by parents or authorities; victim’s complaint is not mandatory\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• A: Consent is invalid if <18 years\\n• C: Force is not required in minors (statutory offence)\\n• D: Case can proceed without victim filing complaint","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bfa0a11b88","n":459,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A 20-year-old sexual assault victim was examined in court \\"in camera.\\" What does it imply?","options":["Open proceeding","Closed proceeding","Recorded in camera","Proceedings in another place"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Closed proceeding\\n\\nClosed proceedings (in camera trial) = A court hearing conducted privately, where public and media are excluded.\\n\\n• Commonly used in sexual offence cases\\n• Only essential persons are allowed:\\n\\n• Judge\\n• Lawyers\\n• Accused\\n• Court staff\\n\\n• Ensures confidentiality and prevents secondary victimization\\n\\nLegal significance:\\n\\n• Mandated under Indian law for rape/sexual assault cases\\n• Victim’s identity is kept confidential\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Open proceeding: Opposite of in camera\\n• Recorded in camera: Refers to video recording, not courtroom privacy\\n• Another place: Not related to meaning","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-494a2d8528","n":460,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A 9-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department after being sexually assaulted by a neighbor. On evaluation, the police confirm that the accused was entrusted with the child\'s care while her parents were away. Under the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, this incident is classified as:","options":["Sexual assault","Aggravated sexual assault","Sexual harassment","Penetrative sexual assault"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Aggravated sexual assault\\n\\nAggravated sexual assault under the POCSO Act, 2012 refers to sexual assault committed under specific aggravating circumstances, such as when the offender:\\n\\n• Is a person in a position of trust or authority (e.g., relative, teacher, guardian, caregiver).\\n• Is a police officer, member of the armed forces, or public servant.\\n• Is a staff member of a hospital, educational institution, or child care institution.\\n• Causes grievous hurt, disability, or life-threatening injury to the child.\\n• Commits the offence during communal violence, natural disasters, or similar situations of vulnerability.\\n\\nIn this case, the accused was entrusted with the child\'s care, making it an aggravated sexual assault.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f65c1f3585","n":461,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A doctor receives two separate court summons on the same day: one to appear as a witness in a murder trial, and another to provide an expert opinion in a case of medical negligence involving wrong leg amputation. According to legal priority, which case should the doctor attend first?","options":["Summon received first","Appearance for murder case","Appearance for providing opinion on negligence","None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Appearance for murder case\\n\\nWhen a doctor receives multiple court summons on the same day, priority is given to criminal cases involving serious offences (e.g., murder) over civil or expert opinion cases.\\n\\n• Hierarchy of legal priority:\\n\\n• Criminal cases (especially grave offences like murder) take precedence\\n• Over civil cases or expert testimony (e.g., medical negligence)\\n\\n• Reason:\\n\\n• Criminal cases involve state prosecution and serious offences against society\\n\\nDoctor’s responsibility:\\n\\n• Attend the higher-priority court (murder case)\\n• Inform the other court in advance and request adjournment","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7edb170692","n":462,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A G5L4 woman has 4 daughters. She now comes for USG examination of her fetus and asks for sex determination. What will you do?","options":["Do USG- check for congenital abnormality, gestational age and sex. But don’t tell the sex to the mother.","Do USG- check for congenital abnormality, gestational age and sex. Tell the sex to the mother.","Do USG- check for congenital abnormality, and gestational age. Don’t check for sex.","Don’t Do the USG."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Do USG- check for congenital abnormality, and gestational age. Don’t check for sex.\\n\\nUnder the PCPNDT Act (Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Techniques Act, India), sex determination of the fetus is strictly prohibited, including not attempting to identify or disclose fetal sex.\\n\\n• Ultrasound (USG) is allowed only for:\\n\\n• Fetal well-being\\n• Gestational age\\n• Detection of congenital anomalies\\n\\n• Sex determination or even attempting to look for fetal sex is illegal\\n\\nDoctor’s responsibility:\\n\\n• Perform USG for valid medical indications only\\n• Refuse request for sex determination\\n• Maintain proper documentation (Form F)\\n• Counsel the patient regarding legal prohibition\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• A: Even checking sex is not allowed\\n• B: Checking + disclosing sex is illegal\\n• D: USG should not be denied if medically indicated","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-d710d92fad","n":463,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A man assaults his colleague and, when apprehended, states that he heard his boss’s voice instructing him that the colleague intended to kill him. He claims that he was not mentally stable and did not understand the nature or consequences of his actions. What is the next appropriate step in the management of this case?","options":["Refer him to a psychiatrist for comprehensive evaluation of mental state","Do not proceed with the trial as he is not legally responsible for his actions","Punish him immediately as the act of violence is proven","Proceed with routine trial without psychiatric referral"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Refer him to a psychiatrist for comprehensive evaluation of mental state\\n\\nIn an accused person showing signs of mental illness (e.g., hallucinations, delusions), the first step is psychiatric evaluation to assess mental state and fitness for trial.\\n\\n• The accused describes auditory hallucinations and paranoid delusions → suggestive of psychosis (e.g., schizophrenia)\\n•\\n\\nTwo important legal assessments:\\n\\n• Fitness to stand trial: Can he understand proceedings and defend himself?\\n• Criminal responsibility (insanity defense): Mental state at the time of the act\\n\\n•\\n\\nDetermines whether the accused is:\\n\\n• Fit or unfit for trial\\n• Eligible for insanity defense (McNaughten rule)\\n\\n• Guides further legal proceedings\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• B: Cannot declare not responsible without evaluation\\n• C: Immediate punishment ignores mental health assessment\\n• D: Trial should not proceed without assessing fitness","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c4b164f95e","n":464,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A man was abducted from his home and was presumed to be dead after long time of search. His son subsequently claimed the rights to his property. According to section 111 of BSA, till how much time the son is not entitled to claim the inheritance?","options":["10 years","7 years","9 years","5 years"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 7 years\\n\\nUnder the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), Section 111 (similar to earlier Evidence Act provisions), a person who has been missing and not heard of for 7 years is presumed dead.\\n\\n• If a person is not heard of for 7 years by those who would naturally have heard from him, the law presumes death after 7 years\\n• Until completion of 7 years, the person is legally considered alive\\n\\nImplication for inheritance:\\n\\n• Legal heirs cannot claim inheritance before 7 years\\n• After 7 years → presumption of death → property rights can be claimed\\n\\nImportant clarification: The law presumes death, but exact time of death is not presumed","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bb4eafdf38","n":465,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A person kills his business rival because of his belief that he would poison him. He was later diagnosed with Schizophrenia. According to the McNaughten rule, what would be the inference?","options":["Declare him not guilty as he was unaware of the implications of his actions","Declare him unfit for trial","Declare him guilty on the charges of murder","Declare him partially guilty"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Declare him not guilty as he was unaware of the implications of his actions\\n\\n•\\n\\nAccording to the McNaughten rule, a person is not criminally responsible if, at the time of the act, a mental illness prevented them from:\\n\\n• Understanding the nature of the act, or\\n• Knowing that the act was wrong or illegal\\n\\nApplication to this case\\n\\n• The accused has schizophrenia\\n• He acted under a delusion (belief that his rival would poison him)\\n• This indicates impaired judgment and loss of reality testing\\n\\nHence, he could not appreciate the wrongfulness of his act\\n\\nThe person should be declared not guilty by reason of insanity, as he was unaware of the implications of his actions at the time of the offense.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1541fe6a05","n":466,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"A victim of sexual assault with 22 weeks of gestation is brought for MTP. Which of these is a true statement?","options":["One Doctor is involved","MTP can be done only to reduce risk of mother","MTP can be carried up to 24 weeks","MTP not allowed after 20 weeks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. MTP can be carried up to 24 weeks\\n\\nMTP Act (2021 Amendment)\\n\\nIndications (Definitions)\\n\\n• Therapeutic → To save life of mother\\n• Eugenic → Fetal abnormalities\\n• Humanitarian → Pregnancy due to rape, incest, minor, mentally ill woman\\n• Social → Contraceptive failure (failure of family planning methods)\\n\\nGestational age-wise provisions\\n\\n1. < 20 weeks\\n\\n• Indications: T / E / H / S\\n\\n• Therapeutic\\n• Eugenic\\n• Humanitarian\\n• Social\\n\\n• Opinion required: 1 Registered Medical Practitioner (RMP)\\n\\n2. 20–24 weeks\\n\\n• Indications: T / E / H\\n\\n• Therapeutic\\n• Eugenic\\n• Humanitarian (As in our case)\\n\\n• Opinion required: 2 Registered Medical Practitioners\\n\\n3. > 24 weeks\\n\\n• Indication: T/ E\\n• Approval required: State Medical Board\\n\\n• Members: OBG specialist + Pediatrician + Radiologist (or sonologist)\\n\\nKey exam points\\n\\n• <20 weeks → 1 doctor\\n• 20–24 weeks → 2 doctors\\n• >24 weeks → Medical Board + only fetal anomaly","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d28efe1e72","n":467,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"According to Section 116 BNS, which of the following is not classified as Grievous Hurt?","options":["Severe pain for 10 days","Broken Tooth","Elbow dislocation","Emasculation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Severe pain for 10 days\\n\\nAccording to 116 BNS (previously 322 IPC), grievous hurt is defined by eight specific clauses:\\n\\n1.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Emasculation - inability of a man to conduct sexual intercourse\\n\\n2.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Permanent privation (loss) of sight of either eye\\n\\n3.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Permanent privation of hearing of either ear\\n\\n4.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Privation of any member or joint\\n\\n5.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Destruction or permanent impairment of power of any member or joint\\n\\n6.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Permanent disfiguration of head or face\\n\\n7.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Fracture or dislocation of bone or tooth (even minor fractures qualify)\\n\\n8.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Any hurt which endangers life OR causes severe bodily pain for 15 days OR prevents ordinary pursuits for 15 days\\n\\nThe new BNS has changed the duration criterion from 20 days (in old IPC) to 15 days. Since \\"severe pain for 5 days\\" doesn\'t meet the minimum 15-day requirement, it would only qualify as simple hurt, not grievous hurt.\\n\\nNote –\\n\\n114-125 BNS: Hurt, Grievous Hurt\\n\\n• 114 BNS: Def. Of hurt\\n• 115 BNS: \\"Voluntarily\\" causing hurt\\n• 116 BNS: Def. Of grievous hurt\\n• 117 BNS: \\"Voluntarily\\" causing grievous hurt\\n• 118 BNS: Hurt or G. Hurt by \\"dangerous weapon\\"\\n• 122 BNS: Hurt or G. Hurt on \\"Provocation\\"\\n• 123 BNS: Hurt by \\"Poison\\"\\n• 124 BNS: G. Hurt by Acid (Throwing or administrating etc)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-05eac51a01","n":468,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"An 18-year-old girl was brought to the emergency by her mother with the history of sexual assault by a 24-year-old co-worker. Mother does not want to inform the police. What would you do as the doctor in charge?","options":["Do not collect materials required as forensic evidence","Obtain consent from the mother and do medicolegal examination","Obtain consent from the girl and proceed with medical and forensic examination.","Do not report the case to the police, as the mother objects to the disclosure of the assault"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Obtain consent from the girl and proceed with medical and forensic examination.\\n\\nIn cases of sexual assault, a patient ≥18 years (adult) has full autonomy and legal capacity to consent for medical and medicolegal procedures.\\n\\n• Informed consent must be obtained from the victim herself, not relatives\\n• Medical examination and forensic evidence collection can proceed with her consent, irrespective of family wishes\\n• The doctor’s priority is:\\n\\n• Treatment\\n• Forensic evidence preservation\\n• Psychological support\\n\\nLegal aspects (India):\\n\\n• As per guidelines and Section 164A CrPC, examination requires victim’s consent\\n• Refusal by relatives is not valid if the patient is an adult\\n• Doctor should not deny examination or evidence collection\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• A: Evidence must be collected if patient consents\\n• B: Consent from mother is not valid for an adult\\n• D: Police intimation may be required, but care should not be withheld","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-265a2449fe","n":469,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"BNS Section 90 deals with?","options":["Abortion with consent of mother","Abortion without consent of mother","Abortion leading to death of mother","Preventing the child from being born alive"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Abortion leading to death of mother\\n\\nRelated BNS sections (updated from IPC):\\n\\n80: Dowry death → ≤7 years of marriage\\n\\n88: Criminal abortion with consent of mother\\n\\n89: Criminal abortion without consent of mother\\n\\n90: Criminal abortion leading to death of mother","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d42f4fd196","n":470,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"If a woman is undergoing MTP after 24 weeks of gestation due to congenital anomaly in the fetus , whose presence is not required for authorization?","options":["Obstetrician","Lawyer","Pediatrician","Sonologist"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lawyer\\n\\nUnder the MTP (Medical Termination of Pregnancy) Act (amended 2021) in India, termination beyond 24 weeks for substantial fetal anomalies requires approval by a Medical Board, not legal authorization from a lawyer.\\n\\n• Medical Board composition typically includes:\\n\\n• Obstetrician/Gynecologist\\n• Pediatrician\\n• Radiologist/Sonologist\\n• Other specialists as required\\n\\n•\\n\\nThe decision is based on medical opinion, not legal proceedings\\n\\n•\\n\\nCourts are involved only in exceptional or disputed cases\\n\\nWhy other options are required:\\n\\n• Obstetrician: Performs and supervises MTP\\n• Pediatrician: Assesses fetal viability/anomalies\\n• Sonologist: Confirms congenital anomalies","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7fd08ae3dc","n":471,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"If faced with a surgical emergency in a child of 15 years for whom no consent is available for life-saving surgery and no time for seeking authority from someone, the next step should be:","options":["Conservative management till lawyer is available","Search for relatives or neighbours","Consent arrangement through Hospital Social Worker","Go ahead with surgery without consent"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Go ahead with surgery without consent\\n\\nThis reflects the principle of emergency/implied consent (also called the \\"doctrine of necessity\\") in medical jurisprudence:\\n\\n• In a genuine life-threatening emergency, when the patient (or in the case of a minor, the parent/guardian) is unable to give consent and there is no time to obtain it, the law implies consent to save life.\\n• The doctrine assumes any reasonable person would want life-saving treatment, so the physician is legally and ethically justified in proceeding.\\n• Delaying care to search for relatives, arrange a social worker, or wait for legal counsel would breach the standard of care and could itself constitute negligence, since the delay could cost the patient their life.\\n• This principle applies regardless of age — a minor in a life-threatening emergency is treated the same way, since parental consent cannot be reasonably obtained in time.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-768e9f839e","n":472,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"What is the minimum age requirement for a woman to undergo Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) under the MTP Act in India?","options":["18 years","25 years","16 years","20 years"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 16 years\\n\\nUnder the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) Act in India, a woman ≥18 years of age or older can give valid consent for MTP on her own.\\n\\nKey concepts:\\n\\n• ≥18 years (adult):\\n\\n• Own consent is sufficient for MTP\\n\\n• <18 years (minor) or mentally ill woman:\\n\\n• Requires guardian’s consent\\n\\nThe MCQ here asks the minimum age for undergoing MTP. There is no minimum age for that as long as consent is given by the valid authority; hence, 16 years is a better answer.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-57d7ad538f","n":473,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Medical Law","text":"Which of the following information needs to be disclosed in order to establish valid consent for surgical treatment? I. Condition and reasons why it warrants surgery II. Type of surgery proposed III. Unexpected hazards of proposed surgery IV. The surgical experience and expertise of the operating surgeon Select the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["I, II and III","I, II and IV","I, III and IV","II, III and IV"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. I, II and III\\n\\nValid informed consent is a legal and ethical requirement before any surgical procedure. For consent to be valid, the patient must have the capacity to decide, give consent voluntarily, and receive adequate information regarding the proposed treatment, including its risks, benefits, alternatives, and consequences of refusal.\\n\\nThe following information must be disclosed:\\n\\n• I. Condition and reasons why it warrants surgery: The patient should understand the diagnosis and why surgery is being recommended.\\n• II. Type of surgery proposed: The nature and purpose of the planned operation must be explained.\\n• III. Unexpected hazards (material risks) of the proposed surgery: Significant risks, complications, benefits, alternatives, and the consequences of not undergoing surgery should be discussed.\\n\\nWhy is IV incorrect?\\n\\n• IV. The surgical experience and expertise of the operating surgeon: Disclosure of the surgeon’s personal experience or expertise is not a routine legal requirement for valid informed consent. Consent focuses on the procedure and its risks, not the surgeon’s credentials, unless specifically asked by the patient or required in exceptional circumstances.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b0aba0634c","n":474,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"At what age, basiocciput fuses with basisphenoid?","options":["18-22 years","22-25 years","14-16 years","12-14 years"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 18-22 years\\n\\nBasiocciput fuses with basisphenoid around 18-22 years.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c2e00f5311","n":475,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Edmond Locard is most famous for?","options":["Study of fingerprints","Theory of exchange","Stature estimation","Forensic ballistics"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Theory of exchange\\n\\nEdmond Locard is famous for his theory of exchange\xa0or\xa0Locard’s exchange principle.\\n\\nThis theory states that\xa0every contact leaves a trace.\xa0When two objects come into contact with one another, each will take something from the other object or leave something behind.\\n\\nLocard is also renowned for his contribution to the improvement of dactylography, an area of study that deals with fingerprints. He developed the science of poroscopy, the study of fingerprint pores, and the impressions produced by these pores.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8580d377b5","n":476,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Luminescent stools are associated with which of the following?","options":["Mercury","Cadmium","Phosphorus","Oxalic acid"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Phosphorus\\n\\nLuminescent stools are seen in acute\xa0phosphorus poisoning.\xa0The vomitus and stools will be luminous in the dark.\\n\\nThe stools may give rise to faint fumes, constituting the\xa0smoky stool syndrome.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8177f0d293","n":477,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"McNaughten\'s rule falls under which Section?\\n\\nBNS- Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita\\nBNSS- Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita","options":["BNSS Section 85 (CrPC 83)","BNS Section 22 (IPC 84)","BNS Section 219 (IPC 184)","BNSS Section 204 (CrPC 184)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. BNS Section 22 (IPC 84)\\n\\nMcNaughten\'s rule falls under Section 22 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (IPC 84).\\n\\nIt states, \\"A person of unsound mind is not liable for an act.\\"\\n\\nDefect: In Mc Naughthen’s rule, only intellectual factors are considered but emotions, delusion, and control of impulses are ignored.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-16d8ed7664","n":478,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Posthumous child is a","options":["Child delivered after the death of parents","Child delivered after the death of biological father","Child born out of wedlock","Fictitious child"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Child delivered after the death of biological father\\n\\nThe child delivered after the death of the biological father, or a child delivered after the death of the biological mother, usually when delivered by a cesarean section is a posthumous child.\\n\\nIllegitimate child is a child born out of wedlock i.e. when the parents are not married.\\n\\nSupposititious child or fictitious child is when a woman pretends to go through pregnancy and delivery. Later, she produces a living child as her own for obtaining money or for the purpose of claiming the property.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d2503df66b","n":479,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Saturnine gout is seen in:","options":["Lead poisoning","Cadmium poisoning","Beryllium poisoning","Mercury poisoning"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lead poisoning\\n\\nSaturnine gout\xa0is seen in\xa0chronic lead intoxication or poisoning.\\n\\nThe features of saturnine gout are the same as primary gout but it affects the\xa0knee more than the first metatarsophalangeal joint\xa0and\xa0tophi rarely develop. Lead exposure should be suspected if a patient presents with the triad of:\\n\\n• Saturnine gout\\n• Hypertension\\n• Renal insufficiency\xa0(renal failure and interstitial fibrosis)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-cea3125be0","n":480,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"What is the odor of the mummified body?","options":["Odorless","Pungent","Putrid","Offensive"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Odorless\\n\\nThe mummified body is odorless.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b8d336b527","n":481,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Which is the first organ to putrefy?","options":["Brain","Heart","Kidney","Prostate"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Brain\\n\\nAmong the given options, the first organ to putrefy is the brain.\\n\\nThe order of organ putrefaction (from first to last):\\n\\n1. Larynx and trachea\\n2. Stomach, intestine, spleen\\n3. Liver and lungs\\n4. Brain\\n5. Heart\\n6. Kidney and the bladder\\n7. Prostate and uterus\\n8. Skin, muscle, tendons\\n9. Bones","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-613d78a168","n":482,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Which metallic poisoning causes proximal convoluted tubule damage with proteinuria and muscle pain ?","options":["Arsenic","Chromium","Mercury","Lead"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Arsenic\\n\\nArsenic poisoning causes PCT damage\xa0with proteinuria and muscle pain.\\n\\nNephropathy is caused by most of the heavy metals. However, muscle pain is seen with arsenic poisoning.\\n\\nSalient features of Arsenic poisoning:\\n\\nAcute poisoning:\xa0Resembles cholera; garlicky odor\\n\\nChronic poisoning: Following features are seen:\\n\\n• Raindrop pigmentation seen on temples, eyelids and neck\\n• Aldrich-Mee’s lines\xa0(transverse white striae on the fingernails)\\n• Hyperkeratosis of palms and soles\\n• Painful paraesthesia of hands and feet\\n• Red velvety appearance of stomach\\n• Blackfoot disease -\xa0a gangrenous condition affecting the extremities\\n\\nTests to detect Arsenic poisoning:\\n\\n• Marsh test and Reinsch test\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\nFreshly prepared hydrated ferric oxide.\\n\\nBAL(British Anti Lewisite)\\n\\nPenicillamine(If BAL not available)\\n\\nNote: Mercury does not cause muscle pain though it commonly causes PCT damage.\\n\\nMee’s lines- Transverse white striae of the fingernails\\n\\nHyperkeratosis of palms and soles\\n\\nRain drop pigmentation","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-23f72359ad","n":483,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Which of the following constitutional article is not related to children?","options":["21A","24","45","42"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 42\\n\\nArticle 42 is not related to children. Article 42 says the state shall make provision for securing just and humane conditions of work and maternity relief.\\n\\nConstitutional Guarantees that are meant specifically for children include:\\n\\n• Article 21 A - Right to free and compulsory elementary education in the 6 -14 year age group.\\n• Article 24 -\xa0Right to be protected from any hazardous employment till the age of 14 years.\\n• Article 39 E -\xa0Right to be protected from being abused.\\n• Article 39 F -\xa0Right to equal opportunities and facilities.\\n• Article 45 -\xa0Right to early childhood care and education for all children until they complete the age of 6 years.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8cc3347340","n":484,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Which of the following poisons cause bluish discoloration of the stomach mucosa on post mortem examination?","options":["Oxalic acid","Sodium amytal","Soneryl","Arsenic"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sodium amytal\\n\\nIn sodium amytal poisoning,\xa0the capsules stain the stomach mucosa and its contents, turquoise blue. Bluish discolouration of the stomach mucosa is also seen in copper sulphate poisoning.\\n\\nThe appearance of the gastric mucosa in specific poisons:\\n\\nPoison\\n\\nAppearance of Gastric Mucosa\\n\\nArsenic\\n\\nRed velvety mucosa\\n\\nSulphuric acid\\n\\nBrown or black with erosion perforation and Carbonization\\n\\nCarbolic acid\\n\\nGrey leathery and thickened mucosa\\n\\nHNO3\\n\\nYellow\\n\\nHCN\\n\\nPink\\n\\nKCN\\n\\nBrick red to brown\\n\\nPhosphorous\\n\\nYellow or grey white\\n\\nSodium amytal, Copper sulphate\\n\\nBlue","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ae0dbe03b0","n":485,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"A patient died from bronchopneumonia following an intracerebral hemorrhage caused by cerebral metastases from a squamous cell carcinoma of the left main bronchus. What should be recorded in the line I (c) of the death certificate?","options":["Bronchopneumonia","Cerebral metastasis","Intracerebral hemorrhage","Squamous cell carcinoma of the left main bronchus"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cerebral metastasis\\n\\nCerebral metastasis will be filled in the line I (c) of the death certificate, as one of the antecedent causes in the order of the events that took place.\\n\\nThe death certificate will be filled in the order of the events that took place, as follows:","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2186ee3421","n":486,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Frotteurism is __________","options":["Obtaining sexual pleasure by wearing clothes of opposite sex","Desire to seek surgery to become member of opposite sex","Sexual gratification by rubbing his private parts against another person","Exposure of one’s genitals to an unsuspecting stranger"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sexual gratification by rubbing his private parts against another person\\n\\n• Frotteurism is obtaining sexual gratification by contact with another person (rubbing his private parts against female buttocks, breasts, thighs etc. in crowds etc). It is punishable under Section 292 BNS (290 IPC, with a fine of up to 200 Rs). Under the BNS, it is permissible to be fined up to Rs. 1000.\\n• Eonism\xa0(also known as\xa0Transvestism) is a sexual perversion where a person\'s whole personality is dominated by a desire to be identified as a member of the opposite sex. Usually seen in males who obtain sexual pleasure by wearing female clothes. There is no hormonal/genital abnormality.\\n• Transsexualism:\xa0It is the desire to become members of the opposite sex and seek surgery for anatomical alterations.\\n• Exhibitionism:\xa0 It involves behaviours over a period of 6 months or more, involving the exposure of one’s genitals to an unsuspecting stranger. This is a punishable offence. Section 296 BNS (294 IPC with imprisonment up to 3 months or fine.)\\nUnder the BNS, this is punishable with imprisonment up to 3 months, or a fine of Rs. 1000, or with both.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-47bd789de7","n":487,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Gastric lavage can be performed in case of poisoning by:","options":["Sulphuric acid","Strychnine","Carbolic acid","Kerosene oil"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Carbolic acid\\n\\nGastric lavage can be performed in case of carbolic poisoning.\\n\\nIn carbolic acid poisoning, if the patient\xa0survives for 48 hrs, there will be carboluria followed by anuria.\xa0The victim will pass dark,\xa0smoky urine\xa0which soon turns\xa0olive green\xa0on standing due to oxidation of hydroquinone and pyrocatechol.\\n\\nIt is a corrosive acid, however, it hardens the GI mucosa producing a\xa0leather bottle stomach\xa0and anesthetizes the nerve endings. This\xa0allows for gastric lavage.\\n\\nContraindications of gastric lavage:\\n\\n• Corrosives poisoning (except carbolic acid)\\n• Convulsants/CNS stimulants\\n• Comatose patients\\n• Volatile poison\\n• Upper GI pathology (eg. esophageal varices)\\n• Hypothermic patient\\n• Severe heart disease\\n• Advanced pregnancy\\n\\nSulphuric acid and kerosene are corrosives and strychnine is a convulsant. Hence gastric lavage is not done in these poisonings.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a98e3cd63b","n":488,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Identical twins have similarities in all of the following except:","options":["Blood group","DNA fingerprinting","Fingerprint pattern","Iris colour"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Fingerprint pattern\\n\\nFingerprint pattern differs in identical twins.\\n\\nFingerprints are formed during intrauterine life due to friction ridges. These ridges are created by pressure on the fingers from touching the surroundings. Hence, it is unique to every individual.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-83dc642af8","n":489,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"In alleged history of gunshot injury, there is burning, blackening, tattooing around the wound. The injury is:","options":["Close shot entry wound","Close shot exit wound","Distant shot entry wound","Distant shot exit wound"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Close shot entry wound\\n\\nBased on the ballistics, the gunshot injury is close shot entry wound.\\n\\n• The main objective of study of a firearm wound (Ballistics) is to establish the range and direction of firing, the ultimate purpose is to determine the weapon used for firing.\\n• Firearm wounds are fundamentally of two mains types: wounds of entry and wound of exit. The wound of entry is the wound through which the bullet or pellets enter the body, and the wound of exit is the wound through which the bullet emerges out of the body.\\n• To determine the Range of firing, following parameters are used:\\n\\n• Entry wound size and shape\\n• Blast effect – Muzzle end imprint and edge eversion\\n• Blackening, Tattooing, Singeing of hair, Abrasion collar and Grease collar\\n\\n• In the above options, Hair should be preserved in order to determine the range of firing which can be examined for carbonisation/dessication in close range, also from lead deposition around the wound to assess range of firing.\\n\\nCharacteristics\\nContact shot\\nClose Shot\\nNear Shot\\nDistant Shot\\n\\nRange\\nIn-touch with skin\\n2.5-7.5 cm\\n30-60 cm\\n>60 cm\\n\\nSize\\nLargest size\\nBullet size\\nSmaller\\nSmallest\\n\\nShape\\nStellate\\nCircular\\nCircular\\nCircular\\n\\nBlast effect\\n\\nMuzzle end imprint\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nEdge eversion\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nOther findings\\n\\nBlackening\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nTattooing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n\\nSingeing\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n-ve\\n-ve\\n\\nAbrasion collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n\\nGrease collar\\n-ve\\n+ve\\n+ve\\n+ve","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-57b164e471","n":490,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Rigor mortis occurs first in:","options":["Eyelids","Intestines","Myocardium","Neck"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Myocardium\\n\\nRigor mortis occurs first in the myocardium.\\n\\nThe order of appearance of rigor mortis: Left chamber of heart is affected first. Other muscles are affected in the given order:\\n\\n1. Involuntary muscles: Heart\\n2. Eyelids\\n3. Neck and lower jaw\\n4. Face\\n5. Chest\\n6. Upper extremities\\n7. Abdomen\\n8. Legs\\n9. Fingers and toes\\n\\nNote- Rigor mortis first occurs in the myocardium but is first seen in eyelids. Here the question is first occurs in and the answer is myocardium.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e63f54a86d","n":491,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following acts as a physiological antidote to scorpion venom?","options":["Physostigmine","Norepinephrine","Prazosin","Atropine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Prazosin\\n\\nPrazosin acts as a physiological antidote for scorpion venom.\\n\\nThe venom is a clear, colorless, proteinous toxalbumen, having a hemolytic and neurotoxic effect. Its toxicity is more than that of snakes, but only a small quantity is injected. The venom is a potent autonomic stimulator, resulting in the release of massive amounts of catecholamines from the adrenals.\\n\\nIf the scorpion has hemolytic venom, the reaction is mainly local and simulates the viper snake bite. The scorpion sting will have only one hole in the center of the reddened area. The extremity will have edema, pain, and reddening. This usually lasts for one to two hours.\\n\\nThe symptoms produced by a neurotoxic venom are similar to a cobra bite. There is usually no marked reaction in the local area. Nausea, vomiting, extreme restlessness, fever, various types of paralysis, cardiac arrhythmias, convulsions, coma, respiratory depression, and death may occur within hours from pulmonary edema or cardiac failure.\\n\\nPrazosin\xa0can counteract the alpha-adrenergic effects of autonomic stimulation, such as hypertension.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-30efb2000f","n":492,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following is commonly used for preservation of viscera in toxicology autopsy?","options":["Glycerin","Rectified sprit","Formalin","Saturated salt solution"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Saturated salt solution\\n\\nA saturated salt solution is used commonly for the preservation of viscera in toxicology autopsy.\\n\\nRectified spirit is indicated for most of the poisons, exceptions being alcohol, kerosene, phosphorus, paraldehyde, carbolic acid, acetic acid, etc.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-76197f885f","n":493,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following statements is true about battered baby syndrome?","options":["Skin lesions and bruises of various ages are seen on the body","It occurs due to the tendency of a child to fall repeatedly","Multiple stab wounds are seen","The syndrome is a result of firearm injuries"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Skin lesions and bruises of various ages are seen on the body\\n\\nSkin lesions and bruises of various ages are a classical feature of a child with battered baby syndrome.\\n\\nA battered child is one who has received repetitive physical injuries as a result of non-accidental\xa0violence, produced by a parent or guardian, there may also be deprivation of nutrition, care, and affection.\\n\\nThe syndrome must be considered in any child:\\n\\n• In whom the degree and type of injury varies with the history given\\n• When injuries are of different ages and in different stages of healing\\n• When there is a purposeful delay in seeking medical attention despite serious injury\\n• Who exhibits evidence of fracture of any bone, subdural hematoma, failure to thrive, soft tissue swelling or skin bruising\\n• Who dies suddenly\\n\\nSkeletal survey, which includes the following, should be done in a case of suspected child abuse:\\n\\n• AP views of the entire skeleton\\n• Dedicated views of hands and feet\\n• Lateral views of appendicular skeleton, skull and spine","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7d460af32c","n":494,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following tests is based on the principle that prior knowledge of the events/activity will influence the suspect’s reaction?","options":["Truth Serum","Polygraph","Brain Mapping","Hypnosis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Brain Mapping\\n\\nBrain Mapping is based on the principle that if the suspect has prior knowledge of the event/activity being described it will reflect in the suspect’s brain wave responses.\\n\\nIt is a technique that measures recognition of familiar stimuli by measuring electrical brain wave responses(P300) to words, phrases, or pictures that are presented on a computer screen.\\n\\nTruth serum : This is based on the principle, that at a point very close to unconsciousness, the subject will be mentally incapable of resisting questioning and will be incapable of lying.\\n\\nPolygraph : It is based on the theory, that when a person lies in the answer to a question, if he/she experiences fear, the emotion of fear will result in certain physiological changes.\\n\\nThe physiological changes are commonly observed are :\\n\\n1. Relative rise in blood pressure\\n2. Pulse rate increases\\n3. Slowing down of breathing/erratic breathing\\n4. Suppression of involuntary muscular movements\\n5. Increased activity of sweat glands","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8550ba2fcc","n":495,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which Section of the IPC/BNS deals with the punishment of perjury?","options":["191/ BNS 227","192/ BNS 228","193/ BNS 229","197/ BNS 234"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 193/ BNS 229\\n\\nThe punishment of perjury is dealt with under IPC 193, has now been updated to BNS 229.\\n\\nPerjury is defined as\xa0the offence of wilfully telling an untruth or making a misrepresentation under oath.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b915c9e562","n":496,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"A 5-year-old child with a history of perianal pain is brought to the OPD. On subjecting the specimen from the perianal region to a test, it produces yellow rhombic crystals of spermine picrate. What is the test done?","options":["Barberio test","Florence test","Takayama test","Teichmann test"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Barberio test\\n\\nThe test done in the scenario is the\xa0Barberio test.\\n\\nIt is used to detect seminal fluid.\xa0When saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of picric acid is added to the spermatic fluid, yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals of spermine picrate are produced.\\n\\nFlorence test:\\n\\n• Used to detect: semen\\n• Stain used: 10%\xa0hydrochloric\xa0acid\xa0and\xa0Florence solution (potassium iodide, iodine, and water)\\n• Finding: If semen is present, dark brown rhombic or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide appear immediately\\n\\nTakayama test\xa0(Haemochromogen crystal test):\\n\\n• Used to detect:\xa0bloodstains\\n• Stain used: Takayama reagent\\n• Finding: If bloodstain is present, pink feathery crystals of haemochromogen or reduced alkaline haematin appear in a few minutes\\n\\nTeichmann test (Haemin Crystal Test):\\n\\n• Used to detect:\xa0bloodstains\\n• Stain used: glacial acetic acid and a small crystal of sodium chloride\\n• Finding: If bloodstain is present, faint yellowish-red to brownish-black rhombic crystals of haemin or haematin chloride appear.\xa0When hydrogen peroxide is added, the haematin crystals give bubbles of gas","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-29ceb779b9","n":497,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"A child who is a victim under POCSO act is brought to the department of forensic medicine for age estimation. The X-ray image of the hand is shown below. What is the likely age of the child?","options":["4 years","7 years","10 years","13 years"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 13 years\\n\\nThe child\'s age is likely to be 13 years.\\n\\nIn the given X-ray image of the wrist and hand, all the carpal bones are seen. The lower epiphysis of the first metacarpal has not united with the shaft. This suggests that the age of the child is between 8 to 15 years.\\n\\nAdditionally, the presence of the hook of the hamate in the radiograph suggests that the age is between 13-14 years.\\n\\nThe age of ossification of the carpal bones:\\n\\nCarpal bone\\nAge of ossification\\n\\nCapitate\\n1-3 months\\n\\nHamate\\n2-4 months\\n\\nTriquetrum\\n2-3 years\\n\\nLunate\\n2-4 years\\n\\nScaphoid\\n4-6 years\\n\\nTrapezium\\n4-6 years\\n\\nTrapezoid\\n4-6 years\\n\\nPisiform\\n8-12 years\\n\\nHook of the Hamate\\n13-14 years\\n\\nThe age of fusion of joints:\\n\\nBone/ joint\\nMaximum age at fusion\\n\\nElbow joint centers\\nMetacarpals\\n16 years\\n\\nWrist joint centers\\n19 years\\n\\nHip bone centers\\n22 years","img":false,"images":["/bimg/cde0cb444082bc392c02.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-b53ad56d05","n":498,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"A deceased male was brought to the morgue for a post-mortem examination. His relatives reported that he had a tattoo over his skin, which was not found on examination. Which of the following structures would you examine?","options":["Lymph nodes","Liver","Arteries","Skin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lymph nodes\\n\\nThe lymph nodes\xa0should be examined in this case.\\n\\nSome amount of material or dye used for creating tattoo marks is transported through lymph to the regional lymph nodes.\xa0Hence, in cases where a tattoo mark has disappeared\xa0naturally or has been removed, the lymph nodes must be examined.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-98422aef19","n":499,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"In the case of rape of a young child, the hymen is usually:","options":["Ruptured since it is superficially situated","Ruptured since it is very thin","Unruptured since it is deeply situated","Unruptured since it is highly elastic"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Unruptured since it is deeply situated\\n\\nIn the case of a\xa0young child, the hymen is deeply situated and usually intact after intercourse. As the vagina is very small, it is impossible for the penetration of the adult organ to take place.\\n\\nIn children, there are few or no signs of general violence, as the child is unaware of the assault and incapable of resisting. A few indicators for inquiry are:\\n\\n• Pain on urination and/or defecation\\n• Abdominal pain\\n• Inability to sleep\\n• Sudden withdrawal from peers/adults\\n• Feelings of anxiety\\n• Nervousness\\n• Helplessness\\n• A feeling of ending one’s life","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c683171885","n":500,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"In the civil negligence case against a doctor who bears the onus of proof?","options":["Patient","Police not under rank of sub inspector","Magistrate","Doctor"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Patient\\n\\nIn the case of civil negligence, the burden of proof is on the patient.\\n\\nThe question of civil negligence arises when a suit is filed in a civil court:\\n\\n• By a patient or his/her relatives against the doctor for getting compensation from the doctor if he/she has suffered an injury due to the doctor\'s negligence.\\n• By a doctor against the patient or his/her relatives to collect the fees, the payment of which has been refused due to alleged professional negligence by the doctor.\\n\\nIn either case, the patient has to prove that the doctor has committed professional negligence.\\n\\nTo prove negligence, all the following four elements must be present:\\n\\n• Duty -\xa0A duty of care by the doctor must exist.\\n• Dereliction -\xa0The physician must maintain the standard of a \'prudent physician\' under similar circumstances. If the physician fails to maintain the standard of care, it is called dereliction.\\n• Direct causation -\xa0The negligent act or omission must directly cause the injury without any intervening cause.\\n• Damage -\xa0The negligent act must cause damage of a type that would have been foreseen by a reasonable physician.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-85999463b4","n":501,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"The most common drug which causes physical dependence is?","options":["Ketamine","Heroin","LSD","Phencyclidine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Heroin\\n\\nHeroin is the most common drug which can cause physical dependence.\\n\\nPhysical dependence is defined is a condition where withdrawal of a drug after its prolonged use causes physiological and emotional illness, often referred to as the withdrawal syndrome.\xa0Neither physical dependence nor withdrawal symptoms occur with hallucinogens. Although it is necessary to note that a user can develop a psychological dependence on the insight-inducing experiences of episodes of hallucinogen use. Hence, all 3 hallucinogens - ketamine, LSD, and Phencyclidine (PCP) does not cause physical dependence.\\n\\nAccording to the world drug report 2019, released by the United Nations office on drugs and crime (UNODC), the most common drugs used across the world (in decreasing order) include:\\n\\n• Cannabis\\n• Opioids\\n• Amphetamine and prescription stimulants\\n• Ecstacy\\n• Cocaine\\n\\nAmong the opioids, the commonly used group of drugs are opiates which include drugs like opium, morphine, and heroin.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f63c6f620a","n":502,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Which of the following poison can be found even in skeletonised remains of the body?","options":["Arsenic","Carbon monoxide","Hydrogen sulphide","Phosphorus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Arsenic\\n\\nArsenic can be found even in skeletonized remains of the body.\\n\\nIt becomes fixed in the cancellous tissues of the long bones by the conversion of phosphates to arsenates.\xa0The elimination of arsenic in this form is much slower and can be detected in the bones for several years after death.\\n\\nHeavy metals such as\xa0lead, copper,\xa0mercury, including metalloids like arsenic and\xa0antimony, may be found in bones for a prolonged period in cases of chronic poisoning. They are also deposited in hair, nails, etc. For these reasons, when heavy metal poisoning is suspected, these specimens are collected in addition to the routine viscera and body fluids.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-dfa6bb9411","n":503,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A guy was playing in a garden. Suddenly, he collapsed and was rushed to the hospital. His friend who played with him in the garden informed the doctors that he ate one of the fruits in the garden post which he seemed to have developed these symptoms. He also had irritability, restlessness, dry hot skin, and was unable to pass urine and stools. Identify the poison and its appropriate antidote.","options":["Datura, Pralidoxime","Datura, Physostigmine","Yellow oleander, Digoxin","Yellow oleander, Physostigmine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Datura, Physostigmine\\n\\nThe clinical vignette in the question is suggestive of\xa0Datura poisoning\xa0for which the appropriate antidote is\xa0physostigmine, a reversible acetylcholine esterase antagonist. It is an effective treatment for severe anticholinergic poisoning.\\n\\nDatura poisoning:\\n\\nThe common names include thorn apple, Jimson weed, and Devil\'s trumpet.\xa0The active ingredients\xa0include the anticholinergic alkaloids atropine, scopolamine, and hyoscyamine. The seeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked.\\n\\nToxic manifestations of Datura:\\n\\n• Mnemonic: \\"hot as a hare, blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, and mad as a hatter\\"\\n• Also, remember\xa0the D\'s of Datura\\n\\n• Dry hot skin (Temp rises up to 42 C, a scarlet rash may also be appreciated).\\n• Dilatation of pupils along with loss of accommodation\\n• Diplopia\\n• Dryness of the mouth and throat, unquenchable thirst\\n• Dysphagia\\n• Dilatation of cutaneous blood vessels\\n• Dysarthria (Difficulty in talking)\\n• Drunken gait\\n• Delirium\\n• Drowsiness\\n• Death due to respiratory or cardiac failure\\n\\nTreatment\\n\\n• Gastric lavage or decontamination with activated charcoal or tannic acid. Gastric emptying is often delayed, and the small, plentiful seeds can become trapped among the gastrointestinal folds after ingestion; thus gastric decontamination can be an important therapy.\\n• Physostigmine\xa0in severe toxicity given 1-2 mg IM/IV after every half hour as required. It is a\xa0physiological antidote. Pilocarpine may also be given.\\n\\nMedico-legal importance:\\n\\nIn India, datura is used as a stupefying poison prior to robbery, kidnapping, and rape. It is also known as a rail-road poison, as it is commonly encountered during a journey. Robbers disguised as saints may offer ‘prasad’ mixed with datura seeds and rob the passengers.\\n\\nYellow oleander:\\n\\nAll parts of the plant are poisonous. The active principles are\xa0thevatin, and thevatoxin, which are\xa0cardiac glycosides. The nectar of the flower yields poisonous honey. Symptoms include burning sensation in the mouth, tingling of the tongue, dryness of throat, vomiting, diarrhea, headache, dilated pupils, irregular action of heart followed by drowsiness, coma, collapse, and death.\\n\\nThe given image shows the flowers of\xa0Cerebra thevatia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fe2abdd976","n":504,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A guy was sitting on the balcony which was overlooking a park. He always used to sit nude on his balcony and derived sexual gratification by observing the people that look at him. This is best described as?","options":["Voyeurism","Fetishism","Exhibitionism","Masochism"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Exhibitionism\\n\\nThe given scenario is suggestive of\xa0exhibitionism.\\n\\nIt involves behaviors over a period of 6 months or more, involving the exposure of one’s genitals to an unsuspecting stranger. The individual obtains sexual gratification from this. This is a punishable offense (Section\xa0294 IPC/ Section 296 BNS) with imprisonment up to 3 months or a fine.\\n\\nThe important and common sexual perversions are summed in the pearl attached below. The various punishments in the legal provisions include:\\n\\nIPC section\\n\\nBNS Section\\n\\nSection it deals with\\n\\nPunishment\\n\\n290\\n\\n292\\n\\nPunishment for public nuisance (Frotteurism)\\n\\nFine Up to Rs.1000\\n\\n294\\n\\n296\\n\\nObscene acts and songs (Exhibitionism)\\n\\nUp to 3 months of imprisonment or fine (upto Rs. 1000)or both\\n\\n297\\n\\n301\\n\\nTrespassing on burial places/ wounding the feeling of any person /indignity to any human corpse\xa0(Necrophilia, Necrophagia)\\n\\nUp to 1 year of imprisonment or fine or both","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5e020c3a1a","n":505,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A man was working in a field and he suddenly collapsed on a hot summer afternoon. On examination, there were no physical signs of dehydration. His serum electrolytes level was normal. The doctor found that the body temperature was 106 degrees Fahrenheit. Which of the following symptoms is least likely to be seen in this patient?","options":["Hot skin","Hypotension","Sweating","Disorientation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sweating\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette in a patient with no signs of dehydration and\xa0body temperature of 106oF\xa0is highly suggestive of\xa0heatstroke.\xa0Sweating is absent in heatstroke.\\n\\nHeatstroke/ thermic fever/ heat hyperpyrexia is due to the failure of the heat regulation center of the hypothalamus. It\xa0is caused by failure of cutaneous circulation and sweating. The predisposing factors include high temperature, increased humidity, minor infections, old age, lack of acclimatization, alcoholism.\\n\\nHeatstroke\xa0can be:\\n\\n• Exertion-induced\xa0heat stroke is\xa0seen in people with prolonged exposure to or physical exertion in high temperatures, usually without adequate training.\\n• Sedentary\xa0heatstroke is a disease of elderly or infirm people whose cardiovascular systems are\xa0unable to adapt\xa0to the stress of a hot environment and release sufficient heat such that body temperature rises.\\n\\nAll the signs of shock are present. Patients usually present with a sudden collapse accompanied by loss of consciousness.\xa0Typical vital sign abnormalities include tachypnea, tachycardias, hypotension, and a widened pulse pressure. Other specific signs include\xa0rapid Kussmaul type of breathing, the absence of sweating with\xa0dry, hot skin, delirium, coma, convulsions, rhabdomyolysis, multiorgan failure.\\n\\nAlthough there is no single specific diagnostic test, clinical features of exposure to heat stress, CNS dysfunction, and core temperature >104.9-degree Fahrenheit (> 40.5 degrees Celsius) help establish the preliminary diagnosis.\xa0The body temperature must be measured rectally.\\n\\nTreatment includes\xa0rapidly cooling the body with ice water till the rectal temperature falls below 1020F.\\n\\nAutopsy findings include the presence of\xa0edema and reduction of Purkinje\'s cells in the cerebellum.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-38b5b221cd","n":506,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A woman gave birth to twins. The father who believed that they did not belong to him requested DNA testing. After testing, it was found that one child did not belong to him. This case would be best described as?","options":["Superfetation","Superfecundation","Supposititious child","Atavism"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Superfecundation\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario where one twin is found to not belong to the father of the other twin is suggestive of\xa0heteroparental\xa0superfecundation.\\n\\nSuperfecundation is the fertilization of\xa02 different ova\xa0in the\xa0same menstrual cycle (as suggested by the same gestational age)\xa0by separate acts of coitus. It can occur even after artificial reproductive therapy. Hence, after embryo transfer, intercourse must be avoided. Heteropaternal superfecundation is used to refer to instances of two different males fathering fraternal twins. This leads to the possibility of twins also being half-siblings. This\xa0may give rise to the doubt of adultery and infidelity.\\n\\nOption A: Superfetation\xa0is the fertilization of 2 different ova released in\xa0different menstrual cycles\xa0(i.e ovulation and fertilization\xa0during\xa0pregnancy). This can occur\xa0within 12 weeks\xa0of pregnancy after which the decidual space is obliterated and a detailed evaluation will reveal disparities in gestational ages.\\n\\nOption C: Supposititious child\xa0is\xa0also known as a fictitious child. In such cases, a woman\xa0pretends to be pregnant and generally\xa0substitutes a male child for a female child born of her, or after an abortion. This is done for\xa0the purpose of claiming property.\\n\\nOption D: Atavism\xa0is the reappearance of a characteristic in an individual after several generations of absence. For example, the child may not resemble his parents but resemble his ancestors. This is attributed to the recombination of genes.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1fe3d1a507","n":507,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A young teenage boy with a history of drug abuse had been instilled with a shot of intravenous cocaine at a party. He is rushed to the emergency room by his friends. Some of the symptoms were diaphoresis, headache, and acute coronary spasm. Which of the following symptoms noted would go against the likely diagnosis of cocaine poisoning?","options":["Myocardial ischemia","Bradycardia","Hyperthermia","Agitation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bradycardia\\n\\nCocaine intoxication presents with\xa0tachycardia\xa0and not bradycardia. Thus, the presence of bradycardia would go against the diagnosis of cocaine intoxication.\\n\\nCocaine is an alkaloid deliriant extracted from\xa0Erythroxylum coca.\\n\\nAcute intoxication with cocaine leads to symptoms spanning 2 stages-\\n\\n1. Stage of excitement/euphoria - associated with hypervigilance, anxiety, hyperactivity along with hyper-adrenergic signs of mydriasis, tachycardia, elevated blood pressure, hyperthermia,\xa0and cardiac arrhythmias.\\n2. Stage of depression - associated with loss of reflexes, muscle paralysis, circulatory and respiratory failure, and coma.\\n\\nChronic intoxication is characterized by toxic delirium and tactile hallucination of bugs (cocaine bugs)\xa0or vermin crawling under the skin (formication).\\n\\nCommon complications include\xa0cerebrovascular accidents\xa0and\xa0myocardial ischemia.\\n\\nTreatment\xa0includes intravenous diazepam for controlling seizures. For ventricular arrhythmias, propranolol may be given intravenously. If the patient has psychosis, haloperidol may be given. Treatment of chronic cocaine abuse requires psychiatric support.\\n\\nCocaine withdrawal\xa0is a triphasic syndrome that presents after the sudden stoppage of chronic cocaine use. The phases are as follows -\\n\\n1. Crash phase (9 hours - 4 days): Anorexia, depression, excessive craving, hypersomnia, fatigue.\\n2. Normal mood, anxiety, and anhedonia (4 - 7 days)\\n3. Extinction phase (7 - 10 days): Absence of withdrawal symptoms but increased susceptibility to relapse.\\n\\nTreatment for withdrawal\xa0includes bromocriptine and amantadine. They are useful in reducing cocaine craving.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-91a1945923","n":508,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"Identify the lesion shown in the image below.","options":["Laceration","Incised wound","Laceration looking incised wound","Incised looking laceration"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Incised looking laceration\\n\\nThe image depicts a wound with\xa0linear splitting of the tissue,\xa0sharp margins,\xa0and\xa0crushed hair follicles, which is suggestive of an\xa0incised-looking lacerated wound.\\n\\nIncised-looking lacerated wounds\xa0are seen at sites where the skin overlies an area of the bone where there is scanty underlying subcutaneous tissue. A blunt force\xa0causes linear splitting of the tissue which results in the laceration resembling an incised wound. There will be extensive tissue crushing but the margins will be clear and\xa0delineable.\\n\\nCommon areas\xa0are\xa0Scalp, Forehead, Eyebrows, Zygoma, Lower jaw, Perineum, Shin, and Iliac crest.\\n\\nLaceration\xa0(Option-A)\\n\\nA laceration is a tear or a split in the skin, mucous membrane, muscle, or any internal organ produced by blunt force. The full thickness of the skin is penetrated.\\n\\nFeatures of laceration:\\n\\n• Margins are irregular with pieces of tissue attached in between called\xa0tissue tags or bridges.\xa0Tears that are seen at the ends, diverging away from the main laceration are called\xa0swallow tails.\\n• Bruising\xa0is\xa0seen around the margins.\\n• Deeper tissues are unevenly divided with tissue tags.\\n• Hair bulbs are crushed.\\n• Depth of the wound contains abundant foreign material such as dust particles, paint material of the vehicle involved, etc\\n• In skin lacerations, usually one of the margins overhangs the other. This helps in assessing the direction of the force.\\n• A laceration, on healing, produces a permanent scar.\\n\\nClassification of a laceration:\\n\\n• Split laceration (incised - like laceration)\\n\\n• This occurs when the skin is\xa0crushed between two hard objects\\n• For example, during a fall, the scalp gets crushed between the skull from inside and hard ground outside. This results in a wound, which usually mimics an incised one unless examined closely.\\n\\n• Stretch laceration\\n\\n• As the name implies, it occurs due to the\xa0over-stretching of the skin, beyond its elasticity.\\n• This typically occurs in the setting of a fracture, where the fracture fragment may stretch and pierce the skin to project out\\n\\n• Avulsed laceration\\n\\n• An avulsion is a lesion where the\xa0skin separates\xa0or peels off by tearing from the underlying tissues. It may\xa0overhang the wound.\\n• This is common in road traffic accidents.\\n\\n• Tears -\xa0Tearing of skin and subcutaneous tissue can occur from localized impact\\n• Cuts -\xa0Injury leads to a deep and wide injury leading to a cut\\n\\nIncised wound (Option-B)\\n\\nAn incised wound\xa0is a clean-cut wound made with a sharp-edged instrument, through the skin and subcutaneous tissues, including blood vessels. It is more in length than depth. The edges are well-defined and usually everted, free from contusions and abrasions. It is generally spindle-shaped. The depth is more at the commencement and progressively becomes shallower, known as tailing. Tailing determines the direction of the force applied when creating the wound.\\n\\nThe image below shows two spindle-shaped incisions.\\n\\nLaceration-looking incised wounds\xa0(Option C)\\n\\nIn areas of the body where a loose layer of muscle fibres is attached to the skin, the edges of the incision may become inverted, adherent, and wrinkled leading to it simulating a lacerated wound. Such wounds are called\xa0lacerated-looking incised wounds.\xa0These are commonly seen in\xa0Scrotum and Axilla.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/bd5f62272516f4e5ea69.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-31b418c9ae","n":509,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"The below phenomenon is due to","options":["Case of torture falanga","Case of suspension/hanging more than 72 hours","Case of immersion in water for 36 hours","Case of colliquative liquefaction due to immersion in hot water"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Case of immersion in water for 36 hours\\n\\nThe given image shows maceration of the skin of the soles suggestive of washerwoman\'s feet/ washerwoman\'s skin.\xa0It is seen in a case of immersion in water for 36 hours.\\n\\nWasherwoman\'s skin occurs if\xa0the\xa0body of the person has been immersed in water for a\xa0few hours to 3 days. The first signs tend to be on\xa0areas with an appreciable keratin layer\xa0(such as the fingertips, palms, backs of the hands, and later the soles) where the surface becomes wrinkled, pale, and sodden. This is called washerwoman’s skin.\xa0The\xa0first site\xa0affected is the\xa0fingerpads. It affects the hands earlier than the feet.\\n\\nWhen a body falls into water in average temperate climates, the following is an approximate guide to timing:\\n\\nFinding\\nTime since immersion\\n\\nNo wrinkling of finger pads\\nLess than a few hours\\n\\nWrinkled fingers, palms, and feet\\nFew hours to 3 days\\n\\nEarly decomposition (Head and abdomen)\\n4-10 days\\n\\nThe marbling of veins, peeling of the epidermis\\n2-4 weeks\\n\\nMuscle loss with bone exposure\\n1-2 months\\n\\nThe other signs of immersion are:\\n\\n1. Cutis anserina\xa0- goose-flesh or piloerection: This is usually found when the body is immersed in cold water.\\n2. Post-mortem hypostasis\xa0- If the water is still, the postmortem hypostasis affects the heads and the limbs. This is because the chest and abdomen float higher because of the lungs and the abdominal gases.\\n3. Foreign bodies in respiratory passages and stomach -\xa0Sand may be found deep in the respiratory passages and stomach, especially if the body has been rolled by the waves on a beach. This is not evidence of live aspiration.\\n\\nNote:\xa0Signs of immersion just indicate that the body was immersed in water, they are not evidence of death from drowning.\\n\\nFalanga\xa0(Option-A) also known as Falaka or Bastinado, is a method of torture that involves repeatedly striking the soles of the feet with an object such as a truncheon, iron rod, cable, bamboo cane, wooden pole, or rifle butt.\\n\\nGlove-stocking-like staining\xa0(Option-B)\xa0is used to describe the purple-colored postmortem staining visualized in the\xa0dependent parts\xa0of the upper and lower limbs of a hanging victim. It is absent in postmortem hanging, where the pattern of hypostasis does not correspond with the position of suspension of the body\\n\\nLiquefaction or colliquative changes\xa0begins by\xa05 days\xa0after death and becomes prominent in 10 days. Soft tissues change into semisolid masses which are black in color and get separated from bones and fall off.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/4869dcdd23351b9a06bd.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-c13c6c984c","n":510,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"What is the active principle of the poison shown in the image?","options":["Ricin","Bhilawanol","Abrin","Calotropin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bhilawanol\\n\\nThe given image shows the\xa0seeds\xa0of\xa0Semecarpus anacardium, and\xa0its active principles are\xa0bhilawanol\xa0and semicarpol.\xa0Common names are\xa0marking nut, bhilwan, bibva, bhela, and oriental cashew.\\n\\nThe image shows black, heart-shaped seeds with hard rinds that identify them as seeds of S. anacardium. Within the fruit is a thick fleshy pericarp that yields the active principle, which is a brown oily resinous fluid called\xa0bhilawanol.\\n\\nThe juice\xa0when applied locally to the skin produces irritation, painful blisters, itching, and eczematous eruption. The\xa0seeds are used to produce an\xa0artificial bruise. Symptoms such as pain, fever, and painful passage of brownish urine\xa0may be seen.\\n\\nA large dose taken orally can produce blisters in the mouth and throat, with gastroenteritis. It also produces dyspnea, cyanosis, tachycardia, coma, and death. The fatal\xa0period is\xa012 to 24 hours.\\n\\nMedicolegal importance\xa0is the use of\xa0the juice by malingerers\xa0to fabricate wounds. Instilled in the eyes it can cause conjunctivitis or ophthalmia due to its local irritant effect.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Ricin is the active principle of\xa0Ricinus communis.\xa0It is a toxalbumin or phytotoxin that inhibits protein synthesis by affecting the ribosomal RNA polymerase. The seeds are flat to oval-shaped and have dark brown markings (shown below). It is a\xa0chemical warfare\xa0agent.\\n\\nOption C: Abrin\xa0is the active principle of\xa0Abrus precatorius.\xa0The seeds are\xa0egg-shaped, scarlet\xa0in color, and have a\xa0black spot\xa0at one end (shown below).\xa0On ingestion, it causes gastroenteritis with\xa0bloody diarrhea, rectal bleeding, tachycardia, and circulatory collapse. Injecting the active substance can mimic\xa0viperine snakebite.\xa0Rarely, it may lead to\xa0tetanic convulsions.\\n\\nThe seeds of A. precatorius are decocted and mixed with datura, opium, onion, spirit, and water to form a paste. From this, small sharp needles called suis are made. They are used for hunting animals and even for\xa0homicide purposes.\\n\\nOption D: Calotropin is one of the active principles of\xa0Calotropis.\xa0The toxic parts of the plant are the stem, branches, leaves, and milky white latex (Madar juice). When applied locally, it can mimic bruises, and form pustules and vesicles. In the eyes, it can lead to conjunctivitis. On ingestion, it causes abdominal pain, vomiting, mydriasis, and tetanic convulsions and can eventually lead to collapse and death.\\n\\nIt is shown in the image below.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/4d0e6b6fb3f16848916d.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-02665e7f5f","n":511,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"Which of the following is true regarding the post mortem change shown in the image given below?","options":["Seen within 24 hours of death","Due to sulphemoglobin accumulation and formation","Due to aseptic non bacterial cause","It is associated with a case of lightning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Due to sulphemoglobin accumulation and formation\\n\\nThe postmortem change shown in the given image is\xa0marbling.\xa0It is\xa0seen when blood in the vessels is haemolysed to sulfmeth-hemoglobin, which stains the vessel walls and the adjacent tissues, giving rise to a marbled appearance.\\n\\nMarbling:\\n\\nThe marbling of the skin\xa0begins at\xa0about\xa024 hours\xa0and becomes prominent in\xa036–48 hours\xa0after death.\xa0It is a visible postmortem change that is characterized by linear branching patterns on skin that correspond to the course of superficial veins. Intestinal bacteria infest the venous system and there is subsequent gas production and hemolysis which stains the vessel walls.\xa0This becomes prominent in the summer and is distinctly appreciable in the superficial veins of the abdomen, shoulders, chest, and inguinal region.\\n\\nLichtenberg\'s\xa0figures\xa0or\xa0filigree\xa0burns\xa0are\xa0pathognomonic\xa0for\xa0lightning injury.\xa0It is a superficial, thin, tortuous, fern-like pattern of erythema in the skin. It indicates the path taken by the electrical discharge and tends to follow skin creases and the long axis of the body. They are also known as\xa0arborescent\xa0burns or\xa0feathering\xa0as they have a resemblance to the branches of a tree. Lichtenberg\'s figures occur due to the\xa0rupture of small vessels. They are usually seen over the shoulder or flank within an hour of injury and disappear in 1-2 days.\\n\\nMedico-legal aspect: Its recognition may be lifesaving in the unaccompanied comatose patient and is important because even delayed resuscitation of lightning victims can be very successful.\\n\\nThe image below shows the filigree burns seen in a case of lightning.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/ab5234b78622f494ec3b.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-358b99fa8e","n":512,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"While recording evidence in the court of law, the lawyer asked the witness, \\"Did A kill B in your presence?\\" The witness said, \\"Yes.\\" This type of question would be permitted in which procedural route in the court of law?","options":["Examination-in-chief","Direct examination","Cross examination","Re-direct examination"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cross examination\\n\\nIn the given scenario, the kind of question asked by the lawyer to the witness is an example of a\xa0yes/no question\xa0or\xa0leading question.\xa0Leading questions are allowed\xa0during\xa0cross-examination\xa0according to\xa0Section 143 IEA (Indian Evidence Act).\\n\\nLeading questions\xa0(Sec. 141 IEA) are defined as questions that suggest the answer that the person asking the question wishes or expects to receive. It is favourable for the case of the summoning counsel or in alignment with their claim.\xa0They are not allowed during the direct examination(Examination-in-Chief) conducted by the summoning counsel (Option A, B). The only exception to this is in the event that the Judge is satisfied that the witness is hostile, leading questions may be allowed during direct examination.\\n\\nIn cross-examination, the witness is examined by the defence lawyer or adverse party with the objective to elicit favourable facts to their case, to test the accuracy of the witness statements, unearthing new or old facts, and also, to discredit the witness. While leading questions are allowed in cross-examination, the Judge can disallow questions that are indecent or scandalous (Sec. 151 IEA) or questions asked with an intention to insult, annoy or offend the witness in any form (Sec. 152 IEA). There is\xa0no time limit\xa0to the duration of cross-examination.\\n\\nThe sequence of examination in the recording of evidence is as follows -\\n\\nSequence of Examination\\nObjective\\n\\nExamination-in-Chief/Direct examination by the party that summoned the witness\\n\\nTo elicit the facts of the case from the witness. Leading questions are not allowed.\\n\\nCross-examination by the defence lawyer\\n\\nTo elicit facts favourable to their case and test the reliability of the witness. Leading questions are allowed.\\n\\nRe-examination by the party that summoned the witness\\n\\nTo resolve doubts that formed during cross-examination and re-emphasize the party\'s perspective","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-83e7ba8b68","n":513,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"A 45-year-old female patient is told about the benefits and complications of hysterectomy and she agrees to the procedure. What kind of consent is this?","options":["Informed consent","Implied consent","Opt out","Passive consent"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Informed consent\\n\\nThis kind of consent is informed consent\xa0since the patient has been made aware of the benefits and complications and has agreed to the procedure.\\n\\nInformed consent is one given by the patient after understanding the nature of the condition, proposed treatment, outcomes of the recommended treatment, alternative options to treatment, and the\xa0risks and benefits involved. The patient can accept or reject the procedure. This is also called the rule of full disclosure.\\n\\nConsent refers to a voluntary agreement, compliance, or permission. It can be expressed or implied.\\n\\nImplied consent (option B)\xa0is that which is not written or expressly stated. It is implied that the patient is giving consent to examination in a general sense. For example, when a patient visits the hospital with an illness, it implies that he has given consent to a physical examination to find out the nature of his illness. Another example of implied consent is when a patient exposes his arm for injection. It is legally effective.\\n\\nExpressed consent can be oral or written and refers to consent in which the terms are clearly stated in distinct and explicit language.\\n\\nOpt out (Option C) refers to a patient\'s refusal of the procedure, test, or treatment. It is the physician\'s duty to disclose the risks or consequences of refusal\xa0adequately and appropriately to the patient.\\n\\nPassive consent (Option D) is not commonly described. However, in regard to research, it refers to parents signing and returning a form if they do not want to allow their child to participate in research.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-77c637851e","n":514,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"A 56-year-old man presents with fatigue, pallor, and abdominal pain. He reports memory loss and reveals that he works at a battery recycling plant. On examination, a noticeable foot drop in both lower extremities is seen. Laboratory testing shows elevated blood lead levels. Which enzyme is affected in the given scenario?","options":["Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) dehydratase","Uroporphyrinogen III","Uroporphyrinogen I","Pyruvate dehydrogenase"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) dehydratase\\n\\nThe given scenario is suggestive of chronic\xa0lead poisoning, and the enzyme affected is aminolevulinic acid dehydratase or ALA dehydratase.\\n\\nLead is a heavy metal, which mainly acts by\xa0depressing the enzymes in heme synthesis, blocking the enzyme aminolevulinic acid dehydratase and inhibiting ferrochelatase.\\n\\nChronic lead poisoning also referred to as plumbism or saturnism occurs following inhalation of lead dust or vapors that arise as a result of burning of paints, batteries, smouldering, glass blowing, and polishing.\\n\\nFeatures of lead poisoning:\\n\\n• Facial pallor, which is the earliest and most consistent sign\\n• Anemia\\n• Burtonian line, or the lead line, is seen on the gums as a blue line. It occurs as a result of lead sulphide formation, which is deposited as granules in the upper jaw at the junction of the teeth.\\n• Abdominal colic, constipation\\n• Lead palsy is a late manifestation, commonly seen in adults and is characterized by numbness, hyperesthesia, and muscle cramps, followed by weakness. Other features like foot drop, wrist drop, and fatigue may be seen.\\n• Lead encephalopathy\xa0is characterized by vomiting, convulsions, headache, insomnia, visual disturbances, and irritability.\\n\\nDiagnosis is mainly by history and clinical findings. Increased urinary lead and porphyrin excretion are suggestive of chronic lead poisoning. Radiograph, erythrocyte porphyrin assays, and basophilic stippling in the peripheral smear are also useful.\\n\\nProphylaxis in the form of proper ventilation, personal hygiene, periodic medical examination, supplementation of calcium-rich diet and sulphuric acid in the water, and weekly saline purgative in factory works is useful.\\n\\nManagement includes British Anti-Lewisite, or BAL,\xa0for severe acute poisoning, with and without encephalopathy;\xa0ethylenediamine tetraacetic acid (EDTA) for moderate poisoning; and\xa0D-penicillamine\xa0in mild cases.\\n\\nThe below image shows basophilic stippling.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fd558f74bf","n":515,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"A dead body is brought for evaluation. On post-mortem examination, a ligature that was completely encircling the neck, horizontal, and below the level of the thyroid was seen. There was no dribbling of saliva. What is the cause of death?","options":["Throttling","Ligature strangulation","Gagging","Hanging"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ligature strangulation\\n\\nThe most likely cause of death in a case where the ligature is completely encircling the neck, horizontal, and below the level of the thyroid,\xa0with no saliva dribbling,\xa0is\xa0ligature strangulation.\\n\\nStrangulation is a type of asphyxia where there is neck constriction without suspension of the body. The pressure on the neck is applied either by bare hands, a ligature, or by any other instrument.\\n\\nWhen strangulation is done by using a ligature like a constricting band or rope, it is called ligature strangulation. The constricting band is tightened by a force other than the victim\'s body weight. It is characterized by the\xa0ligature mark,\xa0which is transverse and completely encircles the neck below the thyroid cartilage. There is\xa0no dribbling of saliva. Bleeding may be present in the ears, nose, and mouth.\\n\\nLigature and manual strangulations are mostly homicidal. Marks of violence are usually seen on other parts of the body.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Throttling or manual strangulation occurs due to compression of the neck by humans leading to asphyxia. Thumb impression and finger marks may be seen over the victim\'s neck.\\n\\nOption C: Gagging is a type of asphyxia due to forcible closure of the mouth by a cloth or closing the mouth and nose and is tied around the head.\\n\\nOption D: Hanging is characterized by an incomplete and oblique ligature mark on the neck. It is usually seen higher, between the chin and larynx. Saliva often dribbles from the mouth.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-244f22ee42","n":516,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"A patient came with complaints of hair loss. His wife mentions that she has noticed some behavioral changes. The doctor notices that there is a loss of eyebrows on the lateral side. He then comes to a conclusion by examining the nails. What is the type of poisoning in this case?","options":["Thallium","Arsenic","Mercury","Lead"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Thallium\\n\\nThe given scenario of hair loss, loss of eyebrows on the lateral side along with behavioral and nail changes point to\xa0thallium poisoning.\\n\\nThallium poisoning occurs due to an overdose or idiosyncrasy. Thallium sulfate and thallium acetate are the two salts of thallium used in dyes, in the glass industry, and as rodenticides and pesticides.\\n\\nMild cases of poisoning have features of joint pain, loss of appetite, and drowsiness. Severe cases present with colic, vomiting, muscle pain, convulsions, psychosis, tremors, delirium, and coma are seen in severe cases.\xa0Hair loss is seen after 2 weeks and a characteristic feature is loss of the outer thirds of the eyebrows.\xa0Mees lines\xa0are seen on the nails.\xa0Maculopapular rashes in a butterfly pattern on the face are characteristic.\\n\\nIn chronic poisoning, alopecia, skin rashes, peripheral neuropathy, and mental confusion are present.\\n\\nTreatment is mainly by stomach wash with potassium iodide or Prussian blue, activated charcoal. Hemodialysis is useful and forced diuresis can be given.\\n\\nThe image below shows Mees lines.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-491c1de11b","n":517,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"A surgeon returns home from a party after many pegs of alcohol and was called to perform an emergency operation. During the operation, the assisting staff noticed the surgeon\'s hand shaking and instruments falling. He eventually nicks an artery and the patient collapses. Under which of the following terms will this incident be tried?","options":["Criminal negligence","Civil negligence not amounting to criminal negligence","Therapeutic misadventure","Dichotomy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Criminal negligence\\n\\nThe incident mentioned in the given scenario will be tried under criminal negligence.\\n\\nCriminal negligence is when a doctor lacks a gross skill or care while treating, which results in serious injury/death of the patient, or when an illegal act is performed by a doctor or when an assaulted person dies.\xa0Most cases of criminal negligence occur due to drunkenness or usage of drugs, which leads to impaired efficiency.\\n\\nSome of the examples are amputation of the wrong finger; leaving an instrument, tube, or sponge in the abdomen; incompetent administration of general anesthetic; and administrating the wrong substance in the eye, leading to visual loss.\\n\\nThe doctor is prosecuted by the police and charged in criminal court, when it is proved that no medical doctor in his ordinary senses would have done it. Contributory negligence cannot be used as a defense in criminal negligence.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Civil negligence\xa0occurs as a result of lacking care and skill in the profession. Contributory negligence can be cited as a defense in civil negligence.\\n\\nOption C: Therapeutic misadventure\xa0is a case that causes injury or death to the individual due to an unintentional act by a doctor or hospital. The\xa0physician is not liable for the injuries unless some negligence on his part contributed to the occurrence of the injury.\\n\\nOption D: Dichotomy is\xa0a type of unethical act, wherein a physician offers or receives any gift or bonus for referring or procuring any patient for medical or surgical treatment.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e62969f505","n":518,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"During the court proceedings, the defense lawyer asks a leading question, which the prosecutor appeals against. The judge grants the appeal. Leading questions are not allowed in all of the following except?","options":["Re-examination","Cross examination","Examination in chief","Dying declaration"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cross examination\\n\\nLeading questions are not allowed in all of the given options except cross-examination, i.e leading questions are allowed during cross-examination.\\n\\nAs per section 141 of the Indian Evidence Act, a leading question is one which is suggestive of the answer that is desired or one which includes a fact and leads to the admitting of a conclusive answer, such as yes or no.\\n\\nCross-examination is one wherein the witness is questioned by the opposite party\'s lawyer, i.e the lawyer for the accused or the defense lawyer. It is undertaken with the objective\xa0to demonstrate favorable facts to their case, to test the accuracy of the witness statements, to unearth new or old facts, and to discredit the witness.\\n\\nThe sequence of examination while recording evidence is as follows:\\n\\n1. Examination-in-Chief/direct examination by the party that summoned the witness to elicit all the facts from the witness. Leading questions are not allowed\xa0(option C). The only exception is if the judge is satisfied that the witness is hostile.\\n2. Cross-examination\xa0is done by the defence lawyer.\\n3. Re-examination\xa0is done by the party that summoned the witness to correct or clarify or detail the statements made by the witness during cross-examination. Leading questions are not allowed (option A).\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption D: A dying declaration is an oral or written statement made by a person who is dying. The doctor should certify that the person is conscious and with normal mental faculties before the declaration is recorded. It is recorded by the executive magistrate, or a doctor in the presence of two witnesses, if there is no time for the magistrate to come. Leading questions should not be asked.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-022d5e60f3","n":519,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"Identify the gun that caused the wound and the range of the shot.","options":["Shot gun, intermediate range","Shot gun, close range","Pistol, near shot","Pistol, close shot"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pistol, near shot\\n\\nThe given image showing a\xa0gunshot entry wound with powder burn around the wound is likely due to a near-shot injury by a\xa0pistol.\\n\\nA near-shot injury is one when the range of flame is within 60 cm.\\n\\nThe features of a near-shot wound are a circular entry wound with the size same as that of the bullet. The edges are lacerated, inverted, and surrounded by a narrow zone of abrasion. There are no signs of burning or singeing.\xa0Powder tattooing around the entry wounds\xa0is another important feature.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: In an\xa0intermediate range\xa0which is up to 7 feet, the edge of the wound is abraded and the appearance is referred to as a rat hole. There is no burning and the tattooing is appreciable till 3-4 feet and then starts disappearing.\\n\\nOption B: In shotgun injuries due to a close-range\xa0i.e between contact and 3 feet, the entry wound is circular or elliptical with features of the scorching, singing, and less dense tattooing.\\n\\nOption D: In a close shot pistol injury, \xa0the range of flame is within 5-8 cm with a circular entry wound. Other features such as burning, singeing, blackening and tattooing of skin are seen in and around the entry wound.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/3e2be895c1e84e057c8c.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-659fa40d8a","n":520,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"Identify the type of wound from the image.","options":["Defense wounds","Antemortem wound","Postmortem wound","Hesitation cuts"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Defense wounds\\n\\nThe given image shows irregular and ragged cut wounds\xa0over the palmar surface of the hands and fingers, which is suggestive of defense wounds.\\n\\nDefense wounds\xa0are the wounds that occur as a result of the victim\'s attempt to save himself from an injury.\\n\\nIt can be active or passive. Active defense wounds occur when the victim tries to grasp the weapon, while passive wounds result when the victim raises the hands, arms, or legs in an attempt to protect the eyes, head, or neck.\\n\\nThe morphology of the wound depends upon the type of weapon and the attack. In a sharp injury due to a single-edged weapon, a single cut may be seen on the palm or the bends of fingers. In the case of a double-edged sharp weapon, cuts are seen on both palm and fingers.\xa0The cuts are irregular in-depth and are ragged.\\n\\nThe defense wounds signify homicide, and in females, they often indicate sexual assault.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions B and C: Antemortem and postmortem wounds are of medicolegal importance while evaluating trauma. They can be differentiated by the following features:\\n\\nFeature\\nAntemortem wounds\\nPostmortem wounds\\n\\nEdges\\nSwollen edges with wound gapes\\nClosely approximated with no wound gapes\\n\\nHemorrhage\\nArterial type of hemorrhage\\nVenous hemorrhage\\n\\nSpurting\\nSpurting of blood seen with firmly coagulated blood\\nNo spurts and no clotting\\n\\nVital reaction\\nInflammation and repair seen\\nNo signs of inflammation and repair\\n\\nOption D: Hesitation cuts are a feature of self-inflicted wounds or suicide. It typically appears as multiple, small, and superficial wounds on the skin, at the beginning of an incised wound. The common sites are the throat, wrist, and front of the chest.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/bf8d47c46425adcef243.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-a5186be24b","n":521,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"A 10-year-old male child came to the casualty with difficulty in walking and pain in the perianal region. On subjecting the specimen from the perianal region to a test, it produces yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals with picric acid. What is the test done?","options":["Barberio test","Florence test","Teichmann test","Acid phosphatase test"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Barberio test\\n\\nThe test done in the scenario is the\xa0Barberio test.\\n\\nBarberio is used to detect\xa0seminal fluid.\xa0When a saturated aqueous or alcoholic solution of picric acid is added to the spermatic fluid, yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals of spermine picrate are produced.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Florence test is used to detect semen. If semen is present, dark brown rhombic or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide appear immediately\\n\\nOption C: The Teichmann test (hemin crystal test) is used to detect\xa0bloodstains.\xa0If bloodstain is present, faint yellowish-red to brownish-black rhombic crystals of hemin or hematin chloride appear.\xa0When hydrogen peroxide is added, the hematin crystals give bubbles of gas.\\n\\nOption D: The acid\xa0phosphatase test\xa0is a quantitative test for the presence of acid phosphatase enzyme in the semen.\xa0High levels of acid phosphatase from\xa0prostatic secretions\xa0are present in seminal stains (Normal vaginal secretions have low levels of acid phosphatase). Positive reactions are found till 36 hours after which the enzyme levels fall and disappear within 72 hours. In the absence of demonstrable sperm or aspermia, this test can be considered conclusive.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b1b195b335","n":522,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"A child before playing consumed fruit from the garden. After some time he developed a high fever, confusion, photophobia, and unable to urinate. What are the likely causative agent and the appropriate antidote used in this case?","options":["Datura, Pralidoxime","Datura, Physostigmine","Yellow oleander, Pralidoxime","Yellow oleander, Physostigmine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Datura, Physostigmine\\n\\nThe clinical vignette in the question is suggestive of\xa0Datura poisoning,\xa0for which the appropriate antidote is\xa0physostigmine, a reversible acetylcholine esterase antagonist. It is an effective treatment for severe anticholinergic poisoning.\\n\\nDatura poisoning:\\n\\nThe common names include thorn apple, Jimson weed, and Devil\'s trumpet.\xa0The active ingredients\xa0include the anticholinergic alkaloids atropine, scopolamine, and hyoscyamine. The seeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked.\\n\\nToxic manifestations of Datura:\\n\\n• Mnemonic: \\"hot as a hare, blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, and mad as a hatter\\"\\n• Also, remember\xa0the D\'s of Datura\\n\\n• Dry hot skin (Temp rises up to 42 C, a scarlet rash may also be appreciated).\\n• Dilatation of pupils along with loss of accommodation\\n• Diplopia\\n• Dryness of the mouth and throat, unquenchable thirst\\n• Dysphagia\\n• Dilatation of cutaneous blood vessels\\n• Dysarthria (Difficulty in talking)\\n• Drunken gait\\n• Delirium\\n• Drowsiness\\n• Death due to respiratory or cardiac failure\\n\\nTreatment\\n\\n• Gastric lavage or decontamination with activated charcoal or tannic acid: Gastric emptying is often delayed, and the small, plentiful seeds can become trapped among the gastrointestinal folds after ingestion; thus, gastric decontamination can be an important therapy.\\n• Physostigmine\xa0in severe toxicity given 1-2 mg IM/IV after every half hour as required: It is a\xa0physiological antidote. Pilocarpine may also be given.\\n\\nMedico-legal importance:\\n\\nIn India, Datura is used as a stupefying poison prior to robbery, kidnapping, and rape. It is also known as a rail-road poison, as it is commonly encountered during a journey. Robbers disguised as saints may offer ‘prasad’ mixed with Datura seeds and rob the passengers.\\n\\nYellow oleander:\\n\\nAll parts of the plant are poisonous. The active principles are\xa0thevatin\xa0and thevatoxin, which are\xa0cardiac glycosides. The nectar of the flower yields poisonous honey. Symptoms include a burning sensation in the mouth, tingling of the tongue, dryness of the throat, vomiting, diarrhea, headache, dilated pupils, irregular action of the heart followed by drowsiness, coma, collapse, and death.\\n\\nTreatment includes\\n\\n• Gastric lavage\\n• Single-dose activated charcoal\\n• To combat acidosis IV sodium lactate and 5% glucose can be given\\n• Atropine, adrenaline, or noradrenaline (if blood pressure is low)\\n• Digoxin-specific antibodies\\n\\nThe given image shows the flowers of\xa0Cerebra thevatia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5ff3fff802","n":523,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"A farmer was sleeping in the field, and he felt a sting on his leg. He saw something moving away quickly. He then got drowsy and was taken to the hospital. He developed pain around the site and continued to bleed profusely from the wound site. The wound became red with blisters. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Viper","Cobra","Wasp bite","Scorpion"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Viper\\n\\nBased on the given clinical findings, the most likely cause is a bite of\xa0viper snake, resulting in a\xa0haemotoxic envenomation.\\n\\nThe Russell\'s viper has a flat and triangular head with a\xa0V-shaped mark, white in color, with the angle of the V pointing forwards. Along the\xa0back, it has\xa0three rows\xa0of\xa0diamond-shaped spots. Other points of identification include broad plates on its belly, the small scales on the head, and a shield beneath the tail divided into two rows. It is commonly found throughout India.\\n\\nPatients with Russell\'s viper bite usually present with severe pain, and redness around the bite site. Swelling usually develops followed by blisters in and around the wound site. Persistent bleeding from the bite site is a constant feature. Patients may develop hemorrhagic syndrome due to bleeding from various sites like gums, nose, rectum, etc.\xa0In severe cases, shock may develop. Bleeding and clotting time are usually prolonged. Death usually occurs due to shock or hemorrhage.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Cobra venom usually produces neurotoxic effects with minimal local effects. Neurological manifestations of cobra venom include ptosis, paralysis of the face, palate, jaws, tongue, neck muscles, muscles of deglutition, and respiratory muscles.\\n\\nOption C: Wasp stings cause immediate pain, a wheal and flare reaction, and local edema and swelling that subside in a few hours. Multiple stings can lead to vomiting, diarrhea, generalized edema, dyspnea, hypotension, and collapse.\\n\\nOption D: Scorpion sting usually presents with localized redness and burning pain which is unable to localize due to its radiation along the dermatomes involved, localized swelling, and punctate hemorrhagic spots. Other features include headache, profuse perspiration, chest discomfort, paraesthesias, hypersalivation, cold extremities, and sometimes, priapism. Hypertension usually develops early, while pulmonary edema and myocarditis may develop later.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-33c4e60731","n":524,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"A patient was brought to casualty with seizures. He had multiple episodes and was taking herbal medicines for the same. He was conscious in between the episodes. During the episodes, he had arching of his back. What is the likely cause?","options":["Strychnine","Ricinus","Nerium odorum","Datura"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Strychnine\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette suggests the diagnosis of strychnine poisoning.\\n\\nStrychnine poisoning occurs if a person ingests crushed seeds of\xa0Strychnos nux vomica.\xa0If swallowed uncrushed, the seeds of nux vomica have no poisonous action.\xa0The fatal dose is 15-30 mg of strychnine or one crushed seed.\\n\\nStrychnine is a competitive antagonist of glycine at the postganglionic receptors in the motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord. This leads to increased activity in the motor neurons.\\n\\nPatients typically present with\\n\\n• Convulsions are clonic initially but eventually become tonic.\\n• Risus sardonicus results from the contraction of the jaws and facial muscles in which the corners of the mouth are drawn back.\\n• The convulsions are most marked in anti-gravity muscles so that the body typically arches in hyperextension, the posture known as opisthotonus.\\n• If the spasm involves the abdominal muscles, the body bends forward (emprosthotonos), or to the side (pleurosthotonus).\\n• If death is preceded by a severe convulsion, as in strychnine poisoning, it causes an increase in the body temperature known as postmortem\xa0caloricity.\\n\\nThe first step in the management of strychnine poisoning is to control convulsions\xa0with benzodiazepines or barbiturates to control seizures, and the patients should be placed in a dark room to avoid triggering further convulsions. Activated charcoal can be used to adsorb unabsorbed poison after a thorough stomach wash. Acidification of urine can be done to increase the excretion of strychnine.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Ricinus poisoning leads to symptoms such as the\xa0burning of the oral mucosa,\xa0salivation, nausea, vomiting, severe abdominal pain, and\xa0bloody diarrhea. It may also cause drowsiness, convulsions,\xa0delirium, and death.\\n\\nOption C: Nerium poisoning presents with headache, dizziness, respiratory difficulty, and nausea. Ingestion causes difficulty in swallowing and articulation, abdominal pain, vomiting, profuse frothy salivation, and diarrhea. Blood pressure falls, fibrillation, AV block, coma, respiratory paralysis, and death can occur due to cardiac failure.\\n\\nOption D: Datura poisoning presents with\\n\\n• Dry hot skin (Temperature rises up to 42\xb0C, a scarlet rash may also be appreciated)\\n• Dilatation of pupils along with loss of accommodation\\n• Diplopia\\n• Dryness of the mouth and throat, unquenchable thirst\\n• Dysphagia\\n• Dilatation of cutaneous blood vessels\\n• Dysarthria (Difficulty in talking)\\n• Drunken gait\\n• Delirium\\n• Drowsiness\\n• Death due to respiratory or cardiac failure","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2aa51e748a","n":525,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"BNS Section 90 (IPC 314) deals with","options":["Causing miscarriage with the consent of the mother","Causing miscarriage without the consent of the mother","Death of the mother by act done with intent to cause miscarriage","Causing the death of the quick unborn child by an act amounting to culpable homicide"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Death of the mother by act done with intent to cause miscarriage\\n\\nBNS Section 90 (IPC 314)\xa0deals with the death of the mother due to miscarriage.\\n\\nImportant sections of IPC relating to miscarriages:\\n\\nBNS Section\\n\\nIPC section\\n\\nDeals with\\n\\n88\\n\\n312\\n\\nCausing miscarriage with consent\\n\\n89\\n\\n313\\n\\nCausing miscarriage without consent\\n\\n90\\n\\n314\\n\\nDeath of the mother by act done with intent to cause miscarriage\\n\\n91\\n\\n315\\n\\nPreventing the child from being born alive or causing it to die after birth\\n\\n92\\n\\n316\\n\\nCausing the death of the quick unborn child by an act amounting to culpable homicide\\n\\nNote: A quick child is a fetus that has developed to such a stage that it moves within the womb of the mother. It shall mean an unborn child whose heart is beating, who is experiencing electronically measurable brain waves, who is discernibly moving, and who is so far developed and matured as to be capable of surviving the trauma of birth with the aid of usual medical care and facilities available in this state.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9ad3345e89","n":526,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A 30-year-old man who was found dead was brought in for autopsy. The doctor notices a greyish-white waxy layer over the body with preserved facial features. Which of the following statements is true concerning adipocere formation?","options":["It occurs in hot and dry temperatures","It occurs in cold temperatures","It is a type of body preservation with saponification of fats","Occurs within few hours of death"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. It is a type of body preservation with saponification of fats\\n\\nThe given scenario indicates adipocere formation, which is a late postmortem change where body fats undergo saponification through anaerobic hydrolysis and hydrogenation, resulting in a waxy substance. Therefore, the correct answer is that it is a type of body preservation with saponification of fats.\\n\\nAdipocere\xa0formation or corpse wax, is a\xa0late sign of death, that occurs days to months after death. A wax-like organic substance is formed by the anaerobic hydrolysis or hydrogenation of fat in tissues. It has a greyish-white appearance with a distinctive offensive or sweetish smell.\\n\\nAdipocere formation varies widely, taking as little as three weeks in temperate regions during summer, but typically requires several months for significant changes. In rare cases, the entire body may be affected, with complete conversion of an adult limb taking three to six months.\\n\\nMedicolegal importance is as follows:\\n\\n• It is the surest sign of death\\n• It gives a rough estimate of the time since death\\n• The cause of death can be identified.\\n• In the face, the features are well preserved and therefore used in identification.\\n\\nPostmortem changes are classified based on time:\\n\\n• Immediate signs of death (at the time of somatic or clinical death):\\n\\n• Insensibility and loss of voluntary power\\n• Cessation of respiration\\n• Cessation of circulation\\n\\n• Early signs of death (cellular death that is observed in the first 12 to 24 hours of death):\\n\\n• Pallor and loss of elasticity of the skin\\n• Eye changes\\n• Primary flaccidity of muscles\\n• Algor mortis/Postmortem cooling\\n• Postmortem lividity/Suggillation\\n• Rigor mortis\\n\\n• Late signs of death:\\n\\n• Putrefaction\\n• Adipocere\\n• Mummification","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8258334d4b","n":527,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A 32-year-old woman G3P2L2 came to you in active labor. The labour process was arrested at around cervical dilatation of 8 cm. She was counselled regarding the need for an emergency cesarean section. The baby was delivered by cesarean section and was doing well but the mother developed intractable postpartum hemorrhage. Conservative measures were tried initially but failed. Emergency hysterectomy was done as a life-saving procedure. What are the medicolegal implications of this situation?","options":["Patient could sue the doctor for removing her uterus","Hysterectomy was done as a life saving procedure, so the doctor is legally protected","Consent can be taken from the patient on OT table","Counsellling of patient and attendants is not needed"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hysterectomy was done as a life saving procedure, so the doctor is legally protected\\n\\nAccording to\xa0BNS Section 30\xa0(IPC 92), a doctor is permitted to\xa0perform life-saving procedures\xa0on a patient with\xa0life-threatening injuries. For example, in this case where conservative measures have failed to control intractable postpartum haemorrhage, an emergency hysterectomy can be performed without obtaining consent in order to save the patient\'s life.\\n\\nThe guiding principle is whether the delay in treatment in order to obtain consent would result in harm to the patient. This is the doctrine of anticipation. Under some circumstances like emergencies, informed consent may be neither possible nor necessary.\\n\\nBNS Section 30 (IPC 92) states that nothing is an offence by reason of any harm which it may cause to a person for whose benefit it is done in good faith, even without that person’s consent, if the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent, or if that person is incapable of giving consent, and has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ea1b63ba79","n":528,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A 36-year-old man died following a RTA. The doctor informed the police officer and shifted the body to the mortuary. Autopsy in this case will be conducted at the request of _______\\n\\nRTA - Road Traffic Accident","options":["Forensic expert","Investigating police officer","Defense lawyer","Prosecution lawyer"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Investigating police officer\\n\\nIn the case of the 36-year-old man who died following an RTA, the autopsy will be conducted at the request of the investigating police officer.\\n\\nAccording to BNSS Section 194 (Section 174 CrPC), a police inquest is the standard procedure for investigating unnatural deaths in India. This process is typically carried out by a police officer, not below the rank of Senior Head Constable, who is responsible for conducting a preliminary inquiry into the circumstances surrounding the death.\\n\\nDuring the police inquest, the investigating officer visits the scene of the incident, examines the body, and documents any visible injuries or signs of trauma. The officer’s report will detail these findings and assess whether the death appears to be suicidal, homicidal, accidental, or suspicious. If the situation warrants, the body will be sent for a postmortem examination to be conducted by a qualified medical professional to determine the exact cause of death.\\n\\nIn certain cases, a magistrate inquest [BNSS 196 (Section 176 CrPC)] may be conducted instead of, or in addition to, a police inquest. This is particularly true in cases involving deaths under special circumstances, such as lock-up deaths, deaths while in police custody, custodial rape, death in a psychiatric hospital, dowry death or deaths resulting from police actions. The magistrate inquest is carried out by a District Magistrate, Subdivisional Magistrate, or another authorized magistrate, and involves a more formal judicial inquiry into the cause of death.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-716bfbb15e","n":529,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A 5-year-old child has consumed overdose of iron syrup, around 60mg/kg. Which among the following is contraindicated?","options":["BAL","Deferoxamine","Calcium EDETATE","DTPA"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. BAL\\n\\nThe clinical scenario points to iron toxicity, which is managed with deferoxamine (also known as\xa0desferrioxamine), calcium EDETATE and DTPA whereas Dimercaprol, also known as BAL (British anti-Lewisite), is used to treat arsenic, mercury poisoning, and Wilson\'s disease.\\n\\nLarge amounts of ferrous salts can be fatal in children. Initial symptoms include abdominal pain, diarrhea, vomiting, and gastrointestinal bleeding. In severe cases, cyanosis and hyperventilation due to acidosis can develop. This can sometimes be followed by an asymptomatic phase. Later, hypoglycemia and hepatic and renal failure can develop. Pyloric stenosis and gastric scarring can develop due to its corrosive nature. Ultimately, if left untreated, the child can die.\\n\\nThe gastric contents can be used for an iron color test to estimate the iron concentration in plasma. As iron tablets are radiopaque, an X-ray can be used to assess the number of pills taken. Deferoxamine (Option B)\xa0is selectively used as a chelating agent to manage cases of acute iron poisoning, hemochromatosis, and patients with iron overload from repetitive blood transfusions. Desferrioxamine can be administered intramuscularly, intravenously, or subcutaneously but not orally. Side effects include pruritus, wheals, rash, and anaphylaxis.\\n\\nDeferiprone and deferasirox are other oral iron chelators used to treat iron overload in thalassemia patients.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption C : Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) is used for acute lead poisoning. It is not suitable for the management of chronic lead poisoning. The calcium salt of EDTA can be used in the management of heavy metal poisonings where the metal in question has a higher affinity for EDTA than calcium in vivo. Because of their lower affinity to EDTA, mercury and arsenic cannot be effectively chelated.\\n\\nOption D : Diethylenetriamene pentaacetate (DTPA) is a chelating agents which works by binding and holding on to radioactive materials (plutonium, americium, and curium) and poisons like iron. Calcium DTPA is well tolerated and effective as desferrioxamine in mobilising iron than Zinc DTPA.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e65515de1d","n":530,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A large number of people who consumed cheap alcohol presented to the emergency department with abdominal pain, confusion, and decreased vision. Methyl alcohol level in blood was detected to be around 20 mg %. On assessment which of the following metabolites will be found in blood?","options":["Glycolic acid and Glyoxylic acid","Formic acid and lactic acid","Glycolic acid and lactic acid","Oxalic acid and formic acid"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Formic acid and lactic acid\\n\\nThe given case scenario is suggestive of methanol poisoning. In these cases, formic acid and lactic acid are typically the metabolites found in the blood.\\n\\nMethyl alcohol, or methanol, is commonly present in cheap or illicit alcoholic beverages due to its lower cost compared to ethyl alcohol. These inexpensive alcohols may be improperly distilled or adulterated, leading to methanol contamination. When ingested, methanol is absorbed into the bloodstream and metabolized by the liver, where it is first converted into formaldehyde, a highly toxic intermediate. Formaldehyde is then further oxidized into formic acid, which is responsible for many of the severe symptoms associated with methanol poisoning.\\n\\nThe accumulation of formic acid leads to significant metabolic acidosis, which manifests as confusion, abdominal pain, and visual disturbances. The visual symptoms can be particularly severe, including blurred vision or even blindness, due to the toxic effects of formic acid on the optic nerve. In addition to formic acid, methanol\'s disruption of normal oxidative enzyme activity results in the buildup of lactic acid, contributing further to acidosis. Brain edema, acute renal failure, and severe metabolic derangement are also seen.\\n\\nThe principles of treatment for this condition predominantly involve using fomepizole or ethanol to inhibit the metabolic conversion of these relatively non-toxic alcohols to their toxic metabolites.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-951a7d9335","n":531,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A man sexually aroused when he wears his female partner\'s dress and heels. He denies getting attracted to male. What is this condition called?","options":["Gay sexual preference","Transvestic fetishism","Gender dysphoria","Voyeurism"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Transvestic fetishism\\n\\nThe given scenario is suggestive of transvestic fetishism.\\n\\nIt\xa0is a\xa0paraphilia characterized by recurrent sexually arousing fantasies, sexual urges, or behavior involving the wearing of clothes of the opposite sex and appearing as a member of the opposite sex. It is also called\xa0transvestic fetishism. It is usually seen in males who obtain sexual pleasure by wearing female clothes.\\n\\nFetishistic transvestism is a paraphilia whereas transvestism is a gender identity disorder.\xa0There is no hormonal/genital abnormality in fetishistic transvestism.\\n\\nOption A : Gay sexual preference is due to attraction to people of one\'s own gender.\\n\\nOption C: Gender dysphoria is a term that\xa0describes a sense of unease that a person has due to\xa0a mismatch between their biological sex and their gender identity. This sense of unease or dissatisfaction can lead to depression and anxiety and can have a harmful impact on daily life.\\n\\nOption D : Voyeurism/Peeping Tom/Scotophilia is sexual gratification by secretly watching others getting undressed, taking a bath, performing sexual intercourse, etc.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a5ae35fd8d","n":532,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A patient presented to the OPD with findings as shown below. Identify the type of poisoning in this patient.\\n\\nOPD- outpatient department","options":["Chronic malnutrition","Copper","Mercury","Arsenic"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Arsenic\\n\\nThe image showing the raindrop pattern of skin pigmentation and the Aldrich-Mees lines on the fingernails of the patient are suggestive of chronic arsenic poisoning.\\n\\nArsenic in its metallic form is not poisonous, but when it unites with oxygen, it forms poisonous vapours of arsenic trioxide. These interfere with cellular respiration, damaging mitochondrial enzymes. It is present in fruit sprays, copper and gold mineral deposits, weed killers, insecticides, rat poisons, wallpapers, and artificial flowers.\\n\\nThere are several types of arsenic poisoning:\\n\\n• Fulminant\xa0- When massive doses are rapidly absorbed, death occurs in 1–3 hours due to shock and peripheral vascular failure.\\n• Gastrointestinal\xa0- This is the most common form of acute poisoning and resembles bacterial food poisoning. There is a constriction in the throat and difficulty in swallowing. The stools are dark-colored, foul smelling, and bloody initially and later take a colorless odorless watery form similar to the rice-water stools of cholera.\\n• Chronic poisoning - It occurs due to repeated daily exposure. The main features include finely mottled, raindrop pigmentation that appears on the skin of flexures, temples, eyelids, and neck. In prolonged contact, hyperkeratosis of the palms and soles occurs along with the development of a transverse white line in the fingernails called the\xa0Aldrich-Mees lines.\\n\\nTests to detect arsenic poisoning:\\n\\n• 24-hour urinary excretion of arsenic\\n• Detection of arsenic by atomic absorption spectroscopy\\n• Marsh\xa0and Reinsch\'s tests are now considered obsolete.\\n\\nThe preferred treatment option is freshly prepared hydrated ferric oxide, which converts toxic arsenic to non-toxic ferric arsenite. DMSA (Succimer), British anti-Lewisite (BAL), and penicillamine\xa0can also be used.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Chronic malnutrition leads to distinct skin and nail changes depending on the specific condition. In kwashiorkor, there is flaky paint dermatosis with reddish-brown scaling, thin and sparse hair featuring a \\"flag sign,\\" and soft nails. In marasmus, patients exhibit dry, wrinkled skin, slow-growing brittle hair, and nails, often accompanied by growth retardation.\\n\\nOption B: Acute copper poisoning is due to inhalation of a large amount of copper dust or fumes, which leads to upper respiratory tract irritation, conjunctivitis, and sinus irritation. Contact with the skin may lead to irritant contact dermatitis and a greenish-blue discolouration of the skin.\\n\\nIn chronic poisoning, symptoms mostly resemble those of\xa0Wilson\'s disease, mainly including gradual anaemia, peripheral neuritis, and muscle atrophy.\\n\\nOption C: Acute mercury poisoning presents with corrosive bronchitis with fever, chills, and dyspnea, which may progress to pulmonary edema and fibrosis. The symptom profile in children is similar to that of Kawasaki disease\xa0(mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome). Chronic exposure to elemental mercury leads to a classic triad of gingivitis, tremors, and neuropsychiatric changes.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/fbdffbad974fabeee4d7.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-5f4cb56871","n":533,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A person comes to emergency after consumption of a substance, the form and dose of which is not known to the relatives. The patient has tachypnea and hypotension. On metabolic assessment, high anion gap metabolic acidosis is noted with hypocalcemia. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Methyl alcohol","Ethylene glycol","Datura","Digoxin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ethylene glycol\\n\\nThe patient\'s presentation of tachypnea, hypotension, high anion gap metabolic acidosis, and hypocalcemia, is suggestive of ethylene glycol poisoning.\\n\\nEthylene glycol is a clear, colourless, and odourless liquid commonly used as an antifreeze. It is known for its bitter-sweet taste and is absorbed mainly through ingestion rather than skin contact. Upon ingestion, ethylene glycol is metabolized in the liver to several toxic metabolites, including glycolic acid and oxalic acid, which contribute to its harmful effects.\\n\\nInitially, ethylene glycol poisoning can cause symptoms such as vomiting, lethargy, and ataxia, progressing to severe effects including tachycardia, tachypnea, and circulatory collapse within 12-24 hours. As the condition progresses, typically within one to three days, patients may experience hypocalcemia, oliguria, tubular necrosis, and acute kidney injury (AKI). A notable feature of ethylene glycol poisoning is the formation of calcium oxalate crystals in the urine, which can lead to renal failure.\\n\\nThe postmortem findings\xa0often include cerebral edema, chemical meningoencephalitis, and significant damage to the liver and kidneys. Oxalate crystals may be observed in the brain, spinal cord, and kidneys, further confirming the diagnosis.\\n\\nFor treatment, immediate measures include gastric lavage, activated charcoal, and administration of ethanol or 4-methyl pyrazole (fomepizole), which inhibit the enzyme alcohol dehydrogenase to prevent further metabolism of ethylene glycol into toxic compounds. Additional treatments such as intravenous sodium bicarbonate and calcium gluconate are used to address metabolic acidosis and hypocalcemia, respectively, while hemodialysis is indicated in severe cases with significant renal impairment or high serum ethylene glycol concentrations.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Methyl alcohol (Methanol) and Ethylene glycol poisoning can present very similarly. However, the key distinguishing feature of methanol poisoning is the presence of eye symptoms (as opposed to kidney damage in ethylene glycol) - like blurring of vision/loss of vision due to retinal damage by formate metabolites.\\n\\nMethanol--Alcohol Dehydrogenase (ADH) ⇒ Formaldehyde ⇒ (Retinal damage)\\n\\nEthylene glycol--Alcohol Dehydrogenase (ADH) ⇒ Glyoxyaldehyde ⇒ (Kidney damage)\\n\\nOption C: Datura is used as a stupefying poison before robbery, kidnapping, and rape. The active ingredients include the anticholinergic alkaloids atropine, scopolamine, and hyoscyamine. The seeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked. Toxic manifestations\xa0include dilatation of pupils along with loss of accommodation, diplopia, dryness of the mouth and throat, unquenchable thirst, dysphagia, dilatation of cutaneous blood vessels, dysarthria, drunken gait, delirium, drowsiness and death due to respiratory or cardiac failure.\\n\\nOption D: Digoxin toxicity can occur in the elderly and patients with renal impairment and presents with nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, confusion, blurred vision with a characteristic yellowing of vision (compared to the “starry night” painting), and irregular heart rhythms. Overdose and resultant arrhythmias are managed with anti-digoxin Fab fragments (Digibind or Digifab).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4a95ace682","n":534,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A police officer brought a child to the hospital. There is redness and tenderness around perianal region. A rectal swab is taken and the image given below is the finding. What test is it indicative of?","options":["Barberio test","Florence test","Teichmann test","Takayama test"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Barberio test\\n\\nThe test done in this scenario is the Barberio test.\\n\\nBarberio test is used to detect seminal fluid.\xa0When a saturated aqueous or alcoholic\xa0picric\xa0acid solution\xa0is added to the spermatic fluid, yellow needle-shaped rhombic crystals of spermine picrate are produced.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: The Florence test detects semen. If semen is present, dark brown rhombic or needle-shaped crystals of choline iodide appear immediately.\\n\\nOption C: The Teichmann test (hemin crystal test) is used to detect bloodstains. If bloodstain is present, faint yellowish-red to brownish-black rhombic crystals of hemin or hematin chloride appear. When hydrogen peroxide is added, the hematin crystals give bubbles of gas.\\n\\nOption D: \xa0Hemochromogen crystals are seen in the Takayama test. It is a microchemical test used to detect bloodstains. The stain used is Takayama reagent (Sodium hydroxide, pyridine, glucose). If bloodstain is present, pink feathery crystals of hemochromogen or reduced alkaline hematin appear in a few minutes. The test is negative if the crystals are not formed in 30 minutes. This test is more reliable and gives positive results with old stains also but not a definitive test to detect blood stains.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/88d86a9a8c5738978a4e.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-9a634b3a87","n":535,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A pregnant female comes to a gynaecologist, who sends her for USG. On USG, twin pregnancy was noted with about a one-month difference in the age of fetuses. Which of the following is true with regard to this patient?\\n\\nUSG- Ultrasonogram","options":["Superfecundation","Superfetation","Supposititious child","Posthumous child"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Superfetation\\n\\nThe above phenomenon is known as\xa0superfetation. It\xa0occurs when there is fertilization of 2 different ova released in different menstrual cycles. The consequence is two children are born at the same time and one of them might be mature while another might be immature or two fully developed fetuses are born at intervals ranging from one to three months apart.\\n\\nOvulation usually stops with the onset of pregnancy, but in a few individuals, ovulation may be possible till the first trimester, after which the decidual cavity is obliterated. The fertilization of the newly released ovum in a woman who is already pregnant may take place following coitus.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Superfecundation is the fertilization of\xa02 different ova\xa0in the\xa0same menstrual cycle\xa0by separate acts of coitus. Heteropaternal superfecundation is used to refer to instances of two different males fathering fraternal twins. This leads to the possibility of twins also being half-siblings. This\xa0may raise doubts regarding adultery and infidelity.\\n\\nOption C: A supposititious child is also known as a fictitious child. In such cases, a woman pretends to be pregnant and substitutes a living child as her own, or may substitute a male child for her female child, or abortion. This is done to claim property. This can be identified by verifying the signs of pregnancy and delivery in the woman and by DNA fingerprinting.\\n\\nOption D: A posthumous child is a child born alive after the death of his or her father who was conceived before the death of the father.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bfcb9b7710","n":536,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A sexual assault survivor had to testify in court and an in-camera trial was conducted. What is an \\"in-camera\\" trial?","options":["Open court proceedings","Closed court proceedings","Proceedings recorded on camera","Proceeding held in a different location"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Closed court proceedings\\n\\n\'In-camera trial\' is a closed court proceeding in which the case is heard in private i.e. in court where general public is excluded.\\n\\nBNSS sections related to court proceeding are tabulated below:\\n\\nBNSS\\n\\nCrPC\\nDeals with\\n\\n63-71\\n\\n61-69\\n\\nSummons\\n\\n181\\n\\n162\\n\\nOral statements made to the police and recorded by the police should not be signed\\n\\n195\\n\\n175\\n\\nPower to summon persons by a police officer\\n\\n196\\n\\n176\\n\\nMagistrate inquest\\n\\n350\\n\\n312\\n\\nExpenses of complaints and witnesses\\n\\n366\\n366(2)\\n\\n327\\n\\nCriminal court is generally deemed to be an open court.\\nIn-camera trial for rape\\n\\n389\\n\\n350\\n\\nSummary procedure for punishment for non-attendance by a witness in obedience to the summons\\n\\n395\\n\\n357\\n\\nOrder to pay compensation","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-870d106567","n":537,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A woman gave birth to a child 8 months after the death of her husband. The in-laws claim that the child is not their son, however, the DNA testing confirms the paternity. The child is known as ____________","options":["Illegitimate child","Posthumous child","Surrogate child","Supposititious child"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Posthumous child\\n\\nThe child delivered after the death of the biological father, or a child delivered after the death of the biological mother, usually when delivered by a cesarean section is a posthumous child.\\n\\nLegal issues involved with a posthumous child are legitimacy, inheritance of property, and the case of compensation for slander against the mother.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Illegitimate child is a child born out of wedlock i.e. when the parents are unmarried.\\n\\nOption C: A surrogate\xa0child is born out of a pregnancy in which a woman gives birth to a baby for a person who is not able to have children.\\n\\nOption D: Supposititious child or fictitious child is when a woman pretends to go through pregnancy and delivery. Later, she produces a living child on her own to obtain money or claim the property.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d63e6e7f74","n":538,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"An 18-year-old boy was brought by police, and accused of sexual assault, but the boy claims to be a minor (16 years old). Which radiographs will you order to solve this discrepancy?","options":["Elbow and hip joint","Elbow and ankle joint","Shoulder and skull joint","Wrist and knee joint"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Wrist and knee joint\\n\\nWrist and knee joint radiographs will be ordered to solve this discrepancy as lower end of radius and upper end of tibia fuse at 18-19 years. If these joints are fused, It is certain that the boy is more than 18 years and his claim is false.\\n\\nBone/ joint\\nMax age of fusion\\n\\nWrist joint centers (lower end of ulna and radius)\\n\\n19 years\\n\\nKnee joint centres\\n\\n(lower end of femur, upper end of tibia and upper end of fibula)\\n\\n20 years\\n\\nShoulder joint (head of humerus, greater tuberosity and lesser tuberosity)\\n19 years\\n\\nHip joint (head of femur, greater trochanter)\\n17 years\\n\\nElbow joint centers (lower end of humerus, upper end of radius, olecranon to ulna)\\n17years\\n\\nAnkle joint\\n17 years","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a76b818b9a","n":539,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"During the post mortem examination following finding is seen. What is this indicative of ____________","options":["Choking","Throttling","Hanging","Smothering"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Smothering\\n\\nAbrasions around the mouth, cheeks, lips or lesions within the lips may reliably indicate smothering.\\n\\nThe non-specific signs of death due to asphyxia:\\n\\n• Petechial haemorrhages\xa0in the face, eyebrows, eyelids, forehead, ears, conjunctiva, sclera - When they occur in the visceral pleura, they are called\xa0Tardieu’s spots\\n• Emphysematous, congested lungs with serosanguinous fluid exudates in alveoli.\\n• Cyanosis or dark-coloured blood due to accumulation of CO2.\\n• Congestion of the larynx with slight oozing\\n\\nPressure marks on the face can occur as a part of normal postmortem changes. Circumoral and circumnasal pallor can be caused merely by passive pressure of the dependent head after death.\\n\\nNote: The coexistence of these findings, in themselves, does not prove that death resulted from mechanical asphyxia. All these phenomena are non-specific and are in no way peculiar to this mode of death. They are of no value in proving that death resulted from mechanical asphyxia.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/59e64d9b897bd77673a5.webp"]},{"bankId":"qb-0440143812","n":540,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"In the case of drowning, following autopsy, an organism was isolated from the water present in the internal organs. Which of the following is true regarding the isolated organism?","options":["Present only in the bone marrow","Dried outer wall contains silica and chlorophyll","It is a microscopic unicellular bacteria","It is not heat and acid resistant"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Dried outer wall contains silica and chlorophyll\\n\\nDiatoms enter pulmonary circulation on antemortem drowning. Diatoms are a class of microscopic unicellular algae (option C) found suspended in most natural waters. Their cell walls are impregnated with silica, chlorophyll, diatomic, and a brown pigment.\\n\\nThe drowning fluid and the particles in it, e.g., diatoms and plankton, pass from the ruptured alveolar wall into lymph channels and pulmonary veins and thus enter the left heart. Only a live body with circulation could transport diatoms from the lungs to the brain, bone marrow, liver and other viscera, and skeletal muscle. They are also found in bile and urine. Bone marrow is highly suitable and reliable.\\n\\nIsolation of diatoms involves acid digestion of tissue - bone marrow, lung, blood, and kidneys with subsequent centrifugation and the final residue is aspirated and examined for diatoms, which are birefringent, using dark ground illumination. A water sample is collected at the time of body retrieval in a clean container and the similarity of different species of diatom is compared. Diatoms are both heat and acid-resistant (option D).\\n\\n• The presence of diatoms in the lung, brain, liver, kidneys, and bone marrow (option A) of the femur (best site for analysis) or humerus is suggestive proof of antemortem drowning.\\n• Since diatoms resist putrefaction, the diatom test may have some value in the examination of decomposed bodies.\\n• The test is negative in postmortem submersion and in dry drowning.\\n\\nDiatoms are ubiquitous in the environment and may enter circulation via the gastrointestinal tract (as contaminants of foods)or via the respiratory tract (which are present in the air). Still, the diatom test is considered the gold standard for the diagnosis of typical drowning.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/df11d218632b88088f85.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-20224fc584","n":541,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"Rigor mortis is well established in 12-24 hours after death. What is the order of appearance of rigor mortis?","options":["Toe to head","Head to toe","Centre to periphery","All the body parts simultaneously"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Head to toe\\n\\nThe order of appearance of rigor mortis is from head to toe.\\n\\nRigor mortis (cadaveric rigidity or death stiffening) refers to the stiffening and shortening of muscles following death. The basic sequence of the rigor mortis begins from the head and continues downwards the body. It disappears in the same order of appearance. Nysten\'s rule describes the sequential onset of rigor mortis in the various muscle groups.\\n\\nThe muscles are affected in the given order:\\n\\n1. Involuntary muscles - Heart (left chamber of the heart is affected first)\\n2. Eyelids\\n3. Neck and lower jaw\\n4. Face\\n5. Chest\\n6. Upper extremities\\n7. Abdomen\\n8. Legs\\n9. Fingers and toes","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e87c5d1bbd","n":542,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"The following findings were noted during the autopsy of a patient with a gunshot injury. What is the likely range of the gunshot?","options":["Cannot be opined","Distant range","Contact range","Close range"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Close range\\n\\nThe likely range of the gunshot injury shown above is close range, characterized by a circular wound with blackening and tattooing.\\n\\nTo determine the range of firing, the following parameters are used:\\n\\n• The entry wound size and shape\\n• Blast effect – Muzzle end imprint and edge eversion\\n• Blackening, Tattooing, Singeing of hair, Abrasion collar, and Grease collar\\n• Hair should be preserved to determine the range of firing which can be examined for carbonization/desiccation\xa0in close range, also from lead deposition around the wound to assess the range of firing.\\n\\nBlackening occurs due to the deposit of smoke and can be removed with a wet cloth. This can be seen up to around 15 cm of firing. Whereas, tattooing occurs due to the deposition of unburnt powder particles\xa0and are difficult to remove. They are also called stippling or peppering. This is seen up to 50 cm of firing.\\n\\nAbrasion collar:\xa0Gyroscopic movement of the bullet, the skin\'s elasticity, and the grease and dirt particles on the bullet\'s surface are the major causative factors of abrasion and grease collar. They are seen on the skin around the wound of entry at all ranges. Depending on the angle of entry made by the bullet, i.e.\\n\\n• A perpendicular entry of the bullet develops the abrasion collar of a rounded shape.\\n• An acute angular entry of the bullet develops the abrasion collar of an oblique or oval shape.\\n• Some contusion present in the abraded collar is known as contusion collar\\n\\nGrease collar:\xa0Mostly seen on the clothing and is seen as a black deposit, sharply outlined as if printed (better seen on light colour clothes and fair-skinned individuals). Its medicolegal importance is that it helps derive\xa0information such as entry wound\xa0and direction of firing.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/99f0036e89e625b168d1.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-555bb15237","n":543,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"The railway police has bought a person with suspected poisoning. On examination, he was confused with slurred speech, hot skin, dry mouth, dilated pupils. What is the probable poisoning ?","options":["Cocaine","Datura","Cannabis","Alcohol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Datura\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0Datura poisoning.\\n\\nThe two species are jimson weed (Datura stramonium) and angel’s trumpet (Datura candida).\\n\\nSeeds or other parts of the plant can be ingested or smoked. Poisoning leads to\xa0delirium, hallucinations, and seizures. Additionally, other classic anticholinergic effects, such as mydriasis, tachycardia, dry mouth and skin, blurred vision, urinary retention, and hyperthermia may present.\\n\\nGastric emptying is often delayed, and the small, plentiful seeds can become trapped among the gastrointestinal folds after ingestion. Thus gastric decontamination can be an important therapy.\\n\\nPhysostigmine, a reversible acetylcholine esterase antagonist, is an effective treatment for severe anticholinergic poisoning.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2d6c80bbdc","n":544,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"‘Latrodectism’, characterized by local pain and sweating, is caused by the bite of which one of the following animals?","options":["Widow spider","King cobra","Lonomia caterpillar","Solenopsis ants"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Widow spider\\n\\nLatrodectism is the clinical syndrome caused by envenomation from Latrodectus species (widow spiders), especially the black widow spider.\\n\\nLatrodectus venom contains α-latrotoxin, causing excessive neurotransmitter release.\\n\\nTypical features include:\\n\\n• Severe local pain\\n• Sweating\\n• Muscle cramps and rigidity\\n• Abdominal pain\\n• Autonomic disturbances\\n\\nWhy not other options?\\n\\n• B. King cobra: Causes predominantly neurotoxic paralysis due to postsynaptic neurotoxins.\\n• C. Lonomia caterpillar: Causes hemorrhagic syndrome due to venom-induced coagulopathy.\\n• D. Solenopsis ants (fire ants): Cause painful pustular skin lesions and local inflammatory reactions.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7a100621ee","n":545,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 1-year-old infant accidentally ingests an organophosphate pesticide. Which drug reverses acetylcholinesterase enzyme blockade?","options":["Atropine","Pralidoxime","Glycopyrrolate","Physostigmine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pralidoxime\\n\\nMechanism of Action of Pralidoxime (2-PAM)\\n\\n• Pralidoxime is a cholinesterase reactivator used in the treatment of organophosphate (OP) poisoning. It restores the activity of acetylcholinesterase (AChE) by removing the organophosphate bound to the enzyme before the process of aging occurs.\\n\\nStepwise Mechanism\\n\\n1. Organophosphate poisoning\\n\\n• Organophosphates phosphorylate the active serine residue at the esteratic site of acetylcholinesterase.\\n• This inactivates AChE, preventing the breakdown of acetylcholine.\\n•\\n\\nAcetylcholine accumulates at muscarinic, nicotinic, and CNS synapses, producing a cholinergic crisis.\\n\\n•\\n\\nBinding of pralidoxime\\n\\n•\\n\\nPralidoxime binds to the unoccupied anionic site of the inhibited acetylcholinesterase enzyme.\\n\\n•\\n\\nIts oxime (-NOH) group is a potent nucleophile.\\n\\n•\\n\\nRemoval of the organophosphate\\n\\n•\\n\\nThe oxime group attacks the phosphorus atom of the enzyme-bound organophosphate.\\n\\n• The organophosphate is displaced from the active serine residue and forms a stable pralidoxime–organophosphate complex.\\n• This frees the enzyme and reactivates acetylcholinesterase, allowing normal hydrolysis of acetylcholine.\\n\\nImportant distinction:\\n\\n• Atropine blocks muscarinic effects only\\n• Pralidoxime reverses the enzyme inhibition itself","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-84a223c364","n":546,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 12-year-old boy is brought to the emergency in an unconscious state with a history of shallow breathing and increasing drowsiness noticed for two hours. He is noticed to have a dry mouth, cold extremities, is limp and has pin point pupils. The emergency physician suspects poisoning. Which one of the following antidotes should be administered?","options":["Flumazenil","Adrenaline","Pralidoxime","Naloxone"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Naloxone\\n\\nThe child has classic features of opioid poisoning:\\n\\n• Pinpoint pupils (miosis)\\n• Respiratory depression with shallow breathing\\n• CNS depression → drowsiness/unconsciousness\\n• Cold extremities\\n\\nNaloxone is a competitive opioid receptor antagonist and is the antidote of choice for suspected opioid toxicity, particularly when respiratory depression is present.\\n\\nWhy not other options?\\n\\n• A. Flumazenil: Antidote for benzodiazepine poisoning; may precipitate seizures and withdrawal in certain patients.\\n• B. Adrenaline: Used for anaphylaxis and cardiac arrest, not opioid poisoning.\\n• C. Pralidoxime: Reactivates acetylcholinesterase in organophosphate poisoning; typically associated with cholinergic features such as salivation, lacrimation, bronchorrhea, and miosis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-65a152116c","n":547,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 13-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after accidentally ingesting tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) tablets. On examination, he is found to have dry mouth, mydriasis, tachycardia, and palpitations. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment in this case?","options":["Physostigmine","Lithium","Naloxone","Flumazenil"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Physostigmine\\n\\nTricyclic antidepressant (TCA) poisoning produces an anticholinergic toxidrome characterized by dry mouth, mydriasis, tachycardia, and CNS disturbances. In such cases, physostigmine can be used to reverse central and peripheral anticholinergic effects.\\n\\n• TCAs block muscarinic receptors leading to anticholinergic symptoms\\n• Features include dry skin, dilated pupils, tachycardia, delirium, and arrhythmias\\n• Physostigmine is a reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that crosses the blood-brain barrier and counteracts anticholinergic toxicity\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Naloxone is used for opioid poisoning\\n• Flumazenil is used for benzodiazepine overdose\\n• Lithium has no role in acute poisoning management here","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0083c23f13","n":548,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 16-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department by her mother with an alleged history of abdominal pain. Ultrasonography reveals a 16-week fetus. On further questioning, she discloses a history of forceful sexual assault by a 22-year-old neighbour. As the medical officer, all of the following are medicolegally significant except:","options":["Taking consent for medicolegal examination from parents","Preserving fetal tissues for DNA fingerprinting","MTP can be done on the opinion of one doctor","Inform the police"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Taking consent for medicolegal examination from parents\\n\\nIn cases of sexual assault involving a minor (<18 years), the victim is legally capable of providing informed consent for her own medical examination if she has the capacity to understand the nature and consequences of the examination. Therefore, parental consent is not mandatory for the medicolegal examination of an adolescent who is able to provide informed consent. Such cases are governed by the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, and reporting to the police is mandatory.\\n\\n• Taking consent from parents is not mandatory if the 16-year-old survivor is competent to give informed consent for medical examination. Consent should be obtained from the survivor herself.\\n\\nWhy are the other options correct?\\n\\n• B. Preserving fetal tissues for DNA fingerprinting: Essential medicolegal evidence for establishing paternity and linking the accused.\\n• C. MTP can be done on the opinion of one doctor: For a 16-week pregnancy, termination is permissible on the opinion of one registered medical practitioner, provided it is in accordance with the provisions of the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) Act.\\n• D. Inform the police: Mandatory reporting is required under the POCSO Act for all sexual offences involving individuals below 18 years of age.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-40324fd724","n":549,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 21-year-old married woman with 10 weeks of gestation presents for medical termination of pregnancy (MTP). Written informed consent for the procedure should be obtained from:","options":["Only the woman","Both the woman and her husband","One of the woman’s parents","The police"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Only the woman\\n\\nUnder the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) Act, 1971 (amended in 2021) in India, consent of the pregnant woman alone is sufficient for termination of pregnancy if she is 18 years or older and mentally competent. Husband’s consent is not required, irrespective of marital status.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Both the woman and her husband: Incorrect. Husband’s consent has no legal requirement under the MTP Act.\\n• C. One of the woman’s parents: Required only if the woman is <18 years of age or is mentally ill (guardian’s consent).\\n• D. The police: Police permission is not required for MTP, even in pregnancies resulting from rape.\\n\\n| Patient | Whose Consent is Required?\\n\\n| ------- | --------------------------\\n\\n| Adult (≥18 years), mentally competent | Only the woman’s consent\\n\\n| Minor (<18 years) | Guardian’s consent\\n\\n| Mentally ill woman | Guardian’s consent","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8d48709347","n":550,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 23-year-old girl was brought dead to the emergency department. She was found dead in the bathroom. Cherry red coloured post-mortem staining was present. She had been complaining of light-headedness and giddiness for the past few months. On investigation, the bathroom was found to be ill-ventilated, and a geyser had been installed recently. In toxicological analysis, an irrespirable gas was detected. Which of the following gases is most likely responsible for her death?","options":["Carbon monoxide","Carbon dioxide","Hydrogen sulfide","Methane"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Carbon monoxide\\n\\nCarbon monoxide (CO) poisoning should be suspected in patients with cherry-red postmortem lividity, especially in closed, poorly ventilated environments (e.g., bathrooms with gas geysers).\\n\\n• Cherry-red discoloration of skin/postmortem staining (due to carboxyhemoglobin)\\n• Odorless, colorless, non-irritant gas → “silent killer”\\n• Symptoms: headache, dizziness, giddiness, confusion → collapse and death\\n\\nPathophysiology:\\n\\n• CO has 200–250\xd7 affinity for hemoglobin\\n• Forms carboxyhemoglobin → ↓ oxygen delivery → tissue hypoxia\\n• Also inhibits cytochrome oxidase → cellular respiration failure\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• CO₂: Asphyxiant but no cherry-red color\\n• Hydrogen sulfide: Rotten egg smell, greenish discoloration\\n• Methane: Simple asphyxiant, no specific discoloration","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ba31cda027","n":551,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 24-year-old female is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after ingesting a large quantity of aspirin tablets. She complains of ringing in the ears, rapid breathing, and agitation. Arterial blood gas analysis demonstrates a mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis. What is the most appropriate initial treatment to enhance aspirin elimination from the body?","options":["Gastric lavage followed by activated charcoal","Intravenous sodium bicarbonate to achieve urinary alkalinization","Intravenous N-acetylcysteine infusion","Intravenous ammonium chloride administration"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Intravenous sodium bicarbonate to achieve urinary alkalinization\\n\\nAcute salicylate (aspirin) poisoning initially stimulates the medullary respiratory center, producing respiratory alkalosis, followed by accumulation of organic acids and uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation, leading to high anion gap metabolic acidosis.\\n\\nThe patient has classic features of acute salicylate poisoning:\\n\\n• Tinnitus\\n• Hyperventilation\\n• Agitation\\n• Mixed respiratory alkalosis + metabolic acidosis\\n\\nIntravenous sodium bicarbonate:\\n\\n• Increases urinary pH to 7.5–8.0\\n• Converts salicylic acid to its ionized form (salicylate)\\n• Prevents tubular reabsorption\\n• Enhances renal elimination\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Gastric lavage followed by activated charcoal: Activated charcoal may be useful within a few hours of ingestion, but urinary alkalinization is the preferred initial therapy to enhance elimination in symptomatic salicylate poisoning. Gastric lavage is rarely indicated.\\n• C. Intravenous N-acetylcysteine infusion: Antidote for acetaminophen (paracetamol) poisoning, not aspirin toxicity.\\n• D. Intravenous ammonium chloride administration: Acidifies urine, decreasing salicylate excretion and worsening toxicity; therefore, it is contraindicated.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ebc3bd8a40","n":552,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 28-year-old man has accused his coworker of sodomy. Which test should be performed within 24 hours on the victim to confirm sodomy?","options":["Collect rectal swabs","Collect oral swabs","Anal discharge","Nail scrapings"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Collect rectal swabs\\n\\nIn cases of alleged anal sexual assault (sodomy), rectal (anal) swabs should be collected as early as possible, preferably within 24 hours, to detect semen, spermatozoa, seminal acid phosphatase, prostate-specific antigen (PSA/p30), and DNA evidence. Early collection maximizes the likelihood of recovering forensic evidence before it is lost due to defecation, washing, or degradation.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Collect rectal swabs: ✔ Correct. Most important forensic specimen in suspected anal penetration.\\n• B. Collect oral swabs: Indicated only when oral sexual assault is alleged.\\n• C. Anal discharge: May be present but is not a standardized forensic specimen; swabs are required for laboratory analysis.\\n• D. Nail scrapings: Useful for recovering the assailant’s DNA in cases involving struggle or scratching, but they do not confirm anal penetration.\\n\\nHigh-yield Pearl:\\n\\n• Sexual assault evidence collection should be performed as early as possible.\\n• Anal assault → Rectal swabs\\n• Oral assault → Oral swabs\\n• Vaginal assault → High vaginal/cervical swabs\\n• Always collect DNA samples, clothing, and document injuries while maintaining the chain of custody.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a858449348","n":553,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 38-year-old man of Afghan origin arrived in India and was found dead in his hotel room in Delhi. At autopsy, multiple oval-shaped plastic/latex packets containing narcotic substances were found in the stomach. Rupture of these packets caused fatal toxicity. What is this practice called?","options":["Body masking","Body packing","Body stuffing","Body pushing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Body packing\\n\\nBody packing is the deliberate swallowing or insertion of multiple well-wrapped packets of illicit drugs (e.g., cocaine, heroin, opioids) into body cavities, most commonly the gastrointestinal tract, for the purpose of smuggling across international borders. Rupture of a packet can result in massive drug absorption and fatal overdose.\\n\\n• Body packing involves planned concealment of drugs in multiple securely wrapped latex/plastic packets for transportation.\\n• Packet rupture may cause sudden fatal toxicity, making it an important medicolegal cause of death.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Body masking: Refers to ingestion of substances to mask drug detection in biological samples; it is not a method of drug concealment.\\n• C. Body stuffing: Involves hurried swallowing of a few poorly wrapped packets to avoid police detection, carrying a higher risk of early rupture.\\n• D. Body pushing: Refers to concealment of drugs in body orifices (e.g., rectum or vagina) rather than swallowing multiple packets.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e6f99479c2","n":554,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 40-year-old female consumed a white colored rodenticide powder and presents with a potassium level of 2.5 mEq/L. She has irregular pulse and muscle paralysis. ECG shows multiple ventricular ectopic beats. What is the suspected poison?","options":["Aluminum Phosphide","Zinc Phosphide","Barium Carbonate","Warfarin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Barium Carbonate\\n\\nBarium carbonate poisoning is due to a white rodenticide powder which classically presents with severe hypokalemia, leading to muscle paralysis and life-threatening arrhythmias.\\n\\n• Barium blocks potassium channels → drives K⁺ into cells → marked hypokalemia (↓ serum K⁺)\\n• Results in:\\n\\n• Muscle weakness → paralysis\\n• Cardiac arrhythmias (ventricular ectopics, tachyarrhythmias)\\n• GI symptoms (vomiting, diarrhea)\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• Aluminum/Zinc phosphide: Cause shock, metabolic acidosis, not hypokalemia\\n• Warfarin: Causes bleeding, coagulopathy, not arrhythmias or paralysis","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-304726688b","n":555,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 40-year-old man with a history of chronic alcoholism is admitted to the hospital 48 hours after his last alcohol intake. He presents with altered consciousness, severe agitation, and disorientation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Cocaine intoxication","Delirium tremens","Alcohol withdrawal seizure","Wernicke\'s encephalopathy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Delirium tremens\\n\\nDelirium tremens (DTs) is the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal, typically occurring 48–96 hours after cessation in chronic alcoholics. It is characterized by:\\n\\n• Altered consciousness (confusion, disorientation)\\n• Severe agitation and delirium\\n• Autonomic hyperactivity (tachycardia, hypertension, sweating, fever)\\n• Hallucinations (visual > auditory)\\n\\nKey Differentiation:\\n\\n• Alcohol withdrawal seizures → occur earlier (6–24 hours), brief, generalized tonic-clonic, without prolonged delirium\\n\\n• Wernicke’s encephalopathy → triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia (due to thiamine deficiency), not marked agitation\\n\\n• Cocaine intoxication → causes agitation and sympathetic overdrive but not linked to alcohol withdrawal timeline","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-07564d087f","n":556,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 40-year-old patient with a history of chronic alcohol use presents with complaints of confusion and ataxia. Upon examination, nystagmus and sixth nerve palsy were observed. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Wernicke\'s Encephalopathy","Korsakoff Syndrome","Delirium Tremens","De Clerambault Syndrome"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Wernicke\'s Encephalopathy\\n\\nClassic triad (very high-yield)\\n\\n1.\xa0Confusion (mental status changes)\\n\\n2.\xa0Ophthalmoplegia (nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy, gaze palsy)\\n\\n3.\xa0Ataxia (gait instability)\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect\\n\\n• Korsakoff syndrome: Characterized by anterograde amnesia, confabulation, and memory impairment, not acute ophthalmoplegia or ataxia.\\n• Delirium tremens: Severe alcohol withdrawal with agitation, tremors, hallucinations, and autonomic instability.\\n• De Clerambault syndrome: Erotomania (delusional belief that someone is in love with the patient).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-df0b0d6fa5","n":557,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 42-year-old HIV-positive patient died, and his body was brought for post-mortem. The method of autopsy to be done is?","options":["Ghon technique","Letulle technique","Virchow technique","Rokitansky technique"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Rokitansky technique\\n\\nIn cases with high risk of infection (e.g., HIV-positive bodies), the preferred autopsy method is the Rokitansky technique, which involves in-situ dissection with minimal removal of organs.\\n\\n• Organs are examined within the body cavity\\n• Minimal handling and removal\\n• Reduces exposure to blood/body fluids\\n\\nWhy preferred in HIV/infectious cases:\\n\\n• Decreases risk of:\\n\\n• Needle-stick injuries\\n• Aerosol generation\\n• Direct contact with infected tissues\\n\\nComparison:\\n\\n• Letulle: En masse removal → higher exposure risk\\n• Virchow: Organ-by-organ removal → more handling\\n• Ghon: En bloc removal → moderate exposure","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-143be031c0","n":558,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 45-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with acute confusion, agitation, tremors, visual hallucinations, sweating, tachycardia, and fever, occurring 2-3 days after the abrupt cessation of alcohol. What is the most likely cause of these symptoms?","options":["Alcoholic hallucinosis","Wernicke\'s encephalopathy","Acute psychosis","Delirium tremens"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Delirium tremens\\n\\nDelirium tremens (DT) is the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal, occurring 48–96 hours (2–4 days) after abrupt cessation or reduction of alcohol intake. It is characterized by confusion, agitation, coarse tremors, visual hallucinations, autonomic hyperactivity (tachycardia, hypertension, sweating), fever, and seizures, and constitutes a medical emergency requiring prompt treatment with benzodiazepines, thiamine, and supportive care.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Alcoholic hallucinosis: Occurs 12–24 hours after alcohol cessation with predominantly auditory hallucinations and a clear sensorium (no delirium or marked autonomic instability).\\n• B. Wernicke’s encephalopathy: Due to thiamine deficiency; presents with the classic triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia, rather than tremors and autonomic hyperactivity.\\n• C. Acute psychosis: May cause hallucinations and delusions but is not temporally related to alcohol withdrawal and lacks the prominent autonomic features of DT.\\n\\n| Condition | Typical Onset After Last Drink | Key Features\\n\\n| --------- | ------------------------------ | ------------\\n\\n| Alcoholic hallucinosis | 12–24 hours | Auditory hallucinations, clear sensorium\\n\\n| Withdrawal seizures | 6–48 hours | Generalized tonic-clonic seizures\\n\\n| Delirium tremens | 48–96 hours | Delirium, visual hallucinations, tremors, fever, autonomic instability\\n\\n| Wernicke’s encephalopathy | Not a withdrawal syndrome | Confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia (thiamine deficiency)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d20cc5f010","n":559,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 45-year-old farmer came with accidental consumption of a pesticide. He complained of frequent urination and excessive salivation. Which one of the following toxidromes is most likely to be associated with this poisoning?","options":["Serotonergic","Cholinergic","Adrenergic","Hypnotic"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cholinergic\\n\\nOrganophosphate pesticides are a common cause of poisoning in farmers. They irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase, leading to accumulation of acetylcholine at muscarinic, nicotinic, and central nervous system synapses, producing the cholinergic toxidrome.\\n\\nThe patient’s frequent urination and excessive salivation are characteristic muscarinic manifestations of cholinergic excess.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Serotonergic: Characterized by agitation, hyperreflexia, clonus, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability due to excess serotonin.\\n• C. Adrenergic: Produces tachycardia, hypertension, mydriasis, diaphoresis, and agitation due to sympathetic overactivity.\\n• D. Hypnotic: Causes CNS depression, drowsiness, respiratory depression, and reduced consciousness.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1de8b38232","n":560,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 45-year-old man with a history of chronic alcohol use had been abstinent for the last 3 months while on de-addiction therapy. He consumed 60 mL of alcohol at a social gathering. Within 15–20 minutes, he developed nausea, vomiting, red rashes, throbbing headache, palpitations, and hypotension. Which of the following drug was he most likely taking for alcohol dependence?","options":["Naltrexone","Topiramate","Disulfiram","Acamprosate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Disulfiram\\n\\nDisulfiram is an alcohol deterrent that irreversibly inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, leading to accumulation of acetaldehyde when alcohol is consumed. This produces the disulfiram-ethanol reaction, characterized by flushing, nausea, vomiting, throbbing headache, palpitations, hypotension, sweating, and dizziness, discouraging further alcohol intake.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Naltrexone: An opioid receptor antagonist that reduces alcohol craving and relapse; it does not cause an aversive reaction with alcohol.\\n• B. Topiramate: Used off-label to reduce alcohol consumption by decreasing craving; does not cause a disulfiram-like reaction.\\n• D. Acamprosate: Modulates glutamate and GABA neurotransmission to maintain abstinence; does not produce an adverse reaction with alcohol.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ac3caa822c","n":561,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 55-year-old male with a history of chronic alcoholism presents to the emergency department with vomiting, altered sensorium, and hypotension. His last binge drinking episode was 7 days ago. An ultrasound reveals a small liver with coarse echotexture and mild portal vein dilatation. Given these findings, what is the most appropriate next step in managing this patient?","options":["Administer lactulose and wait for stool passage","Perform endoscopy to assess for varices","Initiate treatment with thiamine and lorazepam","Administer chlordiazepoxide"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Initiate treatment with thiamine and lorazepam\\n\\nIn a chronic alcoholic presenting with altered sensorium \\\\~1 week after last drink, suspect alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens) → requires urgent treatment with benzodiazepines + thiamine.\\n\\n• Timeline:\\n\\n• 6–24 hrs → tremors\\n• 24–48 hrs → seizures\\n• 48–96 hrs (up to 7 days) → delirium tremens\\n\\n• Features:\\n\\n• Confusion, agitation, autonomic instability\\n• Vomiting, hypotension\\n\\nManagement priorities:\\n\\n• Thiamine (before glucose) → prevents Wernicke encephalopathy\\n• Lorazepam (benzodiazepine) → controls withdrawal symptoms and prevents seizures\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Lactulose: For hepatic encephalopathy, but this is withdrawal delirium\\n• Endoscopy: Not emergency priority\\n• Chlordiazepoxide: Used in mild/moderate withdrawal; lorazepam preferred in severe cases or liver disease","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-786268c7b3","n":562,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 58-year-old patient presents with signs and symptoms suggestive of heavy metal toxicity. Laboratory investigations confirm the presence of heavy metal ions in the patient\'s bloodstream. The medical team is considering chelation therapy to bind and facilitate the excretion of the toxic metals. Which of the following agents is NOT typically used for the treatment of heavy metal toxicity?","options":["BAL (British Anti-Lewisite)","Oximes","EDTA (Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid)","Penicillamine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Oximes\\n\\nChelation therapy\\n\\nChelators act like molecular claws → Bind toxic metals → form complexes → excreted\\n\\nChelation therapy for heavy metals uses agents like BAL, EDTA, and penicillamine to bind metal ions and enhance excretion. Oximes (e.g., pralidoxime) are not chelators; they reactivate acetylcholinesterase in organophosphate poisoning. They target phosphorylated enzymes, not heavy metals. Hence oximes are not used in metal chelation.\\n\\nOrganophosphate binds AChE\\n\\n↓\\n\\nOxime given\\n\\n↓\\n\\nReactivates AChE\\n\\n↓\\n\\nRestores cholinergic function\\n\\n| Drug | Metal\\n\\n| ---- | -----\\n\\n| BAL | Arsenic, Mercury\\n\\n| EDTA | Lead\\n\\n| Penicillamine | Copper\\n\\n| Deferoxamine | Iron\\n\\n| Dimercaptosuccinic acid (DMSA) | Lead","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-525261eb27","n":563,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A 6-year-old child presents with a history of chronic exposure to mercury through contaminated fish consumption. Which clinical finding is most characteristic of chronic mercury poisoning in this patient?","options":["Burtonian line on bones","Neuropsychiatric features","Mees lines","Basophilic stippling in RBC"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Neuropsychiatric features\\n\\nChronic mercury poisoning (especially organic mercury from contaminated fish) classically presents with Danbury tremors (erethism) and neuropsychiatric disturbances.\\n\\n• Erethism (Mad hatter syndrome):\\n\\n• Irritability, mood swings, shyness, memory loss\\n\\n• Danbury tremors:\\n\\n• Fine tremors → progress to coarse, generalized tremors\\n\\n• Other features:\\n•\\n\\nMinamata disease → methyl mercury from fish\\n\\n• Visual defects + sensory loss + ataxia + cognitive decline\\n• Mercuria lentis\\n\\nBrown deposits on anterior lens capsule\\n• Acrodynia (pink disease) – mainly in children\\n\\nPink, painful, peeling hands & feet + paresthesia + photophobia\\n\\nImportant Differentials\\n\\n* Arsenic → Hyperkeratosis + Mees lines\\n\\n* Lead → Lead lines (X-ray) + basophilic stippling","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-9f2c158176","n":564,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A boy who went to the school consumed some unknown substance & came to the emergency with pulmonary oedema. On examination silver nitrate breath test is positive. What is the antidote for the substance consumed?","options":["MgSO4","Prussian blue","Physostigmine","Atropine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. MgSO4\\n\\nA positive silver nitrate breath test indicates phosphine gas exposure, most commonly due to aluminium phosphide poisoning, which can present with pulmonary edema and cardiovascular collapse.\\n\\n• Aluminium phosphide releases phosphine (PH₃) in the body\\n• Phosphine causes mitochondrial toxicity → cellular hypoxia → multi-organ failure\\n• Silver nitrate test turns black due to phosphine\\n\\nManagement principle:\\n\\n• No specific antidote, but Magnesium sulfate (MgSO₄) is used as supportive therapy\\n\\n• Stabilizes cardiac membranes\\n• Reduces arrhythmias and oxidative injury\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Prussian blue: Thallium/cesium poisoning\\n• Physostigmine: Anticholinergic toxicity\\n• Atropine: Organophosphate poisoning","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1edd348ae8","n":565,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A child presents with anemia and wrist drop. A peripheral blood smear image is provided. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Mercury poisoning","Lead poisoning","Arsenic poisoning","Cadmium poisoning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lead poisoning\\n\\nThe child has:\\n\\n• Anemia\\n• Wrist drop (radial nerve palsy)\\n• Peripheral smear showing coarse basophilic stippling of RBCs\\n\\nThese findings are classic for lead poisoning (plumbism).\\n\\nLead poisoning causes:\\n\\n• Microcytic hypochromic anemia with coarse basophilic stippling\\n• Peripheral neuropathy → Wrist drop (adults) or foot drop\\n• In children: encephalopathy, developmental delay, irritability, learning disability\\n• GI symptoms: abdominal colic, constipation\\n\\nLead inhibits:\\n\\n• δ-Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) dehydratase\\n• Ferrochelatase ⭐\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Mercury poisoning: Causes erethism (behavioral changes), tremors, gingivitis, acrodynia; does not cause basophilic stippling with wrist drop.\\n• C. Arsenic poisoning: Causes severe gastroenteritis, garlic odor, Mees’ lines, peripheral neuropathy, but not basophilic stippling.\\n• D. Cadmium poisoning: Causes renal tubular dysfunction and osteomalacia (Itai-itai disease), not wrist drop with stippled RBCs.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-b23f5c9c79","n":566,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A farmer was suspected of some unknown substance poisoning, as he presented with bronchorrhoea, sweating and excessive salivation. Examination revealed bradycardia with bilateral miosis. Consider the following statements regarding his poisoning: 1. The symptoms represent Anti Cholinergic Syndrome. 2. Activation of Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) enzyme is the underlying mechanism. 3. Intravenous Atropine should be given for treatment. Which of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 and 2","2 and 3","1 and 3","3 only"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 3 only\\n\\nThe farmer has classic cholinergic poisoning, most likely due to an organophosphate pesticide:\\n\\n• Bronchorrhea\\n• Excessive salivation\\n• Sweating\\n• Bradycardia\\n• Miosis\\n\\n1. The symptoms represent Anti Cholinergic Syndrome: Incorrect\\n\\n• These are features of cholinergic syndrome.\\n• Anticholinergic toxicity causes dry mouth, mydriasis, tachycardia, urinary retention and hot, dry skin.\\n\\n2. Activation of Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) enzyme is the underlying mechanism: Incorrect\\n\\n• Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, causing accumulation of acetylcholine at muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.\\n\\n3. Intravenous Atropine should be given for treatment: Correct\\n\\n• Atropine competitively blocks muscarinic receptors and rapidly treats bronchorrhea, bronchospasm, bradycardia and secretions.\\n• Pralidoxime (2-PAM) is also given in organophosphate poisoning to reactivate AChE, particularly when administered early.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9e995083a0","n":567,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A man is capable of performing sexual intercourse in general but is unable to have sexual intercourse with one particular woman. This condition is known as:","options":["Quoad hanc","Frigidity","Vaginismus","Dhat syndrome"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Quoad hanc\\n\\nQuoad hanc impotence is a form of relative impotence in which a man is capable of normal sexual intercourse generally but is unable to perform with one particular woman. The dysfunction is usually psychogenic rather than due to an organic cause and has important medicolegal implications in cases involving annulment of marriage or paternity disputes.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Frigidity: Decreased sexual desire or inability to achieve sexual arousal/orgasm in women (now more appropriately termed female sexual interest/arousal disorder).\\n• C. Vaginismus: Involuntary spasm of the pelvic floor muscles causing painful or impossible vaginal penetration in women.\\n• D. Dhat syndrome: A culture-bound syndrome characterized by anxiety related to perceived semen loss, commonly seen in South Asia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-00817381a9","n":568,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A man wears female heels and a female dress to get sexual arousal. He is not interested in males. What is the diagnosis ?","options":["Transvestic Fetishism","Gender Dysphoria","Homosexuality","Voyeurism"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Transvestic Fetishism\\n\\nTransvestic fetishism is a type of paraphilia in which a person (typically male) derives sexual arousal from cross-dressing in clothes of the opposite sex, without a desire to change gender identity.\\n\\n• Core feature: Sexual arousal linked to wearing opposite-gender clothing\\n• The individual:\\n\\n• Is usually heterosexual\\n• Identifies with their biological sex\\n\\n• Behavior is driven by fetishistic interest, not gender identity issues\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Gender dysphoria: Distress due to mismatch between gender identity and biological sex (not primarily sexual arousal)\\n• Homosexuality: Attraction to same sex, unrelated to clothing\\n• Voyeurism: Arousal from observing others secretly","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-99cb1b6978","n":569,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient came with complains of hair loss. His wife mentions that she has noticed some behavioral changes. The doctor notices that there is a loss of eyebrows on the lateral side. He then comes to a conclusion by examining the nails. What is the type of poisoning in this case?","options":["Thallium","Arsenic","Mercury","Lead"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Thallium\\n\\nThallium poisoning should be suspected in a patient with alopecia (hair loss), neuropsychiatric changes, and characteristic nail findings (Mees’ lines).\\n\\n• Alopecia (including scalp hair and lateral eyebrows)\\n• Peripheral neuropathy & behavioral changes (confusion, irritability)\\n• Mees’ lines (transverse white lines on nails)\\n\\nThallium interferes with cellular metabolism (mitochondrial function). High affinity for hair follicles and nervous tissue\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Arsenic: Also shows Mees’ lines but prominent GI symptoms + garlic odor + hyperkeratosis, not marked alopecia\\n• Mercury: Neuropsychiatric symptoms (erethism) but no alopecia\\n• Lead: Causes neuropathy (wrist/foot drop) and anemia, not hair loss","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a84debb67a","n":570,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient consumed a toxic substance and presented with tachycardia, tachypnea, hypertension, and hypocalcemia. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Iron","Methanol","Ethylene Glycol","Digoxin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ethylene Glycol\\n\\nEthylene glycol poisoning is characterized by high anion gap metabolic acidosis with hypocalcemia, along with tachycardia, tachypnea, and hypertension.\\n\\n• Metabolism:\\n\\n• Ethylene glycol → glycolic acid + oxalic acid\\n\\n• Oxalic acid binds calcium → hypocalcemia\\n• Leads to:\\n\\n• Calcium oxalate crystal deposition (kidneys) → renal failure\\n• Metabolic acidosis (glycolic acid)\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• CNS depression (early)\\n• Tachypnea (compensatory for acidosis)\\n• Cardiac instability\\n• Hypocalcemia signs (tetany, arrhythmias)\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Iron: GI symptoms, metabolic acidosis but no hypocalcemia\\n• Methanol: Visual disturbances, no hypocalcemia\\n• Digoxin: Arrhythmias, hyperkalemia (not hypocalcemia)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4d19d564e3","n":571,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient is brought to the emergency department in a comatose state with hypotension and depressed respiratory rate. Which of the following is the most appropriate antidote for management?","options":["Atropine","Naloxone","Naltrexone","Flumazenil"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Naloxone\\n\\nOpioid poisoning presents with the classic triad of coma, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils, and the drug of choice for immediate reversal is naloxone.\\n\\n• Naloxone is a pure opioid receptor antagonist\\n• Rapidly reverses:\\n\\no Respiratory depression\\n\\no CNS depression\\n\\n• Given IV/IM/IN in emergency settings\\n\\n• Displaces opioids (e.g., morphine, heroin)\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Atropine: For organophosphate poisoning (cholinergic crisis)\\n\\n• Naltrexone: Long-acting antagonist, used in de-addiction, not emergency\\n\\n• Flumazenil: Antidote for benzodiazepine overdose","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f3be88d04c","n":572,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient presented with features as given in the image below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Arsenic","Mercury","Lead","Copper"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Arsenic\\n\\nChronic arsenic poisoning presents with characteristic cutaneous changes and nail findings (Mees lines).\\n\\n• Raindrop pigmentation on skin (hyperpigmented and hypopigmented spots over trunk)\\n• Mees lines:\\n\\n• Transverse white lines across nails\\n• Do not move with nail growth initially\\n\\n• Arsenic interferes with cellular metabolism and keratinization\\n• Causes chronic dermatological and systemic toxicity\\n\\nAssociated features:\\n\\n• Hyperkeratosis of palms and soles\\n• Peripheral neuropathy\\n• Increased risk of skin cancers\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Mercury: Tremors, neuropsychiatric symptoms (erethism)\\n• Lead: Burtonian line (gum), anemia\\n• Copper: Kayser–Fleischer rings (Wilson disease)","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-8a06e0ac47","n":573,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient presents with a garlicky odour on the breath and blood-stained diarrhea, which later progresses to colorless watery stools and vomiting. The patient also reports pain in the pharynx. Based on this clinical presentation, what is the most likely type of poisoning?","options":["Arsenic","Phosphorus","Aluminium phosphide","Lead"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Arsenic\\n\\nArsenic poisoning classically presents with garlic-like odor of breath, severe gastrointestinal irritation, and progressive diarrhea.\\n\\n• Acute arsenic toxicity causes:\\n\\n• Burning pain in mouth and throat (pharynx)\\n• Vomiting\\n• Blood-stained diarrhea → later rice-water (colorless watery) stools\\n\\n• Mechanism:\\n\\n• Capillary damage + enzyme inhibition (cellular respiration)\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• Garlic odor\\n• Severe GI symptoms (mimics cholera)\\n• Hypotension, dehydration\\n• Later: neuropathy, Mees lines (chronic)\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Phosphorus: Garlic odor + luminous vomitus, hepatic failure\\n• Aluminium phosphide: Shock, metabolic acidosis, no severe GI hemorrhagic diarrhea\\n• Lead: Abdominal colic, anemia, neuropathy (no acute severe diarrhea)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e0f27237d1","n":574,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient presents with respiratory depression, hypotension, pinpoint pupils, and unconsciousness. What is the antidote of choice?","options":["Pralidoxime","Atropine","Flumazenil","Naloxone"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Naloxone\\n\\nThe triad of respiratory depression, coma (unconsciousness), and pinpoint pupils (miosis) is characteristic of opioid poisoning. Naloxone, a competitive opioid receptor antagonist with the highest affinity for μ-opioid receptors, is the antidote of choice and rapidly reverses respiratory and central nervous system depression.\\n\\n• Naloxone rapidly reverses the effects of opioid overdose, especially respiratory depression, which is the major cause of mortality.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Pralidoxime: Reactivates acetylcholinesterase in organophosphorus poisoning; used along with atropine.\\n• B. Atropine: Antimuscarinic agent used for organophosphorus poisoning; does not reverse opioid toxicity.\\n• C. Flumazenil: Benzodiazepine receptor antagonist used in benzodiazepine overdose.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1301aac8ef","n":575,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient presents with urinary crystals as shown in the image below. What can be the cause?","options":["Ethylene Glycol","Methyl Alcohol","Aconite","Organophosphate Poisoning"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Ethylene Glycol\\n\\nEthylene glycol poisoning is suggested by the presence of calcium oxalate crystals in urine, often described as envelope-shaped (octahedral) crystals.\\n\\nEthylene glycol (antifreeze) is metabolized to:\\n\\n• Glycolic acid → metabolic acidosis\\n• Oxalic acid → combines with calcium → calcium oxalate crystals\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• High anion gap metabolic acidosis\\n• Renal failure (due to crystal deposition in tubules)\\n• CNS depression (early)\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• Methanol: Visual disturbances (optic nerve toxicity), no oxalate crystals\\n• Aconite: Cardiotoxic (arrhythmias)\\n• Organophosphate: Cholinergic symptoms","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-0e98e975ec","n":576,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient with a history of chronic alcohol use has developed confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia. Which vitamin deficiency is responsible for this presentation?","options":["Vitamin B1","Vitamin B2","Vitamin B6","Vitamin C"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Vitamin B1\\n\\nA chronic alcohol user presenting with confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia suggests Wernicke’s encephalopathy, which is caused by deficiency of thiamine (Vitamin B1).\\n\\nWhy it occurs in alcoholics:\\n\\n• Poor nutritional intake\\n• Reduced intestinal absorption of thiamine\\n• Impaired storage in the liver\\n• Decreased conversion to its active form\\n\\nClassic triad of Wernicke’s encephalopathy:\\n\\n1. Confusion\\n\\n2.\xa0Ataxia\\n\\n3.\xa0Ophthalmoplegia (nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy)\\n\\nImportant clinical point:\\n\\nIn suspected Wernicke’s encephalopathy, thiamine must be administered before glucose, because giving glucose first can precipitate or worsen neurological damage.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e7fe9e4406","n":577,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A patient with suicidal tendency ingested some substance. He presents with tachycardia, dilated pupil, and hypertension. Identify the drug.","options":["Cocaine","Heroin","Morphine","Chlorpheniramine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cocaine\\n\\nCocaine toxicity produces a sympathomimetic toxidrome characterized by tachycardia, hypertension, and mydriasis (dilated pupils).\\n\\n• Mechanism: Inhibits reuptake of norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin → excess sympathetic activity\\n•\\n\\nResults in:\\n\\n• Tachycardia\\n• Hypertension\\n• Mydriasis\\n• Agitation, hyperthermia\\n\\n•\\n\\nSympathomimetic toxidrome:\\n\\n• ↑ HR, ↑ BP, dilated pupils, sweating\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Heroin / Morphine (opioids):\\n\\n• Pinpoint pupils (miosis)\\n• Respiratory depression\\n\\n• Chlorpheniramine (antihistamine):\\n\\n• Anticholinergic features, but less severe hypertension/tachycardia compared to cocaine","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-eaeb30a1c5","n":578,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A person derives sexual pleasure only from non-living objects, like touching shoes and undergarments. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Fetishism","Voyeurism","Exhibitionism","Frotteurism"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Fetishism\\n\\nFetishism is a type of paraphilia in which sexual arousal is derived from non-living objects (e.g., shoes, undergarments) or specific non-genital body parts.\\n\\n• Sexual gratification depends on the presence or use of the fetish object\\n• Common objects: clothing, footwear, fabrics\\n\\nDifferentiation from other paraphilias:\\n\\n• Voyeurism: Arousal from watching others (undressing/sexual activity)\\n• Exhibitionism: Arousal from exposing genitals to others\\n• Frotteurism: Arousal from touching/rubbing against non-consenting individuals","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7ccf102df8","n":579,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A pregnant woman undergoes USG examination. She is found to have twins, but one-month age gap in twins on USG scan. What is this phenomenon called?","options":["Superfetation","Superfecundation","Illegitimate child","Suppositious child"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Superfetation\\n\\nSuperfetation is a rare phenomenon in which a second ovum is fertilized and implanted during an ongoing pregnancy, leading to fetuses of different gestational ages.\\n\\n• Occurs when:\\n\\n• Ovulation happens after an existing pregnancy\\n• A second conception takes place\\n\\n• Results in:\\n\\n• Different fetal sizes/ages on ultrasound\\n\\nDifferentiate from other options:\\n\\n• Superfecundation:\\n\\n• Fertilization of two ova from same cycle\\n• May have different fathers\\n• Fetuses are same gestational age\\n\\n• Superfetation:\\n\\n• Conceptions at different times → different ages\\n\\n• Illegitimate child: Legal term, not biological phenomenon; refers to the child being born before marriage.\\n• Suppositious child:\\n\\nA child that is falsely claimed or substituted (e.g., pretending someone else’s child is yours).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-719d854ad5","n":580,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A woman with a history of alleged rape undergoes forensic examination. A microscopic examination of a vaginal wet mount demonstrates motile spermatozoa. Based on this finding, the most likely time elapsed since the alleged sexual assault is:","options":["12–24 hours","24–48 hours","48–72 hours","More than 72 hours"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 12–24 hours\\n\\nIn forensic examination following alleged sexual assault, the presence of motile spermatozoa on a vaginal wet mount indicates recent sexual intercourse, generally within the preceding 12–24 hours. As time passes, sperm lose motility first, while non-motile sperm and sperm heads may persist for longer durations.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. 24–48 hours: Sperm may still be present, but motility is usually lost.\\n• C. 48–72 hours: Mostly non-motile sperm or sperm heads remain.\\n• D. More than 72 hours: Detection of motile sperm is highly unlikely.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a5b979022a","n":581,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A young child is brought to the emergency department after a history of paint ingestion. On examination, he is noted to have colicky abdominal pain. Accidental ingestion of which of the following substances is the most likely cause?","options":["Lead paint","Opioid","Atropine","Organophosphate"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lead paint\\n\\nLead poisoning should be suspected in children with history of paint ingestion and colicky abdominal pain (“lead colic”).\\n\\n• Source: Lead-based paints (common in old houses)\\n\\n• Mechanism: Lead interferes with enzymes of heme synthesis\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• Abdominal colic (pain) ; Constipation ; Anemia\\n\\n• Neurobehavioral changes (irritability, learning issues)\\n\\n• Severe cases: encephalopathy\\n\\n• Basophilic stippling in RBCs\\n\\n• Lead lines on gums (Burtonian line) and metaphysis (children)\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Opioids: CNS depression, pinpoint pupils\\n\\n• Atropine: Anticholinergic features (dry, dilated pupils)\\n\\n• Organophosphates: Cholinergic symptoms","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-07b539262e","n":582,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"A young male presents with suspected poisoning and has pin-point pupils. Which of the following can be the poison.","options":["Opioid","Cocaine","Datura","Amphetamine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Opioid\\n\\nPinpoint pupils (miosis) in a poisoning case are classically seen in opioid toxicity.\\n\\n• Opioids (e.g., morphine, heroin):\\n\\n• Cause miosis, respiratory depression, and CNS depression\\n• Mechanism: Stimulation of Edinger–Westphal nucleus → parasympathetic constriction of pupils\\n\\nClassic triad of opioid poisoning:\\n\\n• Pinpoint pupils\\n• Respiratory depression\\n• Decreased level of consciousness\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Cocaine: Mydriasis (dilated pupils), sympathetic stimulation\\n• Datura: Anticholinergic → mydriasis, dry skin, delirium\\n• Amphetamine: Sympathomimetic → dilated pupils, agitation","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1a0de0c4c6","n":583,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Consider the following agents and the cardiac effects in case of overdose or poisoning: 1. Prolonged PR interval – Lithium 2. Prolonged QTc interval – Amiodarone 3. Wide QRS complex – Kerosene ingestion 4. Tachycardia – Organophosphates How many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Only two pairs\\n\\nElectrocardiographic (ECG) abnormalities are important diagnostic clues in poisoning and drug overdose. Different toxins produce characteristic conduction disturbances and arrhythmias, making ECG interpretation an essential component of toxicology management.\\n\\nWhy is B correct?\\n\\n• 1. Prolonged PR interval – Lithium ✅\\n\\n• Lithium toxicity can cause sinus node dysfunction, first-degree AV block (prolonged PR interval), T-wave flattening/inversion, and bradyarrhythmias.\\n\\n• 2. Prolonged QTc interval – Amiodarone ✅\\n\\n• Amiodarone prolongs ventricular repolarization, causing QT interval prolongation, although it has a relatively low risk of torsades de pointes compared with other Class III antiarrhythmics.\\n\\n• 3. Wide QRS complex – Kerosene ingestion ❌\\n\\n• Kerosene poisoning primarily causes aspiration pneumonitis and CNS depression. Wide QRS complexes are classically seen with tricyclic antidepressant (TCA), sodium channel blocker, or cocaine overdose, not kerosene.\\n\\n• 4. Tachycardia – Organophosphates ❌\\n\\n• Organophosphate poisoning typically causes bradycardia due to excessive muscarinic stimulation, although tachycardia may occasionally occur early because of nicotinic effects.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-567651dd0d","n":584,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Consider the following pairs. How many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Only two pairs\\n\\nAbnormal urine discoloration can provide an important clue to the drug or poison ingested. Recognizing characteristic urine colors helps in the early diagnosis of poisoning and guides appropriate management.\\n\\nWhy is the answer B (Only two pairs)?\\n\\n• 1. Pink – Cephalosporin ✅\\n\\n• Some cephalosporins (especially cefdinir, particularly with iron-containing preparations) may cause pink to reddish discoloration of urine.\\n\\n• 2. Brown – Chloroquine ✅\\n\\n• Chloroquine poisoning may produce dark brown urine, often due to hemoglobinuria or myoglobinuria in severe toxicity.\\n\\n• 3. Greenish blue – Phenazopyridine ❌\\n\\n• Phenazopyridine classically causes orange to red-orange urine, not greenish-blue.\\n• Greenish-blue urine is classically seen with methylene blue, amitriptyline, propofol, indomethacin, and triamterene.\\n\\n• 4. Red-orange – Amitriptyline ❌\\n\\n• Amitriptyline is associated with blue-green urine, whereas red-orange urine is characteristic of phenazopyridine or rifampicin.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-19f03436e8","n":585,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Consider the following statements regarding organophosphate-induced delayed polyneuropathy (OPIDN): 1. It is a rare complication and occurs 2–3 weeks after acute exposure. 2. It is a feature of poisoning with certain organophosphorus compounds such as triorthocresyl phosphate. 3. Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase causes motor polyneuropathy with no evidence of sensory loss. 4. There is no specific therapy although regular physiotherapy may limit deformity caused by muscle wasting. Which of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 2 and 4\\n\\nOrganophosphate-Induced Delayed Neuropathy (OPIDN) is a rare neurological complication that develops 1–3 weeks after exposure to certain organophosphate compounds. Unlike the acute cholinergic syndrome, OPIDN results from inhibition of neuropathy target esterase (NTE) rather than acetylcholinesterase, leading to distal axonal degeneration and a sensorimotor polyneuropathy. Treatment is mainly supportive, with rehabilitation and physiotherapy.\\n\\nWhy is C correct?\\n\\n• 1. It is a rare complication and occurs 2–3 weeks after acute exposure✅: OPIDN typically develops 1–3 weeks after exposure.\\n• 2. It is a feature of poisoning with certain organophosphorus compounds such as triorthocresyl phosphate✅: Triorthocresyl phosphate (TOCP) is the classic organophosphate associated with OPIDN.\\n• 4. There is no specific therapy although regular physiotherapy may limit deformity caused by muscle wasting✅: No antidote is effective once OPIDN develops; physiotherapy and rehabilitation are the mainstay of treatment.\\n\\nIncorrect statement:\\n\\n• 3. Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase causes motor polyneuropathy with no evidence of sensory loss❌: OPIDN is due to inhibition of neuropathy target esterase (NTE), not acetylcholinesterase, and produces a sensorimotor neuropathy with both motor and sensory involvement.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a110130e62","n":586,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Consider the following statements regarding paracetamol toxicity: 1. Toxicity is caused by an intermediate reactive metabolite that binds covalently to cellular proteins causing cell death. 2. Antidotes for paracetamol act by replenishing hepatic glutathione and should be administered to all patients with acute poisoning. 3. Intravenous acetylcysteine is a highly efficacious antidote if administered within 8 hours of the overdose. 4. Methionine may be used as an alternative antidote but it is less effective than acetylcysteine. Which of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 4 only","1, 2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3 and 4\\n\\nParacetamol (Acetaminophen) overdose is a common cause of acute liver failure. Toxicity occurs when the reactive metabolite N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI) is produced in excess and hepatic glutathione stores are depleted, allowing NAPQI to bind to hepatocyte proteins and cause centrilobular (zone III) hepatic necrosis. Early administration of N-acetylcysteine (NAC) is highly effective in preventing liver injury.\\n\\nWhy is D correct?\\n\\n• 1. Toxicity is caused by an intermediate reactive metabolite that binds covalently to cellular proteins causing cell death✅: NAPQI is the toxic metabolite responsible for hepatocellular injury.\\n• 2. Antidotes for paracetamol act by replenishing hepatic glutathione and should be administered to all patients with acute poisoning✅: N-acetylcysteine (NAC) and methionine replenish glutathione stores and are indicated in patients with potentially toxic overdose based on treatment guidelines.\\n• 3. Intravenous acetylcysteine is a highly efficacious antidote if administered within 8 hours of the overdose✅: NAC is most effective when started within 8 hours, but it still provides benefit even if given later.\\n• 4. Methionine may be used as an alternative antidote but it is less effective than acetylcysteine✅: Methionine is an alternative when NAC is unavailable, but NAC is the preferred antidote.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e6d390c6e1","n":587,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Consider the following statements with regard to elapid snakebite: 1. Regardless of the anatomic site of bite, walking of victim should be discouraged. 2. Pressure immobilization should be used, wherever possible. 3. Ptosis can be present after elapid bite. Which of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 3\\n\\nElapid snakes (e.g., cobra and krait) produce predominantly neurotoxic envenomation. First aid aims to delay venom absorption until the patient reaches a healthcare facility for anti-snake venom (ASV) and supportive care.\\n\\n• Statement 1. Regardless of the anatomic site of bite, walking of victim should be discouraged: Correct. Walking or unnecessary movement should be discouraged, irrespective of the bite site, as muscle activity increases lymphatic flow and accelerates venom spread.\\n• Statement 2. Pressure immobilization should be used, wherever possible: Correct. Pressure immobilization technique (PIT) with splinting is recommended, wherever feasible, especially for neurotoxic (elapid) bites, to slow lymphatic spread of venom.\\n• Statement 3. Ptosis can be present after elapid bite: Correct. Ptosis is often the earliest clinical sign of neurotoxic envenomation and may progress to ophthalmoplegia, dysphagia, respiratory muscle paralysis, and respiratory failure.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c3684c9829","n":588,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"During fetal autopsy, what is the order of examination to differentiate between live and stillbirth?","options":["Abdomen &gt; Thorax &gt;Head","Thorax &gt; Head &gt; Abdomen","Head &gt; Abdomen &gt; Thorax","Head &gt; Thorax &gt; Abdomen"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Head > Abdomen > Thorax\\n\\nIn fetal autopsy, the correct order of examination to differentiate live birth vs stillbirth is:\\n\\nHead → Abdomen → Thorax\\n\\nRationale for this sequence:\\n\\n• Head (first):\\n\\n• Assess for intracranial hemorrhage, molding, caput succedaneum\\n• Helps identify birth-related trauma\\n\\n• Abdomen (second):\\n\\n• Prevents artificial entry of air into thorax during dissection\\n• Allows assessment of diaphragm position and abdominal organs\\n\\n• Thorax (last):\\n\\n• Essential for hydrostatic (lung float) test\\n• Determines whether the baby breathed (live birth) or not\\n\\nOpening thorax first may introduce air → false-positive lung float test\\n\\n• Helps differentiate:\\n\\n• Live birth (lungs float)\\n• Stillbirth (lungs sink)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-92fb219ae3","n":589,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Exchange transfusion as a method to enhance excretion of poison is indicated in:","options":["Methemoglobinemia","Carbamazepine poisoning","Theophylline poisoning","Barbiturate poisoning"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Methemoglobinemia\\n\\nExchange transfusion is indicated in severe methemoglobinemia when methylene blue is ineffective or contraindicated (e.g., G6PD deficiency) or in life-threatening cases with very high methemoglobin levels. It rapidly removes methemoglobin-containing red blood cells and replaces them with normal erythrocytes, restoring oxygen-carrying capacity.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Carbamazepine poisoning: Treated with multiple-dose activated charcoal and hemodialysis/hemoperfusion in severe poisoning.\\n• C. Theophylline poisoning:\xa0Severe toxicity is managed with hemodialysis.\\n• D. Barbiturate poisoning: Severe poisoning may require hemodialysis (especially phenobarbital), not exchange transfusion.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4526468248","n":590,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Following the siren warning after a toxic gas leak from a factory, what should be the next immediate step taken by civilians according to disaster management and public health safety guidelines?","options":["Open all windows in home for ventilation","Close all windows and cracks in home","Go out of home and inform others","Talk to friends on phone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Close all windows and cracks in home\\n\\nIn the event of a toxic gas leak, the immediate protective action for civilians is “shelter-in-place”, which includes closing all windows, doors, and sealing cracks to prevent entry of toxic fumes.\\n\\n• Toxic gases spread through air currents and enter homes via openings and ventilation systems\\n• Sealing the environment reduces exposure and inhalation risk\\n• Additional measures:\\n\\n• Turn off fans, AC, and ventilation systems\\n• Use wet cloths/masks to reduce inhalation\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Opening windows: Increases exposure\\n• Going outside: Higher risk of direct exposure\\n• Calling friends: Delays protective action","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-78fc0f0e73","n":591,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Fomepizole is an antidote used to treat poisoning from which of the following substances? I. Methanol II. Digoxin III. Cocaine IV. Ethylene glycol Select the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["I and II","II and III","III and IV","I and IV"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. I and IV\\n\\nFomepizole is the antidote of choice for poisoning due to methanol and ethylene glycol. It acts by competitively inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH), thereby preventing the formation of toxic metabolites responsible for organ damage.\\n\\n• I. Methanol ✅\\n\\n• Methanol is metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde and formic acid, which cause metabolic acidosis and optic nerve toxicity (blindness).\\n• Fomepizole blocks this metabolism.\\n\\n• II. Digoxin ❌\\n\\n• Digoxin poisoning is treated with Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digoxin immune Fab).\\n\\n• III. Cocaine ❌\\n\\n• Cocaine toxicity is managed with benzodiazepines, supportive care, and control of hypertension and hyperthermia.\\n• Fomepizole has no role.\\n\\n• IV. Ethylene glycol ✅\\n\\n• Ethylene glycol is metabolized to glycolic acid and oxalic acid, causing severe metabolic acidosis, acute kidney injury, and calcium oxalate crystal deposition.\\n• Fomepizole prevents the formation of these toxic metabolites.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-93024793e8","n":592,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Hydrostatic (floatation) test shows that both the lung and liver specimens do not float in water. What is the correct interpretation?","options":["Respiration present; decomposition absent","Respiration absent; decomposition present","Respiration absent; decomposition absent","Respiration present; decomposition present"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Respiration absent; decomposition absent\\n\\nThe hydrostatic (lung flotation) test is used in forensic medicine to determine whether a newborn breathed after birth.\\n\\n• Lungs that float → Air is present → Respiration occurred\\n• Lungs that sink → No air → Respiration did not occur (stillbirth)\\n\\nThe liver flotation test (Breslau’s second life test) helps identify decomposition:\\n\\n• Liver floats → Putrefaction gases present → Decomposition present\\n• Liver sinks → No putrefaction gases → Decomposition absent\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Respiration present; decomposition absent: Lungs would float.\\n• B. Respiration absent; decomposition present: Lung may sink, but liver would float due to putrefaction gases.\\n• D. Respiration present; decomposition present: Both lung and liver would float.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-bfaec083d5","n":593,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Identify the type of hymen shown in the image.","options":["Imperforate hymen","Semilunar hymen","Septate hymen","Annular hymen"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Septate hymen\\n\\nSeptate Hymen\\n\\nIn this condition, a fibrous band runs across the hymenal opening, dividing it into two openings.\\n\\nThe hymen forms from the junction of the M\xfcllerian ducts and urogenital sinus.\\n\\nIncomplete degeneration of hymenal membrane\\n\\n↓\\n\\nPersistence of central septum\\n\\n↓\\n\\nSeptate hymen\\n\\nTreatment is simple:\\n\\n• Minor surgical excision of the septum\\n• Done under local anesthesia","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-180b8c17de","n":594,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"In 18th century British East India company used to transport which substance of abuse to China:","options":["Opium","Marijuana","Cocaine","Magic mushroom"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Opium\\n\\nIn the 18th–19th century, the British East India Company extensively traded opium from India to China, leading to widespread addiction and major historical consequences.\\n\\n• Opium was cultivated in British-controlled India and exported to China\\n• Resulted in large-scale substance dependence in China\\n• Led to the Opium Wars between Britain and China\\n\\nForensic and public health relevance:\\n\\n• Opium is a natural opioid derived from Papaver somniferum\\n• Acts as a CNS depressant causing:\\n\\n• Analgesia\\n• Euphoria\\n• Respiratory depression (in overdose)\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Marijuana: Cannabis use, not historically traded in this context\\n• Cocaine: Derived from coca plant (South America)\\n• Magic mushrooms: Hallucinogens, not part of this trade","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2d2a1300e9","n":595,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"In a case of RTA (Road Traffic Accident), a person has died. Who can order an autopsy?","options":["Public Prosecutor","Defendant Lawyer","Police Officer","Forensic Medicine Doctor"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Police Officer\\n\\nIn cases of unnatural or suspicious death (e.g., Road Traffic Accident), a medicolegal autopsy is ordered by a police officer (Investigating Officer) under legal provisions.\\n\\n• Governed by Section 174 CrPC (India)\\n• Police conduct inquest and decide the need for autopsy\\n• The body is then sent for postmortem examination by a forensic doctor\\n\\nRoles clarified:\\n\\n• Police officer: Orders autopsy\\n• Forensic medicine doctor: Performs autopsy\\n• Public prosecutor / defense lawyer: No role in ordering autopsy\\n\\nClinical/forensic significance:\\n\\n• Ensures proper legal documentation of cause of death\\n• Important in accidents, suicides, homicides, unknown deaths","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b547a2bb11","n":596,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"In a case where a dead body is found with severely charred burns, how can fractures and internal injuries be identified?","options":["Virtual autopsy","Traditional photography","Psychological autopsy","In situ autopsy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Virtual autopsy\\n\\nVirtual autopsy (virtopsy) is a non-invasive postmortem imaging technique that uses CT and/or MRI to detect fractures, internal hemorrhage, foreign bodies, gas collections, and internal organ injuries, especially when conventional autopsy is difficult, such as in severely charred, decomposed, or mutilated bodies.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Traditional photography: Documents external findings only and cannot identify internal injuries or fractures.\\n• C. Psychological autopsy: A retrospective assessment of the deceased’s mental state, mainly used in suspected suicides; it does not evaluate physical injuries.\\n• D. In situ autopsy: Refers to examination of the body at the scene but does not provide detailed internal imaging like virtual autopsy.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2c46595b68","n":597,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"In case of a child with an acute poisoning by ingestion, activated charcoal may be used in which one of the following situations?","options":["Iron ingestion","Antiepileptic ingestion","Corrosive ingestion","Lithium ingestion"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Antiepileptic ingestion\\n\\nActivated charcoal is useful in acute poisoning with drugs that are adsorbed by charcoal, provided the patient presents within 1 hour of ingestion (or later for sustained-release preparations) and has a protected airway. It is effective for many drugs, including antiepileptics (e.g., carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin).\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Iron ingestion: Activated charcoal does not adsorb iron.\\n• C. Corrosive ingestion: Contraindicated because it does not bind corrosives and may interfere with endoscopy or increase aspiration risk.\\n• D. Lithium ingestion: Activated charcoal does not adsorb lithium.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bf2665819b","n":598,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"In methanol poisoning, which of these is increased in the blood?","options":["Formic acid and glycolic acid","Oxalic and formic acid","Glycolic and lactic acid","Formic acid and lactic acid"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Formic acid and lactic acid\\n\\nIn methanol poisoning, toxic metabolites lead to accumulation of formic acid and lactic acid, resulting in high anion gap metabolic acidosis.\\n\\n• Methanol metabolism:\\n\\n• Methanol → formaldehyde → formic acid (toxic metabolite)\\n\\n• Formic acid:\\n\\n• Causes metabolic acidosis\\n• Responsible for optic nerve toxicity (visual disturbances, blindness)\\n\\n• Lactic acid:\\n\\n• Increases due to cellular hypoxia and impaired oxidative metabolism\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• Visual symptoms: “Snowfield vision,” blurred vision\\n• CNS depression\\n• Severe metabolic acidosis\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Glycolic acid, oxalic acid: Seen in ethylene glycol poisoning, not methanol","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3d8a8b1000","n":599,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"In what sequence do muscles develop rigor mortis, progressing from early to late?","options":["Orbicularis oculi, neck, face, thorax, upper limb","Orbicularis oculi, neck, facial muscles, upper limb, thorax","Neck, muscles of eyelid, upper limb, thorax","Neck, muscles of eyelid, facial muscles, thorax, upper limb"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Orbicularis oculi, neck, face, thorax, upper limb\\n\\nRigor mortis follows a cephalocaudal progression (Nysten’s law)—from small, active muscles to larger muscle groups.\\n\\nCorrect sequence (early → late):\\n\\n• Orbicularis oculi (eyelids)\\n• Neck\\n• Facial muscles\\n• Thorax\\n• Upper limbs\\n• Abdomen\\n• Lower limbs\\n\\nTimeline (approximate):\\n\\n• Begins: 1–2 hours after death\\n• Fully established: 6–12 hours\\n• Disappears: 24–48 hours\\n\\nForensic significance:\\n\\n• Helps in estimation of time since death\\n• Assists in determining body position after death","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ccf41d9400","n":600,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Match the following poison and site of action :","options":["A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4","A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1","A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3","A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4\\n\\n• Datura (anticholinergic alkaloids) predominantly affects the CNS.\\n• Nicotine acts mainly on autonomic ganglia and myocardium, producing prominent CVS effects.\\n• Nitric acid is a corrosive, causing direct injury to skin and mucosa.\\n• Nux vomica (strychnine) acts on the spinal cord, blocking inhibitory glycine receptors.\\n\\nCorrect matching: A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-9b82c8f9c3","n":601,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Match the following poisons to their corresponding effect:","options":["A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4","A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1","A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1\\n\\nOrganic irritants are plant-derived gastrointestinal irritants—croton is a classic example.\\n\\nMetallic irritants include heavy metals such as lead, causing corrosive GI symptoms.\\n\\nDeliriants produce delirium and hallucinations—cocaine can cause toxic psychosis and agitation.\\n\\nDepressants cause CNS depression—opioids are prototypical depressants.\\n\\nHence, the correct matching is A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-4f2c5627fe","n":602,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Match the following poisons with their characteristic findings:","options":["1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a","1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b","1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c","1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a\\n\\nRecognition of the characteristic clinical signs and postmortem findings of common poisons is essential in forensic medicine, as these findings provide important clues to the diagnosis and guide toxicological investigations.\\n\\nCorrect Match:\\n\\n• 1. Carbon monoxide → d. Cherry-red postmortem staining\\n• 2. Organophosphorus compounds → c. Pinpoint pupils (miosis)\\n• 3. Cyanide → b. Bitter almond odor\\n• 4. Phosphorus (yellow phosphorus) → a. Garlic odor","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-ef15b03d9d","n":603,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Match the following substances with their category: a. Hallucinogen b. Somniferous c. Inebriant d. Deliriant 1. LSD 2. Alcohol 3. Datura 4. Opium","options":["1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-b","1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b","1-a, 2-c, 3-d, 4-b","1-a. 2-c. 3-b. 4-d"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1-a, 2-c, 3-d, 4-b\\n\\nPsychoactive substances are classified based on their predominant effect on the central nervous system, which is essential for forensic identification and clinical management.\\n\\nCorrect matching:\\n\\n• LSD → Hallucinogen (a)\\n• Alcohol → Inebriant (c)\\n• Datura → Deliriant (d)\\n• Opium → Somniferous (b)\\n\\nKey concepts:\\n\\n• Hallucinogens (LSD): Cause perceptual disturbances, hallucinations, altered reality\\n• Inebriants (Alcohol): Cause euphoria, impaired judgment, loss of coordination\\n• Deliriants (Datura): Cause confusion, agitation, delirium, anticholinergic toxidrome\\n• Somniferous (Opium): Cause sedation, sleep, CNS depression","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7a4791244f","n":604,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Match the following:","options":["A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4, E-5","A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3, E-5","A-2, B-1, C-3, D-5, E-4","A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4, E-5"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4, E-5\\n\\n| Disorder | Key Feature\\n\\n| -------- | -----------\\n\\n| Eonism (Transvestism) | Wearing opposite sex’s clothes for sexual pleasure or identity\\n\\n| Frotteurism | Rubbing genitals against others in crowds\\n\\n| Necrophilia | Sexual activity with corpses\\n\\n| Exhibitionism | Exposing genitals to unsuspecting people\\n\\n| Passive Algolagnia | Sexual pleasure from receiving pain (masochism)","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-8ec2995bc1","n":605,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Match the poison with its antidote: 1. Methanol 2. Opium 3. Benzodiazepine 4. Arsenic a. Fomepizole b. Flumazenil c. BAL d. Naloxone","options":["1-a, 2-d, 3-b, 4-c","1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c","1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-d","1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-a, 2-d, 3-b, 4-c\\n\\nCorrect matching:\\n\\n• Methanol → Fomepizole (a)\\n• Opium → Naloxone (d)\\n• Benzodiazepine → Flumazenil (b)\\n• Arsenic → BAL (Dimercaprol) (c)\\n\\nKey concepts:\\n\\n• Methanol: Metabolized to toxic formic acid → Fomepizole inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase\\n• Opioids (Opium): Cause respiratory depression → Naloxone is a competitive opioid antagonist\\n• Benzodiazepines: CNS depression → Flumazenil reverses GABA-A receptor effects\\n• Arsenic: Heavy metal poisoning → BAL chelates arsenic for excretion","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b0267725af","n":606,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Methylene blue is used as an antidote for?","options":["Phosphorus","Aluminum phosphide","Mercury","Nitrites"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Nitrites\\n\\nMethylene blue is the antidote for methemoglobinemia, commonly caused by nitrite/nitrate poisoning.\\n\\n• Nitrites oxidize hemoglobin (Fe\xb2⁺ → Fe\xb3⁺) → form methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen\\n• Leads to functional hypoxia despite normal oxygen levels\\n\\nMechanism of action of methylene blue:\\n\\n• Acts as an electron donor via NADPH-dependent pathway\\n• Reduces methemoglobin (Fe\xb3⁺) back to hemoglobin (Fe\xb2⁺)\\n• Restores oxygen-carrying capacity\\n\\nClinical features of methemoglobinemia:\\n\\n• Cyanosis not improving with oxygen\\n• Chocolate-brown colored blood\\n• Dyspnea, fatigue, confusion\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Phosphorus / Aluminium phosphide: No role of methylene blue\\n• Mercury: Treated with chelators (e.g., BAL, DMSA)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d80287b0c3","n":607,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Most industrial workers are exposed to lead as an occupational hazard. Which of the following statements regarding lead poisoning are correct? 1. Lead poisoning is also called plumbism. 2. Basophilic stippling of red blood cells is seen. 3. Progressive massive fibrosis of lungs is seen. 4. Coproporphyrin in urine (CPU) is a useful screening test. Select the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 2 and 4\\n\\nLead poisoning (plumbism) is a common occupational hazard in battery manufacturing, painting, smelting, and printing industries. It causes microcytic hypochromic anemia with basophilic stippling of RBCs due to impaired heme synthesis.\\n\\nWhy is statement 3 incorrect?\\n\\n• 3. Progressive massive fibrosis of lungs is seen❌: Incorrect. It is a feature of coal workers’ pneumoconiosis/silicosis, not lead poisoning.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-262c2abbd9","n":608,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Poisoning with which of the following drugs causes hypothermia?","options":["Digoxin","Chlorpromazine","Iron","Alcohol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Chlorpromazine\\n\\nHypothermia is a characteristic feature of poisoning with phenothiazines (e.g., chlorpromazine) due to depression of the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center, along with peripheral vasodilation. Although alcohol intoxication can also cause hypothermia, chlorpromazine is the classic drug tested in toxicology MCQs.\\n\\nWhy are the other options incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Digoxin: Poisoning typically presents with cardiac arrhythmias, nausea, vomiting, visual disturbances (yellow vision), and hyperkalemia, not hypothermia.\\n• C. Iron: Iron poisoning causes gastrointestinal bleeding, metabolic acidosis, shock, and hepatic failure, not hypothermia.\\n• D. Alcohol: Alcohol can cause hypothermia by causing vasodilation and CNS depression, but chlorpromazine is the classical drug associated with hypothermia in poisoning questions.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c172753640","n":609,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Police brought a person from the railway track with features of dry mouth, dilated pupil, dry skin, slurring of speech, and delirium. What could be the possible diagnosis?","options":["Morphine","Alcohol","Cocaine","Dhatura"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Dhatura\\n\\nThe clinical picture of dry mouth, dilated pupils (mydriasis), dry skin, slurred speech, and delirium is characteristic of anticholinergic toxicity, classically seen in Datura poisoning.\\n\\n• Datura (contains atropine, scopolamine, hyoscyamine) → blocks muscarinic receptors\\n• Produces anticholinergic syndrome\\n\\nClassic features (mnemonic):\\n\\n• “Dry as a bone” → dry skin, dry mouth\\n• “Blind as a bat” → mydriasis\\n• “Hot as a hare” → hyperthermia\\n• “Red as a beet” → flushed skin\\n• “Mad as a hatter” → delirium\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Morphine: Pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression\\n• Alcohol: CNS depression, not anticholinergic features\\n• Cocaine: Dilated pupils but with sweating and agitation, not dry skin","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4cd47715aa","n":610,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Regarding oximes use in organophosphate poisoning, which statement is false? 1. Oximes cause more marked reactivation of acetylcholinesterase at skeletal neuromuscular junctions than at autonomic sites 2. Oximes contain quaternary nitrogen and bind to the anionic site of acetylcholinesterase 3. Clinical benefit of oximes does not depend on the type of organophosphate poisoning 4. Oximes react with phosphorus atom of organophosphates attached at the esteratic site and regenerate acetylcholinesterase","options":["3 only","1 and 2","1, 2 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 3 only\\n\\nReference from Katzung pharmacology:\\n\\n• “Organophosphates cause phosphorylation of the serine OH group at the active site of cholinesterase. The oxime group (=NOH) has a very high affinity for the phosphorus atom, for which it competes with serine OH. These oximes can hydrolyze the phosphorylated enzyme and regenerate active enzyme from the organophosphorus-cholinesterase complex if the complex has not “aged”\\n• It is most effective in regenerating the cholinesterase associated with skeletal muscle neuromuscular junctions.\\n• Pralidoxime and obidoxime are ineffective in reversing the central effects of organophosphate poisoning because each has positively charged quaternary ammonium groups that prevent entry into the CNS. Diacetylmonoxime, on the other hand, crosses the blood-brain barrier and, in experimental animals, can regenerate some of the CNS cholinesterase.”\\n\\nStatement 1. Oximes cause more marked reactivation of acetylcholinesterase at skeletal neuromuscular junctions than at autonomic sites: Correct. Oximes (e.g., pralidoxime) are especially effective at:\\n\\n• Neuromuscular junctions\\n• Improving muscle weakness/paralysis\\n\\nStatement 2. Oximes contain quaternary nitrogen and bind to the anionic site of acetylcholinesterase: Correct. Oximes:\\n\\n• Have quaternary nitrogen\\n• Bind to the anionic site of AChE\\n\\nStatement 3. Clinical benefit of oximes does not depend on the type of organophosphate poisoning: False. Clinical efficacy DOES depend on:\\n\\n• Type of organophosphate\\n• Time before “aging” occurs\\n\\nSome OP compounds respond poorly to oximes.\\n\\nStatement 4. Oximes react with phosphorus atom of organophosphates attached at the esteratic site and regenerate acetylcholinesterase: Correct. Oximes remove phosphate from inhibited acetylcholinesterase and regenerate enzyme activity.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1edbf02bf9","n":611,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Ricin causes toxicity primarily by:","options":["Inhibition of DNA synthesis","Inhibition of protein synthesis","Inhibition of RNA synthesis","Inhibition of ATP synthesis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Inhibition of protein synthesis\\n\\nRicin is a highly potent plant toxin derived from the seeds of the castor bean plant (Ricinus communis). It is a type II ribosome-inactivating protein (RIP) that causes cell death by irreversibly inhibiting protein synthesis.\\n\\n• Ricin consists of:\\n\\n• A chain: Enzymatically active; depurinates the 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit.\\n• B chain: Binds to cell surface glycoproteins and facilitates toxin entry into cells.\\n\\n• The A chain removes a specific adenine residue from 28S rRNA, preventing binding of elongation factors and halting protein synthesis.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Inhibition of DNA synthesis: Seen with drugs such as methotrexate or cytarabine, not ricin.\\n• C. Inhibition of RNA synthesis: Characteristic of actinomycin D or rifampicin.\\n• D. Inhibition of ATP synthesis: Seen with mitochondrial toxins (e.g., cyanide indirectly inhibits oxidative phosphorylation), not ricin.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-14dd758718","n":612,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"The method of autopsy carried out en masse to remove from tongue to prostate is?","options":["Virchow technique","Rokitansky technique","Ghon technique","Letulle technique"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Letulle technique\\n\\nThe Letulle technique of autopsy involves en masse removal of all thoracic, abdominal, and pelvic organs as a single block (from tongue to prostate).\\n\\n• Entire organ block is removed in continuity\\n• Allows better study of:\\n\\n• Anatomical relationships\\n• Spread of disease (e.g., malignancy, infection)\\n\\nComparison with other techniques:\\n\\n• Virchow technique: Organs removed one by one (individual removal)\\n• Rokitansky technique: In-situ dissection with minimal removal\\n• Ghon technique: Organs removed in organ blocks (en bloc), but not entire viscera together","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-60ce2957a6","n":613,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"This powder is used is a poison itself but is used in the treatment of which poisoning?","options":["Phosphorus","Arsenic","Mercury","Lead"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Phosphorus\\n\\nThe blue powder shown represents copper sulfate (CuSO₄), which is used in the management of phosphorus poisoning. Copper sulfate acts as:\\n\\n• Emetic → induces vomiting to expel ingested phosphorus\\n• Chemical antidote → oxidizes phosphorus and forms a protective coating (copper phosphide), reducing absorption\\n\\nClinical relevance in phosphorus poisoning:\\n\\n• Common source: rat poison, fireworks\\n• Features:\\n\\n• Garlic odor\\n• Luminous vomitus/stools\\n• Hepatic failure\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• Arsenic, Mercury, Lead: Treated with chelating agents (BAL, EDTA, DMSA), not copper sulfate","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-b301dcba50","n":614,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Urinary alkalinization as a method for enhancing excretion for poisoning is aimed at achieving:","options":["Serum pH 7.5–8","Urine pH 7.5–8","Serum pH &gt; 8","Urine pH &gt; 8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Urine pH 7.5–8\\n\\nUrinary alkalinization is used to enhance the elimination of weak acids (e.g., salicylates, phenobarbital) by ion trapping. The goal is to achieve a urine pH of 7.5–8.0 using intravenous sodium bicarbonate, which increases renal excretion of these toxins.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Serum pH 7.5–8: Excessive systemic alkalemia is dangerous and is not the therapeutic target.\\n• C. Serum pH >8: Incompatible with life; never a treatment goal.\\n• D. Urine pH >8:\xa0Unnecessary and difficult to achieve; target is 7.5–8.0.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8bdb6633fb","n":615,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"What is the antidote for belladonna poisoning?","options":["Physostigmine","Amitriptyline","Atropine","Flumazenil"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Physostigmine\\n\\nBelladonna poisoning results from ingestion of plants containing anticholinergic alkaloids such as atropine, hyoscyamine, and scopolamine. These agents competitively block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, producing the classic anticholinergic syndrome.\\n\\nPhysostigmine is the specific antidote because it is a reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that crosses the blood-brain barrier, thereby reversing both central and peripheral anticholinergic effects.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Amitriptyline: A tricyclic antidepressant with anticholinergic properties; it can worsen belladonna poisoning.\\n• C. Atropine: One of the toxic alkaloids in belladonna; it is not an antidote.\\n• D. Flumazenil: A benzodiazepine antagonist used for benzodiazepine overdose, with no role in anticholinergic poisoning.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d384b92ed2","n":616,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"What is the antidote of cyanide poisoning? A. Amyl Nitrite B. Sodium Nitrite C. Sodium Thiosulphate D. Benzyl Thiocyanate E. Sodium Nitroprusside","options":["ABC","ABCD","AB","ACD"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. ABC\\n\\nCyanide poisoning is rapidly fatal due to inhibition of cytochrome oxidase, leading to cellular hypoxia despite adequate oxygenation.\\n\\nAntidote regimen (Cyanide antidote kit):\\n\\n• Amyl nitrite (A) → inhalational, immediate use\\n• Sodium nitrite (B) → induces methemoglobinemia (binds cyanide)\\n• Sodium thiosulfate (C) → acts as sulfur donor, converts cyanide → thiocyanate (less toxic, excreted)\\n\\nMechanism summary:\\n\\n• Nitrites → form methemoglobin, which binds cyanide\\n• Thiosulfate → enhances detoxification via rhodanese enzyme\\n\\nWhy not others:\\n\\n• Benzyl thiocyanate: Not used clinically\\n• Sodium nitroprusside: Can actually release cyanide, not an antidote","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6519285a99","n":617,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which of the following best describes Zasko’s phenomenon?","options":["A postmortem tendon reflex after death","Collection of blood in dependent parts","Fragmentation of blood in retinal vessels","Increase in potassium level in vitreous"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A postmortem tendon reflex after death\\n\\nZasko’s phenomenon refers to the presence of postmortem tendon reflexes seen shortly after death.\\n\\n• After death, there may be transient neuromuscular excitability\\n• Mechanical stimulation (e.g., tapping a tendon) can produce a reflex-like muscle contraction\\n•\\n\\nOccurs in the early postmortem period before complete rigor mortis sets in\\n\\n•\\n\\nHelps in estimation of time since death\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Postmortem lividity: Collection of blood in dependent parts\\n• Retinal vessel fragmentation: Seen in certain asphyxial deaths\\n• Vitreous potassium: Used for time since death estimation, not reflexes","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2e6b927ec5","n":618,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which of the following centrally acting compounds does not depress the respiratory centre?","options":["Barbiturates","Opium","Gelsemium","Strychnine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Strychnine\\n\\nStrychnine is a central nervous system stimulant that causes disinhibition of spinal motor neurons by competitively blocking glycine receptors in the spinal cord. Unlike barbiturates, opioids, and gelsemium, it does not depress the respiratory centre. Death occurs due to sustained spastic muscle contractions leading to respiratory muscle spasm and asphyxia, rather than central respiratory depression.\\n\\n• Strychnine antagonizes glycine-mediated inhibitory neurotransmission in the spinal cord.\\n• Produces painful generalized muscle spasms, opisthotonus, and convulsions while the patient remains fully conscious.\\n• Respiratory failure results from tetanic spasm of respiratory muscles, not depression of the medullary respiratory centre.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Barbiturates: Cause dose-dependent CNS and respiratory centre depression.\\n• B. Opium (opioids): Depress the medullary respiratory centre, leading to respiratory failure.\\n• C. Gelsemium: Causes CNS depression with respiratory centre depression and respiratory paralysis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3a2d978859","n":619,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which of the following combinations of lab test results is indicative of heavy alcohol consumption, with more than 60% sensitivity and specificity?","options":["Gamma glutamyl transferase (GGT) &gt;35 U/L and alkaline phosphatase &gt;45 U/L","Carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT) &gt;20 U/L and serum uric acid &lt;7 mg/dL","High-normal MCV (&gt;91 fL) and CDT &lt;20 U/L","GGT &gt;35 U/L and CDT &gt;20 U/L"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. GGT >35 U/L and CDT >20 U/L\\n\\nNo single laboratory test is sufficiently sensitive or specific for detecting chronic heavy alcohol consumption. However, the combination of elevated Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) and Carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT) improves diagnostic accuracy, with more than 60% sensitivity and specificity for identifying heavy alcohol use.\\n\\n• GGT >35 U/L indicates chronic alcohol-induced hepatic enzyme induction but lacks specificity.\\n• CDT >20 U/L is a more specific biomarker of chronic heavy alcohol intake (typically ≥50–80 g ethanol/day for at least 1–2 weeks).\\n• Combining GGT and CDT provides better diagnostic performance than either marker alone.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. GGT > 35 U/L + alkaline phosphatase > 45 U/L: Alkaline phosphatase is not a marker of alcohol consumption and is elevated in cholestatic liver disease and bone disorders.\\n• B. CDT > 20 U/L + low serum uric acid (< 7 mg/dL): Heavy alcohol use is more commonly associated with hyperuricemia, not low uric acid.\\n• C. High-normal MCV (> 91 fL) + low CDT (< 20 U/L): Macrocytosis (raised MCV) may occur with chronic alcohol use, but a low CDT argues against significant heavy alcohol consumption.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-40e05cb0d6","n":620,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which of the following is contraindicated in Iron overdose?","options":["BAL","Desferrioxamine","EDTA","All of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. BAL\\n\\nIn iron overdose, the specific antidote is Desferrioxamine, while BAL (Dimercaprol) is contraindicated because it can form toxic complexes with iron and worsen toxicity.\\n\\n• Iron poisoning causes:\\n\\n• Severe GI irritation (vomiting, bleeding)\\n• Metabolic acidosis\\n• Hepatic failure\\n\\n• Desferrioxamine chelates iron → forms ferrioxamine (excreted in urine)\\n\\nWhy BAL is contraindicated:\\n\\n• Forms lipid-soluble toxic complexes with iron\\n• Enhances iron toxicity instead of reducing it\\n\\nOther chelators:\\n\\n• EDTA: Used for lead poisoning, not iron","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-263bed8aa7","n":621,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which of the following is false regarding adipocere formation?","options":["Facial features are not preserved making identification difficult","It inhibits growth of putrefactive bacteria","It is commonly seen in bodies immersed in water","It has distinct offensive or sweetish smell"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Facial features are not preserved making identification difficult\\n\\nAdipocere is a postmortem change where body fat undergoes hydrogenation → waxy, soap-like substance formation, typically in moist, anaerobic environments.\\n\\nA. Facial features are not preserved making identification difficult →\xa0 False\\n\\n• Adipocere preserves facial features, often aiding identification.\\n\\nB. It inhibits growth of putrefactive bacteria →\xa0 True\\n\\n• The process retards decomposition by inhibiting bacterial activity.\\n\\nC. It is commonly seen in bodies immersed in water → True\\n\\n• Classic setting: wet soil, water, sealed moist environments\\n\\nD. It has distinct offensive or sweetish smell →\xa0 True\\n\\n• Typically described as rancid, sweetish, or cheesy odor\\n• Best preservation → adipocere & mummification\\n• Adipocere favors: moisture + anaerobic + fat-rich tissues\\n• Timeframe: can begin within 1–3 weeks, well formed by months\\n• Common sites: cheeks, buttocks, breasts (fat-rich areas)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9189827dcf","n":622,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which of the following statements are correct regarding scorpion stings? 1. Scorpion injects venom from a stinger on their tail. 2. Most stings are painful, but relatively harmless. 3. Anticholinergics can be used if antivenom is unavailable. Select the correct answer using the code given below.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1 and 2 only\\n\\nScorpion venom is injected through the stinger on the tail and causes an autonomic storm due to massive release of catecholamines and acetylcholine. Most scorpion stings cause severe local pain only and are self-limiting, but some (especially Mesobuthus tamulus in India) can cause life-threatening cardiovascular and respiratory complications.\\n\\nWhy not statement 3?\\n\\n• Anticholinergics are not recommended for scorpion envenomation.\\n• If antivenom is unavailable, prazosin is the drug of choice to counter the autonomic effects, along with supportive care.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1f961705d2","n":623,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which of the following statements regarding snakebite is incorrect.","options":["Viper bite is vasculotoxic, and give ASV in systemic envenomation","Cobra bite is myotoxic","Cobra belongs to Elapidae family","All snake venom contains Hyaluronidase"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cobra bite is myotoxic\\n\\nCobra envenomation is primarily neurotoxic, not myotoxic, making option B incorrect.\\n\\n• Cobra (Elapidae family):\\n\\n• Venom is neurotoxic → causes ptosis, respiratory paralysis\\n\\n• Viper (Viperidae family):\\n\\n• Venom is vasculotoxic/hemotoxic → causes bleeding, coagulopathy\\n\\n• Sea snakes:\\n\\n• Venom is predominantly myotoxic → causes muscle breakdown, myoglobinuria\\n\\nOther important points:\\n\\n• ASV (Anti-snake venom) is indicated in systemic envenomation (e.g., bleeding, neuroparalysis)\\n• Hyaluronidase is present in most snake venoms → helps spread of venom","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0f1c529424","n":624,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which one of the following drugs helps to maintain abstinence by reducing craving for alcohol?","options":["Apomorphine","Acamprosate","Atropine","Azathioprine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Acamprosate\\n\\nAcamprosate is an anti-craving medication used to maintain abstinence in patients with alcohol use disorder after detoxification.\\n\\n• Modulates glutamatergic (NMDA) neurotransmission and enhances GABAergic activity.\\n• Reduces alcohol craving and decreases the risk of relapse. It does not treat acute alcohol withdrawal.\\n\\nWhy are the other options incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Apomorphine: False.\\n\\n• A dopamine agonist used mainly for Parkinson disease and as a rescue therapy for “off” episodes.\\n• Not used in alcohol dependence.\\n\\n• C. Atropine: False.\\n\\n• An antimuscarinic agent used for bradycardia, organophosphate poisoning, and pre-anesthetic medication.\\n• No role in alcohol dependence.\\n\\n• D. Azathioprine: False.\\n\\n• An immunosuppressant used in autoimmune diseases and organ transplantation.\\n• No role in alcohol use disorder.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a601f586f5","n":625,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which one of the following is the correct description of Mee’s lines, seen in chronic arsenic poisoning?","options":["Transverse white lines on nails of fingers and toes","Transverse red lines on the skin of palms and soles","Transverse white lines on the skin of palms and soles","Transverse red lines on the nails of fingers and toes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Transverse white lines on nails of fingers and toes\\n\\nMee’s lines are transverse white (leukonychia striata) lines that appear across the nails of the fingers and toes following systemic arsenic poisoning. They result from temporary disruption of nail matrix keratinization and gradually move distally as the nail grows.\\n\\nMee’s lines are a classical physical finding in chronic arsenic poisoning.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Transverse red lines on the skin of palms and soles: Not a feature of arsenic poisoning.\\n• C. Transverse white lines on the skin of palms and soles: Incorrect; Mee’s lines occur on the nails, not the skin.\\n• D. Transverse red lines on the nails of fingers and toes: Incorrect; Mee’s lines are white, not red.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5c59a45c79","n":626,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which one of the following prevents gastrointestinal absorption of thallium?","options":["Calcium carbonate","Prussian blue","Penicillamine","Potassium permanganate"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Prussian blue\\n\\nThallium poisoning is a rare but potentially fatal heavy metal intoxication. Prussian blue (ferric hexacyanoferrate) is the antidote of choice because it binds thallium in the gastrointestinal tract, interrupting its enterohepatic circulation and preventing further gastrointestinal absorption and reabsorption, thereby enhancing fecal elimination.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Calcium carbonate: Used as an antacid and phosphate binder; it has no role in thallium poisoning.\\n• C. Penicillamine: Chelating agent used for Wilson’s disease, copper poisoning, cystinuria, and occasionally lead poisoning, but not for thallium.\\n• D. Potassium permanganate: Previously used as a gastric oxidizing agent in certain poisonings but has no role in preventing thallium absorption.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f6672c95e1","n":627,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which paraphilia is characterized by a male obtaining sexual gratification from cross-dressing?","options":["Fetishism","Eonism","Frotteurism","Exhibitionism"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Eonism\\n\\nUnderstand that eonism (transvestic fetishism) is a paraphilia in which a heterosexual male derives sexual arousal or gratification from wearing clothing traditionally associated with females. It should be distinguished from gender dysphoria, where cross-dressing is related to gender identity rather than sexual arousal.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Fetishism: Sexual arousal is obtained from inanimate objects or specific non-genital body parts, not from cross-dressing itself.\\n• C. Frotteurism: Sexual gratification is achieved by rubbing or touching a non-consenting person, usually in crowded places.\\n• D. Exhibitionism: Involves exposing one’s genitals to an unsuspecting stranger for sexual excitement.\\n\\nHigh-yield Pearl:\\n\\n| Paraphilia | Characteristic Feature\\n\\n| ---------- | ----------------------\\n\\n| Eonism (Transvestic fetishism) | Sexual gratification from cross-dressing\\n\\n| Fetishism | Sexual arousal from objects/body parts\\n\\n| Frotteurism | Touching/rubbing against a non-consenting person\\n\\n| Exhibitionism | Exposing genitals to strangers","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-580ea691f9","n":628,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Which poisoning is indicated when gastric aspirate turns black upon addition of silver nitrate (AgNO₃)?","options":["Aluminium phosphide","Mercury","Lead","Barium"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Aluminium phosphide\\n\\nAluminium phosphide poisoning is suggested when gastric aspirate turns black on addition of silver nitrate (AgNO₃).\\n\\n• Aluminium phosphide releases phosphine gas (PH₃) in the stomach\\n• Phosphine reacts with silver nitrate → black precipitate (silver phosphide)\\n• This reaction is used as a bedside diagnostic test\\n\\nClinical features of aluminium phosphide poisoning:\\n\\n• Severe metabolic acidosis\\n• Refractory shock\\n• Garlic or rotten fish odor (phosphine)\\n• High mortality\\n\\nWhy not other options:\\n\\n• Mercury, Lead, Barium: Heavy metal poisonings → do not give this silver nitrate reaction","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5e824c2948","n":629,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","topic":"Pyqs","text":"Whole Bowel Irrigation (WBI) as a method of gastrointestinal decontamination is useful for poisoning with:","options":["Alcohol","Iron","Kerosene","Organophosphorus"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Iron\\n\\nWhole Bowel Irrigation (WBI) is a method of gastrointestinal decontamination in which large volumes of polyethylene glycol-electrolyte solution (PEG-ELS) are administered orally or via a nasogastric tube to rapidly flush the gastrointestinal tract. It is particularly useful for substances that are poorly adsorbed by activated charcoal, such as iron.\\n\\nWhy are the other options incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Alcohol ❌\\n\\n• Alcohols are rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, so WBI is ineffective.\\n\\n• C. Kerosene ❌\\n\\n• Gastric decontamination is contraindicated due to the high risk of aspiration pneumonitis.\\n\\n• D. Organophosphorus ❌\\n\\n• Management focuses on airway support, atropine, and pralidoxime; WBI has no routine role.","img":false}]}')}}]);